            <!-- ─────────── §1.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.1 The Problem Before Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>You've built <strong>easypj</strong> — a Django <code>jobpost</code> app where employers list jobs filtered by Skills, Location, and Author, backed by PostgreSQL. It works perfectly on your laptop. But how do you run it in production so thousands of users can reach <code>/jobs/</code> reliably, 24/7, while you sleep?</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Container Orchestration</h5>
                    <p><strong>Container orchestration</strong> is the automated management of containerized applications across many machines — placement, scaling, networking, healing, and rollout — done by <em>software, not humans</em>. Kubernetes is the industry-standard orchestrator, and as a CKA administrator you build and maintain the orchestration platform itself.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Evolution of Deployment</h4>
                <p>Deployment has moved through four eras. Each fixed the previous one's pain — and introduced new challenges:</p>
                <div class="evolution-strip">
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🖥️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">2000s</div>
                        <h5>Bare Metal</h5>
                        <p>One app per physical server. No isolation, massive waste. easypj would need its own dedicated box.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status bad">❌ Wasteful &amp; rigid</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">💻</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">2010s</div>
                        <h5>Virtual Machines</h5>
                        <p>Many VMs per server. Better isolation, but each runs a full guest OS — heavy and slow to provision.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status ok">⚠️ Heavy overhead</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">📦</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">2015+</div>
                        <h5>Containers</h5>
                        <p>Lightweight, fast, shared kernel. But managing hundreds by hand across servers is a nightmare.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status ok">🟡 Almost there…</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item active">
                        <div class="evo-icon">☸️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">2018+</div>
                        <h5>Kubernetes</h5>
                        <p>Orchestrated containers: auto-heal, auto-scale, zero-downtime deploys. The admin builds &amp; maintains this platform.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Production-ready</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Without Kubernetes vs With Kubernetes — the easypj story</div>
<pre>
  ❌ WITHOUT KUBERNETES — one box, all your eggs in it
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ 🖥️  ONE server   (you hand-installed the whole stack)                      │
  │     🐍 easypj-web :8000      🐘 postgres :5432      🌐 nginx :80            │
  │                                                                          │
  │   💀 the process crashes   →  the WHOLE site is down until you log in      │
  │   📈 traffic doubles       →  buy &amp; configure another server, by hand      │
  │   🚀 ship a new version    →  scp files, restart, pray; no clean rollback  │
  │   🌙 3 AM page             →  YOU wake up and fix it                        │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                              │
                          ────▼────   the SAME app, orchestrated:
  ✅ WITH KUBERNETES — a self-healing, self-scaling fleet
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              🌐 users ──▶ Service / Ingress   (one stable address)         │
  │                                │ load-balances across replicas           │
  │              ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐                       │
  │              ▼                 ▼                 ▼                        │
  │         📦 easypj-web      📦 easypj-web      📦 easypj-web    :8000         │
  │         └── Node 1 ──┘     └── Node 1 ──┘     └── Node 2 ──┘                │
  │         🐘 postgres :5432   ── mounts ──▶  💾 PVC   (data survives)          │
  │                                                                          │
  │   🔄 a pod crashes  →  recreated in seconds      📈 load up  →  autoscaled  │
  │   🚀 new version    →  rolling update, 0 downtime    😴 3 AM  →  you sleep   │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Containers Alone Aren't Enough</h4>
                <p>Docker gives you <em>a</em> container. Kubernetes gives you an <em>army</em> of containers that manage themselves. Compare the two on-call realities:</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>😰 Running easypj WITHOUT Kubernetes</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>🔴 SSH in at 3 AM when the process crashes</li>
                            <li>🔴 Restart Django by hand every time it dies</li>
                            <li>🔴 No load balancing — one box serves all <code>/jobs/</code> traffic</li>
                            <li>🔴 Deploy = SCP files + pray nothing breaks</li>
                            <li>🔴 Scale = buy a server, install everything from scratch</li>
                            <li>🔴 Rollback = restore from backup (if you made one)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>🎉 Running easypj WITH Kubernetes</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ Pod crashes → restarted in seconds; you stay asleep</li>
                            <li>✅ Traffic spikes → HPA adds pods automatically</li>
                            <li>✅ New version → rolling update, zero downtime</li>
                            <li>✅ Bad deploy → <code>kubectl rollout undo</code>, instant</li>
                            <li>✅ Node fails → pods migrate to healthy nodes</li>
                            <li>✅ Config change → update a ConfigMap, pods pick it up</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Make It Concrete — Anatomy of a 3 AM Outage</h4>
                <p>Abstract benefits are easy to nod along to. Here's the same incident — easypj's process dies at 3:00 AM — played out second-by-second in both worlds. This is <em>why</em> teams adopt Kubernetes:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Same crash, two timelines</div>
<pre>
  ❌ WITHOUT KUBERNETES                        ✅ WITH KUBERNETES
  ───────────────────────────────────────     ───────────────────────────────────────
  03:00  easypj process crashes 💥             03:00  easypj pod crashes 💥
  03:00  users get 502s, /jobs/ is DOWN        03:00  kubelet's liveness probe fails
  03:12  monitoring finally pages you 📟        03:01  K8s restarts the container (10s) 🔄
  03:18  you wake up, find your laptop 😴       03:01  readiness probe passes → back in
  03:25  VPN in, SSH to the box                       the Service's endpoints
  03:32  read logs, guess the cause             03:01  users never noticed ✅
  03:40  restart by hand, watch it              03:00  YOU ARE ASLEEP 😴 the whole time
  03:45  service restored — 45 MIN DOWN ⛔
  next day: write the post-mortem 📝            next day: nothing to write 🎉

  Downtime:  ~45 minutes, 1 very tired human    Downtime:  ~10 seconds, 0 humans
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 What just happened (preview of mechanisms)</h5>
                    <p>In the "with" column, three Kubernetes features cooperated automatically: a <strong>liveness probe</strong> detected the dead container, a <strong>controller</strong> restarted it to honor "I want 1 running," and a <strong>readiness probe + Service</strong> kept traffic away until it was healthy again. You'll build all three on easypj in Part 4 — for now, just notice that <em>detection, recovery, and traffic-routing</em> all happened with no human in the loop.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy</h5>
                    <p>A lone server is a <strong>solo musician</strong> — if they stop, the music stops. Kubernetes is the <strong>conductor of an orchestra</strong>: it decides who plays what and instantly covers anyone who drops out, so the performance never stops.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What an Orchestrator Actually Does — the 6 Hard Problems</h4>
                <p>"Orchestration" is an abstract word. Concretely, Kubernetes automates <strong>six</strong> jobs you would otherwise do by hand. Every feature in this entire guide is one of these six in disguise — so it's worth memorizing the list:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>#</th><th>Problem</th><th>By hand (without K8s)</th><th>Kubernetes does it via…</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Scheduling</strong> — where does each container run?</td><td>You pick a server and SSH in</td><td>kube-scheduler places pods by resources/affinity</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Self-healing</strong> — what if it dies?</td><td>You get paged, restart it manually</td><td>Controllers recreate pods; probes restart sick ones</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Scaling</strong> — handle more/less load</td><td>Buy + configure servers; edit configs</td><td><code>kubectl scale</code> / HPA / Cluster Autoscaler</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Service discovery + load balancing</strong></td><td>Hard-code IPs; configure nginx upstreams</td><td>Services + CoreDNS give a stable name &amp; VIP</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Rollouts &amp; rollbacks</strong></td><td>Copy files, restart, hope; restore backups</td><td>Deployments: rolling update + <code>rollout undo</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Config &amp; secret management</strong></td><td>Edit files on each box; secrets in plaintext</td><td>ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets injected at runtime</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>"Why Can't I Just Use Docker + a Restart Script?"</h4>
                <p>This is <em>the</em> beginner question — and answering it precisely is what makes the rest of the guide click. A bash script wrapping <code>docker run</code> in a <code>while true; do … done</code> loop really can keep a container alive… <strong>on one machine</strong>. The catch is that every serious production need is a <em>distributed-systems</em> problem, and a single-machine script is structurally blind to the rest of the fleet:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>What you actually need</th><th>Why a single-box script <em>structurally can't</em> do it</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Place each container on the machine with free CPU/RAM</td><td>The script only sees its own box — it has no view of the whole fleet's spare capacity</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Keep running when a machine dies</td><td>If the box dies, the script dies with it — nothing is left to react or move the work</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>One stable address for N moving replicas</td><td>Replicas come and go with changing IPs; a script can't track them or load-balance across them</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Zero-downtime version upgrades</td><td>Needs <em>coordinated, health-gated</em> replacement across replicas — not a blind local restart</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Identical config &amp; secrets everywhere</td><td>A script edits one box at a time; configuration drift creeps in across the fleet</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Kubernetes is the program that holds the <strong>whole-cluster view</strong> and solves all of these continuously. That's the real leap: from <em>"keep this process up on this box"</em> to <em>"keep this <strong>service</strong> healthy across the entire fleet."</em> Containers gave us a portable <em>unit</em>; Kubernetes gives us a brain that places, heals, connects, and upgrades thousands of those units without a human deciding <em>where</em> or <em>when</em>.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The blind spot — a script sees one box; Kubernetes sees the cluster</div>
<pre>
   🔧 DOCKER + RESTART SCRIPT                ☸️  KUBERNETES (the orchestrator)
   ┌──────────────────────────┐             ┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🖥️ node-1  (all it sees)  │             │  sees ALL nodes + their free capacity   │
   │   while true:             │             │  ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐       │
   │     docker run easypj     │             │  │ node-1 │ │ node-2 │ │ node-3 │       │
   │   ↳ restarts here ONLY    │             │  │ 60% used│ │ 20% used│ │ 90% used│      │
   │   ✗ node dies? script too │             │  └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘       │
   │   ✗ no idea node-2 exists │             │   ↳ "place the new easypj pod on node-2 │
   │   ✗ can't load-balance    │             │      (most free), and if node-1 dies,   │
   │   ✗ can't roll out safely │             │      reschedule its pods elsewhere"      │
   └──────────────────────────┘             └───────────────────────────────────────┘
        one machine's view                          the whole-cluster view
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🐮 Mental Model: Pets vs Cattle</h5>
                    <p>The single most important shift Kubernetes asks of you:</p>
                    <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <div class="card-icon-lg">🐶</div>
                            <h5>Pets (the old way)</h5>
                            <p>Servers have names, are hand-fed, and you nurse them back to health when sick. One dies → crisis. <em>"server-prod-01 is acting up again."</em></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="info-card highlight">
                            <div class="card-icon-lg">🐄</div>
                            <h5>Cattle (the K8s way)</h5>
                            <p>Pods are numbered, identical, and disposable. One dies → it's replaced automatically and you never notice. You manage the <em>herd</em>, not the individual.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0">This is why pods are <strong>ephemeral</strong> (they come and go), why you never SSH into one to "fix" it, and why state lives <em>outside</em> the pod (in a database or a PersistentVolume). Internalize this now — it explains 80% of Kubernetes design decisions.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Admin Perspective</h5>
                    <p>Your job isn't just deploying apps — it's <strong>building and maintaining the platform</strong> that makes all of the "with Kubernetes" column possible: installing the cluster, managing certificates, upgrading versions, backing up etcd, and troubleshooting at the infrastructure level.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.2 What Is Kubernetes? (The Simple Answer)</h3>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Official Definition</h5>
                    <p><strong>"An open-source system for automating deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications."</strong></p>
                    <p>In plain English: Kubernetes is an <em>operating system for your containers</em>. You tell it <strong>what</strong> you want — "3 copies of easypj:v2, always running" — and it figures out <strong>how</strong> to make that true across your cluster, and keeps it true.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🚢 Analogy: the Shipping Port (why the logo is a ship's wheel)</h5>
                    <p>The name comes from the Greek <em>κυβερνήτης</em> — "<strong>helmsman</strong>," the person who steers a ship. The ☸️ logo is a ship's wheel. The analogy runs deep:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Shipping port 🚢</th><th>Kubernetes ☸️</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>A standard <strong>shipping container</strong> — any cargo, any ship, any crane</td><td>A <strong>container image</strong> — runs the same anywhere</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The <strong>ship</strong> that carries many containers</td><td>A <strong>node</strong> running many pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The <strong>harbor master</strong> deciding which ship docks where</td><td>The <strong>scheduler</strong> deciding which node runs each pod</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The <strong>manifest</strong> — the document listing what's aboard</td><td>Your YAML <strong>manifest</strong> — what should be running</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The <strong>helmsman</strong> steering it all to the goal</td><td><strong>Kubernetes</strong> steering actual state toward desired</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">You don't tell the helmsman every rudder movement — you give the destination and trust the steering. That's declarative control in one image.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Heart of It: Declarative Control</h4>
                <p>You describe the <strong>end state</strong> you want; Kubernetes continuously makes reality match it — restarting, rescheduling, and scaling on its own. You never say "start a container on node 2."</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Declarative — you DECLARE, Kubernetes DELIVERS</div>
<pre>
  📝 YOU WRITE (deploy.yaml)                ⚙️ KUBERNETES CONTINUOUSLY GUARANTEES
  ┌───────────────────────────┐            ┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ apiVersion: apps/v1       │            │ ✅ exactly 3 easypj pods are RUNNING     │
  │ kind: Deployment          │   apply    │ ✅ every pod runs image easypj:v2        │
  │ spec:                     │  ───────▶  │ ✅ each pod capped at 256Mi memory       │
  │   replicas: 3             │            │ ✅ a pod crashes  → restarted in seconds │
  │   image: easypj:v2        │            │ ✅ a node dies    → pods rescheduled     │
  │   resources: 256Mi        │            │ ✅ new version    → rolled out, 0 downtime│
  └───────────────────────────┘            └───────────────────────────────────────┘
        "DESIRED state"                              "ACTUAL state"
        (you declare it; stored in etcd)             (what's really running on nodes)
              │                                              ▲
              │                  🔄 THE RECONCILE LOOP        │
              └──▶ observe ACTUAL ──▶ compare to DESIRED ──▶ act to close the gap ──┘
                   (runs forever — this is essentially the ONLY thing Kubernetes does)

  ┌─ what "act to close the gap" actually means ─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   actual 2  &lt; desired 3   →  create 1 pod         pod is unhealthy   →  restart it  │
  │   actual 4  &gt; desired 3   →  delete 1 pod         image v1 ≠ v2      →  roll out v2  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  💡 You never list the STEPS — only the DESTINATION. The loop figures out the rest.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why "Declarative + Reconciliation" Is Such a Big Deal</h4>
                <p>It's easy to read "you declare desired state" and shrug. But this single design choice is <em>why</em> Kubernetes won — it hands you three superpowers for free, none of which a step-by-step (imperative) system can offer:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">♻️</div>
                        <h5>1 · Idempotency</h5>
                        <p>Apply the same manifest once or a hundred times — same result. If reality already matches, the loop finds no gap and does nothing. No "already exists" errors, no half-applied state.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>2 · Self-healing</h5>
                        <p>Because the loop never stops checking, a crash or a dead node is just a <em>new gap</em> to close. Recovery isn't a special code path — it's the <em>same</em> reconciliation running again.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>3 · Git as source of truth</h5>
                        <p>Desired state is a file, so it can live in Git — reviewed, versioned, diffed, rolled back. That's the entire basis of <strong>GitOps</strong> (Chapter 39).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>Contrast that with an imperative script ("create this, then start that, then…"). If it fails halfway, you're left in an unknown in-between state, and re-running it may double-create or error out. The declarative model sidesteps all of that: <strong>you never describe the steps, only the destination</strong> — so there's no fragile sequence to get wrong, and the cluster is free to reach the goal whatever way is currently safe. Hold onto this; literally every feature ahead (Deployments, HPA, rollouts, operators) is just a controller closing a gap.</p>

                <h4>Quick Facts</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">☸️</div>
                        <h5>Name Origin</h5>
                        <p><strong>K8s</strong> = K + <em>8 letters</em> + s. Greek <em>κυβερνήτης</em> = "helmsman" — steering the ship.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏛️</div>
                        <h5>Born at Google</h5>
                        <p>Evolved from Google's internal <strong>Borg</strong>. Open-sourced 2014, donated to the <strong>CNCF</strong>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔤</div>
                        <h5>Written in Go</h5>
                        <p>Built in Google's Go for high concurrency, compiled performance, and clean APIs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>Apache 2.0</h5>
                        <p>Fully open-source — free to use, modify, and run commercially.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes Lineage — from Borg to K8s</div>
<pre>
    2003 ───────────────▶ 2013 ───────────────▶ 2014 ───────────────▶ 2018 ─────▶ today
  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐
  │ 🏛️ Google BORG    │─▶│ 🔬 Google OMEGA   │─▶│ ☸️  KUBERNETES     │─▶│ 🎓 CNCF GRADUATE  │
  │ internal cluster │  │ next-gen         │  │ Borg's ideas,    │  │ 1st CNCF project │
  │ manager; ran     │  │ scheduler        │  │ rebuilt &amp; OPEN-  │  │ to graduate;     │
  │ Gmail, Search,   │  │ research:        │  │ SOURCED, donated │  │ now among the    │
  │ YouTube, Maps    │  │ flexible queues, │  │ to the CNCF.     │  │ largest open-    │
  │ for 15+ years    │  │ multi-scheduler  │  │ Written in Go.   │  │ source projects  │
  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘
   "run everything       lessons learned        "give everyone        the de-facto
    in containers"        → cleaner design        Borg, for free"       standard ☸️
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kubernetes by the Numbers</h4>
                <div class="stat-bar-group">
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">CNCF survey — organizations using/evaluating K8s</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">96%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:96%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Fortune 100 companies running Kubernetes</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">77%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width:77%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">GitHub stars — top-tier OSS project</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">112K+</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:88%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Active contributors</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">3,500+</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width:70%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Declarative vs Imperative — See the Difference</h4>
                <p>This distinction is the heart of working with Kubernetes (and a recurring exam theme). <strong>Imperative</strong> = you give step-by-step commands ("do this, now"). <strong>Declarative</strong> = you describe the end state in a file and let K8s reconcile it. Same result, very different philosophy:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">imperative — fast, one-off</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Tell K8s exactly what to do, step by step</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj \
  --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=5
kubectl set image deployment/easypj \
  easypj=easypj:v3
<span class="comment"># Great for speed on the exam,</span>
<span class="comment"># but nothing records your intent.</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">declarative — the K8s way</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Describe the end state in a file…</span>
<span class="comment"># deploy.yaml:  replicas: 5, image: easypj:v3</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml
<span class="comment"># Run it again? No harm — already matches.</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml   <span class="comment"># idempotent</span>
<span class="comment"># The file is the source of truth → Git,</span>
<span class="comment"># code review, and GitOps all work.</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Best of both on the exam</h5>
                    <p>Generate YAML <em>imperatively</em>, then apply it <em>declaratively</em>: <code>kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml</code>, tweak, then <code>kubectl apply -f d.yaml</code>. You get imperative speed <em>and</em> a declarative artifact.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Kubernetes Is <em>NOT</em></h4>
                <p>Understanding the boundaries is as important as understanding the features — it stops you from expecting things K8s doesn't do:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏗️</div>
                        <h5>Not a build tool</h5>
                        <p>It does <strong>not</strong> build container images. You build with Docker/BuildKit/Kaniko in CI; K8s only <em>runs</em> the resulting images.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔁</div>
                        <h5>Not a CI/CD system</h5>
                        <p>It doesn't run your test pipeline. Tools like GitHub Actions, Jenkins, or Argo/Flux sit <em>around</em> K8s to deliver into it.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">☁️</div>
                        <h5>Not a PaaS</h5>
                        <p>Unlike Heroku, it won't "just run my code." It orchestrates <em>containers</em> — you still package the app and declare how it runs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗄️</div>
                        <h5>Not a database (and won't manage your data)</h5>
                        <p>etcd stores <em>cluster</em> state, not your app data. Persisting easypj's PostgreSQL data is <em>your</em> job (PersistentVolumes, backups).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Remember the equation</h5>
                    <p><strong>Desired State → [control loops] → Actual State</strong>. Every Kubernetes feature you'll learn — Deployments, self-healing, autoscaling, rollouts — is just a controller closing the gap between what you declared and what's actually running.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.3 The CKA Perspective: Administrator vs Developer</h3>
                <p>There are two Kubernetes certifications from the Linux Foundation, and they test fundamentally different skills. Knowing the boundary tells you exactly what to study.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👩‍💻</div>
                        <h5>CKAD — Developer</h5>
                        <p>"I deploy &amp; configure <strong>apps</strong> on a cluster someone else runs."</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Create Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps</li>
                            <li>Configure probes &amp; resource limits</li>
                            <li>Build multi-container pods</li>
                            <li>Manage the application lifecycle</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>CKA — Administrator ← YOU</h5>
                        <p>"I <strong>build, operate &amp; troubleshoot the cluster itself</strong>."</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Install clusters with kubeadm</li>
                            <li>Manage certificates, PKI &amp; RBAC</li>
                            <li>Back up &amp; restore etcd</li>
                            <li>Upgrade versions, drain &amp; patch nodes</li>
                            <li>Configure networking (CNI, CoreDNS)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The administrator owns EVERYTHING below the app</div>
<pre>
          🌐 users ───▶ GET /jobs/        (the easypj app, serving traffic)
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   👩‍💻 DEVELOPER (CKAD) — owns the APP layer
        📦 Pods · Deployments · Services · ConfigMaps · Secrets · health probes
  ───────────────────────────── ⬇ the dividing line ⬇ ───────────────────────────
   🔧 ADMINISTRATOR (CKA ← YOU) — owns EVERYTHING below the app:
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────┐
   │ 🛡️  Access &amp; security    RBAC · ServiceAccounts · PodSecurity       │ Arch 25% │
   │ 🌐 Networking            CNI · CoreDNS · kube-proxy · Ingress       │ Net  20% │
   │ 💾 Storage               PV · PVC · StorageClasses · CSI            │ Stor 10% │
   │ 🧠 Control plane         apiserver · etcd · scheduler · ctrl-mgr    │ Arch 25% │
   │ 🔐 Cluster lifecycle     kubeadm install · upgrades · certs / PKI   │ Arch 25% │
   │ 🖥️  Nodes &amp; runtime       kubelet · containerd · drain / cordon      │ Arch/Tbl │
   │ 🩺 Keeping it alive      troubleshoot · etcd backup/restore · HA    │ Tbl  30% │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────┘
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
          🖧 infrastructure   (VMs · bare metal · cloud instances)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CKA Exam Domain Weights</h4>
                <p>The CKA tests five domains. Know exactly where each topic fits — and that <strong>troubleshooting is the single biggest slice</strong>:</p>
                <div class="stat-bar-group">
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">25%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:25%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Workloads &amp; Scheduling</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">15%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width:15%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Services &amp; Networking</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">20%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:20%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Storage</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">10%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width:10%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Troubleshooting</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">30%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:30%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Troubleshooting is 30% of the CKA</h5>
                    <p>Unlike the CKAD (which is about building apps), the CKA dedicates <strong>30%</strong> to fixing broken clusters — failed nodes, crashed control-plane components, networking and DNS issues — under time pressure. Part 9 of this guide is built entirely around a repeatable diagnostic method for exactly this.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Five Domains, Explained — What Each One Actually Tests</h4>
                <p>The percentages above are just bars until you know what hides behind each. Here's the plain-English meaning of every domain, the kind of task you'll be handed, and where easypj meets it in this guide — so the exam blueprint becomes a concrete study plan instead of five numbers:</p>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📋 The CKA 2025 blueprint, decoded</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏗️</span> Cluster Architecture <strong>25%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Build &amp; configure the cluster itself: install with <code>kubeadm</code>, manage RBAC, set up Helm/Kustomize, understand the control plane. <em>Typical task:</em> "create a ServiceAccount + Role + RoleBinding so easypj can read its ConfigMap." → Parts 1, 3, 7, 11.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚙️</span> Workloads &amp; Scheduling <strong>15%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Run apps correctly: Deployments, rolling updates/rollbacks, scaling, scheduling (affinity, taints), resource limits, config/secrets. <em>Typical task:</em> "roll easypj to v2 with zero downtime, then roll it back." → Part 4.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> Services &amp; Networking <strong>20%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Connect &amp; expose: Services, DNS/CoreDNS, Ingress, the Gateway API, NetworkPolicies. <em>Typical task:</em> "expose easypj on a NodePort and restrict who may reach the DB." → Part 5.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💾</span> Storage <strong>10%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Persist data: PersistentVolumes, PVCs, StorageClasses, dynamic provisioning. <em>Typical task:</em> "give easypj's PostgreSQL a 5Gi PVC that survives a pod restart." → Part 6.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🩺</span> Troubleshooting <strong>30%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Diagnose &amp; fix broken things: NotReady nodes, crashed control-plane pods, failing Services/DNS, stuck workloads. <em>Typical task:</em> "easypj pods are Pending / the node is NotReady — find and fix it." → Part 9 (the biggest, most point-dense domain).</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 How to read these weights as a strategy</h5>
                    <p>Troubleshooting (30%) + Architecture (25%) = <strong>over half the exam</strong>, and both reward <em>method</em> over memorization. Storage is only 10% but its tasks are quick, high-certainty points — never skip them. The smart play: bank the fast Storage/Workloads tasks first, then spend your remaining time on the heavier Troubleshooting and Architecture problems.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>CKA vs CKAD — Who Owns What</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>CKAD (Developer)</th><th>CKA (Administrator) ← YOU</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Focus</strong></td><td>App deployment &amp; config</td><td>Cluster lifecycle &amp; infrastructure</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Format</strong></td><td>2 h · ~15–20 hands-on tasks</td><td>2 h · ~15–20 hands-on tasks</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Passing score</strong></td><td>66%</td><td>66%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>Not tested</td><td>Backup, restore, snapshot</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubeadm</strong></td><td>Not tested</td><td>Install, upgrade, configure</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Certificates</strong></td><td>Not tested</td><td>CSR approval, TLS, rotation</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Node management</strong></td><td>Not tested</td><td>Drain, cordon, troubleshoot</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Networking</strong></td><td>Services, Ingress basics</td><td>CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy, NetworkPolicy</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>CKA Exam Logistics — Quick Reference</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Exam Format</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Duration:</strong> 2 hours</li>
                            <li><strong>Tasks:</strong> 15–20 performance-based</li>
                            <li><strong>Passing:</strong> 66%</li>
                            <li><strong>Environment:</strong> live Linux terminal (browser)</li>
                            <li><strong>Version:</strong> latest stable K8s</li>
                            <li><strong>Retake:</strong> 1 free retake included</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📚</div>
                        <h5>Allowed Resources</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code></li>
                            <li>✅ <code>kubernetes.io/blog</code></li>
                            <li>✅ <code>github.com/kubernetes</code></li>
                            <li>❌ No other sites, notes, or AI tools</li>
                            <li>💡 Practice navigating the docs <em>fast</em></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Kubernetes Release Cycle — What an Admin Must Track</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes ships <strong>three minor versions a year</strong> (~every 4 months: v1.31, v1.32, v1.33…). As the admin, keeping the cluster inside the supported window — and upgrading it safely — is <em>your</em> recurring job, and a guaranteed exam task. Each release has a life:</p>
                <div class="evolution-strip">
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🆕</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">Month 0</div>
                        <h5>New Release</h5>
                        <p>e.g. v1.33 ships — new features, deprecations, and bug fixes land.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Latest</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item active">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🛡️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">~0–14 months</div>
                        <h5>Supported</h5>
                        <p>Gets patch releases (security &amp; bug fixes). The CKA tests within this window.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Patched</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">⚠️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">14+ months</div>
                        <h5>End of Life</h5>
                        <p>No more patches. Running EOL = a growing security risk → you must upgrade.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status bad">❌ Unsupported</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Golden Upgrade Rule: one minor version at a time</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes only supports upgrading <strong>one minor version at a step</strong> — <code>1.31 → 1.32 → 1.33</code>. You <strong>cannot skip</strong> (no 1.31 → 1.33 jump). There's also a <strong>version-skew</strong> rule: the kubelet may lag the API server by up to 3 minors but never run ahead of it. The CKA puts you in front of a real cluster and asks you to upgrade it with <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (control plane first, then workers) — you'll do exactly this in Chapter 28.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Exam Day Actually Looks Like</h4>
                <p>Knowing the format removes half the stress. The CKA is taken <strong>remotely, in your browser</strong>, proctored live through the PSI platform — here's the flow end-to-end:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Check-in</h5><p>Show photo ID, do a 360° room scan via webcam. Desk must be clear; you're watched the whole time.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>The terminal</h5><p>A real Linux terminal in the browser, with multiple clusters. You SSH/context-switch between them per task.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>15–20 tasks</h5><p>Each is weighted and independent. A score bar shows weights — do the cheap, high-value ones first.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Docs allowed</h5><p>One extra tab to <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code> (+ blog, GitHub). Nothing else. Practice copy-paste from the docs.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Flag &amp; move on</h5><p>Stuck &gt; a few minutes? Flag it, move on, return later. Never leave a task blank — partial credit is real.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Results &amp; retake</h5><p>Score by email in ~24 h. 66% to pass. One <strong>free retake</strong> included; the cert is valid <strong>3 years</strong>.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 The #1 exam-day habit: set context first, every task</h5>
                    <p>Each task may use a <em>different</em> cluster/namespace. The most common way to lose points is doing correct work in the <strong>wrong place</strong>. Start every single task with the context switch the question gives you:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">muscle memory for the exam</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config use-context &lt;cluster-from-the-question&gt;
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;   <span class="comment"># then work</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>Free, realistic practice: the <strong>killer.sh</strong> CKA simulator (2 sessions included with your exam registration) mirrors the real environment closely — Chapter 45 builds a full timed simulator on easypj.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three Kubernetes Certifications</h4>
                <p>The Linux Foundation offers three hands-on Kubernetes certs. They build on each other — and the CKA (this guide) is the foundation the others assume:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👩‍💻</div>
                        <h5>CKAD — Developer</h5>
                        <p><strong>Focus:</strong> deploying &amp; configuring apps. <br><strong>Owns:</strong> Pods, Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, probes, multi-container patterns.</p>
                        <p><span class="badge">2 h · 66% to pass</span></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>CKA — Administrator ← this guide</h5>
                        <p><strong>Focus:</strong> building &amp; operating the cluster. <br><strong>Owns:</strong> install, etcd, certs, upgrades, nodes, RBAC, networking, storage, troubleshooting.</p>
                        <p><span class="badge badge-exam">2 h · 66% to pass</span></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>CKS — Security Specialist</h5>
                        <p><strong>Focus:</strong> hardening &amp; securing clusters. <br><strong>Owns:</strong> Pod Security, supply-chain, runtime security, network policy, audit. <em>Requires a valid CKA first.</em></p>
                        <p><span class="badge">2 h · 67% to pass</span></p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Which order?</h5>
                    <p>CKA is the natural starting point: it's the platform layer the other two stand on. CKAD overlaps with CKA on workloads; CKS literally <em>requires</em> you to already hold the CKA. Master this guide and you're well-positioned for all three.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy</h5>
                    <p>CKAD <strong>furnishes the rooms</strong>; CKA <strong>builds &amp; maintains the house</strong> — foundations, plumbing, and wiring included; CKS <strong>installs the locks, alarms, and security cameras</strong>. This guide is the house.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.4 Kubernetes Core Concepts — The Big Picture</h3>
                <p>Everything in Kubernetes is built from five ideas. Lock these in now — they're the mental model you'll use for the entire CKA journey.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-5">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗂️</div>
                        <h5>1 · Cluster</h5>
                        <p>A set of machines (nodes) Kubernetes manages as a single unit.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧠</div>
                        <h5>2 · Control Plane</h5>
                        <p>The "brain" — makes all scheduling &amp; orchestration decisions.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💪</div>
                        <h5>3 · Worker Nodes</h5>
                        <p>The "muscle" — actually run your containerized apps.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>4 · Pod</h5>
                        <p>Smallest deployable unit — 1+ containers sharing IP &amp; storage.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>5 · Desired State</h5>
                        <p>You declare <em>what</em> you want; K8s continuously keeps it that way.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Words You'll Keep Hearing — a Beginner's Glossary</h4>
                <p>Before the diagrams, here's a plain-English decoder for the terms that appear on every page from here on. Bookmark this — you won't feel lost again:</p>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📖 Core vocabulary</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> container</div><div class="anatomy-value">A packaged, isolated process — your app + its dependencies. The thing that actually runs easypj.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🖼️</span> image</div><div class="anatomy-value">The read-only template a container is started from, e.g. <code>easypj:v2</code>. Built once, run anywhere.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> pod</div><div class="anatomy-value">The smallest thing K8s schedules — one (or a few) containers that share an IP &amp; storage.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💪</span> node</div><div class="anatomy-value">A machine (VM or physical) that runs pods. The cluster is a set of nodes.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗂️</span> cluster</div><div class="anatomy-value">All your nodes + the control plane, managed as one system.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏷️</span> namespace</div><div class="anatomy-value">A virtual folder that partitions a cluster (e.g. <code>easypj</code>, <code>kube-system</code>) for isolation &amp; quotas.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📄</span> manifest</div><div class="anatomy-value">A YAML file describing a desired object (a Deployment, Service…). The "what you want."</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🧩</span> object / resource</div><div class="anatomy-value">Any thing K8s tracks in etcd — Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Node. Everything is an object.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> controller</div><div class="anatomy-value">A loop that watches objects and drives actual → desired (e.g. the Deployment controller).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⌨️</span> kubectl</div><div class="anatomy-value">The command-line tool you use to talk to the API server ("koob-cuddle" / "koob-control").</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Objects Nest — the Workload Hierarchy</h4>
                <p>Beginners often blur Pod, ReplicaSet, and Deployment. They're <strong>layers</strong>, each wrapping the one below and adding a capability. You almost always create the <em>outermost</em> one and let it manage the rest:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod → Container (inside a Namespace)</div>
<pre>
  🏷️ Namespace: easypj   (a virtual folder that isolates these objects)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ 🚀 Deployment  "easypj-web"                       ← you create THIS     │
  │    adds ▶ rolling updates · rollback · revision history                │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  "easypj-web-6d8f9"   (one per pod-template version) │ │
  │  │    adds ▶ "keep exactly N pods alive"  → self-healing              │ │
  │  │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐   N = 3      │ │
  │  │  │ 📦 Pod         │  │ 📦 Pod         │  │ 📦 Pod         │            │ │
  │  │  │  …-6d8f9-x7k2 │  │  …-6d8f9-m9p4 │  │  …-6d8f9-q2j7 │            │ │
  │  │  │ ┌──────────┐ │  │ ┌──────────┐ │  │ ┌──────────┐ │            │ │
  │  │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │ ← container│ │
  │  │  │ └──────────┘ │  │ └──────────┘ │  │ └──────────┘ │            │ │
  │  │  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘            │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │  🕓 old ReplicaSet "easypj-web-5a1c" → scaled to 0, kept for rollback   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   naming chain ▶  Deployment ──(+ pod-template-hash)──▶ ReplicaSet ──(+ random)──▶ Pod
   rule of thumb ▶  you create the Deployment; it makes the ReplicaSet; the RS makes the
                    Pods; each Pod runs your container(s). You almost never make a bare Pod.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why not just create Pods directly?</h5>
                    <p>A bare Pod is a <em>pet</em> — if it dies, it's gone for good (nothing recreates it). Wrap it in a ReplicaSet and you get self-healing; wrap that in a Deployment and you also get rolling updates &amp; rollback. That's why in real life you create <strong>Deployments</strong>, almost never naked Pods.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Architecture — Bird's-Eye View</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One easypj cluster at a glance</div>
<pre>
                          👩‍💻 admin / CI
                               │  kubectl   (HTTPS, port 6443)
                               ▼
   ╔══════════════════════ 🧠 CONTROL PLANE — "the brain" (decides) ══════════════════════╗
   ║                                                                                       ║
   ║   ┌─────────────────────┐      watch       ┌──────────────────────────┐               ║
   ║   │ 📋 kube-scheduler     │ ───────────────▶ │                          │               ║
   ║   │  "which node fits?"   │                  │   📡 kube-apiserver        │               ║
   ║   └─────────────────────┘                  │   the ONE front door     │               ║
   ║   ┌─────────────────────┐      watch       │   authn → authz →        │   read /      ║
   ║   │ 🔄 controller-manager │ ───────────────▶ │   admission → validate   │ ◀═══════════▶ ║
   ║   │  "keep 3 replicas up" │                  │                          │   write       ║
   ║   └─────────────────────┘                  └──────────────────────────┘     ▲         ║
   ║                                                                              ║         ║
   ║                                                            ┌─────────────────╨──────┐  ║
   ║   ⚠️ ONLY the API server touches etcd. Every other          │ 🗄️  etcd  (key-value DB) │  ║
   ║      component watches/acts THROUGH the API server.        │     = ALL cluster state │  ║
   ║                                                            └────────────────────────┘  ║
   ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
                  │  scheduler binds pod → node ; the node's kubelet then pulls & runs it
   ═══════════════╪═══════════════════ 🌐 flat pod network (CNI: Calico / Cilium) ═══════════════
                  ▼
   ┌──────────── 💪 WORKER NODE 1 ────────────┐     ┌──────────── 💪 WORKER NODE 2 ────────────┐
   │ 🤖 kubelet  ·  🔀 kube-proxy  ·  🐋 containerd │     │ 🤖 kubelet  ·  🔀 kube-proxy  ·  🐋 containerd │
   │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐ │     │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
   │ │ 📦 easypj-web   :8000   ip 10.244.1.5     │ │     │ │ 🐘 postgres     :5432   ip 10.244.2.4    │ │
   │ │ 📦 easypj-web   :8000   ip 10.244.1.6     │ │     │ │    └─ mounts 💾 PVC  (durable disk)       │ │
   │ │ 📦 easypj-web   :8000   ip 10.244.1.7     │ │     │ │ 🤖 kubelet ── reports status ──▶ API svr │ │
   │ └────────────────────────────────────────┘ │     │ └────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────┘     └───────────────────────────────────────────┘

   DESIRED (declared, stored in etcd):  3 × easypj-web  +  1 × postgres
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶  reconciled, forever
   ACTUAL (actually running on the nodes):  exactly that  ✅

   Legend   ──▶ request / data flow      ◀── watch = a component observes desired state via the API
            📡 API server = the hub (only it reads/writes 🗄️ etcd)   🌐 CNI = the network all pods share
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Preview — the components (full deep-dive in Chapter 3)</h5>
                    <p>You'll meet each component properly in <strong>Chapter 3: Kubernetes Architecture</strong>. For now, just learn the one-liners — they make every later chapter click.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🧠 Control Plane Components — the admin must know these (Architecture = 25%)</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📡</span> kube-apiserver</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>front door</strong> — ALL communication goes through it (kubectl, node heartbeats, controllers). It's the <em>only</em> component that talks to etcd. If it's down, the cluster is blind.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗄️</span> etcd</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The cluster's <strong>memory</strong> — a distributed key-value store holding ALL cluster state. <strong>CKA tests backup &amp; restore.</strong> Lose etcd with no backup = lose the whole cluster.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> kube-scheduler</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>matchmaker</strong> — watches for unscheduled Pods and picks the best node by resources, affinity, and taints/tolerations.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> kube-controller-manager</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>watchdog</strong> — runs control loops that compare desired vs actual and act ("only 2 of 3 replicas → create 1 more").</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">☁️</span> cloud-controller-manager</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Cloud glue</strong> (optional) — integrates with AWS/Azure/GCP for load balancers, disks, and node lifecycle. Absent on bare-metal.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What Happens When Each Component Goes Down?</h4>
                <p>Memorizing what each component <em>does</em> is half the battle; the deeper understanding — and what the 30% troubleshooting domain really tests — is knowing what <strong>breaks</strong> when each one fails. This table is worth more than any definition, because it turns "the scheduler schedules pods" into "if pods are stuck <code>Pending</code>, look at the scheduler":</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>If this is down…</th><th>What you observe</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td><code>kubectl</code> fails; nothing can be changed. <em>Running pods keep running.</em></td><td>It's the only door to the cluster — no reads/writes, no scheduling, no controller actions. The cluster "freezes" but doesn't crash.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>API server can't serve → whole control plane unusable. Lost with no backup = cluster gone.</td><td>It holds <em>all</em> state. No etcd, no source of truth to read or write.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>New pods stay <code>Pending</code> forever. Existing pods are unaffected.</td><td>Nothing is left to assign pods to nodes — they sit unscheduled.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>No self-healing: a crashed pod isn't replaced, scaling/rollouts stall, dead nodes aren't acted on.</td><td>The reconciliation loops live here — without them, desired ≠ actual is never corrected.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong> (on a node)</td><td>That node goes <code>NotReady</code>; after a grace period its pods are rescheduled elsewhere.</td><td>No agent to run/report pods on that node, so the control plane writes it off.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The one insight that makes troubleshooting click</h5>
                    <p>The control plane is the <strong>brain</strong>; running pods are the <strong>body</strong>. Knock out the brain (API server / etcd) and the body keeps moving on reflex — your apps stay up, you just can't <em>change</em> anything. This is why an etcd or apiserver outage is silent to users but paralyzing to admins, and why "my app still works but <code>kubectl</code> is dead" points straight at the control plane, not the workload. Match the <em>symptom</em> to the <em>component</em> and you've solved most of the exam's troubleshooting tasks before typing a command.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>💪 Worker Node Components</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🤖</span> kubelet</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>node agent</strong> — takes pod specs from the API server, makes the containers run &amp; stay healthy, and reports node status back.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔀</span> kube-proxy</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>network plumber</strong> — programs the node's dataplane rules so Services route to the right pods.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> container runtime</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>engine</strong> that actually runs containers — containerd (default) or CRI-O via the CRI. Docker-shim was removed in v1.24.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Desired vs Actual State — the Declarative Magic</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Reconciliation Loop</div>
<pre>
                 ┌────────────────── 🔄 ONE CONTROLLER, RUNNING FOREVER ──────────────────┐
                 │                                                                        │
     ┌───────────▼───────────┐      ┌───────────────────┐      ┌─────────────────────────┐
     │ 1. 👁️ OBSERVE           │ ───▶ │ 2. 🧮 DIFF          │ ───▶ │ 3. 🛠️ ACT                 │
     │ read ACTUAL (cluster)   │      │ does ACTUAL match  │      │ create / delete / update │
     │ + DESIRED (from etcd)   │      │ DESIRED? a gap?    │      │ objects to close the gap │
     └─────────────────────────┘      └───────────────────┘      └────────────┬────────────┘
                 ▲                                                             │
                 └──────────────────────────── repeat ◀─────────────────────────┘

  easypj tick-by-tick — the Deployment controller, desired replicas = 3:
    t0   observe 0 pods    · diff: need 3    · act: create 3            → 3 running
    t1   observe 3 pods    · diff: none       · act: nothing  (idempotent — no churn)
    t2   a pod crashes 💥   · observe 2 pods  · diff: need 1   · act: create 1  → 3 running
    t3   you set image v2  · diff: v1 ≠ v2    · act: replace pods one-by-one  → all v2

  💡 Recovery is NOT a special code path — it's the SAME loop noticing a NEW gap.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How a Pod Gets Created — the Full Journey</h4>
                <p>Here's the single most clarifying flow in all of Kubernetes: what <em>actually</em> happens between you typing <code>kubectl apply</code> and easypj serving traffic. Watch how every component plays its part. (Chapter 3 dissects each step; this is the map.)</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubectl apply → a running easypj pod</div>
<pre>
  👩‍💻 kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml
        │  (1) HTTPS request
        ▼
  📡 kube-apiserver ──(2) authn → authz → admission → validate──┐
        │                                                       │
        │  (3) write DESIRED object                             │
        ▼                                                       │
  🗄️ etcd  "Deployment easypj wants 3 pods"  ◀─────────────────┘
        │
        │  (4) controller-manager sees Deployment → makes ReplicaSet → 3 Pod objects
        │      (Pods exist but have NO node yet → "Pending")
        ▼
  📋 kube-scheduler  (5) "which node fits?"  → binds Pod → Node 2 (writes to API→etcd)
        │
        ▼
  🤖 kubelet on Node 2  (6) "a pod was assigned to me!"
        │   ├─ 🐋 tells containerd to PULL easypj:v2 and START the container
        │   └─ 🌐 CNI plugin gives the pod its IP
        ▼
  📦 easypj pod RUNNING :8000   (7) kubelet reports status ──▶ API server ──▶ etcd
        │
        ▼
  🔄 controllers keep watching forever: actual=3 = desired=3  ✅  (reconciled)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Submit</h5><p>kubectl sends your YAML to the API server over TLS.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Admit</h5><p>API server authenticates, authorizes (RBAC), runs admission, validates.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Persist</h5><p>Desired state is written to etcd — the single source of truth.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Expand</h5><p>Controllers turn the Deployment into a ReplicaSet into Pod objects (Pending).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Schedule</h5><p>The scheduler picks the best node and binds each pod to it.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Run</h5><p>That node's kubelet pulls the image, starts the container, CNI assigns an IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">7</div><h5>Report</h5><p>kubelet reports status up; controllers reconcile forever.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Why this matters for troubleshooting</h5>
                    <p>Every "my pod won't run" problem lives at <em>one</em> of these steps. <strong>Pending</strong> = stuck at step 5 (scheduler can't place it). <strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> = step 6 (kubelet can't pull). <strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> = step 6 (container starts then dies). Knowing the journey tells you exactly <em>where</em> to look — the core skill behind the 30% troubleshooting domain.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why declarative matters for the CKA</h5>
                    <p>The exam rewards working declaratively: declare desired state, <code>kubectl apply -f</code>, and trust the controllers. It's also the key to troubleshooting — when something breaks, you compare <strong>desired state</strong> (in etcd) against <strong>actual state</strong> (on the nodes) and find the gap.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.5 The Kubernetes Ecosystem</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes is a <strong>core that everything plugs into</strong>. It sits at the center of a large ecosystem of runtimes, plugins, and platforms — all governed by the <strong>Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)</strong>. As an admin you'll touch many of these daily.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The ecosystem orbit — a core everything plugs into</div>
<pre>
                  📦 PACKAGING                          🔄 GitOps / DELIVERY
               Helm · Kustomize                          Argo CD · Flux
                        ╲                                    ╱
                         ╲                                  ╱
   🐋 RUNTIME (CRI)    ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐    🌐 NETWORK (CNI)
   containerd · CRI-O ─┤                                        ├─ Calico · Cilium · Flannel
                       │        ☸️   KUBERNETES  CORE             │
   💾 STORAGE (CSI)    │   the orchestration engine:            │    📊 OBSERVABILITY
   EBS · PD · Ceph ───┤   schedule · heal · scale · reconcile   ├─ Prometheus · Grafana
   NFS                 │        (drives desired → actual)        │    Loki · OpenTelemetry
                       └────────────────────────────────────────┘
                         ╱                                  ╲
                        ╱                                    ╲
                  🕸️ SERVICE MESH                       🛡️ SECURITY / POLICY
               Istio · Linkerd                          Kyverno · OPA Gatekeeper

   ☁️  Everything around the core is governed by the CNCF (Cloud Native Computing Foundation).
   💡 CRI · CNI · CSI are the three "sockets" Kubernetes DEFINES — vendors just supply the plugs.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Key Ecosystem Categories for the CKA</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>Container Runtimes (CRI)</h5>
                        <p><strong>containerd</strong> (default since v1.24), <strong>CRI-O</strong>. K8s talks to them via the Container Runtime Interface; the Docker shim was removed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>CNI Plugins (networking)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Calico</strong> &amp; <strong>Cilium</strong> (with NetworkPolicy), <strong>Flannel</strong> (simple overlay), <strong>Weave</strong>. You install a CNI during cluster setup — no CNI = NotReady nodes.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Packaging</h5>
                        <p><strong>Helm</strong> (templated charts — the "apt for K8s") and <strong>Kustomize</strong> (template-free overlays). Both are 2025 CKA topics.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>GitOps</h5>
                        <p><strong>ArgoCD</strong>, <strong>FluxCD</strong> — Git is the source of truth; a controller syncs the cluster to match and self-heals drift.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🕸️</div>
                        <h5>Service Mesh</h5>
                        <p><strong>Istio</strong>, <strong>Linkerd</strong> — observability, traffic management, and mTLS between services without touching app code.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📊</div>
                        <h5>Observability</h5>
                        <p><strong>Prometheus</strong> (metrics), <strong>Grafana</strong> (dashboards), <strong>Loki</strong> (logs), <strong>Jaeger</strong> / <strong>OpenTelemetry</strong> (traces). The CKA expects <code>kubectl top</code> + metrics-server.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CNCF — who governs Kubernetes?</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>Cloud Native Computing Foundation</strong> (part of the Linux Foundation) stewards Kubernetes and 170+ related projects through three maturity levels — <strong>Sandbox → Incubating → Graduated</strong>. Kubernetes was the first to graduate (2018). The CNCF also runs the CKA &amp; CKAD certifications.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="arch-layers">
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-app">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🎓</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Graduated</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Production-proven, widely adopted</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Kubernetes</span><span>Prometheus</span><span>containerd</span><span>CoreDNS</span><span>etcd</span><span>Helm</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-k8s">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🧪</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Incubating</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Growing adoption, maturing features</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Argo</span><span>Cilium</span><span>Knative</span><span>Kyverno</span><span>Backstage</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-infra">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🌱</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Sandbox</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Early-stage, experimental</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Headlamp</span><span>OpenCost</span><span>Inspektor Gadget</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What "Cloud Native" Actually Means</h4>
                <p>"Cloud native" is the phrase behind the CNCF's name and gets thrown around constantly — but it's rarely defined. It's not "runs in the cloud." It's a <strong>way of building software</strong> so it can be operated by software (like Kubernetes). Five properties define it, and easypj will gain each one as we go:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Containerized</h5><p>Each piece is packaged with its dependencies into an image — portable and isolated. (easypj → a container in Ch 2.)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧩</div><h5>Loosely coupled</h5><p>Independent services talk over the network (web ↔ DB), so each can scale, fail, and deploy on its own.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🤖</div><h5>Dynamically orchestrated</h5><p>A platform schedules, scales, and heals it automatically — no human placing processes on machines.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>Declarative</h5><p>Desired state lives in version-controlled manifests, not in someone's head or a runbook.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>Resilient &amp; observable</h5><p>It expects failure and recovers; it exposes metrics, logs, and traces so you can see what it's doing.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why the term matters to you</h5>
                    <p>Every tool in the orbit above exists to deliver one of these five properties. So "cloud native" isn't marketing — it's the <em>checklist</em> the whole ecosystem is built around. When you finish this guide, easypj will satisfy all five, and you'll be the administrator who keeps the platform that makes that possible running.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three "Sockets" — How Kubernetes Stays Pluggable (CRI · CNI · CSI)</h4>
                <p>Why does such a huge ecosystem exist? Because Kubernetes itself doesn't run containers, wire up networks, or attach disks. Instead it defines three <strong>standard interfaces</strong> — think of them as universal sockets — and lets vendors plug in. This is <em>the</em> reason Kubernetes runs identically on your laptop, on bare metal, and on every cloud.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes defines the socket; you choose the plug</div>
<pre>
                       ☸️  KUBERNETES  (defines 3 standard interfaces)
                                       │
        ┌──────────────────────┬───────┴───────────────┬──────────────────────┐
        ▼                      ▼                        ▼
   🔌 CRI                  🔌 CNI                    🔌 CSI
   Container Runtime IF    Container Network IF      Container Storage IF
   kubelet asks:           K8s asks:                 K8s asks:
   "run / stop this        "give this pod            "attach this disk
    container"              an IP &amp; connect it"        to the pod"
        │                      │                         │
   any plug speaking ▼     any plug speaking ▼       any plug speaking ▼
   🐋 containerd · CRI-O   🌐 Calico · Cilium ·       💾 AWS EBS · GCP PD ·
                             Flannel                    Ceph · NFS
        │                      │                         │
        ▼                      ▼                         ▼
   starts the easypj       easypj pod 10.244.1.5     postgres data lives on a
   container on the node   reaches any pod, no NAT   real disk, survives restarts

   💡 Swap any plug (e.g. containerd → CRI-O) and Kubernetes ITSELF doesn't change.
      THAT is why one set of easypj manifests runs on a laptop, bare metal &amp; every cloud.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>CRI — Runtime</h5>
                        <p>How the kubelet asks something to <strong>run a container</strong>. Default: <strong>containerd</strong>. (You'll configure this in Chapter 9 when installing the cluster.)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>CNI — Network</h5>
                        <p>How pods get an <strong>IP and reach each other</strong>. You install a CNI (Calico/Cilium) right after <code>kubeadm init</code> — without one, nodes stay NotReady (Chapter 17).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>CSI — Storage</h5>
                        <p>How a <strong>real disk</strong> gets attached to a pod for durable data — exactly how easypj's PostgreSQL keeps its files (Chapter 22).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The big idea</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes is a <strong>platform for building platforms</strong>: it owns the orchestration logic and delegates the "hardware-touching" parts to pluggable drivers. Whenever you see "install a CNI" or "pick a StorageClass," you're choosing a plug for one of these sockets. Keep this model in your head — it makes Parts 5 (networking) and 6 (storage) click instantly.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.6 How Companies Use Kubernetes (a day in an admin's life)</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes isn't just for tech giants. Companies of every size run it — scale changes the <em>job</em>, not the <em>fundamentals</em>.</p>

                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Company Size</th><th>K8s Setup</th><th>Admin Tasks</th><th>Team</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>🏠 <strong>Startup</strong> (5–20)<br>e.g. easypj</td>
                            <td>1 cluster, few nodes. Minikube → managed cloud (EKS/AKS/GKE).</td>
                            <td>Deploy, monitor, basic troubleshooting, simple CI/CD.</td>
                            <td>1 admin does it all</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>🏢 <strong>Mid-size</strong> (50–200)</td>
                            <td>2–5 clusters. Separate dev / staging / prod; multi-team RBAC.</td>
                            <td>CI/CD, RBAC, upgrades, monitoring, cost control, policies.</td>
                            <td>2–4 admins</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>🏙️ <strong>Enterprise</strong> (500+)</td>
                            <td>Many HA, multi-region clusters; service mesh; custom networking.</td>
                            <td>HA &amp; DR, compliance, GitOps, multi-cluster, platform engineering.</td>
                            <td>Dedicated platform team</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>A Day in the Life of a CKA Administrator</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Morning Check</h5>
                        <p>Review dashboards; check node health, pod restarts, and overnight alerts.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Troubleshoot</h5>
                        <p>Investigate failed pods/nodes: <code>describe</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Ship Changes</h5>
                        <p>Apply upgrades/RBAC/config via GitOps or PRs — staging first, then prod.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Secure &amp; Audit</h5>
                        <p>Review audit logs, rotate certificates that are due, verify NetworkPolicies, and check RBAC for over-permissioned accounts.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Maintain</h5>
                        <p>Drain → patch → uncordon a node; verify the latest etcd backup restores.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">6</div>
                        <h5>Capacity Plan</h5>
                        <p>Watch resource trends; add nodes or right-size requests/limits.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Real Companies Running Kubernetes at Scale</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎵</div><h5>Spotify</h5><p>Migrated 150+ services to K8s; deployments dropped from hours to minutes.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛒</div><h5>Shopify</h5><p>Survives Black Friday by auto-scaling clusters to ~10× normal load.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎮</div><h5>Pokémon GO</h5><p>Launched on GKE; scaled from 1× to ~50× expected traffic in days.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏦</div><h5>Capital One</h5><p>Runs regulated banking workloads on K8s — proof it works under compliance.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Organizations Actually Adopt Kubernetes (and What It Costs)</h4>
                <p>Spotify and Capital One didn't adopt Kubernetes for the logo — they adopted it because it pays back three concrete dividends. Understanding these tells you <em>when</em> K8s is the right call (and, honestly, when it isn't):</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚀</div>
                        <h5>1 · Velocity</h5>
                        <p>Ship more often, safely. Rolling updates + instant rollback + a declarative pipeline turn risky, hours-long deploys into routine, minutes-long ones — so teams release many times a day instead of monthly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>2 · Resilience</h5>
                        <p>Self-healing + rescheduling + HA mean a crashed pod or a dead node is a non-event, not a 3 AM page. Uptime stops depending on a human being awake.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💰</div>
                        <h5>3 · Efficiency</h5>
                        <p>Bin-packing many containers onto shared nodes (and autoscaling them) drives up utilization and drives down the cloud bill — versus one fat VM per app sitting mostly idle.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>There's a fourth, quieter dividend: <strong>portability</strong>. Because workloads target the Kubernetes API (not a specific cloud), the same easypj manifests run on your laptop, on bare metal, and on any cloud — which is real leverage against lock-in.</p>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚖️ The honest trade-off — it isn't free</h5>
                    <p>Those dividends are bought with <strong>operational complexity</strong>: a control plane to run (or pay a cloud to run), certificates that expire, upgrades to perform, networking and storage to wire up, and a real learning curve. That complexity <em>is the administrator's job</em> — which is precisely why the CKA exists and why this skill is valuable. The rule of thumb: Kubernetes pays off when you have <em>multiple services, real scale/uptime needs, and frequent releases</em>; for a single low-traffic app, it's usually more machine than the job requires (see the "when NOT to use it" box below).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Managed vs Self-Managed — Who Runs the Control Plane?</h4>
                <p>There are two ways to get a cluster, and the difference decides what's <em>your</em> job. The CKA deliberately tests the <strong>self-managed</strong> path (you run everything), because once you understand that, managed clusters are easy.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>🛠️ Self-managed (kubeadm) — what the CKA tests</th><th>☁️ Managed (EKS / GKE / AKS)</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Control plane</strong></td><td>You install &amp; run it (API server, etcd, scheduler…)</td><td>The cloud runs &amp; upgrades it for you</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd backups</strong></td><td>Your responsibility (Chapter 29)</td><td>Handled by the provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Upgrades</strong></td><td>You run <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (Chapter 28)</td><td>Click/CLI; you still upgrade node pools</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Nodes</strong></td><td>You join &amp; maintain them</td><td>Managed node pools / autoscaling</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>You still own</strong></td><td colspan="2" class="tac">Workloads · RBAC · NetworkPolicy · storage · ingress — <strong>everything in this guide transfers to both</strong></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why learn the hard (self-managed) way?</h5>
                    <p>Because it makes you <em>unbreakable</em>. When a managed cluster misbehaves, the engineers who understand etcd, certificates, and the kubelet are the ones who fix it. Chapter 36 maps every skill here onto EKS/GKE/AKS — same concepts, the provider just runs the control plane.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚫 Honest Take: When NOT to Use Kubernetes</h5>
                    <p>Maturity means knowing the limits. Kubernetes is <em>overkill</em> when:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>A single small app, low traffic</strong> — a managed PaaS (Heroku, Render, Fly.io) or one VM is simpler and cheaper.</li>
                        <li><strong>No one to operate it</strong> — K8s adds real operational complexity; without an admin (you!) it becomes a liability.</li>
                        <li><strong>Static workloads that never scale</strong> — if you'll never need scaling, healing, or rolling updates, you're paying complexity for nothing.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">Use it when you have <strong>multiple services</strong>, need <strong>resilience &amp; scale</strong>, ship <strong>frequently</strong>, or want a <strong>portable</strong> platform across clouds — which is exactly where easypj is headed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Where you fit in — the easypj journey</h5>
                    <p>This guide follows <strong>easypj</strong> — our Django <code>jobpost</code> board — from a single-server app to a production-grade cluster. You'll containerize it, deploy it, scale it, secure it, break it, fix it, and ship it. By the end you'll pass the CKA <em>and</em> be able to run real clusters.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 1.7 OBJECT MODEL & API — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.7 The Object Model &amp; the Kubernetes API</h3>
                <p>Everything you will ever create is a <strong>Kubernetes object</strong> — a persisted record of intent the cluster works to make real. Four ideas make the whole API click: the <em>shape</em> of every object, how you <em>name</em> it, how the API is <em>organised &amp; versioned</em>, and the <em>ways you apply</em> it. Master these and manifests stop being magic.</p>

                <h4>① The four required fields — in every manifest, no exceptions</h4>
                <p>Every object you write carries the same four top-level fields. Omit one — or mis-case <code>kind</code> — and the apiserver rejects the manifest before it ever reaches etcd.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>apiVersion</code></td><td>Which API group + version defines this object (so the server knows its schema)</td><td><code>apps/v1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td>The object type — what you're creating</td><td><code>Deployment</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>metadata</code></td><td>Identity: <code>name</code> (required), <code>namespace</code>, <code>labels</code>, <code>annotations</code>, and the server-set <code>uid</code></td><td><code>name: easypj-web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>spec</code></td><td>Your <strong>desired state</strong> — the shape varies per <code>kind</code></td><td><code>replicas: 3</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② <code>spec</code> vs <code>status</code> — desired vs actual, inside the object itself</h4>
                <p>Almost every object has two halves: a <code>spec</code> you write (the goal) and a <code>status</code> the cluster writes (reality). The reconciliation loop from §1.4 is just "keep <code>status</code> matching <code>spec</code>." You never edit <code>status</code> — <em>reading</em> it is how you troubleshoot.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o yaml (trimmed)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:                 <span class="comment"># 👈 YOU declare this — desired state</span>
  replicas: 3
status:               <span class="comment"># 👈 KUBERNETES writes this — actual state (read-only)</span>
  replicas: 3
  readyReplicas: 2    <span class="comment"># only 2 are Ready right now → a controller is closing the gap</span>
  conditions:
  - type: Available
    status: "False"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>③ Naming rules — why <code>easypj_web</code> is rejected</h4>
                <p>Object names aren't free-form. Most follow the <strong>DNS Subdomain</strong> rule; a few (pods, nodes, namespaces) follow the stricter <strong>DNS Label</strong> rule. Break them and you get a validation error, not a running object.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Used by</th><th>Max</th><th>Allowed</th><th>✅ valid · ❌ invalid</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Subdomain</strong></td><td>most objects: Deployments, ConfigMaps, Secrets…</td><td>253</td><td>lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 - .</code>; start/end alphanumeric (may start with a digit)</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web</code>, <code>app.prod</code> · ❌ <code>easypj_web</code>, <code>EasyPJ</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Label</strong></td><td>namespaces, nodes &amp; many short names</td><td>63</td><td>lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 -</code> (<strong>no dots</strong>); <strong>start with a letter</strong>, end alphanumeric</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web-1</code> · ❌ <code>my.pod</code>, <code>POD-1</code>, <code>9web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Label (RFC 1035)</strong></td><td>Service names</td><td>63</td><td>same, but <strong>must start with a letter</strong> (a feature gate relaxes this)</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web</code> · ❌ <code>9web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>UID</strong></td><td>every object (set by the server)</td><td>—</td><td>cluster-unique, auto-generated, never user-supplied</td><td>tells apart two objects that reused a name over time</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>④ API groups, versions &amp; stability levels</h4>
                <p>The API is split into <strong>groups</strong>, each <strong>versioned independently</strong>. The <code>apiVersion</code> you write picks the group + version; the stability ladder tells you what's safe to depend on.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how apiVersion resolves to an API path</div>
<pre>
   apiVersion: v1          → CORE group    → /api/v1           Pod · Service · ConfigMap · Namespace · Node
   apiVersion: apps/v1     → "apps" group  → /apis/apps/v1     Deployment · ReplicaSet · StatefulSet · DaemonSet
   apiVersion: batch/v1    → "batch" group → /apis/batch/v1    Job · CronJob
   apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1                    Role · RoleBinding · ClusterRole
   └─ kubectl api-resources   lists every kind → its group, version &amp; whether it's namespaced
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Means</th><th>On by default?</th><th>In a name</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Alpha</strong></td><td>experimental — may change or vanish, may have bugs</td><td>no (needs a feature gate)</td><td><code>v1alpha1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Beta</strong></td><td>well-tested; details may still change before GA</td><td>varies (newer betas off)</td><td><code>v1beta1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Stable / GA</strong></td><td>production-ready, backward-compatible</td><td>yes</td><td><code>v1</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0"><strong>Deprecation policy:</strong> a <strong>GA</strong> (stable) API version is <strong>never removed within a major version</strong> — a <code>v1</code> API lasts for all of Kubernetes 1.x; a deprecated <strong>beta</strong> version is dropped after <strong>9 months or 3 releases</strong> (whichever is longer); <strong>alpha</strong> can change or vanish in any release. There's <strong>no LTS</strong>: each minor release is maintained ~14 months (≈12 months support + a ~2-month maintenance window) — read the release notes and upgrade before EOL.</p></div>

                <h4>⑤ Three ways to manage objects — and why the exam loves <code>apply</code></h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Command</th><th>Idempotent?</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Imperative command</td><td><code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v2</code></td><td>no — errors if it exists</td><td>exam speed &amp; quick fixes; keeps no file</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Imperative object config</td><td><code>kubectl create -f f.yaml</code> / <code>replace -f</code></td><td>no</td><td>rarely — forces delete-then-recreate</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Declarative</strong></td><td><code>kubectl apply -f f.yaml</code></td><td><strong>yes</strong> — run 100×, same result</td><td>production &amp; GitOps; the default to reach for</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip"><p class="mb-0">💡 <strong>Server-Side Apply</strong> (<code>kubectl apply --server-side</code>) moves the merge into the apiserver and tracks <em>field ownership</em>, so several controllers can co-own one object without clobbering each other. ⚡ <strong>Exam speed:</strong> <code>kubectl create deploy … --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml</code> scaffolds a manifest you then edit &amp; <code>apply</code>.</p></div>

                <h4>⑥ Add-ons — what a fresh cluster still needs</h4>
                <p>A brand-new cluster can schedule pods but is missing pieces you'll assume are there. These ship as <strong>add-ons</strong> (themselves pods), not part of the core binaries.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>🧭 CoreDNS</h5><p>In-cluster DNS — every pod resolves <code>name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code> here. Normally installed; no DNS → no name-based service discovery.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>📊 metrics-server</h5><p>Feeds CPU/memory to the API. <strong>Required</strong> for <code>kubectl top</code> and the HorizontalPodAutoscaler. Not installed by default.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🌍 Ingress controller</h5><p>Terminates &amp; routes external HTTP(S) to Services (e.g. ingress-nginx). You must pick &amp; install one — none ships by default.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🖥️ Dashboard</h5><p>Optional web UI. Handy, never required for admin work — and one more thing to secure if you deploy it.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — the reconciliation loop that powers everything</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Self-healing isn't magic — it's a <strong>closed control loop</strong> running forever inside the controller-manager. Each controller does three things, over and over:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌──────────── CONTROL LOOP (runs continuously) ────────────┐
   │                                                          │
   │   👁️ OBSERVE  ──▶  🧮 DIFF  ──▶  🛠️ ACT  ──┐             │
   │   read actual     desired vs     make a change           │
   │   state from      actual          (create/delete/        │
   │   the API          (a gap?)        update an object)     │
   │        ▲                                    │            │
   │        └────────────────────────────────────┘            │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj example: desired replicas=3, actual=2  →  create 1 pod  →  repeat
                    </pre>

                    <h5>🗺️ The Full Trace — Deploying easypj with 3 Replicas, Step by Step</h5>
                    <p>Here is the loop playing out for real. Notice that <strong>no single component drives the whole thing</strong> — each controller independently reconciles its own slice, and the result emerges:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
 1. USER declares desired state (deploy.yaml):
    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ apiVersion: apps/v1                        │
    │ kind: Deployment                           │
    │ spec:                                      │
    │   replicas: 3                              │
    │   template:                                │
    │     spec:                                  │
    │       containers:                          │
    │       - name: web                          │
    │         image: easypj:v2                   │
    └───────────────────────────────────────────┘

 2. kubectl apply → API server → persisted in etcd
    /registry/deployments/easypj/easypj-web = {replicas:3, image:v2}

 3. Deployment controller OBSERVES: desired replicas=3, image=v2
                            ACTUAL: no ReplicaSet, no Pods   → DIFF: everything missing
 4. Deployment controller ACTS:  creates ReplicaSet easypj-web-7d8f9

 5. ReplicaSet controller OBSERVES: want 3 pods (app=easypj-web)
                             ACTUAL: 0 pods                  → DIFF: 3 needed
 6. ReplicaSet controller ACTS:  creates 3 Pod objects (nodeName empty → Pending)

 7. Scheduler OBSERVES: 3 Pods with no node
                ACTS:  filters + scores nodes, binds each Pod → a Node

 8. kubelet (on each chosen node) OBSERVES: a Pod is assigned to me
                                     ACTS:  CRI pull image → create → start container
                                            CNI assigns the pod an IP

 9. easypj pods RUNNING :8000  → kubelet reports status up to the API server → etcd

10. Controllers keep watching forever: actual=3 = desired=3 ✅
    ── crash a pod? ReplicaSet makes a new one.  kill a node? Scheduler re-places.
    ── At NO step did anyone say "start these pods on these nodes." It EMERGED.
                    </pre>

                    <h5>🔬 Level-Triggered vs Edge-Triggered — Why Controllers Never "Miss" an Event</h5>
                    <p>The deep reason self-healing is reliable: Kubernetes is <strong>level-triggered</strong>. Controllers don't depend on catching every event — they periodically re-read the <em>whole</em> state. A dropped watch event (network blip) is simply corrected on the next resync.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Edge-triggered (imperative systems)</th><th>Level-triggered (Kubernetes)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Relies on receiving every event</td><td>Periodically re-reads the full state</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Missed event → permanently inconsistent</td><td>Missed event → caught on next resync</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Start Apache" — if it crashes, nobody restarts it</td><td>"3 replicas should exist" — a crash is auto-replaced</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Needs complex error handling</td><td>Self-healing by design</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <div class="uth-callout">
                        <strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> When you <code>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code> you are NOT telling Kubernetes "do these steps" — you're declaring "I want 3 easypj:v2 replicas." The controllers figure out the steps. That's <em>why</em> <code>apply</code> is idempotent: run it 100 times, same result as once. No state machine, no step sequence — just a declared end state and loops that converge on it.
                    </div>
                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> almost <em>everything</em> in Kubernetes is an API object stored in etcd and reconciled by a controller — Deployments, Services, Nodes, even certificates and namespaces. Master this one pattern and the whole system becomes predictable: to understand any feature, ask "what object, and which controller reconciles it?" This is what makes Kubernetes a universal <em>control plane</em>, not just a container runner.</p>

                    <h5>① How a controller <em>really</em> OBSERVES — list-watch, informers &amp; the work queue</h5>
                    <p>The loop above says "observe actual state," but <em>how</em>? Naively you'd poll — "is it 3 yet? is it 3 yet?" — which would hammer the API server with thousands of controllers asking constantly. Real Kubernetes never polls. It uses the <strong>list-watch</strong> pattern behind an <strong>informer</strong>: LIST the full state once, then WATCH a live stream of changes, keeping a local cache in sync.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🗄️ etcd ──change──▶ 📡 apiserver (watch cache)
                            │  streams events:  ADDED · MODIFIED · DELETED
                            ▼
                 ┌────────────────────────────┐
                 │ 🧠 INFORMER (in the controller)│
                 │  • LIST once → fill a local  │   the cache = a live, in-memory
                 │    cache (the "store")       │   mirror of cluster state, so the
                 │  • WATCH → apply deltas      │   controller reads it WITHOUT
                 │  • fire event handlers       │   touching etcd every time
                 └───────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │ enqueue the changed object's KEY  (namespace/name)
                                 ▼
                 ┌────────────────────────────┐
                 │ 📥 WORK QUEUE                │   dedups duplicates · rate-limits ·
                 │                              │   retries failures with backoff
                 └───────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │ a worker pops one key
                                 ▼
   🔄 Reconcile(key):  read DESIRED + ACTUAL from the cache  →  diff  →  act
                       (this is the OBSERVE → DIFF → ACT loop, in code)</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>❌ Naive polling</th><th>✅ list-watch + informer (real K8s)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>API-server load</td><td>constant, heavy (every controller, every second)</td><td>one LIST + a single long-lived WATCH stream</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Latency to react</td><td>up to the poll interval</td><td>near-instant — changes are pushed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Reads of "actual state"</td><td>hit the API/etcd each time</td><td>served from the informer's in-memory cache</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Missed events</td><td>silently lost</td><td>a periodic <strong>resync</strong> re-LISTs and re-reconciles everything (this <em>is</em> level-triggering in practice)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Failures</td><td>DIY retry logic</td><td>work queue dedups, rate-limits &amp; retries with backoff</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 So "observe actual state" is cheap</strong> because the controller reads its own <em>informer cache</em>, not etcd — and the cache is kept fresh by one shared WATCH. The periodic resync is the safety net that makes missed events impossible to matter.</div>

                    <h5>② Inside the API server — what <code>kubectl apply</code> passes through before it's saved</h5>
                    <p>"The API server validates and persists" hides a precise 6-stage pipeline. Knowing it tells you <em>exactly</em> which stage rejected a request from the error message alone:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml ─▶ 📡 kube-apiserver
        │
        ├─ 1. 🔐 Authentication      WHO are you?        cert / token / OIDC
        ├─ 2. 🛂 Authorization       MAY you do this?    RBAC (verb + resource)
        ├─ 3. 🧬 Mutating admission   change the object   webhooks: inject sidecars, set defaults
        ├─ 4. 📋 Schema validation    is it well-formed?  OpenAPI schema check
        ├─ 5. ✅ Validating admission policy gate         PodSecurity, ValidatingWebhooks, quota
        └─ 6. 💾 persist to etcd      write + bump resourceVersion  ──▶ controllers WATCH it</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Typical rejection you'd see</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Who is calling?</td><td><code>Unauthorized</code> (401) — bad/expired cert or token</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Authorization (RBAC)</td><td>Are they allowed?</td><td><code>Forbidden</code> (403) — "User cannot create deployments…"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Mutating admission</td><td>—</td><td>(usually silent — it <em>edits</em> the object, e.g. adds a sidecar)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Schema validation</td><td>Is the YAML valid?</td><td><code>error validating data: unknown field…</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Validating admission</td><td>Does it meet policy?</td><td><code>admission webhook denied the request</code> / PodSecurity violation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Persist to etcd</td><td>—</td><td><code>Conflict</code> (409) if resourceVersion is stale (see ③)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Read the error, name the stage.</strong> "Forbidden" = stage 2 (fix RBAC). "unknown field" = stage 4 (fix the YAML). "admission webhook denied" = stage 5 (a policy). This single mapping shortcuts a huge share of troubleshooting.</div>

                    <h5>③ etcd under the hood — Raft consensus, MVCC &amp; optimistic concurrency</h5>
                    <p>Stage 6 above ("persist to etcd") is where state actually lives. etcd is a distributed key-value store that stays <em>consistent across replicas</em> via the <strong>Raft</strong> algorithm and versions <em>every</em> change via <strong>MVCC</strong>. Every Kubernetes object is just a key:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Every object is ONE key in etcd (the "/registry" tree):
     /registry/deployments/easypj/easypj-web        = { …spec…, resourceVersion: 4821 }
     /registry/replicasets/easypj/easypj-web-6d8f9  = { …spec…, …status… }
     /registry/pods/easypj/easypj-web-6d8f9-x7k2    = { …spec…, …status… }
     /registry/services/easypj/easypj-web           = { … }

   Writes go through RAFT — a MAJORITY (quorum) must agree before a commit:
     ┌─────────┐   append entry   ┌─────────┐   replicate   ┌─────────┐
     │ etcd-1  │ ───────────────▶ │ etcd-2  │ ────────────▶ │ etcd-3  │
     │(leader) │ ◀─────ack─────── │(follower)│ ◀────ack───── │(follower)│
     └─────────┘                  └─────────┘               └─────────┘
        committed only once a MAJORITY acks  →  3 members tolerate 1 down (quorum = 2)
        ⚠️ lose quorum (2 of 3 down) → etcd goes READ-ONLY → the cluster "freezes"</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>MVCC + revision</td><td>every write bumps a global <em>revision</em>; old versions are kept until compaction</td><td>lets a WATCH replay changes from a point in time → reliable informers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resourceVersion</code></td><td>the per-object version stamped on every read/write</td><td>the basis of optimistic concurrency (below)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Optimistic concurrency</td><td>an update carries the rv you read; if it changed meanwhile → <code>409 Conflict</code></td><td>two controllers can't clobber each other — <strong>no locks needed</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Raft quorum</td><td>a majority of members must agree to commit</td><td>use ODD counts; losing quorum makes the API read-only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Compaction / defrag</td><td>prune old revisions &amp; reclaim disk</td><td>keeps etcd healthy — a real Day-2 admin task (Ch 29)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>Optimistic concurrency is the quiet hero that lets the level-triggered loop stay correct even when many controllers touch the same object at once — <em>no locks, just retry-on-conflict</em>:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   controller A reads easypj-web   (resourceVersion 4821)
   controller B reads easypj-web   (resourceVersion 4821)
   A writes its change            → accepted → object is now rv 4822
   B writes with rv 4821          → ❌ 409 CONFLICT  (someone moved first)
   B re-reads (gets rv 4822), recomputes the diff, writes again → ✅ accepted
                   └──────────────── this is just the loop running again ────────────────┘</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 It's all one machine.</strong> The reconcile loop (OBSERVE→DIFF→ACT), the informer/watch that feeds OBSERVE, the API-server pipeline that guards every write, and etcd's revisions + optimistic concurrency are not separate features — they're one coherent design. That's why the right question for <em>any</em> Kubernetes behaviour is always: "which object, which controller, what's the gap?"</div>

                    <h5>🔬 Deeper still — why the loop scales &amp; survives failures</h5>
                    <p>Four production mechanisms keep that loop fast, cheap and reliable across thousands of objects and flaky networks:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What it does</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Shared informer</strong></td><td>one <code>LIST</code>+<code>WATCH</code> per <em>resource type</em>, cached in memory and shared by every controller that needs it</td><td>API-server load is <strong>O(resource types)</strong>, not O(controllers) — a 50th controller adds no new watch on etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Work queue + rate limiter</strong></td><td>a failed reconcile is re-queued with <strong>exponential back-off</strong> (~5ms, doubling, capped ~1000s), deduped, and reset on success</td><td>a broken resource is retried <em>calmly</em>, not hammered thousands of times a second</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>WATCH bookmarks</strong></td><td>the apiserver streams periodic <code>BOOKMARK</code> events ("still here, at <code>resourceVersion</code> X") even when nothing changed</td><td>a client can tell "quiet" from "stalled" and resume from the last rv without a full re-LIST</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>410 Gone</strong></td><td>watching from a <code>resourceVersion</code> older than the apiserver's history window returns <strong>HTTP 410 Gone</strong></td><td>forces a fresh <code>LIST</code> + resync so caches can't silently drift — and is why aggressive etcd compaction (Ch 29) shrinks that window</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The pattern, named:</strong> read → compute → write <em>with</em> the <code>resourceVersion</code> you read → on <code>409 Conflict</code>, re-read &amp; retry. That's <strong>lock-free optimistic concurrency</strong>: two controllers touching one object never deadlock — the loser just retries and still makes progress.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Orchestration automates operations</strong> — placement, scaling, healing, networking, and rollout, done by software instead of a human at 3 AM.</li>
                    <li><strong>Declarative, not imperative</strong> — you declare desired state; control loops drive actual state toward it, forever.</li>
                    <li><strong>The administrator owns everything below the app</strong> — install, certs/PKI, etcd, upgrades, nodes, networking, storage, and troubleshooting.</li>
                    <li><strong>The API server is the only path to etcd</strong> — every component and every <code>kubectl</code> goes through it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Troubleshooting is 30% of the exam</strong> — the biggest single domain; method beats memorization.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>"Kubernetes replaces Docker."</strong> No — Kubernetes <em>orchestrates</em> containers; a runtime (containerd/CRI-O) still runs them. Only the Docker <em>shim</em> was removed (v1.24), not container images.</li>
                    <li><strong>Thinking imperatively</strong> ("start a container on node 2"). Wrong mental model — you declare <em>what</em> (3 replicas) and let K8s decide <em>where/how</em>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing CKA with CKAD.</strong> CKAD builds apps; CKA builds and operates the <em>cluster</em>. etcd, kubeadm, certs, and node management are CKA-only.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Control plane" = "master node."</strong> Modern Kubernetes dropped the word <em>master</em> — the exam says <strong>control plane node</strong>. Know its four components: API server, scheduler, controller-manager, etcd.</li>
                    <li><strong>"A Pod is a container."</strong> A Pod can hold several containers sharing one network namespace &amp; storage (sidecar / init-container patterns). The Pod, not the container, is the unit K8s schedules.</li>
                    <li><strong>Desired ≠ current state.</strong> The exam constantly probes whether you grasp the reconciliation loop — controllers continuously compare the two and self-heal the gap.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Think in desired state.</strong> When something's wrong, ask "what's declared vs what's actually running?" — that's the whole troubleshooting model.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl explain</code> is your in-exam manual.</strong> <code>kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive</code> beats hunting the docs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Read events first.</strong> <code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code> and <code>describe</code> usually name the cause before you guess.</li>
                    <li><strong>Architecture quick-recall mnemonic:</strong> <code>kube-apiserver</code> → front door · <code>etcd</code> → brain · <code>kube-scheduler</code> → which-node · <code>kube-controller-manager</code> → reconciliation. All four run as <strong>static pods</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — edit a file there and the kubelet recreates the pod.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 1)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">orient yourself in any cluster</span>
                </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Cluster endpoints (API server + CoreDNS URLs)</span>
kubectl cluster-info

<span class="comment"># Nodes, roles, versions, internal IPs</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide

<span class="comment"># Control-plane health (older clusters)</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses        <span class="comment"># a.k.a. cs</span>

<span class="comment"># Every API resource type + its short name + apiVersion</span>
kubectl api-resources

<span class="comment"># Client + server (cluster) versions</span>
kubectl version</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p>In <strong>5 minutes, from memory</strong>, sketch one cluster and label every box: the control-plane components (API server, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager), the worker-node components (kubelet, kube-proxy, runtime), and 3 easypj pods spread across 2 nodes. Then draw an arrow from "<code>replicas: 3</code>" to "3 pods running" and label it <em>reconciliation</em>.</p>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
<pre class="uth-diagram">
   🗂️ CLUSTER
   ┌──────────────── 🧠 CONTROL PLANE ────────────────┐
   │ 📡 API server · 🗄️ etcd · 📋 scheduler · 🔄 controller-manager │
   └──────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┘
                          │  (API server is the only one that talks to etcd)
   ┌────── 💪 NODE 1 ──────┐        ┌────── 💪 NODE 2 ──────┐
   │ 🤖 kubelet · 🔀 kube-proxy │     │ 🤖 kubelet · 🔀 kube-proxy │
   │ 🐋 runtime (containerd)│       │ 🐋 runtime (containerd)│
   │ 📦 easypj  📦 easypj    │       │ 📦 easypj             │
   └──────────────────────┘        └──────────────────────┘
       "replicas: 3"  ──reconciliation──▶  3 pods always running ✅
</pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> control-plane = API server · etcd · scheduler · controller-manager (the "brain"); each node = kubelet · kube-proxy · container runtime (the "muscle"); the 3 easypj pods are spread across the 2 nodes; and the arrow is the control loop closing desired (3) → actual (3). If you drew all of that, you've got Chapter 1's mental model cold.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 1 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Define "container orchestration" in one sentence.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The automated deployment, scaling, healing, and networking of containerized applications across many machines — performed by software, not humans.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Containers solved "works on my machine"; orchestration solves "works across a fleet, reliably, unattended." Kubernetes is the industry-standard orchestrator, and the CKA is about <em>running the orchestrator itself</em>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's pod crashes at 3 AM. What does Kubernetes do, and <em>why</em> does it work even if a controller missed the crash event?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A controller observes that <strong>actual ≠ desired</strong> (2 pods running, 3 wanted) and recreates the missing pod automatically — no human needed.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>It's <strong>level-triggered</strong>: the loop reacts to the current <em>state</em>, not to the crash <em>event</em>. Even if the event was missed or the controller restarted, the next iteration still sees the gap and fixes it — which is why self-healing is reliable.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Give the one-line definition of Kubernetes.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>"An open-source system for automating deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications."</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The operative word is <strong>automating</strong> — you declare intent, K8s carries it out and keeps it true. Born from Google's Borg, open-sourced 2014, CNCF-governed, written in Go.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain desired-vs-actual state using a <code>replicas: 3</code> example.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Desired</strong> = what you declared (<code>replicas: 3</code>, stored in etcd). <strong>Actual</strong> = what's really running on the nodes. A controller continuously diffs them: if actual is 2, it creates one pod; if actual is 4, it deletes one.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This gap-closing loop is the core mental model for the whole exam. Troubleshooting = "find where actual diverged from desired, and why the controller can't close the gap."</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List three tasks that belong to the CKA but NOT the CKAD.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Any three of: install a cluster with <strong>kubeadm</strong>; <strong>etcd</strong> backup &amp; restore; approve/rotate <strong>certificates</strong> (PKI); <strong>cluster upgrades</strong>; <strong>node management</strong> (drain/cordon); configure <strong>CNI/CoreDNS</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>All are <em>below the app</em>. CKAD stays at the workload layer (Pods/Deployments/Services/ConfigMaps); CKA owns the infrastructure those run on.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the four control-plane components and the one that stores all cluster state.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>kube-apiserver</strong>, <strong>etcd</strong>, <strong>kube-scheduler</strong>, <strong>kube-controller-manager</strong> (plus optional cloud-controller-manager). State lives in <strong>etcd</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Only the API server talks to etcd — everything else goes through the API server. That's why an etcd backup <em>is</em> a cluster backup, and why losing etcd without a backup loses the cluster.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a Pod, and what do containers inside one share?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A Pod is the smallest deployable unit — one or more containers scheduled together. Containers in a pod share the <strong>network namespace</strong> (one IP, reach each other on <code>localhost</code>) and can share <strong>storage volumes</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is why a sidecar (e.g. a log shipper) can read the app's files and talk to it on localhost. easypj-web runs one container; a sidecar pattern would add a second to the same pod.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is <code>kubectl apply</code> idempotent — running it twice causes no harm?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because it's <strong>declarative</strong>: it sets the desired state, it doesn't issue step-by-step commands. If the cluster already matches, the reconciliation loop finds no gap and does nothing.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Contrast with imperative <code>kubectl create</code>, which errors if the object already exists. Declarative + reconciliation is what makes GitOps ("re-apply the repo continuously") safe.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Match each tool to its job: Calico · Helm · ArgoCD · Prometheus · containerd.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Calico</td><td>CNI — pod networking + NetworkPolicy</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Helm</td><td>Packaging — templated charts</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>ArgoCD</td><td>GitOps — sync cluster to Git</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Prometheus</td><td>Observability — metrics</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>containerd</td><td>Container runtime (CRI)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Each plugs into a Kubernetes extension point: CNI (networking), runtime (CRI), plus higher-level tooling for packaging, delivery, and monitoring. Knowing the category matters more than the brand.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Sort "admin-owns vs dev-owns": etcd backup · a Deployment · RBAC · a ConfigMap · a node upgrade.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>🔧 CKA admin owns</th><th>👩‍💻 CKAD dev owns</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>etcd backup</td><td>a Deployment</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>RBAC</td><td>a ConfigMap</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>a node upgrade</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>RBAC is borderline — devs may <em>request</em> roles, but defining and granting cluster access is an admin responsibility. The rule of thumb: if it's <em>below the app</em>, it's CKA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-scenario"></span>
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 1 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — 10 drills that mirror how the CKA actually asks: a broken state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A freshly built cluster has its control plane up, but worker nodes show <code>NotReady</code>. You SSH to a worker and find the node agent isn't running. Which component is it, and how do you recover?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>kubelet</strong> — the per-node agent that (1) registers the node with the API server and (2) starts/monitors the pods assigned to it. No kubelet ⇒ the node can't report in ⇒ <code>NotReady</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Why is it down? Read the logs first.</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -30

<span class="comment"># Start it and make it survive reboots</span>
sudo systemctl enable --now kubelet

<span class="comment"># Verify from the control plane</span>
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On the CKA, <strong>NotReady → always check the kubelet first</strong>: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>. Common root causes are a stopped service, a bad <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, swap enabled, a missing CNI, or a down container runtime (Chapter 32 drills all of these).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the four control-plane components and each one's primary job. Where do they run on a kubeadm cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver</code></td><td>Front door — REST API, authn/authz/admission; the <em>only</em> component that talks to etcd</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>etcd</code></td><td>Distributed key-value store — the single source of truth for all cluster state</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-scheduler</code></td><td>Assigns unscheduled pods to nodes (resources, affinity, taints/tolerations)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-controller-manager</code></td><td>Runs reconciliation loops (Deployment, ReplicaSet, Node… controllers)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On a kubeadm cluster all four run as <strong>static pods</strong> defined in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> on the control-plane node — edit a manifest there and the kubelet recreates the pod. The topology is a <strong>star</strong>: every other component (scheduler, controller-manager, kubelet) talks <em>only</em> through the API server, never directly to etcd.</p>
                            <p class="mb-0">There's an optional <strong>fifth</strong> component — the <code>cloud-controller-manager</code> — on managed/cloud clusters; it runs the cloud-specific loops (provision LoadBalancers, manage node lifecycle &amp; routes) so the core four stay cloud-agnostic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment specifies <code>replicas: 3</code>. You manually delete one of its pods. What happens, and which component is responsible?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>ReplicaSet controller</strong> (inside kube-controller-manager) sees current=2 ≠ desired=3 and immediately creates a replacement — count returns to 3.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">watch self-healing happen</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3
kubectl get pods                 <span class="comment"># 3 running</span>
kubectl delete pod easypj-xxxxx-yyyyy
kubectl get pods                 <span class="comment"># still 3 — a new one was created!</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The declarative model in action: you declared "3," and the control loop (Observe → Diff → Act) continuously enforces it. Controllers never stop watching — this self-healing is heavily tested. (To remove the pod permanently you must change the <em>desired</em> state — scale down or delete the Deployment.)</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a control-plane node and a worker node? Which components run on each — and why does the kubelet run on <em>both</em>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Control-plane node:</strong> the management components — <code>kube-apiserver</code>, <code>etcd</code>, <code>kube-scheduler</code>, <code>kube-controller-manager</code> (as static pods).</p>
                        <p><strong>Worker node:</strong> runs your workloads — needs <code>kubelet</code>, <code>kube-proxy</code>, and a <strong>container runtime</strong> (containerd/CRI-O).</p>
                        <p><strong>Both:</strong> kubelet and kube-proxy run on control-plane nodes too — because the control-plane components <em>are</em> static pods, and only the kubelet can run static pods.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Say "control-plane node," not "master." By default control-plane nodes carry the taint <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code> so ordinary workloads (like easypj) don't land there. That taint — and how to tolerate/remove it — is exactly the kind of thing the scheduling chapter (14) covers.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code>. Trace the request through the architecture — which components act, and in what order?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <ol>
                            <li><strong>kubectl</strong> → HTTPS request to the API server</li>
                            <li><strong>kube-apiserver</strong> → authenticate → authorize (RBAC) → admission → validate</li>
                            <li><strong>etcd</strong> → API server persists the Deployment object</li>
                            <li><strong>Deployment controller</strong> → creates a ReplicaSet</li>
                            <li><strong>ReplicaSet controller</strong> → creates Pod objects (<code>nodeName</code> empty)</li>
                            <li><strong>kube-scheduler</strong> → binds each pod to a node</li>
                            <li><strong>kubelet</strong> (on the node) → pulls image via the runtime, starts the container; CNI assigns an IP</li>
                        </ol>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>It's <strong>asynchronous and event-driven</strong> — no single orchestrator. Each component watches the API server for changes relevant to it and acts independently. That loose coupling is what makes Kubernetes resilient: if the scheduler is briefly down, pods just queue as <code>Pending</code> and get placed when it returns. This flow is the backbone of every troubleshooting scenario (Part 9).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>etcd</code>, why is backing it up critical, and what command takes a snapshot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>etcd</code> is a distributed, consistent key-value store holding <strong>all</strong> cluster state — every Pod, Service, Secret, ConfigMap, RBAC rule, and CRD. Lose it without a backup and the whole cluster is gone (an etcd snapshot effectively <em>is</em> a cluster backup).</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">guaranteed exam task</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Snapshot (flags come from the etcd static-pod manifest)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /tmp/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify it's readable</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /tmp/etcd-backup.db --write-table</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three things the exam checks: (1) use <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code>; (2) supply the three TLS flags — read the exact paths from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>; (3) the endpoint is usually <code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code>. Full backup <em>and restore</em> is drilled end-to-end in Chapter 29.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>kube-proxy</code> do? If it stops on a node, what breaks — and what still works?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kube-proxy</code> programs each node's dataplane (iptables / IPVS) so traffic to a <strong>Service</strong> VIP is load-balanced to the backing pods. It watches the API server for Service &amp; EndpointSlice objects.</p>
                        <p><strong>If it's down on a node:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>❌ Service (ClusterIP / NodePort) traffic stops reaching pods</li>
                            <li>❌ DNS via CoreDNS may fail (CoreDNS is reached <em>through</em> a Service)</li>
                            <li>✅ Pod-to-pod by pod IP still works (that's the CNI, not kube-proxy)</li>
                            <li>✅ <code>kubectl exec</code> / <code>logs</code> still work (API server → kubelet directly)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Key distinction for troubleshooting: <strong>kube-proxy = Service networking</strong>; the <strong>CNI plugin = pod-to-pod networking</strong>. So if pods can ping each other by IP but Services are unreachable, suspect kube-proxy (Chapter 34 makes this a 4-layer method).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Container Runtime Interface (CRI)? Name two CRI runtimes. Why was Docker's direct support removed in v1.24 — and do my images still work?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>CRI</strong> is the standard interface the kubelet uses to talk to <em>any</em> container runtime without runtime-specific code. Two runtimes: <strong>containerd</strong> (most common, extracted from Docker) and <strong>CRI-O</strong> (purpose-built, common on OpenShift).</p>
                        <p>Docker didn't implement CRI directly, so K8s shipped a <code>dockershim</code> translation layer — extra complexity for no gain (Docker uses containerd internally anyway). v1.24 removed the shim. <strong>Your images still work</strong> — they're OCI images, a universal format; only the <em>runtime plumbing</em> changed.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node-level: use crictl, not docker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps                 <span class="comment"># list running containers</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>After v1.24 you debug containers on a node with <code>crictl</code> (talks to containerd via CRI), not <code>docker</code>. This matters in Chapter 32 when the API server is down and you must diagnose from the node itself.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Imperative vs declarative management — what's the difference, and what's the recommended CKA workflow?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Imperative</strong> = tell K8s the steps (<code>kubectl create/run/expose</code>) — fast, one-off, no YAML. <strong>Declarative</strong> = describe the end state in YAML + <code>kubectl apply -f</code> — idempotent, version-controllable, repeatable.</p>
                        <p><strong>CKA gold standard:</strong> generate YAML imperatively, then apply it declaratively:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative generation → declarative apply</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Scaffold YAML instantly — don't write it from scratch</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; easypj.yaml

<span class="comment"># Edit in vim (add probes, resources…), then apply</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj.yaml</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Speed wins the exam. <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> scaffolds correct YAML in seconds; you tweak the bits a one-liner can't express (multi-container, volumes, probes). Pure <code>kubectl create</code> is fine for trivial objects, but the hybrid is the habit to build.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're asked to verify overall cluster health under exam time pressure. Write the command sequence and say what each reveals.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">top-down health check</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get nodes                       <span class="comment"># 1. all nodes Ready?</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system         <span class="comment"># 2. control-plane + CNI + CoreDNS Running?</span>
kubectl cluster-info                    <span class="comment"># 3. API + CoreDNS endpoints reachable</span>
kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20   <span class="comment"># 4. recent failures</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running   <span class="comment"># 5. anything CrashLoop/Error/Pending?</span>
kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox:1.28 --rm -it --restart=Never \
  -- nslookup kubernetes               <span class="comment"># 6. DNS resolving?</span>
<span class="comment"># 7. if a node is NotReady: ssh in → systemctl status kubelet → journalctl -u kubelet</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A systematic <strong>top-down diagnosis</strong>: nodes → control-plane pods → events → DNS → node logs. Look for all nodes <code>Ready</code>, all kube-system pods <code>Running</code>, no <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>, and DNS resolving <code>kubernetes</code>. If a static-pod component is down, check its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and that node's kubelet logs — the exact method Part 9 turns into reflex.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 1 Visual Summary — from your YAML to a running easypj pod</div>
<pre>
  👩‍💻 YOU write          🧠 CONTROL PLANE ("the brain")           💪 WORKER NODES ("muscle")
  ┌──────────────┐       ┌──────────────────────────────┐       ┌───────────────────────────┐
  │ deploy.yaml  │ ①apply│ 📡 apiserver — the ONE door    │       │ 🤖 kubelet · 🔀 kube-proxy   │
  │ replicas: 3  │ ─────▶ │   ② writes DESIRED ─▶ 🗄️ etcd   │ ④bind │ 🐋 containerd               │
  │ image:       │       │   ③ 📋 scheduler picks a node   │ ─────▶│  ┌─────────────────────┐  │
  │  easypj:v2   │       │   ④ 🔄 controllers reconcile    │       │  │📦 easypj-web :8000    │  │
  │ resources:   │       │      desired vs actual,        │       │  │📦 easypj-web :8000    │  │
  │  256Mi       │       │      FOREVER                   │ ⑥stat │  │📦 easypj-web :8000    │  │
  └──────────────┘       └──────────────────────────────┘ ◀───── │  └─────────────────────┘  │
                         ⑤ kubelet pulls image · 🌐 CNI gives each pod an IP · starts it      │
                                                                  └───────────────────────────┘
   ① declare desired   ② persist to etcd   ③ schedule to a node   ④ reconcile (keep actual = desired)
   ⑤ kubelet + CNI run it   ⑥ status reported back ─▶ etcd   ……and the loop never stops.
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   You declared "3 easypj-web on v2." Kubernetes GUARANTEES 3 are always up — it heals, scales &amp;
   rolls out on its own. THAT is Kubernetes ☸️ — and operating this whole machine is the CKA's job.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> You write a <code>deploy.yaml</code> that <em>describes</em> the end result you want (3 replicas of <code>easypj-web</code> on image <code>easypj:v2</code>) and <code>apply</code> it. You never tell Kubernetes <em>how</em> — only <em>what</em>.</p>
                    <p>The control plane is the brain: the <strong>apiserver</strong> is the single front door, it persists your desired state to <strong>etcd</strong>, the <strong>scheduler</strong> picks a node for each pod, and <strong>controllers</strong> keep comparing <em>desired vs actual</em> and acting on the gap — forever. The worker nodes are the muscle: the <strong>kubelet</strong> pulls the image and starts the containers, <strong>CNI</strong> gives each pod an IP, and status flows back to etcd.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> this never-ending "make actual match desired" loop is <em>reconciliation</em> — the core idea of the whole platform. Because of it, a crashed pod is replaced, a scale-up just works, and a new version rolls out automatically. Operating this machine is exactly what the CKA tests.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 1 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 2 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch2">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 2 · Containers &amp; Docker — The Unit Kubernetes Runs</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">
                Kubernetes schedules, heals, and scales one thing: <strong>containers</strong>. Before you can operate a
                cluster, you have to understand the unit it manages — what a container actually <em>is</em>, how an image
                is built from cached layers, and which runtime runs it on the node. This is also where troubleshooting
                begins: when an easypj pod won't start, the answer is almost always in the image or the runtime.
            </p>

            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 Hands-On</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐳 Docker · containerd · CRI</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐍 easypj / jobpost</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Why containers solve the <strong>"works on my machine"</strong> problem</li>
                    <li>✅ Containers vs Virtual Machines — how each isolates, and when to use which</li>
                    <li>✅ Docker basics: <strong>Dockerfile → image → container → registry</strong>, and how layers cache</li>
                    <li>✅ Build a production, multi-stage, non-root image for <strong>easypj</strong></li>
                    <li>✅ The essential <code>docker</code> / <code>crictl</code> commands — and which to use on a node</li>
                    <li>✅ The <strong>CRI</strong>, containerd vs CRI-O, and why Docker was removed in v1.24</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🐳 Container-runtime knowledge on the CKA</h5>
                <p>The CKA doesn't ask you to write Dockerfiles, but it <strong>assumes</strong> you understand container runtimes — because they show up everywhere you'll be graded:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Cluster install</strong> — you configure <code>containerd</code> (and its cgroup driver) during kubeadm setup.</li>
                    <li><strong>Troubleshooting</strong> — when a pod fails, you inspect the runtime with <code>crictl</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>NotReady nodes</strong> — a dead container runtime takes the node down with it.</li>
                    <li><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> — understanding images &amp; layers is how you diagnose failed pulls.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <h4>🧭 Section Map — the Chapter 2 Learning Arc</h4>
            <p>This chapter walks the lifecycle of the unit Kubernetes runs: from <strong>why</strong> containers exist, to <strong>what</strong> they are vs VMs, to <strong>how</strong> you build and run one for easypj, to the <strong>runtime</strong> the cluster actually uses.</p>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Chapter 2 Roadmap — from "works on my machine" to a running pod</div>
<pre>
   ▶ YOU ARE HERE
   "easypj runs on my laptop but crashes in prod. How do I package it once, run it anywhere?"
        │
        ▼
   2.1 😩 WHY CONTAINERS ─────▶ 2.2 ⚖️ CONTAINERS vs VMs ─────▶ 2.3 🐳 DOCKER BASICS
   the "works on my machine"    shared kernel = MB &amp; ms          Dockerfile → image →
   problem, solved by packaging vs a full guest OS per app       container → registry; layers
        │                                                              │
        ▼                                                              ▼
   2.6 🔌 RUNTIMES &amp; THE CRI ◀───── 2.5 🛠️ docker / crictl ◀───── 2.4 📦 BUILD easypj's IMAGE
   containerd / CRI-O; why          the commands (and which        a multi-stage, non-root
   Docker left the node in v1.24    one works on a real node)      production Dockerfile
        │
        ▼
   ✅ READY — you understand the artifact (image) and the engine (runtime) Kubernetes drives.
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Answers the question…</th><th>One-line takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-1"><strong>2.1</strong> Why Containers</a></td><td>Why does this exist?</td><td>Package the app + everything it needs → runs identically everywhere.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-2"><strong>2.2</strong> Containers vs VMs</a></td><td>What is a container, really?</td><td>A process sharing the host kernel — MB &amp; milliseconds, not GB &amp; minutes.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-3"><strong>2.3</strong> Docker Basics</a></td><td>How is an image made &amp; shipped?</td><td>Dockerfile → build → image (cached layers) → registry → run.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-4"><strong>2.4</strong> Build easypj</a></td><td>How do I package easypj?</td><td>A multi-stage, non-root Dockerfile = small, fast, safe.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-5"><strong>2.5</strong> Commands</a></td><td>How do I inspect containers?</td><td><code>docker</code> on your laptop; <code>crictl</code> on a cluster node.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-6"><strong>2.6</strong> Runtimes &amp; CRI</a></td><td>What runs it in the cluster?</td><td>containerd/CRI-O via the CRI — Docker's daemon left the node in v1.24.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-7"><strong>2.7</strong> Images in K8s</a></td><td>How does K8s pull &amp; manage images?</td><td>Tag/digest, imagePullPolicy defaults, pull secrets, ImagePullBackOff, image GC.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.1 ─────────── -->

            <section class="section-block" id="s2-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.1 Why Containers? The "Works on My Machine" Problem</h3>
                <p>Every developer has lived this nightmare: easypj runs perfectly on your laptop, then explodes the moment it hits a server. A container ends the argument by packaging <strong>everything</strong> the app needs — code <em>and</em> its entire environment — into one immutable, portable unit.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The "works on my machine" problem — and the fix</div>
<pre>
   👩‍💻 your laptop                          🖥️ production server
   ┌────────────────────────┐              ┌────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ easypj runs ✅          │   deploy →   │ 💀 ModuleNotFoundError: 'psycopg2'   │
   │ Python 3.13 · Django   │              │    (different Python · no psycopg2 · │
   │ psycopg2 · env vars set │              │     missing env vars · different OS) │
   └────────────────────────┘              └────────────────────────────────────┘

   THE FIX — pack EVERYTHING into one image 📦, ship the box, not the recipe:
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 📦 easypj:v1                                                   │
   │   • Python 3.13            • Django 5.2.5                      │
   │   • gunicorn · psycopg2    • easypj source + settings          │
   │   • env / config baked in  • a slim Debian userland            │
   │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────   │
   │  runs IDENTICALLY:   laptop ✅   staging ✅   production ✅      │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Container</h5>
                    <p>A <strong>container</strong> is a lightweight, standalone, executable package that bundles everything needed to run a piece of software: code, runtime, system tools, libraries, and settings. Crucially, containers <strong>share the host's OS kernel</strong> — which is what makes them far lighter and faster than virtual machines.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🚢 Analogy — the shipping container</h5>
                    <p>Before standardized shipping containers, every cargo was loaded by hand and broke in transit. The steel box changed the world because <em>the same sealed unit</em> rides any ship, truck, or crane without anyone caring what's inside. A software container is the same idea: a standard box the cluster can pick up and run anywhere, without caring whether it holds easypj, nginx, or PostgreSQL.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Four Properties That Matter</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Portable</h5>
                        <p>The same image runs on Linux, Mac, Windows, cloud, or bare metal — identical behavior everywhere.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>Isolated</h5>
                        <p>Each container gets its own filesystem, network, and process space — no dependency conflicts between apps.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>Fast</h5>
                        <p>Starts in seconds (not minutes like a VM) — it shares the host kernel, so there's no full OS to boot.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📐</div>
                        <h5>Lightweight</h5>
                        <p>Megabytes, not gigabytes — run dozens (even hundreds) of containers on a single machine.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What's Inside a Container — and What Is <em>Not</em></h4>
                <p>This is the misconception that trips up almost everyone, and clearing it up makes the rest of the chapter click: a container image does <strong>not</strong> contain an operating system. It bundles the app's <em>userland</em> — your code, language runtime, and shared libraries — but it borrows the <strong>host's kernel</strong>. That single fact is why containers are MB not GB, and why a Linux container needs a Linux kernel underneath.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>✅ IN the image (userland — shipped)</th><th>❌ NOT in the image (shared from the host)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Your code (easypj) + settings</td><td>The Linux <strong>kernel</strong> itself</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Python 3.13 runtime + pip packages</td><td>Kernel modules &amp; device drivers</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>System libs (<code>libc</code>, <code>libpq</code>) from a slim Debian</td><td>The CPU / memory scheduler</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The default command (<code>gunicorn …</code>)</td><td>Namespaces &amp; cgroups (these <em>are</em> kernel features)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">An image is userland on top of the host's kernel</div>
<pre>
   📦 easypj image  (everything you ship)        ── all containers share ──▶  one kernel
   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ easypj code · gunicorn         │            ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ Python 3.13 · psycopg2         │  ───runs──▶ │ 🐧 HOST LINUX KERNEL                   │
   │ libc · libpq (slim Debian)     │   on top of │   scheduling · namespaces · cgroups ·  │
   │ (NO kernel in here!)           │            │   drivers · filesystems                 │
   └──────────────────────────────┘            └──────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 So how do containers run on a Mac or Windows?</h5>
                    <p>They don't, directly — those OSes have no Linux kernel. Docker Desktop quietly runs a <strong>tiny Linux VM</strong> and starts your containers inside it. On a real Kubernetes <em>node</em> (which is Linux), there's no such trick — the container runs natively on the node's kernel. This is also why a Linux image can't run on a Windows kernel and vice-versa: the userland must match the kernel it's calling.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why this is the foundation of everything ahead</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes never runs your <em>code</em> — it runs your <em>image</em>. Every later topic (pods, deployments, rollouts, probes) is just the cluster choreographing copies of an image like <code>easypj:v1</code>. Get the image right here, and the rest of the guide is choreography.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.2 Containers vs Virtual Machines</h3>
                <p>Both VMs and containers give you isolation — but at fundamentally different levels, and that difference explains every "containers are light and fast" claim. A <strong>VM virtualizes hardware</strong> (a full guest OS per app); a <strong>container virtualizes the OS</strong> (all containers share the host kernel).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">VM architecture vs container architecture</div>
<pre>
   VIRTUAL MACHINES                          CONTAINERS
   ┌───────────┐ ┌───────────┐              ┌───────────┐ ┌───────────┐
   │  App A     │ │  App B     │             │ 🐍 easypj  │ │ 🌐 nginx   │
   │  Libs      │ │  Libs      │             │  Libs      │ │  Libs      │
   │ ┌────────┐ │ │ ┌────────┐ │             └─────┬─────┘ └─────┬─────┘
   │ │Guest OS│ │ │ │Guest OS│ │  ← a WHOLE         │   shared    │
   │ └────────┘ │ │ └────────┘ │    OS per app      ▼             ▼
   └───────────┘ └───────────┘             ┌────────────────────────┐
   ┌───────────────────────────┐           │ 🐋 container runtime     │ (containerd)
   │       Hypervisor           │           ├────────────────────────┤
   ├───────────────────────────┤           │   Host kernel  (SHARED) │ ← the key difference
   │         Host OS            │           ├────────────────────────┤
   ├───────────────────────────┤           │        Host OS          │
   │         Hardware           │           ├────────────────────────┤
   └───────────────────────────┘           │        Hardware         │
     heavy → GIGABYTES · MINUTES            └────────────────────────┘
                                              light → MEGABYTES · SECONDS
</pre>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>🖥️ Virtual Machine</th><th>📦 Container</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Size</strong></td><td>Gigabytes (a full OS per VM)</td><td>Megabytes (shares the kernel)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Startup</strong></td><td>Minutes (OS must boot)</td><td>Seconds / ms (just a process start)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>OS</strong></td><td>Full guest OS per VM</td><td>Shares the host kernel</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Isolation</strong></td><td>Strong (hardware-level)</td><td>Process-level (namespaces + cgroups)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Performance</strong></td><td>Near-native (hypervisor overhead)</td><td>Native (direct kernel access)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Density</strong></td><td>~10–20 per server</td><td>~100–1000 per server</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>A different OS, or strong isolation</td><td>Same OS, many microservices</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA reality: nodes ARE VMs (usually)</h5>
                    <p>In most real clusters, Kubernetes worker nodes are themselves <strong>virtual machines</strong> in a cloud (EC2, Azure VM, GCE). So you actually use <em>both</em> layers: VMs as the host infrastructure, and containers for the workloads on top. The administrator manages both — VMs for the cluster machines, containers for what runs on them.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The flip side of a shared kernel</h5>
                    <p>Sharing the host kernel is what makes containers light — but it also means weaker isolation than a VM. A kernel exploit or a privileged container can potentially affect the host. That's <em>why</em> later chapters lock containers down: non-root users, dropped capabilities, seccomp, and Pod Security Standards (Ch 27). Light ≠ automatically safe.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.3 Docker Basics for Kubernetes Admins</h3>
                <p>Docker created the container ecosystem. Even though Kubernetes no longer uses Docker as a <em>runtime</em>, Docker's concepts and tooling are still how you build and reason about images — so the vocabulary is essential.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Docker workflow — recipe to running container to registry</div>
<pre>
   📝 Dockerfile  ──docker build──▶  📀 Image  ──docker run──▶  📦 Container
   (the recipe)                      (read-only               (a running
                                      template)                instance)
                                         │
                                         │ docker push
                                         ▼
                                   🏪 Registry   ──docker pull──▶  🤖 a K8s node
                                   (Docker Hub / ECR / GCR)        pulls &amp; runs it
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>Dockerfile</h5>
                        <p>A text recipe that defines how to build an image: the base OS, dependencies, your code, and the startup command.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📀</div>
                        <h5>Image</h5>
                        <p>The built, read-only template. Like a <em>class</em> in code — it defines the container but isn't running yet.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Container</h5>
                        <p>A running instance of an image. Like an <em>object</em> created from a class — live and executing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏪</div>
                        <h5>Registry</h5>
                        <p>Where images are stored &amp; distributed: Docker Hub (public), ECR (AWS), GCR (GCP), ACR (Azure).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Layers — How Docker Images Are Built (and Cached)</h4>
                <p>An image isn't one blob — it's a <strong>stack of read-only layers</strong>, one per Dockerfile instruction. Each layer is content-addressed and cached, which is the single most important thing to understand for fast builds:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj image — layer stack (bottom = base, top = your code)</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Container layer (read-WRITE)                 │ ← runtime changes, ephemeral
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │  L5  CMD ["gunicorn", "easypj.wsgi…"]         │ ← startup command
   │  L4  COPY . .            (easypj source)      │ ← changes MOST → put it LAST
   │  L3  RUN pip install -r requirements.txt      │ ← Python deps (slow; cache this!)
   │  L2  COPY requirements.txt .                  │ ← the deps list
   │  L1  FROM python:3.13-slim   (base OS+Python) │ ← shared base, pulled once
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   💡 Layers are cached. Edit L4 (your code) → only L4+L5 rebuild; L1–L3 are reused.
      THAT is why you COPY requirements.txt and pip install BEFORE COPY . .
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why layer order matters for the CKA</h5>
                    <p>When you debug an <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, knowing images are layers helps you reason about <em>where</em> it broke — a missing base image, a build failure, or a registry-auth problem. As an admin you also configure <code>imagePullSecrets</code> so nodes can pull from private registries (Ch 23/25).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Dockerfile Instructions You'll Actually Use</h4>
                <p>You don't need all 18 Dockerfile instructions — about ten do 99% of the work. Here's the full working set, each with what it does and the gotcha that bites people:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Instruction</th><th>What it does</th><th>Note / gotcha</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>FROM</code></td><td>The base image to build on</td><td>Pin it (<code>python:3.13-slim</code>), never <code>:latest</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>WORKDIR</code></td><td>Sets (and creates) the working dir</td><td>Use it instead of <code>RUN cd …</code> (cd doesn't persist)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>COPY</code></td><td>Copies files from build context → image</td><td>Order matters for caching; respects <code>.dockerignore</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ADD</code></td><td>Like COPY but also untars / fetches URLs</td><td>Prefer <code>COPY</code> — ADD's magic causes surprises</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>RUN</code></td><td>Executes a command at BUILD time → new layer</td><td>Chain with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> to keep layers small</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ENV</code></td><td>Sets an env var (persists into the running container)</td><td>Available at build AND run time</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ARG</code></td><td>A build-time-only variable</td><td>Gone at runtime; <strong>don't</strong> use for secrets</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>EXPOSE</code></td><td>Documents the port the app listens on</td><td>Documentation only — doesn't publish the port</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>USER</code></td><td>Switches the user for later steps + runtime</td><td>Set a non-root user before <code>CMD</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>CMD</code> / <code>ENTRYPOINT</code></td><td>The default process the container runs</td><td>See the breakdown below — the #1 confusion</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>CMD vs ENTRYPOINT — the Classic Confusion</h4>
                <p>Both define what runs when the container starts, but they combine in a specific way, and Kubernetes overrides them with its <em>own</em> fields. Get this straight once:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ENTRYPOINT = the program · CMD = its default arguments</div>
<pre>
   ENTRYPOINT ["gunicorn"]          ← the fixed executable (hard to override)
   CMD ["easypj.wsgi","-b","0.0.0.0:8000"]   ← default args (easy to override)

   container runs:  gunicorn easypj.wsgi -b 0.0.0.0:8000
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   only CMD, no ENTRYPOINT:   CMD ["gunicorn","easypj.wsgi"]
        → runs that, but `docker run img python manage.py shell` REPLACES it entirely
   ENTRYPOINT + CMD:          `docker run img --workers 5`  → gunicorn easypj.wsgi --workers 5
                              (CMD's defaults are swapped, ENTRYPOINT stays)
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>In the Dockerfile</th><th>In Kubernetes (pod spec)</th><th>Overrides…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>ENTRYPOINT</code></td><td><code>command:</code></td><td>the executable</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>CMD</code></td><td><code>args:</code></td><td>the arguments</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The K8s payoff</h5>
                    <p>When a pod sets <code>command:</code> / <code>args:</code>, it's directly overriding the image's <code>ENTRYPOINT</code> / <code>CMD</code>. That's how you run a one-off (e.g. <code>python manage.py migrate</code>) from the <em>same</em> easypj image without rebuilding it — exactly what Jobs do in Chapter 16. Use the <strong>exec form</strong> (<code>["gunicorn","…"]</code>), not the shell form, so signals (SIGTERM on pod stop) reach your process for clean shutdown.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Layers also speed up pod startup</h5>
                    <p>Because layers are cached <em>on the node</em>, once one easypj pod has pulled the base + deps layers, the next pod on that node reuses them and starts almost instantly. Shared base images across your apps = less to pull, faster scaling.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.4 Building the easypj Image</h3>
                <p>Now let's package easypj into the image Kubernetes will pull and run as pods. We'll go straight to the <strong>production-grade, multi-stage, non-root</strong> pattern — the way you'd actually ship it.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Dockerfile.production — multi-stage</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-docker"><span class="comment"># ── Stage 1: builder — compile/install deps with build tools ──</span>
FROM python:3.13-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /app
ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends gcc libpq-dev \
    &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
COPY requirements.txt .                       <span class="comment"># copy deps list FIRST (cache layer)</span>
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt

<span class="comment"># ── Stage 2: runtime — slim final image, NO build tools ──</span>
FROM python:3.13-slim
WORKDIR /app
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
COPY --from=builder /install /usr/local       <span class="comment"># just the installed packages</span>
COPY . .                                      <span class="comment"># easypj source (changes most → last)</span>
RUN python manage.py collectstatic --noinput
RUN useradd -m app                            <span class="comment"># create a non-root user…</span>
USER app                                      <span class="comment"># …and run as it (security!)</span>
EXPOSE 8000
CMD ["gunicorn", "easypj.wsgi:application", "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000", "--workers", "3"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">.dockerignore — keep junk &amp; secrets out of the image</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Don't copy these into the build context / image</span>
.git/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.env                <span class="comment"># never bake secrets into an image!</span>
db.sqlite3
venv/
env/
node_modules/
*.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Always run as non-root</h5>
                    <p>Notice the <code>useradd app</code> + <code>USER app</code> lines. Running a container as root is a real risk — if an attacker breaks out, they land as root on the node. The image declares the intent; in Kubernetes you <em>enforce</em> it with a <code>securityContext</code> (<code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>) and Pod Security Standards (Ch 27).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Multi-Stage? Size &amp; Safety</h4>
                <p>The builder stage has compilers and <code>-dev</code> headers; the runtime stage copies only the <em>installed result</em> and throws the builder away. Smaller image = faster pulls, less attack surface, fewer CVEs.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Multi-stage build — discard the toolchain, keep the app</div>
<pre>
   ❌ single stage:  python:3.13 + gcc + libpq-dev + build tools + app   →  ~900 MB 😬
   ✅ multi-stage:   python:3.13-slim + installed deps + app (no tools)  →  ~150 MB ✅

   ┌─────────────────────────┐   COPY --from=builder   ┌─────────────────────────┐
   │ Stage 1: BUILDER        │   only the /install     │ Stage 2: RUNTIME        │
   │  gcc, libpq-dev, headers │  ─────────────────────▶ │  slim base + deps + app │
   │  pip install …           │   (build tools are      │  runs as non-root        │
   │  💥 DISCARDED after build │    left behind)         │  ← THE FINAL IMAGE       │
   └─────────────────────────┘                         └─────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Build &amp; Test Workflow</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — build, run, verify, push</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Build the image (tag it — never rely on :latest)</span>
docker build -f Dockerfile.production -t easypj:v1 .

<span class="comment"># Run it locally and map the port</span>
docker run -d -p 8000:8000 --name easypj-app easypj:v1

<span class="comment"># Verify it serves /jobs/</span>
curl http://localhost:8000/jobs/

<span class="comment"># Inspect logs if it misbehaves</span>
docker logs easypj-app

<span class="comment"># Tag &amp; push to a registry so Kubernetes can pull it later</span>
docker tag easypj:v1 myregistry.io/easypj:v1
docker push myregistry.io/easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Security Checklist</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>Do ✅</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Use slim / distroless base images</li>
                            <li>Run as non-root (<code>USER app</code>)</li>
                            <li>Pin versions (<code>python:3.13-slim</code>, not <code>:latest</code>)</li>
                            <li>Scan images (Trivy / Grype) in CI</li>
                            <li>Use <code>.dockerignore</code> to exclude secrets</li>
                            <li>Set <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> in K8s</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>Avoid ❌</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Running as root inside the container</li>
                            <li><code>:latest</code> (unpredictable, non-reproducible)</li>
                            <li>Baking secrets / <code>.env</code> into the image</li>
                            <li>Full-OS base images (Ubuntu 1 GB+)</li>
                            <li>Leaving build tools in the final image</li>
                            <li>Skipping vulnerability scans</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Build Context — What <code>docker build .</code> Actually Sends</h4>
                <p>That trailing <code>.</code> in <code>docker build -t easypj:v1 .</code> is not "the Dockerfile" — it's the <strong>build context</strong>: the whole directory is tarred up and shipped to the build engine, which is why a fat context (a <code>.git</code> folder, a venv, node_modules) makes builds slow and can leak secrets into the image. The <code>.dockerignore</code> is what trims it.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">docker build . — the context is the directory, not just the Dockerfile</div>
<pre>
   your project dir (the "context")            🔨 build engine (BuildKit)
   ┌───────────────────────────┐              ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ Dockerfile                 │  ──tar the── │ reads Dockerfile, runs each   │
   │ requirements.txt           │   WHOLE dir  │ instruction → cached layers   │
   │ easypj/  manage.py         │  ──────────▶ │ (.dockerignore'd files never  │
   │ .git/  venv/  .env  ✂️       │   (minus     │  enter the context)           │
   │   ^ excluded by .dockerignore│  ignored)   └──────────────────────────────┘
   └───────────────────────────┘
   💡 A 5 MB context builds fast; a 2 GB context (venv/.git) crawls and risks leaking .env.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>ENV vs ARG — Build-Time vs Run-Time Variables</h4>
                <p>A frequent source of broken builds and leaked secrets. They look similar but live in different worlds:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th><code>ARG</code></th><th><code>ENV</code></th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Available at…</td><td>build time only</td><td>build time AND run time</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>In the running container?</td><td>❌ gone</td><td>✅ present (e.g. <code>DEBUG=False</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Set via</td><td><code>docker build --build-arg VER=1.2</code></td><td>baked in, or overridden by K8s env</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Secrets?</td><td>❌ no — visible in image history</td><td>❌ no — use Secrets/ConfigMaps in K8s</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Never bake secrets into an image (any layer)</h5>
                    <p>Both <code>ARG</code> and <code>ENV</code> values are visible in the image's layer history (<code>docker history</code>) — and anyone who can pull the image can read them. easypj's <code>SECRET_KEY</code> and DB password belong in Kubernetes <strong>Secrets</strong>, injected at runtime (Ch 23), never in the Dockerfile.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Going Smaller Still — Distroless &amp; Scratch</h4>
                <p>Slim is good; <strong>distroless</strong> is the next level — an image with <em>only</em> your app and its runtime: no shell, no package manager, almost no OS userland. Tiny attack surface, tiny size.</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5><code>-slim</code></h5><p>Minimal Debian + your runtime. Has a shell for debugging. The pragmatic default (what we used for easypj).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>distroless</h5><p><code>gcr.io/distroless/python3</code> — just Python + your app. No shell, no apt. Hardest to attack; debug via ephemeral containers (Ch 31).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚛️</div><h5><code>scratch</code></h5><p>Literally empty. Only for static binaries (Go/Rust). Not for Python, which needs a userland.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                    <p>Build <code>easypj:v1</code> from the multi-stage Dockerfile, run it with <code>-p 8000:8000</code>, and <code>curl /jobs/</code>. Then edit a source file and rebuild — watch the <code>pip install</code> layer stay <em>cached</em> while only the code layer rebuilds. Finally run <code>docker history easypj:v1</code> to see the layers and prove no secret leaked in.</p>
                    <details class="drill">
                        <summary>Show solution</summary>
                        <div class="drill-solution">
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) build &amp; run</span>
docker build -f Dockerfile.production -t easypj:v1 .
docker run -d -p 8000:8000 --name easypj easypj:v1
curl http://localhost:8000/jobs/          <span class="comment"># → HTML/JSON from easypj ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># 2) edit a .py file, rebuild — note the cache</span>
docker build -f Dockerfile.production -t easypj:v1 .
<span class="comment"># =&gt; ... RUN pip install ...   ---&gt; Using cache   ✅  (deps layer reused)</span>
<span class="comment"># =&gt; COPY . .                  ---&gt; running        (only your code layer rebuilds)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3) inspect layers; confirm no secret baked in</span>
docker history easypj:v1                   <span class="comment"># each line = one layer; no .env / SECRET_KEY</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>What you should observe:</strong> the second build reuses the <code>pip install</code> layer (<em>Using cache</em>) and only re-runs <code>COPY . .</code> — proof that ordering deps before source pays off. <code>docker history</code> shows the layer stack with no secret values, because <code>.env</code> is excluded by <code>.dockerignore</code> and config comes from K8s at runtime.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.5 Essential Container Commands</h3>
                <p>You'll <strong>build</strong> images with <code>docker</code> on your laptop, but on a real cluster node Docker often isn't installed — there you use <code>crictl</code> (the CRI CLI) to inspect what the kubelet is running. Know both, and know <em>which</em> to reach for.</p>

                <h4>docker — on your build host</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th><th>CKA relevance</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>docker build -t easypj:v1 .</code></td><td>Build an image from a Dockerfile</td><td>Prep images for K8s</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker run -p 8000:8000 easypj:v1</code></td><td>Run a container, expose a port</td><td>Test before deploying</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker ps</code></td><td>List running containers</td><td>Debug outside K8s</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>View a container's logs</td><td>Mirrors <code>kubectl logs</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker exec -it &lt;id&gt; sh</code></td><td>Shell into a container</td><td>Mirrors <code>kubectl exec</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker images</code></td><td>List local images</td><td>Check what's cached</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker push &lt;registry&gt;/easypj:v1</code></td><td>Push an image to a registry</td><td>Required so K8s can pull it</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker pull &lt;image&gt;</code></td><td>Pull an image from a registry</td><td>Pre-cache on nodes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker stop &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Stop a running container</td><td>Graceful shutdown</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker system prune</code></td><td>Clean up unused images/containers</td><td>Reclaim node disk</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>docker ↔ crictl — the same moves, on a node</h4>
                <p>On a node, <code>crictl</code> talks straight to the CRI (containerd/CRI-O). The verbs are nearly identical to Docker, so the muscle memory transfers:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>🐳 docker (build host)</th><th>🔧 crictl / ctr (on the node)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>List containers</td><td><code>docker ps</code></td><td><code>crictl ps</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View logs</td><td><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Shell in</td><td><code>docker exec -it &lt;id&gt; sh</code></td><td><code>crictl exec -it &lt;id&gt; sh</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Inspect</td><td><code>docker inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>crictl inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List images</td><td><code>docker images</code></td><td><code>crictl images</code> / <code>ctr images ls</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Runtime info</td><td><code>docker info</code></td><td><code>crictl info</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>ctr</code> vs <code>crictl</code> — don't mix them up</h5>
                    <p><code>crictl</code> is the <strong>Kubernetes-aware</strong> CRI client — it sees pods/containers the way the kubelet does. <code>ctr</code> is containerd's <em>low-level</em> tool and is namespace-scoped (use <code>-n k8s.io</code> to see K8s containers). For troubleshooting cluster workloads, reach for <code>crictl</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three containerd CLIs — Which One, When</h4>
                <p>On a containerd node you'll meet three tools. They overlap, so people get lost — here's the one-line rule for each:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it is</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>crictl</code></td><td>The CRI debug CLI (K8s view: pods + containers)</td><td><strong>Troubleshooting cluster pods</strong> on a node — your default</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>nerdctl</code></td><td>A <strong>Docker-compatible</strong> CLI for containerd</td><td>Building/running containers by hand with familiar <code>docker</code>-style commands (<code>nerdctl run</code>, <code>nerdctl build</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ctr</code></td><td>containerd's raw, low-level client</td><td>Deep containerd internals; namespace-scoped (<code>-n k8s.io</code>) — rarely needed</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Mental model</h5>
                    <p><code>nerdctl</code> ≈ "docker, but for containerd" (great when you miss Docker's ergonomics on a node). <code>crictl</code> ≈ "what the kubelet sees" (great for debugging actual pods). <code>ctr</code> ≈ "the engine's bare-metal wrench." For the CKA, <code>crictl</code> is the one that earns points.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>A Container's Lifecycle — the States You'll See</h4>
                <p>Whether via <code>docker ps</code>, <code>crictl ps</code>, or <code>kubectl describe pod</code>, a container moves through a small set of states. Recognizing them tells you instantly whether something is healthy, stuck, or dead:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Container lifecycle states</div>
<pre>
   📥 created ──start──▶ ▶️ running ──stop (SIGTERM→SIGKILL)──▶ ⏹️ stopped ──rm──▶ 🗑️ removed
                          │   ▲
                   pause  │   │ unpause
                          ▼   │
                       ⏸️ paused                  💥 exited (code ≠ 0)  ← crashed; K8s → CrashLoopBackOff
                                                  ✅ exited (code 0)    ← finished cleanly (e.g. a Job)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   In Kubernetes you mostly see:  Running · Terminated(reason: Completed | Error | OOMKilled) · Waiting
   (Waiting covers ContainerCreating, ImagePullBackOff, CrashLoopBackOff — the kubelet is retrying.)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.6 Container Runtimes: Beyond Docker</h3>
                <p>Since Kubernetes <strong>v1.24</strong>, Docker is no longer a supported runtime. The kubelet now talks to runtimes directly through the <strong>Container Runtime Interface (CRI)</strong> — and as the admin, you install &amp; configure that runtime on every node.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — CRI (Container Runtime Interface)</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>CRI</strong> is the standard gRPC "socket" the kubelet uses to tell a runtime "run / stop / list this container." Any CRI-compliant runtime plugs in. This is one of the three extension interfaces from Chapter 1 (CRI · CNI · CSI) — the runtime is swappable, so Kubernetes itself never has to care which one you picked.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Container runtime evolution — Docker out of the data path (v1.24)</div>
<pre>
   BEFORE v1.24                          AFTER v1.24
   🤖 kubelet                            🤖 kubelet
      │                                     │  CRI (gRPC)
   dockershim  ❌ (removed)              ┌──┴───────┐
      │                                  ▼          ▼
   🐳 Docker daemon                  🐋 containerd   🔴 CRI-O
      │                                  │          │
   containerd                          📦          📦   containers
      │                              (direct — no Docker daemon, no shim)
   📦 containers
   💡 Docker IMAGES still work everywhere (they're OCI). Only the node-side
      runtime changed. You still BUILD with Docker; nodes RUN via containerd.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>containerd</h5>
                        <p>The industry standard, extracted from Docker itself. Default for kubeadm; used by GKE, EKS, AKS. Lightweight, stable, CNCF-graduated.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Default runtime for kubeadm clusters</li>
                            <li>CLIs: <code>ctr</code> (low-level), <code>crictl</code> (K8s-native)</li>
                            <li>Config: <code>/etc/containerd/config.toml</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div>
                        <h5>CRI-O</h5>
                        <p>Purpose-built for Kubernetes — nothing more. Preferred by Red Hat / OpenShift. Minimal footprint, releases version-aligned to K8s.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Used by OpenShift &amp; Fedora CoreOS</li>
                            <li>Version-locked to Kubernetes versions</li>
                            <li>Config: <code>/etc/crio/crio.conf</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The #1 runtime gotcha: cgroup-driver mismatch</h5>
                    <p>The kubelet and the container runtime must use the <strong>same cgroup driver</strong> — on modern systemd hosts that means <code>systemd</code>. If containerd is left on the <code>cgroupfs</code> default while the kubelet expects <code>systemd</code>, the <strong>kubelet won't start and the node stays NotReady</strong>. The fix is to set <code>SystemdCgroup = true</code> in <code>/etc/containerd/config.toml</code> and restart containerd (you'll do this in Ch 9).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Runtime Troubleshooting — Start Here on a NotReady Node</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — on the node</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Is the runtime even running?</span>
systemctl status containerd

<span class="comment"># Did the kubelet complain about the CRI?</span>
journalctl -u kubelet | grep -i "container runtime"

<span class="comment"># List containers + check the CRI endpoint, via crictl</span>
crictl ps
crictl info | grep -i runtime

<span class="comment"># Restart the runtime if it's wedged</span>
sudo systemctl restart containerd</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Full Call Chain — Who Actually Starts the Container</h4>
                <p>"containerd runs the container" is true but vague. There are several layers between the kubelet's decision and a process appearing on the node, and knowing them tells you <em>exactly</em> which component to suspect when a container won't start:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">From kubelet to a running process — the runtime stack</div>
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet            "start this pod's containers"
      │  CRI (gRPC over a unix socket: /run/containerd/containerd.sock)
      ▼
   🐋 containerd         high-level runtime: pulls image, unpacks layers (snapshots),
      │                  manages container lifecycle &amp; the image store
      ▼
   🔧 containerd-shim    one per container; keeps the container alive &amp; reparents it
      │                  if containerd restarts (so a runtime upgrade ≠ killing pods)
      ▼
   ⚙️ runc               low-level OCI runtime: the program that actually creates it —
      │                  clone() with namespace flags + cgroups + pivot_root, then exec
      ▼
   📦 your process       gunicorn (PID 1 inside the container) — easypj is now running
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Job</th><th>If it's broken you'd see…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td>Decides &amp; calls the CRI</td><td>Node NotReady; nothing starts</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CRI socket</strong></td><td>The gRPC endpoint</td><td><code>crictl</code> "connect: no such file or directory"</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>containerd</strong></td><td>Pull/unpack image, manage lifecycle</td><td>ImagePull errors; runtime down → NotReady</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>containerd-shim</strong></td><td>Babysit each container</td><td>Rare; containers die when containerd restarts</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>runc</strong></td><td>clone()+cgroups+pivot_root, exec</td><td>"failed to create container" / permission errors</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why the shim matters (a real-world win)</h5>
                    <p>Because each container has its own <strong>shim</strong> holding it, you can <em>restart or upgrade containerd itself without killing running pods</em> — the shims keep the containers alive and re-attach. That's the kind of detail that separates "knows the commands" from "understands the system," and it's why a runtime hiccup doesn't automatically take your workloads down.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Building Images Without a Docker Daemon</h4>
                <p>If nodes no longer run Docker, how does CI build the easypj image — especially when the build itself runs <em>as a pod inside the cluster</em>? You don't need the Docker daemon at all. Several tools build OCI images without it (and without root), which matters because giving CI access to a Docker socket is a serious security hole:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧰</div>
                        <h5>BuildKit</h5>
                        <p>The modern build engine (now Docker's default too). Faster, parallel, better caching. Runs standalone as <code>buildkitd</code> — no full Docker needed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🪄</div>
                        <h5>kaniko</h5>
                        <p>Builds from a Dockerfile <strong>inside a container/pod</strong>, no daemon, no privileged access. The go-to for building images in a K8s-based CI pipeline.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔨</div>
                        <h5>buildah</h5>
                        <p>Red Hat's daemonless builder (pairs with <code>podman</code>). Scriptable, rootless, OCI-native. Common on OpenShift.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Why this matters for security</h5>
                    <p>The old pattern — mounting <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> into a CI container so it can build — effectively grants <strong>root on the node</strong> (the Docker daemon runs as root). Daemonless, rootless builders (kaniko/buildah) exist precisely to kill that risk. You'll wire one into the easypj CI/CD pipeline in Chapter 39.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Node Image Garbage Collection — Keeping Disk From Filling</h4>
                <p>Every image a node pulls is cached on its disk (the <strong>imagefs</strong>). Over time that fills up — and a full disk triggers <code>DiskPressure</code>, which evicts pods. So the <strong>kubelet automatically garbage-collects</strong> unused images; as the admin, you tune the thresholds:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubelet image garbage collection — high/low watermarks</div>
<pre>
   node disk usage (imagefs) ▲
   100% ┤
        │   imageGCHighThresholdPercent (default 85%) ─────────── ▶ GC TRIGGERS
    85% ┤■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■
        │   kubelet deletes UNUSED images, oldest-first, until…
    80% ┤■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■  ◀── imageGCLowThresholdPercent (default 80%)
        │   (images in use by running pods are NEVER removed)
     0% ┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶ time
   ⚠️ cross 85% and never recover → DiskPressure taint → pods evicted (Ch 30/32).
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Admin takeaways on image GC</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>It's automatic, but the <strong>thresholds are yours to set</strong> in the kubelet config (<code>imageGCHighThresholdPercent</code> / <code>LowThresholdPercent</code>).</li>
                        <li>Manual cleanup on a node: <code>crictl rmi --prune</code> removes unused images now.</li>
                        <li>This is the K8s automation of <code>docker system prune</code> — and a frequent root cause when a node mysteriously goes <code>NotReady</code> with <code>DiskPressure</code>.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Where this leads</h5>
                    <p>You now know the <em>artifact</em> (an OCI image of cached layers), <em>how it's built</em> (with or without Docker), and the <em>engine</em> that runs it (the full kubelet → CRI → containerd → shim → runc stack). Chapter 3 zooms out to the whole control plane that decides <em>where</em> these containers run; Chapter 9 has you install &amp; configure the runtime as part of <code>kubeadm</code>; and Chapter 32 returns here when a node goes NotReady.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 2.7 IMAGES IN KUBERNETES — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.7 Images in Kubernetes — Pull Policy, Registries &amp; Failures</h3>
                <p>You built <code>easypj:v1</code> in §2.4. Now the <em>cluster</em> side: how Kubernetes <strong>names</strong>, <strong>pulls</strong>, <strong>authenticates</strong>, and <strong>garbage-collects</strong> images — and the pull errors you'll diagnose on the exam.</p>

                <h4>① Image reference syntax &amp; defaults</h4>
                <p>A full reference is <code>[REGISTRY[:PORT]/]NAME[:TAG][@DIGEST]</code>. Leave parts off and Kubernetes fills in surprising defaults:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>It resolves to</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>easypj</code></td><td><code>docker.io/library/easypj:latest</code> — default registry <em>and</em> default tag</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>easypj:v1</code></td><td><code>docker.io/library/easypj:v1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>registry.example.com:5000/easypj:v1</code></td><td>your private registry on port 5000</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>easypj@sha256:9f2a…</code></td><td>pinned by <strong>digest</strong> — exact bytes, ignores tags</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning"><p class="mb-0">⚠️ A bare image with no registry comes from <strong>Docker Hub</strong> (<code>docker.io</code>), not your private registry — a classic "why is it pulling the wrong thing?" surprise.</p></div>

                <h4>② Tags vs digests — mutable name vs immutable bytes</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🏷️ Tag (<code>:v1</code>)</h5><p>Human-friendly but <strong>mutable</strong> — someone can re-push <code>:v1</code> to different bytes, so two nodes may run different builds of "the same" tag.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>🔒 Digest (<code>@sha256:…</code>)</h5><p><strong>Immutable</strong> &amp; tamper-evident — the same digest is byte-identical everywhere, forever. Pin production with <code>easypj:v1@sha256:…</code>.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>③ <code>imagePullPolicy</code> — the default depends on your tag (exam favourite)</h4>
                <p>If you don't set <code>imagePullPolicy</code>, Kubernetes <em>infers</em> it from the tag — the single most-missed image fact on the exam:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Image tag</th><th>Default policy</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>:latest</code> or no tag</td><td><code>Always</code></td><td>pulls from the registry on every pod start</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>a fixed tag (<code>:v1</code>)</td><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>pulls only if the node lacks it (cached otherwise)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>@sha256:…</code> digest</td><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>cached; the digest already guarantees the bytes</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">Override explicitly with <code>Always</code> / <code>IfNotPresent</code> / <code>Never</code> (<code>Never</code> = it must already be on the node). <strong>Gotcha:</strong> running <code>:latest</code> in production means an unannounced re-pull can change your app under you — always deploy a fixed tag or a digest.</p>

                <h4>④ Private registries — <code>imagePullSecrets</code></h4>
                <p>To pull from a private registry, create a docker-registry Secret and reference it — on the Pod, or (cleaner) on the ServiceAccount so every pod inherits it.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — create the registry secret</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com --docker-username=ci --docker-password=$PASS</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod spec — reference it</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  imagePullSecrets:
  - name: regcred          <span class="comment"># ← pod-level: this pod only</span>
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: registry.example.com/easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><p class="mb-0">💡 Attach <code>imagePullSecrets</code> to the namespace's ServiceAccount and <em>every</em> pod using it pulls privately with no per-pod config. A missing/wrong secret shows up as <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> with an <em>"unauthorized"</em> event.</p></div>

                <h4>⑤ Pull failure states — read the event, fix the cause</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Means</th><th>Usual cause → fix</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>ErrImagePull</code></td><td>a pull attempt just failed</td><td>transient / registry hiccup → kubelet retries</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ImagePullBackOff</code></td><td>repeated failures, backing off (up to ~5 min)</td><td>wrong name/tag · missing <code>imagePullSecrets</code> · registry unreachable</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>InvalidImageName</code></td><td>the reference is malformed</td><td>fix the image string (typo / bad characters)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">Diagnose with <code>kubectl describe pod easypj-web</code> → the <strong>Events</strong> section names the exact error.</p>

                <h4>⑥ Why a pod sits in <code>ContainerCreating</code> — serial pulls</h4>
                <p class="mb-0">By default the kubelet pulls images <strong>one at a time per node</strong> (to avoid hammering the registry). Several big images on a fresh node = pods wait in <code>ContainerCreating</code> for minutes. Pre-pulling images onto nodes — or smaller images (§2.4) — makes pods start faster. Tunable via the kubelet's <code>serializeImagePulls</code> / <code>maxParallelImagePulls</code>.</p>

                <h4>⑦ Image garbage collection — the disk watermarks</h4>
                <p class="mb-0">The kubelet auto-deletes unused images when node disk crosses <code>imageGCHighThresholdPercent</code> (default <strong>85%</strong>), oldest first, until it drops to <code>imageGCLowThresholdPercent</code> (default <strong>80%</strong>). Images in use by running pods are never removed. If disk stays high the node gets a <strong>DiskPressure</strong> condition and starts evicting pods (Ch 3 §node health / Ch 30). Manual sweep: <code>crictl rmi --prune</code>.</p>

                <h4>⑧ Beyond — RuntimeClass (choosing a stronger sandbox)</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>What it is</h5><p>A <code>RuntimeClass</code> object maps a name → a runtime <em>handler</em> the node supports; a pod opts in with <code>spec.runtimeClassName</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>Why you'd use it</h5><p>Run untrusted / multi-tenant workloads in a stronger sandbox — <strong>gVisor</strong> (user-space kernel) or <strong>Kata</strong> (lightweight VM) — instead of default <code>runc</code>. Adds per-pod overhead.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — what a container REALLY is: namespaces, cgroups &amp; union filesystems</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>A container is <strong>not</strong> a tiny VM. It's an ordinary Linux <strong>process</strong> with three kernel features applied: <strong>namespaces</strong> (what it can <em>see</em>), <strong>cgroups</strong> (what it can <em>use</em>), and a <strong>union filesystem</strong> (what it can <em>read/write</em>). That's the whole trick — and understanding it is what turns "OOMKilled" and "Exit 137" from mysteries into one-glance diagnoses.</p>

                    <h5>🗺️ The three pillars of isolation</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌──────────────────────── HOST LINUX KERNEL (shared by all containers) ────────────────────┐
   │   🪟 NAMESPACES (visibility)     📊 CGROUPS (limits)        💾 UNION FS (layers)            │
   │   PID · NET · MNT · UTS          CPU · Memory · IO          overlay2 (lowerdir = image,    │
   │   IPC · USER · CGROUP · TIME     PIDs · HugeTLB             upperdir = writable top)        │
   └───────────────┬──────────────────────┬────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┘
                   │                       │                        │
                   ▼                       ▼                        ▼
   ┌──────────────────────────────── 📦 the easypj container = a PROCESS ──────────────────────┐
   │  PID ns   → sees itself as PID 1            (host sees PID 48291)                          │
   │  NET ns   → its own eth0 (veth → host bridge); the whole POD shares this ns               │
   │  MNT ns   → sees /app as root              (host: /var/lib/containerd/…)                   │
   │  UTS ns   → hostname "easypj-web-6d8f9-x7k2"                                               │
   │  USER ns  → sees UID 0, mapped to UID 1000 on the host (rootless)                          │
   │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────   │
   │  CPU cgroup    → limited to 500m (0.5 core) → over-use is THROTTLED (slowed)               │
   │  Memory cgroup → limited to 512Mi          → over-use is OOM-KILLED (Exit 137)             │
   │  PIDs cgroup   → max N processes            → fork() fails with EAGAIN                       │
   │  rootfs        → overlay2: read-only image layers + one writable upper layer               │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>

                    <h5>🪟 The 8 namespaces — how a container gets its own view of the world</h5>
                    <p>When containerd starts easypj, it calls <code>clone()</code>/<code>unshare()</code> with namespace flags. Each namespace isolates one dimension:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>Isolates</th><th>Why it matters in K8s</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>PID</strong></td><td>Process IDs — container sees its own PID 1</td><td><code>kubectl exec</code> PIDs ≠ host PIDs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NET</strong></td><td>Interfaces, routes, iptables</td><td>Each <em>pod</em> gets one NET ns, shared by its containers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>MNT</strong></td><td>Mount points / root filesystem</td><td>emptyDir, hostPath, PVC all mount here</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>UTS</strong></td><td>Hostname &amp; domain</td><td>The pod's hostname</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>IPC</strong></td><td>Shared memory, semaphores</td><td>Containers in a pod share IPC</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>USER</strong></td><td>UID/GID mappings</td><td><code>runAsUser</code>, rootless containers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CGROUP</strong></td><td>The cgroup root it can see</td><td>Hides the host's cgroup tree</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>TIME</strong></td><td>Clock offsets</td><td>Newer kernels (5.6+)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>💾 Union filesystem — how image layers become a writable rootfs</h5>
                    <p>overlay2 stacks the read-only image layers under a single writable layer, presenting one merged view:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ upperdir (READ-WRITE)  → all runtime writes land here          │ ← EPHEMERAL: gone when
   │   …/&lt;container-id&gt;/upper                                        │   the container dies!
   ├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │            ▼  merged, overlaid view the process sees           │
   ├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ lowerdir (READ-ONLY image layers, newest on top):             │
   │   L3  sha256:jkl…   easypj source code                        │
   │   L2  sha256:ghi…   pip packages                              │
   │   L1  sha256:def…   python:3.13-slim base                     │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   read:  check upper → L3 → L2 → L1 → ENOENT      write: copy-up from lower, then modify
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA insight — Exit Code 137 = OOMKilled.</strong> 137 = 128 + SIGKILL(9). The container blew past its <code>resources.limits.memory</code> and the kernel's OOM killer terminated it. Confirm with <code>kubectl describe pod easypj-web-…</code> → <code>State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled</code>. The fix: raise the memory limit or cut the app's usage. And because <code>upperdir</code> is ephemeral, <em>any</em> data a restarted container wrote is gone — which is the whole reason Volumes (Ch 21) exist.</div>

                    <h5>📊 cgroups — how a memory/CPU limit is actually enforced (v1 vs v2)</h5>
                    <p>"cgroups limit resources" is where most explanations stop. Here's the real mechanism, because it directly explains throttling, OOMKills, and the kubelet's QoS classes. A <strong>cgroup</strong> is a kernel-managed group of processes with accounting + limits; your easypj container is placed in one, and the kubelet writes its limits into special files:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Pod spec field</th><th>cgroup setting (v2)</th><th>What the kernel does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>resources.requests.cpu: 250m</code></td><td><code>cpu.weight</code> (relative share)</td><td>Under contention, you get a <em>proportional</em> slice — a guarantee, not a cap</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resources.limits.cpu: 500m</code></td><td><code>cpu.max</code> (quota per period)</td><td>Exceed it → process is <strong>THROTTLED</strong> (slowed), never killed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resources.limits.memory: 512Mi</code></td><td><code>memory.max</code></td><td>Exceed it → the <strong>OOM killer</strong> terminates it → <code>Exit 137</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resources.requests.memory</code></td><td>(scheduling + <code>memory.low</code>)</td><td>Used to place the pod; soft-protects that much memory</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>cgroups v1 (older)</th><th>cgroups v2 (modern default)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Hierarchy</td><td>One tree <em>per controller</em> (cpu, memory, … separate)</td><td>One <strong>unified</strong> tree for all controllers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Controllers could disagree / be mounted differently</td><td>Cleaner, consistent limits + pressure metrics (PSI)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Kubernetes</td><td><strong>Deprecated (v1.35)</strong> — kubelet refuses to start on a v1 node by default (override: <code>failCgroupV1: false</code>)</td><td>Recommended &amp; the default; required for some features (PSI-based eviction, MemoryQoS)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is exactly why CPU and memory behave differently.</strong> CPU is <em>compressible</em> → over-limit = throttle (your app just runs slower). Memory is <em>incompressible</em> → over-limit = OOMKill (you can't "slow down" RAM). That asymmetry drives every right-sizing decision in Chapter 15.</div>

                    <h5>📥 How an image pull actually works (manifest → config → layer blobs)</h5>
                    <p>"It pulls the image" hides a precise OCI flow — and understanding it explains layer caching, multi-arch images, and most ImagePull failures:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   crictl/kubelet asks containerd for  easypj@sha256:abc…
        │
        ▼  1. GET the MANIFEST (or manifest LIST for multi-arch)
   🏪 registry  ──▶  manifest list:  amd64 → sha256:111…   arm64 → sha256:222…
        │            (containerd picks the node's architecture)
        ▼  2. GET the IMAGE CONFIG (json): env, CMD/ENTRYPOINT, user, the layer order
        ▼  3. GET each LAYER BLOB by digest (only the ones not already cached!)
   📦 layers:   sha256:def… (base)   sha256:ghi… (deps)   sha256:jkl… (easypj code)
        │
        ▼  4. containerd UNPACKS each blob into a snapshot, then overlay2 stacks them
   🗂️ ready rootfs  ──▶  runc creates the container from it
   💡 Step 3 is content-addressed: if another image already pulled sha256:def…, it's
      REUSED from the node — that's why shared base images make pods start fast.
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 ImagePull failures, decoded.</strong> Fails at step 1 (manifest) → wrong name/tag or "not found". Fails with "unauthorized" → private registry, no <code>imagePullSecret</code>. Fails at step 3 (a blob) → network/registry flake or disk-full on the node. Knowing <em>which</em> step broke turns a vague <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> into a targeted fix.</div>

                    <h5>🧩 Which namespaces are shared <em>inside</em> a pod</h5>
                    <p>A pod's containers don't each get 8 fresh namespaces — they <strong>share some</strong> (that's what makes them a pod) and isolate the rest:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>Isolates</th><th>Shared across a pod's containers?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>NET</strong></td><td>interfaces, IP, ports</td><td>✅ yes — one pod IP; that's why containers reach each other on <code>localhost</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>IPC</strong></td><td>shared memory, semaphores</td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>PID</strong></td><td>process IDs</td><td>optional — only with <code>shareProcessNamespace: true</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>MNT · UTS · USER · CGROUP</strong></td><td>mounts · hostname · UID maps · cgroup view</td><td>❌ no — each container is isolated</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The pause container</strong> holds the shared NET + IPC namespaces open, so app containers join them — which is why the pod keeps its IP even as individual containers restart.</div>

                    <h5>📂 overlayfs — how the writable layer really behaves (copy-on-write)</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   READ  /app/manage.py    → search top-down: upper? no → L3? no → L2? no → L1 base? YES   (no copy)
   WRITE /app/cache.txt    → the file is COPIED UP into the writable upper layer, then changed there
   DELETE /usr/bin/oldtool → can't touch the read-only image, so overlayfs writes a "whiteout"
                             marker that simply HIDES the file from the container's view
   ⚠️ the upper layer is the container's ephemeral R/W scratch — gone when the container is removed
      (exactly why durable pod data needs a Volume / PVC — Ch 6)
</pre>

                    <h5>🚪 Exit codes — decode them at a glance</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Exit</th><th>Means</th><th>First move</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>clean exit</td><td>fine (Job done; <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> still restarts it)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>1</code>–<code>128</code></td><td>app error / crash</td><td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>137</code></td><td>128 + 9 (SIGKILL) → usually <strong>OOMKilled</strong></td><td>raise the memory limit / fix a leak (<code>describe</code> shows <code>OOMKilled</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>143</code></td><td>128 + 15 (SIGTERM)</td><td>graceful stop — or shutdown ran past the grace period</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>139</code></td><td>128 + 11 (SIGSEGV)</td><td>app/runtime bug (segfault)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> images and runtimes follow the <strong>OCI</strong> (Open Container Initiative) image &amp; runtime specs, which is why a Docker-built image runs under containerd/CRI-O unchanged. Layers are content-addressed by <code>@sha256:</code> digest (pin these for true reproducibility). Every pod also has a tiny <strong>pause/sandbox</strong> container that <em>holds</em> the shared network + IPC namespaces so app containers can join them. <code>privileged: true</code> effectively switches namespace isolation OFF — which is why restricted Pod Security forbids it. For stronger isolation without a full VM, sandboxed runtimes like <strong>gVisor</strong> (user-space kernel) and <strong>Kata</strong> (lightweight microVM) plug in via <code>RuntimeClass</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Containers share the host kernel</strong> — they're processes with namespaces (visibility) + cgroups (limits) + union FS (layers), so they're MB &amp; milliseconds, not GB &amp; minutes.</li>
                    <li><strong>An image is a stack of cached layers.</strong> Order the Dockerfile cheap→expensive (deps before source) so edits don't bust the <code>pip install</code> cache.</li>
                    <li><strong>Multi-stage + non-root</strong> = small, fast-pulling, lower-CVE production images.</li>
                    <li><strong>Kubernetes runs containers via the CRI</strong> (containerd / CRI-O), not Docker — the dockershim was removed in v1.24. You still build with Docker.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>crictl</code> is your node-level lifeline</strong> when kubectl/kubelet is down.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>cgroup-driver mismatch.</strong> containerd default (<code>cgroupfs</code>) vs kubelet's <code>systemd</code> → kubelet won't start, node stays NotReady. Set <code>SystemdCgroup = true</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>:latest</code> is a trap.</strong> It's non-reproducible and defaults <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to <code>Always</code> (tagged images default to <code>IfNotPresent</code>) — surprise pulls and surprise versions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Running as root.</strong> A container breakout as root = root on the node. Always <code>USER</code> + <code>runAsNonRoot</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Kubernetes removed Docker, so my images break."</strong> No — images are OCI; only the node runtime changed.</li>
                    <li><strong>Using <code>docker</code> on a node.</strong> It may not be installed; use <code>crictl</code> (and <code>ctr -n k8s.io</code> for containerd internals).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Debug image issues fast:</strong> <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; | grep -A8 Events</code> — it names ImagePullBackOff vs OOMKilled vs CrashLoop instantly.</li>
                    <li><strong>Order layers for cache hits</strong> — COPY requirements + <code>pip install</code> BEFORE <code>COPY . .</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pin digests</strong> (<code>image@sha256:…</code>) for reproducible, tamper-evident deploys.</li>
                    <li><strong>⚡ crictl is your lifeline</strong> — When the <strong>apiserver or kubelet is down</strong>, <code>kubectl</code> is useless — but the containers are still running on the node. <code>crictl ps</code> / <code>crictl logs</code> let you see and debug them <em>directly</em> via the runtime. This is exactly the move the Troubleshooting domain (30%) wants from you (Ch 32).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 2)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">build host (docker) + node (crictl)</span>
                </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># build host</span>
docker build -t easypj:v1 .          <span class="comment"># build image from Dockerfile</span>
docker run -d -p 8000:8000 easypj:v1 <span class="comment"># run + expose</span>
docker push myregistry.io/easypj:v1  <span class="comment"># push so K8s can pull</span>

<span class="comment"># on a node (Docker may be absent) — talk to the CRI directly</span>
crictl ps                            <span class="comment"># list running containers</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;            <span class="comment"># view logs</span>
crictl images                        <span class="comment"># images cached on this node</span>
crictl info | grep -i runtime        <span class="comment"># which runtime + endpoint</span>
systemctl status containerd          <span class="comment"># is the runtime up?</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Containerize easypj &amp; Inspect It Like an Admin</h5>
                <p>A chapter capstone that exercises the whole pipeline — try each step before peeking:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Build the multi-stage <code>easypj:v1</code> and confirm it's slim.</li>
                    <li>Run it and hit <code>/jobs/</code>.</li>
                    <li>On a cluster node, use <code>crictl</code> (not <code>docker</code>) to find the running container, read its logs, and identify the runtime.</li>
                    <li>Verify the node's cgroup driver is <code>systemd</code>.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">1–2 · build &amp; run (build host)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">docker build -f Dockerfile.production -t easypj:v1 .
docker images easypj                 <span class="comment"># ~150MB (slim, multi-stage) ✅ not ~900MB</span>
docker run -d -p 8000:8000 --name easypj easypj:v1
curl http://localhost:8000/jobs/     <span class="comment"># easypj responds ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">3–4 · inspect on a cluster node (no docker, no kubectl)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps | grep easypj              <span class="comment"># find the container ID</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;            <span class="comment"># its stdout/stderr (like kubectl logs)</span>
crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt; | grep -i image   <span class="comment"># which image it runs</span>
crictl info | grep -i runtime        <span class="comment"># runtime = containerd, + the CRI endpoint</span>

<span class="comment"># 4) confirm cgroup driver matches the kubelet (must be systemd)</span>
grep -i SystemdCgroup /etc/containerd/config.toml   <span class="comment"># → SystemdCgroup = true ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>What you proved:</strong> easypj ships as a small OCI image; the node runs it via <strong>containerd</strong> (Docker is nowhere on the data path); <code>crictl</code> gives you full visibility <em>without</em> kubectl; and the cgroup driver lines up so the kubelet stays healthy. That's the entire Chapter 2 mental model exercised end-to-end.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A — CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 2 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">State two concrete differences between a VM and a container.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) A VM runs a <strong>full guest OS</strong> on a hypervisor (gigabytes, boots in minutes); a container <strong>shares the host kernel</strong> (megabytes, starts in milliseconds). (2) VM isolation is hardware-level/strong; container isolation is process-level via namespaces + cgroups.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>🖥️ Virtual Machine</th><th>📦 Container</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Kernel</td><td>its own full guest OS on a hypervisor</td><td>shares the host kernel</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Size · start</td><td>GBs · boots in minutes</td><td>MBs · starts in milliseconds</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Isolation</td><td>hardware-level (strong)</td><td>process-level — namespaces + cgroups</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The shared kernel is the root cause of every "light &amp; fast" property — and of the weaker isolation that later security chapters harden.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. Give three likely causes and how to confirm.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Likely: (1) wrong image name/tag, (2) private registry with no <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, (3) registry unreachable / rate-limited. Confirm with <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> and read the <strong>Events</strong> — it states the exact pull error.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Likely cause</th><th>Event says…</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>wrong image name/tag</td><td><code>not found</code> / <code>manifest unknown</code></td><td>correct the image reference</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>private registry, no creds</td><td><code>unauthorized</code></td><td>add an <code>imagePullSecrets</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>registry unreachable / throttled</td><td><code>timeout</code> / <code>429</code></td><td>fix networking · mirror the image</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Events is always the first stop. "unauthorized" → credentials; "not found" → name/tag typo; "timeout" → network/registry.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does a multi-stage build produce a smaller, safer image?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>builder</strong> stage holds compilers and <code>-dev</code> headers; the <strong>runtime</strong> stage copies only the installed result and discards the builder. Less in the final image = smaller size, faster pulls, and a smaller attack surface / fewer CVEs.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>easypj's image drops from ~900 MB to ~150 MB this way, and nothing exploitable (gcc, build deps) ships to production.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl</code> is unavailable on a node. How do you list and log the containers?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <strong><code>crictl</code></strong>, which talks to the CRI directly:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the node — talk to the CRI directly</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps                  <span class="comment"># list running containers</span>
crictl logs &lt;id&gt;           <span class="comment"># view a container's logs</span>
crictl inspect &lt;id&gt;        <span class="comment"># full details: mounts, env, state</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>When the apiserver/kubelet is down, the containers still run — crictl is the node-level lifeline (Ch 32).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the CRI, and what replaced dockershim in v1.24?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>Container Runtime Interface</strong> is the standard gRPC API the kubelet uses to drive a runtime. v1.24 removed the <code>dockershim</code> translation layer; nodes now run containers via a CRI-native runtime — <strong>containerd</strong> (default) or <strong>CRI-O</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Docker-built images still work (they're OCI) — only the node-side runtime path changed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Reorder a Dockerfile so <code>pip install</code> stays cached across code edits.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Copy the deps list and install <em>before</em> copying the source, so editing code only busts the last layer:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Dockerfile — deps before source</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-docker">COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt   <span class="comment"># cached unless deps change</span>
COPY . .                                             <span class="comment"># only THIS layer busts on a code edit</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Layers cache top-down by content; put what changes <em>least</em> (deps) low, what changes <em>most</em> (your code) high.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name three ways to shrink the easypj image.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) Multi-stage build (drop build tools); (2) a slim/distroless base (<code>python:3.13-slim</code> not full <code>python</code>); (3) a <code>.dockerignore</code> to exclude <code>.git</code>, caches, venvs. (Bonus: combine <code>RUN</code> layers, <code>--no-cache-dir</code> for pip.)</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Smaller images pull faster (quicker pod starts/scaling) and expose fewer packages to scan/exploit.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why run as non-root, and how do you do it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>If an attacker escapes a root container, they're root on the node. Declare a user in the image <em>and</em> enforce it in the pod:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">image (Dockerfile) + pod (securityContext)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Dockerfile
RUN useradd app
USER app
---
# Pod spec
securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Defense in depth — the image declares intent; the cluster enforces it cluster-wide via Pod Security Standards, so a bad image can't slip through (Ch 27).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is <code>:latest</code> dangerous, and what should you use instead?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>:latest</code> is a moving pointer — two nodes can run different builds, and it defaults <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to <code>Always</code>. Use <strong>pinned tags</strong> (<code>easypj:v1</code>) or, best, an immutable <strong>digest</strong> (<code>easypj@sha256:…</code>).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Reproducibility &amp; rollback depend on knowing <em>exactly</em> which bytes ran. Digests are tamper-evident too.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node's kubelet won't start, citing cgroups. What's the fix?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The runtime and kubelet disagree on the cgroup driver. Set the runtime to <code>systemd</code>, then restart both:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/containerd/config.toml → systemd driver</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">[plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc.options]
  SystemdCgroup = true

sudo systemctl restart containerd kubelet    <span class="comment"># node should go Ready</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On systemd hosts both must use the <code>systemd</code> driver; a mismatch leaves the node NotReady (Ch 9).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 2 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. You run <code>kubectl describe pod easypj-web</code> and see the event: <em>"Failed to pull image 'myregistry.io/app:v2': rpc error: code = Unknown desc = Error response from daemon: unauthorized"</em>. What is the most likely cause and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The image is stored in a <strong>private registry</strong> and the pod doesn't have credentials configured. Fix by creating an <code>imagePullSecret</code> and referencing it in the pod spec:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">fix the pull auth</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create the secret with registry credentials</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=myregistry.io \
  --docker-username=myuser \
  --docker-password=mypass

<span class="comment"># Add to pod spec under spec.imagePullSecrets:</span>
<span class="comment"># imagePullSecrets:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - name: regcred</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>ImagePullBackOff</code> means the kubelet failed to pull the container image. The "unauthorized" error specifically indicates authentication failure. Common causes: (1) Private registry without <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, (2) Expired/wrong credentials, (3) Wrong registry URL. On the CKA exam, always check the <strong>Events</strong> section of <code>kubectl describe pod</code> — it tells you exactly why the pull failed. Other ImagePullBackOff causes include typos in the image name or tag not existing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>crictl</code> and <code>docker</code> commands? On a Kubernetes node running containerd, which tool should you use to list running containers and why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <strong><code>crictl</code></strong>. It communicates directly with the CRI (Container Runtime Interface) and works with any CRI-compliant runtime (containerd, CRI-O).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Task</th><th>crictl Command</th><th>Docker Equivalent</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>List containers</td><td><code>crictl ps</code></td><td><code>docker ps</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>List images</td><td><code>crictl images</code></td><td><code>docker images</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>View logs</td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Inspect container</td><td><code>crictl inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>docker inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Since Kubernetes v1.24 removed <code>dockershim</code>, Docker is no longer a supported runtime. Most clusters now use <code>containerd</code> directly. The <code>crictl</code> tool is the standard debugging tool for CRI runtimes. On the CKA exam, if you SSH to a node and need to inspect containers at the runtime level, <code>crictl</code> is the expected tool. Configure it via <code>/etc/crictl.yaml</code> to point at the runtime socket (e.g., <code>unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code>).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain the difference between the three <code>imagePullPolicy</code> values: <code>Always</code>, <code>IfNotPresent</code>, and <code>Never</code>. What is the default policy when you use the <code>:latest</code> tag versus a specific tag like <code>:v1.2</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong><code>Always</code></strong> — kubelet always contacts the registry to check for newer image. Re-pulls if digest changed.</li>
                            <li><strong><code>IfNotPresent</code></strong> — kubelet uses the local cached image if available; pulls only if not cached.</li>
                            <li><strong><code>Never</code></strong> — kubelet never pulls; uses local image only. Fails if image isn't cached.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p><strong>Defaults:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>:latest</code> tag → defaults to <code>Always</code></li>
                            <li>Specific tag (e.g., <code>:v1.2</code>) → defaults to <code>IfNotPresent</code></li>
                            <li>No tag specified → treated as <code>:latest</code> → defaults to <code>Always</code></li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a common trap on the CKA. If you specify <code>image: nginx</code> without a tag, Kubernetes treats it as <code>nginx:latest</code> with <code>imagePullPolicy: Always</code>. This means every pod restart will contact the registry. In air-gapped or exam environments, this can cause unexpected <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> errors. Best practice: always use specific tags and explicitly set <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to avoid surprises.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a <strong>container image layer</strong>? Why are layers important for both storage efficiency and pod startup speed in Kubernetes?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A container image is built from <strong>read-only layers</strong>. Each layer represents a filesystem change from a Dockerfile instruction (<code>FROM</code>, <code>RUN</code>, <code>COPY</code>, etc.). Layers are:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Cached and shared</strong> — If two images share a base layer (e.g., <code>python:3.11-slim</code>), the layer is stored and downloaded only once</li>
                            <li><strong>Content-addressable</strong> — Each layer is identified by its SHA256 digest, enabling deduplication</li>
                            <li><strong>Immutable</strong> — once built, a layer never changes; containers add a thin writable layer on top</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>For Kubernetes: shared layers mean that if node already has the base image cached, pulling a new version only downloads changed layers, dramatically speeding up pod startup.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>While the CKA doesn't test image building, understanding layers helps diagnose issues: (1) Large images cause slow pulls and startup — use slim/alpine bases. (2) <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code> leverages the layer cache. (3) If a node's disk fills up with cached images, use <code>crictl rmi --prune</code> to clean unused images. The kubelet also has garbage collection settings (<code>imageGCHighThresholdPercent</code>) that automatically clean old images when disk pressure is detected.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod spec references a <strong>multi-container pod</strong> with an init container and a sidecar container. Explain the difference between init containers and regular sidecar containers in terms of execution order and lifecycle.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Init containers:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Run <strong>sequentially before</strong> any app containers start</li>
                            <li>Must complete successfully (exit 0) before the next init container or app containers start</li>
                            <li>If an init container fails, the pod restarts according to its <code>restartPolicy</code></li>
                            <li>Common uses: wait for a dependency, clone a git repo, populate a shared volume</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p><strong>Sidecar containers:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Run <strong>alongside</strong> the main app container for the entire pod lifetime</li>
                            <li>Share the pod's network namespace (communicate via <code>localhost</code>) and volumes</li>
                            <li>Common uses: log shipping, service mesh proxy, monitoring agent</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  initContainers:            <span class="comment"># Run first, sequentially</span>
    - name: init-db-check
      image: busybox
      command: ['sh', '-c', 'until nslookup db-service; do sleep 2; done']
  containers:                <span class="comment"># Run together after init completes</span>
    - name: easypj-web       <span class="comment"># Main container</span>
      image: easypj:v1
    - name: log-shipper      <span class="comment"># Sidecar</span>
      image: fluentd:latest</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKA frequently tests multi-container pod patterns. Key points: (1) Init containers share volumes with app containers but NOT the same array — they're under <code>initContainers:</code>. (2) As of Kubernetes v1.28+, there's a native <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> for sidecar containers declared in <code>initContainers</code> that makes them true sidecars. (3) All containers in a pod share the same IP address and port space. A sidecar on port 8080 conflicts with the app on 8080.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to troubleshoot a pod on a worker node. The <code>kubectl</code> command is not available on the node. What tools can you use to inspect containers, view logs, and check images directly on the node?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all running containers on the node</span>
sudo crictl ps

<span class="comment"># List all containers (including stopped/failed)</span>
sudo crictl ps -a

<span class="comment"># View logs of a specific container</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
sudo crictl logs --tail 50 &lt;container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># Inspect container details (config, state, mounts)</span>
sudo crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># List all pods on this node</span>
sudo crictl pods

<span class="comment"># List cached images</span>
sudo crictl images

<span class="comment"># Check kubelet status and logs</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On the CKA exam, you will encounter scenarios where you must SSH to a node and troubleshoot without <code>kubectl</code>. The two essential tools are: (1) <strong><code>crictl</code></strong> — for container-level inspection, and (2) <strong><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code></strong> — for kubelet logs showing why pods fail to start. Remember that <code>crictl</code> talks to the container runtime (containerd) via the CRI socket, while <code>kubectl</code> talks to the API server. They show different views of the same containers.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the OCI (Open Container Initiative) and why does it matter that Docker images are OCI-compliant? Can you use Docker-built images on a Kubernetes cluster running containerd?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Yes, absolutely.</strong> Docker-built images work perfectly on containerd or CRI-O clusters because they conform to the <strong>OCI Image Specification</strong>.</p>
                        <p>The OCI defines two standards:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>OCI Image Spec</strong> — how container images are structured (layers, manifests, configs)</li>
                            <li><strong>OCI Runtime Spec</strong> — how containers are executed (runc, crun)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Docker, containerd, CRI-O, and Podman all produce and consume OCI-compliant images. The tool you use to <em>build</em> the image is irrelevant — what matters is the resulting image format.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A common misconception is that removing Docker from Kubernetes means Docker images no longer work. This is false. The removal of <code>dockershim</code> only affects the <em>runtime layer</em>, not the <em>image format</em>. You can build with Docker, Buildah, Kaniko, or any OCI-compliant tool and run the images on any CRI-compliant runtime. The CKA may test this conceptual understanding in a scenario where someone incorrectly believes images need to be "converted" for containerd.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> state. What does this status mean, and what are the systematic steps to diagnose the root cause?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> means the container starts, crashes, and Kubernetes keeps restarting it with exponentially increasing delays (10s, 20s, 40s... up to 5 minutes).</p>
                        <p><strong>Diagnostic steps:</strong></p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pod status and restart count</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -o wide

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check events for clues</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web | grep -A 20 "Events:"

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check container logs (current attempt)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check previous crash logs</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web --previous

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check if it's an OOMKill</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web | grep -i "OOMKilled"

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check if command/args are wrong</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -o yaml | grep -A 5 "command\|args"</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> is one of the most common CKA troubleshooting scenarios. Common root causes: (1) <strong>Application error</strong> — app crashes on startup (check <code>kubectl logs --previous</code>), (2) <strong>Wrong command/args</strong> — container entrypoint fails, (3) <strong>Missing ConfigMap/Secret</strong> — referenced env vars don't exist, (4) <strong>OOMKilled</strong> — container exceeds memory limit, (5) <strong>Liveness probe failure</strong> — probe kills the container before it's ready. The <code>--previous</code> flag is crucial because it shows logs from the <em>last crashed</em> instance, not the currently starting one.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a <strong>container registry</strong>? Explain the full image reference format <code>registry/repository:tag@sha256:digest</code> and what each part means.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A container registry is a storage and distribution system for container images. The full reference format:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">anatomy</span></div>
<pre><code>registry.example.com/namespace/image-name:tag@sha256:abc123...

│                    │          │          │    │
├─ Registry host     │          │          │    └─ Digest (immutable hash)
│  (docker.io if     │          │          └─ Tag (mutable label)
│   omitted)         │          └─ Image name
│                    └─ Namespace/org
└─ Full reference</code></pre></div>
                        <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>nginx</code> → <code>docker.io/library/nginx:latest</code></li>
                            <li><code>mycompany/app:v2</code> → <code>docker.io/mycompany/app:v2</code></li>
                            <li><code>gcr.io/my-project/app:v1</code> → explicit registry</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Understanding image references is critical for troubleshooting <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> errors on the CKA. Common mistakes: (1) Omitting the registry for private images, (2) Using <code>:latest</code> when a specific tag is needed, (3) Not specifying <code>imagePullSecrets</code> for private registries. The <strong>digest</strong> (<code>@sha256:...</code>) is the most reliable way to pin an image — tags are mutable and can be overwritten, but digests are content-addressable and immutable.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You SSH to a Kubernetes worker node and run <code>crictl ps</code> but get the error: <em>"connect: no such file or directory"</em>. What is wrong and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <code>crictl</code> tool cannot find the container runtime socket. Fix by configuring the runtime endpoint:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Option 1: Set environment variable</span>
export CONTAINER_RUNTIME_ENDPOINT=unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Create/edit crictl config file</span>
sudo cat &gt; /etc/crictl.yaml &lt;&lt;EOF
runtime-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
image-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
timeout: 10
debug: false
EOF

<span class="comment"># Now try again</span>
sudo crictl ps</code></pre></div>
                        <p><strong>Common socket paths:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>containerd: <code>unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code></li>
                            <li>CRI-O: <code>unix:///var/run/crio/crio.sock</code></li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKA exam may present a node where <code>crictl</code> isn't configured. The tool needs to know which socket to connect to — this is the CRI endpoint. If <code>/etc/crictl.yaml</code> doesn't exist or has the wrong socket path, <code>crictl</code> will fail. To find the correct socket, check what runtime is installed: <code>systemctl status containerd</code> or <code>systemctl status crio</code>. Then set the endpoint accordingly. This is a quick fix that can save you from being stuck on a troubleshooting question.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 2 Visual Summary — easypj source → image → running pod</div>
<pre>
   👩‍💻 your code            🔨 BUILD (laptop / CI)              🏪 REGISTRY            ☸️ KUBERNETES NODE
   ┌──────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────┐         ┌────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────┐
   │ easypj source │ ─────▶ │ Dockerfile (multi-    │ ──push─▶ │ easypj:v1   │ ─pull─▶ │ 🤖 kubelet → CRI       │
   │ + requirements│  docker│ stage, non-root)     │         │ (cached     │        │ 🐋 containerd starts   │
   │               │  build │ → 📀 image = layers   │         │  layers)    │        │ 📦 the container       │
   └──────────────┘        └──────────────────────┘         └────────────┘        │   namespaces + cgroups │
                                                                                    │   + overlay2 rootfs    │
   ① build once  ──────────  ② push to a registry  ──────────  ③ nodes pull &amp; run ──┘  :8000  ✅
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   One immutable image, built once, runs identically everywhere — and the cluster runs it via the CRI,
   not Docker. That artifact is the unit every later chapter schedules, scales, heals, and secures.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> You take the <code>easypj</code> source plus its dependencies and a <strong>Dockerfile</strong> (multi-stage, non-root) and <code>docker build</code> it into an <strong>image</strong> — a stack of read-only layers. You build it <em>once</em>, then <strong>push</strong> it to a registry where the layers are cached and shareable.</p>
                    <p>When a node needs to run it, the <strong>kubelet</strong> asks the container runtime (containerd) — through the <strong>CRI</strong>, not Docker — to <strong>pull</strong> the image and start a container. That container is just a normal Linux process that the kernel isolates with <strong>namespaces</strong> (what it can see), <strong>cgroups</strong> (what it can use), and an <strong>overlay</strong> root filesystem (its files).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the image is one immutable artifact that runs identically on every node — that reproducibility is why containers exist. From here on, this image is the <em>unit</em> every later chapter schedules, scales, heals, and secures.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 2 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 3 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch3">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 3 · Kubernetes Architecture — The Admin's Blueprint</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Every troubleshooting question is really one question: <strong>"which component failed?"</strong> — and you can't fix what you can't name. This chapter is the map: every part of the control plane and every part of a worker node, what each does, what breaks when it's gone, and the exact end-to-end journey from <code>kubectl apply</code> to a running easypj pod. It is the foundation the entire rest of the guide stands on.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 Architecture</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 CKA Core (25%)</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐍 easypj / jobpost</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The whole cluster at a glance — <strong>control plane</strong> (brain) + <strong>worker nodes</strong> (muscle)</li>
                    <li>✅ Every control-plane component: <strong>API server · etcd · scheduler · controller-manager</strong> (+ cloud-controller-manager)</li>
                    <li>✅ Every worker-node component: <strong>kubelet · kube-proxy · container runtime</strong></li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>How a Pod is born</strong> — the full 8-step journey, grounded on easypj</li>
                    <li>✅ The three network layers (pod / service / external) and what routes each</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Static pods</strong> — the admin-only concept that runs the control plane itself</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🏗️ Architecture knowledge is CKA gold — it's the foundation for ~55% of the exam</h5>
                <p>Every troubleshooting task assumes you know how the components interact. Match the symptom to the component and you've half-solved it:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Node NotReady?</strong> → check the <code>kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pods stuck Pending?</strong> → check the <code>scheduler</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>State lost?</strong> → restore <code>etcd</code> (backup/restore is tested directly).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl</code> hangs?</strong> → the <code>apiserver</code> is down; debug from the node with <code>crictl</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <h4>🧭 Section Map — the Chapter 3 Learning Arc</h4>
            <p>We zoom from the whole cluster, into each half (control plane, then nodes), then trace one pod's entire life across them, and finish with the network and the static-pod trick that bootstraps it all.</p>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Chapter 3 Roadmap — name every part, then watch it work</div>
<pre>
   3.1 🗺️ THE WHOLE CLUSTER ──▶ 3.2 🧠 CONTROL PLANE ──▶ 3.3 💪 WORKER NODES
   brain + muscle, one map      apiserver·etcd·scheduler   kubelet·kube-proxy·
        │                       ·controller-manager        container runtime
        ▼                                                        │
   3.6 📌 STATIC PODS  ◀──── 3.5 🌐 NETWORKING ◀──── 3.4 🐣 HOW A POD IS BORN
