                    <tr><td><a href="#s3-7"><strong>3.7</strong> Node health &amp; comms</a></td><td>How does the CP know a node is alive?</td><td>Conditions + Lease heartbeat + 40s/5min eviction; secure apiserver hub.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.1 ─────────── -->

            <section class="section-block" id="s3-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.1 Cluster Architecture Overview</h3>
                <p>A Kubernetes cluster is two layers: a <strong>control plane</strong> (the brain — makes every decision) and one or more <strong>worker nodes</strong> (the muscle — actually run the workloads). As a CKA admin you install, configure, and repair <em>both</em>. Everything else in this chapter is a zoom-in on one of these two halves.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The complete cluster — brain + muscle</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────────── ☸️ CLUSTER ───────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  🧠 CONTROL PLANE  (decides)                                                            │
   │   ┌──────────────┐ ┌────────────┐ ┌────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────────┐    │
   │   │ 📡 apiserver  │ │ 🗄️ etcd     │ │ 📋 scheduler    │ │ 🔄 controller-manager       │    │
   │   │ the ONLY door │ │ ALL state  │ │ places pods    │ │ reconcile loops             │    │
   │   │ :6443         │ │ :2379/2380 │ │ filter→score→  │ │ (node · ReplicaSet ·        │    │
   │   │ authN/Z/admit │ │ (Raft)     │ │ bind           │ │  endpoint · serviceaccount) │    │
   │   └──────┬───────┘ └────────────┘ └────────────────┘ └────────────────────────────┘    │
   │          │   ⚠️ ONLY the apiserver talks to etcd; every component &amp; kubectl goes        │
   │          │      THROUGH the apiserver (the single choke point).                          │
   │  ────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │
   │  💪 WORKER NODES  (run)                                                                  │
   │   ┌──────▼──────────────────┐                  ┌─────────────────────────┐              │
   │   │ Node 1                   │                  │ Node 2                   │             │
   │   │ 🤖 kubelet  🌐 kube-proxy │                  │ 🤖 kubelet  🌐 kube-proxy │             │
   │   │ 🐋 containerd            │                  │ 🐋 containerd            │             │
   │   │ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐   │                  │ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐   │             │
   │   │ │📦 easypj│ │📦 easypj│   │                  │ │📦 easypj│ │🐘 postgres│ │             │
   │   │ │  web    │ │  web    │   │                  │ │  web    │ │  :5432   │ │             │
   │   │ └────────┘ └────────┘   │                  │ └────────┘ └────────┘   │             │
   │   └─────────────────────────┘                  └─────────────────────────┘              │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="arch-layers">
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-app">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🧠</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Control Plane</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Makes every orchestration decision</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>kube-apiserver</span><span>etcd</span><span>kube-scheduler</span><span>controller-manager</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-k8s">
                        <span class="layer-icon">💪</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Worker Nodes</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Run the actual workloads</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>kubelet</span><span>kube-proxy</span><span>containerd/CRI-O</span><span>Pods</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-infra">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🌐</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Networking Layer</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Connects everything together</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>CNI plugin</span><span>CoreDNS</span><span>Service network</span><span>Pod network</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The mental shortcut</h5>
                    <p>For the rest of your CKA life, sort every problem into one bucket: is it a <strong>decision</strong> problem (control plane — won't schedule, won't reconcile, can't reach the API) or a <strong>run</strong> problem (a node — container won't start, NotReady, networking)? That single split routes you to the right component before you type a command.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.2 Control-Plane Components — Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>These are the components you maintain, troubleshoot, and restore. For each, learn three things: its <strong>job</strong>, its <strong>config location</strong>, and — most exam-relevant — <strong>what breaks when it's gone</strong>.</p>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🍽️ In plain English — the cluster as a restaurant</h5>
                    <p>If the components feel abstract, map them to a busy restaurant — it makes every role (and every failure) intuitive:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Restaurant</th><th>Kubernetes</th><th>So if it's gone…</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>🛎️ the <strong>front desk</strong> — every order/request goes through it</td><td>📡 <strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>nobody can place or change orders (no kubectl)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>📖 the <strong>order ledger</strong> — the single written record of every order</td><td>🗄️ <strong>etcd</strong></td><td>lose the ledger = lose the whole restaurant's state</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🪑 the <strong>host</strong> who seats each party at the best table</td><td>📋 <strong>scheduler</strong></td><td>new parties wait at the door (pods Pending)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🧑‍💼 the <strong>floor manager</strong> watching everything &amp; fixing gaps</td><td>🔄 <strong>controller-manager</strong></td><td>no one refills empty tables (no self-healing)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>👨‍🍳 the <strong>line cook</strong> at each station who actually makes the food</td><td>🤖 <strong>kubelet</strong> (on each node)</td><td>that station stops cooking (node NotReady)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🏃 the <strong>runner</strong> delivering plates to the right table</td><td>🌐 <strong>kube-proxy</strong></td><td>food's made but never reaches diners (Services break)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">Notice the front desk (apiserver) is the only one who reads/writes the ledger (etcd) — exactly like the real cluster. Keep this picture; the rest of §3.2 just adds detail to each role.</p>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2a">a) 📡 kube-apiserver — the front door</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📡 kube-apiserver</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>only</strong> entry point. Every <code>kubectl</code> call, every kubelet, every controller talks to it — and it's the only component that reads/writes etcd.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔐</span> Pipeline</div><div class="anatomy-value">Each request: <strong>authentication</strong> (certs/tokens) → <strong>authorization</strong> (RBAC) → <strong>admission</strong> (validate/mutate) → persist to etcd.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📂</span> Config</div><div class="anatomy-value">Static-pod manifest: <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Port</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>6443</code> (secure HTTPS)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">No <code>kubectl</code>, no new pods, nodes can't report status. <strong>Existing pods keep running</strong> — the kubelet is independent.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2b">b) 🗄️ etcd — the cluster database</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🗄️ etcd — the brain's memory</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">A distributed key-value store holding <strong>ALL</strong> cluster state under <code>/registry/…</code> — pods, services, secrets, RBAC, everything.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> Consensus</div><div class="anatomy-value">Uses <strong>Raft</strong> — a quorum (majority) must agree on every write. That's why HA clusters run 3 or 5 etcd members.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Ports</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>2379</code> (client) · <code>2380</code> (peer)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> CKA</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Backup &amp; restore is directly tested</strong> — know <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> / <code>restore</code> cold.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 etcd lost = cluster lost</h5>
                    <p>Lose etcd without a backup and your <em>entire</em> cluster — every pod, service, secret, and RBAC rule — is gone. This is precisely why "back up etcd" is the #1 admin task and a guaranteed exam item (Ch 29).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd backup (CKA skill)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># snapshot (cert paths come from /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># verify it's readable</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2c">c) 📋 kube-scheduler — the matchmaker</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📋 kube-scheduler</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches for Pods with no node assigned, evaluates every node, and binds the pod to the best fit.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> Process</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Filter</strong> (which nodes <em>can</em> run it?) → <strong>Score</strong> (which is best?) → <strong>Bind</strong> (assign the winner).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📊</span> Considers</div><div class="anatomy-value">CPU/mem requests, nodeSelector, affinity/anti-affinity, taints/tolerations, PV topology, spread constraints.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">New pods stay <code>Pending</code> (existing pods unaffected). Spot it: <code>kubectl get pods -A | grep Pending</code>.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Watch</h5><p>See a Pod with empty <code>nodeName</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Filter</h5><p>Drop nodes that can't fit (resources, taints, affinity)</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Score</h5><p>Rank the survivors (spread, balance, preferences)</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Bind</h5><p>Assign the top node → write to etcd → kubelet takes over</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2d">d) 🔄 kube-controller-manager — the watchdog</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔄 kube-controller-manager (many controllers, one process)</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🖥️</span> Node ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches node heartbeats; marks a node <code>NotReady</code> after ~40s of silence, evicts its pods after ~5 min.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> ReplicaSet ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Keeps the pod count right — spec says 3, only 2 running → creates 1 more.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔗</span> Endpoint ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Wires Services to Pods by maintaining the EndpointSlice (the list of ready pod IPs).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👤</span> ServiceAccount ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Creates the default ServiceAccount for each new namespace.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">No self-healing: crashed pods aren't replaced, scaling/endpoints stall, dead nodes aren't acted on.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">A controller loop — the heart of Kubernetes</div>
<pre>
   ┌─────────── CONTROLLER LOOP (runs forever) ───────────┐
   │  👁️ OBSERVE  ──▶  🧮 COMPARE  ──▶  🛠️ ACT  ──┐         │
   │  actual state    desired vs       reconcile          │
   │  "2 pods"        actual "want 3"  "create 1 pod"      │
   │      ▲                                    │           │
   │      └────────────────────────────────────┘           │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj ReplicaSet: spec replicas=3, see 2 → create 1 → repeat
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2e">e) ☁️ cloud-controller-manager — the cloud glue (optional)</h4>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Only on cloud clusters</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>cloud-controller-manager</strong> integrates a cloud provider: provisioning LoadBalancers, managing routes, and handling node lifecycle (detecting deleted VMs). Bare-metal/kubeadm clusters don't run it. It exists so the core controllers stay cloud-agnostic — another example of Kubernetes keeping vendor specifics behind a plug.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.3 Worker-Node Components — Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>Every node — worker <em>and</em> control-plane — runs three things that actually make containers happen: the <strong>kubelet</strong> (agent), <strong>kube-proxy</strong> (service routing), and a <strong>container runtime</strong>. These are where "run" problems live.</p>

                <h4>a) 🤖 kubelet — the node agent</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🤖 kubelet</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Receives PodSpecs from the API server, drives the runtime to start/stop containers, runs probes, and reports status back.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚡</span> Key fact</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>The kubelet is NOT a pod</strong> — it's a <code>systemd</code> service on the node. Check it with <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📂</span> Config</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Port</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>10250</code> (HTTPS API)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> Troubleshoot</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> — most common causes: expired cert, runtime down, swap on, or cgroup-driver mismatch.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>b) 🌐 kube-proxy — the network plumber</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🌐 kube-proxy</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Programs each node's dataplane so a <strong>Service VIP</strong> load-balances to the right backing Pod IPs.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚙️</span> Modes</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>iptables</strong> (default), <strong>IPVS</strong> (faster at 1000+ services), <strong>nftables</strong> (newer).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> Runs as</div><div class="anatomy-value">A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> (one pod per node) in <code>kube-system</code> — unlike the kubelet, it <em>is</em> a pod.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💡</span> Remember</div><div class="anatomy-value">kube-proxy handles <em>Service</em> networking; the <strong>CNI</strong> handles <em>pod-to-pod</em> networking. Pods ping by IP but Services fail → suspect kube-proxy.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>c) 🐋 container runtime</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div><h5>containerd</h5><p>The default for kubeadm, EKS, AKS, GKE. CNCF-graduated. Drives the actual container lifecycle via the CRI.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div><h5>CRI-O</h5><p>Kubernetes-native, minimal. Used by OpenShift; versioned in lockstep with K8s.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA triage: node shows NotReady? Check in this order</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> — is the agent running?</li>
                        <li><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> — what's the error? (cert / swap / CNI / cgroup)</li>
                        <li><code>systemctl status containerd</code> — is the runtime up?</li>
                        <li><code>crictl ps</code> — can the runtime even list containers?</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Everything the kubelet Actually Does</h4>
                <p>"The kubelet runs containers" undersells it — it's the busiest agent in the cluster and the prime suspect in most node problems. Knowing its full job list tells you exactly what can break:</p>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🤖 the kubelet's responsibilities</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> Register</div><div class="anatomy-value">Registers the node with the apiserver on startup (creates the Node object, advertises capacity/labels).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💓</span> Heartbeat</div><div class="anatomy-value">Renews a <strong>node Lease</strong> (in <code>kube-node-lease</code>) every few seconds so the control plane knows it's alive — stop renewing → NotReady.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👁️</span> Watch</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches the apiserver for Pods bound to <em>its</em> node (and the static-pod dir on disk).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> Run</div><div class="anatomy-value">Drives the CRI to pull images and start/stop containers; sets up the pod sandbox.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💾</span> Mount</div><div class="anatomy-value">Attaches/mounts volumes via the CSI before starting containers (emptyDir, PVC, configMap/secret).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🩺</span> Probe</div><div class="anatomy-value">Runs liveness/readiness/startup probes → restarts dead containers, gates traffic on readiness.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📊</span> Report</div><div class="anatomy-value">Reports pod &amp; node status back to the apiserver; exposes stats (cAdvisor) for <code>kubectl top</code>.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🧹</span> Evict</div><div class="anatomy-value">Under node pressure (disk/memory) it evicts pods and applies the matching taint (e.g. DiskPressure).</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why this matters: the kubelet is "trusted" but standalone</h5>
                    <p>Because the kubelet reads its work from the apiserver but acts <em>independently</em>, two important things follow: (1) if the <strong>apiserver dies, running pods keep running</strong> — the kubelet doesn't need it to keep containers alive; and (2) the kubelet can run <strong>static pods</strong> with no control plane at all, which is exactly how kubeadm bootstraps the cluster (§3.6). It's the one component that bridges "declared in the API" and "actually running on Linux."</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.4 How a Pod Is Born — The Full Journey</h3>
                <p>This is the single most important sequence in Kubernetes — every troubleshooting task assumes it. Watch what happens after <code>kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml</code>, and notice that <strong>no single component runs the show</strong>: each one reconciles its own slice and the pod emerges.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>kubectl → apiserver</h5><p>HTTPS request to <code>:6443</code> with a client cert.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>authN → authZ → admit</h5><p>Who are you? (cert) · Allowed? (RBAC) · Valid? (admission).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>apiserver → etcd</h5><p>The Deployment object is persisted — desired state recorded.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>controllers</h5><p>Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod objects (still nodeless).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>scheduler</h5><p>Sees a nodeless Pod → filter → score → binds to a node.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>kubelet</h5><p>On that node: pulls <code>easypj:v2</code>, starts the container, CNI gives it an IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">7</div><h5>kube-proxy</h5><p>Programs Service routing to the new Pod IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">8</div><h5>Running ✅</h5><p>kubelet reports status → apiserver → etcd; the loops keep watching.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml → a Running pod</div>
<pre>
   👩‍💻 kubectl apply
        │ ① HTTPS :6443 + client cert
        ▼
   📡 apiserver ──② authN → authZ(RBAC) → admission──┐
        │ ③ persist Deployment                       │
        ▼                                            │
   🗄️ etcd  (desired state)  ◀───────────────────────┘
        │ ④ controller-manager: Deployment → ReplicaSet → 3 Pod objects (Pending)
        ▼
   📋 scheduler ── ⑤ filter → score → BIND each Pod → a node
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet (on the node) ── ⑥ 🐋 pull easypj:v2 · start container · 🌐 CNI assigns IP
        ▼
   🌐 kube-proxy ── ⑦ program Service routing to the new Pod IP
        ▼
   📦 easypj Running :8000 ── ⑧ status ─▶ apiserver ─▶ etcd     (loops keep reconciling)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The troubleshooting map — match the symptom to the broken step</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Connection refused / kubectl hangs</strong> → step 1–3 (apiserver/etcd down)</li>
                        <li><strong>Forbidden / Unauthorized</strong> → step 2 (RBAC or certs)</li>
                        <li><strong>Pod stuck Pending</strong> → step 5 (scheduler can't place it)</li>
                        <li><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> → step 6 (kubelet can't pull the image)</li>
                        <li><strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> → step 6 (container starts, then dies)</li>
                        <li><strong>Service unreachable</strong> → step 7 (kube-proxy / endpoints)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🍽️ In plain English — the same journey, as a restaurant order</h5>
                    <p>Tie it back to §3.2's restaurant: <em>"You hand your order to the <strong>front desk</strong> (apiserver), which checks you're allowed and writes it in the <strong>ledger</strong> (etcd). The <strong>floor manager</strong> (controllers) turns 'a table of 3' into three seat requests; the <strong>host</strong> (scheduler) seats each at a table (node); the <strong>line cook</strong> there (kubelet) actually makes the dish (starts the container); and the <strong>runner</strong> (kube-proxy) makes sure diners can find it (Service routing)."</em></p>
                    <p class="mb-0">Same eight steps, zero jargon — and the failure map still works: nobody seated = the host (scheduler) is out; food never delivered = the runner (kube-proxy) is out.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.5 Cluster Networking Overview</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes networking has one founding rule: <strong>every Pod gets its own unique IP</strong>, and all Pods can reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong> — one flat network. A CNI plugin makes that real. Keep the layers straight and networking stops being scary (full depth in Part 5).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The four network layers — from the wire up to the user</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🌍 EXTERNAL traffic (users, internet)                          │
   │    enters via: NodePort · LoadBalancer · Ingress · Gateway     │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🔗 SERVICE network  (stable virtual IPs, e.g. 10.96.0.0/12)    │
   │    kube-proxy DNATs a Service VIP → a ready Pod IP             │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 📦 POD network  (real pod IPs, e.g. 10.244.0.0/16)            │
   │    assigned by the CNI · flat · every pod reaches every pod     │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🖧 NODE network  (the machines/VMs, e.g. 192.168.1.0/24)       │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj-web pod 10.244.1.5  ──reaches──▶  easypj-db 10.244.2.4 (no NAT)
   a client hits the Service VIP → kube-proxy picks a ready easypj-web pod
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CNI Plugins — Your Choice as Admin</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐯</div><h5>Calico</h5><p>Most popular. Full NetworkPolicy, BGP routing. Common in CKA environments.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐝</div><h5>Cilium</h5><p>eBPF-based, deep observability (Hubble), high performance. NetworkPolicy +.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🟦</div><h5>Flannel</h5><p>Simple VXLAN overlay, easy setup — but <strong>no NetworkPolicy</strong>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🕸️</div><h5>Weave</h5><p>Encrypted mesh, auto-discovery, NetworkPolicy support.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CKA task: install a CNI right after <code>kubeadm init</code></h5>
                    <p>A fresh cluster's pods (including CoreDNS) sit <code>Pending</code> and nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> until a CNI is installed — e.g. <code>kubectl apply -f calico.yaml</code>. "No CNI" is a classic NotReady cause: the fix is install a CNI, not restart the kubelet.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Why it's all pluggable — the three "sockets" (CRI · CNI · CSI)</div>
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet   ──CRI──▶  🐋 containerd / CRI-O          RUN containers
   pod network ──CNI──▶  🌐 Calico / Cilium / Flannel   WIRE pods (give IPs)
   volumes     ──CSI──▶  🧱 EBS / PD / Ceph / NFS driver ATTACH storage
   📖 CRI = runtime · CNI = network · CSI = storage — swap the plug, K8s is unchanged.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Rules of the Kubernetes Network Model</h4>
                <p>Every CNI must honour the same contract — four rules that make the "flat network" promise concrete. Memorize these; they're the foundation of all Part 5 troubleshooting:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Rule</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td>Every Pod gets its <strong>own unique IP</strong></td><td>No port-mapping juggling — each pod is a "real host" on the network</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td>All Pods reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong></td><td>The destination sees the <em>real</em> source pod IP (flat L3 network)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td>Nodes reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong></td><td>The kubelet &amp; health checks talk to pods directly</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4</td><td>A Pod sees <strong>its own IP</strong> as others see it</td><td>No surprising address translation inside the pod</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CoreDNS — names instead of IPs</h5>
                    <p>Pods come and go with changing IPs, so apps address each other by <em>name</em>. <strong>CoreDNS</strong> (a Deployment in <code>kube-system</code>, fronted by the <code>kube-dns</code> Service, usually <code>10.96.0.10</code>) resolves Service names to ClusterIPs — e.g. <code>easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local</code>. Every pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points at it. If name resolution breaks cluster-wide, suspect CoreDNS first (full chapter: Ch 19).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ kube-proxy vs CNI — keep them separate when debugging</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>CNI</strong> implements rules 1–4 (pod-to-pod). <strong>kube-proxy</strong> implements the <em>Service</em> abstraction on top (VIP → pod). So: pods can ping each other by IP but a Service is unreachable → <strong>kube-proxy/endpoints</strong> problem; pods can't reach each other by IP at all → <strong>CNI</strong> problem. That one split routes most networking tickets.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.6 Static Pods — an Admin-Only Concept</h3>
                <p>Static pods are managed <strong>directly by the kubelet</strong> on one node — not by the scheduler or API server. They're how kubeadm runs the control plane itself, and they're tested directly on the CKA.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Regular pods</h5><p>Created via the API server (kubectl) → stored in etcd → scheduled by the scheduler → managed by controllers.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📌</div><h5>Static pods</h5><p>Created by the kubelet from YAML in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. The kubelet watches that dir and owns the lifecycle. Drop a file → pod appears; remove it → pod gone.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The control plane IS static pods</h5>
                    <p>On a kubeadm cluster, the apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager, and etcd all run as <strong>static pods</strong> — which is why you configure them by editing YAML in the manifests dir (the kubelet reloads automatically):</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — the control-plane static pods</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml  kube-apiserver.yaml  kube-controller-manager.yaml  kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep controlplane
<span class="comment"># etcd-controlplane                       1/1  Running   ← note the -&lt;nodename&gt; suffix</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-controlplane             1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-controlplane    1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-controlplane             1/1  Running</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Creating a Custom Static Pod</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-easypj.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: static-easypj
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Static-pod facts the exam loves</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>You <strong>cannot</strong> <code>kubectl delete</code> a static pod — the kubelet instantly recreates it. Remove the <em>manifest file</em> instead.</li>
                        <li>The kubelet creates a read-only <strong>"mirror pod"</strong> in the API so you can see it; that's why the name carries a <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix.</li>
                        <li>The watched directory is set by <code>staticPodPath</code> in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>.</li>
                        <li>Static pods (kubelet-managed) ≠ DaemonSets (API-managed) — both put one pod per node, but only DaemonSets are API objects.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>etcd Restore — the Companion Procedure</h4>
                <p>You saw <em>backup</em> in §3.2; restore is the other half (and a classic exam task). Always restore to a <strong>new</strong> data dir, then repoint the etcd static pod at it:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd restore</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) restore the snapshot into a NEW directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># 2) edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml → point the data hostPath</span>
<span class="comment">#    volume from /var/lib/etcd  →  /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># 3) the kubelet sees the manifest change &amp; recreates etcd automatically</span>
<span class="comment"># 4) verify</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Restore gotcha</h5>
                    <p><code>--data-dir</code> must be a <strong>new</strong> path (e.g. <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>), then repoint the manifest. Restoring over the live dir while etcd runs can corrupt it. Full DR drill in Chapter 29.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Component Quick Reference — Ports · Config · Logs</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Port</th><th>Config / manifest</th><th>Logs</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td><code>6443</code></td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td><code>2379/2380</code></td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system etcd-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td><code>10259</code></td><td><code>…/kube-scheduler.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-scheduler-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>controller-manager</strong></td><td><code>10257</code></td><td><code>…/kube-controller-manager.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-controller-manager-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td><code>10250</code></td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td><td><code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-proxy</strong></td><td><code>10256</code></td><td>ConfigMap <code>kube-proxy</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-proxy-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>containerd</strong></td><td>—</td><td><code>/etc/containerd/config.toml</code></td><td><code>journalctl -u containerd</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CoreDNS</strong></td><td><code>53</code></td><td>ConfigMap <code>coredns</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system coredns-*</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key file paths to memorize</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — static-pod manifests (control plane)</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> — all cluster certs (CA, server, client)</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/admin.conf</code> — the admin kubeconfig</li>
                        <li><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> — kubelet config (incl. <code>staticPodPath</code>)</li>
                        <li><code>/var/lib/etcd/</code> — etcd data directory</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/cni/net.d/</code> &amp; <code>/opt/cni/bin/</code> — CNI config &amp; binaries</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 3.7 NODE HEALTH & CP↔NODE COMMS — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.7 Node Health &amp; Control-Plane ↔ Node Communication</h3>
                <p>§3.3 named the node's components; this is how the control plane <em>knows a node is alive</em>, <em>how much it can schedule onto it</em>, and <em>how the two halves talk securely</em>. It's the backbone of "node NotReady" troubleshooting (30% domain).</p>

                <h4>① Node status conditions — the health report</h4>
                <p>Each Node object carries a set of <strong>conditions</strong> (see them with <code>kubectl describe node</code>). <code>Ready=True</code> means healthy &amp; schedulable; the four "pressure" conditions are early-warning flags.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Problem state</th><th>Typical cause</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Ready</code></td><td><code>True</code> = healthy · <code>False</code>/<code>Unknown</code> = problem</td><td>kubelet down, lost heartbeat, runtime/CNI broken</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>MemoryPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = low memory</td><td>node RAM nearly exhausted → starts evicting pods</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>DiskPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = low disk</td><td>full disk / image cache → eviction + image GC</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>PIDPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = too many processes</td><td>fork bomb / runaway workload</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>NetworkUnavailable</code></td><td><code>True</code> = node network not configured</td><td>CNI not installed / route missing</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② Heartbeats, the node Lease &amp; the eviction timeline</h4>
                <p>A node proves it's alive two ways: the kubelet renews a lightweight <strong>Lease</strong> in the <code>kube-node-lease</code> namespace (one per node, ~every 10s — the cheap, frequent heartbeat) <strong>and</strong> posts a fuller <code>.status</code> update whenever something changes (or periodically). When heartbeats stop, the node controller runs a fixed clock:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what happens when a node goes silent</div>
<pre>
   t = 0s     kubelet stops renewing its Lease / status   (node crashed, network cut)
   t ≈ 50s    node controller marks the node  Ready = Unknown   (node-monitor-grace-period)
              └─ adds taint  node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute   (or …/not-ready)
   t ≈ 5min   pods without a matching toleration are EVICTED &amp; rescheduled elsewhere
              └─ eviction is rate-limited so a big outage doesn't stampede the cluster
   kubectl get lease -n kube-node-lease     ← see each node's heartbeat
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0">🔑 This is why "I rebooted a node and my pods vanished a few minutes later" is <em>expected</em> — the ~5-minute eviction timer fired. A pod's <code>tolerations</code> for <code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable</code> / <code>not-ready</code> (with <code>tolerationSeconds</code>) control how quickly it's moved.</p></div>

                <h4>③ Capacity vs Allocatable — what the scheduler actually sees</h4>
                <p>A node's <strong>Capacity</strong> is its total hardware; <strong>Allocatable</strong> is what's left for your pods after reservations for the kubelet, the OS, and an eviction buffer. The scheduler only ever fills <em>Allocatable</em> — which is why a pod can sit <code>Pending</code> even though the node "looks" half-empty.</p>
                <pre class="diagram-box">
   NODE CAPACITY   (e.g. 4 CPU / 16Gi)
   ├─ kube-reserved       kubelet, container runtime
   ├─ system-reserved     OS, sshd, systemd
   ├─ eviction-threshold  safety buffer (e.g. memory.available &lt; 100Mi)
   └─ ALLOCATABLE   ◀── the only slice the scheduler can place pods into
   kubectl describe node node-1     → shows Capacity and Allocatable side by side
</pre>

                <h4>④ Control-plane ↔ node communication — the trust asymmetry</h4>
                <p>Traffic from a node <em>into</em> the control plane is secured by default; traffic from the control plane <em>out</em> to nodes is the leg you must harden.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Path</th><th>Security</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Node → control plane</strong></td><td>kubelet / kube-proxy → apiserver <code>:6443</code></td><td>✅ TLS + auth + RBAC by default — the one hub everything dials</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CP → kubelet</strong></td><td>apiserver → kubelet <code>:10250</code> (logs, <code>exec</code>, <code>port-forward</code>, metrics)</td><td>⚠️ HTTPS, but the apiserver won't verify the kubelet's cert unless you set <code>--kubelet-certificate-authority</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CP → nodes / pods / services</strong></td><td>apiserver → cluster IPs</td><td>⚠️ plain HTTP by default — unsafe over untrusted networks; use the <strong>Konnectivity</strong> service for a secured tunnel</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><p class="mb-0">💡 Mental model: <strong>everything dials the apiserver</strong> (secure, single front door); the apiserver dialing back out to nodes is the weaker leg — <strong>Konnectivity</strong> (the konnectivity-agent/server pair) wraps it in a managed tunnel for production.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how the control plane stays consistent &amp; highly available</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The components in §3.2 are simple to name; the <em>machinery</em> that makes several copies of them cooperate without conflict is where real understanding lives. Four mechanisms do the heavy lifting.</p>

                    <h5>① Leader election via Lease — how scheduler &amp; controller-manager run HA</h5>
                    <p>You can run 3 schedulers and 3 controller-managers for high availability, but if all of them acted at once they'd fight (three controllers each creating the "missing" pod). So they're <strong>active-passive</strong>: only one is the leader at a time, chosen by a <strong>Lease</strong> object the others watch.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   3 kube-scheduler replicas         🗄️ a Lease object in kube-system
   ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ sched-1  │ │ sched-2  │ │ sched-3  │ ────▶ │ Lease "kube-scheduler"    │
   │ LEADER ✅ │ │ standby  │ │ standby  │      │ holder: sched-1           │
   └────┬─────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘      │ renewTime: …(every ~2s)   │
        │ only the leader schedules            └──────────────────────────┘
        └─ stops renewing (crash) → a standby grabs the Lease → becomes leader
   kubectl get lease -n kube-system   ← see who holds it
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why it matters:</strong> "I have 3 control-plane nodes but only one scheduler seems to work" is <em>correct</em> — the other two are hot standbys. Check <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code> to see the current holder.</div>

                    <h5>② The Binding subresource — how the scheduler actually places a pod</h5>
                    <p>The scheduler doesn't "move" a pod. It computes the best node, then makes a tiny API call — a <strong>Binding</strong> — that sets the pod's <code>nodeName</code>. The kubelet on that node then notices "a pod is assigned to me" and runs it. Scheduling is just one field being written.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   scheduler picks node2  ──POST /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods/easypj-web-x/binding──▶ 📡 apiserver
   apiserver sets pod.spec.nodeName = "node2"  ──▶ 🗄️ etcd
   🤖 kubelet(node2) is watching pods where nodeName == its own → sees it → starts it
   💡 you can hand-schedule by setting nodeName yourself (bypasses the scheduler entirely)
</pre>

                    <h5>③ API Priority &amp; Fairness (APF) — keeping the apiserver alive under load</h5>
                    <p>Because <em>everything</em> goes through the apiserver, a flood of requests (a buggy controller, a stampede of kubelets) could starve it. <strong>APF</strong> classifies incoming requests into priority levels and fair-queues them, so critical traffic (leader election, node heartbeats) isn't drowned out by bulk list calls.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>FlowSchema</td><td>Matches requests (by user/verb/resource) → assigns them to a priority level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>PriorityLevelConfiguration</td><td>Reserves concurrency shares + a fair queue per level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Effect</td><td>A misbehaving client is throttled (HTTP 429) instead of taking down the API for everyone</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>④ The aggregation layer — extending the API itself</h5>
                    <p>Beyond CRDs, you can register a <strong>second API server</strong> behind the main one via an <code>APIService</code>. The apiserver proxies a slice of the API path to it. That's how <code>metrics-server</code> serves <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> (powering <code>kubectl top</code>) — it's not a CRD, it's an aggregated apiserver.</p>
                    <h5>⑤ REST + watch — the API machinery everything is built on</h5>
                    <p>The apiserver is a <strong>RESTful</strong> server: every object is a URL, and the usual HTTP verbs act on it. The magic extra is <strong>WATCH</strong> — a long-lived stream of changes that lets components react instantly instead of polling. That single mechanism is how the scheduler, controllers, and kubelets all stay in sync.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   REST verbs on a resource:
     GET    /apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments/easypj-web   read one
     LIST   /apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments              read all
     POST / PUT / PATCH / DELETE                                     create / replace / modify / remove

   WATCH (the key to reactivity):
     GET …/deployments?watch=true&amp;resourceVersion=4821
        ──▶ keeps the connection OPEN and streams events as they happen:
            ADDED {…}   MODIFIED {…rv:4822}   MODIFIED {…rv:4830}   DELETED {…}
     each event carries the new resourceVersion → a client can resume the stream
     after a disconnect from exactly where it left off (no missed &amp; no duplicate work)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>LIST then WATCH</td><td>Get the full state once, then only the deltas — the pattern every informer uses</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resourceVersion</code></td><td>A cursor: "stream changes since here" + the basis of optimistic concurrency</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>watch cache</td><td>The apiserver serves watches from memory, so thousands of watchers don't hammer etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl get -w</code></td><td>Is literally a watch stream you can see in your terminal</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this is the whole design:</strong> components never call <em>each other</em> — they all watch the apiserver and react to changes. That's the loose coupling that makes Kubernetes resilient: a component that was briefly down just re-LISTs and resumes watching, catching up to current state automatically.</div>

                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Tie it together:</strong> the apiserver isn't just a CRUD endpoint — it's a REST + watch hub (the API machinery), a consistency &amp; fairness hub (APF), a placement coordinator (Binding), an HA arbiter (Leases), and an extensible surface (aggregation). Master these and "the control plane" stops being a black box.</div>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> reads can be served from the apiserver's <em>watch cache</em> (fast, slightly behind) or forced consistent; every object carries a <code>resourceVersion</code> for optimistic concurrency (Ch 1 UTH); controllers are built on <strong>informers</strong> (shared list-watch + cache) feeding <strong>workqueues</strong> with rate-limiting and retries; and the node controller's NotReady (~40s) → eviction (~5min) timers are why a briefly-down node doesn't instantly lose its pods.</p>

                    <h5>⑥ The full pod-creation trace — what happens <em>inside</em> §3.4's 8 steps</h5>
                    <p>§3.4 showed the <em>journey</em>; here is the <em>internal trace</em> — the named admission plugins, the scheduler's filter/score plugins, and the exact <strong>CRI</strong> calls the kubelet makes. This is the depth CKA troubleshooting questions live at.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
kubectl run easypj-web --image=easypj:v1
   │
   ▼  STAGE 1 — apiserver: authN → authZ → admission → etcd
 📡 kube-apiserver
   ├─ a. AuthN      validate the bearer token (who are you?) → user: easypj-admin
   ├─ b. AuthZ      RBAC: may easypj-admin CREATE pods in ns easypj? → yes
   ├─ c. Admission chain (mutate, then validate):
   │       MutatingAdmissionWebhook   → inject sidecars / defaults
   │       LimitRanger                → apply default requests &amp; limits
   │       PodSecurity                → enforce baseline / restricted
   │       ValidatingAdmissionWebhook → reject if a policy is violated
   ├─ d. WRITE      /registry/pods/easypj/easypj-web  → 🗄️ etcd (quorum write)
   └─ e. RETURN     201 Created → "pod/easypj-web created"
   │
   ▼  STAGE 2 — scheduler: watch → filter → score → bind
 📋 kube-scheduler  (watches pods where .spec.nodeName == "")
   ├─ FILTER (predicates) — drop nodes that CAN'T run it:
   │     NodeReady? · CPU/mem fits requests? · nodeSelector/affinity? · taints tolerated? · hostPort free?
   ├─ SCORE  (priorities) — rank the survivors 0–100:
   │     NodeResourcesFit(LeastAllocated) · NodeResourcesBalancedAllocation · ImageLocality · NodeAffinity
   └─ BIND: POST …/pods/easypj-web/binding → sets .spec.nodeName = "node-1" → 🗄️ etcd
   │
   ▼  STAGE 3 — kubelet → CRI → containerd → runc
 🤖 kubelet(node-1)  (watches pods assigned to it)
   └─ calls the CRI (gRPC), in order:
        RunPodSandbox   → create the pause container + network namespace (CNI assigns the IP)
        PullImage       → fetch easypj:v1 layers from the registry
        CreateContainer → set up namespaces + cgroups + overlay rootfs
        StartContainer  → exec the easypj process
      containerd builds the OCI bundle → runc create → runc start
        runc: clone() with namespace flags · write cgroups · pivot_root · exec
   └─ kubelet reports status → Running → Ready (once probes pass) → 🗄️ etcd
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Map a failure to its stage:</strong> hangs at <code>kubectl</code> → Stage 1 (apiserver unreachable); stuck <code>Pending</code> → Stage 2 (nothing survived filtering — taints / resources / selector); stuck <code>ContainerCreating</code> → Stage 3 <code>RunPodSandbox</code>/<code>PullImage</code> (CNI, or image/registry); <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> → Stage 3 <code>StartContainer</code> (the app itself — read <code>kubectl logs</code>).</div>

                    <h5>⑦ Static pods — the bootstrap paradox solved</h5>
                    <p>The control plane (apiserver, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager) runs <em>as pods</em> — which is a chicken-and-egg problem: how do you run pods before the apiserver that schedules them even exists? The answer is the <strong>static pod</strong> — the kubelet reads manifests straight from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and runs them itself, with no apiserver and no scheduler involved.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   boot order on a control-plane node
   ① kubelet starts             (a systemd service — it does NOT need the API)
   ② reads /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
   ③ runs the apiserver static pod    (CRI → containerd → runc)
   ④ the apiserver is now up
   ⑤ kubelet registers a read-only MIRROR pod for it in the API
        → visible as kube-apiserver-&lt;nodename&gt;   (that's where the suffix comes from)
   ⑥ kubelet runs the remaining manifests: etcd, scheduler, controller-manager
   ⑦ control plane operational → normal, API-driven scheduling begins
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔁 Restarting a static pod (you can't <code>kubectl delete</code> it):</strong> move the manifest out and back —
                    <code>sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/ &amp;&amp; sleep 20 &amp;&amp; sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet sees the file vanish (stops the pod), then reappear (recreates it). Editing the manifest in place also triggers a restart.</div>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA insight:</strong> if you SSH to a control-plane node and <code>kubectl</code> won't respond, the apiserver static pod may be down — there's no API to ask. Check it directly: <code>sudo crictl ps | grep kube-apiserver</code> (is the container running?) and <code>sudo ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (is the manifest present?). A missing or corrupt manifest takes the entire control plane down.</div>

                    <h5>⑧ A few more control-plane internals worth knowing</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Lease fields</strong></td><td>a leader-election Lease carries <code>holderIdentity</code> (who holds it), <code>leaseDurationSeconds</code> (TTL — ~15s for scheduler/controller-manager) and <code>renewTime</code> (the leader rewrites it every couple of seconds). If a renewal is missed past the TTL, a standby claims the Lease — so failover takes up to about one lease duration.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>APF: 429 vs 503</strong></td><td><strong>429 Too Many Requests</strong> = API Priority &amp; Fairness fair-queued you and the queue/wait was exceeded → <em>back off &amp; retry</em>. <strong>503</strong> = a priority level with no queueing, or the server is genuinely overloaded → <em>investigate</em>. <code>exempt</code> flows (e.g. <code>system:masters</code>, health checks) bypass APF so a misconfig can't lock you out of the API.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Admission <code>failurePolicy</code></strong></td><td>if a webhook errors or times out: <code>Fail</code> (default) rejects the request — safe for policy/security; <code>Ignore</code> lets it through — for optional webhooks. <strong>Mutating runs before validating</strong> by design, so validators judge the <em>final</em> object (after sidecars/defaults are injected).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Control plane decides, worker nodes run</strong> — sort every problem into "decision" vs "run" and you've found the half to look in.</li>
                    <li><strong>Only the apiserver talks to etcd</strong> — and everything else talks only to the apiserver (the single choke point).</li>
                    <li><strong>The kubelet is a systemd service, not a pod</strong> — debug it with <code>systemctl</code> / <code>journalctl</code>, never <code>kubectl</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>The control plane runs AS static pods</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — edit the YAML, the kubelet reloads.</li>
                    <li><strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong> are the three plug points (runtime · network · storage).</li>
                    <li><strong>Match symptom → component:</strong> NotReady=kubelet · Pending=scheduler · lost state=etcd · kubectl dead=apiserver.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubelet ≠ pod.</strong> It's systemd — <code>kubectl get pods</code> won't show it; <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> will.</li>
                    <li><strong>You can't <code>kubectl delete</code> a static pod</strong> — the kubelet recreates it. Remove the manifest file from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>apiserver is the single choke point.</strong> If it's down, <code>kubectl</code> is useless — switch to <code>crictl</code> / <code>journalctl</code> on the node.</li>
                    <li><strong>Static pod vs DaemonSet.</strong> Both = one pod per node, but static = kubelet-managed (mirror pod, <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix), DaemonSet = API object.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scheduler vs controller-manager.</strong> Scheduler only <em>places</em> pods; the controller-manager handles replication, nodes, endpoints. Don't conflate.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>apiserver down?</strong> Debug from the node: <code>crictl ps -a</code>, <code>crictl logs &lt;apiserver-id&gt;</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pods Pending?</strong> Suspect the scheduler — <code>kubectl get pods -A | grep Pending</code> then check the scheduler pod/logs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Editing a control-plane component?</strong> Just edit its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet restarts it for you.</li>
                    <li><strong>Find the HA leader:</strong> <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 3)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">inspect the cluster's anatomy</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -n kube-system            <span class="comment"># control-plane + CNI + CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses             <span class="comment"># cs — control-plane health (older clusters)</span>
kubectl get lease -n kube-system          <span class="comment"># who holds leader election</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/             <span class="comment"># the static-pod manifests</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide                 <span class="comment"># node roles, versions, IPs</span>

<span class="comment"># node-level (when the apiserver is down)</span>
crictl ps -a                              <span class="comment"># containers via the CRI</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
journalctl -u kubelet -f                  <span class="comment"># live kubelet logs</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Map the Control Plane</h5>
                <p>On a control-plane node — predict each output before running it:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>List the static-pod manifests.</li>
                    <li>List the control-plane pods and note the <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix.</li>
                    <li>Identify the leader of the scheduler/controller-manager.</li>
                    <li>Tail the apiserver logs.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/              <span class="comment"># etcd · kube-apiserver · kube-scheduler · kube-controller-manager</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep controlplane   <span class="comment"># all 4 Running, -controlplane suffix (mirror pods)</span>
kubectl get lease -n kube-system kube-scheduler kube-controller-manager   <span class="comment"># HOLDER column = leader</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-controlplane | tail -20        <span class="comment"># apiserver logs</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>What you proved:</strong> the control plane is just static pods the kubelet runs; their mirror pods carry the node-name suffix; HA components elect one leader via a Lease; and you can read any control-plane component's logs with <code>kubectl logs</code> (or <code>crictl logs</code> if the apiserver itself is down).</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A — CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 3 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the architecture — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which component is the ONLY one that reads/writes etcd?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>kube-apiserver</strong>. Every other component (scheduler, controller-manager, kubelet) reaches state <em>through</em> the apiserver, never etcd directly.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This star topology is why an etcd backup = a cluster backup, and why securing/operating the apiserver is so critical.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node shows <code>NotReady</code>. Which agent do you check first, and how?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>kubelet</strong> (it's a systemd service, not a pod), then the runtime and CNI:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node NotReady — first checks (on the node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">systemctl status kubelet        <span class="comment"># is the agent even up?</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -f        <span class="comment"># why did it fail?</span>
systemctl status containerd     <span class="comment"># runtime alive?</span>
ls /etc/cni/net.d/              <span class="comment"># CNI configured?</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>NotReady = the node can't report healthy. Usual causes: kubelet down, swap on, expired cert, missing CNI, cgroup-driver mismatch.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Trace what happens after <code>kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Each actor reconciles its own slice — no central script:</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">what kubectl apply sets in motion</div>
<pre>
 kubectl apply ─▶ 📡 apiserver (authN → authZ → admission) ─▶ 🗄️ etcd   (persist desired state)
                       │
                       ├─ 🔄 controller-manager:  Deployment ─▶ ReplicaSet ─▶ Pods
                       └─ 📋 scheduler:  filter → score → BIND (sets nodeName)
                              ▼
                    🤖 kubelet:  pull image → start container → 🌐 CNI assigns the pod IP
                              ▼
                    🔀 kube-proxy programs Service routing ─────▶ Running ✅
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>No central script — each actor reconciles its own slice. Knowing the order lets you map any symptom to a step (§3.4).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The scheduler is down. What symptom appears, and on which objects?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>New pods stay <code>Pending</code></strong> (no node gets assigned). Existing pods keep running. Spot it: <code>kubectl get pods -A | grep Pending</code>, then check the scheduler pod/logs.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The scheduler only sets <code>nodeName</code>; with it down, pods are created but never bound.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a static pod on a node, then delete it. Which files/commands?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The kubelet watches a directory; creating/deleting the <em>file</em> creates/deletes the pod:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">static pod — create &amp; delete (on the node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># create: drop a manifest where the kubelet watches</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-easypj.yaml   <span class="comment"># path = staticPodPath in /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># delete: remove the FILE — `kubectl delete` just gets recreated</span>
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-easypj.yaml</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Static pods are owned by the kubelet, not the API; the API only shows a read-only mirror pod.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the network layers and what routes each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Three layers, each programmed by a different actor (all riding on the node/infra network):</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Addresses</th><th>Who programs it</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>Pod network</strong></td><td>real pod IPs (flat, no NAT)</td><td>the <strong>CNI</strong> plugin</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Service network</strong></td><td>stable virtual IPs (ClusterIP)</td><td>apiserver assigns the IP · EndpointSlice controller tracks Ready pods · <strong>kube-proxy</strong> programs the routing</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>External</strong></td><td>NodePort · LoadBalancer · Ingress/Gateway</td><td>the cluster edge</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Pods can fail to reach each other (CNI) independently of Services failing (kube-proxy) — keep the layers separate when debugging.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which ports does etcd listen on, and for what?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Two ports, two jobs:</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>2379</code></td><td>client traffic — the apiserver (and <code>etcdctl</code>) connect here</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>2380</code></td><td>peer traffic — etcd member ↔ member for Raft; only matters in multi-member HA</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>etcdctl uses <code>--endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379</code>; 2380 only matters in multi-member HA.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is the apiserver the cluster's single choke point?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Every read/write of state, every component's watch, and every kubectl call flows through it — and it's the only thing that touches etcd. Down apiserver → no scheduling, no reconciliation, no kubectl (though running pods continue).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>That's also why it gets HA replicas behind a load balancer and APF to stay alive under load (UTH).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain a controller's reconcile loop using the ReplicaSet example.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>OBSERVE → COMPARE → ACT, forever — a deleted pod is just the next gap to close:</p>
                        <pre class="diagram-box">
        ┌──────────── reconcile loop (runs forever) ───────────┐
        ▼                                                      │
   OBSERVE actual ──▶ COMPARE to desired ──▶ ACT to close gap ─┘
   (2 pods now)        (spec replicas: 3)     (create 1 pod)
   delete a pod → next pass sees 2 ≠ 3 again → recreates it
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Level-triggered: it reacts to current state, not events, so it self-heals even if an event is missed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a "mirror pod" and why does its name end in <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>When the kubelet runs a static pod, it creates a read-only <strong>mirror pod</strong> in the API so you can <code>kubectl get</code> it. The kubelet appends its node name (e.g. <code>etcd-controlplane</code>) to show which node owns it — and to keep names unique across nodes.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>You can view but not control a mirror pod via the API; the real lifecycle lives with the kubelet and the manifest file.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 3 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken state or a "trace it" question, with the real answer.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You SSH to the control plane node and find that <code>kubectl get nodes</code> returns <em>"The connection to the server was refused"</em>. The kubelet is running. What is the most likely problem and how do you diagnose it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The <strong>kube-apiserver</strong> is down. Since it's a static pod, diagnose as follows:</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if the api server container is running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if the API server container is running</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep kube-apiserver

<span class="comment"># 2. If not running, check the static pod manifest</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Check kubelet logs for manifest errors</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet | grep -i "error\|apiserver" | tail -20

<span class="comment"># 4. Common fixes:</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix YAML syntax errors in the manifest</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix wrong certificate paths (--tls-cert-file, --tls-private-key-file)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix wrong etcd endpoints (--etcd-servers)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix port conflicts (--secure-port)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is a <strong>classic CKA troubleshooting scenario</strong>. The kube-apiserver runs as a static pod managed by the kubelet. If the manifest file in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> has errors, the kubelet cannot start the pod. Key diagnostic path: <code>crictl ps</code> → <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> → check manifest → fix → kubelet automatically restarts the static pod. No <code>kubectl apply</code> needed — static pods are managed by the kubelet directly from filesystem watching.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>static pods</strong>? Where are they defined, which component manages them, and how are they different from regular pods created via the API server?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>Static pods</strong> are pods managed directly by the <strong>kubelet</strong> on a specific node, without going through the API server.</p>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th>Static Pods</th>
                                            <th>Regular Pods</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Managed by</td>
                                            <td>kubelet (filesystem watcher)</td>
                                            <td>API server → scheduler → kubelet</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Defined in</td>
                                            <td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></td>
                                            <td>etcd (via kubectl/API)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Visible in API</td>
                                            <td>Yes, as a <em>mirror pod</em> (read-only)</td>
                                            <td>Yes, fully managed</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Can be deleted via kubectl</td>
                                            <td>No — delete the file to remove</td>
                                            <td>Yes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods are how the control plane bootstraps itself — <code>kube-apiserver</code>, <code>etcd</code>, <code>kube-scheduler</code>, and <code>kube-controller-manager</code> all run as static pods. The kubelet watches the <code>staticPodPath</code> (configured in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>) and automatically creates/restarts pods from YAML files found there. On the CKA, you may be asked to create a static pod: simply place a valid pod manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and the kubelet will start it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the <strong>kube-scheduler's</strong> decision process. When a new pod is created without a <code>nodeName</code>, what steps does the scheduler take to place it on a node?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The scheduler follows a <strong>two-phase process</strong>:</p>
                                <p><strong>Phase 1: Filtering</strong> — Eliminates nodes that can't run the pod:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Insufficient CPU/memory resources</li>
                                    <li>Taints that the pod doesn't tolerate</li>
                                    <li>Node affinity rules not satisfied</li>
                                    <li>PodAntiAffinity constraints</li>
                                    <li>Unschedulable nodes (<code>cordon</code>ed)</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p><strong>Phase 2: Scoring</strong> — Ranks remaining nodes by preference:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Resource balance (spread workloads evenly)</li>
                                    <li>Node affinity preferences (soft rules)</li>
                                    <li>Pod topology spread constraints</li>
                                    <li>Image locality (node already has the image cached)</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p>The highest-scoring node wins. The scheduler then sets <code>spec.nodeName</code> via the API server.</p>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The CKA tests scheduling concepts heavily (15% of the exam). Key scenarios: (1) Pod stuck in <code>Pending</code> — all nodes filtered out (check taints, resources, node selectors). (2) Pod scheduled to wrong node — scoring preferences not configured correctly. (3) Manually bypass the scheduler by setting <code>spec.nodeName</code> directly in the pod spec. If a pod is Pending, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> events will show <em>"0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had taint..."</em> — this tells you exactly which filter failed.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The <code>kube-controller-manager</code> runs many controllers. Name at least 5 built-in controllers and describe what each one is responsible for.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Controller</th>
                                            <th>Responsibility</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Node Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Monitors node heartbeats (every 10s), marks NotReady after 40s, evicts pods after 5min</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>ReplicaSet Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Ensures the correct number of pod replicas are running at all times</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Deployment Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Manages ReplicaSets for rolling updates, rollbacks, and scaling</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Endpoint Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Populates Endpoints objects (maps Services to pod IPs)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>ServiceAccount Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Creates default ServiceAccount + token for every new namespace</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Job Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Tracks Job completions and creates new pods until the desired completion count is reached</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Namespace Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Cleans up resources when a namespace is deleted (cascading deletion)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>All these controllers run as goroutines inside a single <code>kube-controller-manager</code> binary/pod. Each controller follows the same <strong>Observe → Diff → Act</strong> pattern. On the CKA exam, understanding controllers helps diagnose issues: if replicas aren't being created, the controller-manager might be down. If nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> for too long without pod eviction, check the node controller's <code>--pod-eviction-timeout</code> flag.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How does the <code>kubelet</code> know which static pod manifests to watch? What configuration file controls the <code>staticPodPath</code>, and how would you find it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find kubelet's config file</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Find kubelet's config file</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -- --config
<span class="comment"># Typically: --config=/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the staticPodPath in kubelet config</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># Output: staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># List static pod manifests</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml  kube-apiserver.yaml  kube-controller-manager.yaml  kube-scheduler.yaml</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA exam, you may need to create a static pod on a specific node. The steps are: (1) SSH to the target node, (2) Find the <code>staticPodPath</code> from the kubelet config, (3) Place your pod YAML file in that directory, (4) The kubelet automatically detects and creates the pod. To find the kubelet config: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> shows the systemd unit file, which contains the <code>--config</code> flag pointing to the config file. Default path is almost always <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the role of <strong>CoreDNS</strong> in a Kubernetes cluster? If DNS resolution fails inside a pod, what troubleshooting steps would you take?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>CoreDNS</strong> provides cluster-internal DNS resolution. It runs as a Deployment in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace and resolves:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li><code>service-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code> → ClusterIP</li>
                                    <li><code>pod-ip-dashed.namespace.pod.cluster.local</code> → Pod IP</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p><strong>Troubleshooting DNS failure:</strong></p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if coredns pods are running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if CoreDNS pods are running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># 2. Check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># 3. Verify the kube-dns Service exists</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># 4. Test DNS from inside a pod</span>
kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox --rm -it --restart=Never \
  -- nslookup kubernetes.default

<span class="comment"># 5. Check pod's /etc/resolv.conf</span>
kubectl exec easypj-web -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># Should point to kube-dns ClusterIP (usually 10.96.0.10)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>DNS is critical infrastructure in Kubernetes. Every pod gets a <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> pointing to the CoreDNS service's ClusterIP. If DNS fails, almost everything breaks — service discovery, external API calls, etc. Common causes: (1) CoreDNS pods not running, (2) kube-dns Service ClusterIP changed, (3) Network policy blocking DNS traffic on port 53, (4) CoreDNS ConfigMap misconfigured, (5) kube-proxy not programming iptables rules for the kube-dns service. This is a high-priority CKA troubleshooting topic.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the difference between the <code>kube-apiserver</code>'s <strong>authentication</strong>, <strong>authorization</strong>, and <strong>admission control</strong> stages. What happens at each stage when a request arrives?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>Every API request passes through three stages in order:</p>
                                <ol>
                                    <li><strong>Authentication</strong> — <em>"Who are you?"</em> — Verifies identity via client certificates, bearer tokens, or service account tokens. If authentication fails → <code>401 Unauthorized</code></li>
                                    <li><strong>Authorization</strong> — <em>"Are you allowed to do this?"</em> — Checks RBAC rules (Role/ClusterRole + RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding). If denied → <code>403 Forbidden</code></li>
                                    <li><strong>Admission Control</strong> — <em>"Should we modify or reject this?"</em> — Webhooks and built-in plugins that can mutate (change defaults) or validate (reject invalid) requests. Examples: <code>LimitRanger</code> (set default resource limits), <code>NamespaceLifecycle</code> (reject requests to terminating namespaces)</li>
                                </ol>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash">Request → [Authentication] → [Authorization] → [Admission] → etcd
              401?                  403?              Reject/Mutate</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding this pipeline is critical for CKA RBAC and security questions. When you get a "forbidden" error, it's authorization (RBAC). When you get "unauthorized", it's authentication (bad/expired cert or token). Admission controllers can silently modify your objects — for example, <code>DefaultStorageClass</code> adds a storage class to PVCs that don't specify one. The CKA tests RBAC extensively (creating Roles, ClusterRoles, Bindings). Remember: Roles are namespace-scoped, ClusterRoles are cluster-wide.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">A Kubernetes node's kubelet is configured with <code>--pod-cidr=10.244.1.0/24</code>. What does this mean, and how does the CNI plugin use this information to assign IP addresses to pods?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The <code>--pod-cidr</code> defines the <strong>subnet of IP addresses</strong> allocated to pods on that specific node. Each node gets a unique pod CIDR from the cluster's overall pod network CIDR (e.g., <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>).</p>
                                <p><strong>How it works:</strong></p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Controller-manager assigns each node a unique <code>/24</code> subnet from the cluster CIDR</li>
                                    <li>The CNI plugin (Flannel, Calico, Cilium) reads the node's pod CIDR</li>
                                    <li>When a pod is created, the CNI assigns an IP from that node's subnet</li>
                                    <li>Node 1: <code>10.244.0.0/24</code> → pods get <code>10.244.0.2</code>, <code>10.244.0.3</code>, etc.</li>
                                    <li>Node 2: <code>10.244.1.0/24</code> → pods get <code>10.244.1.2</code>, <code>10.244.1.3</code>, etc.</li>
                                </ul>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding pod networking is essential for the CKA's "Services & Networking" domain (20%). The pod CIDR is configured during cluster initialization (<code>kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code>). Each node gets a unique subnet. The CNI plugin handles the actual IP assignment and inter-node routing. If pods can't communicate across nodes, check: (1) CNI plugin is installed, (2) pod CIDRs don't overlap with node IPs, (3) firewall rules allow inter-node pod traffic.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code> and see that <code>scheduler</code> shows <code>Unhealthy</code>. What could cause this, and how would you fix it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if the scheduler pod is running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if the scheduler pod is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep scheduler

<span class="comment"># 2. If not running, check the static pod manifest</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Check kubelet logs for scheduler errors</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet | grep scheduler

<span class="comment"># 4. Check scheduler logs directly</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-scheduler-controlplane

<span class="comment"># 5. Common issues:</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Wrong kubeconfig path (--kubeconfig flag)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Certificate expired or wrong path</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Port conflict (--secure-port)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - YAML syntax error in manifest file</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><strong>Note:</strong> <code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code> (or <code>cs</code>) is <strong>deprecated</strong> since v1.19, but may still appear on the CKA. It checks health endpoints of control plane components. If the scheduler is unhealthy: (1) The scheduler static pod may have crashed — check its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>, (2) The scheduler's health endpoint may be unreachable. Symptoms of a dead scheduler: new pods stay in <code>Pending</code> state because nothing assigns them to nodes. Existing pods continue running — only new scheduling decisions are blocked.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Draw or describe the complete communication flow between all Kubernetes components when a user runs <code>kubectl delete pod easypj-web</code>. Which components are involved and what happens at each step?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>Pod deletion flow:</strong></p>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl delete pod easypj-web
        │
        ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│  kube-apiserver  │ ← Receives DELETE request
│  1. Authenticate │
│  2. Authorize    │
│  3. Set deletion │
│     timestamp    │
└────────┬────────┘
         │ Updates etcd: pod.deletionTimestamp = now
         │ pod.deletionGracePeriodSeconds = 30 (default)
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│    kubelet       │ ← Watches API, sees deletionTimestamp
│  1. Send SIGTERM │
│  2. Wait 30s     │    to containers
│  3. Send SIGKILL │    (if still running)
│  4. Report back  │
└────────┬────────┘
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│  kube-apiserver  │ ← kubelet confirms termination
│  Remove from     │
│  etcd            │
└─────────────────┘
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│ Endpoint ctrl    │ ← Removes pod IP from Service endpoints
└─────────────────┘</code></pre>
</div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Key points for the CKA: (1) Deletion is <strong>graceful</strong> by default — containers get SIGTERM and 30 seconds to shut down. (2) The <code>--grace-period=0 --force</code> flags skip the grace period (useful for stuck pods). (3) The Endpoint controller removes the pod from Service endpoints <em>concurrently</em> with the grace period, so in-flight requests may still reach a terminating pod. (4) <code>preStop</code> hooks run before SIGTERM — use them for graceful shutdown logic. Understanding pod lifecycle is critical for troubleshooting stuck deletions and graceful application shutdown.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 3 Visual Summary — the whole cluster, annotated</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────── ☸️ CLUSTER ───────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🧠 CONTROL PLANE (decides)        every request &amp; watch flows through apiserver │
   │   📡 apiserver :6443  ── the ONLY door, the ONLY etcd client                    │
   │        │  authN → authZ(RBAC) → admission                                       │
   │        ├── 🗄️ etcd :2379/2380   ALL state (Raft quorum)                          │
   │        ├── 📋 scheduler          filter → score → BIND (sets nodeName)           │
   │        └── 🔄 controller-mgr     reconcile loops (node·RS·endpoint·SA)           │
   │  ───────────────────────────── apiserver ⇅ nodes (watch) ──────────────────────│
   │ 💪 WORKER NODE                                                                   │
   │   🤖 kubelet (systemd, not a pod) · 🌐 kube-proxy (Service routing) · 🐋 runtime  │
   │   🌐 CNI gives each 📦 pod a flat IP   ·   💾 CSI attaches volumes                │
   │   ┌────────────┐ ┌────────────┐                                                 │
   │   │📦 easypj-web│ │🐘 postgres  │   the control plane itself = static pods in     │
   │   │  :8000      │ │  :5432      │   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ (kubelet-run)      │
   │   └────────────┘ └────────────┘                                                 │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Decide (control plane) vs Run (nodes) · only apiserver→etcd · symptom→component = the exam.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> A cluster splits cleanly into two halves. The <strong>control plane decides</strong>; the <strong>worker nodes run</strong>. The <strong>apiserver</strong> (:6443) is the only way in and the <em>only</em> component that talks to <strong>etcd</strong> — every request first passes authentication → authorization (RBAC) → admission. etcd (:2379) holds <em>all</em> cluster state, kept consistent by Raft quorum.</p>
                    <p>Behind the apiserver, the <strong>scheduler</strong> filters and scores nodes then <em>binds</em> each pod (sets <code>nodeName</code>), and the <strong>controller-manager</strong> runs the reconcile loops (node, ReplicaSet, endpoint, service-account…). On each worker node, the <strong>kubelet</strong> runs the containers (it's a systemd service, not a pod), <strong>kube-proxy</strong> programs Service routing, the <strong>runtime</strong> executes containers, <strong>CNI</strong> gives each pod a flat IP, and <strong>CSI</strong> attaches storage. The control plane itself runs as <em>static pods</em> the kubelet starts from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> hold the "decide vs run" split and "only the apiserver touches etcd" in your head, and troubleshooting becomes a lookup — match the <em>symptom</em> to the responsible <em>component</em>. That mapping is the heart of the exam's Architecture and Troubleshooting domains.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <!-- ::CH3-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 3 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 4 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch4">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 4 · Core Kubernetes Objects — The Admin's Toolkit</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">These six objects are the <strong>nouns</strong> of every exam task and every easypj manifest: <strong>Pod, ReplicaSet, Deployment, Service, Namespace</strong>, and the <strong>labels</strong> that wire them together. Chapter 3 showed you the machines; this chapter shows you the things you actually create on them. Master how they nest and reference each other and you can read any manifest at a glance.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 Core Objects</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Workloads 15% · Services 20%</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐍 easypj / jobpost</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Pods</strong> — the smallest unit; single vs multi-container (sidecar)</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>ReplicaSets</strong> — self-healing by keeping a desired pod count</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Deployments</strong> — rolling updates, rollback, the ownership chain</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Services</strong> — stable networking for ephemeral pods (4 types)</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Namespaces</strong> — what they isolate, and what they don't</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Labels · Selectors · Annotations</strong> — the glue that connects everything</li>
                    <li>✅ The full <strong>object relationship map</strong> — the one picture to memorize</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Services &amp; Networking <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🧩 Object mastery = exam speed</h5>
                <p>Almost every CKA task is "create object X, wire it to Y, fix why Z isn't working." You'll create Deployments, expose them with Services, and chase down empty endpoints. Learn each object <strong>imperatively</strong> (fast <code>kubectl create … $do</code>) <em>and</em> <strong>declaratively</strong> (the YAML), and watch the <strong>labels</strong> — they're where most wiring bugs hide.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <h4>🧭 Section Map — the Chapter 4 Learning Arc</h4>
            <p>We climb the workload ladder (Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment), add stable networking (Service) and isolation (Namespace), learn the glue (labels), then assemble everything into one relationship map.</p>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Chapter 4 Roadmap — the objects, and how they connect</div>
<pre>
   4.1 📦 POD ──wrapped by──▶ 4.2 🎛️ REPLICASET ──wrapped by──▶ 4.3 🚀 DEPLOYMENT
   smallest unit             keeps N alive (self-heal)         rolling updates + rollback
        │                                                              │
        ▼                                                              ▼
   4.4 🔌 SERVICE ──────▶ 4.5 🏷️ NAMESPACE ──────▶ 4.6 🔖 LABELS / SELECTORS
   stable IP + DNS         virtual cluster (isolation)         the glue that wires it all
        │
        ▼
   4.7 🗺️ THE RELATIONSHIP MAP — Deployment→RS→Pods · Service finds Pods by label ·
        ConfigMap/Secret feed env · Ingress→Service · PVC→PV→StorageClass · RBAC governs
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ✅ READY — read any easypj manifest and say exactly what each object does &amp; how it links.
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Object</th><th>One-line takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-1"><strong>4.1</strong> Pods</a></td><td>📦 Pod</td><td>1+ containers sharing one IP &amp; storage; ephemeral — replaced, never repaired.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-2"><strong>4.2</strong> ReplicaSets</a></td><td>🎛️ ReplicaSet</td><td>Keeps a desired count alive via a label selector (self-healing).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-3"><strong>4.3</strong> Deployments</a></td><td>🚀 Deployment</td><td>Manages ReplicaSets; adds rolling updates &amp; rollback.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-4"><strong>4.4</strong> Services</a></td><td>🔌 Service</td><td>Stable VIP + DNS load-balancing to pods by selector.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-5"><strong>4.5</strong> Namespaces</a></td><td>🏷️ Namespace</td><td>Virtual partitions of one cluster; don't isolate nodes/PVs.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-6"><strong>4.6</strong> Labels</a></td><td>🔖 Labels</td><td>Key/value tags that select &amp; wire objects; annotations don't select.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-7"><strong>4.7</strong> Relationship map</a></td><td>🗺️ All</td><td>How every object connects — the picture to memorize.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-8"><strong>4.8</strong> Pod lifecycle</a></td><td>⏱️ Pod states</td><td>Phases, container states, restartPolicy, conditions, termination, probes.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-9"><strong>4.9</strong> Rollouts &amp; scope</a></td><td>🎚️ Details</td><td>maxSurge/maxUnavailable 25%, port mapping, label grammar, namespaced vs cluster.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.1 ─────────── -->

            <section class="section-block" id="s4-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.1 Pods — The Smallest Unit</h3>
                <p>A Pod is the <strong>smallest deployable unit</strong> — one or more containers that share a network namespace (one IP, reachable over <code>localhost</code>) and storage volumes. You rarely <em>create</em> pods directly (you use Deployments), but you <em>debug</em> them constantly.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod anatomy — single vs multi-container (sidecar)</div>
<pre>
   SINGLE-CONTAINER POD (most common)        MULTI-CONTAINER POD (sidecar pattern)
   ┌──────────────────────────┐             ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 📦 POD   IP 10.244.1.5     │             │ 📦 POD   IP 10.244.1.8  (shared net)   │
   │  ┌────────────────────┐   │             │  ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐    │
   │  │ 🐍 easypj-web        │   │             │  │ 🐍 easypj      │ │ 🌐 nginx       │    │
   │  │ Django gunicorn:8000│   │             │  │ Django :8000 │ │ static :80   │    │
   │  └────────────────────┘   │             │  └──────┬───────┘ └──────┬───────┘    │
   │                            │             │         │   localhost     │            │
   │                            │             │         └────────┬────────┘            │
   │                            │             │              ┌───▼────┐ shared 💾       │
   │                            │             │              │ volume │ (emptyDir)      │
   │                            │             │              └────────┘                │
   └──────────────────────────┘             └──────────────────────────────────────┘
   one container = the norm                   localhost:8000 = Django · localhost:80 = nginx
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">💡</div><h5>Key pod facts</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Every container in a pod shares the <strong>same IP</strong></li>
                            <li>They talk to each other over <code>localhost</code></li>
                            <li>Pods are <strong>ephemeral</strong> — replaced, never repaired</li>
                            <li>Never rely on a pod's name or IP — both change</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5>Essential pod commands</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- sh</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-pod.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-pod
  labels:
    app: easypj
    tier: web                <span class="comment"># labels = how other objects find this pod</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000
    resources:
      requests:
        memory: "128Mi"        <span class="comment"># scheduling floor (guaranteed)</span>
        cpu: "250m"
      limits:
        memory: "256Mi"        <span class="comment"># hard ceiling (OOMKilled if exceeded)</span>
        cpu: "500m"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why "ephemeral" is the whole point</h5>
                    <p>A bare Pod is a <em>pet</em>: delete it and it's gone for good — nothing recreates it, and its IP vanishes. That's exactly why you wrap pods in controllers (next) and why apps reach each other through <strong>Services</strong> (§4.4), never by pod IP. Treat pods as disposable cattle and Kubernetes' design suddenly makes sense.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Init Containers, Sidecars &amp; the Pause Container</h4>
                <p>A pod can hold more than one container, and they don't all behave the same way. There are three roles worth knowing — and one hidden container you never write but always have:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>When it runs</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>init container</strong></td><td>BEFORE app containers, <em>to completion</em>, in order — the next won't start until it exits 0</td><td>wait for <code>easypj-db</code> to accept connections, or run <code>manage.py migrate</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>app container</strong></td><td>The long-running main process(es)</td><td><code>gunicorn easypj.wsgi</code> on :8000</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>sidecar</strong></td><td>ALONGSIDE the app for its whole life (a special init container with <code>restartPolicy: Always</code>)</td><td>an nginx static-file server, or a log shipper</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>🔒 pause</strong></td><td>The hidden "sandbox" — created first, <em>holds</em> the pod's network + IPC namespaces</td><td>auto-created; app/sidecar containers join its namespaces</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod startup order — init runs to completion, then the rest share the sandbox</div>
<pre>
   pod created
      │
      ▼  🔒 pause container starts → reserves the pod IP + network namespace
      ▼  ⏳ initContainer "wait-for-db"   runs → exits 0
      ▼  ⏳ initContainer "migrate"       runs → exits 0   (ordered, must succeed)
      ▼  ▶️ app: 🐍 easypj  +  sidecar: 🌐 nginx   start together, share the sandbox
   Running ✅
   ── if an init container fails → pod stuck in Init:0/2 → it never reaches Running
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why init containers are the clean answer to "wait for X"</h5>
                    <p>Instead of baking retry/wait logic into easypj's startup, an init container does the waiting (e.g. until <code>easypj-db:5432</code> answers) and the app container starts <em>only</em> once the dependency is ready. And the <strong>pause container</strong> is why all containers in a pod share one IP: they all join <em>its</em> network namespace — so a crashing app container can restart without the pod losing its IP. You'll use init containers for real in Chapter 16 (Jobs/migrations).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.2 ReplicaSets — Maintaining the Count</h3>
                <p>A ReplicaSet keeps a <strong>desired number of identical pods</strong> running, using a label <strong>selector</strong> to know which pods are "its." If one dies, it creates a replacement — that's self-healing. You rarely create a ReplicaSet directly; a Deployment manages it for you.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ReplicaSet self-healing — always 3 replicas</div>
<pre>
   ReplicaSet easypj-web-rs   (replicas: 3, selector: app=easypj,tier=web)

   HEALTHY                        POD 2 CRASHES                  SELF-HEALED
   ┌────┐ ┌────┐ ┌────┐          ┌────┐ ┌────┐ ┌────┐          ┌────┐ ┌────┐ ┌────┐
   │📦 1│ │📦 2│ │📦 3│          │📦 1│ │💀 2│ │📦 3│          │📦 1│ │🆕 4│ │📦 3│
   └────┘ └────┘ └────┘          └────┘ └────┘ └────┘          └────┘ └────┘ └────┘
   3/3 Running ✅                 2/3 ⚠️                          3/3 Running ✅
                                     │ RS: "want 3, see 2"          ▲ new pod created
                                     └──────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 ReplicaSet vs Deployment</h5>
                    <p>A ReplicaSet only ensures <em>count</em>. A <strong>Deployment</strong> adds rolling updates, rollback history, and version tracking on top — and creates/owns the ReplicaSet for you. In practice you always use Deployments; ReplicaSets are the layer doing the actual self-healing underneath.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.3 Deployments — Managing the Application Lifecycle</h3>
                <p>The Deployment is the <strong>standard way to run a stateless app</strong>. You declare desired state (image, replicas, resources); the controller makes it real and adds rolling updates, rollbacks, and revision history — by managing a chain of ReplicaSets.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Deployment ownership chain (old ReplicaSets kept for rollback)</div>
<pre>
   🚀 Deployment: easypj-web
   ├── manages ─▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  easypj-web-7f8b4  (current, v3)
   │                ├── owns ─▶ 📦 easypj-web-7f8b4-abc1
   │                ├── owns ─▶ 📦 easypj-web-7f8b4-abc2
   │                └── owns ─▶ 📦 easypj-web-7f8b4-abc3
   ├── keeps ──▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  easypj-web-6d5a2  (old v2, scaled to 0)
   └── keeps ──▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  easypj-web-5c3f1  (old v1, scaled to 0)
   💡 old ReplicaSets at 0 replicas = your instant rollback history.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🪆 In plain English — the wrapping (nesting) model</h5>
                    <p>Pod, ReplicaSet, and Deployment aren't competing things — each <strong>wraps</strong> the one below and adds a single capability. Think Russian dolls, smallest inside:</p>
<pre class="diagram-box">
   🚀 Deployment        "give me rolling updates + rollback + history"
     └─ 🎛️ ReplicaSet    "keep exactly N copies alive"   (self-healing)
          └─ 📦 Pod      "schedule these container(s) together, one IP"
               └─ 🐋 Container   "run my easypj process"
</pre>
                    <p class="mb-0">You create the <strong>outermost</strong> doll (the Deployment) and it builds the rest. That's why you almost never make a bare Pod or a lone ReplicaSet — you'd give up the capabilities the outer wrappers add. Need a one-off task instead of a long-running app? Swap the Deployment for a <strong>Job</strong> (Ch 16): same Pod inside, different outer doll.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-deployment.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  labels:
    app: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                  <span class="comment"># ← MUST match the template labels below</span>
      tier: web
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1                   <span class="comment"># at most 1 EXTRA pod while rolling</span>
      maxUnavailable: 0             <span class="comment"># zero-downtime: never dip below desired</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj                 <span class="comment"># ← MUST match the selector above</span>
        tier: web
        version: v3
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: easypj:v3
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: "128Mi"         <span class="comment"># scheduling floor (guaranteed)</span>
            cpu: "250m"
          limits:
            memory: "256Mi"         <span class="comment"># hard ceiling (OOMKilled if exceeded)</span>
            cpu: "500m"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pod Lifecycle — the Phases &amp; Statuses You'll See</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod phases + the STATUS column you read in kubectl get pods</div>
<pre>
   ┌─────────┐    ┌─────────┐    ┌───────────┐
   │ Pending │──▶ │ Running │──▶ │ Succeeded │ (exit 0 — Jobs)
   └────┬────┘    └────┬────┘    └───────────┘
        │              └──▶ ┌────────┐
        └──────────────────▶│ Failed │ (exit ≠ 0)
                            └────────┘
   STATUS column (what kubectl actually shows):
   ContainerCreating  image pulling / volumes mounting
   Running            at least one container up
   Completed          all containers exited 0
   CrashLoopBackOff   container keeps crashing (growing backoff)
   ImagePullBackOff   can't pull the image
   Pending            not yet scheduled to a node
   Terminating        being gracefully deleted
   Init:N/M           init containers still running
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Reading those statuses for the exam</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Pending</strong> → scheduler can't place it (no fit), ResourceQuota, or unbound PVC</li>
                        <li><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> → wrong image/tag, private registry without <code>imagePullSecrets</code></li>
                        <li><strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> → <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> for the crash reason</li>
                        <li><strong>Init:0/1</strong> → an init container is stuck — <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;init&gt;</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Deployment Operations (CKA Speed)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — the rollout toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3   <span class="comment"># fast create</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5                      <span class="comment"># scale</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web easypj=easypj:v4              <span class="comment"># rolling update</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web                         <span class="comment"># watch it</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web                        <span class="comment"># revisions</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web                           <span class="comment"># roll back 1</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web --to-revision=2           <span class="comment"># to a specific rev</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The strategy knobs</h5>
                    <p><code>maxSurge: 1</code> allows one extra pod during the update; <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> means never drop below the desired count → <strong>zero downtime</strong>. Deep dive (incl. canary/blue-green) in Chapter 13.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.4 Services — Stable Network Endpoints</h3>
                <p>Pods get random IPs that change on every restart — so you can never point a client at a pod IP. A <strong>Service</strong> gives a <strong>stable virtual IP + DNS name</strong> and load-balances across the pods its selector matches (its "endpoints").</p>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Reachable from</th><th>Use case</th><th>Port</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>ClusterIP</code></td><td>Inside the cluster only</td><td>Internal (the default)</td><td>any</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>NodePort</code></td><td>Outside, via <code>&lt;nodeIP&gt;:port</code></td><td>Dev / testing</td><td>30000–32767</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>LoadBalancer</code></td><td>Outside, via a cloud LB</td><td>Production (cloud)</td><td>any</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ExternalName</code></td><td>DNS alias (CNAME)</td><td>Point at an external host</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service types — who can reach each</div>
<pre>
   🔒 ClusterIP (default)        🚪 NodePort                  ☁️ LoadBalancer
   ┌──────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────┐        ┌──────────────────┐
   │ in-cluster only   │         │ &lt;nodeIP&gt;:30080    │        │ cloud LB          │
   │ VIP 10.96.0.10    │         │      │            │        │ 203.0.113.50      │
   │      │            │         │      ▼            │        │      │            │
   │      ▼            │         │ VIP 10.96.0.11    │        │      ▼            │
   │ 📦 easypj  📦 easypj│         │      │            │        │ VIP 10.96.0.12    │
   └──────────────────┘         │      ▼            │        │      │            │
                                │ 📦 easypj 📦 easypj│        │      ▼            │
                                └──────────────────┘        │ 📦 easypj 📦 easypj│
                                                             └──────────────────┘
   all three pick a READY pod by label selector (app=easypj) and load-balance to it
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-service.yaml — ClusterIP + NodePort</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># internal Service (default) — stable VIP inside the cluster</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-svc
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: easypj           <span class="comment"># matches the pod labels</span>
    tier: web
  ports:
  - port: 80              <span class="comment"># the Service port (what clients call)</span>
    targetPort: 8000      <span class="comment"># the container port (gunicorn listens here)</span>
---
<span class="comment"># external dev access — same pods, reachable from outside the cluster</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-nodeport
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: easypj
    tier: web
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8000
    nodePort: 30080       <span class="comment"># reachable at &lt;nodeIP&gt;:30080 (range 30000–32767)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — expose fast (CKA)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000          <span class="comment"># ClusterIP</span>
kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 \
  --type=NodePort --name=easypj-nodeport
kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc      <span class="comment"># the pod IPs this Service routes to (empty = bug!)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Service DNS — How Pods Find Each Other by Name</h4>
                <p>Every Service gets an automatic DNS name from CoreDNS, so easypj-web can reach easypj-db without ever knowing its IP:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service DNS resolution &amp; cross-namespace access</div>
<pre>
   from a pod in the easypj namespace, all of these reach the DB Service:
     easypj-db                              ← same namespace (short)
     easypj-db.easypj                       ← name.namespace
     easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local     ← the full FQDN
   pattern:  &lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local

   cross-namespace:  easypj-web (ns: easypj)  ──curl postgres.data.svc.cluster.local──▶
                     🐘 postgres Service (ns: data)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Debugging DNS &amp; endpoints</h5>
                    <p>Service unreachable by name? Check CoreDNS (<code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code>) and test from a pod: <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- nslookup easypj-db</code>. Unreachable by VIP? Check <code>kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc</code> — empty endpoints = the selector doesn't match any ready pod (the #1 Service bug; full depth in Part 5).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Two More Service Behaviours Worth Knowing</h4>
                <p>Beyond the four types, two knobs come up constantly — especially the moment you hit stateful apps like easypj's database.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>Headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>)</h5>
                        <p>No VIP, no load-balancing. DNS returns the <strong>individual pod IPs</strong> directly. This is what <strong>StatefulSets</strong> use so clients can address a <em>specific</em> replica by a stable name like <code>postgres-0.easypj-db…</code> (Ch 16).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📌</div><h5>sessionAffinity: ClientIP</h5>
                        <p>Pins a client to the <em>same</em> backend pod for a timeout window instead of load-balancing every request — useful for sticky sessions. Default is <code>None</code> (spread freely).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 How kube-proxy actually routes (iptables vs IPVS)</h5>
                    <p>A Service VIP isn't a real interface — it's a <strong>rule</strong>. kube-proxy programs the node so traffic to the VIP is DNAT'd to a ready pod IP:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>iptables</strong> (default): a chain of rules with random probability per endpoint — simple, but rule lookup is O(n) at thousands of services.</li>
                        <li><strong>IPVS</strong>: a kernel load-balancer with hash tables — O(1), better at large scale, more LB algorithms (round-robin, least-conn).</li>
                        <li><strong>nftables</strong>: the newer successor to iptables mode.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">Check the mode: <code>kubectl -n kube-system logs ds/kube-proxy | grep -i proxier</code>. This is why "Services are slow at scale" → consider IPVS (Ch 18/34).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.5 Namespaces — Virtual Clusters</h3>
                <p>Namespaces are <strong>logical partitions</strong> of one cluster — used to isolate teams and environments. Each can carry its own ResourceQuota, RBAC, and NetworkPolicy, so <code>easypj-dev</code> and <code>easypj-prod</code> live side-by-side without colliding.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Namespace isolation — the easypj example</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────── ☸️ CLUSTER ────────────────────────────────┐
   │  ┌─────────────────────┐        ┌─────────────────────┐                     │
   │  │ 🟢 easypj-dev         │        │ 🔴 easypj-prod        │                    │
   │  │  1 replica · DEBUG=on │        │  3 replicas · DEBUG=off│                   │
   │  │  low limits           │        │  high limits           │                   │
   │  └─────────────────────┘        └─────────────────────┘                     │
   │  ┌─────────────────────┐        ┌─────────────────────┐                     │
   │  │ 🔧 kube-system        │        │ 📊 monitoring         │                    │
   │  │  CoreDNS · kube-proxy │        │  Prometheus · Grafana  │                   │
   │  └─────────────────────┘        └─────────────────────┘                     │
   │  each namespace can have its OWN: ResourceQuota · RBAC Roles · NetworkPolicy │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Four Built-in Namespaces</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>default</h5><p>Where objects land with no namespace set. Avoid it in production.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5>kube-system</h5><p>Control-plane add-ons: CoreDNS, kube-proxy, metrics-server. Don't deploy apps here.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌍</div><h5>kube-public</h5><p>Readable by all (even unauthenticated). Holds the cluster-info ConfigMap.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">💓</div><h5>kube-node-lease</h5><p>Node heartbeat Lease objects — used for fast node-health detection.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — namespace commands</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl get namespaces
kubectl get pods -n easypj-dev
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># make it the default</span>
kubectl get pods -A                                            <span class="comment"># across ALL namespaces</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The #1 exam trap: wrong namespace = zero marks</h5>
                    <p>Every CKA task names a namespace at the top. Create the resource in the wrong one and you score nothing even if it's perfect. Always pass <code>-n &lt;ns&gt;</code> or switch context first. Also know what namespaces <strong>don't</strong> isolate: <strong>nodes</strong> and <strong>cluster-scoped objects</strong> (PersistentVolumes, Nodes, StorageClasses, ClusterRoles) live outside any namespace.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>ResourceQuota — Capping a Namespace</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-quota.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "4"
    requests.memory: 8Gi
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: 16Gi
    pods: "20"
    services: "10"
    persistentvolumeclaims: "5"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 ResourceQuota vs LimitRange</h5>
                    <p><strong>ResourceQuota</strong> caps the <em>namespace total</em> (all pods combined). <strong>LimitRange</strong> sets per-pod/container defaults &amp; maxes. Use both: Quota bounds the team, LimitRange stops any single pod from being greedy (full depth in Ch 15/41).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.6 Labels, Selectors &amp; Annotations</h3>
                <p>Labels are the <strong>glue of Kubernetes</strong> — key/value tags that <em>select</em> objects. They wire Services→Pods, Deployments→Pods, NetworkPolicies and RBAC. Annotations look similar but are non-identifying metadata that <em>never</em> select anything.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔖</div><h5>Labels — for selection</h5>
                        <p>Used by selectors to match &amp; group. Keep them meaningful.</p>
                        <ul><li><code>app: easypj</code></li><li><code>tier: web | db</code></li><li><code>env: prod</code></li><li><code>version: v3</code></li></ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>Annotations — for metadata</h5>
                        <p>For tools &amp; humans. Never used for selection.</p>
                        <ul><li><code>description: "easypj job board"</code></li><li><code>owner: "team-alpha"</code></li><li><code>kubernetes.io/change-cause: "v3"</code></li><li><code>prometheus.io/scrape: "true"</code></li></ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">How labels connect objects (the Service selector)</div>
<pre>
   🔌 Service easypj-svc  (selector: app=easypj, tier=web)
        │ matches pods whose labels include BOTH
        ▼
   ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐
   │ 📦 Pod        │  │ 📦 Pod        │  │ 📦 Pod        │
   │ app: easypj  │  │ app: easypj  │  │ app: easypj  │
   │ tier: web    │  │ tier: web    │  │ tier: db     │  ← tier mismatch
   │ ✅ MATCHED    │  │ ✅ MATCHED    │  │ ❌ NOT matched│
   └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘
   💡 change a label and you change what's selected — the source of most wiring bugs.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — label commands</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label pod easypj-pod tier=web          <span class="comment"># add a label to a live object</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels                 <span class="comment"># see all labels</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj                 <span class="comment"># filter by selector</span>
kubectl get pods -l 'app=easypj,tier=web'      <span class="comment"># AND</span>
kubectl get pods -l 'app in (easypj, other)'   <span class="comment"># set-based</span>
kubectl label pod easypj-pod tier-             <span class="comment"># remove a label (trailing dash)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The easypj label convention</h5>
                    <p>Throughout this guide easypj uses <code>app=easypj</code> · <code>tier=web|db</code> · <code>version=vN</code>. Consistent labels are what make Services, Deployments, NetworkPolicies, and <code>kubectl -l</code> filters all "just work."</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Recommended Labels (a Real Convention)</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes defines a standard set of <code>app.kubernetes.io/*</code> labels so tools (dashboards, Helm, meshes) can understand <em>any</em> app the same way. Our short <code>app=easypj</code> is fine for learning; the well-known set is what you'll meet in production:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Recommended label</th><th>Meaning</th><th>easypj value</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/name</code></td><td>The application's name</td><td><code>easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/component</code></td><td>The role within the app</td><td><code>web</code> / <code>db</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/instance</code></td><td>This specific install</td><td><code>easypj-prod</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/version</code></td><td>The app version</td><td><code>v2</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/managed-by</code></td><td>What deploys it</td><td><code>Helm</code> / <code>kubectl</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>Two Kinds of Selector</h4>
                <p>Selectors come in two flavours — know both, because Services/ReplicaSets use the first and you'll use the second on the command line:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🟰</div><h5>Equality-based</h5>
                        <p>Simple key=value (AND of all). What <code>matchLabels</code> in a Service/Deployment uses.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -l app=easypj,tier=web</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔢</div><h5>Set-based</h5>
                        <p><code>in</code>, <code>notin</code>, <code>exists</code>. More expressive; used in <code>matchExpressions</code> and rich queries.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -l 'tier in (web,db),version!=v1'
kubectl get pods -l 'app'      <span class="comment"># has the label at all</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 matchLabels vs matchExpressions</h5>
                    <p>In a Deployment/Service spec, <code>selector.matchLabels</code> is the easy equality form; <code>selector.matchExpressions</code> unlocks set-based rules (e.g. "tier in (web,db)"). Both compile to the same selection — use matchLabels unless you genuinely need the extra power.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.7 The Object Relationship Map</h3>
                <p>This is the one picture to memorize — how every object you've met wires together into a running easypj. If you can redraw this from memory, you can read (and debug) almost any manifest.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">How easypj wires together — the relationship map</div>
<pre>
   🌍 Ingress / 🚪 Gateway
        │ routes host/path
        ▼
   🔌 Service ──selector(app=easypj)──▶ 📦 Pods
        ▲                                  ▲ owned by
   🚀 Deployment ──manages──▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet ─┘ creates
        │ the pod template references:
        ├─ ⚙️ ConfigMap   (envFrom — non-secret config)
        ├─ 🔒 Secret      (envFrom — credentials)
        ├─ 💾 PVC ──▶ PV ──▶ 🧱 StorageClass   (durable storage)
        └─ 🛡️ ServiceAccount  ◀── RBAC (Role + Binding) controls access
   🗂️ Namespace contains ALL of the above   ·   🧯 NetworkPolicy firewalls pod traffic
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   💡 LABELS are the glue: a Service finds Pods ONLY by matching labels — not by name or IP.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 How to use this map for troubleshooting</h5>
                    <p>Almost every "it's not working" follows an edge of this graph: <em>Service has no endpoints</em> → the selector↔label edge is broken; <em>pod can't read config</em> → the ConfigMap/Secret edge; <em>forbidden</em> → the ServiceAccount/RBAC edge; <em>data lost on restart</em> → the PVC edge. Find the broken edge, fix that object.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 4.8 POD LIFECYCLE & HEALTH — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.8 Pod Lifecycle &amp; Health Probes</h3>
                <p>A pod isn't simply "up" or "down" — it moves through <strong>phases</strong>, its containers have their own <strong>states</strong>, and probes decide whether it's <strong>Ready</strong> for traffic. This is the vocabulary behind every "why is my pod stuck?" task.</p>

                <h4>① Pod phases — <code>.status.phase</code></h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Pending</code></td><td>accepted, not running yet — being scheduled, or pulling images</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Running</code></td><td>bound to a node; ≥1 container running / starting / restarting</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Succeeded</code></td><td>all containers exited 0 and won't restart (Jobs / one-offs)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Failed</code></td><td>all containers terminated; at least one exited non-zero</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Unknown</code></td><td>the node's kubelet can't be reached (usually a comms problem)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② Container states — <code>.status.containerStatuses[].state</code></h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning · what to read</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Waiting</code></td><td>not yet running — the <code>reason</code> says why (<code>ContainerCreating</code>, <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Running</code></td><td>started — has a <code>startedAt</code> timestamp</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Terminated</code></td><td>finished — records <code>exitCode</code> (137 = OOM/SIGKILL, 0 = clean), <code>reason</code>, <code>finishedAt</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>③ <code>restartPolicy</code> — Always · OnFailure · Never</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Restarts when…</th><th>Used by</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Always</code> (default)</td><td>the container exits for <em>any</em> reason</td><td>Deployments / long-running services</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>OnFailure</code></td><td>only on a non-zero exit</td><td>batch Jobs that may retry</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Never</code></td><td>never — pod goes Succeeded/Failed</td><td>one-shot tasks</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0">🔁 Restarts use <strong>exponential back-off</strong> (10s, 20s, 40s … capped at 5 min). A container that keeps crashing shows <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> — that's the back-off <em>waiting</em>, not an error in itself; read <code>kubectl logs</code> for the real cause.</p></div>

                <h4>④ Pod conditions — the path to <code>Ready</code> (and to a Service endpoint)</h4>
                <p>A pod's <code>.status.conditions</code> flip True in order. Only a <code>Ready</code> pod is added to its Service's endpoints — this is the answer to "pod is Running but gets no traffic."</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">conditions → endpoint membership</div>
<pre>
   PodScheduled ─▶ Initialized ─▶ ContainersReady ─▶ Ready ──▶ added to Service endpoints
   (got a node)    (init done)    (all probes pass)   (serving)
      ✗ stuck = no node (taints/resources)        ✗ Ready=False ⇒ removed from endpoints
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>⑤ Termination — the graceful shutdown sequence</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what happens on a pod delete</div>
<pre>
   1. apiserver sets metadata.deletionTimestamp         (pod → Terminating)
   2. pod removed from Service endpoints                 (stops new traffic)
   3. kubelet sends SIGTERM to each container            (app should drain &amp; exit)
   4. grace period: spec.terminationGracePeriodSeconds   (default 30s)
   5. still alive? → SIGKILL (forced)                    then cleanup
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><p class="mb-0">💡 Set <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> to match how long easypj needs to finish in-flight requests, and handle SIGTERM in the app for zero-downtime rollouts. <code>kubectl delete pod --grace-period=0 --force</code> skips the wait — only for genuinely stuck pods.</p></div>

                <h4>⑥ Health probes — startup, liveness, readiness</h4>
                <p>Probes let Kubernetes act on app health (deep config is in Part 4; the concepts belong here):</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Probe</th><th>Question</th><th>On failure</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>startup</strong></td><td>has the app finished booting?</td><td>holds off liveness/readiness; kills it if it never starts (good for slow JVMs/DBs)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>liveness</strong></td><td>is it still alive (not deadlocked)?</td><td><strong>restarts</strong> the container</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>readiness</strong></td><td>can it serve traffic right now?</td><td><strong>removes</strong> the pod from Service endpoints (no restart)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">Each probe uses one of four methods: <code>httpGet</code> (HTTP 2xx/3xx), <code>tcpSocket</code> (port opens), <code>exec</code> (command exits 0), or <code>grpc</code> (gRPC health check, stable since v1.27); tuned with <code>initialDelaySeconds</code>, <code>periodSeconds</code>, <code>failureThreshold</code>.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 4.9 ROLLOUTS · PORTS · LABELS · SCOPE — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-9">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.9 Rollouts, Service Ports, Label Rules &amp; Resource Scope</h3>
                <p>The exam-critical details that make the §4.3–4.6 objects actually behave: how a rollout paces itself, how Service ports map, the exact label grammar, and which resources live inside a namespace.</p>

                <h4>① Deployment rollout mechanics</h4>
                <p>A Deployment's <code>strategy</code> is <code>RollingUpdate</code> by default (the alternative, <code>Recreate</code>, kills all old pods first = downtime). The roll is paced by two knobs, plus a history limit for rollback:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>maxSurge</code></td><td><strong>25%</strong></td><td>extra pods allowed <em>above</em> desired during the roll (higher = faster)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>maxUnavailable</code></td><td><strong>25%</strong></td><td>pods allowed <em>down</em> during the roll (0 = zero-downtime, needs surge)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>revisionHistoryLimit</code></td><td><strong>10</strong></td><td>old ReplicaSets kept at 0 replicas for instant rollback</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">manage a rollout</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web easypj=easypj:v2   <span class="comment"># trigger a rolling update</span>
kubectl rollout status  deploy/easypj-web             <span class="comment"># watch it progress</span>
kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web             <span class="comment"># list revisions</span>
kubectl rollout undo    deploy/easypj-web             <span class="comment"># roll back to previous</span>
kubectl rollout undo    deploy/easypj-web --to-revision=3
kubectl rollout pause|resume deploy/easypj-web        <span class="comment"># batch edits into one roll</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0">⚠️ The Deployment default is <strong>25% / 25%</strong>. A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> differs: <code>maxUnavailable</code> defaults to <code>1</code>, no surge by default. And a Deployment's <code>.spec.selector</code> is <strong>immutable</strong> after creation.</p></div>

                <h4>② Service ports — <code>port</code> vs <code>targetPort</code> vs <code>nodePort</code></h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the three ports of a Service</div>
<pre>
   client ──▶ Service:PORT ──▶ TARGETPORT on the pod ──▶ app listening there
              (e.g. 80)         (e.g. 8000)               easypj on :8000

   NodePort type also opens:  &lt;anyNodeIP&gt;:NODEPORT ──▶ Service:port ──▶ targetPort
                              nodePort range 30000–32767  (auto-assigned if unset)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><p class="mb-0">⚠️ <code>targetPort</code> must match the container's real port (easypj = <code>8000</code>) or you get "connection refused" from a healthy-looking Service. <code>port</code> defaults to <code>targetPort</code> if omitted; a <code>nodePort</code> outside 30000–32767 is rejected.</p></div>

                <h4>③ Cross-namespace DNS — short name vs FQDN</h4>
                <p class="mb-0">Within one namespace, a pod reaches a Service by its <strong>short name</strong> (<code>easypj-db</code>). Across namespaces the short name fails — use the <strong>FQDN</strong> <code>service.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>. Example: easypj-web in <code>staging</code> calling the DB in <code>data</code> → <code>easypj-db.data.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>

                <h4>④ Label grammar &amp; set-based selectors</h4>
                <p>A label key is <code>[prefix/]name</code>: an optional DNS-subdomain prefix, then a name ≤ 63 chars from <code>a–z A–Z 0–9 - _ .</code> starting/ending alphanumeric. Values follow the same ≤ 63-char rule (or empty). Selectors come in two forms:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>equality (AND)</td><td><code>app=easypj,tier=web</code></td><td><code>matchLabels</code>, <code>kubectl -l</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>set — <code>in</code></td><td><code>tier in (web,db)</code></td><td><code>matchExpressions</code>, <code>-l</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>set — <code>notin</code></td><td><code>tier notin (cache)</code></td><td><code>matchExpressions</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>set — <code>exists</code></td><td><code>tier</code> (has it) · <code>!tier</code> (lacks it)</td><td><code>matchExpressions</code> / <code>-l</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>⑤ Namespaced vs cluster-scoped resources</h4>
                <p>Some objects live <em>inside</em> a namespace; others are cluster-wide. Deleting a namespace deletes everything namespaced in it — cluster-scoped objects survive.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Namespaced</th><th>Cluster-scoped</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Pod · Deployment · ReplicaSet · Service · ConfigMap · Secret · PVC · Role · RoleBinding · Ingress · NetworkPolicy</td><td>Node · PersistentVolume · StorageClass · Namespace · ClusterRole · ClusterRoleBinding · CustomResourceDefinition</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">See which is which: <code>kubectl api-resources --namespaced=true</code> and <code>kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false</code>.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — ownerReferences, cascading deletion &amp; how a Service finds pods</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The relationship map isn't a metaphor — those edges are real fields in etcd. Understanding the actual links explains cascading deletes, "why did my pods come back," and empty endpoints.</p>

                    <h5>① ownerReferences — the real parent-child links (and cascading deletion)</h5>
                    <p>Each Pod a ReplicaSet creates carries an <code>ownerReferences</code> field pointing at that ReplicaSet; the ReplicaSet points at the Deployment. That chain is what makes "delete the Deployment" also remove its ReplicaSets and Pods — <strong>garbage collection</strong> follows the owner links downward.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🚀 Deployment easypj-web
        ▲ ownerReference
   🎛️ ReplicaSet easypj-web-7f8b4
        ▲ ownerReference
   📦 Pod easypj-web-7f8b4-abc1     (metadata.ownerReferences → the ReplicaSet)

   delete the Deployment → GC deletes the RS → GC deletes the Pods   (cascades down)
   delete a Pod only     → the RS sees "want 3, have 2" → makes a new one (why it "comes back")
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>GC / delete mode</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Background</strong> (default)</td><td>Owner deleted immediately; children cleaned up in the background</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Foreground</strong></td><td>Children deleted first, then the owner (owner shows "deleting" until done)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Orphan</strong></td><td><code>--cascade=orphan</code> — delete only the owner, leave children running (adoptable later)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>② The EndpointSlice controller — how a Service actually reaches pods</h5>
                    <p>A Service doesn't hold pod IPs. The <strong>EndpointSlice controller</strong> watches pods matching the Service's selector and writes the list of <em>ready</em> pod IPs into EndpointSlice objects; kube-proxy programs those into the dataplane. That's the whole "selector → endpoints" mechanism — and why a label typo yields empty endpoints.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🔌 Service easypj-svc (selector app=easypj,tier=web)
        │ EndpointSlice controller watches matching + READY pods
        ▼
   🗂️ EndpointSlice easypj-svc-x9k2 → [10.244.1.5:8000, 10.244.1.6:8000, 10.244.1.7:8000]
        │ kube-proxy programs iptables/IPVS
        ▼
   client → Service VIP → DNAT → one of those pod IPs
   ⚠️ selector ≠ pod labels, OR no pod passes readiness → EndpointSlice empty → 503/timeout
</pre>

                    <h5>③ GVK / GVR &amp; the RESTMapper — how <code>kubectl</code> resolves a "kind"</h5>
                    <p>When you type <code>kubectl get deploy</code>, the client maps the short name → a <strong>GVK</strong> (Group/Version/Kind: <code>apps/v1, Deployment</code>) → a <strong>GVR</strong> (Group/Version/Resource: <code>apps/v1/deployments</code>) → the REST path <code>/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments</code>. That's why every manifest needs <code>apiVersion</code> + <code>kind</code>, and how CRDs add brand-new kinds.</p>

                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 spec vs status.</strong> Every object splits into <code>spec</code> (your desired state — you write it) and <code>status</code> (the controller's report — you read it). Controllers reconcile spec→reality and write status back. <code>kubectl get -o yaml</code> shows both; you almost never edit status by hand.</div>

                    <h5>④ Finalizers — why a "deleted" object gets stuck Terminating</h5>
                    <p>Deletion isn't always instant. If an object has a <strong>finalizer</strong> (a string in <code>metadata.finalizers</code>), a <code>delete</code> doesn't remove it — it just sets <code>deletionTimestamp</code> and the object sits in <strong>Terminating</strong> until some controller finishes cleanup and removes its finalizer. Only when the list is empty does the API actually delete it.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl delete pvc easypj-data
        │
        ▼  API sets metadata.deletionTimestamp  (object now "Terminating")
        │  metadata.finalizers: [kubernetes.io/pvc-protection]   ← still present
        ▼  the responsible controller does cleanup (detach the volume…)
        ▼  controller REMOVES its finalizer from the list
        ▼  finalizers empty → API garbage-collects the object for real ✅
   ⚠️ controller gone/broken → finalizer never removed → object STUCK Terminating forever
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The classic "stuck namespace."</strong> A Namespace stuck <code>Terminating</code> almost always means something inside it still has a finalizer (or an aggregated API is down). You diagnose with <code>kubectl get ns &lt;n&gt; -o yaml</code> (look at <code>status</code> + <code>spec.finalizers</code>) and fix the root cause — force-removing a finalizer is a last resort, since it skips the cleanup it was protecting.</div>

                    <h5>🧹 Control the cascade — and the cross-namespace ownership rule</h5>
                    <p>You can override the default deletion behaviour with <code>--cascade</code>, and ownership has one hard constraint:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl delete deploy easypj-web                       # --cascade=background (default): owner gone now, GC sweeps children
   kubectl delete deploy easypj-web --cascade=foreground  # children deleted FIRST; owner stays Terminating until they're gone
   kubectl delete deploy easypj-web --cascade=orphan      # delete the Deployment; LEAVE the ReplicaSet + Pods running
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 ownerReferences can't cross namespaces.</strong> A namespaced owner and its dependent must live in the <em>same</em> namespace (only a cluster-scoped object — e.g. a Node — may own across them). That rule is why deleting a namespace cleanly garbage-collects everything inside it.</div>

                    <h5>🔗 EndpointSlice vs the legacy Endpoints object</h5>
                    <p>The old <code>Endpoints</code> API packed every backend IP into one object — which became huge and churn-heavy for large Services. <strong>EndpointSlices</strong> shard them into pieces of <strong>≤ 100 endpoints</strong> (so a 1,000-pod Service ≈ 10 small slices that update independently). EndpointSlices are the default; the legacy <code>Endpoints</code> API is now deprecated. Only <strong>Ready</strong> pods land in a slice — the exact mechanism behind "Running but receiving no traffic."</p>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> <strong>server-side apply</strong> tracks field ownership in <code>managedFields</code> so multiple actors (you, a controller, a GitOps tool) can co-own one object without clobbering each other; <code>generateName</code> lets the API mint unique names (how RS pods get their random suffix); and the spec/status split is itself a <em>subresource</em> (<code>/status</code>, <code>/scale</code>) with its own RBAC — which is how <code>kubectl scale</code> works without granting full edit.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Pod &lt; ReplicaSet &lt; Deployment</strong> — you create the Deployment; it owns the RS; the RS owns the Pods (via ownerReferences).</li>
                    <li><strong>Pods are ephemeral</strong> — replaced not repaired; never rely on a pod name or IP.</li>
                    <li><strong>Services give stable networking</strong> — a VIP + DNS name that load-balances to pods by <em>label selector</em>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Namespaces isolate</strong> teams/envs — but NOT nodes or cluster-scoped objects (PV/Node/StorageClass).</li>
                    <li><strong>Labels are the glue</strong> — Service↔Pod, Deployment↔Pod, NetworkPolicy, RBAC all wire by labels; annotations never select.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Selector ≠ template labels.</strong> A Deployment's <code>selector.matchLabels</code> must match its pod-template labels, or it won't manage them; a Service's selector must match pod labels or endpoints are <strong>empty</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deleting a Pod under a Deployment</strong> just respawns it — change the <em>desired</em> state (scale/delete the Deployment) instead.</li>
                    <li><strong>Annotations aren't selectable.</strong> Put anything you need to <em>match</em> in labels, not annotations.</li>
                    <li><strong>Wrong namespace = 0 marks.</strong> Always set <code>-n</code> / context first.</li>
                    <li><strong>port vs targetPort.</strong> <code>port</code> is the Service's; <code>targetPort</code> is the container's (8000 for easypj). Mismatch = connection refused.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Generate, don't hand-write:</strong> <code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; d.yaml</code> then edit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Expose fast:</strong> <code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>First Service check:</strong> <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — empty = selector/readiness problem.</li>
                    <li><strong>Change live objects:</strong> <code>kubectl label</code> / <code>kubectl annotate</code>; filter with <code>-l</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 4)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the core-object toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get all -n easypj                 <span class="comment"># deploys, rs, pods, svc at a glance</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj -o wide    <span class="comment"># filter by label</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3
kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000
kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc          <span class="comment"># does the Service find pods?</span>
kubectl label pod easypj-pod tier=web     <span class="comment"># label / annotate live objects</span>
kubectl explain deployment.spec --recursive   <span class="comment"># in-exam field reference</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Wire It Together &amp; Watch It Self-Heal</h5>
                <p>Build a self-healing workload end to end — predict each result first:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Create the <code>easypj</code> namespace + a Deployment (3 replicas).</li>
                    <li>Expose it as a Service and confirm it has 3 endpoints.</li>
                    <li>Delete one pod and watch the ReplicaSet replace it.</li>
                    <li>Break the Service selector and watch endpoints go empty, then fix it.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj
kubectl -n easypj create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3
kubectl -n easypj expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000
kubectl -n easypj get endpoints easypj-web        <span class="comment"># 3 pod IPs ✅</span>

kubectl -n easypj delete pod -l app=easypj-web --field-selector status.phase=Running | head -1
kubectl -n easypj get pods -w                      <span class="comment"># a new pod appears → back to 3/3</span>

<span class="comment"># break it: point the Service selector at a label no pod has</span>
kubectl -n easypj patch svc easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"nope"}}}'
kubectl -n easypj get endpoints easypj-web         <span class="comment"># &lt;none&gt; — empty! 💀</span>
kubectl -n easypj patch svc easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"easypj-web"}}}'   <span class="comment"># fixed ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>What you proved:</strong> the ReplicaSet self-heals to the desired count (owner links + reconcile loop), and a Service's endpoints are <em>entirely</em> driven by the selector↔label match — break that edge and traffic stops, even though the pods are perfectly healthy.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A — CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 4 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the core objects — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Pod vs Deployment — when would you create each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Create a bare <strong>Pod</strong> only for a quick one-off/debug; for anything real, create a <strong>Deployment</strong>:</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>📦 bare Pod</th><th>🚀 Deployment</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Self-heals?</td><td>no — gone for good if it dies</td><td>yes (via its ReplicaSet)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Scale · roll out · roll back?</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Use for</td><td>a quick one-off / debug pod</td><td>anything real</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A bare pod is a pet; a Deployment manages cattle. In practice you almost never create naked pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Service has 0 endpoints. Give the two most common causes.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) The Service <strong>selector doesn't match</strong> any pod's labels. (2) Pods match but <strong>none is Ready</strong> (readiness probe failing). Confirm: <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> + compare selector to pod labels.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Only ready, label-matching pods land in the EndpointSlice — both conditions must hold.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do Namespaces isolate — and what do they NOT isolate?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>They scope <em>namespaced</em> objects and can carry RBAC/Quota/NetworkPolicy — but cluster-scoped objects live outside any namespace:</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>✅ Namespaces DO scope</th><th>❌ Namespaces do NOT scope</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Pods · Services · Deployments · ConfigMaps · Secrets</td><td>Nodes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Roles · RoleBindings · ResourceQuota · NetworkPolicy</td><td>PersistentVolumes · StorageClasses</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><em>(namespaced)</em></td><td>ClusterRoles · ClusterRoleBindings · CRDs <em>(cluster-scoped)</em></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Namespaces are a logical, not a hard security/physical, boundary — that's what NetworkPolicy + RBAC add.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Add the label <code>tier=web</code> to a running Deployment.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">label a live object</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label deployment easypj-web tier=web
kubectl label deployment easypj-web tier=web --overwrite   <span class="comment"># change an existing value</span>
<span class="comment"># to label the PODS, edit the pod template — selectors match pod labels, not the Deployment's</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Labeling the Deployment object ≠ labeling its pods — the pod template's labels are what selectors match.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Label vs annotation — the difference and a use for each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>🔖 Label</th><th>📝 Annotation</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>identify &amp; <strong>select</strong></td><td>non-identifying metadata</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Selectable?</td><td>yes — <code>-l</code>, <code>matchLabels</code></td><td>never</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Example</td><td><code>app=easypj</code></td><td><code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> · <code>prometheus.io/scrape</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If you need to <em>match</em> on it, it's a label. If it's just info, it's an annotation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the ownership chain for a pod named <code>easypj-web-7d4-x9k2</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The name literally encodes the chain; each layer links up via <code>ownerReferences</code>:</p>
                        <pre class="diagram-box">
   🚀 Deployment   easypj-web
        │ owns (ownerReference)
        ▼
   🎛️ ReplicaSet   easypj-web-7d4            ← Deployment name + pod-template-hash
        │ owns
        ▼
   📦 Pod          easypj-web-7d4-x9k2       ← ReplicaSet name + random suffix
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The name literally encodes the chain — and that chain drives cascading deletion.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why are Pods "ephemeral," and what's the implication for IPs?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A pod is never repaired — when it dies it's replaced by a <em>new</em> pod with a new name and a new IP. So you must never hard-code a pod IP; reach pods through a <strong>Service</strong> (stable VIP + DNS) instead.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the entire reason Services exist — stable networking over a moving set of pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List the 4 Service types and who can reach each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Reachable by</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>ClusterIP</strong> <em>(default)</em></td><td>in-cluster only</td><td>internal service-to-service</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>NodePort</strong></td><td><code>&lt;nodeIP&gt;:30000–32767</code></td><td>dev / bare-metal external access</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td><td>a cloud load balancer</td><td>cloud production</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>ExternalName</strong></td><td>DNS CNAME (no proxying)</td><td>alias an external host</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ClusterIP for internal, NodePort for dev/bare-metal, LoadBalancer for cloud prod, ExternalName to alias something outside.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You delete a Pod managed by a Deployment. What happens and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The ReplicaSet immediately creates a <strong>replacement</strong> — its reconcile loop sees "want 3, have 2" and acts. To actually remove it, lower the desired state (<code>kubectl scale</code>) or delete the Deployment.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>You changed actual, not desired; the controller just closes the gap again.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">From the relationship map: how does a ConfigMap reach a container?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The pod template references it three ways; the kubelet injects it when the container starts:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">3 ways a ConfigMap reaches a container</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ① all keys → env vars</span>
envFrom:
- configMapRef:
    name: easypj-config
<span class="comment"># ② one key → one env var (optionally renamed)</span>
env:
- name: DB_HOST
  valueFrom:
    configMapKeyRef:
      name: easypj-config
      key: db_host
<span class="comment"># ③ keys → files in a mounted volume (can hot-reload)</span>
volumes:
- name: cfg
  configMap:
    name: easypj-config</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Env values are fixed at start; file mounts can hot-reload. Full detail in Chapter 23.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 4 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — produce the object or the fix with real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Create a pod named <code>web-pod</code> in namespace <code>production</code> using the image <code>nginx:1.25-alpine</code> with the label <code>app=web</code> and a resource request of 100m CPU and 128Mi memory. Write the imperative command and the equivalent YAML.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative approach (generate yaml</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative approach (generate YAML, then add resources)</span>
kubectl run web-pod --image=nginx:1.25-alpine \
  --labels=app=web \
  -n production \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > web-pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Then edit to add resources, or use the full YAML:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: web-pod
  namespace: production
  labels:
    app: web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web-pod
    image: nginx:1.25-alpine
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: "100m"
        memory: "128Mi"

<span class="comment"># Apply it</span>
kubectl apply -f web-pod.yaml</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is a fundamental CKA task. The hybrid approach — imperative generation + declarative editing — is the fastest strategy. Key points: (1) <code>kubectl run</code> only creates pods (not deployments since v1.18+). (2) <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> generates YAML without creating the resource. (3) Resource requests affect scheduling — the scheduler only places the pod on nodes with at least 100m CPU and 128Mi memory available. (4) Always create the namespace first if it doesn't exist: <code>kubectl create namespace production</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the relationship between a <strong>Deployment</strong>, a <strong>ReplicaSet</strong>, and a <strong>Pod</strong>? If you create a Deployment with 3 replicas, how many of each object exist?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The hierarchy is: <strong>Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods</strong></p>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">Deployment (nginx-deploy)
  └── ReplicaSet (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8)     ← 1 ReplicaSet
        ├── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-abc12)  ← Pod 1
        ├── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-def34)  ← Pod 2
        └── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-ghi56)  ← Pod 3

Total: 1 Deployment + 1 ReplicaSet + 3 Pods = 5 objects</code></pre>
</div>
                                <p><strong>Ownership chain:</strong> Each object has an <code>ownerReference</code> pointing to its parent. Deleting the Deployment cascades down — deletes the ReplicaSet, which deletes the pods.</p>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding this hierarchy is critical for CKA troubleshooting. Key points: (1) You should <strong>never</strong> create ReplicaSets directly — always use Deployments. (2) During rolling updates, the Deployment creates a <em>new</em> ReplicaSet and scales it up while scaling the old one down. (3) Old ReplicaSets are kept (with 0 replicas) for rollback — controlled by <code>spec.revisionHistoryLimit</code> (default: 10). (4) If you see orphaned pods not managed by a Deployment, check for missing ownerReferences.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the three Service types: <code>ClusterIP</code>, <code>NodePort</code>, and <code>LoadBalancer</code>. For each type, describe who can access the service and provide an example command to create one.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Type</th>
                                            <th>Accessible From</th>
                                            <th>Port Range</th>
                                            <th>Use Case</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>ClusterIP</code></td>
                                            <td>Inside cluster only</td>
                                            <td>Any</td>
                                            <td>Internal services (DB, cache)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>NodePort</code></td>
                                            <td>External via node IP</td>
                                            <td>30000-32767</td>
                                            <td>Dev/test external access</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>LoadBalancer</code></td>
                                            <td>External via cloud LB</td>
                                            <td>Any</td>
                                            <td>Production external access</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">clusterip (default)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ClusterIP (default)</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 --target-port=80

<span class="comment"># NodePort</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --type=NodePort --port=80 --target-port=80

<span class="comment"># LoadBalancer</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --target-port=80</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Services provide stable networking for ephemeral pods. Each Service gets a permanent <strong>ClusterIP</strong> and DNS name. Key CKA concepts: (1) <code>NodePort</code> automatically creates a <code>ClusterIP</code> underneath — it's an extension. (2) <code>LoadBalancer</code> creates both <code>ClusterIP</code> and <code>NodePort</code> underneath. (3) The <code>selector</code> in the Service spec must match labels on the pods — if they don't match, the service has no endpoints. (4) <code>kubectl expose</code> is the fastest way to create a service on the CKA exam.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You create a Deployment with <code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3</code>. Now you need to update the image to <code>easypj:v2</code>. What command do you use, and what happens internally during the rolling update?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">update the image</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update the image</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web easypj-web=easypj:v2

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># What happens internally:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Deployment controller creates a NEW ReplicaSet (easypj-web-v2-xxxxx)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Scales new RS up: 0 → 1</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Scales old RS down: 3 → 2</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Repeats until new RS has 3 and old RS has 0</span>
<span class="comment"># (controlled by maxSurge and maxUnavailable)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check rollout history</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check rollout history</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Rollback if v2 is broken</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Rollback to a specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web --to-revision=1</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Rolling updates are a core CKA task. The Deployment's <code>strategy.rollingUpdate</code> settings control the pace: <code>maxSurge</code> (how many extra pods above desired count) and <code>maxUnavailable</code> (how many pods can be down during the update). Default is <code>25%</code> for both. The old ReplicaSet is kept with 0 replicas so you can <code>rollout undo</code>. Pro tip: to record the change cause, add <code>--record</code> (deprecated but still works) or annotate the deployment with <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>Labels</strong> and <strong>Selectors</strong> in Kubernetes? How do they connect Services to Pods? Give an example where a Service fails to route traffic because of a label mismatch.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>Labels</strong> are key-value pairs attached to objects. <strong>Selectors</strong> are queries that match labels to connect objects together.</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod has label: app=web</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod has label: app=web</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: web-pod
  labels:
    app: web        # ← Label
    version: v1

<span class="comment"># Service selects pods with app=web</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: web-svc
spec:
  selector:
    app: web        # ← Must EXACTLY match pod label
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8080

<span class="comment"># ❌ BROKEN: Service selector doesn't match pod labels</span>
spec:
  selector:
    app: frontend   # ← "frontend" ≠ "web" → NO endpoints!</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose: check if service has endpoints</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Diagnose: check if service has endpoints</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># If ENDPOINTS shows &lt;none&gt;, selector doesn't match any pods</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Label/selector mismatches are one of the <strong>most common CKA troubleshooting scenarios</strong>. If traffic doesn't reach pods: (1) Check <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — empty means no matching pods. (2) Compare <code>kubectl get svc &lt;svc&gt; -o yaml | grep -A 5 selector</code> with <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code>. (3) Labels must match <strong>exactly</strong> — <code>app: web</code> ≠ <code>App: web</code> (case sensitive). ReplicaSets and Deployments also use selectors to find their pods — same matching rules apply.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Create a Namespace called <code>exam-prep</code>, then create a Deployment named <code>api-server</code> in that namespace with 2 replicas of <code>httpd:2.4</code>. Finally, expose it as a NodePort service on port 80. Write all the imperative commands.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create namespace</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace exam-prep

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create deployment</span>
kubectl create deployment api-server \
  --image=httpd:2.4 \
  --replicas=2 \
  -n exam-prep

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Expose as NodePort</span>
kubectl expose deployment api-server \
  --type=NodePort \
  --port=80 \
  --target-port=80 \
  -n exam-prep

<span class="comment"># Verify everything</span>
kubectl get all -n exam-prep

<span class="comment"># Access the service</span>
kubectl get svc api-server -n exam-prep
<span class="comment"># Note the NodePort (e.g., 31234)</span>
<span class="comment"># Access via: http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:31234</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This 3-command sequence is a <strong>bread-and-butter CKA task</strong>. Timing tip: this should take under 60 seconds. Key points: (1) Always specify <code>-n &lt;namespace&gt;</code> or use <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=exam-prep</code> to avoid mistakes. (2) <code>--port</code> is the service port, <code>--target-port</code> is the container port. (3) Kubernetes auto-assigns a NodePort from 30000-32767 unless you specify <code>--node-port=XXXXX</code>. (4) <code>kubectl get all</code> shows deployments, replicasets, pods, and services in one view.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubectl create</code> and <code>kubectl apply</code>? When would you use each one on the CKA exam?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th><code>kubectl create</code></th>
                                            <th><code>kubectl apply</code></th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Type</td>
                                            <td>Imperative</td>
                                            <td>Declarative</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>If exists</td>
                                            <td>Error: "already exists"</td>
                                            <td>Updates (merges changes)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Idempotent</td>
                                            <td>No</td>
                                            <td>Yes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Best for</td>
                                            <td>First-time creation, speed</td>
                                            <td>Updates, CI/CD, repeatability</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cka strategy: use create for speed, apply for edits</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># CKA strategy: use create for speed, apply for edits</span>
kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > web.yaml
vim web.yaml  <span class="comment"># add resources, probes, volumes</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml

<span class="comment"># Later, edit and re-apply</span>
vim web.yaml  <span class="comment"># change replicas, image</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml  <span class="comment"># applies only the diff</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA, use <code>create</code> with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate quick scaffolds, then <code>apply</code> to submit them. If a question says "create," either works. If a question says "update" or "modify," you need <code>apply</code> or <code>kubectl edit</code> or <code>kubectl patch</code>. Pro tip: <code>kubectl apply</code> stores the last-applied configuration as an annotation, enabling three-way merge diffs on subsequent applies.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">A pod is in <code>Pending</code> state. When you describe it, events show: <em>"0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu"</em>. What does this mean and what are your options to fix it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The scheduler cannot find any node with enough free CPU to satisfy the pod's <code>resources.requests.cpu</code>. Options to fix:</p>
                                <ol>
                                    <li><strong>Reduce the pod's CPU request:</strong>
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">or edit the deployment</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl edit pod &lt;name&gt;  <span class="comment"># or edit the deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># Change resources.requests.cpu to a lower value</span></code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                    <li><strong>Free up resources</strong> by deleting/scaling down other workloads:
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -10
kubectl scale deployment heavy-app --replicas=1</code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                    <li><strong>Add more nodes</strong> to the cluster to provide additional capacity</li>
                                    <li><strong>Check ResourceQuotas</strong> — a namespace quota may be artificially limiting available resources:
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get resourcequota -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                </ol>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The "Insufficient" message is from the scheduler's <strong>filtering phase</strong>. It checked all 3 nodes and none had enough allocatable CPU. On the CKA, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> events section is your best friend for diagnosing Pending pods. Other common Pending causes: node taints without matching tolerations, node selector/affinity not matching, PVC unbound (waiting for PV). Always read the specific message — it tells you exactly which filter failed and on how many nodes.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>Annotations</strong> in Kubernetes? How do they differ from Labels, and when would you use annotations instead of labels?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th>Labels</th>
                                            <th>Annotations</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Purpose</td>
                                            <td>Identify and select objects</td>
                                            <td>Attach non-identifying metadata</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Used in selectors</td>
                                            <td>Yes (<code>selector: app=web</code>)</td>
                                            <td>No</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Size limit</td>
                                            <td>63 chars value</td>
                                            <td>256KB total</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Example</td>
                                            <td><code>app: web, tier: frontend</code></td>
                                            <td><code>description: "Main web app"</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">add annotation</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add annotation</span>
kubectl annotate deployment web kubernetes.io/change-cause="Updated to v2"

<span class="comment"># Add label</span>
kubectl label pod web-pod tier=frontend

<span class="comment"># Filter by label (annotations can't be used in selectors)</span>
kubectl get pods -l tier=frontend</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Labels are for <strong>identification and grouping</strong>. Annotations are for <strong>metadata storage</strong>. Common annotations you'll see on the CKA: (1) <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> — records why a rollout happened, (2) <code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration</code> — stored by <code>kubectl apply</code>, (3) Ingress controller annotations (e.g., <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target</code>). The CKA may ask you to annotate objects or add labels for selection — know the syntax for both.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to scale a Deployment from 3 replicas to 5, then verify the rollout completed. What are three different ways to accomplish this on the CKA exam?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl scale (fastest)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl scale (fastest)</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl edit (in-place edit)</span>
kubectl edit deployment easypj-web
<span class="comment"># Change spec.replicas from 3 to 5, save and exit</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl patch (JSON patch)</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'

<span class="comment"># Verify (all methods)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment easypj-web
kubectl get deployment easypj-web
<span class="comment"># Ready: 5/5</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl scale</code> is the fastest method for the CKA exam. However, <code>kubectl edit</code> is useful when you need to change multiple fields at once. <code>kubectl patch</code> is scriptable and good for automation. Key insight: <strong>scaling does NOT trigger a rollout</strong> — it only changes the replica count on the <em>current</em> ReplicaSet. Only image changes, env changes, or other spec updates trigger new rollouts. Verify scaling with <code>kubectl get deployment</code> — check that READY shows <code>5/5</code>, not <code>3/5</code> (which would indicate pods are still starting). You can also use <code>kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web</code> to see all 5 pods.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 4 Visual Summary — the objects, wired together</div>
<pre>
   🌍 Ingress/Gateway ─routes─▶ 🔌 Service ─selector(app=easypj)─▶ 📦 Pods
                                    ▲                                ▲ owned by
                               🚀 Deployment ─manages─▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet ┘ creates (self-heal)
                                    │ pod template pulls in:
                                    ├─ ⚙️ ConfigMap / 🔒 Secret (envFrom)
                                    ├─ 💾 PVC ─▶ PV ─▶ 🧱 StorageClass
                                    └─ 🛡️ ServiceAccount ◀─ RBAC governs access
   🗂️ Namespace contains it all   ·   🧯 NetworkPolicy firewalls it   ·   🔖 LABELS are the glue
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   You create a Deployment → it owns a ReplicaSet → which owns Pods; a Service finds those Pods
   by LABEL and gives them a stable address. That sentence is 80% of every CKA workload task.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> The objects nest. You create a <strong>Deployment</strong>; it <em>manages</em> a <strong>ReplicaSet</strong>; the ReplicaSet <em>creates and self-heals</em> the <strong>Pods</strong>. You never make the lower layers by hand — you describe the top one and ownership cascades down.</p>
                    <p>A pod's template pulls in everything it needs: <strong>ConfigMap/Secret</strong> for configuration (<code>envFrom</code>), a <strong>PVC → PV → StorageClass</strong> chain for storage, and a <strong>ServiceAccount</strong> (governed by RBAC) for API access. Out front, a <strong>Service</strong> uses a <em>label selector</em> (<code>app=easypj</code>) to find the ever-changing pods and give them one stable address; <strong>Ingress/Gateway</strong> routes external traffic to that Service. Around it all, a <strong>Namespace</strong> contains the objects and a <strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> firewalls them.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> <em>labels are the glue</em> — nothing is wired by name, everything is matched by label. "Create a Deployment → it owns a ReplicaSet → which owns Pods → a Service selects them by label" is the single sentence behind roughly 80% of every CKA workload task.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <!-- ::CH4-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 4 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 1 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part1-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟢 Part 1 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Foundations — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You started unable to picture a cluster; now you can name every component, explain what breaks without each, trace a pod from <code>kubectl apply</code> to Running, and read any easypj manifest. That mental model is the platform the rest of the guide builds on.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Part 1 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Kubernetes automates</strong> deploy / scale / heal via <em>desired-state reconciliation</em> — you declare, control loops deliver (Ch 1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Containers share the host kernel</strong> (namespaces + cgroups + union FS); K8s runs them via the <strong>CRI</strong>, not Docker (Ch 2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Control plane = apiserver + etcd + scheduler + controller-manager; nodes = kubelet + kube-proxy + runtime.</strong> Only the apiserver talks to etcd (Ch 3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Core objects:</strong> Pod &lt; ReplicaSet &lt; Deployment; Service for stable networking; Namespace for isolation; <strong>labels</strong> wire it all together (Ch 4).</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> "which component?" thinking powers the whole 25% Architecture + 30% Troubleshooting domains.</li>
                    <li>🔑 <code>cluster-info</code> · <code>get nodes -o wide</code> · <code>api-resources</code> · <code>explain</code> · <code>crictl</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 1 Visual Summary — easypj source → image → desired state → running pods</div>
<pre>
   🐍 easypj source ─build─▶ 📀 easypj:v1 ─push─▶ 🏪 registry        (Ch 2: the unit)
                                                     │ pull
   📝 deploy.yaml ──kubectl apply──▶ 📡 apiserver ──▶ 🗄️ etcd          (Ch 1: declare desired)
   (replicas: 3)                       │ authN/Z/admit  (desired state)
                                       ├─ 🔄 controllers: Deployment→ReplicaSet→Pods
                                       └─ 📋 scheduler: bind each Pod → a node       (Ch 3: the brain)
                                              ▼
                            🤖 kubelet ─🐋 run─▶ 📦📦📦 easypj pods ─🌐 CNI gives IPs   (Ch 3: the muscle)
                                              ▼
                            🔌 Service (selector app=easypj) ─load-balances─▶ the pods   (Ch 4: stable net)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   one app, described as desired state, that the cluster keeps alive — heal, scale, roll out, repeat. ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> You build the <code>easypj</code> app into an image (<code>easypj:v1</code>) and push it to a registry — that image is the portable <em>unit of deployment</em> (Ch 2). You then <strong>declare</strong> what you want in <code>deploy.yaml</code> (e.g. 3 replicas) and run <code>kubectl apply</code>: the <strong>apiserver</strong> authenticates, authorizes, and admits the request, then writes that desired state into <strong>etcd</strong> — the single source of truth (Ch 1).</p>
                    <p>From there the cluster's <strong>brain</strong> takes over (Ch 3): <strong>controllers</strong> expand <code>Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods</code>, and the <strong>scheduler</strong> picks a node for each Pod. On that node the <strong>muscle</strong> acts — the <strong>kubelet</strong> tells the container runtime (via the CRI) to start the containers, and the <strong>CNI</strong> hands every Pod an IP. A <strong>Service</strong> with selector <code>app=easypj</code> then gives those churning Pods one stable address and load-balances across them (Ch 4).</p>
                    <p><strong>The one idea to keep:</strong> nothing here is a one-off command — it's all <em>desired state</em> the cluster reconciles toward, continuously. That's why it can heal a dead pod, scale on demand, and roll out a new version on its own. Master this loop and the other 13 parts are just variations on it.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END PART 1 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 2 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part2">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟢 Part 2 · Hands-On Basics</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Drive the Cluster</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 5–8 · Beginner → Intermediate · <em>"Make it run"</em></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner → Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 4 Chapters</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~7.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🛠️ Hands-On Basics</span>
            </div>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Part 1 gave you the mental model; Part 2 puts your hands on the controls. You'll stand up a real, local cluster, master <strong>kubectl</strong> as your remote control, learn the <strong>YAML</strong> Kubernetes speaks, and deploy <strong>easypj</strong> for the first time — watching every Part 1 concept turn into a live, running object.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you'll build by the end:</strong> Minikube + kubectl installed, contexts under control, and easypj deployed end-to-end — Namespace → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service — reachable in your browser at <code>/jobs/</code>.</div>
            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🚗 Real-World Analogy: Driving a Car You Didn't Build</h5>
                <p>You don't need to know how the engine was manufactured to drive well — you need to know the controls. Part 2 hands you the keys: <strong>kubectl</strong> is the steering wheel and pedals, YAML is the owner's manual written in the car's own language, and Minikube is a driveway to practice in before you're ever on the highway. By the end you won't just know Kubernetes exists — you'll have actually driven it, watched it respond, and put your own app on the road.</p>
<pre>  DRIVING A CAR                                KUBERNETES OPERATION
  ───────────────                              ─────────────────────
  🔑 Get the keys, sit in the driveway ──────▶  Ch 5 — Minikube + kubeconfig
  🎛️  Learn the pedals &amp; dashboard    ──────▶  Ch 6 — kubectl verbs &amp; explain
  📖 Read the owner's manual           ──────▶  Ch 7 — YAML, dry-run, apply
  🚦 Actually drive it onto the road   ──────▶  Ch 8 — deploy easypj end-to-end</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"kubectl talks directly to my containers."</td>
                            <td>kubectl only ever talks to the API server. It never touches a container, a node, or etcd directly — every command is a request that the API server validates, persists, and hands off to a controller.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"YAML indentation is just a style preference."</td>
                            <td>YAML is whitespace-significant. One misplaced space silently nests a field under the wrong parent — the file still "looks right" but describes a completely different object, which is why exam candidates lose time to invisible indentation bugs.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"<code>kubectl apply</code> and <code>kubectl create</code> do the same thing."</td>
                            <td><code>create</code> is imperative and errors if the object already exists. <code>apply</code> is declarative and idempotent — safe to re-run, and it's how you'll manage everything once you move past one-off commands.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — Part 2</div>
<pre>
   Ch 5 — ENVIRONMENT   ─▶   Ch 6 — kubectl    ─▶   Ch 7 — YAML        ─▶   Ch 8 — DEPLOY easypj
   a cluster to use          the remote control      the language             put it all together
   Minikube + kubeconfig     verbs · explain         manifests · dry-run      CM · Secret · Deploy · Svc
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: from "I have a cluster" to "easypj is live and reachable at /jobs/".
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 5 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch5">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 5 · Setting Up Your Environment</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Before you can <em>administer</em> a cluster, you need one to practice on. This chapter installs the tools, connects you to a cluster, and drills the one skill every exam question opens with — <strong>switching context</strong> — all anchored on deploying <strong>easypj</strong>.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🛠️ Setup</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">💻 Hands-On</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Choose the right local Kubernetes tool — Minikube, Kind, k3s, kubeadm, Docker Desktop</li>
                    <li>✅ Install &amp; start Minikube with the optimal settings for CKA practice</li>
                    <li>✅ Install kubectl and confirm it connects to your cluster</li>
                    <li>✅ Read a <strong>kubeconfig</strong> — the file that controls every cluster connection</li>
                    <li>✅ Switch between clusters, contexts &amp; namespaces (used in <em>every</em> exam question)</li>
                    <li>✅ Verify cluster health and enable the essential add-ons</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">kubeconfig <span class="cka-weight">EVERY QUESTION</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 The CKA exam environment — context first, always</h5>
                <p>The exam gives you <strong>multiple pre-built clusters</strong>. <em>Every</em> question opens with "Use context <code>k8s-cluster-X</code>" — run <code>kubectl config use-context …</code> <strong>before you touch anything</strong>, or you'll do perfect work on the wrong cluster and score zero.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Context switching</strong> is the first action of nearly every task — make it muscle memory.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl is pre-installed</strong>, but your own <code>alias k=kubectl</code> + shell completion saves real minutes.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubernetes.io/docs</strong> is the one reference allowed in-exam — know how to navigate it fast.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
