            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-1"><strong>9.1</strong> What is kubeadm?</a></td><td>What builds a cluster, and what's left to me?</td><td>kubeadm does certs + control plane + join; you bring infra + CNI.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-2"><strong>9.2</strong> Pre-requisites</a></td><td>What must be true on every node first?</td><td>swap off · modules · sysctl · containerd (SystemdCgroup) · toolchain + hold.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-3"><strong>9.3</strong> Init control plane</a></td><td>How do I create the cluster?</td><td><code>kubeadm init</code> → copy admin.conf → SAVE the join command.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-4"><strong>9.4</strong> Install a CNI</a></td><td>Why are my nodes NotReady?</td><td>No pod network until a CNI is applied — then nodes go Ready.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-5"><strong>9.5</strong> Join workers</a></td><td>How do nodes join — and if I lost the token?</td><td><code>kubeadm join</code> with token + ca-hash; regenerate with <code>token create</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-6"><strong>9.6</strong> Verify</a></td><td>Is the cluster actually healthy?</td><td>all nodes Ready · kube-system Running · deploy easypj as a smoke test.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-7"><strong>9.7</strong> Practice</a></td><td>How do I drill this to reflex?</td><td>build 3-node, recover token, add CP node, fix a NotReady worker.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-8"><strong>9.8</strong> Required ports</a></td><td>What firewall ports must be open?</td><td>6443 · 2379-2380 · 10250 · 10259/10257 · 30000-32767.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.1 ─────────── -->

            <section class="section-block" id="s9-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.1 What is kubeadm?</h3>
                <p><code>kubeadm</code> is the <strong>official cluster bootstrapper</strong>. Point it at a freshly-installed Linux machine and it does the genuinely hard, error-prone parts of standing up Kubernetes: it generates a whole <strong>PKI</strong> (a CA plus certs for every component), writes the control-plane <strong>static-pod manifests</strong>, brings up <strong>etcd</strong>, configures the <strong>kubelet</strong>, and prints a <code>join</code> command that lets workers enrol securely. What it deliberately does <em>not</em> do is provision machines or choose your networking — those stay your call.</p>
                <div class="analogy-box"><h5>🧠 In plain English</h5><p>Think of kubeadm as the <strong>contractor who builds the house</strong>, not the one who buys the land or picks the paint. You supply the plot (VMs, OS, runtime); kubeadm pours the foundation and frames the structure (certs, control plane, etcd); you still choose the "plumbing" (the CNI network plugin) and move in the furniture (your apps). That division is exactly why a fresh <code>kubeadm init</code> cluster sits <code>NotReady</code> until <em>you</em> install a CNI.</p></div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubeadm — what it does vs what it leaves to you</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ YOU provide (the infra)          🔧 kubeadm handles (the hard parts)        ❌ NOT kubeadm's job
   ─────────────────────────────      ──────────────────────────────────        ─────────────────────────
   VMs / bare metal                   ✅ bootstrap the control plane             VM provisioning
   OS installed (Ubuntu/etc.)         ✅ generate the TLS PKI (CA + certs)       (Terraform / cloud console)
   network connectivity               ✅ create the etcd member                  OS install &amp; patching
   swap disabled                      ✅ configure &amp; start the kubelet           CNI install (you choose it)
   container runtime (containerd)     ✅ render static-pod manifests             LoadBalancer for HA CP
   kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl installed  ✅ issue the bootstrap join token          app deployment (kubectl's job)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5><code>kubeadm init</code></h5><p>Run <strong>once, on the control-plane node</strong>. Generates the PKI, brings up apiserver + etcd + scheduler + controller-manager as static pods, and prints the join command.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div><h5><code>kubeadm join</code></h5><p>Run <strong>on every worker</strong> (and extra control-plane nodes). Uses a <em>token</em> + <em>CA cert hash</em> to authenticate to the cluster and register the node.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the build pipeline — six moves, in order</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ provision    ⚙️ prerequisites   🔧 kubeadm init    🌐 install CNI     🔗 kubeadm join    ✅ cluster Ready
      VMs/OS    ─▶  swap·modules   ─▶  (control plane) ─▶ (pod network)  ─▶  (each worker)  ─▶  all nodes Ready
                    ·containerd                            nodes Ready                          → run easypj
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   skip a step and the next one fails — this exact order is what the exam tests.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 kubeadm vs the alternatives</h5><p><strong>kubeadm</strong> = the CKA-tested, do-it-yourself bootstrapper (you own upgrades, etcd, certs). <strong>Managed</strong> services (EKS/GKE/AKS) hide the control plane entirely. <strong>kops/kubespray</strong> wrap kubeadm with infra automation. The exam uses kubeadm precisely because it makes <em>you</em> the administrator of every layer.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.2 Pre-requisites for All Nodes</h3>
                <p>Before <code>kubeadm</code> will run, <strong>every node</strong> — control plane <em>and</em> workers — must be prepared identically. Each of these five steps fixes a specific way the kubelet or runtime would otherwise refuse to work, and each is a classic "broken node" the exam asks you to repair. Do all five on every machine.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div><h5>Hardware</h5><p>2+ CPUs · 2 GB+ RAM · a <strong>unique</strong> hostname &amp; MAC per node · full network connectivity</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div><h5>Swap OFF</h5><p>the kubelet refuses to start with swap on — disable it now <em>and</em> in <code>/etc/fstab</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐳</div><h5>Container runtime</h5><p>containerd (recommended) with the <strong>systemd</strong> cgroup driver</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Toolchain</h5><p><code>kubeadm</code> + <code>kubelet</code> + <code>kubectl</code>, same version, <strong>held</strong></p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Step A — disable swap</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · swap</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo swapoff -a                              <span class="comment"># off right now (this boot)</span>
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab     <span class="comment"># and permanently (comment the fstab line)</span>
free -h | grep Swap                          <span class="comment"># verify</span>
<span class="comment"># Swap:            0B          0B          0B</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why swap must be off</h5><p>Kubernetes schedules by <em>memory requests/limits</em> and assumes a pod that hits its memory limit is OOM-killed deterministically. Swap breaks that contract (a "full" node could limp along on disk), so the kubelet refuses to start unless swap is off (or you explicitly opt into the newer, still-niche swap support). On the exam: a node <code>NotReady</code> with the kubelet crash-looping → check <code>swapoff</code> first.</p></div>

                <h4>Step B — load kernel modules</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf   <span class="comment"># load on every boot</span>
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF
sudo modprobe overlay        <span class="comment"># overlay = the overlayfs storage driver containers use</span>
sudo modprobe br_netfilter   <span class="comment"># br_netfilter = lets iptables see bridged pod traffic</span>
lsmod | grep -E "overlay|br_netfilter"   <span class="comment"># verify both are loaded</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step C — sysctl for bridged pod traffic</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables  = 1   <span class="comment"># iptables sees IPv4 bridge traffic</span>
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1   <span class="comment"># …and IPv6</span>
net.ipv4.ip_forward                 = 1   <span class="comment"># route packets between pods/nodes</span>
EOF
sudo sysctl --system                       <span class="comment"># apply without a reboot</span>
sysctl net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables  <span class="comment"># → ... = 1</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why these two settings exist</h5><p>Pods talk over a Linux <strong>bridge</strong>. Without <code>bridge-nf-call-iptables=1</code>, packets crossing that bridge bypass iptables — so kube-proxy's Service rules and NetworkPolicies silently don't apply. Without <code>ip_forward=1</code>, the node won't route packets between pods and other nodes at all. Symptom of missing these: pods schedule but can't reach Services or each other.</p></div>

                <h4>Step D — containerd with the systemd cgroup driver</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · containerd</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo apt-get update &amp;&amp; sudo apt-get install -y containerd
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml   <span class="comment"># write a default config</span>

<span class="comment"># CRITICAL — switch the cgroup driver to systemd (must match the kubelet)</span>
sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/' /etc/containerd/config.toml

sudo systemctl restart containerd &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl enable containerd
sudo systemctl status containerd      <span class="comment"># Active: active (running)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 The #1 hidden "broken node": <code>SystemdCgroup</code> mismatch</h5><p>Modern Linux uses <strong>systemd</strong> to manage cgroups. The kubelet and the runtime <em>must agree</em> on the driver — both systemd. If containerd is left on the default <code>SystemdCgroup = false</code> (cgroupfs) while the kubelet uses systemd, you get two competing cgroup managers: pods fail to start, the kubelet logs cgroup errors, and the node flaps. This is a favourite exam trap because the node looks "almost" healthy. Always verify this one line.</p></div>

                <h4>Step E — install &amp; pin the toolchain</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo apt-get install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl gpg
<span class="comment"># add the Kubernetes apt repo (one minor-version stream, e.g. v1.34)</span>
curl -fsSL https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.34/deb/Release.key \
  | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg
echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg] \
  https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.34/deb/ /' \
  | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl   <span class="comment"># ← pin: an apt upgrade must NOT break the node</span>
kubeadm version                              <span class="comment"># kubeadm version: ...Major:"1", Minor:"34"...</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Why <code>apt-mark hold</code> matters</h5><p>Without a hold, a routine <code>apt upgrade</code> could jump the kubelet a minor version ahead of the control plane — violating the version-skew policy and breaking the node. To upgrade <em>deliberately</em> (Ch 23) you <code>unhold</code> → upgrade → <code>hold</code> again. The apt-repo URL is also <strong>per-minor</strong> (<code>…/v1.34/…</code>), so upgrading minors means editing that URL too.</p></div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">A</div><h5>Swap off</h5><p><code>swapoff -a</code> + fstab</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">B</div><h5>Modules</h5><p><code>overlay</code> · <code>br_netfilter</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">C</div><h5>Sysctl</h5><p>bridge-nf-call · ip_forward</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">D</div><h5>containerd</h5><p>SystemdCgroup = true</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">E</div><h5>Toolchain</h5><p>install + <code>hold</code></p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.3 Initialize the Control Plane</h3>
                <p>With every node prepared, run <code>kubeadm init</code> on the <strong>control-plane node only</strong>. This single command generates the cluster's entire PKI, writes the control-plane static-pod manifests, brings up etcd and the apiserver, and prints the command workers use to join. It's the most important command in cluster creation — and the exam expects you to know its key flags and what to do immediately after.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane only · kubeadm init</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo kubeadm init \
  --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 \        <span class="comment"># must match your CNI (Flannel uses this)</span>
  --apiserver-advertise-address=192.168.56.10 \  <span class="comment"># this node's IP</span>
  --kubernetes-version=v1.34.0

<span class="comment"># ── abbreviated output ──</span>
<span class="comment"># [init] Using Kubernetes version: v1.34.0</span>
<span class="comment"># [certs] Generating "ca", "apiserver", "etcd/ca", "etcd/server" …</span>
<span class="comment"># [kubeconfig] Writing "admin.conf", "kubelet.conf" …</span>
<span class="comment"># [control-plane] Creating static Pod manifests for apiserver/scheduler/controller-manager</span>
<span class="comment"># [etcd] Creating static Pod manifest for local etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># [mark-control-plane] Marking the node as control-plane</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Your Kubernetes control-plane has initialized successfully!</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># To start using your cluster:</span>
<span class="comment">#   mkdir -p $HOME/.kube</span>
<span class="comment">#   sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config</span>
<span class="comment">#   sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Then you can join any number of worker nodes by running:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm join 192.168.56.10:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1a2b3c…</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔧 <code>kubeadm init</code> flags — what each one does</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> --pod-network-cidr</div><div class="anatomy-value">the IP range pods get. <strong>Must match the CNI you'll install</strong> — Flannel expects <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>, Calico defaults to <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>. Mismatch = pods never get usable IPs.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📡</span> --apiserver-advertise-address</div><div class="anatomy-value">the IP the apiserver advertises to other nodes. Use the control-plane node's real (routable) IP, or workers can't reach <code>:6443</code>.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📌</span> --kubernetes-version</div><div class="anatomy-value">pin the exact version so all components match; otherwise kubeadm picks "latest stable," which may skew from your held packages.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔒</span> --control-plane-endpoint</div><div class="anatomy-value">for <strong>HA</strong>: a shared DNS name / load-balancer fronting all control-plane nodes. Set it even for a single CP if you might add more later (it can't be added afterwards without pain).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📤</span> --upload-certs</div><div class="anatomy-value">stores the control-plane certs in a Secret so extra CP nodes can join with <code>--control-plane</code> (HA). Covered in Ch 33.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Right after init — wire up <code>kubectl</code> (the 3 commands)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · as your normal user (not root)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config

kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME            STATUS     ROLES           AGE   VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># control-plane   NotReady   control-plane   30s   v1.34.0</span>
<span class="comment"># ↑ NotReady is EXPECTED here — there's no CNI yet (§9.4 fixes it)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 SAVE THE JOIN COMMAND — right now</h5><p>The <code>kubeadm join …</code> line printed at the end of <code>init</code> carries a <strong>bootstrap token that expires in 24 hours</strong>. Copy it somewhere safe immediately. Lost it (or it expired)? Regenerate any time with <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> (§9.5) — but on the exam clock, saving it up front is one less thing to fix.</p></div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what <code>kubeadm init</code> actually creates on disk</div>
<pre>
   sudo kubeadm init
     │
     ├─ 🔐 PKI  → /etc/kubernetes/pki/
     │     ca.crt / ca.key ................ the cluster Certificate Authority (root of trust)
     │     apiserver.crt ................... apiserver's TLS serving cert
     │     apiserver-kubelet-client.crt .... apiserver → kubelet auth
     │     etcd/ca.crt · etcd/server.crt ... etcd's own TLS
     │     sa.key / sa.pub ................. signs ServiceAccount tokens
     │
     ├─ 📋 kubeconfigs → /etc/kubernetes/
     │     admin.conf (cluster-admin) · kubelet.conf · controller-manager.conf · scheduler.conf
     │
     ├─ 📦 static-pod manifests → /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
     │     etcd.yaml · kube-apiserver.yaml · kube-controller-manager.yaml · kube-scheduler.yaml
     │
     └─ 🚀 starts everything
           kubelet (systemd) watches that manifests dir → brings up etcd + apiserver +
           scheduler + controller-manager as STATIC PODS; kube-proxy runs as a DaemonSet
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 This is why Ch 11 &amp; 12 work the way they do</h5><p>Everything above lives on the control-plane node's <em>filesystem</em>, managed by the kubelet — not by the scheduler. That's why you fix a broken apiserver by editing a <strong>file</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (Ch 12), and why an <strong>etcd snapshot</strong> (Ch 11) plus <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code> is enough to rebuild a control plane.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.4 Install a CNI Plugin (Pod Networking)</h3>
                <p>A fresh <code>kubeadm</code> cluster has <strong>no pod network</strong> — so every node sits <code>NotReady</code> and CoreDNS stays <code>Pending</code>. The <strong>CNI</strong> plugin is the missing piece: it gives each pod an IP, wires up pod-to-pod routing across nodes, and (depending on the plugin) enforces NetworkPolicies. Install exactly one, on the control plane, and the nodes flip to <code>Ready</code> within a minute.</p>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 KEY TERM — CNI (Container Network Interface)</h5><p>A <strong>standard plug-point</strong>: the kubelet calls a CNI plugin each time a pod is created, and the plugin sets up that pod's network namespace, IP, and routes. Kubernetes ships <em>no</em> built-in pod network on purpose — you choose a CNI that fits your needs. This is one of the three big "interfaces" (CRI for runtime, CNI for network, CSI for storage — §12.5).</p></div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>CNI plugin</th><th>What it's known for</th><th>NetworkPolicy?</th><th>Complexity</th><th>CKA choice?</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Calico</strong></td><td>BGP/IP-in-IP routing, mature policy engine</td><td>✅ full</td><td>🟡 medium</td><td>✅ the most common pick</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Flannel</strong></td><td>dead-simple VXLAN overlay</td><td>❌ none</td><td>🟢 low</td><td>✅ great for learning</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Cilium</strong></td><td>eBPF — high performance &amp; deep observability/security</td><td>✅ rich (L3-L7)</td><td>🔴 high</td><td>⚠️ advanced</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Weave Net</strong></td><td>simple encrypted mesh</td><td>✅ basic</td><td>🟢 low</td><td>⚠️ less common now</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · install ONE CNI</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Option A — Calico (default pod CIDR 192.168.0.0/16)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.28.0/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Option B — Flannel (expects --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 from init)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml

<span class="comment"># watch the nodes flip NotReady → Ready as the CNI pods come up</span>
kubectl get nodes -w
<span class="comment"># control-plane   NotReady → Ready   (≈30-60s after the CNI pods are Running)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">why nodes are NotReady until the CNI is up</div>
<pre>
   kubeadm init done            kubectl apply -f &lt;cni&gt;            CNI pods Running
   ┌──────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────┐            ┌──────────────────┐
   │ node: NotReady   │  ──▶    │ CNI DaemonSet     │   ──▶      │ node: Ready ✅    │
   │ CoreDNS: Pending │         │ lands on each node │            │ CoreDNS: Running │
   │ (no pod network) │         │ sets up CNI config │            │ pods get IPs     │
   └──────────────────┘         └──────────────────┘            └──────────────────┘
   the kubelet reports NotReady while its NetworkReady condition is false —
   i.e. until a CNI has written /etc/cni/net.d/ and its pods are healthy.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 The pod-CIDR must match</h5><p>The <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> you gave <code>kubeadm init</code> must match what the CNI expects (Flannel → <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>). Mismatch = pods get IPs the routing doesn't know about → cross-node pod traffic silently fails. With Calico's manifest you either use its default <code>192.168.0.0/16</code> or edit the manifest's CIDR to match your init value.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.5 Join Worker Nodes</h3>
                <p>With the control plane Ready, each prepared worker joins with the <code>kubeadm join</code> command <code>init</code> printed. The token authenticates the node to the apiserver; the <strong>CA cert hash</strong> lets the worker verify it's talking to the <em>real</em> control plane (not an impostor) before trusting it.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">each worker · kubeadm join</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo kubeadm join 192.168.56.10:6443 \
  --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1a2b3c…

<span class="comment"># ── output ──</span>
<span class="comment"># [preflight] Running pre-flight checks</span>
<span class="comment"># [kubelet-start] Starting the kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># This node has joined the cluster.</span>
<span class="comment"># → back on the control plane, the node appears and goes Ready once the CNI pod lands</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>🔑 Lost or expired the join command? Regenerate it</h4>
                <p>The bootstrap token expires after 24 hours. On the control plane, mint a fresh one — or rebuild the full command (token + correct CA hash) in a single shot:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · token recovery (memorise this)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># the one-liner that rebuilds the WHOLE join command (token + ca-cert-hash)</span>
kubeadm token create --print-join-command
<span class="comment"># kubeadm join 192.168.56.10:6443 --token 9z8y7x… --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1a2b3c…</span>

<span class="comment"># …or do it by hand:</span>
kubeadm token create                         <span class="comment"># a fresh token</span>
kubeadm token list                           <span class="comment"># see tokens + their TTLs</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt -noout -pubkey \
  | openssl rsa -pubin -outform DER 2&gt;/dev/null | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}'   <span class="comment"># the ca-cert-hash</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the complete kubeadm build — one flow, the order that matters</div>
<pre>
   ALL NODES (prep)                          swap off · overlay+br_netfilter · sysctl bridge+ip_forward
   ─────────────────                         · containerd (SystemdCgroup=true) · install kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl + hold
         │
         ▼   CONTROL PLANE only
   kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16   ─►  generates PKI + static-pod manifests + etcd
         │   then: mkdir ~/.kube · cp admin.conf · chown      (+ SAVE the join command!)
         ▼
   kubectl apply -f &lt;CNI&gt;   (Calico / Flannel)     ─►  nodes go Ready (NOT before this!)
         │
         ▼   EACH WORKER
   kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token &lt;t&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;h&gt;
         │   (lost it? kubeadm token create --print-join-command)
         ▼
   ✅ kubectl get nodes → all Ready   ─►  deploy easypj as the smoke test
   ⚠️ NotReady everywhere = no CNI · token expired = regenerate · swap on = init/join refuses
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div><h5>Workers show ROLES &lt;none&gt;</h5><p>Normal. Label one for cosmetics: <code>kubectl label node worker-1 node-role.kubernetes.io/worker=</code> — it changes nothing functionally.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧹</div><h5>Remove a worker cleanly</h5><p>From the CP: <code>kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code> → <code>kubectl delete node worker-1</code>. On the worker: <code>sudo kubeadm reset</code> to wipe its state.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why a CA cert hash, not just a token?</h5><p>The token proves the <em>node</em> to the apiserver. The <code>--discovery-token-ca-cert-hash</code> works the other way — it lets the joining node verify the apiserver's CA before sending credentials, defeating a man-in-the-middle. Skipping verification (<code>--discovery-token-unsafe-skip-ca-verification</code>) exists but is exactly as dangerous as it sounds.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.6 Verifying the Cluster</h3>
                <p>Once every node has joined, four checks confirm the cluster is genuinely operational — not just "up." The last one (a real scheduled pod) is the proof that scheduling, networking, and the runtime all work end-to-end.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · verify the cluster</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. All nodes Ready?</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME            STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION   INTERNAL-IP</span>
<span class="comment"># control-plane   Ready    control-plane   10m   v1.34.0   192.168.56.10</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1        Ready    &lt;none&gt;          5m    v1.34.0   192.168.56.11</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2        Ready    &lt;none&gt;          3m    v1.34.0   192.168.56.12</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. All system pods Running? (CNI pods, CoreDNS, control plane)</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># calico-node-xxxxx (one per node)        1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-… (x2)                          1/1   Running   ← Running, not Pending</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-control-plane                      1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-control-plane            1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-control-plane   1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-xxxxx (one per node)         1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-control-plane            1/1   Running</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Where is the API server?</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes control plane is running at https://192.168.56.10:6443</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. The real test — deploy easypj and confirm it schedules to a WORKER</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=2
kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-…-abcde   1/1   Running   worker-1   ← scheduled off the control plane ✅</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Healthy cluster checklist</h5><ul><li>all nodes <code>STATUS=Ready</code></li><li>every kube-system pod <code>Running</code></li><li>CoreDNS <code>Running</code> (not <code>Pending</code>)</li><li>a test pod schedules to a worker</li></ul></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div><h5>If something's off</h5><ul><li>node <code>NotReady</code> → kubelet down or CNI missing</li><li>CoreDNS <code>Pending</code> → CNI not installed</li><li>pods only on the CP → no workers joined</li><li><code>ImagePullBackOff</code> → registry/image (Ch 2)</li></ul></div>
                </div>
                <h4>🩺 kubeadm troubleshooting decision tree</h4>
                <p>When a freshly-built cluster misbehaves, follow the symptom down to the fix — this single tree resolves the vast majority of "my kubeadm cluster is broken" tasks:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">symptom → cause → fix</div>
<pre>
   kubectl get nodes
     │
     ├─ a node is NotReady ─────────────▶ on that node:
     │     ├─ CNI installed?        no ─▶ kubectl apply -f &lt;calico|flannel&gt;
     │     ├─ containerd running?   no ─▶ sudo systemctl restart containerd
     │     ├─ kubelet running?      no ─▶ journalctl -u kubelet  (swap? cgroup driver?)
     │     └─ swap off?             no ─▶ swapoff -a + fstab
     │
     ├─ kubectl can't connect ─────────▶
     │     ├─ kubeconfig set?       no ─▶ cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config
     │     ├─ apiserver running?    no ─▶ crictl ps -a | grep apiserver ; crictl logs (Ch 12)
     │     └─ firewall blocking 6443? yes ─▶ open 6443 between nodes (§9.8)
     │
     └─ pods stuck Pending ────────────▶
           ├─ workers joined?       no ─▶ kubeadm join (or token create --print-join-command)
           ├─ enough cpu/memory?    no ─▶ lower requests / add a node
           └─ taints blocking?     yes ─▶ tolerate or remove the taint (Ch 14)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.7 Practice Exercises</h3>
                <p>Building clusters is a <strong>muscle-memory</strong> skill — the exam gives you minutes, not hours. Drill these until Exercise 1 takes under 30 minutes and the recovery moves are reflex.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏋️</div><h5>1 · Build a 3-node cluster</h5><p>3 VMs → prereqs on all → <code>kubeadm init</code> → install Calico → <code>join</code> 2 workers → deploy easypj and verify. <strong>⏱️ target: 30 min.</strong></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div><h5>2 · Token recovery</h5><p>Assume the join token expired. Regenerate with <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> and join a fresh worker with it.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5>3 · Fix a broken node</h5><p>Deliberately break a worker (turn swap on, stop containerd, flip <code>SystemdCgroup=false</code>). Diagnose via <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> and restore it to <code>Ready</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>4 · Add a control-plane node</h5><p>Re-init with <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> + <code>--upload-certs</code>, then join a second CP with <code>--control-plane</code> (HA preview, Ch 33).</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>🧪 Capstone drill — rebuild from scratch in &lt; 30 minutes</h5><p>The single best Part-3 exercise: tear a cluster down with <code>kubeadm reset</code> on every node, then rebuild it end-to-end against the §9.5 flow without looking. When that's comfortable, the kubeadm exam questions become free marks.</p></div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Vagrant quick setup for practice</h5><p>The fastest way to get throwaway multi-node VMs locally: a <code>Vagrantfile</code> defining <code>control-plane</code> + <code>worker-1/2</code> on a private network → <code>vagrant up</code> → SSH in and run the prereqs + kubeadm. Cheaper than cloud and resets in seconds with <code>vagrant destroy</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🆘</div><h5>The 3-command debugging lifeline</h5><p>When <em>anything</em> in a kubeadm build is broken, these three answer 90% of it: <code>kubectl get nodes</code> (what's NotReady?) → <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> (which control-plane/CNI pod is unhealthy?) → <code>sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 50</code> (the node-level reason).</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.8 Required Ports (open these between nodes)</h3>
                <p>A cluster only works if its components can reach each other. On a real install (and in firewall-troubleshooting exam tasks) you must know which inbound ports each node type needs. A blocked port produces a <em>specific</em> failure — memorise the mapping and you can diagnose "joins fail" or "<code>kubectl logs</code> hangs" in seconds.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Port(s)</th><th>Component</th><th>Who connects</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td rowspan="5"><strong>Control&nbsp;plane</strong> (inbound)</td><td><code>6443</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td>every client, kubelet &amp; node</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>2379-2380</code></td><td>etcd: client (2379) + peer/Raft (2380)</td><td>apiserver → etcd; etcd ↔ etcd</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>10259</code></td><td>kube-scheduler</td><td>localhost (health/metrics)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>10257</code></td><td>kube-controller-manager</td><td>localhost (health/metrics)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>10250</code></td><td>kubelet API</td><td>control plane → kubelet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td rowspan="3"><strong>Worker</strong> (inbound)</td><td><code>10250</code></td><td>kubelet API</td><td>control plane → kubelet (logs/exec/metrics)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>10256</code></td><td>kube-proxy health</td><td>load balancers / health checks</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>30000-32767</code></td><td>NodePort Service range</td><td>external clients → NodePort Services</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">who dials whom — the ports that must be open</div>
<pre>
        🧑 kubectl / clients ───────────────6443──────────────▶ 📡 apiserver ─┐
                                                                              │ 2379-2380
   🤖 worker kubelet ◀──10250── apiserver        🗄️ etcd ◀────────────────────┘
   🌐 external user ──30000-32767──▶ 🔀 NodePort (worker)      📋 scheduler :10259  🔄 ctrl-mgr :10257
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   control-plane inbound: 6443 · 2379-2380 · 10259 · 10257 · 10250
   worker inbound:        10250 · 10256 · 30000-32767
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 Blocked port → exact symptom</h5><ul><li><strong>6443 blocked</strong> → <code>kubeadm join</code> fails &amp; <code>kubectl</code> can't reach the cluster ("connection refused" to <code>:6443</code>).</li><li><strong>10250 blocked</strong> → <code>kubectl logs</code>/<code>exec</code> hang and <code>kubectl top</code>/metrics fail (apiserver can't reach the kubelet).</li><li><strong>2379-2380 blocked</strong> → etcd can't form/keep quorum → apiserver errors, cluster effectively down.</li><li><strong>30000-32767 blocked</strong> → NodePort Services unreachable from outside.</li></ul></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — what <code>kubeadm init</code> actually does, phase by phase</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p><code>kubeadm init</code> isn't one action — it's an ordered pipeline of <strong>phases</strong>, each of which you can run (or skip) individually with <code>kubeadm init phase &lt;name&gt;</code>. Knowing the order explains every "init failed at step X" message and is the key to repairing a half-built control plane.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubeadm init
     ① preflight ............ check swap/cgroups/ports/CRI; pull images   (fails fast &amp; loud)
     ② certs ................ generate the PKI in /etc/kubernetes/pki  (CA, apiserver, etcd, sa keys)
     ③ kubeconfig .......... write admin/kubelet/controller-manager/scheduler .conf
     ④ etcd ................ render the local etcd static-pod manifest
     ⑤ control-plane ....... render apiserver + controller-manager + scheduler static-pod manifests
     ⑥ kubelet-start ....... write kubelet config + start the kubelet (it then runs the static pods)
     ⑦ upload-config ....... store kubeadm &amp; kubelet config in the kubeadm-config ConfigMap
     ⑧ upload-certs ........ (HA, optional) stash CP certs in a Secret for other CP nodes
     ⑨ mark-control-plane .. taint &amp; label this node as control-plane
     ⑩ bootstrap-token ..... create the join token + RBAC so nodes can bootstrap
     ⑪ kubelet-finalize .... point the kubelet at its rotated, cluster-issued cert
     ⑫ addons .............. deploy CoreDNS + kube-proxy
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why phases matter on the exam:</strong> a broken control plane rarely needs a full re-init. If certs expired you re-run just <code>kubeadm init phase certs</code> (or <code>kubeadm certs renew</code>); if the apiserver manifest is gone you regenerate just <code>kubeadm init phase control-plane apiserver</code>. And <code>kubeadm reset</code> undoes the lot. The phase list is also the troubleshooting order — an init that dies at "preflight" is swap/cgroup/port; one that dies later is usually certs or the kubelet.</div>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Zooming into phases ④–⑤ — etcd is the FIRST pod to start, and bootstraps the whole keyspace.</strong> The control plane has a strict start order because everything else needs a place to persist state. Watching it boot explains why a sick etcd makes the entire apiserver unavailable.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ④/⑤  kubelet sees the static-pod manifests in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ and starts them:
     1. kubelet reads etcd.yaml          → creates the etcd container FIRST
     2. etcd comes up as a SINGLE-NODE cluster (this CP node only)
     3. etcd reports healthy             → only NOW can kube-apiserver start
     4. apiserver connects to etcd (https://127.0.0.1:2379) and writes the INITIAL cluster state
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the first keys kubeadm writes into etcd (the skeleton every cluster starts with):
     /registry/namespaces/default
     /registry/namespaces/kube-system
     /registry/namespaces/kube-public
     /registry/namespaces/kube-node-lease
     /registry/serviceaccounts/kube-system/default
     /registry/configmaps/kube-public/cluster-info        ← how new nodes discover the CP
     /registry/secrets/kube-system/bootstrap-token-*       ← the join tokens (phase ⑩)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   later, when you `kubectl apply` the easypj Deployment, ITS objects land alongside these:
     /registry/deployments/easypj-prod/web
     /registry/pods/easypj-prod/web-<hash>
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why the order is non-negotiable:</strong> the apiserver is <em>stateless</em> — it keeps nothing locally; etcd is the single source of truth. So if etcd isn't healthy, the apiserver crash-loops on startup and <code>kubectl</code> returns <em>"connection refused"</em>. This is the deep reason "etcd down = cluster down," and why phase ④ (etcd) must succeed before phase ⑤ (the rest of the control plane). It also explains restore: you stop the apiserver, restore the etcd snapshot, and the apiserver comes back seeing the cluster exactly as of that snapshot.</div>

                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Beyond the basics</th><th>What to know (so you never need the docs)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>the kubeadm-config ConfigMap</strong></td><td>phase ⑦ stores your cluster's config in <code>kube-system/kubeadm-config</code>; upgrades &amp; new node joins read it. Inspect with <code>kubectl -n kube-system get cm kubeadm-config -o yaml</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>declarative <code>--config</code></strong></td><td>instead of flags, pass a <code>kubeadm init --config=cfg.yaml</code> (a <code>ClusterConfiguration</code> + <code>InitConfiguration</code>) — reproducible, version-controllable installs.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>TLS bootstrapping &amp; bootstrap tokens</strong></td><td>a joining kubelet uses the short-lived bootstrap token to submit a <strong>CSR</strong>; the cluster auto-approves it and issues the kubelet a real cert (which then auto-rotates). That's why a token, not a permanent credential, is enough to join.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong><code>--upload-certs</code> (HA)</strong></td><td>uploads CP certs (encrypted) so additional control-plane nodes join with <code>--control-plane --certificate-key …</code> instead of hand-copying <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>air-gapped installs</strong></td><td><code>kubeadm config images pull</code> pre-pulls every control-plane image; point it at a private registry with <code>--image-repository</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong><code>--ignore-preflight-errors</code> &amp; CRI socket</strong></td><td>bypass a specific preflight check when you know better; <code>--cri-socket unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code> when multiple runtimes are present.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Going deeper than the phase list — the PKI is one trust chain, and it explains everything.</strong> Phase ② doesn't just "make certs"; it builds a <strong>certificate hierarchy</strong> rooted in a self-signed cluster CA. Every component proves its identity with a cert that traces back to that root — which is why one CA can authenticate the whole cluster, and why an expired cert breaks things in predictable ways.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   /etc/kubernetes/pki/
   🔱 ca.crt + ca.key  ── the CLUSTER root CA (self-signed, valid 10 years) ── signs ▼
        ├─ apiserver.crt ................ apiserver's TLS serving cert (SANs: every cluster IP/DNS)
        ├─ apiserver-kubelet-client.crt . lets the apiserver authenticate TO each kubelet (logs/exec)
        └─ (kubelet client certs) ....... each kubelet's identity, auto-issued via the CSR flow
   🔱 etcd/ca.crt ───── a SEPARATE CA just for etcd ── signs ▼
        ├─ etcd/server.crt ............. etcd's serving + peer cert (:2379 client, :2380 peer)
        └─ apiserver-etcd-client.crt ... lets the apiserver authenticate to etcd
   🔑 sa.key / sa.pub ─ NOT a cert — the keypair that SIGNS &amp; verifies ServiceAccount tokens
   front-proxy-ca.crt ─ a third CA for the aggregation layer (extension apiservers, metrics-server)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this is the "to the core" view:</strong> there are <em>two</em> CAs on purpose — compromising the etcd CA shouldn't let you mint apiserver certs, and vice-versa. The certs default to a <strong>1-year</strong> life (the CA lasts 10) — that's why <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code>/<code>renew all</code> exist and why a <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (which auto-renews) keeps long-lived clusters healthy. And because identity is <em>entirely</em> these files, a working control plane can be rebuilt from just <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code> + an etcd snapshot — lose the PKI and even a perfect etcd backup can't re-establish trust. This is the deep reason Ch 11 (etcd) and the PKI together <em>are</em> the cluster.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Prep every node first:</strong> swap off · <code>overlay</code>+<code>br_netfilter</code> · sysctl bridge+ip_forward · containerd <code>SystemdCgroup=true</code> · install + <code>hold</code> the toolchain.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubeadm init</code> on the control plane</strong> → copy <code>admin.conf</code> to <code>~/.kube/config</code> → <strong>save the join command</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Install a CNI</strong> — nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> and CoreDNS <code>Pending</code> until you do.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubeadm join</code> the workers</strong> with token + ca-cert-hash; regenerate with <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ports:</strong> CP needs 6443 · 2379-2380 · 10259/10257 · 10250; workers need 10250 · 10256 · 30000-32767.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Swap on.</strong> <code>kubeadm init/join</code> refuses outright — <code>swapoff -a</code> + comment fstab.</li>
                    <li><strong>No CNI.</strong> All nodes <code>NotReady</code> after init is <em>expected</em> until you apply a network plugin — not a kubelet bug.</li>
                    <li><strong>cgroup-driver mismatch.</strong> containerd <code>SystemdCgroup=false</code> vs systemd kubelet → pods won't start; the subtlest "broken node."</li>
                    <li><strong>Expired join token.</strong> Tokens last 24h — regenerate, don't panic.</li>
                    <li><strong>pod-CIDR mismatch.</strong> <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> must match the CNI's expectation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgot <code>apt-mark hold</code>.</strong> A later <code>apt upgrade</code> skews the kubelet and breaks the node.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> rebuilds a lost join in one line.</li>
                    <li><code>kubeadm config images pull</code> before <code>init</code> = faster init (and works air-gapped).</li>
                    <li><code>kubeadm reset -f</code> on a node to undo everything and start clean.</li>
                    <li><code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> is your first stop for any "node won't come up."</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 9)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the kubeadm toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 --apiserver-advertise-address=&lt;ip&gt;
kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token &lt;t&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;h&gt;
kubeadm token create --print-join-command     <span class="comment"># regenerate a lost join</span>
kubeadm config images pull                    <span class="comment"># pre-pull control-plane images</span>
kubeadm reset -f                              <span class="comment"># undo init/join on a node</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration ; kubeadm certs renew all   <span class="comment"># cert lifecycle</span>
systemctl status kubelet ; journalctl -u kubelet -f        <span class="comment"># the node agent</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide ; kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — bootstrap a control plane &amp; join a worker (from memory)</h5>
                <p>No peeking — this is the core CKA build:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>On all nodes: disable swap, load modules, set sysctls, install containerd with <code>SystemdCgroup=true</code>, install &amp; hold the toolchain.</li>
                    <li>On the CP: <code>kubeadm init</code> with a pod-CIDR, then configure <code>~/.kube/config</code>.</li>
                    <li>Install a CNI and confirm the node goes <code>Ready</code>.</li>
                    <li>Regenerate the join command and join one worker; verify <code>get nodes</code> shows both Ready.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># every node</span>
sudo swapoff -a; sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab
sudo modprobe overlay br_netfilter
echo -e "net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables=1\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=1" | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf; sudo sysctl --system
sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/' /etc/containerd/config.toml; sudo systemctl restart containerd

<span class="comment"># control plane</span>
sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16
mkdir -p ~/.kube; sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config; sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) ~/.kube/config
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml
kubeadm token create --print-join-command      <span class="comment"># copy this</span>

<span class="comment"># worker (paste the printed join command)</span>
sudo kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token &lt;t&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;h&gt;
kubectl get nodes        <span class="comment"># both Ready ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 9 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">kubeadm build &amp; recovery recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Disable swap permanently on a node (commands).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">swap off</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo swapoff -a                            <span class="comment"># now</span>
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab   <span class="comment"># and across reboots</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>swapoff -a</code> only lasts until reboot; commenting the <code>swap</code> line in <code>/etc/fstab</code> makes it stick. The kubelet refuses to start with swap on (unless you explicitly enable the newer swap feature-gate).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the 3 commands you run right after <code>kubeadm init</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">configure kubectl</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>They copy the cluster-admin kubeconfig (<code>admin.conf</code>) into your home dir so <code>kubectl</code> works as your normal user. Run them as that user, not root. (And immediately save the printed join command.)</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">All nodes are <code>NotReady</code> right after init — most likely cause and fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>No CNI installed.</strong> The kubelet's <code>NetworkReady</code> condition is false until a pod-network plugin is applied. Fix: <code>kubectl apply -f &lt;calico|flannel&gt;</code>; nodes go Ready in ~30-60s.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is <em>expected</em> behaviour, not a bug — kubeadm intentionally ships no pod network. CoreDNS staying <code>Pending</code> is the corroborating symptom.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The join token expired — add a new worker anyway (commands).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane → then worker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubeadm token create --print-join-command   <span class="comment"># CP: prints a fresh full join command</span>
<span class="comment"># on the worker, paste it:</span>
sudo kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token &lt;new&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;h&gt;</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--print-join-command</code> bundles a new token with the correct CA hash in one step — far faster than minting a token and computing the hash by hand.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why must <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> match the CNI's expectation?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because the CNI hands pods IPs from, and programs routes for, a specific CIDR. If kubeadm advertises one range and the CNI uses another, pods get IPs the routing fabric doesn't know about → cross-node pod traffic silently fails.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Flannel expects <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>; Calico defaults to <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>. Either pass the matching <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> at init or edit the CNI manifest's CIDR.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The kubelet is crash-looping on a fresh node — where do you look?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager   <span class="comment"># the actual error</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Top suspects in order: swap still on, containerd <code>SystemdCgroup</code> mismatch, container runtime down, or a bad <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>. The journal names which one.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which kernel modules &amp; sysctls are required, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Modules: <code>overlay</code> (overlayfs container storage) and <code>br_netfilter</code> (so iptables sees bridged traffic). Sysctls: <code>net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables=1</code> (kube-proxy/NetworkPolicy apply to bridged pod traffic) and <code>net.ipv4.ip_forward=1</code> (route packets between pods/nodes).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Without them, pods may schedule but can't reach Services or other nodes — a confusing "networking just doesn't work" failure.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Cleanly remove a worker (drain + delete + reset).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">CP, then the worker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data   <span class="comment"># evict workloads</span>
kubectl delete node worker-1                                        <span class="comment"># remove from the API</span>
<span class="comment"># on worker-1 itself:</span>
sudo kubeadm reset -f                                               <span class="comment"># wipe cluster state</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Drain first so pods reschedule elsewhere; delete removes the node object; <code>reset</code> cleans certs/manifests/CNI config off the machine so it could rejoin fresh.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which ports must be open between the control plane and workers?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Control plane (inbound):</strong> <code>6443</code> (apiserver), <code>2379-2380</code> (etcd), <code>10259</code> (scheduler), <code>10257</code> (controller-manager), <code>10250</code> (kubelet). <strong>Workers (inbound):</strong> <code>10250</code> (kubelet), <code>10256</code> (kube-proxy), <code>30000-32767</code> (NodePort).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Memorise by symptom: 6443 down = joins/kubectl fail; 10250 down = logs/exec/metrics fail; 2379-2380 down = etcd loses quorum.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Pin the toolchain so an <code>apt upgrade</code> can't break the node.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pin versions</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl
apt-mark showhold        <span class="comment"># verify they're held</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A hold freezes the package version so a routine upgrade can't skew the kubelet ahead of the control plane. To upgrade deliberately (Ch 23): <code>unhold</code> → upgrade → <code>hold</code> again.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 9 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style "build it / fix it" tasks with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubeadm init</code> and the initialization fails with: <em>"[ERROR Swap]: running with swap on is not supported."</em> What do you do?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">disable swap immediately</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Disable swap immediately</span>
sudo swapoff -a

<span class="comment"># Disable swap permanently (survives reboot)</span>
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab

<span class="comment"># Verify swap is off</span>
free -h
<span class="comment"># Swap line should show zeros: 0B  0B  0B</span>

<span class="comment"># Now retry kubeadm init</span>
sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Kubernetes requires swap to be disabled because the scheduler's resource calculations assume physical memory only. If pods swap to disk, performance becomes unpredictable and memory limits lose meaning. <code>swapoff -a</code> disables it immediately, but editing <code>/etc/fstab</code> makes it permanent across reboots. This is a common CKA pre-requisite step. If you see any error during <code>kubeadm init</code>, always check the <code>[preflight]</code> section — it lists exactly what failed (swap, ports, container runtime, etc.).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">After running <code>kubeadm init</code>, the output prints three important commands. What are they and why is each critical?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">command 1: set up kubeconfig for the current user</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Command 1: Set up kubeconfig for the current user</span>
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config

<span class="comment"># Command 2: Install a CNI plugin (e.g., Calico)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Command 3: Join worker nodes (unique per init)</span>
kubeadm join 192.168.1.10:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef \
    --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:abc123...</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Step</th>
                                            <th>Why Critical</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>kubeconfig setup</td>
                                            <td>Without it, <code>kubectl</code> can't authenticate to the API server</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>CNI plugin</td>
                                            <td>Nodes stay <strong>NotReady</strong> until pod networking is installed</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Join command</td>
                                            <td>Workers need the token + CA hash to securely join the cluster</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>These three steps complete the cluster setup. The kubeconfig file at <code>~/.kube/config</code> contains the cluster endpoint, client certificate, and CA certificate — this is how kubectl authenticates. The CNI (Container Network Interface) plugin provides pod-to-pod networking — without it, pods can't communicate and nodes report NotReady. The join token is valid for 24 hours by default. If it expires: <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> generates a new one.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">All worker nodes show <code>NotReady</code> status. The control plane node is also <code>NotReady</code>. What is the most likely cause?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">most likely cause: no cni plugin installed</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Most likely cause: No CNI plugin installed</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify: check node conditions</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 Conditions
<span class="comment"># Look for: NetworkReady=false, "network plugin is not ready"</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Install a CNI plugin</span>
kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Or for Flannel:</span>
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/flannel-io/flannel/master/Documentation/kube-flannel.yml

<span class="comment"># Verify nodes become Ready (may take 30-60 seconds)</span>
kubectl get nodes -w

<span class="comment"># Other causes of NotReady:</span>
<span class="comment"># - kubelet not running: systemctl status kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># - Container runtime down: systemctl status containerd</span>
<span class="comment"># - Node resource pressure: kubectl describe node | grep Conditions</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>If <strong>all</strong> nodes are NotReady, the cause is almost always a missing CNI plugin. The kubelet reports <code>Ready</code> only when the container runtime AND network plugin are both operational. A single node being NotReady suggests node-specific issues (kubelet crash, disk pressure, memory pressure). On the CKA, always check if CNI is deployed: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -i calico\|flannel\|weave</code>. No CNI pods = no networking = all NotReady.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">A worker node failed to join the cluster. The <code>kubeadm join</code> command returns: <em>"token has expired"</em>. How do you generate a new join command from the control plane node?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the control plane node</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># On the control plane node:</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 1: Create new token and print full join command</span>
kubeadm token create --print-join-command
<span class="comment"># Output: kubeadm join 10.0.0.10:6443 --token abc123.xyz789 \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:...</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: List existing tokens</span>
kubeadm token list
<span class="comment"># TOKEN                     TTL         EXPIRES</span>
<span class="comment"># abcdef.0123456789abcdef   23h         2024-01-02T10:00:00Z</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Create token with custom TTL</span>
kubeadm token create --ttl 2h --print-join-command

<span class="comment"># Then on the worker node, run the printed join command:</span>
sudo kubeadm join 10.0.0.10:6443 --token &lt;new-token&gt; \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt;</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Bootstrap tokens expire after 24 hours by default. The <code>--print-join-command</code> flag is essential — it outputs the complete command including the token AND the CA cert hash. Without the hash, the worker can't verify it's connecting to the legitimate API server (preventing MITM attacks). On the CKA, if a question says "add a worker node," start with <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> on the control plane, then run the output on the worker.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> flag in <code>kubeadm init</code>, and what happens if you choose a CIDR that overlaps with your node network?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the pod-network-cidr defines the ip range for all…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The pod-network-cidr defines the IP range for ALL pods in the cluster</span>
kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16  <span class="comment"># Flannel default</span>
kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=192.168.0.0/16 <span class="comment"># Calico default</span>

<span class="comment"># Common CIDR allocations:</span>
<span class="comment"># Node network:    10.0.0.0/24  (physical/VM IPs)</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod network:     10.244.0.0/16 (pod IPs)</span>
<span class="comment"># Service network: 10.96.0.0/12  (ClusterIP range, default)</span>

<span class="comment"># ❌ OVERLAP PROBLEM:</span>
<span class="comment"># Node network: 10.244.1.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod CIDR:     10.244.0.0/16   ← OVERLAPS!</span>
<span class="comment"># Result: routing breaks — traffic to pods goes to nodes instead</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> tells the CNI plugin which IP range to assign to pods. Each node gets a subnet from this range (e.g., node1 gets 10.244.0.0/24, node2 gets 10.244.1.0/24). If the pod CIDR overlaps with your node IPs, the kernel can't distinguish between pod and node traffic — routing tables conflict, and networking breaks silently. On the CKA, the CIDR is usually provided in the question. Use exactly what they specify. Most CNI plugins also require the CIDR to match their default (Flannel = 10.244.0.0/16, Calico = 192.168.0.0/16).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The kubelet on a worker node is not starting. <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> shows it's "activating (auto-restart)". How do you diagnose this?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check kubelet logs</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kubelet logs</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 50
<span class="comment"># or follow in real-time:</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -f

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Common errors and fixes:</span>

<span class="comment"># Error: "failed to load kubelet config file"</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Check /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml exists and is valid</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml

<span class="comment"># Error: "container runtime is not running"</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Start containerd</span>
sudo systemctl start containerd
sudo systemctl enable containerd

<span class="comment"># Error: "unable to load client CA file"</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Node hasn't joined yet or certs are corrupted</span>
<span class="comment"># Re-join: kubeadm reset && kubeadm join ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify container runtime</span>
crictl info
sudo systemctl status containerd

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kubelet is enabled at boot</span>
sudo systemctl enable kubelet</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>kubelet crash-looping means it starts, encounters an error, exits, and systemd restarts it. <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> is your primary tool — the error message tells you exactly what's wrong. The three most common causes: (1) container runtime not running (containerd/CRI-O), (2) kubelet config file missing or corrupt, (3) node hasn't been joined to a cluster yet. On the CKA, always check in this order: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> → <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> → <code>systemctl status containerd</code>. This pattern solves 90% of node failures.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are the kernel modules and system settings required before installing Kubernetes with kubeadm? List the prerequisites.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">load required kernel modules</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Load required kernel modules</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF
sudo modprobe overlay
sudo modprobe br_netfilter

<span class="comment"># 2. Set required sysctl parameters</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables  = 1
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1
net.ipv4.ip_forward                 = 1
EOF
sudo sysctl --system

<span class="comment"># 3. Disable swap</span>
sudo swapoff -a
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab

<span class="comment"># 4. Install container runtime (containerd)</span>
sudo apt-get install -y containerd
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml
<span class="comment"># Enable SystemdCgroup = true in config.toml</span>
sudo systemctl restart containerd

<span class="comment"># 5. Install kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl</span>
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm kubelet kubectl
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm kubelet kubectl</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>These prerequisites ensure the kernel can handle container networking. <code>br_netfilter</code> allows iptables to see bridged traffic (pod networking). <code>overlay</code> enables the overlay filesystem for container images. <code>ip_forward=1</code> allows packet forwarding between interfaces. <code>apt-mark hold</code> prevents accidental upgrades of kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl during <code>apt upgrade</code> — version skew between components breaks clusters. On the CKA, these are usually pre-configured, but understanding them helps troubleshoot: if pod networking breaks, check that <code>br_netfilter</code> is loaded and <code>bridge-nf-call-iptables = 1</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you completely tear down a Kubernetes cluster installed with kubeadm and start fresh?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on each node (workers first, then control plane)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># On EACH node (workers first, then control plane):</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Reset kubeadm (removes certs, configs, etcd data)</span>
sudo kubeadm reset -f

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Clean up networking</span>
sudo rm -rf /etc/cni/net.d
sudo iptables -F && sudo iptables -t nat -F && sudo iptables -t mangle -F
sudo iptables -X

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Remove kubeconfig</span>
rm -rf $HOME/.kube

<span class="comment"># Step 4: (Optional) Stop kubelet and containerd</span>
sudo systemctl stop kubelet
sudo systemctl stop containerd

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Clean up leftover data</span>
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/etcd
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/kubelet
sudo rm -rf /etc/kubernetes

<span class="comment"># Then re-initialize:</span>
sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubeadm reset</code> undoes what <code>kubeadm init</code> or <code>kubeadm join</code> did — it removes TLS certificates, the local etcd data member, the kubelet config, and the admin kubeconfig. However, it does NOT clean up CNI config or iptables rules — those linger and can cause issues on re-init. On the CKA, you typically won't need to reset, but knowing this helps if you severely break something. Always reset workers BEFORE the control plane to avoid orphan node objects.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What ports must be open on control plane nodes vs worker nodes for a Kubernetes cluster to function? List the components and their ports.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Component</th>
                                            <th>Port</th>
                                            <th>Where</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>API Server</td>
                                            <td><code>6443</code></td>
                                            <td>Control Plane</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>etcd</td>
                                            <td><code>2379-2380</code></td>
                                            <td>Control Plane</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Scheduler</td>
                                            <td><code>10259</code></td>
                                            <td>Control Plane</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Controller Manager</td>
                                            <td><code>10257</code></td>
                                            <td>Control Plane</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Kubelet</td>
                                            <td><code>10250</code></td>
                                            <td>All Nodes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>NodePort Services</td>
                                            <td><code>30000-32767</code></td>
                                            <td>All Nodes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Port conflicts are a common cause of cluster initialization failures. If a firewall blocks port 6443, workers can't reach the API server. If port 2379 is in use, etcd won't start. If 10250 is blocked, the API server can't reach kubelet (which means <code>kubectl exec</code> and <code>kubectl logs</code> fail). On the CKA exam, firewalls are usually pre-configured, but understanding ports helps with troubleshooting: <code>ss -tlnp | grep 6443</code> checks if the API server is listening, <code>nc -zv &lt;control-plane-ip&gt; 6443</code> tests connectivity from a worker.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to set up a cluster with a specific Kubernetes version (v1.29.0). How do you specify the version during installation and ensure all components match?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">install specific versions of kubeadm, kubelet</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Install specific versions of kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl</span>
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.29.0-1.1 kubelet=1.29.0-1.1 kubectl=1.29.0-1.1
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm kubelet kubectl

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Initialize with version flag</span>
sudo kubeadm init --kubernetes-version=v1.29.0 \
  --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify versions match</span>
kubectl version --short
<span class="comment"># Client Version: v1.29.0</span>
<span class="comment"># Server Version: v1.29.0</span>

kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           STATUS   VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># control-plane  Ready    v1.29.0</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1       Ready    v1.29.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Check component versions</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pods -o custom-columns=\
'NAME:.metadata.name,IMAGE:.spec.containers[0].image' | grep kube
<span class="comment"># All images should show v1.29.0</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Version consistency is critical — Kubernetes supports a maximum skew of one minor version between components. kubelet can be one minor version behind the API server, but never ahead. The <code>apt-mark hold</code> command prevents <code>apt upgrade</code> from changing versions. On the CKA, the exam environment usually has specific versions pre-installed, but upgrade questions require you to install exact versions. Always verify with <code>kubectl version</code> and <code>kubeadm version</code> after installation.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 9 Visual Summary — bare nodes to a verified cluster, one flow</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ EVERY NODE (prep)   swapoff · overlay+br_netfilter · sysctl · containerd(SystemdCgroup=true) · kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl+hold
        │
        ▼  CONTROL PLANE
   🔧 kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=…  ─►  PKI + static-pod manifests + etcd + join token
        │   mkdir ~/.kube · cp admin.conf · chown      (SAVE the join command!)
        ▼
   🌐 kubectl apply -f &lt;CNI&gt;   ─►  nodes flip NotReady → Ready, CoreDNS Runs
        │
        ▼  EACH WORKER
   🔗 kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token … --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash …   (lost? token create --print-join-command)
        ▼
   ✅ kubectl get nodes → all Ready  ─►  deploy easypj as the smoke test
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ports: CP 6443·2379-2380·10259·10257·10250 │ worker 10250·10256·30000-32767     ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> you prepare <em>every</em> node identically (swap off, kernel modules, sysctls, containerd on the systemd cgroup driver, the held toolchain). On the control plane, <code>kubeadm init</code> generates the PKI, writes the static-pod manifests, starts etcd, and prints a join command — then you wire up <code>kubectl</code> and <strong>save that join command</strong>. Nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> until you apply a <strong>CNI</strong>; then workers <code>kubeadm join</code> with a token + CA hash.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the order is the exam — <strong>prep all → init CP → install CNI → join workers → verify</strong> — and the two recovery reflexes that save you are <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> (lost join) and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> (broken node). Get the right firewall ports open and the cluster simply works.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH9-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 9 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 10 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch10">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 10 · Cluster Configuration &amp; kubeconfig</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">A running cluster is a <em>configured</em> cluster. This chapter is where you learn to bend Kubernetes to requirements — read and merge <strong>kubeconfig</strong>, tune the <strong>apiserver</strong> and <strong>kubelet</strong> by editing their config, enable <strong>admission controllers</strong>, and navigate <strong>API groups</strong> — and, just as importantly, how to recover when an edit breaks the control plane.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Heavily Tested</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⚙️ Configuration</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Read a <strong>kubeconfig</strong> to the bottom — clusters, users, contexts, and how files merge</li>
                    <li>✅ Manage many clusters from one machine and switch safely between them</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure the <strong>kube-apiserver</strong> via its static-pod manifest (and recover a bad edit)</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure the <strong>kubelet</strong> via <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> + its systemd unit</li>
                    <li>✅ Understand &amp; enable <strong>admission controllers</strong> — where they run and why order matters</li>
                    <li>✅ Navigate <strong>API groups &amp; versioning</strong> — core vs named groups</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">apiserver/kubelet config <span class="cka-weight">CORE TASK</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 The one habit that saves your cluster</h5>
                <p>Configuring the control plane means <strong>editing files</strong> — and a single typo in <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> takes the apiserver (and your <code>kubectl</code>) offline. The exam loves this scenario.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Always back up first:</strong> <code>sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/</code> before any edit.</li>
                    <li><strong>The kubelet auto-restarts</strong> the component when you save the manifest — no <code>kubectl</code> needed (which is good, because it may be down).</li>
                    <li><strong>Recovery = fix the file.</strong> If the apiserver won't come back, your edit is wrong — restore from <code>/tmp</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 10 Roadmap — read config, tune the control plane, recover safely</div>
<pre>
   10.1 kubeconfig ──▶ 10.2 multi-cluster ──▶ 10.3 apiserver config ──▶ 10.4 kubelet config
   clusters·users·         get/use-context        edit the static-pod        /var/lib/kubelet
   contexts·merge          KUBECONFIG merge        manifest (recover!)        config.yaml + unit
        │                                                                          │
        └──────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────-┘
                                            ▼
   10.5 admission controllers ──▶ 10.6 API groups &amp; versioning
   authN→authZ→mutating→validating   core /api/v1 vs named /apis/grp/ver
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-1"><strong>10.1</strong> kubeconfig deep dive</a></td><td>How does kubectl know where/who/scope?</td><td>clusters (where) + users (who) + contexts (combo); files merge by <code>KUBECONFIG</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-2"><strong>10.2</strong> Multiple clusters</a></td><td>How do I juggle many clusters safely?</td><td><code>get/use/set-context</code>; merge precedence; verify before acting.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-3"><strong>10.3</strong> API server config</a></td><td>How do I change apiserver behaviour?</td><td>edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> — back up first!</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-4"><strong>10.4</strong> kubelet config</a></td><td>How do I configure the node agent?</td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> + systemd unit; <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-5"><strong>10.5</strong> Admission controllers</a></td><td>What gates a request after authZ?</td><td>mutating <em>then</em> validating; enable via <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-6"><strong>10.6</strong> API groups &amp; versioning</a></td><td>Where does each kind live in the API?</td><td>core <code>/api/v1</code> vs named <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;ver&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.1 ─────────── -->

            <section class="section-block" id="s10-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.1 kubeconfig — the Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>You met kubeconfig in Ch 5; now we read it to the bottom. A kubeconfig is three lists plus a pointer: <strong>clusters</strong> (where to connect), <strong>users</strong> (who you are), and <strong>contexts</strong> (a named pairing of cluster + user + default namespace), with <code>current-context</code> selecting the active one. Every field below is something the exam can ask you to read or change.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the three lists, joined by a context</div>
<pre>
   ~/.kube/config
   ├─ clusters[]   WHERE   { server: https://10.0.0.1:6443 , certificate-authority(-data): CA }
   ├─ users[]      WHO     { client-certificate(-data)+client-key(-data) | token | exec: &lt;plugin&gt; }
   └─ contexts[]   COMBO   { cluster: prod , user: admin , namespace: easypj-prod }
   current-context: prod   ◀── kubectl acts as THIS (cluster + user + namespace) until you switch
        🖥️ Cluster (WHERE)  +  👤 User (WHO)  +  📂 Namespace (SCOPE)  =  📋 Context
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">~/.kube/config — fully annotated</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
current-context: easypj-prod          <span class="comment"># the active context</span>
clusters:
- name: easypj-prod
  cluster:
    server: https://10.0.0.1:6443      <span class="comment"># WHERE — apiserver URL</span>
    certificate-authority-data: LS0t…  <span class="comment"># the cluster CA (base64) — verifies the server</span>
users:
- name: admin
  user:
    client-certificate-data: LS0t…     <span class="comment"># WHO — your client cert (base64)…</span>
    client-key-data: LS0t…             <span class="comment"># …and its private key</span>
    <span class="comment"># OR a token:        token: eyJhbGci…</span>
    <span class="comment"># OR an exec plugin: exec: { command: aws, args: [eks, get-token, …] }  (cloud auth)</span>
contexts:
- name: easypj-prod
  context:
    cluster: easypj-prod               <span class="comment"># ties a cluster…</span>
    user: admin                        <span class="comment"># …to a user…</span>
    namespace: easypj-prod             <span class="comment"># …and a default namespace</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Holds</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>clusters</code></td><td>server URL + CA cert</td><td><strong>WHERE</strong> do I connect?</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>users</code></td><td>client cert/key, token, or exec plugin</td><td><strong>WHO</strong> am I?</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>contexts</code></td><td>cluster + user + namespace</td><td>the <strong>COMBO</strong> I switch to</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📋 kubeconfig — field by field</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📍</span> clusters[].server</div><div class="anatomy-value">the apiserver URL — <code>https://&lt;ip&gt;:6443</code> (6443 is the default API port)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔐</span> clusters[].certificate-authority(-data)</div><div class="anatomy-value">the cluster CA — kubectl uses it to <em>verify the apiserver's</em> TLS cert (defeats a spoofed server)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👤</span> users[].client-certificate(-data) + client-key</div><div class="anatomy-value">your client cert + key — the apiserver verifies these to <em>authenticate you</em> (CN→username, O→group)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎟️</span> users[].token / exec</div><div class="anatomy-value">an alternative identity — a bearer token (ServiceAccounts, OIDC) or an exec plugin (cloud auth)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔗</span> contexts[].cluster + user + namespace</div><div class="anatomy-value">a named triple binding a cluster to a user, with an optional default namespace</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⭐</span> current-context</div><div class="anatomy-value">which context is active now — changed with <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Embedded vs file-path credentials</h5><p>The <code>-data</code> suffix means the cert/CA is <strong>base64-embedded</strong> in the file; without it (<code>certificate-authority: /path/ca.crt</code>) it's a <em>file reference</em>. kubeadm's <code>admin.conf</code> embeds everything (portable); Minikube uses file paths (so the file is short but not self-contained). Decode an embedded cert with <code>… | base64 -d | openssl x509 -noout -text</code>.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.2 Managing Multiple Clusters</h3>
                <p>Admins juggle several clusters from one laptop — exactly like the exam's ~6 contexts. Four commands cover everything, and the discipline is always the same: <strong>switch → verify → act</strong>.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">context management</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config get-contexts                 <span class="comment"># list all (★ = current)</span>
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME          CLUSTER      AUTHINFO   NAMESPACE</span>
<span class="comment"># *         easypj-prod   easypj-prod  admin      easypj-prod</span>
<span class="comment">#           easypj-dev    easypj-dev   dev-user   easypj-dev</span>

kubectl config use-context easypj-dev       <span class="comment"># SWITCH</span>
kubectl config current-context             <span class="comment"># verify → easypj-dev</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj   <span class="comment"># change default ns</span>
kubectl config view --minify               <span class="comment"># show ONLY the active context (cluster/user/ns)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> merges files (precedence)</div>
<pre>
   export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/work/dev.yaml:~/work/prod.yaml
                         │              │             │
                         └── highest ──┘  (on a name clash, the FIRST file wins)
   kubectl config get-contexts   ─►  shows the UNION of contexts from all files
   kubectl config view --flatten &gt; merged && mv merged ~/.kube/config   ─►  bake the merge into one file
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ⚠️ a stray $KUBECONFIG in your shell silently overrides ~/.kube/config — the #1 "where's my cluster?" bug
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Precedence, top to bottom</h5><p><code>--kubeconfig=FILE</code> (flag) &gt; <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> (colon-separated list, first-wins on clashes) &gt; <code>~/.kube/config</code> (default). <code>--flatten</code> resolves a merged set into one self-contained file — handy after combining a teammate's config.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.3 API Server Configuration</h3>
                <p>The kube-apiserver is configured by its <strong>static-pod manifest</strong> at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. There is no "apiserver config UI" — you edit that file's <code>command:</code> flags, save, and the kubelet (which watches the directory) restarts the apiserver pod with the new flags. Powerful, and dangerous: a typo means the apiserver — and your <code>kubectl</code> — won't come back.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">control plane — who talks to whom (the apiserver is the hub)</div>
<pre>
                              CONTROL PLANE NODE
   ┌──────────────────┐                          ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ 📋 kube-scheduler │──────HTTPS :6443────────▶│ 📡 kube-apiserver         │
   │ "where should     │                          │  the CENTRAL hub —        │
   │  this pod run?"   │                          │  EVERY component talks    │
   └──────────────────┘                          │  through me               │
   ┌──────────────────┐                          │                           │
   │ 🔄 controller-mgr │──────HTTPS :6443────────▶│  :6443 HTTPS API          │
   │ "actual == desired?"                         └─────────────┬─────────────┘
   └──────────────────┘                                        │ HTTPS :2379
   ┌──────────────────┐                                        ▼
   │ 🤖 kubelet (nodes)│──────HTTPS :6443────────▶      ┌──────────────┐
   └──────────────────┘                                  │ 🗄️ etcd       │  ONLY the apiserver
   🧑 kubectl ─────────────HTTPS :6443──────────▶         │ :2379 client  │  talks to etcd
                                                          │ :2380 peer    │
                                                          └──────────────┘
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   it's a STAR: scheduler, controller-manager, kubelets &amp; kubectl all go through the apiserver;
   the apiserver alone reads/writes etcd. Configure the hub → you configure how the cluster behaves.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how an apiserver flag change propagates</div>
<pre>
   you edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml   (add/change a --flag)
        │  save
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet (watching that dir) notices the change
        │  kills the old apiserver static pod, starts a new one with the new flags
        ▼
   📡 new kube-apiserver running   ──►  kubectl works again   (≈15-40s)
   ⚠️ bad flag / YAML typo → the new pod crash-loops → apiserver down → kubectl "connection refused"
      RECOVER: fix the file (or restore your /tmp backup); the kubelet keeps retrying until it's valid.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · the SAFE edit workflow</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/   <span class="comment"># 1. BACK UP first, always</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml        <span class="comment"># 2. edit a --flag under command:</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. watch it restart (kubectl may briefly fail — that's expected)</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep apiserver        <span class="comment"># the container restarts with a new ID</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -w | grep apiserver
<span class="comment"># broke it? restore and wait:</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Key flag</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>--authorization-mode</code></td><td>the authorizers, in order — typically <code>Node,RBAC</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code></td><td>which admission controllers run (e.g. <code>NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</code>) — §10.5</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--etcd-servers</code></td><td>the etcd endpoint(s) the apiserver reads/writes (<code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code></td><td>the CIDR ClusterIPs are allocated from (e.g. <code>10.96.0.0/12</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--advertise-address</code></td><td>the IP the apiserver advertises to the cluster</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--encryption-provider-config</code></td><td>enables encryption-at-rest for Secrets (Ch 11 §11.5)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 Recovering a dead apiserver</h5><p>If <code>kubectl</code> dies after your edit, the apiserver pod is crash-looping on your change. You can't use <code>kubectl</code> — work on the node: <code>sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver</code> and <code>sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> show <em>why</em> (e.g. "unknown flag," "no such file"). Fix the YAML (or restore the <code>/tmp</code> backup); the kubelet relaunches it within ~20s. This exact recovery is a classic exam task — the backup is what makes it a non-event.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.4 kubelet Configuration</h3>
                <p>The kubelet is a <strong>systemd service</strong>, not a pod, so it's configured differently from the control plane. Its settings live in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> (a <code>KubeletConfiguration</code> object), and the flags kubeadm set live in a systemd drop-in. After a change you reload systemd and restart the service.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node · /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml (excerpt)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: KubeletConfiguration
clusterDNS:                 <span class="comment"># the in-cluster DNS Service IP (CoreDNS)</span>
- 10.96.0.10
clusterDomain: cluster.local  <span class="comment"># the DNS suffix for Services/pods</span>
cgroupDriver: systemd       <span class="comment"># MUST match containerd (§9.2)</span>
evictionHard:               <span class="comment"># when the kubelet starts evicting pods</span>
  memory.available: "100Mi"
  nodefs.available: "10%"
authentication:             <span class="comment"># how the apiserver authenticates to this kubelet</span>
  x509: { clientCAFile: /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt }</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node · apply a kubelet change</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml          <span class="comment"># edit clusterDNS, evictionHard, etc.</span>
<span class="comment"># the kubelet's systemd flags live here:</span>
sudo cat /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf
sudo systemctl daemon-reload                   <span class="comment"># REQUIRED after a unit/drop-in change</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -f                  <span class="comment"># watch it come back cleanly</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ <code>daemon-reload</code> before restart</h5><p>If you change the systemd <em>unit</em> or a drop-in (<code>*.conf</code>), you must <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> before <code>restart</code> — otherwise systemd runs the <em>old</em> definition and your change appears to do nothing. (Editing only <code>config.yaml</code> needs just a <code>restart</code>.) When a node misbehaves, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> is always the first place to look.</p></div>
                <p>The kubelet's <code>staticPodPath</code> field and its own kubeconfig deserve a callout, since both are easy to lose track of:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>📁 <code>staticPodPath</code></h5><p>The <code>KubeletConfiguration</code> field <code>staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</code> tells the kubelet where to watch for static pod manifests. Change it and the kubelet starts watching a different directory instead.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🔑 <code>/etc/kubernetes/kubelet.conf</code></h5><p>This is the kubelet's <strong>own kubeconfig</strong> — not the same file as <code>config.yaml</code>. It holds the <code>server</code>, <code>client-certificate</code>, and <code>client-key</code> the kubelet uses to authenticate to the apiserver.</p></div>
                </div>
                <h4>🔬 Hand-authoring a static pod</h4>
                <p>Drop a manifest straight into <code>staticPodPath</code> and the kubelet creates the pod itself — no API server round-trip needed:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node · create a static pod manually</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Static pods: drop a manifest in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet will auto-create the pod (no API server needed!)</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-nginx.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: static-nginx
  namespace: default
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:1.25
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
EOF

<span class="comment"># Verify static pod exists</span>
kubectl get pods | grep static-nginx
<span class="comment"># static-nginx-control-plane   1/1   Running   0   10s</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 The mirror-pod name gives it away</h5><p>Notice the apiserver-visible name is <code>static-nginx-<em>control-plane</em></code> — kubelet always suffixes a static pod's mirror pod with the node name it's running on. That suffix is the tell that you're looking at a static pod, not a Deployment-managed one.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.5 Admission Controllers</h3>
                <p>Admission controllers are gatekeepers the apiserver runs <strong>after authentication &amp; authorization, but before the object is persisted to etcd</strong>. They come in two waves: <strong>mutating</strong> ones can <em>change</em> the object (inject defaults, sidecars), then schema validation runs, then <strong>validating</strong> ones can only <em>accept or reject</em> it. This is where cluster policy lives.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the API request pipeline — every write travels this</div>
<pre>
   kubectl apply -f pod.yaml
        ▼
   🔐 AUTHENTICATION    who are you?     (client cert / token / OIDC)        ← reject → 401
        ▼
   🛂 AUTHORIZATION     are you allowed? (RBAC: verb + resource)             ← reject → 403
        ▼
   🧪 ADMISSION
        ├─ MUTATING controllers + webhooks   (ADD/CHANGE: LimitRanger defaults, sidecars)
        ├─ schema validation                 (OpenAPI: types, required fields)
        └─ VALIDATING controllers + webhooks (ACCEPT/REJECT only: PodSecurity, ResourceQuota)
        ▼
   🗄️ PERSIST to etcd   ─►  controllers + scheduler take over
   💡 mutating ALWAYS runs before validating — defaults are applied, THEN the final object is judged.
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>Type</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>NamespaceLifecycle</code></td><td>validating</td><td>blocks creating objects in a terminating/absent namespace</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>LimitRanger</code></td><td>mutating</td><td>injects default CPU/memory requests/limits from a LimitRange</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ResourceQuota</code></td><td>validating</td><td>rejects a create that would exceed a namespace's quota</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>PodSecurity</code></td><td>validating</td><td>enforces Pod Security Standards (privileged/baseline/restricted) — Ch 26</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>NodeRestriction</code></td><td>validating</td><td>limits what a kubelet can modify (only its own node/pods) — key security default</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>DefaultStorageClass</code></td><td>mutating</td><td>stamps the default StorageClass onto a PVC that omits one</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Mutating/ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</code></td><td>both</td><td>call <em>your</em> external webhooks (Kyverno, OPA, Istio injection) — Ch 38</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · enable a plugin</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># edit the apiserver manifest's command: flags (back up first!)</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,ResourceQuota</span>
<span class="comment"># (kubelet restarts the apiserver on save)</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-control-plane -o yaml \
  | grep enable-admission-plugins        <span class="comment"># verify it took</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why "mutating before validating" matters</h5><p>If validation ran first, a default injected by a mutating controller (say a LimitRanger memory request) could be rejected before it was ever added — or, worse, a sidecar webhook's container would dodge policy checks. Running all mutations first means the <em>final</em> object is the one that's validated. <code>--disable-admission-plugins</code> turns built-ins off; several (like <code>NamespaceLifecycle</code>, <code>NodeRestriction</code>) are on by default.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.6 API Groups &amp; Versioning</h3>
                <p>Every Kubernetes object lives in an <strong>API group</strong> at a <strong>version</strong>. There are two shapes: the original <strong>core</strong> group served at <code>/api/v1</code> (Pods, Services, ConfigMaps…), and <strong>named</strong> groups served at <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;</code> (apps, batch, networking…). The <code>apiVersion</code> field in every manifest is exactly this group/version string.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the API surface — core vs named groups</div>
<pre>
   kube-apiserver
   ├─ /api/v1                       ← the CORE group (no group name): apiVersion: v1
   │     Pod · Service · ConfigMap · Secret · Namespace · Node · PersistentVolumeClaim
   │
   └─ /apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;        ← NAMED groups: apiVersion: &lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;
         apps/v1 .................. Deployment · ReplicaSet · DaemonSet · StatefulSet
         batch/v1 ................. Job · CronJob
         networking.k8s.io/v1 ..... Ingress · NetworkPolicy
         rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1  Role · RoleBinding · ClusterRole…
         storage.k8s.io/v1 ........ StorageClass · CSIDriver
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   version maturity:  v1alpha1 (may vanish) → v1beta1 (usually safe) → v1 (stable, GA)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">discover the API</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl api-resources                 <span class="comment"># every kind → short name, APIVERSION, namespaced?, KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME          SHORTNAMES   APIVERSION   NAMESPACED   KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># pods          po           v1           true         Pod</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy       apps/v1      true         Deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># nodes         no           v1           false        Node</span>

kubectl api-resources --api-group=apps   <span class="comment"># just one group's kinds</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments · replicasets · daemonsets · statefulsets   (all apps/v1)</span>

kubectl api-versions                  <span class="comment"># every served group/version</span>
<span class="comment"># apps/v1 · batch/v1 · networking.k8s.io/v1 · rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 · v1 (core)</span>

kubectl api-resources | grep -i ingress
<span class="comment"># ingresses   ing   networking.k8s.io/v1   true   Ingress</span>
kubectl explain ingress --api-version=networking.k8s.io/v1   <span class="comment"># pin the schema (shows KIND + VERSION)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why groups exist</h5><p>Splitting the API into versioned groups lets Kubernetes evolve features independently and deprecate cleanly — <code>batch</code> can ship a new <code>CronJob</code> version without touching <code>apps</code>. It's also the extension model: a <strong>CRD</strong> (Ch 38) adds your <em>own</em> named group (<code>easypj.example.com/v1</code>) that behaves exactly like a built-in. Full deprecation/conversion mechanics were covered in Ch 7's "Under the Hood."</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how a config change reaches a running control-plane component</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Editing a file and having the apiserver restart "by magic" is really the <strong>kubelet's static-pod reconcile loop</strong>. The kubelet watches <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and treats each YAML there as a pod it must keep running — so a file change <em>is</em> a redeploy.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml   (you save an edit)
        │
        ▼   the kubelet watches this directory (inotify + periodic resync)
   🤖 kubelet sees the manifest's spec changed
        │  → terminates the OLD apiserver static pod
        │  → creates a NEW one from the updated manifest (via the CRI)
        ▼
   📡 new kube-apiserver Running   (its pod is "mirror"-reflected into kube-system, read-only)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the kubelet (a systemd service) configures itself differently: edit /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml,
   then systemctl daemon-reload (if the unit changed) + restart kubelet — there's no manifest to watch.
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Two config models, one chapter:</strong> <em>control-plane components</em> are static pods → configure by editing a <strong>file the kubelet watches</strong> (auto-restart). The <em>kubelet itself</em> is a systemd service → configure via <code>config.yaml</code> + <code>systemctl</code>. Mixing them up ("why didn't my kubelet change apply?") is a common stumble — the kubelet has no one watching <em>it</em>.</div>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>The other "config change" — switching context does almost nothing, on purpose.</strong> <code>kubectl config use-context</code> feels like it connects somewhere, but kubectl is <strong>stateless</strong>: it holds no connection between commands. Switching context just rewrites one line, and the <em>next</em> command reads it fresh.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   $ kubectl config use-context easypj-prod
     • writes ONE line to ~/.kube/config →  current-context: easypj-prod
     • closes NO connection, starts NO process, touches NO other terminal session
   the NEXT kubectl command resolves that context from scratch:
     context easypj-prod → cluster easypj-cluster (server https://10.0.1.100:6443)
                         → user    easypj-admin   (client cert + key)
                         → namespace easypj-prod
     → kubectl builds ONE HTTPS request from those three and sends it
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   $ kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev
     • edits the CURRENT context's namespace line in place (adds it if absent)
     • effect: every later command defaults to -n easypj-dev   (an explicit -n still wins)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why "stateless" matters:</strong> because nothing persists between invocations, two terminals can sit in two different contexts at once (each reads its own <code>$KUBECONFIG</code>/<code>~/.kube/config</code>), and a switch can never "half-apply." It also means a wrong <code>current-context</code> silently aims your next destructive command at the wrong cluster — which is why <code>kubectl config current-context</code> is the cheapest insurance on the exam.</div>

                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Beyond the basics</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>API Priority &amp; Fairness (APF)</strong></td><td>the apiserver fair-queues concurrent requests by <code>FlowSchema</code>/<code>PriorityLevelConfiguration</code> so a noisy client can't starve others; a 429 with a <code>Retry-After</code> is APF shedding load.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>KMS v2 encryption providers</strong></td><td><code>--encryption-provider-config</code> can wrap etcd Secrets with an external KMS (envelope encryption) instead of a local key — Ch 11 §11.5.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>audit logging</strong></td><td><code>--audit-policy-file</code> + <code>--audit-log-path</code> record who did what; a webhook backend ships audit events to an external SIEM.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>aggregation layer</strong></td><td>the front-proxy certs let extension apiservers (e.g. <code>metrics-server</code>, custom APIs) plug into the main API surface under <code>/apis</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong><code>--feature-gates</code> &amp; OIDC</strong></td><td>toggle alpha/beta features per component; <code>--oidc-*</code> flags wire the apiserver to an identity provider for human SSO.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Where the cluster's own config lives — the kubeadm ConfigMaps.</strong> kubeadm doesn't keep its settings in a file on the node; it stores them as <strong>ConfigMaps in <code>kube-system</code></strong>, which <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> and <code>kubeadm join</code> read back. This is also where you look up a running cluster's service/pod CIDR on the exam:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>ConfigMap (kube-system)</th><th>Holds</th><th>Read by</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kubeadm-config</code></td><td><code>ClusterConfiguration</code> — the exact params from <code>kubeadm init</code> (service CIDR, pod CIDR, version…)</td><td><code>kubeadm upgrade</code>, <code>kubeadm join</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubelet-config</code></td><td>the cluster-wide <code>KubeletConfiguration</code></td><td>every kubelet (via <code>--config</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-proxy</code></td><td><code>KubeProxyConfiguration</code></td><td>the kube-proxy DaemonSet</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>coredns</code></td><td>the CoreDNS Corefile</td><td>the CoreDNS Deployment</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>cluster-info</code> (kube-public)</td><td>the cluster endpoint + public CA</td><td><code>kubectl cluster-info</code>, <code>kubeadm join</code> discovery</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Exam move:</strong> need a running cluster's service-CIDR or pod-CIDR (and you don't want to grep the apiserver manifest)? <code>kubectl -n kube-system describe cm kubeadm-config</code> shows the exact values used at <code>init</code> time. It's also where the <code>certificateKey</code> for joining extra control-plane nodes is recorded.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubeconfig</strong> = clusters (where) + users (who) + contexts (combo); files merge via <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> (first-wins), flatten with <code>--flatten</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Configure the control plane by editing its static-pod manifests</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet auto-restarts the component on save. <em>Back up first.</em></li>
                    <li><strong>Configure the kubelet</strong> via <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> + systemd (<code>daemon-reload</code> → <code>restart</code>).</li>
                    <li><strong>Admission runs after authZ</strong>: mutating → schema → validating → persist; enable via <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>API groups:</strong> core <code>/api/v1</code> vs named <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;ver&gt;</code>; discover with <code>api-resources</code>/<code>api-versions</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Manifest typo kills the apiserver.</strong> <code>kubectl</code> dies too — recover by fixing the file on the node (<code>crictl logs</code> shows why).</li>
                    <li><strong>No backup.</strong> Always <code>cp … /tmp</code> before editing a control-plane manifest.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgot <code>daemon-reload</code>.</strong> A kubelet unit change won't apply until you reload systemd, then restart.</li>
                    <li><strong>Stray <code>$KUBECONFIG</code>.</strong> Silently overrides <code>~/.kube/config</code> — "where's my cluster?"</li>
                    <li><strong>Wrong admission order assumption.</strong> Mutating always precedes validating.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Back up, always:</strong> <code>sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/</code> before any edit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Watch it return:</strong> <code>crictl ps | grep apiserver</code> (the container ID changes) or <code>get pods -n kube-system -w</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl config view --minify</code> to confirm cluster/user/namespace fast.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; -o yaml | grep &lt;flag&gt;</code> to verify a flag took.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 10)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">config &amp; control-plane toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config view --minify ; kubectl config get-contexts ; kubectl config use-context &lt;c&gt;
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/   <span class="comment"># back up before editing</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml       <span class="comment"># edit flags → kubelet restarts it</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep apiserver ; sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;       <span class="comment"># when kubectl is down</span>
sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml ; sudo systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl restart kubelet
kubectl api-resources ; kubectl api-versions ; journalctl -u kubelet -f</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — enable an admission plugin, verify, then revert</h5>
                <p>No peeking — practise the back-up-edit-recover loop safely:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Back up <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> to <code>/tmp</code>.</li>
                    <li>Add <code>NamespaceAutoProvision</code> (or <code>NodeRestriction</code>) to <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> and save.</li>
                    <li>Wait for the apiserver to restart, then verify the flag is live.</li>
                    <li>Revert by restoring the backup; confirm <code>kubectl</code> still works.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal (on the control-plane node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># edit:  - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</span>
watch -n2 'sudo crictl ps | grep apiserver'      <span class="comment"># wait for a new container ID</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-$(hostname) -o yaml | grep enable-admission-plugins
<span class="comment"># revert</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
kubectl get nodes        <span class="comment"># kubectl works again ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 10 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Cluster-config recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the 3 kubeconfig sections and what each holds.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>clusters</code></td><td>server URL + CA cert (WHERE)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>users</code></td><td>client cert/key, token, or exec plugin (WHO)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>contexts</code></td><td>cluster + user + namespace (the COMBO)</td></tr></tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>current-context</code> selects the active context; <code>kubectl config view --minify</code> shows only it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Merge two kubeconfig files into one session.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">merge</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/work/dev.yaml
kubectl config get-contexts                    <span class="comment"># union of both</span>
kubectl config view --flatten &gt; ~/.kube/merged  <span class="comment"># bake into one file</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Colon-separated (semicolon on Windows); on a name clash the first file wins. <code>--flatten</code> embeds all certs into one self-contained file.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable the <code>NodeRestriction</code> admission plugin — which file, which flag?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> and add it to <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>: <code>- --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</code>. Save; the kubelet restarts the apiserver.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Back up first. Verify with <code>kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; -o yaml | grep enable-admission</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Find the current <code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code> of the apiserver.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find a flag</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">grep service-cluster-ip-range /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># - --service-cluster-ip-range=10.96.0.0/12</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>All apiserver flags live in its static-pod manifest's <code>command:</code> list — grep the file, or read the live pod's spec.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The apiserver won't start after an edit — how do you recover?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl</code> is down, so work on the node: <code>sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver</code> then <code>sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> to see the error. Fix the YAML (or restore the <code>/tmp</code> backup); the kubelet relaunches it within ~20s.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The component is a static pod the kubelet keeps retrying — you never "start" it manually; you just make the manifest valid.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Core API group vs a named group — give an example <code>apiVersion</code> of each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Core:</strong> <code>apiVersion: v1</code> (Pod, Service, ConfigMap) — served at <code>/api/v1</code>. <strong>Named:</strong> <code>apiVersion: apps/v1</code> (Deployment) — served at <code>/apis/apps/v1</code>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The core group has no group name (just <code>v1</code>); every other group is <code>&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Change the kubelet's <code>clusterDNS</code> and apply it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml      <span class="comment"># edit clusterDNS: [10.96.0.10]</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 20 --no-pager</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>It's a config-file change (not a unit change), so a plain <code>restart</code> suffices — no <code>daemon-reload</code> needed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set a context's default namespace to <code>easypj</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">set namespace</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--current</code> edits the active context so every command defaults to <code>easypj</code> — no more <code>-n</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do mutating vs validating webhooks run in the request flow?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Both run in the <strong>admission</strong> phase, <em>after</em> authN+authZ and <em>before</em> etcd. Order: <strong>mutating</strong> (can change the object) → schema validation → <strong>validating</strong> (accept/reject only).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Mutating-first guarantees the <em>final</em>, defaulted object is the one judged by validation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Discover which API version serves Ingress.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">discover</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl api-resources | grep -i ingress
<span class="comment"># ingresses   ing   networking.k8s.io/v1   true   Ingress</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Always look it up rather than guess — versions drift between releases. <code>api-resources</code> is the source of truth for your cluster.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 10 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style "configure it / fix it" tasks with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are the three main sections of a kubeconfig file? Draw the relationship between them.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the three sections</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The three sections:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. clusters    — WHERE to connect (API server endpoint + CA cert)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. users       — WHO you are (client cert + key, or token)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. contexts    — WHICH combo to use (cluster + user + namespace)</span>

apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
current-context: dev-context    # ← Active context

clusters:
- name: dev-cluster
  cluster:
    server: https://10.0.0.10:6443
    certificate-authority-data: &lt;base64-ca-cert&gt;

users:
- name: dev-admin
  user:
    client-certificate-data: &lt;base64-cert&gt;
    client-key-data: &lt;base64-key&gt;

contexts:
- name: dev-context              # ← Links cluster + user
  context:
    cluster: dev-cluster         # → References clusters[].name
    user: dev-admin              # → References users[].name
    namespace: default           <span class="comment"># Optional default namespace</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">relationship</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Relationship:</span>
<span class="comment">#  Context ──→ Cluster (server URL)</span>
<span class="comment">#     │</span>
<span class="comment">#     └──→ User (credentials)</span>
<span class="comment">#     │</span>
<span class="comment">#     └──→ Namespace (optional default)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>A kubeconfig is essentially a mapping: contexts bind a cluster to a user with an optional default namespace. You can have multiple clusters, users, and contexts in one file. The <code>current-context</code> field determines which context is active. On the CKA, understanding this structure is critical because exam questions give you different contexts per question. Each context points to a different cluster. If you use the wrong context, you'll work on the wrong cluster and fail the question.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You have two kubeconfig files: <code>/root/admin.conf</code> (production cluster) and <code>/root/dev.conf</code> (dev cluster). How do you merge them into one and switch between clusters?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">temporary merge (current session only)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: Temporary merge (current session only)</span>
export KUBECONFIG=/root/admin.conf:/root/dev.conf
kubectl config view  <span class="comment"># Shows both clusters/contexts</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Permanent merge into one file</span>
KUBECONFIG=/root/admin.conf:/root/dev.conf \
  kubectl config view --flatten > ~/.kube/config

<span class="comment"># List all available contexts</span>
kubectl config get-contexts
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME         CLUSTER    AUTHINFO    NAMESPACE</span>
<span class="comment"># *         prod-ctx     prod       prod-admin  default</span>
<span class="comment">#           dev-ctx      dev        dev-admin   dev-ns</span>

<span class="comment"># Switch between clusters</span>
kubectl config use-context prod-ctx   # → production
kubectl config use-context dev-ctx    # → development

<span class="comment"># Verify current context</span>
kubectl config current-context
<span class="comment"># dev-ctx</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>KUBECONFIG</code> accepts a colon-separated list of files (semicolon on Windows). The <code>--flatten</code> flag resolves all external file references and outputs a self-contained config. On the CKA, the exam environment has pre-configured kubeconfig with multiple contexts — one per cluster/question. You MUST switch contexts at the start of each question. Always verify you're in the right context: <code>kubectl config current-context</code>. Working in the wrong context is the #1 reason candidates fail otherwise-correct answers.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are admission controllers? Name three important ones and explain how to enable/disable them.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">admission controllers intercept api requests after…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Admission controllers intercept API requests AFTER auth but BEFORE persistence</span>

<span class="comment"># View currently enabled admission controllers:</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-controlplane \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep admission

<span class="comment"># Important admission controllers:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. NamespaceLifecycle — prevents creating objects in terminating namespaces</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. LimitRanger       — enforces LimitRange defaults in namespaces</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. ResourceQuota     — enforces ResourceQuota limits</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. NodeRestriction   — limits what kubelets can modify</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. PodSecurity       — enforces pod security standards</span>

<span class="comment"># Enable an admission controller:</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># Find or add:</span>
- --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,LimitRanger

<span class="comment"># Disable a specific one:</span>
- --disable-admission-plugins=DefaultStorageClass

<span class="comment"># After saving, the API server restarts automatically (static pod)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Admission controllers are plugins that govern what's allowed in the cluster. They run in order after authentication and authorization. There are two types: <strong>mutating</strong> (can modify the request, like adding default resource limits) and <strong>validating</strong> (can only accept/reject). On the CKA, you may be asked to enable a specific admission controller by editing the API server static pod manifest at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. After saving, kubelet restarts the API server — wait 30-60 seconds for it to come back up. Always verify with <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you find the API group and version for a specific Kubernetes resource? Show multiple methods.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl api-resources (most useful)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl api-resources (most useful)</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep -i deployment
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy   apps/v1   true   Deployment</span>
<span class="comment">#               ↑alias   ↑apiVersion ↑namespaced</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl api-versions (list all)</span>
kubectl api-versions
<span class="comment"># apps/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># networking.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># v1   ← core group (no prefix)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl explain (shows VERSION header)</span>
kubectl explain deployment
<span class="comment"># KIND:     Deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># VERSION:  apps/v1</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: API discovery endpoint</span>
kubectl get --raw /apis/apps/v1 | python3 -m json.tool

<span class="comment"># Quick reference — API groups:</span>
<span class="comment"># Core (v1):        Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Secret, PVC, SA, Node, Namespace</span>
<span class="comment"># apps/v1:          Deployment, ReplicaSet, DaemonSet, StatefulSet</span>
<span class="comment"># networking:       Ingress, NetworkPolicy</span>
<span class="comment"># rbac:             Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding</span>
<span class="comment"># storage:          StorageClass, VolumeAttachment</span>
<span class="comment"># batch/v1:         Job, CronJob</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl api-resources</code> is the definitive answer for the CKA. It shows the short name, API group/version, whether the resource is namespaced, and the kind. The core API group resources (Pod, Service, etc.) use <code>v1</code> with no group prefix in the <code>apiVersion</code>. All other resources use <code>&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;</code> format. The <code>kubectl explain</code> command is also invaluable — it shows the VERSION in its header output, plus detailed documentation of every field.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to create a new context called <code>staging-ctx</code> that connects to cluster <code>staging-cluster</code> as user <code>staging-admin</code> with default namespace <code>staging</code>. Then switch to it.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create the context</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the context</span>
kubectl config set-context staging-ctx \
  --cluster=staging-cluster \
  --user=staging-admin \
  --namespace=staging

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Switch to it</span>
kubectl config use-context staging-ctx
<span class="comment"># Switched to context "staging-ctx"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify</span>
kubectl config current-context
<span class="comment"># staging-ctx</span>

kubectl config view --minify
<span class="comment"># Shows only staging-ctx details</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Test connectivity</span>
kubectl get pods
<span class="comment"># Should show pods in the staging namespace</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl config set-context</code> creates or updates a context entry in the kubeconfig. The <code>--cluster</code> and <code>--user</code> must reference entries that already exist in the kubeconfig's <code>clusters</code> and <code>users</code> sections. Setting <code>--namespace</code> makes every command in this context default to that namespace (no need for <code>-n staging</code> every time). On the CKA, the most common pattern is: <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code> then <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;</code> — this sets the namespace for the current context without creating a new one.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you view and modify the API server configuration? What flags control authentication, authorization, and which admission controllers are active?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">api server is a static pod</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># API Server is a static pod — config is in the manifest</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># Key flags to know:</span>
<span class="comment"># Authentication:</span>
--client-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
--authentication-token-webhook-config-file=...
--oidc-issuer-url=...

<span class="comment"># Authorization (order matters!):</span>
--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC
<span class="comment"># Options: AlwaysAllow, AlwaysDeny, Node, RBAC, Webhook, ABAC</span>

<span class="comment"># Admission Controllers:</span>
--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity
--disable-admission-plugins=DefaultStorageClass

<span class="comment"># To modify: edit the manifest directly</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects the change and restarts the API server</span>

<span class="comment"># Wait and verify:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver
<span class="comment"># Should show Running with RESTARTS incremented by 1</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The API server runs as a static pod, so its config is a YAML manifest at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Editing this file triggers kubelet to restart the pod automatically — no <code>kubectl apply</code> needed. On the CKA, you must be comfortable editing this file. The <code>--authorization-mode</code> flag determines the auth chain — requests are checked against each mode in order. <code>Node,RBAC</code> is the default: first check Node authorization (for kubelet), then RBAC for everything else. Be careful editing — a syntax error will take the API server down, and kubectl won't work until you fix it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubectl config view</code> and <code>kubectl config view --minify</code>? When should you use <code>--raw</code>?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl config view — shows entire kubeconfig (all…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># kubectl config view — shows ENTIRE kubeconfig (all contexts)</span>
kubectl config view
<span class="comment"># Shows all clusters, users, contexts — can be very long</span>

<span class="comment"># kubectl config view --minify — current context ONLY</span>
kubectl config view --minify
<span class="comment"># Shows only the cluster, user, context for current-context</span>

<span class="comment"># kubectl config view --raw — shows actual certificate data</span>
kubectl config view --raw
<span class="comment"># Normally cert data is replaced with "DATA+OMITTED"</span>
<span class="comment"># --raw shows the full base64-encoded certificates</span>

<span class="comment"># Practical example: extract the CA cert</span>
kubectl config view --raw -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.certificate-authority-data}' | base64 -d > ca.crt

<span class="comment"># Extract the current context's server URL</span>
kubectl config view --minify -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.server}'
<span class="comment"># https://10.0.0.10:6443</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>--minify</code> filters the output to only show what's relevant to the current context — essential on the CKA where kubeconfig has many contexts. <code>--raw</code> shows the actual certificate data instead of the <code>DATA+OMITTED</code> placeholder. You'd use <code>--raw</code> when you need to extract certificates for debugging or copying to another config. On the CKA, combine <code>--minify</code> with <code>-o jsonpath</code> to quickly extract specific values like the server URL, namespace, or user name.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The kube-apiserver won't start after you edited its manifest. How do you debug without <code>kubectl</code>?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl is dead when api server is down</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># kubectl is DEAD when API server is down — use these alternatives:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Check container status via CRI</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver
<span class="comment"># Look for Exited containers with exit codes</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check API server container logs</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
<span class="comment"># Shows the actual error message</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Check kubelet logs (kubelet manages static pods)</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 30

<span class="comment"># 4. Check the manifest for syntax errors</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for typos, wrong indentation, missing values</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Validate YAML syntax</span>
python3 -c "import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open('/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml'))"

<span class="comment"># 6. Common mistakes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Extra space in flag: --enable-admission -plugins (wrong!)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing value: --authorization-mode= (empty!)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong file path: --client-ca-file=/wrong/path</span>
<span class="comment"># - Typo: --enbale-admission-plugins (misspelled!)</span>

<span class="comment"># After fixing, kubelet auto-restarts the API server (30-60s)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>When you break the API server, you lose kubectl access — this is the most stressful CKA scenario. Your lifeline is <code>crictl</code> (CRI tool that talks directly to containerd) and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>. The pattern: (1) <code>crictl ps -a</code> to see the crash-looping container, (2) <code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> to read the error, (3) fix the manifest, (4) wait for auto-recovery. Don't panic — kubelet keeps trying to restart the static pod. As soon as the manifest is valid, the API server comes back within 30-60 seconds.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the Kubernetes API request flow? List every step from when you type <code>kubectl get pods</code> to when you see the output.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">complete api request flow</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Complete API request flow:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. kubectl reads ~/.kube/config</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Finds server URL, client cert, CA cert</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. kubectl sends HTTPS GET /api/v1/namespaces/default/pods</span>
<span class="comment">#    → TLS handshake with API server</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. API Server: Authentication</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Validates client certificate (or token/OIDC)</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Identifies: "This is user kubernetes-admin"</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. API Server: Authorization (RBAC)</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Checks: Can kubernetes-admin GET pods in default?</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Evaluates ClusterRoleBindings/RoleBindings</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. API Server: Admission Control</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Runs mutating admission controllers (modify request)</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Runs validating admission controllers (accept/reject)</span>

<span class="comment"># 6. API Server: Read from etcd</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Retrieves pod objects from etcd datastore</span>

<span class="comment"># 7. Response serialization</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Converts internal objects to requested format (JSON/YAML/table)</span>

<span class="comment"># 8. kubectl: Output formatting</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Applies -o wide, -o yaml, --sort-by, JSONPath filters</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding this flow helps with debugging: if authentication fails, check certificates. If authorization fails, check RBAC (Role/ClusterRole bindings). If admission fails, check admission controller configuration. For read operations (GET/LIST), admission controllers may still run (e.g., webhook admission). For write operations (CREATE/UPDATE), all three stages are critical. On the CKA, if you get "forbidden" errors, the problem is at step 4 (RBAC). If you get "denied by admission controller," the problem is at step 5.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you check what API groups and resources are available in your cluster? What is the difference between core API group and named API groups?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all api groups and versions</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all API groups and versions</span>
kubectl api-versions
<span class="comment"># apps/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># batch/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># networking.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># v1   ← This is the CORE group (no name prefix)</span>

<span class="comment"># List all resources with their API group</span>
kubectl api-resources -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME         SHORTNAMES  APIVERSION                    VERBS</span>
<span class="comment"># pods         po          v1                            [create delete get ...]</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments  deploy      apps/v1                       [create delete get ...]</span>
<span class="comment"># ingresses    ing         networking.k8s.io/v1          [create delete get ...]</span>

<span class="comment"># Core group (legacy, no prefix):</span>
<span class="comment"># API path: /api/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># Resources: Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Secret, PVC, Node, Namespace</span>
<span class="comment"># apiVersion in YAML: v1</span>

<span class="comment"># Named groups (newer, prefixed):</span>
<span class="comment"># API path: /apis/apps/v1, /apis/networking.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># Resources: Deployment, Ingress, NetworkPolicy, etc.</span>
<span class="comment"># apiVersion in YAML: apps/v1, networking.k8s.io/v1</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The core group (accessed at <code>/api/v1</code>) contains the original K8s resources and uses just <code>v1</code> as the apiVersion. Named groups (accessed at <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;</code>) were added later for better organization. New features always go into named groups. On the CKA, knowing which group a resource belongs to determines the correct <code>apiVersion</code> in manifests. <code>kubectl api-resources</code> is the ultimate reference — it shows every installed resource, its API group, whether it's namespaced, and its shortname alias.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 10 Visual Summary — request pipeline + the two config models</div>
<pre>
   THE REQUEST PIPELINE (every write)
   kubectl ─▶ 🔐 authN ─▶ 🛂 authZ(RBAC) ─▶ 🧪 admission(mutating ▶ schema ▶ validating) ─▶ 🗄️ etcd ─▶ controllers/scheduler
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   CONFIGURING THE CLUSTER — two models
   control-plane components (apiserver/scheduler/etcd)   →  edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml  →  kubelet auto-restarts (BACK UP!)
   the kubelet itself (a systemd service)                →  edit /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml      →  daemon-reload + restart
   kubeconfig:  clusters(WHERE) + users(WHO) + contexts(COMBO);  merge via $KUBECONFIG (first-wins)
   API surface:  core /api/v1  vs  named /apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;ver&gt;   (api-resources · api-versions)            ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> every write runs the gauntlet — authenticate, authorize, then admission (mutating defaults first, then validation), then it's stored. You shape the cluster by configuring two different kinds of thing: <strong>control-plane components</strong>, which are static pods you reconfigure by editing files the kubelet watches; and the <strong>kubelet</strong> itself, a systemd service you configure with <code>config.yaml</code> + <code>systemctl</code>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the single habit that protects you is <strong>back up before you edit</strong> a control-plane manifest — then recovery is just restoring a file. Know the admission order (mutating → validating), where each config lives, and how to read the API surface, and Chapter 10's tasks are routine.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH10-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 10 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 11 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch11">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 11 · etcd — Backup &amp; Restore</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">etcd is the cluster's entire memory — every object you've ever created lives there. Lose it without a backup and the cluster is <em>gone</em>. That makes <strong>etcd snapshot &amp; restore</strong> the single highest-value skill an administrator has, and an <strong>almost-guaranteed CKA task</strong>. This chapter makes the backup/restore dance automatic.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Almost-Guaranteed Task</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🗄️ etcd</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ What etcd is, what it stores (<code>/registry/*</code>), and why losing it loses the cluster</li>
                    <li>✅ etcd's <strong>Raft</strong> consensus — leader/follower, quorum math, why members are odd</li>
                    <li>✅ Take a <strong>verified snapshot</strong> with <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> + the 3 TLS flags</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Restore</strong> to a new data-dir and repoint the etcd static pod (both places!)</li>
                    <li>✅ Secure etcd: TLS (default) + <strong>encryption at rest</strong> for Secrets</li>
                    <li>✅ Automate nightly backups and check cluster health</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">etcd backup/restore <span class="cka-weight">ALMOST GUARANTEED</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 The task you will almost certainly get</h5>
                <p>"Back up etcd to <code>/opt/backup.db</code>" and "restore etcd from this snapshot" are among the most common CKA tasks — and they're free marks once the muscle memory is there.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Memorise the 4 inputs:</strong> <code>--endpoints</code> + <code>--cacert</code> + <code>--cert</code> + <code>--key</code>. The cert paths live in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Restore to a NEW data-dir</strong> — never the live one — and update <code>etcd.yaml</code> in <em>two</em> places (the <code>--data-dir</code> flag AND the hostPath volume).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></strong> is required for the snapshot commands.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 11 Roadmap — the cluster's memory, backed up &amp; restorable</div>
<pre>
   11.1 WHAT is etcd ──▶ 11.2 Raft &amp; quorum ──▶ 11.3 BACKUP (snapshot save) ──▶ 11.4 RESTORE ──▶ 11.5 SECURE
   KV store · /registry   leader/follower         the 3 TLS flags + verify        new data-dir       TLS + encryption
   Secrets unencrypted    quorum = N/2+1                                          repoint etcd.yaml   at rest
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: snapshot save ▶ 💥 disaster ▶ snapshot restore ▶ repoint static pod ▶ cluster back.
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-1"><strong>11.1</strong> What is etcd?</a></td><td>What holds all cluster state?</td><td>a distributed KV store at <code>/registry/*</code>; Secrets unencrypted by default.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-2"><strong>11.2</strong> Architecture &amp; Raft</a></td><td>How does it stay consistent &amp; HA?</td><td>Raft leader/follower; quorum = N/2+1; odd members (3,5,7).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-3"><strong>11.3</strong> Backup</a></td><td>How do I snapshot it?</td><td><code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> + endpoint + 3 TLS flags; verify with <code>status</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-4"><strong>11.4</strong> Restore</a></td><td>How do I bring it back?</td><td>restore to a NEW data-dir; repoint <code>etcd.yaml</code> (flag + hostPath).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-5"><strong>11.5</strong> Security &amp; encryption</a></td><td>How do I protect it?</td><td>TLS (default) + EncryptionConfiguration for Secrets at rest.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.1 ─────────── -->

            <section class="section-block" id="s11-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.1 What is etcd?</h3>
                <p><strong>etcd</strong> is a distributed, strongly-consistent <strong>key-value store</strong> — and it is the <em>only</em> place Kubernetes keeps state. Every node, pod, Deployment, Secret, ConfigMap, and RBAC rule is a key under <code>/registry/</code>. The apiserver is the sole component that talks to it; everything else goes through the apiserver. That's the whole reason an etcd snapshot <em>is</em> a cluster backup.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🗝️</div><h5>Key-Value Store</h5><p>data is keys → values under <code>/registry/…</code> — simple, fast, and easy to snapshot whole.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div><h5>Distributed</h5><p>runs as a cluster of members (Raft) so it survives a node loss — no single point of failure.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>Strongly Consistent</h5><p>every member agrees on every committed write — reads never return stale, contradictory state.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">👁️</div><h5>Watch API</h5><p>clients <em>watch</em> key ranges and are pushed changes — the engine behind every K8s controller.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="analogy-box"><h5>🧠 In plain English</h5><p>etcd is the cluster's <strong>memory</strong>. The control plane is the brain that thinks, but every thought — every object's desired and current state — is written in etcd. Wipe etcd with no backup and the cluster has total amnesia: the nodes and containers may still be running, but Kubernetes no longer knows they exist. That's why this chapter is the most important in Part 3.</p></div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what's inside etcd — the <code>/registry</code> keyspace</div>
<pre>
   etcd  (key → value, value = the object as protobuf/JSON)
   /registry/
     ├─ namespaces/easypj-dev
     ├─ pods/easypj-dev/easypj-web-7d8f9-abcde
     ├─ deployments/easypj-dev/easypj-web
     ├─ replicasets/easypj-dev/easypj-web-7d8f9
     ├─ services/easypj-dev/easypj-web
     ├─ configmaps/easypj-dev/easypj-config
     ├─ secrets/easypj-dev/easypj-secret        ⚠️ stored UNENCRYPTED by default (§11.5)
     ├─ clusterroles/… · rolebindings/…
     └─ … every object in the cluster
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   only the kube-apiserver reads/writes etcd → one snapshot of etcd = the entire cluster's state
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 Secrets sit unencrypted in etcd by default</h5><p>A Secret's value is only <strong>base64-encoded</strong> in etcd — anyone who can read the etcd data files (or a snapshot) can read your Secrets in plaintext. That's why §11.5 (encryption at rest) and tight file permissions on snapshots matter, and why a leaked <code>etcd-backup.db</code> is a full credential breach.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 What etcd does NOT back up</h5><p>An etcd snapshot captures <em>Kubernetes objects</em> — not the data inside your <strong>PersistentVolumes</strong>. A restored cluster knows the PVC exists, but the actual disk contents are the storage backend's responsibility (Ch 22). Back up app data separately.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s11-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.2 etcd Architecture &amp; Raft</h3>
                <p>For high availability, etcd runs as a <strong>cluster of members</strong> that stay consistent using the <strong>Raft</strong> consensus algorithm. One member is elected <strong>leader</strong> and handles all writes; the rest are <strong>followers</strong>. A write is only committed once a <strong>majority</strong> (quorum) of members has acknowledged it — which is why etcd never loses a committed write but also why it needs an odd number of members.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">3-member etcd — Raft keeps the memory consistent</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────┐
   │ etcd-0   │◄────►│ etcd-1   │◄────►│ etcd-2   │     3 members
   │ LEADER   │      │ follower │      │ follower │     quorum = N/2+1 = 2
   │ :2379 cli│      │          │      │          │     tolerates 1 failure
   │ :2380 peer│     │          │      │          │
   └──────────┘      └──────────┘      └──────────┘
   WRITE: client ─► LEADER ─► replicate to followers ─► commit once a MAJORITY ack ─► ✅ ack client
   READ:  served from any member (linearizable reads go via the leader)
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Members</th><th>Quorum (N/2+1)</th><th>Failures tolerated</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>dev only — no HA</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>2</td><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>✅ the common HA choice</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>3</td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>✅ large/critical clusters</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2 or 4</td><td>2 / 3</td><td>0 / 1</td><td>❌ even = no extra tolerance, just more risk</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Always odd, and beware quorum loss</h5><p>Even counts are pointless: a 4-member cluster tolerates the same 1 failure as a 3-member one but has more to go wrong. If a cluster <strong>loses quorum</strong> (e.g. 2 of 3 members down), etcd goes <strong>read-only / unavailable</strong> and the whole control plane stalls — recovery means restoring members or a snapshot. This is why production etcd is 3 or 5 members across failure domains.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Stacked vs external etcd</h5><p>kubeadm's default is <strong>stacked</strong>: etcd runs as a static pod <em>on each control-plane node</em>, alongside the apiserver. The alternative is <strong>external</strong> etcd on dedicated machines (better isolation, independent scaling). The backup/restore commands are identical; the operational difference is that with external etcd you stop the apiservers manually during a restore (Ch 33).</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s11-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.3 etcd Backup — the Snapshot</h3>
                <p>A backup is a single <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> call. The catch is that etcd is TLS-protected, so <code>etcdctl</code> must authenticate with three certs plus the endpoint — <strong>four inputs you'll memorise</strong>. Get the cert paths from the etcd static-pod manifest, run the snapshot, then <em>always verify it</em>.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · snapshot save</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Snapshot saved at /opt/etcd-backup.db</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔑 The 4 inputs every <code>snapshot save</code> needs</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> --endpoints</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code> — etcd's client port on the local control-plane node</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔐</span> --cacert</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</code> — verifies etcd's server cert</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📄</span> --cert</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</code> — etcdctl's client cert</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗝️</span> --key</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code> — its private key</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Forgot the cert paths? Three ways to find them on the exam</h5><p>Any of these recovers the exact cert flags — use whichever you remember under pressure:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find the cert paths (3 methods)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1 — read them off the running etcd POD</span>
kubectl -n kube-system describe pod etcd-$(hostname) | grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca'

<span class="comment"># Method 2 — grep the etcd static-pod MANIFEST (works even when kubectl is down)</span>
grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file|listen-client' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># --cert-file → --cert · --key-file → --key · --trusted-ca-file → --cacert</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3 — the apiserver's etcd-client flags point at the same CA + a client cert</span>
grep etcd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></pre></div></div>
                <h4>✅ Always verify the snapshot before you trust it</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | fe01c2ab |    95620 |       1480 |     4.6 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>⚡ Speed: alias the TLS flags</h5><p>Save typing all session: <code>alias e='sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key'</code> → then just <code>e snapshot save /opt/backup.db</code> and <code>e endpoint health</code>.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s11-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.4 etcd Restore</h3>
                <p>Restore reads a snapshot into a <strong>fresh data directory</strong>, then you repoint the etcd static pod at it. Two rules save you: restore to a <em>new</em> dir (never the live <code>/var/lib/etcd</code>), and update <code>etcd.yaml</code> in <strong>two</strong> places — the <code>--data-dir</code> flag <em>and</em> the hostPath volume that mounts it.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · restore</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ── STEP 1: restore the snapshot into a NEW data directory ──</span>
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored
<span class="comment"># {"level":"info","msg":"restoring snapshot","path":"/opt/etcd-backup.db",</span>
<span class="comment">#  "data-dir":"/var/lib/etcd-restored","wal-dir":".../member/wal","snap-dir":".../member/snap"}</span>

<span class="comment"># ── STEP 2: repoint the etcd static pod — edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml ──</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment">#  (a) the flag:    BEFORE  - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment">#                   AFTER   - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment">#  (b) the volume:  BEFORE  hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment">#                   AFTER   hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd-restored   ← change BOTH, or the restore is invisible</span>

<span class="comment"># ── STEP 3: the kubelet auto-restarts etcd; watch it come back ──</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -w | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># etcd-control-plane   1/1   Running   0   10s</span>

<span class="comment"># ── STEP 4: verify — cluster is back at the snapshot's point-in-time ──</span>
kubectl get nodes ; kubectl get pods -A        <span class="comment"># everything from the snapshot is back ✅</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">BACKUP ▶ 💥 DISASTER ▶ RESTORE — the #1 admin skill</div>
<pre>
   etcdctl snapshot save ──► 💾 /opt/etcd-backup.db        (needs --endpoints --cacert --cert --key)
        │  verify: snapshot status --write-table
        │
   ─ ─ ─│─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ 💥 DISASTER (someone deleted prod / etcd corrupted) ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─
        ▼
   etcdctl snapshot restore ──► 📁 /var/lib/etcd-RESTORED   (a NEW dir — never the live one!)
        ▼
   edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
        change  --data-dir   AND   the hostPath volume    ──► BOTH, or etcd starts on old/empty data
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet auto-restarts etcd ──► ✅ cluster restored to the snapshot's moment
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 The three restore mistakes that cost marks</h5><ul><li><strong>Restoring into the live data-dir</strong> while etcd is running → corruption. Always a new dir.</li><li><strong>Updating only the flag, not the hostPath</strong> (or vice-versa) → etcd mounts the wrong directory and your restore "didn't work."</li><li><strong>Forgetting <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></strong> → the v2 API doesn't understand snapshots.</li></ul></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 What about HA (multiple etcd members)?</h5><p>On a multi-member cluster you restore the snapshot on <em>one</em> node, then either re-add the others as fresh members or restore the same snapshot to each with matching <code>--initial-cluster</code> settings. For the CKA's single stacked control plane, the steps above are all you need.</p></div>
                <h4>📋 The one-glance etcd backup &amp; restore cheat sheet</h4>
                <p>Everything you need for the exam task, in one place — copy the pattern, swap the paths:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CKA etcd — complete backup &amp; restore cheat sheet</div>
<pre>
   ┌─ BACKUP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db \                       │
   │   --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \                                     │
   │   --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \                               │
   │   --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \                             │
   │   --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key                                │
   │ VERIFY:  etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup.db -w table                   │
   ├─ RESTORE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db \                    │
   │   --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored        ← a NEW dir                     │
   │ THEN edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml — BOTH:                      │
   │   1) - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                                   │
   │   2) volumes → hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd-restored                       │
   │ WAIT: kubelet auto-restarts etcd · VERIFY: kubectl get pods -n kube-system │
   ├─ FORGOT THE CERT PATHS? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <h4>🏥 Checking etcd health &amp; status</h4>
                <p>Before (and after) a backup/restore, confirm etcd is healthy — these show the leader, DB size, members, and write latency:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">etcd health &amp; status (alias the TLS flags as $CERTS)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># is it alive?</span>
etcdctl endpoint health $CERTS
<span class="comment"># https://127.0.0.1:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 2.3ms</span>

<span class="comment"># leader · version · DB size (the &lt;2 GB quota lives here)</span>
etcdctl endpoint status $CERTS --write-out=table
<span class="comment"># +------------------------+--------+---------+---------+-----------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |        ENDPOINT        |   ID   | VERSION | DB SIZE | IS LEADER |</span>
<span class="comment"># | https://127.0.0.1:2379 | abc123 | 3.5.x   | 4.2 MB  |   true    |</span>
<span class="comment"># +------------------------+--------+---------+---------+-----------+</span>

<span class="comment"># the members &amp; their roles (who's the leader, are all started?)</span>
etcdctl member list $CERTS --write-out=table

<span class="comment"># write-latency benchmark (etcd is fsync-bound — slow disks = a sick cluster)</span>
etcdctl check perf $CERTS</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Healthy etcd indicators</h5><ul><li><code>endpoint health</code> says "is healthy"</li><li>DB size well under the quota (~2 GB)</li><li>Raft term stable (not incrementing)</li><li>all members show <code>started</code></li><li>write latency under ~10 ms</li><li>exactly one <code>IS LEADER = true</code></li></ul></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🚨</div><h5>Unhealthy red flags</h5><ul><li><code>endpoint health</code> timeouts/errors</li><li>DB size near/over quota → needs compaction</li><li><code>"no leader"</code> in member list</li><li>high/rising Raft term (frequent elections)</li><li><code>mvcc: database space exceeded</code></li><li>members showing <code>unstarted</code></li></ul></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s11-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.5 etcd Security &amp; Encryption at Rest</h3>
                <p>etcd is protected on the wire by <strong>TLS</strong> already (that's why backups need certs). But the data <em>at rest</em> — including Secrets — is only base64-encoded on disk. <strong>Encryption at rest</strong> closes that gap: you give the apiserver an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> and it encrypts Secret values before writing them to etcd.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">etcd security — defense in depth (4 layers)</div>
<pre>
   ┌─────────────────────────── etcd security layers ───────────────────────────┐
   │ 🧱 Layer 1 — NETWORK ISOLATION                                              │
   │    etcd reachable ONLY from the control plane · firewall :2379 from the      │
   │    apiserver IP only · :2380 from other etcd peers only                      │
   ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🔒 Layer 2 — TLS IN TRANSIT  (default in kubeadm)                            │
   │    apiserver ──TLS──▶ etcd (client certs) · etcd ──TLS──▶ peers (peer certs) │
   │    all verified against /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt                      │
   ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🔐 Layer 3 — ENCRYPTION AT REST  (you enable — §11.5)                        │
   │    EncryptionConfiguration encrypts Secrets BEFORE they hit disk             │
   │    provider: aescbc / secretbox · without it: Secrets are base64 (readable!) │
   ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🛂 Layer 4 — RBAC + AUTH                                                     │
   │    only the apiserver holds etcd client creds · NO user talks to etcd direct │
   │    (everything goes through the apiserver, which RBAC then guards)           │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   threat model: anyone with the etcd data files or a snapshot can read every Secret in plaintext —
   layers 3 &amp; 4 are what stop a leaked backup or a curious user from becoming a breach.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
- resources:
  - secrets                       <span class="comment"># encrypt Secret objects…</span>
  providers:
  - aescbc:                       <span class="comment"># …with AES-CBC (a 32-byte key, base64)</span>
      keys:
      - name: key1
        secret: &lt;base64-32-byte-key&gt;
  - identity: {}                  <span class="comment"># fallback so EXISTING (unencrypted) reads still work</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · enable + re-encrypt</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. point the apiserver at the config (back up the manifest first!)</span>
<span class="comment"># in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml add:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#   + a hostPath volume mounting /etc/kubernetes/enc into the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. re-write every existing Secret so it gets encrypted with the new key</span>
kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ <code>identity</code> first vs second matters</h5><p>The provider list is tried <strong>in order for writes</strong> (first provider encrypts), and <strong>all providers are tried for reads</strong>. Putting <code>aescbc</code> first means new writes are encrypted; keeping <code>identity</code> in the list lets the apiserver still <em>read</em> the old plaintext Secrets until you re-encrypt them. Step 2's <code>get … | replace</code> forces a re-write so everything ends up encrypted. Verify by reading the raw key directly from etcd — an encrypted value starts with <code>k8s:enc:aescbc:…</code>, not readable text.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Beyond aescbc — KMS v2</h5><p><code>aescbc</code> keeps the key in the config file on the node (better than nothing). Production uses a <strong>KMS provider</strong> (<code>kms:</code> v2): the apiserver asks an external key-management service (cloud KMS, Vault) to wrap a per-object data key — so the master key never touches the node. Same <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> flag, a different provider block.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how etcd actually stores &amp; commits your data</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>etcd's durability and consistency come from three pieces working together: a <strong>WAL</strong> (write-ahead log) for crash safety, a <strong>BoltDB</strong> B+tree for the keyspace, and <strong>MVCC</strong> revisions so every change is versioned. Understanding them explains snapshots, "database space exceeded" alarms, and why defrag exists.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   A WRITE (e.g. kubectl scale → apiserver → etcd)
     ① leader appends the change to its WAL (write-ahead log) on disk      ← crash-safe
     ② leader replicates the log entry to followers (Raft)
     ③ once a MAJORITY have persisted it → the entry is COMMITTED
     ④ each member applies it to its BoltDB B+tree (the keyspace) under a NEW revision
     ⑤ leader acks the client
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   MVCC: every write bumps a global revision; old revisions are kept (history) until COMPACTION
   COMPACTION drops old revisions (frees logical space) · DEFRAG returns freed space to the disk
   a SNAPSHOT = a consistent copy of the BoltDB file at one revision (what `snapshot save` writes)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why "database space exceeded" happens:</strong> etcd has a <strong>quota</strong> (<code>--quota-backend-bytes</code>, default ~2 GB). MVCC history + a busy cluster fill it; etcd then raises a <strong>NOSPACE alarm</strong> and goes <em>read-only</em> — the control plane freezes. Recovery: compact to an old revision, <code>defrag</code> to reclaim disk, then <code>etcdctl alarm disarm</code>. This is the deep version of "etcd is full and my cluster won't take writes."</div>

                    <h5>The on-disk layout — what a snapshot actually copies</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   /var/lib/etcd/member/
     ├─ wal/   write-ahead log — sequential, fsync'd; EVERY write lands here FIRST (durability)
     │         0000000000000001-….wal
     └─ snap/  periodic compacted snapshots + the live database
        └─ db  the BoltDB B+tree — all keys in sorted order   ← this file IS what `snapshot save` copies
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   MVCC in practice — every change is a NEW revision; old ones are KEPT until compaction:
     PUT    /registry/pods/easypj-dev/web   → revision 100
     PUT    /registry/pods/easypj-dev/web   → revision 101   (an update — 100 still on disk)
     DELETE /registry/pods/easypj-dev/web   → revision 102   (a tombstone, not an erase)
</pre>

                    <h5>Raft leader election — what happens when the etcd leader dies</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   followers stop hearing the leader's heartbeat
     1. a follower's election timeout fires (~1s, randomised so they don't all start at once)
     2. it becomes a CANDIDATE and increments the term
     3. it requests votes from the other members
     4. a MAJORITY votes yes → it's the new LEADER → starts sending heartbeats
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ⏱️ during an election (~1s) etcd is UNAVAILABLE FOR WRITES → the apiserver briefly can't persist.
      this is why etcd wants low-latency disks &amp; network, and an ODD member count for clean majorities.
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Beyond the basics</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>compaction &amp; defrag</strong></td><td><code>etcdctl compact &lt;rev&gt;</code> drops old MVCC revisions; <code>etcdctl defrag</code> returns the freed pages to the filesystem. <code>--auto-compaction-mode=periodic --auto-compaction-retention=8h</code> automates it.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NOSPACE alarm recovery</strong></td><td><code>etcdctl alarm list</code> → compact + defrag → <code>etcdctl alarm disarm</code> to take writes again.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>member &amp; learner management</strong></td><td><code>etcdctl member add/remove/list</code>; a <strong>learner</strong> joins read-only and catches up before voting — safer HA growth.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>quorum-loss recovery</strong></td><td>lost majority? restore a snapshot with <code>--force-new-cluster</code> on one survivor, then re-add members.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>performance tuning</strong></td><td><code>--heartbeat-interval</code>/<code>--election-timeout</code> for high-latency networks; etcd wants fast disks (it's fsync-bound).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>④ The deepest connection — how an etcd write becomes a cluster-wide event.</strong> MVCC revisions aren't just history; they're the engine behind <em>everything reactive</em> in Kubernetes. This is where Ch 11 (etcd), Ch 6 (<code>get -w</code>) and Ch 1 (controllers/informers) join up:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl scale deploy easypj-web --replicas=5
        ▼
   apiserver writes the new spec to etcd  →  etcd bumps the global REVISION (say 95620 → 95621)
        ▼
   etcd WATCH: every client watching that key range is pushed the change AT that revision
        ▼
   the apiserver is etcd's watcher → it fans the event out to ITS watchers:
        ReplicaSet controller (informer) sees replicas 3→5  →  creates 2 pods
        scheduler sees the new pods (nodeName="")           →  binds them
        kubelet on each node sees its bound pod             →  starts containers
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   resourceVersion (Ch 6) IS the etcd revision. A watch resumes from one; a stale watcher gets
   410 Gone and re-lists. The whole control loop is event-driven on top of etcd's MVCC watch stream.
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this is the "to the core" insight:</strong> nothing in Kubernetes polls. One etcd write at a new revision ripples — via the apiserver's watch — to every controller, the scheduler, and the kubelets, each reacting and writing <em>their</em> change back as a further revision. etcd isn't a passive database the cluster reads; it's the heartbeat whose every tick drives the next reconciliation. That's why losing it (this chapter) loses not just data but the cluster's ability to <em>act</em>.</div>

                    <h5>🔬 Kubernetes Key Space — How K8s Objects Map to etcd Keys</h5>
                    <p>Every Kubernetes resource is stored under a predictable etcd key path. Understanding this layout helps you use <code>etcdctl</code> effectively:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUBERNETES → ETCD KEY MAPPING                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  /registry/                                                                  │
│  ├── namespaces/                                                             │
│  │   ├── default           → Namespace object                                │
│  │   ├── kube-system                                                         │
│  │   └── easypj-prod                                                        │
│  │                                                                           │
│  ├── pods/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;pod-name&gt;    ← v1/Pod                                │
│  │   ├── default/nginx                                                        │
│  │   └── easypj-prod/easypj-web-7d8f9g6h-abcde                              │
│  │                                                                           │
│  ├── deployments/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt; ← apps/v1/Deployment                     │
│  │   └── easypj-prod/easypj-web                                              │
│  │                                                                           │
│  ├── replicasets/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt; ← apps/v1/ReplicaSet                      │
│  │   └── easypj-prod/easypj-web-7d8f9g6h                                    │
│  │                                                                           │
│  ├── services/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;  ← v1/Service                               │
│  │   ├── endpointslices/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt; ← discovery.k8s.io/v1/EndpointSlice│
│  │   └── easypj-prod/easypj-web-svc                                          │
│  │                                                                           │
│  ├── configmaps/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt; ← v1/ConfigMap                             │
│  ├── secrets/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;    ← v1/Secret                                │
│  ├── serviceaccounts/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt; ← v1/ServiceAccount                    │
│  ├── roles/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;       ← rbac/v1/Role                             │
│  ├── rolebindings/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt; ← rbac/v1/RoleBinding                      │
│  │                                                                           │
│  ├── clusterroles/&lt;name&gt;           ← rbac/v1/ClusterRole (CLUSTER-SCOPED)    │
│  ├── clusterrolebindings/&lt;name&gt;    ← rbac/v1/ClusterRoleBinding              │
│  ├── nodes/&lt;node-name&gt;             ← v1/Node                                 │
│  │                                                                           │
│  ├── minions/&lt;node-name&gt;           ← LEGACY (same as nodes/)                 │
│  ├── persistentvolumes/&lt;name&gt;      ← v1/PersistentVolume                     │
│  │                                                                           │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  NAMESPACED resources: /registry/&lt;resource&gt;/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;                  │
│  CLUSTER-SCOPED resources: /registry/&lt;resource&gt;/&lt;name&gt;                          │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>

                    <h5>👁️ etcd Watches — How Controllers Get Real-Time Updates</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes controllers don't poll etcd. They use <strong>watches</strong> — long-lived streaming connections that push changes in real time:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ETCD WATCH MECHANISM                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  1. Controller opens Watch on key prefix:                                    │
│     watch /registry/pods/  (monitor ALL pod changes)                         │
│                                                                              │
│  2. etcd sends INITIAL state (all existing pods):                            │
│     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐                 │
│     │ Event: PUT /registry/pods/default/nginx {revision:100}│                │
│     │ Event: PUT /registry/pods/default/easypj {revision:101}│              │
│     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘                 │
│                                                                              │
│  3. etcd streams INCREMENTAL changes (long-lived gRPC):                     │
│     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐                 │
│     │ Event: PUT /registry/pods/default/new-pod {rev:102}  │  ← Pod created  │
│     │ Event: PUT /registry/pods/default/nginx {rev:103}    │  ← Status update│
│     │ Event: DELETE /registry/pods/default/nginx {rev:104} │  ← Pod deleted  │
│     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘                 │
│                                                                              │
│  4. Controller processes each event → reconciles its resources              │
│                                                                              │
│  WATCH IS CRITICAL: If a controller's watch disconnects, it reconnects      │
│  and replays from the last seen revision. This is why controllers don't     │
│  miss events — level-triggered reconciliation catches anything missed.      │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">watch the pods key prefix live</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl watch /registry/pods --prefix \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre>
                    </div>

                    <h4>🎯 CKA etcd Quick Reference Card</h4>
                    <p>Print this or memorize it. These are the exact commands and paths you need for the CKA exam.</p>
                    <div class="comparison-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Item</th>
                                    <th>Path / Value</th>
                                    <th>Why You Need It</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>etcd CA cert</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</code></td>
                                    <td>--cacert flag for all etcdctl commands</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>etcd server cert</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</code></td>
                                    <td>--cert flag for all etcdctl commands</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>etcd server key</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></td>
                                    <td>--key flag for all etcdctl commands</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Client port</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>2379</code></td>
                                    <td>--endpoints HTTPS URL</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Peer port</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>2380</code></td>
                                    <td>etcd ↔ etcd communication</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Data directory</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>/var/lib/etcd</code></td>
                                    <td>--data-dir for restore</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Static pod manifest</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code></td>
                                    <td>Edit to change --data-dir after restore</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>API version env</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></td>
                                    <td>Always set this before etcdctl commands</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>etcd holds ALL cluster state</strong> at <code>/registry/*</code> — one snapshot = the whole cluster. Secrets are unencrypted by default.</li>
                    <li><strong>Backup:</strong> <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save</code> + <code>--endpoints</code> + the 3 TLS flags; verify with <code>snapshot status --write-table</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Restore</strong> into a <em>new</em> data-dir, then repoint <code>etcd.yaml</code> in <strong>two</strong> places (<code>--data-dir</code> flag AND the hostPath volume).</li>
                    <li><strong>Quorum = N/2+1</strong>; odd members (3 → tolerate 1, 5 → tolerate 2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Encryption at rest</strong> = <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> + <code>--encryption-provider-config</code>; re-encrypt with <code>get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Missing TLS flags.</strong> <code>snapshot save</code> without <code>--cacert/--cert/--key</code> = "context deadline exceeded."</li>
                    <li><strong>Restoring into the live data-dir.</strong> Always a NEW dir, or you corrupt running etcd.</li>
                    <li><strong>Updating only one of the two places</strong> in <code>etcd.yaml</code> (flag vs hostPath) → restore appears to fail.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></strong> → snapshot commands don't exist in v2.</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming etcd backs up PV data.</strong> It backs up <em>objects</em>, not volume contents.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Alias the TLS flags</strong> (see §11.3) so every etcdctl call is two words.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cert paths</strong> are always in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> — grep it instead of guessing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always take a fresh snapshot before a restore</strong> — your current state is itself worth saving.</li>
                    <li><code>etcdctl endpoint health</code> / <code>endpoint status --write-table</code> for a one-line health check.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 11)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the etcdctl toolkit (ETCDCTL_API=3)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># with the TLS flags (alias them):  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=… --cert=… --key=…</span>
etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db        <span class="comment"># BACK UP</span>
etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table   <span class="comment"># verify</span>
etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored   <span class="comment"># RESTORE</span>
etcdctl member list -w table                     <span class="comment"># cluster members</span>
etcdctl endpoint health ; etcdctl endpoint status -w table   <span class="comment"># health</span>
etcdctl alarm list ; etcdctl defrag ; etcdctl alarm disarm   <span class="comment"># NOSPACE recovery</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — back up, "lose" a Deployment, restore it</h5>
                <p>The exact CKA disaster-recovery loop. No peeking:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Snapshot etcd to <code>/opt/etcd-backup.db</code> and verify it.</li>
                    <li>Delete the <code>easypj-web</code> Deployment (the "disaster").</li>
                    <li>Restore the snapshot into a new data-dir and repoint <code>etcd.yaml</code>.</li>
                    <li>Confirm the Deployment is back.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal (control-plane node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">alias e='sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key'
e snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db ; e snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table

kubectl delete deploy easypj-web -n easypj-dev          <span class="comment"># 💥 disaster</span>

sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml            <span class="comment"># --data-dir + hostPath → /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># wait for etcd + apiserver to restart…</span>
kubectl get deploy easypj-web -n easypj-dev             <span class="comment"># it's back ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 11 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">etcd backup/restore recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the full <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> command with TLS flags.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">snapshot save</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Four inputs: endpoint + the three certs. Without them etcdctl can't authenticate to the TLS-protected etcd and times out.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Restore a snapshot and repoint the static pod — every step.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">restore</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># 1) - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># 2) hostPath volume etcd-data → path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet auto-restarts etcd; verify:</span>
kubectl get nodes</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>New data-dir, then update BOTH the flag and the hostPath — the most-failed half of this task is forgetting the volume.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do you find the 3 cert paths etcdctl needs?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find cert paths</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># --cert-file → --cert · --key-file → --key · --trusted-ca-file → --cacert</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>They're under <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code> by default, but reading them off the manifest is foolproof regardless of the cluster's layout.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Verify a snapshot file before trusting it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table
<span class="comment"># shows HASH · REVISION · TOTAL KEYS · TOTAL SIZE</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A non-zero key count and sane size confirm the file is a real, complete snapshot — not an empty/aborted one.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Quorum math: how many failures does a 5-member cluster tolerate?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>2.</strong> Quorum = N/2+1 = 5/2+1 = 3, so the cluster keeps working as long as 3 of 5 are up — i.e. it tolerates 2 failures.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>3 members tolerate 1; 5 tolerate 2; 7 tolerate 3. Even counts add risk without adding tolerance — always odd.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A restore failed with "data-dir not empty" — why, and the fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>You restored into an existing/used directory. Restore into a <strong>fresh</strong> path (or remove the target first): <code>--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code> where that dir doesn't already exist.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>snapshot restore</code> insists on creating a clean data-dir so it can't silently merge with live data — which is exactly the corruption it's protecting you from.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable encryption at rest for Secrets (outline).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Write an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> (provider <code>aescbc</code> with a 32-byte key, then <code>identity</code>), add <code>--encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml</code> (+ a hostPath volume) to the apiserver manifest, then re-write existing Secrets:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">re-encrypt</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>New writes encrypt immediately; the <code>replace</code> forces existing Secrets to be re-written (and thus encrypted). Verify by reading the raw etcd key — it should start <code>k8s:enc:aescbc:</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">External vs stacked etcd — one operational difference.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Stacked:</strong> etcd is a static pod on each control-plane node (kubeadm default) — the kubelet manages it. <strong>External:</strong> etcd runs on dedicated machines; during a restore you stop the apiservers manually (no kubelet auto-restart of etcd).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>External isolates etcd failures from the control plane and scales independently, at the cost of more machines to run.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Check etcd cluster health from the command line.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">health</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=… --cert=… --key=…
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list -w table</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>endpoint health</code> confirms each member responds; <code>member list</code> shows roles and whether the cluster has a leader.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Automate a nightly etcd backup.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A root <strong>cron</strong> entry on the control-plane node is simplest and most reliable (it doesn't depend on the cluster being healthy):</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/cron.d/etcd-backup</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">0 2 * * * root ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-$(date +\%F).db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Host cron beats a Kubernetes CronJob here: if the cluster is sick, a CronJob may not run — but the backup is exactly what you need then. Ship the files off-node and prune old ones.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 11 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style backup/restore/recovery tasks with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write the complete <code>etcdctl</code> command to take a snapshot backup of etcd to <code>/opt/etcd-backup.db</code>. Where do you find the required certificate paths?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find certificate paths from etcd static pod manifest</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find certificate paths from etcd static pod manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep -E "\-\-cert|\-\-key|\-\-ca"
<span class="comment"># --cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</span>
<span class="comment"># --trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Take the snapshot</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify the snapshot</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | abcdef12 |    45678 |       1234 |     5.2 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is a <strong>guaranteed CKA exam question</strong>. The three certificates are mandatory — etcd uses mutual TLS. The trick is knowing where to find them: <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> has the exact paths in its command arguments. Note the flag name differences: etcd uses <code>--cert-file</code> and <code>--trusted-ca-file</code>, but etcdctl uses <code>--cert</code> and <code>--cacert</code>. <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> must be set — API v2 uses different commands and snapshot formats. Always verify with <code>snapshot status</code> to confirm the backup is valid.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write the complete procedure to restore etcd from a snapshot at <code>/opt/etcd-backup.db</code>. What must you update after restoring?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">restore to a new data directory</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Restore to a NEW data directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Update etcd manifest to use the new data directory</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># Change TWO places:</span>
<span class="comment"># a) In container command args:</span>
<span class="comment">#    --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># b) In volumes/volumeMounts hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#    volumes:</span>
<span class="comment">#    - name: etcd-data</span>
<span class="comment">#      hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#        path: /var/lib/etcd-restored    ← Update this</span>
<span class="comment">#        type: DirectoryOrCreate</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Wait for the API server to recover (30-60 seconds)</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects manifest change → restarts etcd → API server reconnects</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl get pods -A
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The restore creates a <strong>brand new</strong> data directory — you must NOT restore to the original <code>/var/lib/etcd</code> because the member IDs won't match. After restoring, you update the etcd manifest in two places: (1) the <code>--data-dir</code> flag in the container command, and (2) the <code>hostPath</code> volume mount. kubelet detects the manifest change and restarts etcd with the restored data. The API server, scheduler, and controller-manager automatically reconnect. Give it 30-60 seconds — don't panic if kubectl is temporarily unavailable.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between etcd's <code>--cert-file</code>/<code>--key-file</code> and <code>--peer-cert-file</code>/<code>--peer-key-file</code>?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Flag</th>
                                            <th>Purpose</th>
                                            <th>Who Connects</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>--cert-file</code></td>
                                            <td>Server cert for client connections</td>
                                            <td>API server → etcd</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>--key-file</code></td>
                                            <td>Server key for client connections</td>
                                            <td>API server → etcd</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>--peer-cert-file</code></td>
                                            <td>Cert for etcd-to-etcd communication</td>
                                            <td>etcd ↔ etcd (HA cluster)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>--peer-key-file</code></td>
                                            <td>Key for etcd-to-etcd communication</td>
                                            <td>etcd ↔ etcd (HA cluster)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">in a single-node etcd</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># In a single-node etcd, peer certs are still configured</span>
<span class="comment"># but only matter in multi-node (HA) etcd clusters</span>

<span class="comment"># Find all cert paths:</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep -E "cert|key|ca"</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd uses two sets of TLS certificates: <strong>client certs</strong> (for API server connections) and <strong>peer certs</strong> (for etcd member replication). In a single control-plane cluster, peer certs exist but aren't actively used for multi-node communication. In HA clusters with 3 or 5 etcd members, peer certs are critical — they secure the Raft consensus protocol traffic between members. On the CKA, for backup/restore you use the <strong>client</strong> certs (<code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code> with etcdctl), not the peer certs.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you check the health of the etcd cluster? What commands show the member list and cluster status?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check etcd health</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check etcd health</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># 127.0.0.1:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal</span>

<span class="comment"># Check etcd member list (HA clusters)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key --write-table
<span class="comment"># Shows all etcd members, their status, and client URLs</span>

<span class="comment"># Check etcd endpoint status (detailed)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint status \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key --write-table
<span class="comment"># Shows DB size, leader, raft index, raft term</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick check via kubectl (simpler)</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pods | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># etcd-controlplane   1/1   Running   0   5d</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd healthchecks are essential before and after backup/restore operations. <code>endpoint health</code> verifies the etcd instance can accept writes. <code>member list</code> shows all members in the cluster (single or HA). <code>endpoint status</code> gives detailed info including DB size and who's the leader. All etcdctl commands need the three cert flags — without them, the connection is refused. On the CKA, always verify etcd health after restore: if the endpoint is unhealthy, check that the <code>--data-dir</code> path matches the restored directory.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the default endpoint for etcd? How do you find the actual endpoint if it's been changed? What's the difference between stacked and external etcd?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">default endpoint</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Default endpoint</span>
https://127.0.0.1:2379

<span class="comment"># Find actual endpoint from etcd manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep listen-client
<span class="comment"># --listen-client-urls=https://127.0.0.1:2379,https://10.0.0.10:2379</span>

<span class="comment"># Or from API server (how API server connects to etcd)</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># --etcd-servers=https://127.0.0.1:2379</span>

<span class="comment"># Stacked etcd: runs ON the control plane node (most common)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Static pod in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># - Data stored at /var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># - Managed by kubelet</span>

<span class="comment"># External etcd: runs on SEPARATE dedicated nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not a static pod — standalone systemd service</span>
<span class="comment"># - API server connects via --etcd-servers=https://&lt;external-ip&gt;:2379</span>
<span class="comment"># - Common in production HA setups</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>In CKA exam environments, etcd is almost always stacked (running on the control plane). But the exam may present external etcd scenarios for backup/restore questions. The key difference: for stacked etcd, use <code>127.0.0.1:2379</code> and certs from <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code>. For external etcd, use the external node's IP and potentially different cert paths. Always check the API server manifest's <code>--etcd-servers</code> flag to confirm the endpoint, and the etcd manifest's <code>--listen-client-urls</code> for what etcd is actually listening on.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You restored etcd from a backup, but some pods that were created AFTER the backup was taken are now missing. Is this expected? What happened?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yes — this is expected behavior</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># YES — this is expected behavior</span>

<span class="comment"># Timeline:</span>
<span class="comment"># T=0:   etcd backup taken (snapshot)</span>
<span class="comment"># T=1:   New deployment "webapp" created (pod exists)</span>
<span class="comment"># T=2:   New configmap "app-config" created</span>
<span class="comment"># T=3:   etcd restore from T=0 snapshot</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># After restore: cluster state = T=0</span>
<span class="comment"># "webapp" deployment is GONE — it didn't exist at T=0</span>
<span class="comment"># "app-config" is GONE — same reason</span>

<span class="comment"># What IS preserved:</span>
<span class="comment"># - All objects that existed at backup time</span>
<span class="comment"># - All RBAC rules from backup time</span>
<span class="comment"># - All namespaces from backup time</span>

<span class="comment"># What IS NOT preserved:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Any objects created AFTER the backup</span>
<span class="comment"># - Any modifications made AFTER the backup</span>
<span class="comment"># - Running pod state (pods restart from desired state)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd restore is a <strong>point-in-time recovery</strong> — it rolls the entire cluster state back to when the backup was taken. Everything after that point is lost. This is why regular backups are critical and why the CKA tests this scenario. In production, etcd snapshots are taken every few hours via CronJobs. The lesson: backups are only as good as their frequency. After a restore, you'll need to recreate any resources that were added between the backup and the restore. Running containers are killed and re-created based on the restored desired state.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">During an etcd restore, you get the error: <em>"Error: data-dir '/var/lib/etcd-restored' already exists"</em>. What should you do?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the restore target directory must be empty or…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The restore target directory must be EMPTY or non-existent</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 1: Use a different directory name</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored-v2

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Remove the existing directory first</span>
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/etcd-restored
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># IMPORTANT: Update etcd manifest to match the new path</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># Update --data-dir AND hostPath volume path</span>

<span class="comment"># Wait for etcd to restart with new data</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcdctl refuses to restore to an existing directory to prevent accidental data loss. Use a unique directory name each time. On the CKA, if you've already done one restore attempt and need to retry, either remove the directory or use a different name. Remember to update BOTH locations in the etcd manifest: the <code>--data-dir</code> command argument AND the <code>hostPath.path</code> in the volumes section. If they don't match, etcd starts with an empty data directory and the cluster appears empty.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you list all keys stored in etcd? How would you find a specific pod's data in etcd?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all keys in etcd (top-level prefixes)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all keys in etcd (top-level prefixes)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get / --prefix --keys-only | head -20 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Kubernetes stores everything under /registry/</span>
<span class="comment"># /registry/pods/default/nginx</span>
<span class="comment"># /registry/deployments/kube-system/coredns</span>
<span class="comment"># /registry/services/specs/default/kubernetes</span>
<span class="comment"># /registry/secrets/default/my-secret</span>

<span class="comment"># Find a specific pod</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/pods/default/nginx \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Count total keys</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get / --prefix --keys-only \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | wc -l</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd stores all Kubernetes objects under <code>/registry/&lt;resource-type&gt;/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;</code>. The data is stored as protocol buffer (protobuf) format, not plain JSON — so the values look like binary. This is rarely needed on the CKA, but understanding the key structure helps debug issues like: "Why does my deleted namespace still show up?" (check if keys still exist). Secrets are stored base64-encoded by default in etcd. If <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> is set up, they're also encrypted at rest.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What happens to the Kubernetes cluster if etcd goes down? Which operations continue to work and which don't?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>✅ Still Works</th>
                                            <th>❌ Doesn't Work</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Running pods continue running</td>
                                            <td>Can't create new pods/resources</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Existing Services route traffic</td>
                                            <td>Can't update or delete anything</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Container processes keep running</td>
                                            <td>No scheduling of new pods</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Node-local operations (kubelet)</td>
                                            <td>No self-healing (crashed pods stay down)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd is the cluster's <strong>source of truth</strong> but not the runtime engine. Running workloads continue because kubelet manages containers locally. However, the API server can't write to etcd, so all mutating operations fail. Controllers can't reconcile (no self-healing), the scheduler can't schedule (no new pods), and kubectl commands that modify state fail. Read operations may work briefly from API server cache, then fail. This is why etcd availability is critical in production — and why HA clusters run 3 or 5 etcd members with Raft consensus.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write a complete etcd backup and restore script that could be used as a CronJob for automated backups. Include verification steps.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
<span class="comment"># etcd-backup.sh — Automated etcd backup script</span>

<span class="comment"># Variables</span>
BACKUP_DIR="/opt/etcd-backups"
TIMESTAMP=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)
BACKUP_FILE="${BACKUP_DIR}/etcd-snapshot-${TIMESTAMP}.db"
CACERT="/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt"
CERT="/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt"
KEY="/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key"

<span class="comment"># Create backup directory</span>
mkdir -p ${BACKUP_DIR}

<span class="comment"># Take snapshot</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save ${BACKUP_FILE} \
  --cacert=${CACERT} --cert=${CERT} --key=${KEY}

<span class="comment"># Verify snapshot</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status ${BACKUP_FILE} \
  --write-table

<span class="comment"># Keep only last 7 backups</span>
ls -tp ${BACKUP_DIR}/*.db | tail -n +8 | xargs -r rm --

echo "Backup completed: ${BACKUP_FILE}"

<span class="comment"># To restore (manual process):</span>
<span class="comment"># ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore ${BACKUP_FILE} \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># Then update /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Automated backups are essential in production. This script takes a timestamped snapshot, verifies it, and keeps only the last 7 backups (rotation). On the CKA, you won't need to write a full script, but understanding the flow is important. The exam tests individual commands — primarily backup (save) and restore. Remember: backup is non-disruptive (online snapshot), but restore requires etcd to restart, which briefly disrupts the cluster. Always take a fresh backup BEFORE performing cluster upgrades or major changes.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 11 Visual Summary — the cluster's memory, saved &amp; restorable</div>
<pre>
   🗄️ etcd = the cluster's memory   (/registry/* — every object; Secrets unencrypted by default)
        │  Raft: leader + followers · quorum N/2+1 · odd members (3→tolerate 1, 5→tolerate 2)
        ▼
   BACKUP   ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db  --endpoints --cacert --cert --key
        │   verify → snapshot status --write-table
        │
   ─ ─ ─│─ ─ ─ ─ 💥 DISASTER ─ ─ ─ ─
        ▼
   RESTORE  etcdctl snapshot restore … --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-RESTORED   (a NEW dir!)
        │   edit etcd.yaml:  --data-dir  AND  hostPath volume  → BOTH
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet restarts etcd → ✅ cluster back at the snapshot point
   SECURE   TLS in transit (default) · EncryptionConfiguration + --encryption-provider-config (at rest)   ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> etcd is the only place cluster state lives, kept consistent by Raft (writes commit on a majority; members are odd so quorum is meaningful). A backup is one <code>snapshot save</code> with the endpoint and three TLS certs — always verified with <code>snapshot status</code>. A restore reads that snapshot into a <em>new</em> data-dir, and you repoint the etcd static pod by editing <code>etcd.yaml</code> in two places.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> memorise the four backup inputs and the two restore edits, and disaster recovery is a 2-minute, free-marks task. Then secure what you backed up — TLS protects the wire, and <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> protects Secrets at rest so a leaked snapshot isn't a breach.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 11 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 12 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch12">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 12 · Managing the Control Plane</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">The control plane runs <em>as pods</em> — specifically <strong>static pods</strong> the kubelet manages from files on disk. That makes managing it a matter of reading logs and editing manifests, often with the apiserver (and <code>kubectl</code>) <strong>down</strong>. This chapter is where Cluster Architecture meets Troubleshooting: inspect health with and without kubectl, modify components safely, and recover from a bad edit.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Arch 25% + Troubleshoot 30%</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎛️ Control Plane</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The control plane <strong>as static pods</strong> — what's in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and who owns them</li>
                    <li>✅ Inspect control-plane health — with kubectl, and with <code>crictl</code> + <code>journalctl</code> when it's down</li>
                    <li>✅ Modify a component safely: back up → edit → wait → verify → recover</li>
                    <li>✅ Tune the scheduler &amp; controller-manager (profiles, PriorityClass, eviction timing)</li>
                    <li>✅ The three extension interfaces as a set: <strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">control-plane recovery <span class="cka-weight">CORE TASK</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 Debugging when <code>kubectl</code> itself is dead</h5>
                <p>The defining skill here is working <em>without</em> kubectl — because a control-plane problem often takes the apiserver offline. The exam loves "the control plane is broken, fix it."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubectl works</strong> → <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> + component logs.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl is dead</strong> → on the node: <code>sudo crictl ps -a</code>, <code>sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Recovery is always:</strong> make the manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> valid — the kubelet does the rest.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 12 Roadmap — read, fix &amp; tune the control plane</div>
<pre>
   12.1 static pods ──▶ 12.2 inspect health ──▶ 12.3 modify safely ──▶ 12.4 tune sched/ctrl-mgr ──▶ 12.5 CRI·CNI·CSI
   manifests dir          kubectl / crictl /        back up→edit→            profiles · PriorityClass    the 3 plug
   kubelet owns them      journalctl (when down)    verify→recover           eviction timing             points
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-1"><strong>12.1</strong> Static pods</a></td><td>What IS the control plane, physically?</td><td>4 manifests in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>; the kubelet runs them.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-2"><strong>12.2</strong> Inspect health</a></td><td>How do I tell what's broken?</td><td>kubectl when up; <code>crictl</code> + <code>journalctl</code> when down.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-3"><strong>12.3</strong> Modify components</a></td><td>How do I change a flag without breaking it?</td><td>back up → edit → wait ~30-60s → verify → recover.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-4"><strong>12.4</strong> Sched/ctrl-mgr tuning</a></td><td>How do I influence placement &amp; timing?</td><td>scheduler profiles · PriorityClass · eviction/monitor timers.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-5"><strong>12.5</strong> CRI · CNI · CSI</a></td><td>What makes Kubernetes pluggable?</td><td>three sockets: runtime · network · storage.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.1 ─────────── -->

            <section class="section-block" id="s12-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.1 The Control Plane as Static Pods</h3>
                <p>On a kubeadm cluster, the control plane isn't a set of system services — it's four <strong>static pods</strong>. The kubelet watches <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and keeps a pod running for each YAML there. No scheduler, no Deployment, no apiserver is involved in their lifecycle — which is exactly why they can bring the cluster up <em>before</em> the apiserver exists, and why you manage them by editing files.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">/etc/kubernetes/manifests/ — edit a file, the kubelet restarts the pod</div>
<pre>
   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
     ├─ etcd.yaml                     ┐
     ├─ kube-apiserver.yaml           ├─ 🤖 the kubelet WATCHES this directory
     ├─ kube-controller-manager.yaml  │     edit a file   → pod auto-restarts (~30-60s)
     └─ kube-scheduler.yaml           ┘     remove a file → pod is deleted
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   each running static pod is reflected into the API as a read-only "mirror pod"
   (that's why `kubectl get pods -n kube-system` shows kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; etc.)
   ⚠️ a YAML typo here = the apiserver won't start = kubectl dies. Fix the file → kubelet recovers it.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Static pod vs mirror pod</h5><p>The kubelet runs the <strong>static pod</strong> directly from the file. It also creates a <strong>mirror pod</strong> in the apiserver so the pod is <em>visible</em> via <code>kubectl</code> — but that mirror is read-only: <code>kubectl delete pod kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt;</code> just makes the kubelet recreate it. To actually stop one, move its manifest out of the directory (a handy trick to force-restart a component: <code>mv … /tmp; sleep 5; mv … back</code>).</p></div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">static pod vs regular pod — how each is managed</div>
<pre>
   REGULAR POD                                  STATIC POD
   ┌───────────────────────────┐               ┌───────────────────────────┐
   │ kubectl apply ─▶ apiserver │               │ YAML placed in            │
   │      ─▶ etcd               │               │ /etc/kubernetes/manifests │
   │           │                │               │        │                  │
   │           ▼                │               │        ▼                  │
   │ scheduler assigns a node   │               │ kubelet detects the file  │
   │           │                │               │ &amp; runs the pod directly    │
   │           ▼                │               │ on THIS node              │
   │ kubelet runs the pod       │               │                           │
   │ ✅ managed by the apiserver │               │ ✅ managed by the kubelet  │
   │ ✅ can run on any node      │               │ ❌ only the local node     │
   │ ✅ kubectl delete works     │               │ ❌ kubectl delete = no-op  │
   │ ✅ can be scaled            │               │ ❌ cannot be scaled        │
   └───────────────────────────┘               └───────────────────────────┘
   the apiserver creates a read-only "mirror pod" for each static pod so it shows in kubectl.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>File placed</h5><p>YAML dropped in the manifests dir</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>kubelet detects</h5><p>inotify + periodic resync</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Mirror created</h5><p>read-only copy POSTed to the API</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Auto-restart</h5><p>edit the file → pod recreated</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>File removed</h5><p>delete the file → pod deleted</p></div>
                </div>
                <h4>🔎 Telling a static pod from a regular one — the identification cheat sheet</h4>
                <p>On the exam you may need to prove a pod is static (or find which node owns it). Three tells distinguish them:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Static pod</th><th>Regular pod</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Name pattern</td><td><code>&lt;component&gt;-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> (e.g. <code>etcd-controlplane</code>)</td><td><code>&lt;deploy&gt;-&lt;hash&gt;-&lt;hash&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ownerReferences.kind</code></td><td><strong>Node</strong> (the kubelet owns it)</td><td>ReplicaSet / DaemonSet / Job</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/config.source</code> annotation</td><td><code>file</code></td><td><code>api</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deleted with <code>kubectl delete</code>?</td><td>❌ recreated from the file</td><td>✅ gone (or recreated by its controller)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">identify a static pod</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].kind}'
<span class="comment"># Node   ← it's static (owned by the kubelet, not a controller)</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system -o yaml | grep 'config.source'
<span class="comment"># kubernetes.io/config.source: file</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath   <span class="comment"># where the kubelet looks</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>💥 What breaks when each component fails</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Component down</th><th>What still works</th><th>What breaks</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>running pods keep running; <strong>Services keep routing</strong> (kube-proxy's iptables/IPVS rules are cached)</td><td>ALL <code>kubectl</code>, all controllers, all scheduling — the cluster is "frozen" (no changes possible)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>running pods run &amp; Services route (cached rules)</td><td>apiserver returns errors / can't read-write state → effectively a dead control plane</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>existing pods run; kubectl works</td><td>NEW pods stay <code>Pending</code> (nothing assigns them a node)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>existing pods run; kubectl works</td><td>no self-healing — a deleted pod isn't recreated, nodes aren't marked NotReady, etc.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet (a node)</strong></td><td>other nodes fine</td><td>that node goes <code>NotReady</code>; its pods aren't managed</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 This matrix IS the diagnosis</h5><p>"New pods stuck Pending but kubectl works" → scheduler. "Deleted pods don't come back" → controller-manager. "kubectl itself is dead" → apiserver or etcd. Reading the symptom backwards to the component is half of every control-plane troubleshooting task.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s12-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.2 Inspecting Control-Plane Health</h3>
                <p>How you inspect the control plane depends on whether the apiserver is up. When it is, kubectl shows everything; when it isn't, you drop to the node and talk to the container runtime and the kubelet directly.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">when kubectl WORKS</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -n kube-system            <span class="comment"># every control-plane + addon pod</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E 'apiserver|etcd|scheduler|controller'
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-scheduler-control-plane   <span class="comment"># a component's logs</span>
kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'        <span class="comment"># apiserver self-check, per gate</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses             <span class="comment"># legacy/deprecated, but still appears on the exam</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">when kubectl is DEAD (on the node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo crictl ps -a | grep -E 'apiserver|etcd|scheduler|controller'   <span class="comment"># container states (incl. exited)</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;            <span class="comment"># WHY a component is crash-looping</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager  <span class="comment"># the kubelet's view (static-pod errors)</span>
ls -l /etc/kubernetes/manifests/          <span class="comment"># are the manifests present &amp; intact?</span>
sudo crictl ps                            <span class="comment"># is the apiserver container even running?</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Health endpoint (on the node)</th><th>Secure port</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td><code>curl -k https://localhost:6443/readyz?verbose</code></td><td>6443</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td><code>curl -k https://localhost:10259/healthz</code></td><td>10259</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td><code>curl -k https://localhost:10257/healthz</code></td><td>10257</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td><code>etcdctl endpoint health</code> (TLS) — or <code>https://localhost:2379/health</code></td><td>2379 / 2380</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td><code>curl -k https://localhost:10250/healthz</code></td><td>10250</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 The health endpoints</h5><p>Each component serves liveness/readiness over HTTP: <code>/healthz</code> (overall), <code>/livez</code> (alive), <code>/readyz</code> (ready to serve) — add <code>?verbose</code> to see each individual check. The apiserver's <code>readyz</code> is what a load balancer probes before sending traffic to a control-plane node; a single failing gate there explains an "intermittent apiserver."</p></div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ <code>crictl</code> needs its endpoint</h5><p>If <code>crictl</code> warns about the runtime endpoint, set it: <code>export CONTAINER_RUNTIME_ENDPOINT=unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code> (or put it in <code>/etc/crictl.yaml</code>). <code>crictl</code> is your apiserver-independent window into what containers are actually doing on the node.</p></div>
                <h4>🩺 The kubectl-less troubleshooting decision tree</h4>
                <p>When <code>kubectl</code> itself is dead, follow this top-down on the control-plane node — it walks you from "is the kubelet even up?" to the exact failing manifest:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">control plane down → find &amp; fix it without kubectl</div>
<pre>
   kubectl get pods -n kube-system
     │
     ├─ ✅ works ─────────▶ normal debugging: check pod status, logs, events
     │
     └─ ❌ "connection refused" / timeout  ─▶  SSH to the control-plane node, then:
           │
           ├─ 1. systemctl status kubelet
           │        active (running) → kubelet OK, go to step 2
           │        inactive/failed  → sudo systemctl start kubelet (then journalctl -u kubelet)
           │
           ├─ 2. sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube
           │        all Running   → likely a network/cert issue, check logs
           │        some Exited   → note the exit codes → step 3
           │        none at all   → manifests missing? → step 4
           │
           ├─ 3. sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;        → read the error → fix the manifest
           │
           ├─ 4. ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
           │        all 4 .yaml present → check their content (bad flag/typo)
           │        a file is missing!  → restore from /tmp backup (or kubeadm init phase …)
           │
           └─ 5. sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50   → the root cause, in plain text
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s12-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.3 Modifying Components Safely</h3>
                <p>Changing any control-plane component means editing its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. The kubelet restarts the pod on save — so a good edit is invisible and a bad one takes the component down. The same five-step ritual makes it safe every time.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml (excerpt)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: kube-apiserver
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  containers:
  - command:                              <span class="comment"># ← ALL the apiserver flags live here</span>
    - kube-apiserver
    - --advertise-address=192.168.56.10
    - --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC
    - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction
    - --etcd-servers=https://127.0.0.1:2379
    - --etcd-cafile=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt
    - --service-cluster-ip-range=10.96.0.0/12
    - --tls-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
    - --tls-private-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key
    image: registry.k8s.io/kube-apiserver:v1.34.0
    volumeMounts:
    - mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/pki      <span class="comment"># ← cert files mounted into the pod</span>
      name: k8s-certs
      readOnly: true
  hostNetwork: true                       <span class="comment"># ← uses the node's network directly</span>
  volumes:
  - name: k8s-certs
    hostPath:
      path: /etc/kubernetes/pki           <span class="comment"># ← from the node's filesystem</span>
      type: DirectoryOrCreate</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Reading this manifest</h5><p>Two things to internalise: <strong>(1)</strong> every apiserver setting is just a line in the <code>command:</code> list — adding a flag means adding a list item. <strong>(2)</strong> the cert files it references are <code>hostPath</code> volumes from the node's <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code> — so a flag pointing at a cert that isn't mounted will crash the pod. <code>hostNetwork: true</code> is why the apiserver binds the node's real <code>:6443</code>.</p></div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the safe edit ritual — back up → edit → wait → verify → recover</div>
<pre>
   ① BACK UP    sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml /tmp/
        ▼
   ② EDIT       add/change a --flag under command:  (sudo vim …)
        ▼
   ③ WAIT       ~30-60s — the kubelet kills the old pod &amp; starts a new one
        ▼
   ④ VERIFY     crictl ps | grep &lt;comp&gt;  (new container ID) · get pods -n kube-system
        ▼
   ⑤ RECOVER    broke it? sudo cp /tmp/&lt;file&gt; /etc/kubernetes/manifests/  → kubelet relaunches
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · modify a component</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml /tmp/   <span class="comment"># ① back up</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml       <span class="comment"># ② edit a flag</span>
watch -n2 'sudo crictl ps | grep controller'    <span class="comment"># ③/④ wait for a new container ID</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-controller-manager-$(hostname) | tail   <span class="comment"># verify it's healthy</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kube-controller-manager.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/  <span class="comment"># ⑤ recover if needed</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 A typo in <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> is special</h5><p>It takes <code>kubectl</code> down with it, so you can't verify or recover <em>through</em> kubectl — work on the node with <code>crictl logs</code> and restore the backup. This is the same recovery you drilled in Ch 10 §10.3; the apiserver is the one component where the backup isn't optional.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s12-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.4 Scheduler &amp; Controller-Manager Tuning</h3>
                <p>Two control-plane components have behaviour you'll occasionally tune: the <strong>scheduler</strong> (how pods are placed) and the <strong>controller-manager</strong> (how fast the cluster reacts to node failures). Both are configured the same way — flags in their static-pod manifest — and both elect a single active leader.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>kube-scheduler</h5><p>Assigns pods to nodes via <strong>filter → score → bind</strong>. Tune with <strong>scheduler profiles</strong> (<code>--config</code>, custom plugin weights) and influence priority with <strong>PriorityClass</strong> objects (higher priority can preempt lower).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div><h5>kube-controller-manager</h5><p>Runs the reconciliation loops. Key timing flags: <code>--node-monitor-grace-period</code> (how long before a silent node is marked NotReady) and the eviction timing that follows.</p></div>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Flag (component)</th><th>Default</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>--node-monitor-grace-period</code> (c-m)</td><td>~50s</td><td>how long without a kubelet heartbeat before a node → <code>NotReady</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--node-monitor-period</code> (c-m)</td><td>5s</td><td>how often node status is checked</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>taint-based eviction (<code>tolerationSeconds</code>)</td><td>300s</td><td>how long a pod tolerates a <code>NotReady</code>/<code>unreachable</code> node before eviction</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--leader-elect</code> (sched &amp; c-m)</td><td>true</td><td>only one replica is active at a time (HA via a Lease)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">a PriorityClass (so critical pods win)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: easypj-critical
value: 1000000             <span class="comment"># higher = scheduled first, can preempt lower-priority pods</span>
globalDefault: false
description: "for easypj's must-run web tier"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 When you'd touch these</h5><p>Lowering <code>--node-monitor-grace-period</code> makes the cluster react to dead nodes faster (quicker pod rescheduling) at the cost of being twitchier on flaky networks. <strong>PriorityClass</strong> + preemption guarantees critical workloads get a node even when the cluster is full. Custom <strong>scheduler profiles</strong> let one scheduler run multiple policies (Ch 14 covers placement in depth).</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s12-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.5 The Extension Interfaces — CRI · CNI · CSI</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes ships almost no opinion about <em>how</em> to run containers, network pods, or attach storage. Instead it defines three <strong>pluggable interfaces</strong> and lets you choose the implementation. Knowing the trio as a set explains how the whole system fits together — and why "the runtime/network/storage" is always someone's plugin, not built-in.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">three sockets that make Kubernetes pluggable</div>
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet ───CRI──▶ 🐳 container runtime     "run this container"     (containerd · CRI-O)
   📦 pod     ───CNI──▶ 🌐 network plugin        "give this pod an IP &amp; routes"  (Calico · Cilium · Flannel)
   💽 volume  ───CSI──▶ 🗄️ storage driver        "attach/mount this volume"   (EBS · Ceph · local-path)
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   each is a stable API contract → swap the implementation without changing Kubernetes itself.
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>Stands for</th><th>Who calls it</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>CRI</strong></td><td>Container Runtime Interface</td><td>the kubelet, to run containers</td><td>containerd, CRI-O</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CNI</strong></td><td>Container Network Interface</td><td>the kubelet, on pod create, to wire networking</td><td>Calico, Cilium, Flannel (Ch 9 §9.4)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CSI</strong></td><td>Container Storage Interface</td><td>kubelet + controllers, to provision/attach/mount volumes</td><td>AWS EBS, Ceph, local-path (Ch 22)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why three separate interfaces?</h5><p>Decoupling lets each layer evolve and compete independently — you can run containerd with Cilium and the EBS CSI driver, or any other mix, and Kubernetes neither knows nor cares. It's the same extensibility philosophy as API groups and CRDs: a stable contract at the boundary, swappable implementations behind it. CRI failures show as pods stuck creating; CNI failures as <code>NotReady</code>/no pod IPs; CSI failures as volumes stuck attaching.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — leader election &amp; how static pods appear in the API</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Two mechanics explain how the control plane stays both <em>highly available</em> and <em>visible</em>: the scheduler and controller-manager use <strong>leader election</strong> so only one is ever active, and the kubelet creates a <strong>mirror pod</strong> so each static pod shows up in <code>kubectl</code>.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   LEADER ELECTION (scheduler &amp; controller-manager, when you run more than one)
     every replica races to update a Lease object:
       kube-system/kube-scheduler   ·   kube-system/kube-controller-manager
     the one that holds the Lease is ACTIVE; others stand by, watching renewTime
     leader dies → its Lease expires (~15s) → a standby grabs it → becomes active
       (this is why HA control planes run 3 of each, but only ONE does work at a time)

   STATIC POD → MIRROR POD
     kubelet runs /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml directly (no scheduler)
     kubelet also POSTs a read-only "mirror pod" to the apiserver → kubectl can SEE it
     deleting the mirror pod doesn't stop it — the kubelet just recreates it from the file
</pre>

                    <h5>① The component interconnection map — who finds whom, and how</h5>
                    <p>The control plane isn't a monolith — it's 5+ independent processes that discover each other through <strong>kubeconfig files, localhost ports, and certs</strong>. Every arrow below is a connection that can break (wrong endpoint, expired cert, bad kubeconfig), so knowing the wiring <em>is</em> the troubleshooting map:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
                         CONTROL PLANE NODE  ·  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
   ┌──────────────┐         ┌───────────────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ 🗄️ etcd.yaml  │◀────────│ 📡 kube-apiserver.yaml     │────────▶│ 🤖 kubelet (NOT static)   │
   │ listen :2379  │  etcd   │   --etcd-servers=          │ kubelet │   --kubeconfig=           │
   │       :2380   │  client │     https://127.0.0.1:2379 │ client  │     /etc/.../kubelet.conf │
   └──────────────┘  certs  │   listen :6443 (TLS)       │ cert    └──────────────────────────┘
   ┌──────────────┐         │   --kubelet-client-cert=…  │         ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ 📋 scheduler  │────────▶│                            │◀────────│ 🔄 controller-manager     │
   │ --kubeconfig= │ schedu- └───────────────────────────┘ ctrl-mgr│   --kubeconfig=           │
   │  scheduler.conf│ ler.conf      ▲                       .conf    │     controller-manager.conf│
   └──────────────┘               │ ServiceAccount token            └──────────────────────────┘
                          🔀 kube-proxy (DaemonSet, every node) ─────┘
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Connects to</th><th>Auth method</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver</code></td><td>etcd (127.0.0.1:2379)</td><td>etcd client certs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver</code></td><td>kubelet (on each node)</td><td><code>apiserver-kubelet-client</code> cert</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-controller-manager</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td><code>controller-manager.conf</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-scheduler</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td><code>scheduler.conf</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubelet</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td><code>kubelet.conf</code> (bootstrap → rotated cert)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-proxy</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td>ServiceAccount token</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Read this as a fault tree:</strong> "controller-manager can't reach the API" → its <code>--kubeconfig</code> (<code>controller-manager.conf</code>) or that cert. "apiserver can't reach etcd" → its <code>--etcd-servers</code> + etcd client certs. Every control-plane failure is a broken <em>edge</em> in this graph — find the edge, fix the file/cert it points at.</div>

                    <h5>② Leader election — how HA control planes avoid split-brain</h5>
                    <p>Run more than one control plane and you have multiple schedulers and controller-managers — but only <strong>one of each may act</strong>, or two would fight (double-scheduling, double-scaling). They coordinate through a <strong>Lease</strong> object in etcd:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl -n kube-system get leases
   NAME                      HOLDER                       AGE
   kube-controller-manager   controlplane-1_abc123        90d
   kube-scheduler            controlplane-1_ghi789        90d

   THE MECHANISM (per component)
     1. each instance tries to write a Lease:
          holderIdentity: controlplane-1_abc123 · leaseDurationSeconds: 15 · renewTime: <ts>
     2. only the one that writes its identity WINS → becomes the LEADER (others stand by)
     3. the leader RENEWS the lease every ~10s (a heartbeat into etcd)
     4. leader fails to renew within 15s → lease expires → a standby grabs it → new leader
     5. old leader comes back, sees a DIFFERENT holderIdentity → steps down  (NO split-brain)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   leader-elected: kube-controller-manager · kube-scheduler   (etcd uses Raft instead; the
   apiserver is stateless — every replica is active behind the load balancer)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters:</strong> on an HA cluster, "the scheduler isn't scheduling" might mean the <em>active</em> leader is wedged while standbys wait — <code>kubectl -n kube-system get lease kube-scheduler -o yaml</code> shows <code>holderIdentity</code> + <code>renewTime</code> (a stale renewTime = the holder is dead but hasn't released). And knowing static pods are file-driven is the whole recovery model: you never <code>kubectl delete</code> a control-plane pod to fix it — you fix its <em>file</em>.</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Beyond the basics</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>lease timing</strong></td><td><code>--leader-elect-lease-duration</code>/<code>-renew-deadline</code>/<code>-retry-period</code> tune failover speed vs stability; defaults give ~15s failover.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>secure ports &amp; bind-address</strong></td><td>components serve metrics/health on <code>10257</code>/<code>10259</code> bound to <code>127.0.0.1</code> by default; <code>--bind-address</code> exposes them (rarely wanted).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>health gates</strong></td><td><code>/livez?verbose</code> and <code>/readyz?verbose</code> list every individual check — invaluable for "apiserver is up but not ready."</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>profiling/metrics</strong></td><td><code>--profiling</code> (off in hardened clusters) and the <code>/metrics</code> endpoint feed Prometheus (Part 13).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubelet staticPodPath</strong></td><td>the watched directory is set by <code>staticPodPath</code> in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> — point it elsewhere and that's where static pods come from.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The control plane runs AS static pods</strong> — 4 manifests in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>; the kubelet owns their lifecycle and auto-restarts on a file change (~30-60s).</li>
                    <li><strong>Diagnose by symptom:</strong> Pending pods → scheduler; no self-healing → controller-manager; kubectl dead → apiserver/etcd.</li>
                    <li><strong>When kubectl is down,</strong> debug on the node with <code>crictl ps -a</code> + <code>crictl logs</code> + <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Modify safely:</strong> back up → edit → wait → verify → recover. A typo in <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> takes kubectl with it.</li>
                    <li><strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong> are the three plug points: runtime · network · storage.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Editing the wrong manifest</strong> or forgetting to back up first.</li>
                    <li><strong>Not waiting ~30-60s</strong> for the kubelet to restart the pod before declaring it broken.</li>
                    <li><strong>apiserver typo</strong> kills <code>kubectl</code> — recover on the node, not through kubectl.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deleting a control-plane pod with kubectl</strong> — the kubelet just recreates the mirror; move the file instead.</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing the symptoms</strong> — Pending (scheduler) vs no-recreate (controller-manager).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>apiserver down?</strong> <code>sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver</code> → <code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> → fix the file.</li>
                    <li><strong>Force-restart a component:</strong> <code>sudo mv &lt;manifest&gt; /tmp; sleep 5; sudo mv /tmp/&lt;manifest&gt; back</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl -n kube-system get lease</code> to see which scheduler/c-m is the active leader.</li>
                    <li>Always <code>cp … /tmp</code> before any control-plane manifest edit.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 12)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control-plane management toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/             <span class="comment"># the 4 control-plane static pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system ; kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-scheduler-$(hostname)
sudo crictl ps -a ; sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;     <span class="comment"># when kubectl is down</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -f
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/&lt;comp&gt;.yaml /tmp/   <span class="comment"># back up before editing</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get lease          <span class="comment"># who holds leader election</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — add a flag to the controller-manager, verify, then revert</h5>
                <p>No peeking — practise the safe-edit ritual on a non-fatal component:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Back up <code>kube-controller-manager.yaml</code> to <code>/tmp</code>.</li>
                    <li>Add <code>--node-monitor-grace-period=20s</code> and save.</li>
                    <li>Wait for the pod to restart; verify the flag is live and the component is healthy.</li>
                    <li>Restore the backup; confirm it's back to normal.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal (control-plane node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml /tmp/
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml
<span class="comment"># add under command:  - --node-monitor-grace-period=20s</span>
watch -n2 'sudo crictl ps | grep controller'        <span class="comment"># new container ID appears</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-controller-manager-$(hostname) \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep node-monitor
<span class="comment"># revert</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kube-controller-manager.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 12 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Control-plane management recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List the control-plane static pods and the directory they live in.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>etcd.yaml</code>, <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, <code>kube-controller-manager.yaml</code>, <code>kube-scheduler.yaml</code> — all in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The kubelet watches that directory and keeps one pod running per file; <code>ls</code> it to confirm what's present.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The scheduler is down — what's the symptom on new pods, and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>New pods stay <code>Pending</code> with no node assigned (existing pods keep running, kubectl works). Fix: check <code>kube-scheduler.yaml</code> and the pod's logs (<code>crictl logs</code> if needed) — usually a bad flag or a manifest that was moved/corrupted.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Only the scheduler assigns <code>spec.nodeName</code>; without it, nothing binds pods to nodes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Add a <code>--feature-gates=...</code> flag to a component safely.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">safe edit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml /tmp/    <span class="comment"># back up</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml        <span class="comment"># add - --feature-gates=...</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep scheduler                              <span class="comment"># wait for new ID</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every component is configured the same way: edit its manifest's <code>command:</code> list, save, let the kubelet restart it — always after a backup.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">ALL control-plane components appear down — what's your debugging order?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Work bottom-up on the node: <strong>(1)</strong> <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> + <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> (is the kubelet even running?), <strong>(2)</strong> <code>ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (are the manifests there?), <strong>(3)</strong> <code>crictl ps -a</code> + <code>crictl logs</code> per container (why each is failing).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If the kubelet is down, nothing static-pod runs — fix that first. Otherwise it's per-manifest errors the kubelet's journal and crictl reveal.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Change the kubelet's <code>staticPodPath</code> (where it looks for static pods).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml       <span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>staticPodPath</code> is a kubelet config setting; it defines the watched directory. Change it and the kubelet sources static pods from the new path after a restart.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Scheduler vs controller-manager — one job each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Scheduler:</strong> assigns unscheduled pods to nodes (filter → score → bind). <strong>Controller-manager:</strong> runs the reconciliation loops that keep actual state matching desired (recreate pods, mark nodes NotReady, etc.).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Scheduler decides <em>where</em>; controller-manager makes <em>desired = actual</em> everywhere else.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Check a component's health endpoint.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">health</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'         <span class="comment"># apiserver, per-gate</span>
curl -k https://127.0.0.1:10259/healthz     <span class="comment"># scheduler (on the node)</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Components serve <code>/healthz</code>, <code>/livez</code>, <code>/readyz</code>; <code>?verbose</code> lists each check so you see exactly which gate is failing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The controller-manager is crash-looping — where do you look?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-controller-manager-$(hostname)   <span class="comment"># if kubectl works</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep controller ; sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;       <span class="comment"># if not</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Usual causes: a bad flag you added, a missing cert/file the manifest references, or a wrong <code>--kubeconfig</code> path. The logs name it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a static pod on a worker node, then remove it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo kubectl run dbg --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml \
  | sudo tee /etc/kubernetes/manifests/dbg.yaml    <span class="comment"># drop a pod manifest in the dir</span>
<span class="comment"># it appears as dbg-&lt;nodename&gt; (a mirror pod). To remove:</span>
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/manifests/dbg.yaml         <span class="comment"># kubelet deletes the pod</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Any manifest in the kubelet's <code>staticPodPath</code> becomes a static pod — not just control-plane ones. Removing the file is how you delete it (kubectl can't).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Match CRI / CNI / CSI to runtime / network / storage.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>Layer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>CRI</strong></td><td>container <strong>runtime</strong> (containerd, CRI-O)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CNI</strong></td><td>pod <strong>network</strong> (Calico, Cilium, Flannel)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CSI</strong></td><td><strong>storage</strong> drivers (EBS, Ceph, local-path)</td></tr></tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Three pluggable contracts: the kubelet uses CRI to run containers and CNI to network pods; CSI handles volume attach/mount.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 12 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (12)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style control-plane troubleshooting with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are static pods? Where are their manifests stored, and how does the kubelet know where to look?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">static pods are managed directly by kubelet</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Static pods are managed directly by kubelet — NOT by the API server</span>
<span class="comment"># They exist only on the node where their manifest file lives</span>

<span class="comment"># Default manifest directory:</span>
/etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># Control plane static pods:</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Finding the static pod path:</span>
<span class="comment"># Option 1: Check kubelet config</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Check kubelet process args</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep static
<span class="comment"># or: systemctl cat kubelet | grep config</span>

<span class="comment"># Create a static pod:</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF > /etc/kubernetes/manifests/my-static.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: my-static
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: nginx
EOF
<span class="comment"># kubelet automatically creates the pod</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods are pods managed by the kubelet daemon on a specific node, without the API server observing them. The kubelet watches the <code>staticPodPath</code> directory and creates/deletes pods when files appear/disappear. On the CKA, you may need to: (1) create a static pod by placing a YAML in the manifests directory, (2) find the manifest path if it's been changed from default, or (3) identify which pods are static pods. Static pods have the node name appended: <code>my-static-controlplane</code>. They can't be deleted with <code>kubectl delete</code> — you must remove the manifest file.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Create a static pod named <code>test-web</code> running <code>nginx:1.25</code> on the control plane node. How do you verify it's running and how do you delete it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create the manifest file</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the manifest file</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF > /etc/kubernetes/manifests/test-web.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-web
  labels:
    app: test-web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:1.25
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify (kubelet creates it within seconds)</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep test-web
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE   NAME                      READY   STATUS    RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># default     test-web-controlplane     1/1     Running   0</span>

<span class="comment"># Note the "-controlplane" suffix — that's the node name</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Delete the static pod</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ This does NOT work permanently:</span>
kubectl delete pod test-web-controlplane
<span class="comment"># kubelet will immediately recreate it!</span>

<span class="comment"># ✅ This works:</span>
rm /etc/kubernetes/manifests/test-web.yaml
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects file removal and deletes the pod</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods cannot be controlled through the API server — <code>kubectl delete</code> removes the mirror pod, but kubelet immediately recreates it from the manifest file. To truly delete a static pod, you must remove or move the YAML file from the <code>staticPodPath</code>. On the CKA, if asked to "create a static pod on node01," you must SSH to node01 first, find its <code>staticPodPath</code> (may differ from default), and place the manifest there. The pod name in <code>kubectl get pods</code> always has <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> appended.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The kube-scheduler is not working — new pods are stuck in <code>Pending</code> with "no nodes are available" even though nodes are <code>Ready</code>. How do you investigate?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if scheduler pod is running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check if scheduler pod is running</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pods | grep scheduler
<span class="comment"># If not listed or CrashLoopBackOff → scheduler is broke</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check scheduler logs</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-scheduler-controlplane

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check scheduler manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for: wrong image, typo in flags, wrong cert path</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Common scheduler issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># a) Manifest file deleted or corrupted</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml

<span class="comment"># b) Scheduler can't reach API server</span>
<span class="comment"># Check --kubeconfig flag points to valid config</span>

<span class="comment"># c) Wrong port or address</span>
<span class="comment"># Default: --bind-address=0.0.0.0 --secure-port=10259</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: If scheduler manifest is missing, recreate:</span>
<span class="comment"># Check another working cluster or use kubeadm defaults</span>
<span class="comment"># Or restore from etcd backup</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The scheduler's job is to assign pods to nodes. If it's down, pods get created (they exist in etcd) but stay <code>Pending</code> because no node is assigned. The CKA frequently tests this: they'll break the scheduler manifest and ask you to fix it. Diagnosis: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> to see if the scheduler pod exists. If it does exist but is crashing, check its logs. If it doesn't exist, check the manifest directory. Common gotcha: the scheduler manifest might be renamed (e.g., <code>kube-scheduler.yaml.bak</code>) — kubelet ignores non-.yaml files.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you identify which pods in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace are static pods vs regular pods?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">static pods have the node name as suffix</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: Static pods have the node name as suffix</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># etcd-controlplane                  ← static (has node name)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-controlplane        ← static</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-controlplane        ← static</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-controlplane ← static</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-5d78c9869d-abc12           ← regular (has ReplicaSet hash)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-4x8n2                   ← regular (part of DaemonSet)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Check ownerReferences</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].kind}'
<span class="comment"># Node   ← Static pods are owned by the Node object</span>

kubectl get pod coredns-5d78c9869d-abc12 -n kube-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].kind}'
<span class="comment"># ReplicaSet   ← Regular pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Check annotations</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.kubernetes\.io/config\.source}'
<span class="comment"># file   ← Created from a file (static pod)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods have three distinguishing features: (1) their name ends with <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code>, (2) their <code>ownerReferences</code> shows kind <code>Node</code> instead of ReplicaSet/DaemonSet, (3) they have the annotation <code>kubernetes.io/config.source: file</code>. On the CKA, the quickest visual check is the name suffix — all four control plane components (apiserver, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager) are static pods with the node name appended. CoreDNS and kube-proxy are regular pods managed by Deployments/DaemonSets.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to add a custom flag <code>--profiling=false</code> to the kube-controller-manager. Walk through the complete process.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find the manifest</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find the manifest</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Add the flag to the command section</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - command:
    - kube-controller-manager
    - --bind-address=127.0.0.1
    - --cluster-signing-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
    - --profiling=false              # ← Add this line
    <span class="comment"># ... existing flags ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Save and exit (:wq)</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects the change and restarts the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify the pod restarted</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-controller-manager-controlplane
<span class="comment"># RESTARTS count should increment</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify the flag is applied</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-controller-manager-controlplane \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep profiling
<span class="comment"># --profiling=false</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Modifying control plane components on the CKA always follows this pattern: edit the static pod manifest → save → wait for restart → verify. The YAML structure has the component flags as a list under <code>spec.containers[0].command</code>. Common mistakes: (1) adding the flag at the wrong indentation level, (2) using <code>=</code> vs space syntax incorrectly (use <code>--flag=value</code>), (3) forgetting that changes take 30-60 seconds to apply. If the pod doesn't restart, check <code>crictl ps -a</code> — a YAML error will prevent the pod from starting.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">All four control plane components suddenly stop working. You can SSH to the control plane node but <code>kubectl</code> returns "connection refused." What is your debugging checklist?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">without kubectl, use system-level tools</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Without kubectl, use system-level tools:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Check kubelet (manages all static pods)</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 30

<span class="comment"># 2. Check container runtime</span>
sudo systemctl status containerd
sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube

<span class="comment"># 3. Check if manifests exist</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># Should have: etcd.yaml, kube-apiserver.yaml,</span>
<span class="comment">#   kube-controller-manager.yaml, kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Check individual containers</span>
sudo crictl ps -a
<span class="comment"># Look for Exited containers and their exit codes</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Read container logs</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;apiserver-container-id&gt;
sudo crictl logs &lt;etcd-container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># 6. Check system resources</span>
df -h           <span class="comment"># Disk full?</span>
free -h         <span class="comment"># Memory exhaustion?</span>
top             <span class="comment"># CPU overload?</span>

<span class="comment"># 7. Common root causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Disk full: /var/lib/etcd grows indefinitely</span>
<span class="comment"># - Kubelet stopped: systemctl start kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># - Manifests deleted: restore from backup</span>
<span class="comment"># - Cert expired: check /etc/kubernetes/pki/</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>When all control plane components are down, the problem is usually kubelet, containerd, or a system-level issue (disk, memory). Start with kubelet — it manages all static pods. If kubelet is running but containers aren't, check <code>crictl ps -a</code> for container status and logs. If kubelet is down, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> shows why. Disk full (<code>/var/lib/etcd</code> growth) is a common cause in long-running clusters. On the CKA, this is the ultimate troubleshooting scenario — practice the kubectl-less debugging flow until it's second nature.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the kubelet's <code>staticPodPath</code>? How do you find it if it's been changed from the default, and how do you change it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">default staticpodpath</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Default staticPodPath</span>
/etc/kubernetes/manifests

<span class="comment"># Method 1: Check kubelet config file</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Check kubelet process arguments</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -oP 'config=\S+'
<span class="comment"># Shows the kubelet config file path</span>
<span class="comment"># Then read that config file</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Check systemd unit file</span>
systemctl cat kubelet
<span class="comment"># Shows ExecStart command and --config flag</span>

<span class="comment"># To change the static pod path:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Edit /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#    staticPodPath: /opt/my-static-pods</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Restart kubelet</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># 3. Place manifests in the new path</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml /opt/my-static-pods/</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The CKA sometimes changes the <code>staticPodPath</code> to test if you know how to find it. Don't assume <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — always verify. The path is in the kubelet config file, typically at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>. The kubelet config location itself is passed via <code>--config</code> flag in the kubelet systemd unit. If asked to "create a static pod on node01," first SSH to node01, find its kubelet config, read the <code>staticPodPath</code>, then place your manifest there.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between the kube-controller-manager and the kube-scheduler? What happens if each one fails individually?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Aspect</th>
                                            <th>kube-scheduler</th>
                                            <th>kube-controller-manager</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Job</strong></td>
                                            <td>Assign pods to nodes</td>
                                            <td>Run reconciliation loops</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>If down</strong></td>
                                            <td>New pods stay Pending</td>
                                            <td>No self-healing, no scaling</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Running pods</strong></td>
                                            <td>Unaffected</td>
                                            <td>Unaffected (until they crash)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Controllers</strong></td>
                                            <td>N/A</td>
                                            <td>ReplicaSet, Namespace, Node, Job, SA, Endpoint, etc.</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The scheduler only handles <strong>initial pod placement</strong> — after a pod is assigned to a node, the scheduler's job is done. The controller-manager runs dozens of control loops: the ReplicaSet controller ensures desired replica count, the Node controller marks unreachable nodes, the Job controller manages batch jobs, etc. If the scheduler fails, existing pods are fine but new pods wait forever. If the controller-manager fails, the cluster can't self-heal — crashed pods aren't replaced, scaling doesn't work, and terminated namespaces aren't cleaned up.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you check the health of all control plane components? Show both <code>kubectl</code> and non-kubectl methods.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl — check pods in kube-system</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl — check pods in kube-system</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># All should show Running, READY 1/1</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: componentstatuses (deprecated but quick)</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses  <span class="comment"># or: kubectl get cs</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                 STATUS    MESSAGE   ERROR</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler            Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager   Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-0               Healthy   ok</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Health check endpoints</span>
curl -k https://localhost:6443/healthz   <span class="comment"># API server</span>
curl -k https://localhost:10259/healthz  <span class="comment"># Scheduler</span>
curl -k https://localhost:10257/healthz  <span class="comment"># Controller Manager</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Without kubectl (when API server is down)</span>
crictl ps | grep kube
<span class="comment"># Shows running control plane containers</span>

systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># kubelet manages all static pods</span>

journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 20
<span class="comment"># kubelet logs show component status</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA, <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> is the fastest health check. Each control plane component exposes a <code>/healthz</code> endpoint for liveness checks. <code>kubectl get cs</code> is deprecated since v1.19 but still works and gives a quick overview. Know the port numbers: API server (6443), scheduler (10259), controller-manager (10257), etcd (2379). When kubectl is unavailable, fall back to <code>crictl ps</code> and <code>journalctl</code>. A healthy cluster has all four static pods running with 0 recent restarts.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You notice that the kube-controller-manager keeps restarting (high RESTARTS count). How do you investigate the cause?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check current status and restart count</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check current status and restart count</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-controller-manager-controlplane
<span class="comment"># READY   STATUS             RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># 0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   15</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check events</span>
kubectl -n kube-system describe pod kube-controller-manager-controlplane
<span class="comment"># Look at Events section for error messages</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check logs (current attempt)</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-controller-manager-controlplane

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check logs from previous crash</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-controller-manager-controlplane --previous

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Common causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># a) Wrong certificate path in manifest</span>
<span class="comment">#    Error: "unable to load client certificate"</span>
<span class="comment">#    Fix: check --kubeconfig and cert file paths</span>

<span class="comment"># b) Can't reach API server</span>
<span class="comment">#    Error: "dial tcp 127.0.0.1:6443: connection refused"</span>
<span class="comment">#    Fix: check if API server is running first</span>

<span class="comment"># c) Invalid flag in manifest</span>
<span class="comment">#    Error: "unknown flag: --typo-flag"</span>
<span class="comment">#    Fix: remove or correct the flag</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Fix the manifest</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml
<span class="comment"># Wait 30-60s for recovery</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>CrashLoopBackOff on a control plane component means it starts, fails, and kubelet keeps restarting it. The <code>--previous</code> flag shows logs from the last crashed instance — crucial because the current instance might have started too recently to show useful logs. On the CKA, control plane troubleshooting follows a consistent pattern: <code>get pods</code> → <code>describe pod</code> → <code>logs --previous</code> → identify error → fix manifest → wait for recovery. The most common CKA trick: they modify a manifest with a typo or wrong path and ask you to fix it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q21</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to enable the <code>PodSecurity</code> admission plugin and configure API audit logging on the kube-apiserver. Walk through the complete process including all required volume mounts.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">audit logging + PodSecurity admission plugin</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Backup the manifest</span>
cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml \
   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml.bak

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create the audit policy file</span>
mkdir -p /etc/kubernetes/audit
cat &lt;&lt;EOF &gt; /etc/kubernetes/audit/audit-policy.yaml
apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
- level: Metadata
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["secrets", "configmaps"]
- level: RequestResponse
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["pods"]
- level: None
  resources:
  - group: ""
    resources: ["events"]
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Edit the API server manifest</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># Add these flags under spec.containers[0].command:</span>
    - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity
    - --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log
    - --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit/audit-policy.yaml
    - --audit-log-maxage=30
    - --audit-log-maxbackup=10
    - --audit-log-maxsize=100

<span class="comment"># Add volume mounts:</span>
    volumeMounts:
    - mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/audit
      name: audit-policy
      readOnly: true
    - mountPath: /var/log/kubernetes
      name: audit-log

<span class="comment"># Add corresponding volumes:</span>
  volumes:
  - hostPath:
      path: /etc/kubernetes/audit
      type: DirectoryOrCreate
    name: audit-policy
  - hostPath:
      path: /var/log/kubernetes
      type: DirectoryOrCreate
    name: audit-log

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Wait 30-60s, then verify</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-controlplane
<span class="comment"># Should be Running with incremented RESTARTS</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify admission plugin</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-controlplane \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' \
  | grep admission
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify audit log is being written</span>
ls -la /var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>API server configuration on the CKA requires modifying three sections of the manifest: (1) <strong>command flags</strong> — add the new flags to the container command list, (2) <strong>volumeMounts</strong> — make host paths available inside the container, (3) <strong>volumes</strong> — define the hostPath sources. The most common mistake is adding flags that reference files inside the container (like <code>--audit-policy-file</code>) without adding the corresponding volume mount — the API server crashes because it can't find the file. Always add new volume mounts and volumes together with any flag that references a file path. For admission plugins, you append to the existing <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> comma-separated list rather than adding a second copy of the flag.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q22</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Create a static pod named <code>nginx-node01</code> running <code>nginx:alpine</code> on <strong>node01</strong> (not the control plane). The static pod path on node01 has been changed from the default. Show the complete process.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">static pod on a worker node with a non-default path</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: SSH to node01</span>
ssh node01

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Find the kubelet config file path</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -oP 'config=\S+'
<span class="comment"># --config=/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># (or check: systemctl cat kubelet)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Read the config to find staticPodPath</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /opt/static-pods    ← NOT the default!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create the static pod manifest in the correct path</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF &gt; /opt/static-pods/nginx-node01.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx-node01
  labels:
    app: nginx-static
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:alpine
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Exit back to control plane</span>
exit

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify from control plane (wait a few seconds)</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep nginx-node01
<span class="comment"># default   nginx-node01-node01   1/1   Running   0</span>
<span class="comment">#                       ↑ node name appended</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: if kubectl shows the pod but it's not starting,</span>
<span class="comment"># SSH back and check kubelet logs:</span>
ssh node01
journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 20 | grep nginx</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is a classic CKA exam scenario. The key steps most candidates miss: (1) <strong>SSH to the target node first</strong> — you can't create a static pod remotely, (2) <strong>find the actual staticPodPath</strong> — never assume <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> because the exam often changes it, (3) <strong>check kubelet config, not the kubelet process args</strong> — the path is in the config file referenced by <code>--config</code>. The pod name in <code>kubectl get pods</code> will be <code>nginx-node01-node01</code> (manifest name + node name suffix). If the pod doesn't appear within 10 seconds, SSH back and check <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> for YAML parsing errors.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 12 Visual Summary — the control plane as static pods + the 3 plug points</div>
<pre>
   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/   ── 🤖 kubelet watches ──▶  edit a file → pod auto-restarts (~30-60s)
     etcd.yaml · kube-apiserver.yaml · kube-controller-manager.yaml · kube-scheduler.yaml
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   DIAGNOSE by symptom:   Pending pods → scheduler   ·   no self-healing → controller-manager
                          kubectl dead → apiserver/etcd  ·  node NotReady → kubelet
   WHEN kubectl IS DOWN:  sudo crictl ps -a · crictl logs &lt;id&gt; · journalctl -u kubelet  →  fix the FILE
   PLUG POINTS:   kubelet ─CRI─▶ runtime   ·   pod ─CNI─▶ network   ·   volume ─CSI─▶ storage          ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> the kubeadm control plane is four static pods the kubelet runs from files — so you manage it by editing those files, and a good edit just restarts the pod. You diagnose by mapping the symptom to the component (Pending → scheduler, no healing → controller-manager, dead kubectl → apiserver/etcd), and when kubectl is gone you drop to <code>crictl</code> + <code>journalctl</code> on the node. Recovery is always "make the manifest valid."</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> back up before every control-plane edit, wait for the restart, and read the runtime/kubelet directly when the apiserver is down. And remember the three interfaces — <strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong> — are what make every layer of Kubernetes swappable.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH12-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 12 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 3 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part3-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 3 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Cluster Installation &amp; Configuration — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You arrived able to <em>drive</em> a cluster; you leave able to <strong>build, configure, protect, and repair</strong> one. From bare Linux nodes you can stand up a multi-node cluster with kubeadm, tune the control plane by editing static-pod manifests, snapshot &amp; restore etcd, and recover when the apiserver itself is down — the core of the CKA's 25% Cluster Architecture domain.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Part 3 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubeadm (Ch 9):</strong> prep every node (swap off · modules · sysctl · containerd SystemdCgroup · toolchain+hold) → <code>kubeadm init</code> the CP → install a CNI (nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> until then) → <code>kubeadm join</code> workers. Save/regenerate the join command.</li>
                    <li><strong>Config (Ch 10):</strong> configure the control plane by editing static-pod manifests in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (kubelet auto-restarts); kubelet via <code>config.yaml</code> + systemd; admission runs <em>mutating → validating</em>; API = core <code>/api/v1</code> vs named <code>/apis/grp/ver</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>etcd (Ch 11):</strong> it holds ALL state — <code>snapshot save</code> (endpoint + 3 TLS certs) + <code>restore</code> (NEW data-dir, repoint <code>etcd.yaml</code> twice) is the #1 admin skill; quorum = N/2+1, odd members; encrypt Secrets at rest.</li>
                    <li><strong>Control plane (Ch 12):</strong> it runs AS static pods — diagnose by symptom, debug with <code>crictl</code>+<code>journalctl</code> when kubectl is down, modify safely (back up→edit→verify→recover).</li>
                    <li><strong>Three plug points:</strong> <strong>CRI</strong> (runtime) · <strong>CNI</strong> (network) · <strong>CSI</strong> (storage).</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> 25% lives here; etcd &amp; kubeadm tasks are near-certain free marks once drilled.</li>
                    <li>🔑 <code>kubeadm init/join/token · etcdctl snapshot save/restore · crictl ps/logs · journalctl -u kubelet</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 3 Visual Summary — bare nodes → a configured, backed-up, self-managed cluster</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ bare Linux nodes
        │  Ch 9: prep all → kubeadm init → CNI → kubeadm join
        ▼
   ☸️ multi-node cluster (control plane = static pods in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/)
        │  Ch 10: edit apiserver/kubelet config · admission (mutating▶validating) · API groups
        │  Ch 12: diagnose by symptom · crictl+journalctl when kubectl down · CRI·CNI·CSI
        ▼
   🗄️ Ch 11: etcd snapshot save (+TLS) ──► 💥 disaster ──► snapshot restore (new data-dir) ──► ✅ recovered
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   you now OWN the cluster: build it, bend it to requirements, back it up, and bring it back. ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 3 turned you from operator into administrator. You build a cluster from nothing with kubeadm (prep → init → CNI → join), configure its control plane by editing the static-pod manifests the kubelet watches, and protect its memory with etcd snapshot/restore. When things break — even when <code>kubectl</code> itself is gone — you diagnose by symptom and fix the file on the node.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the control plane is files + a watching kubelet + etcd's memory. Master the kubeadm build, the back-up-before-you-edit habit, and the etcd backup/restore dance, and you own the 25% Cluster Architecture domain — the densest scoring ground on the exam. Next: Part 4 makes the workloads on top robust and self-healing.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END PART 3 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 4 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part4">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 4 · Workloads &amp; Scheduling</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Run Apps the Right Way</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 13–16 · CKA 15% · <em>"Make it robust, place it well, budget it, specialize it"</em></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 4 Chapters</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~6 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 CKA Workloads &amp; Scheduling (15%)</span>
            </div>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">A bare Pod is fragile: kill its node and it’s gone, bump its image and you hand-edit it, flood it with traffic and it has no way to grow. Part 4 is about the controllers and policies that make workloads <strong>robust and self-healing</strong> — Deployments &amp; rolling updates, autoscaling under load, scheduling so the right pod lands on the right node, resource governance so no pod starves another, and the specialized workload types (Job · CronJob · DaemonSet · StatefulSet). This is the bridge from “I can launch a container” to “I operate a fleet.”</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> turn easypj into a 3-replica, self-healing Deployment with zero-downtime rollouts and autoscaling; pin and spread pods with affinity, taints &amp; topology spread; drain a node safely; set requests/limits and namespace quotas; and pick the right controller for every job — web, database, batch, and per-node agent.</div>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Real-World Analogy &amp; Myth-Busting Table</h4>
                <p>A part-specific real-world analogy (with an ASCII mapping table) and a "Before You Start" myth-vs-reality comparison table go here — matching the pattern established in Parts 1–3.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — the Part 4 pipeline</div>
<pre>
   Ch 13 RUN &amp; SCALE  ──▶  Ch 14 PLACE  ──▶  Ch 15 GOVERN
   Deploy · RS · HPA       affinity / taints     requests/limits/QoS
   rollouts · rollbacks    topology spread       LimitRange · Quota
            └─────────────────────┬─────────────────────┘
                                    ▼
        Ch 16 SPECIALIZE:  Job · CronJob · DaemonSet · StatefulSet
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: take easypj from “a fragile hand-launched Pod” to
   “a fleet that survives node loss, upgrades cleanly, and grows under load.”
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch13">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 13 · Deployments, ReplicaSets &amp; Scaling</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Deployments are the <strong>heart of Kubernetes workload management</strong>. In this chapter you'll master the complete lifecycle — creating, scaling, updating, and rolling back applications. We'll trace how a Deployment creates a ReplicaSet, which in turn creates Pods, and how the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) adds elastic scaling. Every example uses our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django app so you see real-world patterns, not toy demos.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🚀 Workloads</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Create Deployments imperatively and declaratively — know the full YAML anatomy</li>
                    <li>✅ Perform rolling updates and instant rollbacks using <code>kubectl rollout</code></li>
                    <li>✅ Understand how Deployments → ReplicaSets → Pods chain together under the hood</li>
                    <li>✅ Scale manually with <code>kubectl scale</code> and automatically with HPA</li>
                    <li>✅ Choose the right deployment strategy — RollingUpdate vs Recreate</li>
                    <li>✅ Control update pace with <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Deployments Are CKA Exam Bread &amp; Butter</h5>
                <p>Nearly every CKA exam has at least 2-3 tasks involving Deployments. You <strong>must</strong> be fast at:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl create deployment NAME --image=IMG --replicas=N</code> — imperative creation</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl set image deployment/NAME container=IMG:TAG</code> — trigger rolling update</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=N</code> — rollback</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl scale deployment/NAME --replicas=N</code> — manual scaling</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/NAME</code> — verify update progress</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The Chapter 13 learning-arc roadmap (Deployments → ReplicaSets → HPA → Strategies → Probes) goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of §13.1–§13.5 goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.1 Deployments — Complete Lifecycle</h3>
                <p>A Deployment is Kubernetes' <strong>recommended way to manage stateless applications</strong>. It provides declarative updates, self-healing, scaling, and version history — all in one resource.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Concept: Declarative vs Imperative</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes supports both styles. <strong>Imperative</strong>: <code>kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v1</code>. <strong>Declarative</strong>: <code>kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml</code>. On the CKA, use imperative commands to generate YAML fast, then edit if needed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="relationship-map">
                    <div class="rel-hub">Deployment</div>
                    <div class="rel-spokes">
                        <div class="rel-spoke">🎛️ Manages ReplicaSets</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">📦 Creates Pods via RS</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">🔄 Rolling Updates</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">⏪ Rollback History</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">📈 Scale In/Out</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">✅ Self-Healing</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1a">a) Creating a Deployment</h4>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Imperative — Fast Exam Creation</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create deployment (CKA exam speed)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML without creating (save to file, then edit)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > easypj-deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get deployments
kubectl describe deployment easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-deployment.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
  labels:
    app: easypj
    project: jobpost
spec:
  replicas: 3                    <span class="comment"># Desired number of pods</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                <span class="comment"># Must match template labels</span>
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1                <span class="comment"># 1 extra pod during update</span>
      maxUnavailable: 0          <span class="comment"># Never fewer than 3 running</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj              <span class="comment"># Must match selector</span>
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "250m"
            memory: "256Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "500m"
            memory: "512Mi"
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 5
          periodSeconds: 10</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Deployment YAML Anatomy</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔢</span> replicas</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Desired pod count — the controller constantly ensures this number is running</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏷️</span> selector.matchLabels</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How the Deployment finds its pods — <strong>must match</strong> template labels exactly</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> strategy</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">RollingUpdate (default) or Recreate — controls how pods are replaced</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> template</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The Pod spec — this is a "Pod blueprint" stamped out for each replica</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📈</span> maxSurge</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How many extra pods beyond <code>replicas</code> are allowed during an update (number or %)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📉</span> maxUnavailable</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How many pods can be unavailable during an update (number or %). 0 = zero downtime.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1b">b) Scaling</h4>
                <p>Scaling changes the <code>spec.replicas</code> field. The Deployment's ReplicaSet controller immediately creates or deletes pods to match.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Manual Scaling Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Scale up — handle more traffic</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=5

<span class="comment"># Scale down — save resources</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=2

<span class="comment"># Verify scaling</span>
kubectl get deployment easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME      READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj   5/5     5            5           10m</span>

<span class="comment"># Watch pods appear/disappear in real-time</span>
kubectl get pods -w -l app=easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Manual Scaling — Pods Appear Instantly</div>
<pre>
  kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=5

  BEFORE (3 replicas):             AFTER (5 replicas):
  ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐    ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐
  │easypj-1│ │easypj-2│ │easypj-3│    │easypj-1│ │easypj-2│ │easypj-3│ │easypj-4│ │easypj-5│
  │  📦 v1 │ │  📦 v1 │ │  📦 v1 │    │  📦 v1 │ │  📦 v1 │ │  📦 v1 │ │ 🆕 v1  │ │ 🆕 v1  │
  └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘    └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘
                                                                          ↑ NEW       ↑ NEW
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1c">c) Rolling Updates</h4>
                <p>Rolling updates replace pods <strong>one at a time</strong>, ensuring your application stays available throughout the upgrade. This is the default strategy and is <strong>heavily tested on the CKA</strong>.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Triggering a Rolling Update</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update image — triggers rolling update</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj django=easypj:v2

<span class="comment"># Watch rollout progress (CKA exam: verify your work!)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "easypj" rollout to finish:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment">#   2 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment">#   deployment "easypj" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># Check rollout history</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         &lt;none&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Add change cause for audit trail</span>
kubectl annotate deployment/easypj \
  kubernetes.io/change-cause="Update to v2 — new job search feature"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Rolling Update: easypj v1 → v2 (Zero Downtime)</div>
<pre>
  maxSurge: 1       (1 extra pod allowed during update)
  maxUnavailable: 0 (never fewer than 3 running)

  Time 0:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [📦v1]              ← All running v1
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 1:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [📦v1] [🆕v2]       ← v2 pod starting (surge=1)
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 2:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [🗑️  ] [✅v2]       ← v2 ready → old v1 terminating
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 3:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [✅v2] [🆕v2]       ← Next v2 pod starting
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 4:  [📦v1] [🗑️  ] [✅v2] [✅v2]       ← Second v1 terminating
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 5:  [📦v1] [✅v2] [✅v2] [🆕v2]       ← Last v2 starting
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 6:  [✅v2] [✅v2] [✅v2]              ← All v2 ✅ Zero downtime! 🎉

  Key insight: At NO point were there fewer than 3 healthy pods serving traffic!
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1d">d) Rollbacks</h4>
                <p>If v2 is broken, you can instantly rollback. Kubernetes keeps a revision history of ReplicaSets.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam Alert: Rollback Commands</h5>
                    <p>Rollback questions are extremely common. Know these cold:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME</code> — go back one revision</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=N</code> — go to specific revision</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout history deployment/NAME</code> — see all revisions</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Rollback Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Undo last update (go back to previous revision)</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Go to a specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj --to-revision=2

<span class="comment"># Check what happened</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj --revision=2

<span class="comment"># Verify rollback succeeded</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].image}'
<span class="comment"># easypj:v1 easypj:v1 easypj:v1  ← back to v1!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Rollback Flow: v2 Broken → Instant Recovery to v1</div>
<pre>
  Deployment: easypj
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                          │
  │  Revision 1 (v1) ─── Revision 2 (v2) ─── Revision 3    │
  │  ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────┐   │
  │  │ReplicaSet│        │ReplicaSet│        │ReplicaSet│   │
  │  │ (scaled  │  ──→   │ (scaled  │  ──→   │ = rev 1  │   │
  │  │  to 0)   │        │  to 0)   │  undo  │ (active) │   │
  │  └──────────┘        └──────────┘        └──────────┘   │
  │                        ↑ 💀 broken          ↑ ✅ fixed  │
  │                                                          │
  │  kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj                  │
  │  → K8s scales old RS back up, scales broken RS down     │
  │  → Pods running v1 again within seconds! 🎉             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Create</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create deployment</code> or <code>kubectl apply -f</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Verify</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get deploy,rs,pods</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Scale</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl scale deploy --replicas=N</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Update</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl set image deploy/NAME</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Monitor</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl rollout status</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">6</div>
                        <h5>Rollback</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl rollout undo</code> if needed</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1e">e) Pause &amp; Resume — Batch Multiple Changes</h4>
                <p>Sometimes you need to make multiple changes (image + resources + env vars) in one rollout instead of triggering separate updates for each. Pause the deployment, make all changes, then resume to trigger a single rollout.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Pause &amp; Resume Workflow</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pause — no changes will trigger a rollout</span>
kubectl rollout pause deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Make multiple changes (none trigger rollout yet)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj django=easypj:v3
kubectl set resources deployment/easypj -c django \
  --requests=cpu=500m,memory=512Mi
kubectl set env deployment/easypj DEBUG=false

<span class="comment"># Resume — all changes deploy in ONE rollout</span>
kubectl rollout resume deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Verify single rollout applied all changes</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pause/Resume vs Normal — Rollout Count</div>
<pre>
  WITHOUT Pause/Resume:              WITH Pause/Resume:

  Change image → Rollout #1  🔄      kubectl rollout pause
  Change CPU   → Rollout #2  🔄      Change image  ┐
  Change env   → Rollout #3  🔄      Change CPU    ├─ no rollouts
                                      Change env    ┘
  3 separate rollouts!               kubectl rollout resume
  3 ReplicaSets created!              → Rollout #1 only  🔄
  ⚠️ Wasteful &amp; slower               ✅ Single clean rollout!
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.2 ReplicaSets — Under the Hood</h3>
                <p>A ReplicaSet ensures a specified number of pod replicas are running at any time. You <strong>rarely create ReplicaSets directly</strong> — Deployments create and manage them for you. But understanding them is essential for troubleshooting.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why You Need to Know This</h5>
                    <p>When <code>kubectl get pods</code> shows a pod stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, you need to trace: <strong>Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment</strong> to find the root cause. The CKA tests this troubleshooting chain.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod Chain</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  DEPLOYMENT: easypj                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ You manage this layer                                       │ │
  │  │ "I want 3 pods running easypj:v1"                          │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                             │ creates &amp; manages                 │
  │                             ▼                                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  REPLICASET: easypj-7d9f8b6c5d                           │   │
  │  │  Kubernetes manages this layer automatically              │   │
  │  │  selector: matchLabels: { app: easypj }                  │   │
  │  │  replicas: 3                                              │   │
  │  └───────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────┬────────────┘   │
  │          │ owns             │ owns             │ owns           │
  │          ▼                  ▼                  ▼                │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐          │
  │  │ POD          │  │ POD          │  │ POD          │          │
  │  │ easypj-..abc │  │ easypj-..def │  │ easypj-..ghi │          │
  │  │ 📦 easypj:v1│  │ 📦 easypj:v1│  │ 📦 easypj:v1│          │
  │  │ labels:      │  │ labels:      │  │ labels:      │          │
  │  │  app: easypj │  │  app: easypj │  │  app: easypj │          │
  │  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘          │
  │                                                                  │
  │  Delete a pod? → ReplicaSet auto-creates a replacement! 🔄     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">replicaset-example.yaml (for understanding — rarely created directly)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: ReplicaSet
metadata:
  name: easypj-replicaset
  labels:
    app: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:                 <span class="comment"># Which pods this RS owns</span>
      app: easypj
    <span class="comment"># Advanced: matchExpressions</span>
    <span class="comment"># matchExpressions:</span>
    <span class="comment">#   - key: app</span>
    <span class="comment">#     operator: In           # In, NotIn, Exists, DoesNotExist</span>
    <span class="comment">#     values: [easypj, easypj-staging]</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>matchLabels</h5>
                        <p>Simple equality: <code>app: easypj</code>. All labels must match. Most common approach.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div>
                        <h5>matchExpressions</h5>
                        <p>Operators: <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>DoesNotExist</code>. For complex multi-value selectors.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Inspecting the Chain</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># See all three layers at once</span>
kubectl get deploy,rs,pods -l app=easypj

<span class="comment"># NAME                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj   3/3     3            3           5m</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                                DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># replicaset.apps/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d  3         3         3       5m</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-def34  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-ghi56  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>

<span class="comment"># See owner references (pod → RS → Deployment)</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12 -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].name}'
<span class="comment"># easypj-7d9f8b6c5d  ← owned by the ReplicaSet</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Never Delete ReplicaSets Directly</h5>
                    <p>If you delete a ReplicaSet that belongs to a Deployment, the Deployment controller will immediately recreate it. To scale down, always modify the <strong>Deployment</strong>. Delete the Deployment to clean up all RS and Pods together.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 revisionHistoryLimit — Control Revision History</h5>
                    <p>By default, Kubernetes keeps <strong>10 old ReplicaSets</strong> (revision history) for rollback. Each old RS consumes etcd storage. You can control this with <code>spec.revisionHistoryLimit</code>:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 5</code> — keep last 5 revisions (good for production)</li>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 0</code> — keep none (⚠️ no rollback possible!)</li>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 10</code> — default</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.3 Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)</h3>
                <p>The HPA <strong>automatically scales</strong> your Deployment based on observed CPU, memory, or custom metrics. When traffic spikes, HPA adds pods. When traffic drops, it removes them. This is <strong>critical for production</strong> and tested on the CKA.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📋 Prerequisite: Metrics Server</h5>
                    <p>HPA requires the <strong>Metrics Server</strong> to be installed in the cluster. Without it, HPA has no data to make scaling decisions. Verify: <code>kubectl top nodes</code> — if this works, Metrics Server is running.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">HPA — Imperative (CKA Exam Speed)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create HPA bound to deployment</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment easypj --min=3 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70

<span class="comment"># Check HPA status</span>
kubectl get hpa
<span class="comment"># NAME     REFERENCE           TARGETS   MINPODS   MAXPODS   REPLICAS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj  Deployment/easypj   45%/70%   3         10        3          2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Detailed view</span>
kubectl describe hpa easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-hpa.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: easypj-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj
  minReplicas: 3
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 70    <span class="comment"># Scale when avg CPU > 70%</span>
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 80    <span class="comment"># Also watch memory</span>
  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 30   <span class="comment"># React quickly to spikes</span>
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300  <span class="comment"># Wait 5 min before scaling down</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">HPA Scaling Flow — Automatic Response to Load</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  HPA: Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for easypj                      │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 Metrics Server reports: CPU = 85%  (above target of 70%)   │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  🤖 HPA evaluates every 15 seconds:                             │
  │     desiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas × (85% / 70%))       │
  │     desiredReplicas = ceil(3 × 1.21) = 4                        │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  📈 HPA updates: deployment.spec.replicas = 4                   │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  📦📦📦📦  4 pods running → CPU drops to 60%                    │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ... traffic keeps climbing ...                                  │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 CPU = 90%  →  HPA: replicas = 6                             │
  │  📦📦📦📦📦📦  6 pods running → CPU stabilizes at 55%           │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ... traffic decreases (night time) ...                          │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 CPU = 25%  →  HPA waits 5 min (stabilization window)       │
  │               →  then scales down to minReplicas = 3            │
  │  📦📦📦  3 pods — cost efficient! 💰                             │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬆️</div>
                        <h5>Scale Up (Fast)</h5>
                        <p>Default stabilization: <strong>0 seconds</strong>. HPA reacts immediately to load spikes to protect users.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬇️</div>
                        <h5>Scale Down (Slow)</h5>
                        <p>Default stabilization: <strong>300 seconds</strong>. Avoids flapping — waits 5 min to confirm load is truly gone.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Tip: HPA Requires Resource Requests</h5>
                    <p>HPA can only measure utilization as a percentage if <code>resources.requests</code> is set on the container. Without requests, HPA cannot calculate CPU percentage and will report <code>&lt;unknown&gt;/70%</code>. Always set resource requests on Deployments that use HPA.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.4 Deployment Strategies</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes supports two built-in deployment strategies. Choosing the right one depends on your application's ability to handle multiple versions running simultaneously.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Strategy</th>
                                <th>How It Works</th>
                                <th>Downtime</th>
                                <th>When to Use</th>
                                <th>easypj Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>RollingUpdate</strong></td>
                                <td>Replace pods gradually, one (or few) at a time</td>
                                <td>❌ None</td>
                                <td>Default — use for most updates</td>
                                <td>New Django feature release</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Recreate</strong></td>
                                <td>Kill ALL old pods first, then start ALL new pods</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes — brief outage</td>
                                <td>Breaking changes, DB schema migrations, incompatible API versions</td>
                                <td>Major Django version upgrade with DB migration</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">RollingUpdate vs Recreate — Visual Comparison</div>
<pre>
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ROLLINGUPDATE STRATEGY (default)          RECREATE STRATEGY
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  T0: [v1] [v1] [v1]                       [v1] [v1] [v1]
       │                                         │
  T1: [v1] [v1] [v1] [v2] ← surge              [💀] [💀] [💀] ← all killed
       │                                         │
  T2: [v1] [v1] [v2]      ← replace 1           [  ] [  ] [  ] ← downtime! ⚠️
       │                                         │
  T3: [v1] [v2] [v2]      ← replace 2           [v2] [v2] [v2] ← all new
       │
  T4: [v2] [v2] [v2]      ← complete ✅

  Availability:                             Availability:
  ██████████████████ 100%                   ████░░░░████ ~70%
  Never less than minAvailable              Brief outage window

  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>RollingUpdate — Tuning Parameters</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">strategy-examples.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Conservative — zero downtime, slow rollout</span>
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: 1          <span class="comment"># Only 1 extra pod at a time</span>
    maxUnavailable: 0    <span class="comment"># Never fewer than desired replicas</span>

<span class="comment"># Aggressive — faster rollout, allows brief capacity dip</span>
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: "50%"      <span class="comment"># Up to 50% extra pods</span>
    maxUnavailable: "25%" <span class="comment"># Allow 25% to be unavailable</span>

<span class="comment"># Recreate — kill all, then create all</span>
strategy:
  type: Recreate         <span class="comment"># No rollingUpdate params needed</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="stat-bar-group">
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">RollingUpdate — Safety</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★★★</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width: 100%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">RollingUpdate — Speed</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★☆☆</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width: 60%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Recreate — Safety</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★☆☆☆</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width: 40%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Recreate — Speed</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★★★</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width: 100%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Exam Strategy</h5>
                    <p>On the exam, the default RollingUpdate is almost always what you want. Only use <code>Recreate</code> if the question explicitly says "all pods must be running the same version" or mentions database migrations that can't handle mixed versions.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Beyond Built-in: Blue-Green &amp; Canary Deployments</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes only has <strong>RollingUpdate</strong> and <strong>Recreate</strong> built in. Two other common strategies require manual setup or tools like Argo Rollouts / Istio:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Blue-Green</strong> — Run two full Deployments (blue=current, green=new). Switch the Service selector from blue to green once verified. Instant rollback by switching back.</li>
                        <li><strong>Canary</strong> — Deploy new version alongside old with fewer replicas (e.g., 1 of 10). Route a small percentage of traffic to canary. If healthy, gradually increase. Popular with service meshes.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Blue-Green vs Canary — Not Built-in but Important to Know</div>
<pre>
  BLUE-GREEN:                           CANARY:

  ┌─────── Service ──────┐              ┌─────── Service ──────┐
  │  selector: ver=blue  │              │  selector: app=web   │
  └──────────┬───────────┘              └──────────┬───────────┘
             │                                     │
    ┌────────┴────────┐                   ┌────────┴────────┐
    ▼                 ▼                   ▼                 ▼
  Blue Deploy      Green Deploy        v1 Deploy (9)    v2 Deploy (1)
  (current ✅)     (new, testing)      (90% traffic)    (10% canary)
  [v1][v1][v1]     [v2][v2][v2]        [v1]×9           [v2]×1

  Switch: change   Promote: scale      Promote: scale v2 up,
  selector to      green, delete       scale v1 down gradually
  ver=green        blue
</pre>
                </div>

            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.5 Health Probes &amp; Self-Healing</h3>
                <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                    <h4>Coming Soon</h4>
                    <p>This section has not been written yet.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Rolling Updates Actually Work: ReplicaSet Lifecycle, maxSurge/maxUnavailable Math, and Revision History</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Rolling Update — Step-by-Step with ReplicaSet Math</h5>
            <p>When you update a Deployment's image, Kubernetes doesn't modify the existing ReplicaSet. It creates a NEW ReplicaSet and orchestrates a gradual handover. Understanding the math behind maxSurge and maxUnavailable lets you tune updates for speed vs safety.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ROLLING UPDATE — Complete ReplicaSet Lifecycle                               │
│                                                                              │
│  INITIAL STATE: Deployment easypj-web, replicas=3, image=v2.0                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old):                                                         │    │
│  │   desired=3, ready=3                                                │    │
│  │   Pods: [web-abc, web-def, web-ghi] (all v2.0)                     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web easypj=easypj:v2.1                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  STEP 1: Deployment creates NEW ReplicaSet with updated pod template         │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new):                                                         │    │
│  │   desired=0, ready=0  <span class="comment">← Starts at 0                                 </span>│    │
│  │   Pods: []                                                           │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): unchanged (still 3/3)                                  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: Scale up RS-v2 by maxSurge (default 25% = 1 pod)                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new): desired=1, ready=0→1 (pod starting...)                  │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=3, ready=3                                     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  TOTAL PODS: 4 (3 old + 1 new)  <span class="comment">← maxSurge allows extra pod         </span>│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: New pod is Ready → Scale down RS-v1 by 1                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new): desired=1, ready=1                                     │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=2, ready=2  <span class="comment">← One pod terminated               </span>│    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  TOTAL PODS: 3 (2 old + 1 new)  <span class="comment">← Back to desired count             </span>│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  Repeat STEP 2→3 until RS-v1=0, RS-v2=3 (full cutover):                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  RS-v2: 1→2→3 (scaling up)                                           │    │
│  │  RS-v1: 2→1→0 (scaling down)                                         │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  FINAL STATE:                                                                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (current): desired=3, ready=3                                  │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=0, ready=0  <span class="comment">← Kept for rollback!               </span>│    │
│  │   Pods: all terminated                                               │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  maxSurge & maxUnavailable MATH:                                             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ maxSurge:  EXTRA pods allowed ABOVE desired count                   │    │
│  │ maxUnavailable: pods allowed to be UNAVAILABLE during update        │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ Example: replicas=10, maxSurge=25%, maxUnavailable=25%               │    │
│  │   maxSurge pods:     ceil(10 × 0.25) = 3 extra pods allowed         │    │
│  │   maxUnavailable pods: floor(10 × 0.25) = 2 pods can be down        │    │
│  │   → During update: 10-13 pods running, at least 8 pods available    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ ZERO-DOWNTIME CONFIG (safest, slowest):                             │    │
│  │   maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0                                    │    │
│  │   → 1 new pod created, wait for Ready, then 1 old pod removed       │    │
│  │   → NEVER below desired replicas, 1 extra at a time                 │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ FAST UPDATE CONFIG (fastest, riskiest):                              │    │
│  │   maxSurge: 50%, maxUnavailable: 50%                                 │    │
│  │   → Half the pods replaced at once                                   │    │
│  │   → Application must handle 50% traffic loss gracefully             │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  REVISION HISTORY (stored in ReplicaSets):                                   │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Each image update creates a NEW ReplicaSet. Old ones are kept for    │    │
│  │ rollback (default: last 10 revisions).                                │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ $ kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web                          │    │
│  │ REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE                                                │    │
│  │ 1         Initial deploy v2.0                                         │    │
│  │ 2         Update to v2.1                                              │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ $ kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web                              │    │
│  │ → Scales RS-v1 back to 3, scales RS-v2 to 0 (instant rollback!)     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, a stuck rollout nearly always means the new pods can't become Ready. Check: <code>kubectl rollout status deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code> (shows progress), <code>kubectl get rs</code> (see which RS is scaling), <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;new-pod&gt;</code> (find the failure). Common causes: wrong image tag, CrashLoopBackOff, insufficient cluster resources, or readiness probe failing. You can PAUSE a rollout: <code>kubectl rollout pause deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code> and RESUME: <code>kubectl rollout resume deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways for Chapter 13 go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Rolling update is the default</strong> — If the question doesn't specify a strategy, it's <code>RollingUpdate</code>. Only use <code>Recreate</code> when explicitly asked (kills all pods first)</li>
                    <li><strong><code>maxUnavailable</code> + <code>maxSurge</code></strong> — Defaults are 25% each. The exam may ask you to set specific values. Remember: <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> + <code>maxSurge: 1</code> = zero-downtime</li>
                    <li><strong>Rollback requires history</strong> — <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> only works if there's a previous revision. Add <code>--record</code> (deprecated) or use <code>--to-revision=N</code> for precision</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl set image</code> container name</strong> — You must specify the container name: <code>kubectl set image deployment/web nginx=nginx:1.25</code>. Get it from <code>kubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].name}'</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Deployment lifecycle in 60 seconds:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create deploy web --image=nginx:1.24 --replicas=3</code><br>
                <code>kubectl set image deploy/web nginx=nginx:1.25</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout status deploy/web</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout history deploy/web</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/web</code> — instant rollback</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 13)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Command</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Create deployment</td><td><code>kubectl create deployment NAME --image=IMG --replicas=N</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Scale</td><td><code>kubectl scale deployment NAME --replicas=N</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Update image</td><td><code>kubectl set image deployment/NAME container=IMG:TAG</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check rollout</td><td><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View history</td><td><code>kubectl rollout history deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Undo last</td><td><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Go to revision</td><td><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=N</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Autoscale</td><td><code>kubectl autoscale deployment NAME --min=M --max=X --cpu-percent=P</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pause rollout</td><td><code>kubectl rollout pause deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Resume rollout</td><td><code>kubectl rollout resume deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
