            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 13 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 13 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of rollout &amp; scaling mechanics — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to update a Deployment from <code>nginx:1.24</code> to <code>nginx:1.25</code> and ensure zero downtime. Show the command and explain what happens under the hood.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">update the image</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update the image</span>
kubectl set image deployment/webapp nginx=nginx:1.25

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout in real-time</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "webapp" rollout to finish:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment">#   2 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment">#   deployment "webapp" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># What happens under the hood:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. New ReplicaSet (RS-v2) created with image nginx:1.25</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. RS-v2 scaled up by maxSurge (default 25%)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. RS-v1 scaled down by the same amount</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Repeat until RS-v2 has all replicas, RS-v1 has 0</span>

<span class="comment"># Check both ReplicaSets:</span>
kubectl get rs
<span class="comment"># webapp-6d87c8b9f5   3   3   3   2m  (new - nginx:1.25)</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-7f4d8c9a2b   0   0   0   1h  (old - nginx:1.24)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>RollingUpdate</code> strategy (default) ensures zero downtime by gradually replacing old pods with new ones. <code>maxSurge</code> controls how many extra pods can exist during the update (default 25%), and <code>maxUnavailable</code> controls how many pods can be down (default 25%). The old ReplicaSet is kept at 0 replicas for rollback. On the CKA, <code>kubectl set image</code> is the fastest way to trigger an update. Always verify with <code>kubectl rollout status</code> — if the new image is broken, the rollout stalls.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A rolling update is stuck — only 1 of 3 pods updated successfully. The new pod keeps crashing. How do you rollback and what commands show the rollout history?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check rollout status (will show it's stuck)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check rollout status (will show it's stuck)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "webapp" rollout to finish:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>

<span class="comment"># View rollout history</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         &lt;none&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         &lt;none&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 3         &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># See details of a specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/webapp --revision=2

<span class="comment"># Rollback to the previous version</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/webapp

<span class="comment"># Or rollback to a specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/webapp --to-revision=1

<span class="comment"># Verify the rollback</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp
kubectl get deployment webapp -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'
<span class="comment"># nginx:1.24 (back to previous version)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl rollout undo</code> reverses the last rollout by scaling up the previous ReplicaSet and scaling down the current one. It's essentially a new rollout in reverse. <code>--to-revision</code> lets you go back to any historical version. Kubernetes keeps old ReplicaSets (controlled by <code>spec.revisionHistoryLimit</code>, default 10). On the CKA, speed matters: if an update breaks, don't waste time debugging — undo first, investigate later. To make history more useful, record changes: <code>kubectl set image deploy/webapp nginx=nginx:1.25 --record</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between a Deployment's <code>strategy.type: RollingUpdate</code> and <code>strategy.type: Recreate</code>? When would you use each?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Aspect</th>
                                        <th>RollingUpdate</th>
                                        <th>Recreate</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Downtime</td>
                                        <td>Zero</td>
                                        <td>Yes — all old pods killed first</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Old + New versions running</td>
                                        <td>Briefly, yes</td>
                                        <td>Never — only one version at a time</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Use case</td>
                                        <td>Stateless apps, web servers</td>
                                        <td>Schema migrations, single-writer DBs</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">recreate strategy in yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Recreate strategy in YAML:</span>
spec:
strategy:
type: Recreate    <span class="comment"># Kill all old, then start new</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Use <code>Recreate</code> when you cannot have two versions running simultaneously — for example, an app that locks a database or writes to a shared volume. <code>RollingUpdate</code> is the default and is correct for 95% of scenarios. On the CKA, if a question mentions that two versions of the app must NOT run at the same time, the answer is <code>Recreate</code>. If it mentions zero downtime, the answer is <code>RollingUpdate</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you scale a Deployment to 5 replicas? Show three different methods. What happens to existing traffic during scaling?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl scale (imperative, fastest)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl scale (imperative, fastest)</span>
kubectl scale deployment webapp --replicas=5

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl edit (interactive)</span>
kubectl edit deployment webapp
<span class="comment"># Change spec.replicas to 5</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl patch (scriptable)</span>
kubectl patch deployment webapp -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'

<span class="comment"># Method 4: kubectl apply (declarative)</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the YAML file and apply</span>
kubectl apply -f webapp-deployment.yaml

<span class="comment"># During scale UP: existing traffic is unaffected</span>
<span class="comment"># New pods start and become Ready → added to Service endpoints</span>
<span class="comment"># During scale DOWN: pods are terminated gracefully</span>
<span class="comment"># terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30s) allows cleanup</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get deployment webapp
<span class="comment"># READY  UP-TO-DATE  AVAILABLE</span>
<span class="comment"># 5/5    5           5</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl scale</code> is the fastest for the CKA exam. Scaling up adds pods to the ReplicaSet — they start, become Ready, and are added to the Service's Endpoints list (traffic begins flowing). Scaling down selects pods to terminate — they receive SIGTERM, have <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> to shut down cleanly, then are removed from Endpoints (traffic stops). The key insight: the Service only routes to pods that pass their readinessProbe, so scaling is traffic-safe by design.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain the relationship between Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod. What naming convention is used and how do you trace ownership?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">naming chain</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Naming chain:</span>
<span class="comment"># Deployment: webapp</span>
<span class="comment"># ReplicaSet: webapp-6d87c8b9f5     (deploy-name + pod-template-hash)</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod:        webapp-6d87c8b9f5-x7k  (rs-name + random-suffix)</span>

<span class="comment"># Trace ownership: Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment</span>
kubectl get pod webapp-6d87c8b9f5-x7k -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].name}'
<span class="comment"># webapp-6d87c8b9f5  (owned by this ReplicaSet)</span>

kubectl get rs webapp-6d87c8b9f5 -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].name}'
<span class="comment"># webapp  (owned by this Deployment)</span>

<span class="comment"># Visual hierarchy:</span>
<span class="comment"># Deployment (webapp)</span>
<span class="comment">#   ├── ReplicaSet (webapp-6d87c8b9f5) ← current (3 replicas)</span>
<span class="comment">#   └── ReplicaSet (webapp-7f4d8c9a2b) ← old (0 replicas, for rollback)</span>
<span class="comment">#         ├── Pod (webapp-6d87c8b9f5-x7k)</span>
<span class="comment">#         ├── Pod (webapp-6d87c8b9f5-m9p)</span>
<span class="comment">#         └── Pod (webapp-6d87c8b9f5-q2j)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Each Deployment creates a ReplicaSet for each unique pod template (spec change). Each ReplicaSet creates the desired number of Pods. The <code>pod-template-hash</code> label ensures each ReplicaSet is unique to a specific template version. On the CKA, understanding this chain helps with: (1) debugging — if pods aren't being created, check the ReplicaSet, then the Deployment, (2) rollbacks — each version has its own ReplicaSet, (3) identifying orphaned ReplicaSets — old RS with 0 replicas are normal.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)? Create one that scales a Deployment between 2 and 10 replicas based on 50% CPU utilization.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prerequisites: metrics-server must be installed</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Prerequisites: metrics-server must be installed</span>
kubectl top pods  <span class="comment"># If this works, metrics-server is running</span>

<span class="comment"># Create HPA imperatively (fastest on CKA)</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment webapp \
--min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=50

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get hpa
<span class="comment"># NAME    REFERENCE          TARGETS   MINPODS  MAXPODS  REPLICAS</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp  Deployment/webapp  30%/50%   2        10       3</span>

<span class="comment"># YAML equivalent:</span>
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
name: webapp-hpa
spec:
scaleTargetRef:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
name: webapp
minReplicas: 2
maxReplicas: 10
metrics:
- type: Resource
resource:
  name: cpu
  target:
    type: Utilization
    averageUtilization: 50</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>HPA automatically adjusts the Deployment's <code>replicas</code> based on observed metrics. It checks metrics every 15 seconds (default). Important: the Deployment's pods MUST have <code>resources.requests.cpu</code> set — HPA calculates utilization as <code>actual / requested</code>. Without requests, HPA can't compute the percentage. On the CKA, <code>kubectl autoscale</code> is the imperative command. HPA won't scale below <code>minReplicas</code> or above <code>maxReplicas</code>. Metrics-server must be running for HPA to work — if <code>kubectl top pods</code> fails, install/check metrics-server first.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code> in a Deployment's rolling update strategy? If you have 4 replicas with <code>maxSurge: 25%</code> and <code>maxUnavailable: 25%</code>, how many pods exist during the update?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">with 4 replicas, maxsurge: 25%, maxunavailable: 25%</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># With 4 replicas, maxSurge: 25%, maxUnavailable: 25%:</span>
<span class="comment"># maxSurge: 25% of 4 = 1 → max 5 pods total during update</span>
<span class="comment"># maxUnavailable: 25% of 4 = 1 → min 3 pods available</span>

<span class="comment"># During rolling update:</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Create 1 new pod (5 total: 4 old + 1 new)</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 2: When new pod Ready → kill 1 old (4 total: 3 old + 1 new)</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create 1 new pod (5 total: 3 old + 2 new)</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 4: When new pod Ready → kill 1 old (4 total: 2 old + 2 new)</span>
<span class="comment"># ... continues until all 4 are new</span>

<span class="comment"># YAML:</span>
spec:
strategy:
type: RollingUpdate
rollingUpdate:
  maxSurge: 1          <span class="comment"># Can also use absolute number</span>
  maxUnavailable: 1    <span class="comment"># Can also use absolute number</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>maxSurge</code> = how many MORE pods than desired can exist during update. <code>maxUnavailable</code> = how many FEWER pods than desired can be unavailable. Both can be absolute numbers or percentages. Higher <code>maxSurge</code> = faster updates but more resource usage. Higher <code>maxUnavailable</code> = faster updates but less capacity during rollout. Setting <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> ensures full capacity throughout (but requires at least <code>maxSurge: 1</code>). On the CKA, know that percentages are rounded up for <code>maxSurge</code> and rounded down for <code>maxUnavailable</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you pause and resume a Deployment rollout? Why would you want to pause one?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pause the rollout</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pause the rollout</span>
kubectl rollout pause deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># No new ReplicaSets are created, no scaling happens</span>

<span class="comment"># Make multiple changes while paused</span>
kubectl set image deployment/webapp nginx=nginx:1.25
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=nginx \
--requests=cpu=200m,memory=256Mi
kubectl patch deployment webapp -p '{"spec":{"template":{"metadata":{"labels":{"version":"v2"}}}}}'

<span class="comment"># Resume — ALL changes applied as ONE rollout</span>
kubectl rollout resume deployment/webapp

<span class="comment"># This creates a single new ReplicaSet with all changes</span>
<span class="comment"># Instead of 3 separate rollouts (one per change)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pausing a Deployment allows you to make multiple changes that get applied as a single rollout instead of triggering separate rollouts for each change. Without pausing, changing the image triggers rollout #1, then changing resources triggers rollout #2 — wasteful and slow. On the CKA, this is tested less frequently but shows advanced understanding. After resuming, verify with <code>kubectl rollout status</code>. Note: a paused Deployment doesn't scale — <code>kubectl scale</code> also waits until you resume.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Deployment with <code>nginx:1.25</code>, 3 replicas, and set <code>revisionHistoryLimit</code> to 5. What does this setting control?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate and modify</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Generate and modify:</span>
kubectl create deployment webapp --image=nginx:1.25 --replicas=3 \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > webapp.yaml

<span class="comment"># Add revisionHistoryLimit:</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
name: webapp
spec:
replicas: 3
revisionHistoryLimit: 5    # ← Keep only 5 old ReplicaSets
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: webapp
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: webapp
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:1.25

kubectl apply -f webapp.yaml

<span class="comment"># After 7 rollouts:</span>
kubectl get rs
<span class="comment"># Only 5 old ReplicaSets kept (+ 1 current = 6 total)</span>
<span class="comment"># Older ones are garbage-collected</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>revisionHistoryLimit</code> controls how many old ReplicaSets are kept for rollback purposes. Default is 10. Setting it too low means you can't roll back to older versions. Setting it to 0 means no rollback at all (old RS are immediately deleted). On the CKA, this field shows understanding of the Deployment lifecycle. Each old ReplicaSet costs a small amount of API server/etcd storage. In production, 5-10 is typical.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to update a Deployment and record the change cause. Then view the history to see what changed in each revision. Show the complete workflow.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">update with annotation for change tracking</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update with annotation for change tracking</span>
kubectl set image deployment/webapp nginx=nginx:1.25
kubectl annotate deployment/webapp \
kubernetes.io/change-cause="Updated nginx to 1.25 for security patch"

<span class="comment"># View history with change causes</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         Initial deployment with nginx:1.24</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         Updated nginx to 1.25 for security patch</span>

<span class="comment"># See exactly what changed in revision 2</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/webapp --revision=2
<span class="comment"># Shows the full pod template for that revision</span>

<span class="comment"># Rollback with awareness</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/webapp --to-revision=1
kubectl annotate deployment/webapp \
kubernetes.io/change-cause="Rolled back to nginx:1.24 due to bug"</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> annotation populates the CHANGE-CAUSE column in <code>rollout history</code>. Without it, the column shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>. The old <code>--record</code> flag was deprecated — use annotations instead. On the CKA, this shows you understand audit trails for deployments. <code>kubectl rollout history --revision=N</code> shows the complete pod template spec for that revision, letting you compare what changed between versions without needing to inspect ReplicaSets directly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 13 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap goes here.</p>
            </div>

        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch14">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 14 · Scheduling &amp; Node Management</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">The Kubernetes scheduler decides <strong>where</strong> every pod runs. As a CKA administrator, you must master how to influence scheduling decisions — from simple node selectors to complex affinity rules, taints, tolerations, and node maintenance procedures. This chapter is one of the <strong>most heavily tested topics on the CKA exam</strong>.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📍 Scheduling</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ How the Kubernetes scheduler makes placement decisions (filtering → scoring → binding)</li>
                    <li>✅ Use <code>nodeSelector</code> for simple node placement</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure node affinity (required vs preferred) for flexible scheduling</li>
                    <li>✅ Use pod affinity/anti-affinity for co-location and spreading</li>
                    <li>✅ Apply taints and tolerations to control which pods can run on which nodes</li>
                    <li>✅ Perform safe node maintenance with <code>cordon</code>, <code>drain</code>, <code>uncordon</code></li>
                    <li>✅ Spread pods evenly with topology spread constraints</li>
                    <li>✅ Use PriorityClasses for pod preemption</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Scheduling Is a CKA Exam Favourite</h5>
                <p>Expect at least 2-3 scheduling questions. The most common patterns:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Taints &amp; Tolerations</strong> — taint a node, create a pod with/without toleration, verify placement</li>
                    <li><strong>Node Affinity</strong> — label a node, create a pod with nodeAffinity targeting that label</li>
                    <li><strong>Node Maintenance</strong> — cordon → drain → perform work → uncordon</li>
                    <li><strong>Static Pods</strong> — create a manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.1 How the Scheduler Works</h3>
                <p>The <code>kube-scheduler</code> runs as a control plane component. When a new pod is created without a <code>nodeName</code>, it enters the scheduling queue. The scheduler then executes a multi-phase pipeline to find the best node.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Filtering</h5>
                        <p>Which nodes <strong>CAN</strong> run this pod? Checks: sufficient resources, node taints, node selectors, affinity rules, port availability.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Scoring</h5>
                        <p>Which passing node is the <strong>BEST</strong> fit? Scores based on: resource balance, affinity preference, spread, image locality.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Binding</h5>
                        <p>The highest-scoring node wins. Scheduler sets <code>pod.spec.nodeName</code> and the kubelet on that node starts the container.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Scheduler Decision Flowchart — easypj Pod Placement</div>
<pre>
  NEW POD: easypj (requests: 256Mi memory, 250m CPU)
       │
       ▼
  ════════════════ PHASE 1: FILTERING ════════════════
  "Which nodes CAN run this pod?"

  Node 1 (512Mi free, 500m CPU free)   → ✅ PASSES — has enough resources
  Node 2 (128Mi free, 100m CPU free)   → ❌ FAILS  — insufficient memory
  Node 3 (1Gi free, 1000m CPU free)    → ✅ PASSES — has enough resources
  Node 4 (taint: gpu=true:NoSchedule)  → ❌ FAILS  — no matching toleration
       │
       ▼ (only passing nodes continue)
  ════════════════ PHASE 2: SCORING ═════════════════
  "Which passing node is the BEST fit?"

  Node 1: Score 65  — good fit, resources balanced
  Node 3: Score 82  — better fit, more headroom,
                      topology spread bonus, affinity match
       │
       ▼
  ════════════════ PHASE 3: BINDING ═════════════════

  🏆 Winner: Node 3 (highest score: 82)
  → pod.spec.nodeName = "node-3"
  → kubelet on Node 3 pulls image → starts container
  → Pod status: Running ✅
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>Resource Fit</h5>
                        <p>Does the node have enough CPU &amp; memory to satisfy the pod's requests?</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>Label Match</h5>
                        <p>Does the node have the labels required by nodeSelector or nodeAffinity?</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>Taint Check</h5>
                        <p>Does the pod tolerate the node's taints? No toleration = rejected.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📊</div>
                        <h5>Spread Score</h5>
                        <p>How well does this placement spread pods across nodes/zones?</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Static Pods — Bypass the Scheduler</h4>
                <p>Static pods are managed directly by the <strong>kubelet</strong> on a specific node, not by the API server. They're used for control plane components (etcd, apiserver, scheduler itself). The CKA may ask you to create one.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Creating a Static Pod</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Static pods live in the kubelet's manifest directory</span>
<span class="comment"># Default: /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</span>

<span class="comment"># Find the staticPodPath on any node</span>
ssh worker-1 "cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath"
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># Create a static pod (just place a YAML file there)</span>
ssh worker-1 "cat > /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-nginx.yaml" &lt;&lt;'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: static-nginx
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:1.25
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
EOF

<span class="comment"># kubelet auto-starts it! Check from control plane:</span>
kubectl get pods
<span class="comment"># NAME                       READY   STATUS    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># static-nginx-worker-1      1/1     Running   10s</span>
<span class="comment">#                  ^^^^^^^^^ node name appended!</span>

<span class="comment"># Delete: remove the file from /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl delete won't work — kubelet will recreate it!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam: Static Pod Key Facts</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Static pod names always end with <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code></li>
                        <li>They can only be created/deleted by adding/removing YAML from the manifest directory</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl delete</code> on a static pod will NOT permanently remove it — kubelet recreates it</li>
                        <li>Control plane components (apiserver, etcd, scheduler) are static pods on the control plane node</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.2 Node Selectors — Simple Placement</h3>
                <p>The simplest way to constrain a pod to specific nodes. Label a node, then reference that label in the pod spec. It's a <strong>hard requirement</strong> — the pod won't schedule if no node matches.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Label a Node, Then Select It</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Label the node</span>
kubectl label node worker-1 disk=ssd

<span class="comment"># Verify label was applied</span>
kubectl get node worker-1 --show-labels | grep disk

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create pod with nodeSelector (see YAML below)</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-ssd-pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify pod landed on the right node</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-ssd -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME         READY   STATUS    NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-ssd   1/1     Running   worker-1   ← correct!</span>

<span class="comment"># Clean up: remove label</span>
kubectl label node worker-1 disk-</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-ssd-pod.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-ssd
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    disk: ssd                    <span class="comment"># Only schedule on nodes with disk=ssd</span>
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 nodeSelector vs nodeAffinity</h5>
                    <p><code>nodeSelector</code> is simple but limited — it only supports exact equality matching. For "prefer but don't require" or "match any of these values" logic, use <strong>Node Affinity</strong> (see 14.3).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.3 Node Affinity — Advanced Placement</h3>
                <p>Node affinity is the <strong>more powerful version of nodeSelector</strong>. It supports operators (<code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>Gt</code>, <code>Lt</code>) and offers two types: hard rules (required) and soft rules (preferred).</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Type</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>When Pod Can't Match</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>requiredDuringScheduling<wbr>IgnoredDuringExecution</strong></td>
                                <td>Hard rule — MUST match</td>
                                <td>Pod stays Pending forever</td>
                                <td>GPU workloads, compliance zones</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>preferredDuringScheduling<wbr>IgnoredDuringExecution</strong></td>
                                <td>Soft rule — TRY to match</td>
                                <td>Scheduled anywhere available</td>
                                <td>Prefer SSD, prefer same zone</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-node-affinity.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      affinity:
        nodeAffinity:
          <span class="comment"># HARD: Must be on an SSD node</span>
          requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            nodeSelectorTerms:
            - matchExpressions:
              - key: disk
                operator: In
                values:
                - ssd
                - nvme
          <span class="comment"># SOFT: Prefer us-east zone (but ok if not available)</span>
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          - weight: 80           <span class="comment"># 1-100, higher = stronger preference</span>
            preference:
              matchExpressions:
              - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
                operator: In
                values:
                - us-east-1a
                - us-east-1b
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Node Affinity Decision Matrix</div>
<pre>
  Pod: easypj (requires: disk ∈ {ssd, nvme}, prefers: zone ∈ {us-east-1a})

  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Node          disk    zone          Required  Preferred   │
  │  ──────────    ────    ──────────    ────────  ─────────   │
  │  worker-1      ssd     us-east-1a   ✅ PASS   ✅ Match    │ ← Best (score 82)
  │  worker-2      ssd     us-west-1a   ✅ PASS   ❌ No match │ ← OK (score 65)
  │  worker-3      hdd     us-east-1a   ❌ FAIL   —           │ ← Rejected
  │  worker-4      nvme    us-east-1b   ✅ PASS   ✅ Match    │ ← Good (score 78)
  │  worker-5      —       us-east-1a   ❌ FAIL   —           │ ← Rejected
  │                                                             │
  │  Result: Pod scheduled on worker-1 (highest combined score)│
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ "IgnoredDuringExecution" — What It Means</h5>
                    <p>If you <strong>remove a node's label after a pod is already scheduled there</strong>, the pod keeps running. The affinity rule is only checked at scheduling time, not continuously. A future Kubernetes version may add <code>RequiredDuringExecution</code> to also evict running pods.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.4 Pod Affinity and Anti-Affinity</h3>
                <p>While <strong>Node Affinity</strong> places pods relative to <em>nodes</em>, <strong>Pod Affinity/Anti-Affinity</strong> places pods relative to <em>other pods</em>. This is essential for performance (co-locate) and high availability (spread out).</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🤝</div>
                        <h5>Pod Affinity</h5>
                        <p><strong>"Place me near this pod."</strong> Put easypj Django on the same node/zone as its Redis cache for low latency.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">↔️</div>
                        <h5>Pod Anti-Affinity</h5>
                        <p><strong>"Keep me away from that pod."</strong> Spread easypj replicas across different nodes so one node failure doesn't take all of them down.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-pod-antiaffinity.yaml — HA Deployment</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      affinity:
        <span class="comment"># Anti-Affinity: Spread replicas across nodes</span>
        podAntiAffinity:
          requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          - labelSelector:
              matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values:
                - easypj
            topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname  # "different nodes"

        <span class="comment"># Affinity: Place near Redis cache</span>
        podAffinity:
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          - weight: 100
            podAffinityTerm:
              labelSelector:
                matchExpressions:
                - key: app
                  operator: In
                  values:
                  - easypj-redis
              topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone  # "same zone"
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod Anti-Affinity — easypj Replicas Spread Across Nodes</div>
<pre>
  Deployment: easypj (replicas: 3)
  podAntiAffinity: kubernetes.io/hostname (no 2 on same node)

  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐
  │   worker-1     │  │   worker-2     │  │   worker-3     │
  │ ┌────────────┐ │  │ ┌────────────┐ │  │ ┌────────────┐ │
  │ │ easypj-abc │ │  │ │ easypj-def │ │  │ │ easypj-ghi │ │
  │ │   📦 v1    │ │  │ │   📦 v1    │ │  │ │   📦 v1    │ │
  │ └────────────┘ │  │ └────────────┘ │  │ └────────────┘ │
  │                │  │                │  │                │
  │ ┌────────────┐ │  │                │  │                │
  │ │ redis-xyz  │ │  │                │  │                │
  │ │   🔴 cache │ │  │                │  │                │
  │ └────────────┘ │  │                │  │                │
  └────────────────┘  └────────────────┘  └────────────────┘

  ✅ Each node has exactly 1 easypj pod — survives any single node failure!
  ✅ easypj-abc co-located with redis (podAffinity) for low latency
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.5 Taints and Tolerations</h3>
                <p>Taints are the <strong>opposite of affinity</strong> — taints <em>repel</em> pods from a node, while tolerations let specific pods <em>override</em> that repulsion. This is a <strong>CKA must-know concept</strong> and appears on almost every exam.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The Analogy</h5>
                    <p>Think of a node's taint as a "No Entry" sign. A toleration is the "VIP pass" that lets a pod in despite the sign. Without the pass, the pod is rejected.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Effect</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Running Pods</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>NoSchedule</strong></td>
                                <td>New pods WITHOUT tolerance are rejected</td>
                                <td>Existing pods stay</td>
                                <td>Dedicated GPU nodes, team isolation</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>PreferNoSchedule</strong></td>
                                <td>Try NOT to schedule here, but allow if no other option</td>
                                <td>Existing pods stay</td>
                                <td>Soft preference — avoid but don't block</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>NoExecute</strong></td>
                                <td>Reject new pods AND <strong>evict running pods</strong> without tolerance</td>
                                <td><strong>Evicted!</strong></td>
                                <td>Node going into maintenance, toxic node</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Taint &amp; Toleration Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add a taint to a node</span>
kubectl taint node worker-1 gpu=true:NoSchedule

<span class="comment"># Verify taint</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 Taints
<span class="comment"># Taints:  gpu=true:NoSchedule</span>

<span class="comment"># Remove a taint (add dash at the end)</span>
kubectl taint node worker-1 gpu=true:NoSchedule-

<span class="comment"># See control plane taints (this is why your pods don't run on master!)</span>
kubectl describe node control-plane | grep Taints
<span class="comment"># Taints:  node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pod-with-toleration.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: ml-training-job
spec:
  tolerations:
  - key: "gpu"
    operator: "Equal"        <span class="comment"># Match exact value</span>
    value: "true"
    effect: "NoSchedule"
  <span class="comment"># Alternative: tolerate ANY value for the key</span>
  <span class="comment"># - key: "gpu"</span>
  <span class="comment">#   operator: "Exists"    # Don't care about value</span>
  <span class="comment">#   effect: "NoSchedule"</span>
  containers:
  - name: trainer
    image: ml-trainer:v1
    resources:
      limits:
        nvidia.com/gpu: 1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Taints &amp; Tolerations — Access Control</div>
<pre>
  ┌────────────────── Node: gpu-node ──────────────────┐
  │ Taint: gpu=true:NoSchedule   🚫                    │
  │                                                     │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  easypj pod (no toleration)                  │   │
  │  │  → ❌ REJECTED — "You don't have a VIP pass" │   │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                     │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  ml-job pod (toleration: gpu=true)           │   │
  │  │  → ✅ SCHEDULED — "VIP pass accepted!"      │   │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ┌────────────── Control Plane Node ──────────────────┐
  │ Taint: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:      │
  │        NoSchedule                                   │
  │                                                     │
  │  That's why your pods DON'T run on control plane!  │
  │  Only system pods (with tolerations) run here:     │
  │  ├── kube-apiserver                                 │
  │  ├── kube-scheduler                                 │
  │  ├── kube-controller-manager                        │
  │  └── etcd                                           │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Trap: Taint Removal Syntax</h5>
                    <p>To <strong>remove</strong> a taint, append a <code>-</code> (minus sign) at the end. Many candidates forget this on the exam:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Add: <code>kubectl taint node worker-1 key=value:NoSchedule</code></li>
                        <li>Remove: <code>kubectl taint node worker-1 key=value:NoSchedule<strong>-</strong></code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.6 Node Management for Admins</h3>
                <p>Cluster administrators regularly need to perform node maintenance — OS upgrades, kernel patches, hardware replacement. Kubernetes provides three commands for safe node management. This is a <strong>very common CKA exam scenario</strong>.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Cordon</h5>
                        <p>Mark node as <strong>unschedulable</strong>. Existing pods stay, but no new pods can be scheduled here.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Drain</h5>
                        <p><strong>Safely evict</strong> all pods to other nodes. Respects PDB, grace periods, and pod lifecycle.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Maintenance</h5>
                        <p>Perform the actual work — OS upgrade, kernel patch, reboot, hardware swap.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Uncordon</h5>
                        <p>Mark node as <strong>schedulable</strong> again. New pods can now be placed here.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Node Maintenance Workflow</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Cordon — stop new pods from scheduling</span>
kubectl cordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 cordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify: node shows SchedulingDisabled</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME        STATUS                     ROLES    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1    Ready,SchedulingDisabled   worker   30d</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Drain — safely evict all pods</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># evicting pod default/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12</span>
<span class="comment"># evicting pod default/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-def34</span>
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 drained</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Perform maintenance (OS upgrade, reboot, etc.)</span>
<span class="comment"># ... work happens here ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Uncordon — allow pods again</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 uncordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify: node is Ready again (no SchedulingDisabled)</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME        STATUS   ROLES    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1    Ready    worker   30d</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Node Maintenance Flow — Safe Pod Migration</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────── worker-1 (BEFORE) ───────┐     ┌─────── worker-2 ────────┐
  │                                  │     │                          │
  │  📦 easypj-abc    📦 redis-xyz  │     │  📦 easypj-ghi          │
  │  📦 easypj-def                  │     │                          │
  │                                  │     │                          │
  └──────────────────────────────────┘     └──────────────────────────┘

  Step 1: kubectl cordon worker-1
  ┌─── worker-1 (🚫 SchedulingDisabled) ──┐
  │  📦 easypj-abc  📦 redis-xyz  (stay)  │  ← existing pods remain
  │  📦 easypj-def                (stay)  │  ← new pods blocked
  └────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Step 2: kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets
  ┌─── worker-1 (EMPTY) ────┐     ┌─────── worker-2 ────────────┐
  │                          │     │  📦 easypj-ghi (existing)   │
  │  (all pods evicted)      │     │  📦 easypj-abc (migrated)   │
  │                          │     │  📦 easypj-def (migrated)   │
  │  🔧 Maintenance time!   │     │  📦 redis-xyz  (migrated)   │
  └──────────────────────────┘     └──────────────────────────────┘

  Step 3-4: Maintenance → uncordon
  ┌─── worker-1 (✅ Ready) ──┐
  │  Now accepts new pods!    │
  └───────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Drain Gotchas</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> is required if DaemonSet pods exist (they can't be evicted)</li>
                        <li><code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> is needed if pods use <code>emptyDir</code> volumes</li>
                        <li><code>--force</code> only if there are standalone pods (not managed by RS/Deployment) — data will be lost!</li>
                        <li>Without these flags, drain will refuse and show an error — know which flag to add!</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.7 Topology Spread Constraints</h3>
                <p>Topology spread constraints give you fine-grained control over how pods are distributed across topology domains (zones, nodes, regions). Unlike pod anti-affinity which is "avoid same node", topology spread says "distribute <strong>evenly</strong>".</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-topology-spread.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 6
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      topologySpreadConstraints:
      - maxSkew: 1                           <span class="comment"># Max diff in pod count between zones</span>
        topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone  <span class="comment"># Spread across zones</span>
        whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule     <span class="comment"># Hard constraint</span>
        labelSelector:
          matchLabels:
            app: easypj
      - maxSkew: 1
        topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname   <span class="comment"># Also spread across nodes</span>
        whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway    <span class="comment"># Soft constraint</span>
        labelSelector:
          matchLabels:
            app: easypj
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Topology Spread — Even Distribution Across Zones</div>
<pre>
  6 replicas, maxSkew: 1, topologyKey: zone

  ┌───── us-east-1a ──────┐  ┌───── us-east-1b ──────┐  ┌───── us-east-1c ──────┐
  │  📦 easypj-1           │  │  📦 easypj-3           │  │  📦 easypj-5           │
  │  📦 easypj-2           │  │  📦 easypj-4           │  │  📦 easypj-6           │
  │  Count: 2              │  │  Count: 2              │  │  Count: 2              │
  └────────────────────────┘  └────────────────────────┘  └────────────────────────┘

  maxSkew = max(count) - min(count) = 2 - 2 = 0  ✅ (within maxSkew of 1)

  ❌ Without topology spread (all could land on one zone):
  ┌───── us-east-1a ──────┐  ┌───── us-east-1b ──────┐  ┌───── us-east-1c ──────┐
  │  📦📦📦📦📦📦           │  │  (empty)               │  │  (empty)               │
  │  Count: 6   ← BAD!    │  │  Count: 0              │  │  Count: 0              │
  └────────────────────────┘  └────────────────────────┘  └────────────────────────┘
  Zone failure = total outage! 💀
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Topology Spread Parameters</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📏</span> maxSkew</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Maximum allowed difference in pod counts between topology domains. Lower = more even.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗺️</span> topologyKey</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Node label to group by: <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code> (node), <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code> (zone)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🚫</span> whenUnsatisfiable</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>DoNotSchedule</code> (hard — pod stays Pending) or <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> (soft — best effort)</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.8 Priority and Preemption</h3>
                <p>PriorityClasses let you assign importance levels to pods. When the cluster is full, a high-priority pod can <strong>preempt</strong> (evict) a lower-priority pod to make room.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">priority-classes.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># High priority — critical production workloads</span>
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: high-priority
value: 1000000
globalDefault: false
preemptionPolicy: PreemptLowerPriority
description: "Critical production workloads like easypj"
---
<span class="comment"># Low priority — batch jobs, can be evicted</span>
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: low-priority
value: 100
globalDefault: false
preemptionPolicy: Never          <span class="comment"># Don't preempt others</span>
description: "Batch jobs and background tasks"
---
<span class="comment"># Usage in Deployment</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      priorityClassName: high-priority   # ← assign priority
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Preemption — High Priority Pod Evicts Low Priority</div>
<pre>
  Cluster is FULL — no free capacity

  ┌──── worker-1 ─────┐  ┌──── worker-2 ─────┐
  │ 📦 batch-job-1     │  │ 📦 batch-job-3     │
  │ (low-priority:100) │  │ (low-priority:100) │
  │ 📦 batch-job-2     │  │ 📦 batch-job-4     │
  │ (low-priority:100) │  │ (low-priority:100) │
  └────────────────────┘  └────────────────────┘

  NEW POD: easypj (high-priority: 1000000) needs scheduling!

  Scheduler: "No room, but easypj has higher priority..."
  → PREEMPTS batch-job-1 on worker-1

  ┌──── worker-1 ─────┐  ┌──── worker-2 ─────┐
  │ 🌟 easypj          │  │ 📦 batch-job-3     │
  │ (high-priority!)   │  │ (low-priority:100) │
  │ 📦 batch-job-2     │  │ 📦 batch-job-4     │
  │ (low-priority:100) │  │ (low-priority:100) │
  └────────────────────┘  └────────────────────┘

  batch-job-1 was evicted to make room for easypj  ⚡
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 When to Use PriorityClasses</h5>
                    <p>Use <strong>high priority</strong> for production-critical workloads (web servers, APIs). Use <strong>low priority</strong> for batch jobs, CI/CD runners, dev environments. This ensures production always gets resources first.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🧩 Scheduling Mechanisms Comparison — When to Use What</h5>
                    <div class="comparison-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Mechanism</th>
                                    <th>Controls</th>
                                    <th>Direction</th>
                                    <th>Best For</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>nodeSelector</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod → Node</td>
                                    <td>🟢 Attract (hard)</td>
                                    <td>Simple label matching</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Node Affinity</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod → Node</td>
                                    <td>🟢 Attract (hard/soft)</td>
                                    <td>Complex rules, preferred placement</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Pod Affinity</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod → Pod</td>
                                    <td>🟢 Attract (co-locate)</td>
                                    <td>Low latency (app near cache)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Pod Anti-Affinity</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod → Pod</td>
                                    <td>🔴 Repel (spread)</td>
                                    <td>HA (replicas on different nodes)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Taints/Tolerations</strong></td>
                                    <td>Node → Pod</td>
                                    <td>🔴 Repel + allow</td>
                                    <td>Dedicated nodes, maintenance</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Topology Spread</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod distribution</td>
                                    <td>⚖️ Even spread</td>
                                    <td>Balance across zones/nodes</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Static Pods</strong></td>
                                    <td>Fixed to node</td>
                                    <td>📌 Pinned</td>
                                    <td>Control plane components</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>PriorityClass</strong></td>
                                    <td>Pod importance</td>
                                    <td>⬆️ Preempt lower</td>
                                    <td>Critical vs batch workloads</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §14.9 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s14-9">
                <h3 class="section-h">14.9 Practice Exercises</h3>
                <p>These exercises mirror actual CKA exam questions. Practice each one until you can complete it in under 5 minutes.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏋️</div>
                        <h5>Exercise 14.1: Taints &amp; Tolerations</h5>
                        <p>1. Taint <code>node01</code> with <code>env=prod:NoSchedule</code><br>
                        2. Create an nginx pod — verify it does NOT schedule on node01<br>
                        3. Add a toleration — verify it now schedules there<br>
                        4. Remove the taint</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏋️</div>
                        <h5>Exercise 14.2: Node Affinity</h5>
                        <p>1. Label <code>node01</code> with <code>disktype=ssd</code><br>
                        2. Create pod with required nodeAffinity for ssd nodes<br>
                        3. Create pod with preferred nodeAffinity for ssd<br>
                        4. Verify with <code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏋️</div>
                        <h5>Exercise 14.3: Pod Anti-Affinity</h5>
                        <p>1. Create easypj deployment (replicas: 3)<br>
                        2. Add podAntiAffinity — no 2 pods on same node<br>
                        3. Verify: <code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> (all on different nodes)<br>
                        4. Scale to 4 — what happens if only 3 nodes?</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏋️</div>
                        <h5>Exercise 14.4: Node Maintenance</h5>
                        <p>1. Cordon worker-1<br>
                        2. Deploy nginx (replicas: 3) — verify none land on worker-1<br>
                        3. Drain worker-1 safely<br>
                        4. Uncordon and verify</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Exercise 14.1 — Solution (try it yourself first!)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Taint the node</span>
kubectl taint node node01 env=prod:NoSchedule

<span class="comment"># 2. Create nginx pod (no toleration)</span>
kubectl run nginx-test --image=nginx
kubectl get pod nginx-test -o wide
<span class="comment"># → Should be on a different node (not node01)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Create pod WITH toleration</span>
cat &lt;&lt;'EOF' | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx-tolerant
spec:
  tolerations:
  - key: "env"
    operator: "Equal"
    value: "prod"
    effect: "NoSchedule"
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
EOF
kubectl get pod nginx-tolerant -o wide
<span class="comment"># → Can schedule on node01</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Remove taint</span>
kubectl taint node node01 env=prod:NoSchedule-

<span class="comment"># Clean up</span>
kubectl delete pod nginx-test nginx-tolerant</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How the Scheduler Filters and Scores Nodes: Filtering Plugins, Scoring Algorithms, Taints/Tolerations, and Node Affinity Resolution</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ The Scheduling Pipeline — Two-Phase Decision Making</h5>
            <p>The kube-scheduler doesn't randomly assign pods. It runs a sophisticated two-phase algorithm: FILTERING (eliminate infeasible nodes) → SCORING (rank remaining nodes). Understanding this helps you debug "0/3 nodes are available" errors.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SCHEDULING PIPELINE — Full Algorithm                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Pod to schedule: easypj-web (requests: cpu=500m, memory=512Mi)             │
│  Cluster: 5 worker nodes                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ═══════════════════ PHASE 1: FILTERING (5 nodes → ?) ═══════════════════    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ FILTER PLUGIN          CHECK                   N1  N2  N3  N4  N5   │    │
│  │ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │    │
│  │ NodeUnschedulable      node.spec.unschedulable?  ✅  ✅  ✅  ✅  ✅   │    │
│  │ NodeResourcesFit       enough cpu/memory?       ✅  ✅  ❌  ✅  ✅   │    │
│  │                         (N3: only 200m cpu free)                      │    │
│  │ NodeSelector           node labels match?        ✅  ✅  -   ✅  ✅   │    │
│  │ NodeAffinity           affinity rules match?     ✅  ✅  -   ✅  ✅   │    │
│  │ TaintToleration        pod tolerates taints?    ✅  ❌  -   ✅  ✅   │    │
│  │                         (N2: taint gpu=true:NoSchedule)               │    │
│  │ NodePorts              hostPort conflicts?       ✅  -   -   ✅  ✅   │    │
│  │ VolumeLimits           can attach more volumes?  ✅  -   -   ✅  ✅   │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ FILTERED: 3 nodes survive (N1, N4, N5)                               │    │
│  │ ELIMINATED: N2 (taint), N3 (insufficient resources)                  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ═══════════════════ PHASE 2: SCORING (3 nodes → ranking) ═══════════════    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ SCORING PLUGIN          WEIGHT     N1     N4     N5    DESCRIPTION   │    │
│  │ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │    │
│  │ NodeResourcesFit        1.0        85     72     90    Least loaded  │    │
│  │   (LeastAllocated)                                   wins (N5)      │    │
│  │ NodeResourcesBalanced   1.0        78     88     65    Balanced CPU  │    │
│  │   (BalancedAllocation)                               /memory (N4)   │    │
│  │ ImageLocality           1.0        90     50     50    Image cached  │    │
│  │   (image already on node)                             on N1?         │    │
│  │ NodeAffinityPriority    1.0        100    50     50    Preferred     │    │
│  │   (preferredDuringScheduling)                        affinity (N1)  │    │
│  │ InterPodAffinity        2.0        50     100    50    Pods with     │    │
│  │   (preferredDuringScheduling)                        same affinity  │    │
│  │                                                      on N4          │    │
│  │ TaintTolerationPriority 1.0        80     80     100   Prefer no     │    │
│  │   (PreferNoSchedule taint)                           taint nodes    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ FINAL SCORES (weighted sum):                                         │    │
│  │   N1: (85+78+90+100+50+80)/6 = 80.5                                 │    │
│  │   N4: (72+88+50+50+100+80)/6 = 73.3                                 │    │
│  │   N5: (90+65+50+50+50+100)/6 = 67.5  <span class="comment">← LOWEST score                 </span>│    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ 🏆 WINNER: N1 (score 80.5) → Pod bound to N1                       │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  TAINTS & TOLERATIONS — Like a "No Entry" Sign with VIP Passes:             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  TAINT (on node):          TOLERATION (on pod):                      │    │
│  │  ┌─────────────────────┐   ┌──────────────────────────┐            │    │
│  │  │ key=value:Effect     │   │ key: "gpu"               │            │    │
│  │  │                     │   │ operator: "Equal"         │            │    │
│  │  │ Effect types:       │   │ value: "nvidia"           │            │    │
│  │  │  NoSchedule  (hard) │   │ effect: "NoSchedule"      │            │    │
│  │  │  PreferNoSchedule   │   └──────────────────────────┘            │    │
│  │  │    (soft, preferred)│                                            │    │
│  │  │  NoExecute (hard,   │   TOLERATION MATCHES IF:                   │    │
│  │  │    evict existing)  │   1. Same key                              │    │
│  │  └─────────────────────┘   2. Same effect                            │    │
│  │                             3. Either: operator=Exists (any value)   │    │
│  │                                OR: operator=Equal AND same value     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  NODE DRAIN MECHANISM:                                               │    │
│  │  kubectl drain node01 --ignore-daemonsets                            │    │
│  │  1. Cordon node (mark unschedulable)                                 │    │
│  │  2. Evict all pods (respects PDBs, graceful termination)            │    │
│  │  3. DaemonSet pods ignored (recreated automatically)                 │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, when a pod is stuck in Pending with "0/3 nodes are available," use <code>kubectl describe pod</code> and look at the Events section. The scheduler explains WHY each node was rejected: "1 node(s) had taint {gpu: true}," "2 node(s) didn't match Pod's node affinity," "3 Insufficient cpu." Also check: <code>kubectl top nodes</code> (resource usage) and <code>kubectl describe node | grep -A5 Taints</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>nodeSelector vs nodeAffinity</strong> — <code>nodeSelector</code> is simple key=value matching. <code>nodeAffinity</code> supports <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>DoesNotExist</code>. The exam tests both</li>
                    <li><strong>Taint effect matters</strong> — <code>NoSchedule</code> = new pods blocked. <code>NoExecute</code> = existing pods EVICTED. <code>PreferNoSchedule</code> = soft preference. Mixing them up = wrong answer</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl drain</code> flags</strong> — Without <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> it fails on DaemonSet pods. Without <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> it fails on emptyDir volumes. Always use both</li>
                    <li><strong>Cordon ≠ Drain</strong> — <code>cordon</code> only prevents NEW pods. <code>drain</code> evicts existing pods AND cordons. After maintenance: <code>uncordon</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Toleration must EXACTLY match taint</strong> — key, value, AND effect must match. A toleration with the wrong effect won't work</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Node maintenance in 30 seconds:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl drain node01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code><br>
                <em>… perform maintenance (upgrade, reboot) …</em><br>
                <code>kubectl uncordon node01</code><br>
                Done. Pods will be rescheduled back automatically.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 14)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Command</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Label a node</td><td><code>kubectl label node NAME key=value</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Remove label</td><td><code>kubectl label node NAME key-</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Add taint</td><td><code>kubectl taint node NAME key=val:Effect</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Remove taint</td><td><code>kubectl taint node NAME key=val:Effect-</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Cordon node</td><td><code>kubectl cordon NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Drain node</td><td><code>kubectl drain NAME --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Uncordon node</td><td><code>kubectl uncordon NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check node taints</td><td><code>kubectl describe node NAME | grep Taints</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Show node labels</td><td><code>kubectl get nodes --show-labels</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check pod placement</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 6 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Label node <code>node01</code> with <code>disk=ssd</code></li>
                    <li>2. Create a pod with <code>nodeSelector: disk: ssd</code> — verify it lands on node01</li>
                    <li>3. Taint node01: <code>kubectl taint node node01 maintenance=true:NoSchedule</code></li>
                    <li>4. Create another pod WITHOUT toleration — it should go to a different node</li>
                    <li>5. Drain node01, uncordon, verify pods reschedule</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 14 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch14-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 14 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (12)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of scheduling &amp; node-placement rules — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Schedule a pod named <code>gpu-pod</code> to run ONLY on nodes labeled <code>hardware=gpu</code>. Show the imperative generation and the relevant YAML fields.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">first, label the target node</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># First, label the target node</span>
kubectl label node worker-1 hardware=gpu

<span class="comment"># Generate pod YAML (add nodeSelector manually)</span>
kubectl run gpu-pod --image=nvidia/cuda:12.0-base \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > gpu-pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Add nodeSelector to spec:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: gpu-pod
spec:
nodeSelector:         # ← Simple key-value matching
hardware: gpu
containers:
- name: gpu-pod
image: nvidia/cuda:12.0-base

kubectl apply -f gpu-pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify placement</span>
kubectl get pod gpu-pod -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME      READY  STATUS   NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># gpu-pod   1/1    Running  worker-1</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>nodeSelector</code> is the simplest way to constrain pods to specific nodes. It uses exact label matching — the node MUST have all specified labels. If no node matches, the pod stays <code>Pending</code>. On the CKA, <code>nodeSelector</code> is the most commonly tested scheduling mechanism because it's fast to configure. Remember: the label must exist on the node BEFORE the pod is created (or the pod stays Pending). Use <code>kubectl get nodes --show-labels</code> to verify.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node named <code>node01</code> has a taint <code>env=production:NoSchedule</code>. Write a pod YAML that can be scheduled on this node. What happens if you use <code>NoExecute</code> instead?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">apply the taint</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Apply the taint</span>
kubectl taint node node01 env=production:NoSchedule

<span class="comment"># Pod YAML with matching toleration:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: prod-pod
spec:
tolerations:
- key: "env"
operator: "Equal"
value: "production"
effect: "NoSchedule"
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx

<span class="comment"># Taint effects comparison:</span>
<span class="comment"># NoSchedule:    Won't schedule new pods without toleration</span>
<span class="comment">#                Existing pods stay</span>
<span class="comment"># PreferNoSchedule: Try to avoid, but allow if no other option</span>
<span class="comment"># NoExecute:     Won't schedule new pods AND evicts existing</span>
<span class="comment">#                pods without toleration</span>

<span class="comment"># NoExecute with tolerationSeconds:</span>
tolerations:
- key: "env"
operator: "Equal"
value: "production"
effect: "NoExecute"
tolerationSeconds: 3600   <span class="comment"># Stay for 1 hour, then evicted</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>NoSchedule</code> only prevents NEW pods from being scheduled. <code>NoExecute</code> is more aggressive — it also EVICTS pods that are already running on the node unless they have a matching toleration. <code>tolerationSeconds</code> allows a pod to remain for a limited time before eviction. On the CKA, the taint effect is critical: <code>NoSchedule</code> for maintenance windows (existing pods stay), <code>NoExecute</code> for draining workloads. The <code>operator: "Equal"</code> requires exact key+value match; <code>operator: "Exists"</code> matches any value for that key.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to drain a node for maintenance. What sequence of commands should you use, and what safety flags are important?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cordon the node (mark unschedulable)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Cordon the node (mark unschedulable)</span>
kubectl cordon node01
<span class="comment"># node/node01 cordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Drain the node (evict all pods)</span>
kubectl drain node01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># Evicts all pods, ignoring DaemonSet-managed pods</span>

<span class="comment"># Important flags:</span>
<span class="comment"># --ignore-daemonsets    Required! DaemonSet pods can't be evicted</span>
<span class="comment"># --delete-emptydir-data  Required if pods use emptyDir volumes</span>
<span class="comment"># --force                 Required for pods not managed by a controller</span>
<span class="comment">#                         (standalone pods will be LOST forever!)</span>
<span class="comment"># --grace-period=30       Override default termination grace period</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify node is drained</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME     STATUS                     ROLES</span>
<span class="comment"># node01   Ready,SchedulingDisabled   worker</span>

kubectl get pods -o wide | grep node01
<span class="comment"># (no pods, except DaemonSet ones)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Perform maintenance...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Uncordon when done</span>
kubectl uncordon node01
<span class="comment"># node/node01 uncordoned (scheduling re-enabled)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl drain</code> = cordon + evict all pods. Without <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code>, it fails because DaemonSet pods are supposed to run on every node. Without <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code>, it fails for pods using emptyDir (data will be lost). <code>--force</code> deletes standalone pods (not managed by a controller) — these pods are gone permanently since no controller recreates them. On the CKA, <strong>always</strong> include <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code>. If the drain refuses, read the error — it tells you exactly which flag to add.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>nodeSelector</code> and <code>nodeAffinity</code>? When must you use <code>nodeAffinity</code> instead?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Feature</th>
                                        <th>nodeSelector</th>
                                        <th>nodeAffinity</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Operators</td>
                                        <td>Exact match only</td>
                                        <td>In, NotIn, Exists, DoesNotExist, Gt, Lt</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Required vs Preferred</td>
                                        <td>Always required</td>
                                        <td>Both options available</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Complexity</td>
                                        <td>Simple key:value</td>
                                        <td>Complex expressions</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">nodeaffinity — required (hard rule)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># nodeAffinity — required (hard rule)</span>
spec:
affinity:
nodeAffinity:
  requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
    nodeSelectorTerms:
    - matchExpressions:
      - key: disktype
        operator: In
        values: ["ssd", "nvme"]    <span class="comment"># Match either value</span>

<span class="comment"># nodeAffinity — preferred (soft rule)</span>
  preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
  - weight: 80
    preference:
      matchExpressions:
      - key: zone
        operator: In
        values: ["us-east-1a"]</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Use <code>nodeAffinity</code> when you need: (1) multiple possible values (<code>operator: In</code>), (2) negation (<code>NotIn</code>), (3) soft preferences (preferred vs required), or (4) complex expressions. <code>nodeSelector</code> only supports exact single-value matching. On the CKA, <code>nodeAffinity</code> is harder to type from memory — bookmark the docs page. <code>requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution</code> means "enforce at schedule time, don't evict if label changes later." The word <code>required</code> = hard constraint; <code>preferred</code> = best-effort.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Schedule a pod on a specific node named <code>controlplane</code> without using labels or taints. What mechanism allows this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">use nodename to bypass the scheduler entirely</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Use nodeName to bypass the scheduler entirely</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: debug-pod
spec:
nodeName: controlplane    # ← Direct node assignment
containers:
- name: debug
image: busybox
command: ["sleep", "3600"]

<span class="comment"># This pod is NOT scheduled by kube-scheduler</span>
<span class="comment"># It's bound directly to the named node</span>
<span class="comment"># If controlplane doesn't exist → pod stays Pending forever</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pod debug-pod -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS   NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># debug-pod  1/1    Running  controlplane</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>nodeName</code> bypasses the scheduler completely — the pod is directly assigned to the specified node. This ignores all taints, tolerations, nodeSelector, and affinity rules. It's useful for debugging or when the scheduler is down. On the CKA, <code>nodeName</code> appears in questions about "static scheduling" or "scheduling without the scheduler." Drawbacks: if the node goes down, the pod is NOT rescheduled (no controller watching). Use only for temporary debugging, not production workloads.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is stuck in <code>Pending</code> state. <code>kubectl describe pod</code> shows: <em>"0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:}, 2 node(s) didn't match Pod's node affinity/selector."</em> Diagnose and fix.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnosis</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Diagnosis:</span>
<span class="comment"># - 1 node: tainted as control-plane (expected)</span>
<span class="comment"># - 2 nodes: don't match the pod's nodeSelector or affinity</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check what the pod requires</span>
kubectl get pod pending-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeSelector}'
<span class="comment"># {"gpu":"true"}</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check available node labels</span>
kubectl get nodes --show-labels | grep gpu
<span class="comment"># (no output — no node has gpu=true label!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 1: Label a worker node</span>
kubectl label node worker-1 gpu=true

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 2: Remove the nodeSelector from the pod</span>
<span class="comment"># (Delete and recreate without nodeSelector)</span>
kubectl get pod pending-pod -o yaml > pod.yaml
<span class="comment"># Edit pod.yaml: remove nodeSelector section</span>
kubectl delete pod pending-pod
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get pod pending-pod
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Running</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The scheduler message tells you exactly why no node is suitable. On the CKA, this is a common troubleshooting scenario. The diagnostic flow: (1) Check Events in <code>kubectl describe pod</code> for the scheduler message, (2) Extract the pod's scheduling constraints (<code>nodeSelector</code>, <code>affinity</code>, <code>tolerations</code>), (3) Compare against available nodes and their labels/taints. Remember: control-plane nodes have a default taint — pods won't schedule there unless they have the matching toleration.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you remove a taint from a node? Show the command for removing <code>env=production:NoSchedule</code> from <code>node01</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">remove a specific taint (note the trailing minus -)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Remove a specific taint (note the trailing minus -)</span>
kubectl taint node node01 env=production:NoSchedule-
<span class="comment"># node/node01 untainted</span>

<span class="comment"># Remove taint by key only (removes all effects for that key)</span>
kubectl taint node node01 env-
<span class="comment"># Removes env:NoSchedule, env:NoExecute, etc.</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify taint is removed</span>
kubectl describe node node01 | grep -i taint
<span class="comment"># Taints: &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Common mistake — forgetting the minus sign:</span>
kubectl taint node node01 env=production:NoSchedule
<span class="comment"># This ADDS the taint instead of removing it!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The trailing <code>-</code> (minus sign) at the end of the taint specification removes it. This is the same pattern as <code>kubectl label node node01 key-</code> for removing labels. On the CKA, forgetting the <code>-</code> is a common time-wasting error that ADDS the taint instead. If you specify key=value:effect, it removes that exact taint. If you specify just key-, it removes all taints with that key regardless of value or effect.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>podAffinity</code> vs <code>podAntiAffinity</code>? Give a scenario where you'd use each on the CKA exam.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">podaffinity: schedule near pods with specific labels</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># podAffinity: Schedule NEAR pods with specific labels</span>
<span class="comment"># Use case: web pod should be on same node as cache pod</span>
spec:
affinity:
podAffinity:
  requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
  - labelSelector:
      matchExpressions:
      - key: app
        operator: In
        values: ["redis-cache"]
    topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname

<span class="comment"># podAntiAffinity: Schedule AWAY from pods with specific labels</span>
<span class="comment"># Use case: spread replicas across different nodes</span>
spec:
affinity:
podAntiAffinity:
  requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
  - labelSelector:
      matchExpressions:
      - key: app
        operator: In
        values: ["webapp"]
    topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
<span class="comment"># Each webapp replica lands on a different node</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>podAffinity</code> = co-locate pods (low latency between services). <code>podAntiAffinity</code> = spread pods apart (high availability). <code>topologyKey</code> defines the "zone" — <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code> means per-node, <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code> means per-AZ. On the CKA, anti-affinity is more commonly tested — especially "ensure no two replicas of the same app run on the same node." If you have 3 replicas and only 2 nodes with <code>required</code> anti-affinity, the 3rd pod stays Pending.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check which taints are applied to all nodes in the cluster? And how do you find which pods have tolerations?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">view taints on all nodes</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View taints on all nodes</span>
kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=\
NAME:.metadata.name,\
TAINTS:.spec.taints

<span class="comment"># Or with describe</span>
kubectl describe nodes | grep -A5 "Taints:"
<span class="comment"># Taints: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</span>
<span class="comment"># Taints: env=production:NoSchedule</span>

<span class="comment"># JSON path for specific node</span>
kubectl get node node01 -o jsonpath='{.spec.taints[*]}'

<span class="comment"># Find pods with tolerations</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.tolerations}{"\n"}{end}'

<span class="comment"># Or describe a specific pod</span>
kubectl describe pod my-pod | grep -A10 "Tolerations:"
<span class="comment"># Tolerations: node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s</span>
<span class="comment">#              node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Every pod automatically gets two default tolerations: <code>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute</code> and <code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute</code> with 300s tolerance — this is why pods stay for 5 minutes after a node becomes unreachable. On the CKA, quickly checking taints with <code>custom-columns</code> is efficient. The control-plane taint <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code> prevents workload pods from scheduling on the control plane — this is normal and expected. DaemonSet pods (like kube-proxy) tolerate this automatically.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A DaemonSet pod is not running on <code>node02</code> but runs on all other nodes. What are the possible causes and how do you investigate?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if node02 is cordoned</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check if node02 is cordoned</span>
kubectl get node node02
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Ready,SchedulingDisabled ← cordoned!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check taints on node02</span>
kubectl describe node node02 | grep Taints
<span class="comment"># Taints: special=true:NoSchedule</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check DaemonSet tolerations</span>
kubectl get ds monitoring-agent -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.tolerations}'
<span class="comment"># Missing toleration for special=true:NoSchedule!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 1: Add toleration to DaemonSet</span>
kubectl edit ds monitoring-agent
<span class="comment"># Add toleration for the taint</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 2: Remove the taint</span>
kubectl taint node node02 special-

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check node conditions</span>
kubectl describe node node02 | grep -A5 Conditions
<span class="comment"># MemoryPressure: True ← node under memory pressure</span>
<span class="comment"># DiskPressure: True   ← node under disk pressure</span>
<span class="comment"># These auto-add taints that evict pods!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check DaemonSet's nodeSelector</span>
kubectl get ds monitoring-agent -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.nodeSelector}'
<span class="comment"># {"os":"linux"} — does node02 have this label?</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>DaemonSets are designed to run on every node, but they still respect taints and nodeSelectors. Common causes for missing DaemonSet pods: (1) node is cordoned (scheduling disabled), (2) taint without matching toleration, (3) nodeSelector mismatch, (4) node conditions like MemoryPressure auto-add taints. On the CKA, DaemonSet scheduling issues are a classic troubleshooting question. Note: DaemonSets automatically tolerate <code>node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable:NoSchedule</code> (cordon), but if you manually add a custom taint, the DaemonSet must explicitly tolerate it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Deployment with <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> that spreads pods evenly across nodes with a maximum skew of 1. What happens when the constraint can't be satisfied?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
name: easypj-web
spec:
replicas: 6
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: easypj
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: easypj
spec:
  topologySpreadConstraints:
  - maxSkew: 1                           <span class="comment"># Max difference between zones</span>
    topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname   <span class="comment"># Spread across nodes</span>
    whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule      <span class="comment"># Block if can't spread</span>
    labelSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: easypj
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:v1

<span class="comment"># With 3 nodes and 6 replicas:</span>
<span class="comment">#   node1: 2 pods, node2: 2 pods, node3: 2 pods ✅</span>
<span class="comment">#   node1: 3 pods, node2: 2 pods, node3: 1 pod  ❌ skew=2</span>

<span class="comment"># whenUnsatisfiable options:</span>
<span class="comment"># DoNotSchedule  → Pod stays Pending (strict)</span>
<span class="comment"># ScheduleAnyway → Schedule anyway, best-effort (soft)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check distribution:</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj -o wide \
--sort-by='.spec.nodeName'
<span class="comment"># Shows which pod is on which node</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> provide fine-grained control over pod distribution across failure domains (nodes, zones, regions). <code>maxSkew</code> defines the maximum allowed difference in pod count between topology domains. <code>topologyKey</code> determines what defines a domain — <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code> for nodes, <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code> for zones. <code>whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule</code> makes pods Pending if they'd violate the constraint — like a hard anti-affinity but more flexible. On the CKA, topology spread is the modern replacement for <code>podAntiAffinity</code> when you need even distribution with more control.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a PriorityClass? Create a high-priority class and show how a high-priority pod can preempt a lower-priority one.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create priorityclasses</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create PriorityClasses</span>
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
name: high-priority
value: 1000000
globalDefault: false
preemptionPolicy: PreemptLowerPriority
description: "Critical production workloads"
---
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
name: low-priority
value: 100
globalDefault: false
preemptionPolicy: Never     # ← Won't evict others
description: "Background batch jobs"

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Use in a Pod spec</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: critical-app
spec:
priorityClassName: high-priority   # ← Reference by name
containers:
- name: app
image: easypj:v1
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "2"
    memory: "4Gi"

<span class="comment"># What happens during preemption:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Cluster is full — no node has space for critical-app</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Scheduler finds low-priority pods on a node</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Scheduler evicts low-priority pods to make room</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. critical-app gets scheduled</span>

<span class="comment"># Check PriorityClasses:</span>
kubectl get priorityclasses
<span class="comment"># NAME            VALUE      GLOBAL-DEFAULT</span>
<span class="comment"># high-priority   1000000    false</span>
<span class="comment"># low-priority    100        false</span>
<span class="comment"># system-*        very high  (built-in for kube-system)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PriorityClasses assign scheduling priority to pods. When the cluster is full, the scheduler evicts lower-priority pods to make room for higher-priority ones. <code>value</code> is an integer (higher = more important). <code>preemptionPolicy: PreemptLowerPriority</code> (default) allows evicting others; <code>Never</code> means the pod waits without evicting. Kubernetes has built-in system priority classes (e.g., <code>system-cluster-critical</code> value 2000000000) for control plane components. On the CKA, you may need to create a PriorityClass and assign it to a pod. Remember: PriorityClass is <strong>cluster-scoped</strong> (no namespace), and the API group is <code>scheduling.k8s.io/v1</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 14 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch15">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 15 · Resource Management</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Every container in Kubernetes can request a minimum amount of CPU and memory, and be limited to a maximum. Understanding requests, limits, QoS classes, LimitRanges, and ResourceQuotas is critical for <strong>cluster stability</strong> — and prominently tested on the CKA exam. A misconfigured resource setting can crash an entire node.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📊 Resources</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Set CPU and memory requests (scheduling guarantee) and limits (hard ceiling)</li>
                    <li>✅ Understand QoS classes — Guaranteed, Burstable, BestEffort</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure LimitRange for namespace-wide default limits</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure ResourceQuota to cap total namespace consumption</li>
                    <li>✅ Know what happens when CPU is exceeded (throttle) vs memory (OOMKilled)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Resource Questions on the CKA</h5>
                <p>You'll likely be asked to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Create pods with specific resource requests and limits</li>
                    <li>Create LimitRange or ResourceQuota for a namespace</li>
                    <li>Identify why a pod is <code>OOMKilled</code> or stuck in <code>Pending</code></li>
                    <li>Know: <code>kubectl top pods</code> and <code>kubectl top nodes</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §15.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.1 Resource Requests and Limits</h3>
                <p>Every container can specify <strong>requests</strong> (minimum guaranteed) and <strong>limits</strong> (maximum allowed). The scheduler uses requests to decide where to place pods. The kubelet enforces limits at runtime.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Requests</h5>
                        <p><strong>"I need at least this much."</strong> Used by the scheduler for placement. The node must have this amount free or the pod won't be scheduled.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚧</div>
                        <h5>Limits</h5>
                        <p><strong>"Don't use more than this."</strong> Enforced by the kubelet. CPU exceeds limit → throttled. Memory exceeds limit → OOMKilled.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-resources.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:v1
    resources:
      requests:                  <span class="comment"># Scheduling guarantee</span>
        cpu: "250m"              <span class="comment"># 0.25 CPU cores (250 millicores)</span>
        memory: "256Mi"          <span class="comment"># 256 MiB RAM</span>
      limits:                    <span class="comment"># Hard ceiling</span>
        cpu: "500m"              <span class="comment"># 0.5 CPU cores max</span>
        memory: "512Mi"          <span class="comment"># 512 MiB RAM max</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>CPU &amp; Memory Units</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚙️</span> CPU: 1000m = 1 core</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>100m</code> = 0.1 core, <code>250m</code> = 0.25 core, <code>1</code> = 1 full core, <code>2</code> = 2 cores</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💾</span> Memory: Mi, Gi</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>128Mi</code> = 128 MiB, <code>1Gi</code> = 1 GiB. Use <strong>Mi/Gi</strong> (binary) not MB/GB (decimal).</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⏳</span> CPU exceeded</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Throttled</strong> — process slows down but keeps running. Not killed.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> Memory exceeded</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>OOMKilled</strong> — container is killed immediately. Restarts per restartPolicy.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>⏳ CPU Exceeded → Throttled</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Container is <strong>NOT killed</strong></li>
                            <li>Process slows down, capped at the limit</li>
                            <li>No restarts, no OOM events</li>
                            <li>Hard to detect — app just becomes slow</li>
                            <li>Diagnose: <code>kubectl top pod</code> shows CPU pinned at limit</li>
                            <li><strong>Fix:</strong> Increase <code>limits.cpu</code> or optimize app</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>💀 Memory Exceeded → OOMKilled</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Container is <strong>killed immediately</strong> (SIGKILL)</li>
                            <li>Exit code <strong>137</strong> in pod description</li>
                            <li>Pod restarts → often leads to CrashLoopBackOff</li>
                            <li>Easy to detect: status shows OOMKilled</li>
                            <li>Diagnose: <code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Last State → OOMKilled</li>
                            <li><strong>Fix:</strong> Increase <code>limits.memory</code> or fix memory leak</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
<pre>
  Node Capacity: 4 CPU, 8Gi Memory
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────── easypj container ─────────────┐                  │
  │  │                                            │                  │
  │  │  CPU:    [====request 250m====|...limit 500m....]             │
  │  │          ├─── guaranteed ────┤├── can burst ──┤               │
  │  │                                            │                  │
  │  │  Memory: [====request 256Mi===|...limit 512Mi...]             │
  │  │          ├─── guaranteed ────┤├── can use ───┤                │
  │  │                                   ↑ exceed = OOMKill 💀      │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────┘                  │
  │                                                                  │
  │  What happens:                                                   │
  │  ├── Actual CPU  = 300m  → ✅ Within limit, no throttle         │
  │  ├── Actual CPU  = 600m  → ⏳ Throttled to 500m                 │
  │  ├── Actual Mem  = 400Mi → ✅ Within limit, fine                │
  │  └── Actual Mem  = 600Mi → 💀 OOMKilled! Container restarts    │
  │                                                                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Never Skip Resource Settings in Production</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>No requests</strong>: Scheduler doesn't know what the pod needs → random placement → resource contention</li>
                        <li><strong>No limits</strong>: Pod can consume ALL available node memory → OOMs other pods → cascading failure</li>
                        <li><strong>Always set both</strong> on every production container!</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Monitoring Resource Usage</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check actual usage vs requests/limits</span>
kubectl top pods
<span class="comment"># NAME      CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj   120m         180Mi</span>

kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       CPU(cores)   CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   850m         42%    3200Mi           40%</span>

<span class="comment"># Find pods with no resource limits (dangerous!)</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[] | 
  select(.spec.containers[].resources.limits == null) | 
  .metadata.name'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Troubleshooting OOMKilled Pods</h4>
                <p>One of the most common issues on the CKA and in production. Here's a systematic approach:</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Find OOMKilled Pods</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pods -A | grep OOMKilled</code> or <code>grep CrashLoopBackOff</code> (often caused by repeated OOM)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Confirm OOM Reason</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe pod NAME</code> → look for <code>Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Check Current Limits</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pod NAME -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'</code> → see current memory limit</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Check Actual Usage</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl top pod NAME</code> → if usage is close to limit, the limit is too low</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Fix &amp; Verify</h5>
                        <p>Increase memory limit: <code>kubectl set resources deployment/NAME --limits=memory=512Mi</code>. Watch pod restart successfully.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">OOMKilled Diagnosis Steps</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find OOMKilled pods</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep OOMKilled
kubectl get pods -A | grep CrashLoopBackOff   <span class="comment"># often caused by OOM</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the pod's last state</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-abc12 | grep -A5 "Last State"
<span class="comment">#   Last State:  Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#     Reason:    OOMKilled</span>
<span class="comment">#     Exit Code: 137       ← 137 = SIGKILL (memory limit hit)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check current resource config</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-abc12 -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'
<span class="comment"># {"limits":{"memory":"256Mi"},"requests":{"memory":"128Mi"}}</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check actual usage (if pod is running)</span>
kubectl top pod easypj-abc12
<span class="comment"># CPU: 100m    MEMORY: 245Mi   ← close to 256Mi limit!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix — increase memory limit</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/easypj -c django \
  --limits=memory=512Mi --requests=memory=256Mi</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">OOMKill Decision Tree — Is It the Container or the Node?</div>
<pre>
  Pod terminated with Exit Code 137?
       │
       ├── YES → OOMKilled!
       │        │
       │   Was the pod using more memory
       │   than its spec.resources.limits.memory?
       │        │
       │   ┌────┴────┐
       │   YES        NO
       │   │          │
       │   Container  Node-level OOM
       │   limit hit  (node ran out of memory)
       │   │          │
       │   Fix:       Fix: add more nodes,
       │   Increase   reduce other workloads,
       │   memory     or fix memory leaks
       │   limits
       │
       └── Exit Code 1 = app error (not OOM)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §15.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.2 QoS Classes — Quality of Service</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes automatically assigns a <strong>QoS class</strong> to every pod based on its resource configuration. This determines the <strong>eviction priority</strong> when a node runs out of resources.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>QoS Class</th>
                                <th>Criteria</th>
                                <th>Eviction Priority</th>
                                <th>easypj Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Guaranteed</strong></td>
                                <td>Every container has requests == limits (both CPU &amp; memory set and equal)</td>
                                <td>Last to be evicted (safest) 🛡️</td>
                                <td>✅ Production easypj</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Burstable</strong></td>
                                <td>At least one container has requests &lt; limits (or only requests set)</td>
                                <td>Evicted after BestEffort ⚠️</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Dev/staging easypj</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>BestEffort</strong></td>
                                <td>No requests or limits set on any container</td>
                                <td>First to be evicted (riskiest) 💀</td>
                                <td>❌ Never in production</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">QoS Eviction Order — Node Under Memory Pressure</div>
<pre>
  Node memory: 95% used — kubelet starts evicting pods!

  Eviction Order:
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                   │
  │  💀 FIRST: BestEffort pods                       │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ Pod: test-pod (no requests, no limits)      │ │
  │  │ → EVICTED immediately                       │ │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │          │                                        │
  │          ▼ (still need more memory?)              │
  │  ⚠️ SECOND: Burstable pods                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ Pod: dev-easypj (requests &lt; limits)         │ │
  │  │ → EVICTED if using more than requests       │ │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │          │                                        │
  │          ▼ (extreme pressure only)                │
  │  🛡️ LAST: Guaranteed pods                       │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ Pod: prod-easypj (requests == limits)       │ │
  │  │ → Only evicted as absolute last resort      │ │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">QoS Examples</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Guaranteed QoS — requests EQUAL limits</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"          <span class="comment"># same as request</span>
    memory: "512Mi"      <span class="comment"># same as request</span>
<span class="comment"># → QoS: Guaranteed ✅</span>

---
<span class="comment"># Burstable QoS — requests LESS THAN limits</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "250m"
    memory: "256Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"          <span class="comment"># more than request</span>
    memory: "512Mi"      <span class="comment"># more than request</span>
<span class="comment"># → QoS: Burstable ⚠️</span>

---
<span class="comment"># BestEffort QoS — NO resources at all</span>
<span class="comment"># (just don't include the resources section)</span>
<span class="comment"># → QoS: BestEffort 💀</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Check Pod QoS Class</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check QoS class of a pod</span>
kubectl get pod easypj -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'
<span class="comment"># Guaranteed</span>

<span class="comment"># Check QoS of all pods</span>
kubectl get pods -o custom-columns='NAME:.metadata.name,QOS:.status.qosClass'
<span class="comment"># NAME              QOS</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-abc12      Guaranteed</span>
<span class="comment"># dev-app-xyz       Burstable</span>
<span class="comment"># test-pod          BestEffort</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Production QoS Strategy for easypj</h5>
                    <p>For your production stack, aim for these QoS classes:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Guaranteed</strong>: easypj Django (production), PostgreSQL — set requests == limits</li>
                        <li><strong>Burstable</strong>: easypj staging, Redis cache — allow bursting for efficiency</li>
                        <li><strong>BestEffort</strong>: Only for throwaway test pods in dev. Never in production!</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Pro tip: Use <code>Guaranteed</code> for anything facing users. Use <code>Burstable</code> for internal services that benefit from occasional burst capacity.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §15.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.3 LimitRange — Namespace Default Limits</h3>
                <p>A LimitRange sets <strong>default</strong> resource requests and limits for containers in a namespace. It also enforces min/max boundaries. This prevents pods from running without any resource configuration.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">limitrange.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-limits
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  limits:
  - type: Container
    default:                     <span class="comment"># Default LIMITS (if not specified)</span>
      cpu: "500m"
      memory: "512Mi"
    defaultRequest:              <span class="comment"># Default REQUESTS (if not specified)</span>
      cpu: "250m"
      memory: "256Mi"
    max:                         <span class="comment"># Maximum allowed</span>
      cpu: "2"
      memory: "2Gi"
    min:                         <span class="comment"># Minimum allowed</span>
      cpu: "50m"
      memory: "32Mi"
  - type: Pod
    max:
      cpu: "4"
      memory: "4Gi"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">LimitRange in Action</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create namespace and LimitRange</span>
kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl apply -f limitrange.yaml

<span class="comment"># Create pod WITHOUT specifying resources</span>
kubectl run test-pod --image=nginx -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># Check — LimitRange injected default values!</span>
kubectl get pod test-pod -n easypj-dev -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'
<span class="comment"># {"limits":{"cpu":"500m","memory":"512Mi"},"requests":{"cpu":"250m","memory":"256Mi"}}</span>

<span class="comment"># Try to create pod exceeding max</span>
<span class="comment"># → Error: maximum cpu usage per Container is 2, but limit is 4</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">LimitRange — Automatic Defaults</div>
<pre>
  Namespace: easypj-dev (with LimitRange)

  Pod created WITHOUT resources:        Pod received DEFAULTS:
  ┌──────────────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────────────────┐
  │ spec:                    │   →     │ spec:                        │
  │   containers:            │   →     │   containers:                │
  │   - name: nginx          │   →     │   - name: nginx              │
  │     image: nginx         │   →     │     image: nginx             │
  │     <span class="comment"># no resources!      │   →     │     resources:</span>               │
  │                          │   →     │       requests:              │
  │                          │   →     │         cpu: "250m"    ← injected! │
  │                          │   →     │         memory: "256Mi" ← injected!│
  │                          │   →     │       limits:               │
  │                          │   →     │         cpu: "500m"    ← injected! │
  │                          │   →     │         memory: "512Mi" ← injected!│
  └──────────────────────────┘         └──────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §15.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s15-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">15.4 ResourceQuota — Namespace Capacity Control</h3>
                <p>A ResourceQuota limits the <strong>total resources</strong> consumed across all pods in a namespace. This prevents one team from using all cluster resources and is a key tool for multi-tenant clusters.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">resourcequota.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  hard:
    <span class="comment"># Compute resources</span>
    requests.cpu: "4"            <span class="comment"># Total CPU requests across all pods</span>
    requests.memory: "8Gi"       <span class="comment"># Total memory requests</span>
    limits.cpu: "8"              <span class="comment"># Total CPU limits</span>
    limits.memory: "16Gi"        <span class="comment"># Total memory limits</span>

    <span class="comment"># Object count limits</span>
    pods: "10"                   <span class="comment"># Max 10 pods in this namespace</span>
    services: "5"                <span class="comment"># Max 5 services</span>
    persistentvolumeclaims: "5"  <span class="comment"># Max 5 PVCs</span>
    configmaps: "20"
    secrets: "20"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">ResourceQuota Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply quota</span>
kubectl apply -f resourcequota.yaml

<span class="comment"># Check quota usage</span>
kubectl get resourcequota -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME           AGE   REQUEST                                              LIMIT</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-quota   5m    requests.cpu: 750m/4, requests.memory: 768Mi/8Gi    limits.cpu: 1500m/8, limits.memory: 1536Mi/16Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Detailed view</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota easypj-quota -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># Name:                   easypj-quota</span>
<span class="comment"># Namespace:              easypj-dev</span>
<span class="comment"># Resource                Used    Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># --------                ----    ----</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.cpu              1500m   8</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.memory           1536Mi  16Gi</span>
<span class="comment"># pods                    3       10</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu            750m    4</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.memory         768Mi   8Gi</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Resource Control Hierarchy — 3 Levels</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  LEVEL 1: ResourceQuota (namespace total budget)               │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ "easypj-dev can use max 4 CPUs, 8Gi memory total"       │  │
  │  │  Used: 750m / 4 CPU    Used: 768Mi / 8Gi memory         │  │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │       │                                                        │
  │       ▼                                                        │
  │  LEVEL 2: LimitRange (per-container defaults &amp; bounds)       │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │ "Each container: default 250m req, 500m limit"           │  │
  │  │ "Min: 50m CPU, Max: 2 CPU per container"                 │  │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  │       │                                                        │
  │       ▼                                                        │
  │  LEVEL 3: Pod spec (individual container settings)            │
  │  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐              │
  │  │ easypj-1   │  │ easypj-2   │  │ easypj-3   │              │
  │  │ req: 250m  │  │ req: 250m  │  │ req: 250m  │              │
  │  │ lim: 500m  │  │ lim: 500m  │  │ lim: 500m  │              │
  │  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘              │
  │  Total: 750m request, 1500m limit (within quota ✅)           │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ ResourceQuota Requires Resource Specs</h5>
                    <p>When a ResourceQuota is active in a namespace, <strong>every pod must specify resource requests and limits</strong>. Without them, the pod creation will be rejected. Use a LimitRange to auto-inject defaults so developers don't have to specify them manually.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <div class="scenario-header">
                        <span class="scenario-icon">🔥</span>
                        <h4>Scenario: "Pod Creation Rejected — Forbidden"</h4>
                        <span class="badge badge-exam">CKA EXAM</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-content">
                        <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> <code>kubectl run test --image=nginx -n dev</code> → <em>Error: forbidden: failed quota: dev-quota: must specify limits.cpu,limits.memory</em></p>
                        <div class="scenario-steps">
                            <div class="scenario-step">
                                <span class="step-label">Step 1 — Check quota</span>
                                <p><code>kubectl describe resourcequota -n dev</code> → quota exists and enforces limits. No LimitRange present to auto-inject defaults.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="scenario-step">
                                <span class="step-label">Step 2 — Two fixes</span>
                                <p><strong>Option A:</strong> Add resources to pod spec. <strong>Option B:</strong> Create a LimitRange with <code>default</code> and <code>defaultRequest</code> so future pods get automatic defaults.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="scenario-step">
                                <span class="step-label">Step 3 — Verify</span>
                                <p>Re-run <code>kubectl run test --image=nginx -n dev</code> → pod created. <code>kubectl describe resourcequota -n dev</code> → Used values increased.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-outcome">
                            <strong>Lesson:</strong> When ResourceQuota exists, always pair it with a LimitRange. This is a classic CKA trap — the exam expects you to know both work together.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How CPU and Memory Limits Are Actually Enforced by the Linux Kernel: CFS Quotas, Cgroups v2, OOM Scoring, and QoS Classes</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Resource Enforcement — From Kubernetes YAML to Kernel Mechanisms</h5>
            <p>Kubernetes resource limits aren't just suggestions — they're enforced by the Linux kernel through cgroups. Understanding the exact mechanism helps you set correct limits and debug OOMKilled pods.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUBERNETES RESOURCES → LINUX CGROUPS MAPPING                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Pod YAML:                           Kernel Cgroup (cgroups v2):             │
│  ┌────────────────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ resources:                 │      │ /sys/fs/cgroup/kubepods/         │   │
│  │   requests:                │      │   burstable/                     │   │
│  │     cpu: 250m              │─────▶│   pod&lt;uid&gt;/                      │   │
│  │     memory: 256Mi          │      │     cpu.weight: ~25              │   │
│  │                            │      │     memory.min: 256Mi             │   │
│  │   limits:                  │      │                                   │   │
│  │     cpu: 500m              │─────▶│     cpu.max: 50000 100000        │   │
│  │     memory: 512Mi          │      │     memory.max: 512Mi             │   │
│  └────────────────────────────┘      └──────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CPU LIMIT ENFORCEMENT (CFS — Completely Fair Scheduler):                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  Linux CFS uses QUOTA and PERIOD:                                    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │   limits.cpu: 500m → 0.5 CPU cores                                  │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │   CFS settings (kernel):                                             │    │
│  │     cpu.cfs_period_us  = 100000  (100ms — the accounting period)    │    │
│  │     cpu.cfs_quota_us   = 50000   (50ms — max CPU time per period)   │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │   RESULT: Container gets at MOST 50ms of CPU every 100ms.            │    │
│  │           This equals 0.5 cores = 500 millicores.                     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  WHAT HAPPENS WHEN CPU LIMIT IS HIT:                                 │    │
│  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│  │  │ CPU is THROTTLED (not killed):                               │    │    │
│  │  │ • Process is paused by CFS scheduler                         │    │    │
│  │  │ • Resumes in next accounting period                          │    │    │
│  │  │ • Observable as: container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total   │    │    │
│  │  │ • Effect: increased latency, NOT pod termination             │    │    │
│  │  │                                                               │    │    │
│  │  │ $ kubectl top pod easypj-web-xxx                              │    │    │
│  │  │ NAME               CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)                 │    │    │
│  │  │ easypj-web-xxx     498m          489Mi  <span class="comment">← Near limit!        </span>│    │    │
│  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  MEMORY LIMIT ENFORCEMENT (OOM Killer):                                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  limits.memory: 512Mi                                                │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  Cgroup setting: memory.max = 536870912 bytes (512Mi)                │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  WHAT HAPPENS WHEN MEMORY LIMIT IS EXCEEDED:                        │    │
│  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│  │  │ 1. Container tries to allocate more than 512Mi               │    │    │
│  │  │ 2. Kernel memory allocator returns -ENOMEM                   │    │    │
│  │  │ 3. OOM Killer invoked (cgroup-level, not host-level)         │    │    │
│  │  │ 4. Process with highest oom_score in cgroup is KILLED        │    │    │
│  │  │ 5. Container exits with code 137 (128 + SIGKILL)             │    │    │
│  │  │ 6. kubelet detects container death → restarts (if RestartPolicy allows)│    │    │
│  │  │                                                               │    │    │
│  │  │ $ kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxx                         │    │    │
│  │  │ Last State:   Terminated                                      │    │    │
│  │  │   Reason:     OOMKilled                                        │    │    │
│  │  │   Exit Code:  137                                              │    │    │
│  │  │   Started:    Mon, 02 Jun 2026 10:00:00 +0000                 │    │    │
│  │  │   Finished:   Mon, 02 Jun 2026 10:05:23 +0000                 │    │    │
│  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  QOS CLASSES — How Kubernetes Prioritizes Which Pod to Kill First:          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  ┌──────────────────┬─────────────────┬──────────────────────────┐ │    │
│  │  │ QoS CLASS        │ CONDITION        │ OOM KILL ORDER           │ │    │
│  │  ├──────────────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤ │    │
│  │  │ Guaranteed       │ requests ==       │ KILLED LAST (safest)     │ │    │
│  │  │                  │ limits for BOTH   │                          │ │    │
│  │  │                  │ cpu AND memory    │                          │ │    │
│  │  ├──────────────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤ │    │
│  │  │ Burstable        │ At least one      │ KILLED MIDDLE            │ │    │
│  │  │                  │ container has      │                          │ │    │
│  │  │                  │ request < limit   │                          │ │    │
│  │  ├──────────────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤ │    │
│  │  │ BestEffort       │ No requests or    │ KILLED FIRST (riskiest)  │ │    │
│  │  │                  │ limits set        │                          │ │    │
│  │  └──────────────────┴─────────────────┴──────────────────────────┘ │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  CKA RULE: On the exam, always set requests=limits for critical      │    │
│  │  pods (Guaranteed QoS). On a resource-starved node, BestEffort pods  │    │
│  │  die first when the OOM killer runs.                                 │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The CKA frequently tests finding and fixing OOMKilled pods. The diagnostic flow: (1) <code>kubectl get pods</code> → look for RESTARTS column > 0, (2) <code>kubectl describe pod</code> → check Last State for OOMKilled, (3) <code>kubectl top pod</code> → check actual memory usage, (4) Fix: increase <code>resources.limits.memory</code> or reduce app memory usage. Exit code 137 ALWAYS means OOMKilled (128 + 9 SIGKILL).
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Requests vs Limits</strong> — <code>requests</code> = guaranteed minimum (used for scheduling). <code>limits</code> = maximum allowed (enforced at runtime). Scheduler uses requests, NOT limits</li>
                    <li><strong>CPU units</strong> — <code>1</code> = 1 vCPU. <code>500m</code> = 0.5 CPU. <code>100m</code> = 0.1 CPU. The exam loves asking you to convert between these</li>
                    <li><strong>Memory units</strong> — <code>Mi</code> (mebibytes) ≠ <code>M</code> (megabytes). Use <code>Mi</code> for consistency. <code>128Mi</code> = 134MB</li>
                    <li><strong>QoS classes</strong> — <code>Guaranteed</code> (requests=limits for all containers), <code>Burstable</code> (some requests set), <code>BestEffort</code> (no requests/limits). OOM killer targets BestEffort first</li>
                    <li><strong>LimitRange applies to NEW pods only</strong> — Existing pods aren't affected. ResourceQuota blocks creation when the namespace total is exceeded</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip:</strong> Find which pods are hogging resources:<br>
                <code>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu</code> — highest CPU consumers<br>
                <code>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=memory</code> — highest memory consumers<br>
                <code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt; | grep -A5 "Allocated resources"</code> — node-level usage summary</p>
            </div>

            

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 15)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Command</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Check pod resource usage</td><td><code>kubectl top pods</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check node resource usage</td><td><code>kubectl top nodes</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View pod QoS class</td><td><code>kubectl get pod NAME -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View LimitRange</td><td><code>kubectl describe limitrange -n NAMESPACE</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View ResourceQuota</td><td><code>kubectl describe resourcequota -n NAMESPACE</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check OOMKilled pods</td><td><code>kubectl get pods | grep OOMKilled</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View container resources</td><td><code>kubectl get pod NAME -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].resources}'</code></td></tr>
                                            <tr><td>Set container resource requests/limits</td><td><code>kubectl set resources deployment/web -c=nginx --requests=cpu=100m,memory=128Mi --limits=cpu=500m,memory=256Mi</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create a ResourceQuota</td><td><code>kubectl create quota my-quota --hard=pods=10,requests.cpu=4,requests.memory=8Gi -n dev</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>see used vs hard limits</td><td><code>kubectl describe resourcequota -n dev</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-drill"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧪 Drill</h4>
                <p>A hands-on practice drill goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 15 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch15-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 15 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of requests, limits &amp; QoS — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set a container's CPU request to 250m and memory limit to 512Mi. What do <code>m</code> and <code>Mi</code> mean, and what happens if the container exceeds its memory limit?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod with resource requests and limits</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod with resource requests and limits:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: resource-pod
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "250m"        <span class="comment"># 250 millicores = 0.25 CPU core</span>
    memory: "256Mi"    <span class="comment"># 256 Mebibytes</span>
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"        <span class="comment"># 500 millicores = 0.5 CPU core</span>
    memory: "512Mi"    <span class="comment"># 512 Mebibytes</span>

<span class="comment"># CPU units:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1 CPU = 1000m (millicores)</span>
<span class="comment"># 250m = 0.25 = 25% of one core</span>
<span class="comment"># 100m = 0.1 = 10% of one core</span>

<span class="comment"># Memory units:</span>
<span class="comment"># Mi = Mebibytes (1024 * 1024 bytes) — binary</span>
<span class="comment"># M  = Megabytes (1000 * 1000 bytes) — decimal</span>
<span class="comment"># Gi = Gibibytes, G = Gigabytes</span>

<span class="comment"># What happens when limits are exceeded:</span>
<span class="comment"># CPU:    Container is THROTTLED (slowed down, NOT killed)</span>
<span class="comment"># Memory: Container is OOM-KILLED (terminated, CrashLoopBackOff)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CPU is compressible — when a container hits its CPU limit, it's throttled (slowed) but continues running. Memory is incompressible — when a container exceeds its memory limit, the kernel OOM-killer terminates it. The pod restarts and eventually enters <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> if it keeps exceeding the limit. On the CKA, always use <code>Mi</code> (mebibytes) not <code>M</code> (megabytes) — they're subtly different. <code>250m</code> CPU = quarter core. The <code>requests</code> field determines scheduling (scheduler finds a node with enough free resources), while <code>limits</code> enforce runtime caps.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain the three QoS (Quality of Service) classes in Kubernetes. How is the QoS class determined, and which class gets OOM-killed first?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>QoS Class</th>
                                        <th>Condition</th>
                                        <th>OOM Kill Priority</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="color:#4ade80;"><strong>Guaranteed</strong></td>
                                        <td>All containers have requests = limits (both CPU &amp; memory)</td>
                                        <td>Last to be killed (highest priority)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="color:#facc15;"><strong>Burstable</strong></td>
                                        <td>At least one container has a request or limit set</td>
                                        <td>Killed after BestEffort</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="color:#f87171;"><strong>BestEffort</strong></td>
                                        <td>No requests or limits set on any container</td>
                                        <td>First to be killed (lowest priority)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check a pod's qos class</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check a pod's QoS class</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o jsonpath='{.status.qosClass}'
<span class="comment"># Guaranteed</span>

<span class="comment"># Guaranteed example — requests MUST equal limits:</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "256Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"       <span class="comment"># Same as request</span>
    memory: "256Mi"   <span class="comment"># Same as request</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>QoS classes determine which pods are killed first when a node runs out of memory. <code>BestEffort</code> pods are evicted first (no resource guarantees), <code>Burstable</code> pods are next, and <code>Guaranteed</code> pods are last. On the CKA, if a question asks "how to ensure a critical pod is the last to be evicted," the answer is QoS class <code>Guaranteed</code> (set requests = limits for ALL containers in the pod). The QoS class is automatically determined by Kubernetes — you can't set it manually.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a <code>LimitRange</code> in namespace <code>dev</code> that sets default CPU request to 100m, default CPU limit to 500m, and prevents any container from requesting more than 2 CPU.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
name: cpu-limits
namespace: dev
spec:
limits:
- type: Container
default:          <span class="comment"># Default LIMITS (applied if not specified)</span>
  cpu: "500m"
defaultRequest:   <span class="comment"># Default REQUESTS (applied if not specified)</span>
  cpu: "100m"
max:              <span class="comment"># Maximum allowed</span>
  cpu: "2"
min:              <span class="comment"># Minimum allowed</span>
  cpu: "50m"

kubectl apply -f limitrange.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl describe limitrange cpu-limits -n dev
<span class="comment"># Type      Resource  Min   Max   Default  Default Request</span>
<span class="comment"># Container cpu       50m   2     500m     100m</span>

<span class="comment"># Now any pod in namespace 'dev' without CPU specs</span>
<span class="comment"># automatically gets: requests.cpu=100m, limits.cpu=500m</span>
<span class="comment"># Any pod requesting more than 2 CPU is REJECTED</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>LimitRange</code> operates at the namespace level and applies to INDIVIDUAL pods/containers. <code>default</code> sets the default limit, <code>defaultRequest</code> sets the default request. <code>max</code> and <code>min</code> enforce hard boundaries — pods that violate them are rejected at admission. On the CKA, LimitRange prevents developers from deploying pods without resource constraints. Confusable with <code>ResourceQuota</code> — LimitRange = per-pod/container defaults and constraints, ResourceQuota = total namespace-wide limits.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a <code>ResourceQuota</code> in namespace <code>production</code> that limits the namespace to maximum 10 pods, 4 CPU cores total, and 8Gi total memory for requests.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative (fastest on cka)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative (fastest on CKA):</span>
kubectl create quota prod-quota \
--hard=pods=10,requests.cpu=4,requests.memory=8Gi \
-n production

<span class="comment"># Equivalent YAML:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
name: prod-quota
namespace: production
spec:
hard:
pods: "10"
requests.cpu: "4"
requests.memory: "8Gi"
limits.cpu: "8"
limits.memory: "16Gi"

<span class="comment"># Check current usage vs limits</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota prod-quota -n production
<span class="comment"># Resource          Used   Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># --------          ----   ----</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.cpu        2500m  8</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.memory     4Gi    16Gi</span>
<span class="comment"># pods              6      10</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu      1200m  4</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.memory   3Gi    8Gi</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>ResourceQuota</code> limits the TOTAL resources consumed by ALL pods in a namespace. Once a quota is set, every new pod MUST specify <code>resources.requests</code> — otherwise creation fails. This is why <code>LimitRange</code> and <code>ResourceQuota</code> are often used together: LimitRange provides defaults so pods without explicit resources still work under the quota. On the CKA, <code>kubectl create quota</code> is the fastest imperative command. <code>kubectl describe resourcequota</code> shows the Used vs Hard columns — essential for troubleshooting "forbidden: exceeded quota" errors.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is being OOM-killed repeatedly. <code>kubectl describe pod</code> shows <code>Last State: Terminated — Reason: OOMKilled</code>. How do you diagnose and fix this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check current memory limits</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check current memory limits</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -o jsonpath=\
'{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}'
<span class="comment"># 128Mi  ← Too small!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check actual memory usage (if pod is running)</span>
kubectl top pod oom-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME      CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># oom-pod   50m         245Mi   ← Using 245Mi but limit is 128Mi!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Increase the memory limit</span>
<span class="comment"># For a Deployment:</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=app \
--limits=memory=512Mi --requests=memory=256Mi

<span class="comment"># For a standalone pod (must delete and recreate):</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -o yaml > pod.yaml
<span class="comment"># Edit pod.yaml: change limits.memory to 512Mi</span>
kubectl delete pod oom-pod
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify pod is stable</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -w
<span class="comment"># Watch for a few minutes — no more restarts</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>OOMKilled means the container exceeded its memory limit and was killed by the Linux kernel's Out-Of-Memory killer. The fix is almost always: increase <code>limits.memory</code>. But first check <code>kubectl top pod</code> to see actual usage — if a pod is using gigabytes of memory, increasing the limit is just a band-aid; the app may have a memory leak. On the CKA, OOMKilled troubleshooting is: (1) describe pod → see OOMKilled in Last State, (2) top pod → see actual usage, (3) increase limit or fix the application. For Deployments, <code>kubectl set resources</code> triggers a new rollout.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>requests</code> and <code>limits</code>? What happens if you set limits but not requests?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">requests = guaranteed minimum resources</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># requests = GUARANTEED minimum resources</span>
<span class="comment"># limits   = MAXIMUM allowed resources</span>

<span class="comment"># Scheduling: scheduler uses REQUESTS to find a node</span>
<span class="comment"># A node with 2 CPU and 4Gi can fit:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Pod A: requests.cpu=500m, requests.memory=1Gi → 1.5 CPU, 3Gi left</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Pod B: requests.cpu=1,    requests.memory=2Gi → 0.5 CPU, 1Gi left</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Pod C: requests.cpu=1 → DOESN'T FIT (only 0.5 CPU left)</span>

<span class="comment"># What if you set limits but NOT requests?</span>
resources:
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "256Mi"
<span class="comment"># requests will be automatically set EQUAL to limits!</span>
<span class="comment"># This creates a Guaranteed QoS pod</span>

<span class="comment"># What if you set requests but NOT limits?</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"
<span class="comment"># No upper bound — container can use all available resources</span>
<span class="comment"># This creates a Burstable QoS pod</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>requests</code> determine scheduling — the scheduler ensures the node has enough free (unrequested) resources. <code>limits</code> enforce runtime caps. When you set limits without requests, Kubernetes automatically sets requests = limits (creating a Guaranteed pod). This is important because: (1) the pod gets exactly what it needs, no more, (2) it's the last to be OOM-killed. On the CKA, understanding this automatic behavior prevents confusion about QoS classes. The total requests on a node can never exceed node capacity, but total limits CAN (overcommit).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod creation fails with: <em>"Error from server (Forbidden): pods "test" is forbidden: exceeded quota: dev-quota, requested: requests.cpu=500m, used: requests.cpu=3800m, limited: requests.cpu=4"</em>. What's wrong and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the quota math</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The quota math:</span>
<span class="comment"># Quota limit:    requests.cpu = 4 (4000m)</span>
<span class="comment"># Currently used: requests.cpu = 3800m</span>
<span class="comment"># Requesting:     requests.cpu = 500m</span>
<span class="comment"># Total would be: 3800m + 500m = 4300m > 4000m limit → DENIED</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 1: Reduce the new pod's CPU request</span>
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "200m"   <span class="comment"># 3800m + 200m = 4000m ← fits exactly</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 2: Delete unused pods to free quota</span>
kubectl get pods -n dev
<span class="comment"># Find idle/unused pods and delete them</span>
kubectl delete pod idle-pod -n dev

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 3: Increase the quota (if authorized)</span>
kubectl edit resourcequota dev-quota -n dev
<span class="comment"># Change hard.requests.cpu to "8"</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current quota usage</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota dev-quota -n dev
<span class="comment"># Resource       Used    Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu   3800m   4</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The error message is very clear: used (3800m) + requested (500m) = 4300m exceeds the hard limit (4000m). On the CKA, quota errors are a common troubleshooting scenario. Read the error carefully — it tells you the exact numbers. Fix by: (1) reducing the pod's request, (2) freeing up quota by deleting other pods, or (3) increasing the quota. <code>kubectl describe resourcequota</code> is your diagnostic tool. Important: quotas track REQUESTS, not actual usage — even if pods are idle, their reserved quota counts against the limit.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use <code>kubectl top</code> to check resource consumption? What prerequisite must be installed?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prerequisite: metrics-server must be installed</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Prerequisite: metrics-server must be installed</span>
<span class="comment"># Check if metrics-server is running:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep metrics-server

<span class="comment"># Check node resource usage</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           CPU(cores)  CPU%  MEMORY(bytes)  MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># controlplane   450m        22%   1200Mi         31%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1       280m        14%   890Mi          23%</span>

<span class="comment"># Check pod resource usage (current namespace)</span>
kubectl top pods
<span class="comment"># NAME        CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-1    120m        256Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># redis       80m         128Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># All namespaces</span>
kubectl top pods -A

<span class="comment"># Sort by CPU (find resource hogs)</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu

<span class="comment"># Sort by memory</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=memory

<span class="comment"># Specific pod containers</span>
kubectl top pod webapp-1 --containers
<span class="comment"># POD       NAME    CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-1  nginx   100m        200Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-1  sidecar 20m         56Mi</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl top</code> requires <code>metrics-server</code> to be installed and running in the cluster. Without it, you get "error: Metrics API not available." On the CKA exam environment, metrics-server is usually pre-installed. <code>kubectl top</code> shows ACTUAL current usage (not requests/limits). Use <code>--sort-by=cpu</code> or <code>--sort-by=memory</code> to quickly find the heaviest consumers. The <code>--containers</code> flag breaks down usage per container in a pod — useful when a sidecar might be the memory hog, not the main container.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>kubectl set resources</code> command? Show how to update a Deployment's container resources without editing YAML.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">set both requests and limits for a specific…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Set both requests and limits for a specific container</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=nginx \
--requests=cpu=200m,memory=256Mi \
--limits=cpu=500m,memory=512Mi

<span class="comment"># Set only limits (requests auto-set to same value)</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=nginx \
--limits=cpu=1,memory=1Gi

<span class="comment"># Remove all resource constraints</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp -c=nginx \
--limits=cpu=0,memory=0 --requests=cpu=0,memory=0

<span class="comment"># This triggers a new rollout (new ReplicaSet)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp

<span class="comment"># Verify the change</span>
kubectl get deployment webapp -o jsonpath=\
'{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}'
<span class="comment"># {"limits":{"cpu":"500m","memory":"512Mi"},</span>
<span class="comment">#  "requests":{"cpu":"200m","memory":"256Mi"}}</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl set resources</code> is the imperative way to modify resource requests/limits without editing YAML. It's much faster than <code>kubectl edit</code> on the CKA exam. The <code>-c</code> flag specifies which container (required for multi-container pods). This command modifies the Deployment's pod template, triggering a rolling update. It's equivalent to editing the YAML and applying it. Combine with <code>rollout pause/resume</code> if you need to make multiple changes in one rollout.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A namespace has a <code>ResourceQuota</code> but no <code>LimitRange</code>. You try to create a pod without specifying resources and get: <em>"Error: failed quota: must specify requests.cpu, requests.memory"</em>. Why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the problem</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The problem:</span>
<span class="comment"># When a ResourceQuota exists that tracks cpu/memory,</span>
<span class="comment"># EVERY pod must specify requests for those resources.</span>
<span class="comment"># Without a LimitRange providing defaults, quota can't</span>
<span class="comment"># calculate how much to charge for the pod → rejected.</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 1: Add resources to the pod spec</span>
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 2: Create a LimitRange for defaults</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
name: default-limits
namespace: dev
spec:
limits:
- type: Container
defaultRequest:
  cpu: "100m"
  memory: "128Mi"
default:
  cpu: "500m"
  memory: "256Mi"

<span class="comment"># Now pods without explicit resources get the defaults</span>
<span class="comment"># and quota can track them properly</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>When a <code>ResourceQuota</code> tracks CPU/memory, Kubernetes MUST know how much each pod "costs" to charge against the quota. Without explicit requests on the pod or a <code>LimitRange</code> providing defaults, the admission controller can't determine the cost → rejects the pod. This is why best practice is: always pair <code>ResourceQuota</code> with a <code>LimitRange</code> in the same namespace. On the CKA, this is a common "gotcha" — the error message tells you to specify resources, but creating a LimitRange with defaults is the scalable fix.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 15 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch16">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 16 · Jobs, CronJobs, DaemonSets &amp; StatefulSets</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Deployments aren't the only workload type. Kubernetes provides specialized controllers for <strong>run-to-completion tasks</strong> (Jobs), <strong>scheduled tasks</strong> (CronJobs), <strong>one-pod-per-node daemons</strong> (DaemonSets), and <strong>stateful applications</strong> (StatefulSets). Mastering when to use each is critical for the CKA exam.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⚙️ Workload Types</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Create Jobs for one-off tasks (database migrations, batch processing)</li>
                    <li>✅ Schedule recurring tasks with CronJobs (backups, reports)</li>
                    <li>✅ Deploy DaemonSets for per-node system services (logging, monitoring)</li>
                    <li>✅ Understand StatefulSets for databases and stateful applications</li>
                    <li>✅ Choose the right workload type for any scenario</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Workload Type Questions on the CKA</h5>
                <p>The CKA often asks you to create Jobs and CronJobs. Know these imperative shortcuts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl create job NAME --image=IMG -- COMMAND</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl create cronjob NAME --image=IMG --schedule="*/5 * * * *" -- COMMAND</code></li>
                    <li>DaemonSets require YAML (no imperative shortcut)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §16.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.1 Jobs — Run to Completion</h3>
                <p>A Job creates one or more pods and ensures they <strong>run to successful completion</strong>. Unlike Deployments (which run forever), Jobs stop once the task is done. Perfect for batch processing, database migrations, and one-off scripts.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Imperative Job Creation</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a simple job</span>
kubectl create job db-migrate --image=easypj:v1 -- python manage.py migrate

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML first (exam tip: always verify before applying)</span>
kubectl create job db-migrate --image=easypj:v1 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml -- python manage.py migrate > job.yaml

<span class="comment"># Check job status</span>
kubectl get jobs
<span class="comment"># NAME         COMPLETIONS   DURATION   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># db-migrate   1/1           12s        30s</span>

<span class="comment"># View job pods</span>
kubectl get pods --selector=job-name=db-migrate
<span class="comment"># NAME               READY   STATUS      RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># db-migrate-abc12   0/1     Completed   0          45s</span>

<span class="comment"># Check logs</span>
kubectl logs job/db-migrate</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-migrate-job.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-db-migrate
spec:
  completions: 1              <span class="comment"># How many pods must complete successfully</span>
  parallelism: 1              <span class="comment"># How many pods run simultaneously</span>
  backoffLimit: 3             <span class="comment"># Retry up to 3 times before marking failed</span>
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 300  <span class="comment"># Timeout: kill after 5 minutes</span>
  ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 60 <span class="comment"># Auto-delete finished job after 60s</span>
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never    <span class="comment"># Jobs: Never or OnFailure (NOT Always!)</span>
      containers:
      - name: migrate
        image: easypj:v1
        command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate"]
        env:
        - name: DATABASE_URL
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: easypj-db-secret
              key: url</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Job Configuration Fields</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">✅</span> completions</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How many pods must succeed. Default: 1. Set to N for batch: process N items.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚡</span> parallelism</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How many pods run at once. Default: 1. Increase for faster batch processing.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> backoffLimit</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Retry count before job is marked Failed. Default: 6. Set lower for fast failure.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⏱️</span> activeDeadlineSeconds</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Hard timeout. Job is killed if it runs longer, regardless of retries.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗑️</span> ttlSecondsAfterFinished</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Auto-cleanup. Completed/failed job is deleted after this many seconds.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔁</span> restartPolicy</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Never</strong> (creates new pod on failure) or <strong>OnFailure</strong> (restarts container in same pod). Never <code>Always</code>!</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Job Lifecycle — Run, Complete, Stop</div>
<pre>
  Job: easypj-db-migrate (completions: 1, backoffLimit: 3)

  Attempt 1:
  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Pod: migrate │ → Runs python manage.py migrate
  │   🔄 Running │ → ...
  │   ✅ Success │ → Exit code 0
  └──────────────┘
  Job Status: Complete ✅  (1/1 completions)

  ─── What if it fails? ───

  Attempt 1:
  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Pod: migrate │ → DB connection refused
  │   ❌ Failed  │ → Exit code 1
  └──────────────┘
  Attempt 2:  (backoff: 10s wait)
  ┌──────────────┐
  │ Pod: migrate │ → DB now available
  │   ✅ Success │ → Exit code 0
  └──────────────┘
  Job Status: Complete ✅  (1/1 completions, 1 retry)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Trap: restartPolicy</h5>
                    <p>Jobs CANNOT use <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> (that's for Deployments). If you forget this, the Job will be rejected. Use <code>Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Batch Processing with Parallelism</h4>
                <p>Jobs can process multiple items in parallel. Set <code>completions</code> for how many must finish and <code>parallelism</code> for how many run at once.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">batch-processing-job.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-image-processor
spec:
  completions: 10       <span class="comment"># 10 total items to process</span>
  parallelism: 3        <span class="comment"># Run 3 pods at a time</span>
  backoffLimit: 5
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
      - name: processor
        image: easypj:v1
        command: ["python", "manage.py", "process_images"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Batch Job: 10 Completions, 3 Parallel</div>
<pre>
  Job: easypj-image-processor (completions: 10, parallelism: 3)

  Wave 1: [📦 Pod-1] [📦 Pod-2] [📦 Pod-3]   ← 3 running
               ✅         ✅         ✅
  Wave 2: [📦 Pod-4] [📦 Pod-5] [📦 Pod-6]   ← 3 running
               ✅         ✅         ✅
  Wave 3: [📦 Pod-7] [📦 Pod-8] [📦 Pod-9]   ← 3 running
               ✅         ✅         ✅
  Wave 4: [📦 Pod-10]                        ← 1 running (last item)
               ✅
  Job Status: Complete ✅  (10/10 completions)

  Total time: ~4 waves instead of 10 sequential runs!
  Parallelism gives ~3x speedup ⚡
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §16.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.2 CronJobs — Scheduled Tasks</h3>
                <p>CronJobs are like Unix <code>cron</code> but in Kubernetes. They create Jobs on a schedule — perfect for backups, reports, cleanup tasks, and periodic data processing.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Imperative CronJob Creation</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a CronJob — daily backup at 2 AM</span>
kubectl create cronjob easypj-backup \
  --image=easypj:v1 \
  --schedule="0 2 * * *" \
  -- python manage.py dumpdata --output=/backup/db.json

<span class="comment"># Check CronJob</span>
kubectl get cronjobs
<span class="comment"># NAME             SCHEDULE      SUSPEND   ACTIVE   LAST SCHEDULE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-backup    0 2 * * *     False     0        30s             5m</span>

<span class="comment"># View recently created Jobs</span>
kubectl get jobs --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-backup-cronjob.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: easypj-backup
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"          <span class="comment"># Daily at 2:00 AM</span>
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid       <span class="comment"># Don't start new if previous still running</span>
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3   <span class="comment"># Keep last 3 successful job records</span>
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1       <span class="comment"># Keep last 1 failed job record</span>
  startingDeadlineSeconds: 200    <span class="comment"># If job can't start in 200s, skip this run</span>
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      backoffLimit: 2
      template:
        spec:
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          containers:
          - name: backup
            image: easypj:v1
            command:
            - /bin/sh
            - -c
            - "python manage.py dumpdata | gzip > /backup/$(date +%Y%m%d).json.gz"
            volumeMounts:
            - name: backup-volume
              mountPath: /backup
          volumes:
          - name: backup-volume
            persistentVolumeClaim:
              claimName: backup-pvc</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Schedule</th>
                                <th>Meaning</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>*/5 * * * *</code></td><td>Every 5 minutes</td><td>Health checks, cache refresh</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>0 2 * * *</code></td><td>Daily at 2:00 AM</td><td>Database backup</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>0 0 * * 0</code></td><td>Every Sunday at midnight</td><td>Weekly report generation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>0 */6 * * *</code></td><td>Every 6 hours</td><td>Data sync, cleanup</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>30 8 1 * *</code></td><td>1st of each month at 8:30 AM</td><td>Monthly invoice generation</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Cron Expression Format</div>
<pre>
  ┌───────────── minute (0-59)
  │ ┌───────────── hour (0-23)
  │ │ ┌───────────── day of month (1-31)
  │ │ │ ┌───────────── month (1-12)
  │ │ │ │ ┌───────────── day of week (0-6, Sun=0)
  │ │ │ │ │
  * * * * *

  Examples:
  0 2 * * *     = "At 02:00 every day"
  */5 * * * *   = "Every 5 minutes"
  0 0 * * 0     = "At midnight every Sunday"
  30 8 1 * *    = "At 08:30 on the 1st of every month"
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>concurrencyPolicy: Forbid</h5>
                        <p>Don't start new job if previous is still running. Prevents overlap. Safest for DB backups.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>concurrencyPolicy: Replace</h5>
                        <p>Kill running job and start a new one. Good for "latest data only" scenarios.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Third Option: concurrencyPolicy: Allow (default)</h5>
                    <p><code>Allow</code> lets multiple jobs run simultaneously. If CronJob fires every 5 minutes but a job takes 7 minutes, you'll have overlapping runs. This is fine for independent tasks (e.g., sending reports) but <strong>dangerous for database operations</strong> where concurrent writes could corrupt data. Always set <code>Forbid</code> for DB backups.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §16.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.3 DaemonSets — One Pod Per Node</h3>
                <p>A DaemonSet ensures that <strong>exactly one copy of a pod runs on every node</strong> (or every selected node). When a new node joins the cluster, the DaemonSet automatically schedules a pod there. When a node is removed, the pod is garbage-collected.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Common DaemonSet Use Cases</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>📋 <strong>Log collectors</strong> — Fluentd, Filebeat, Promtail</li>
                        <li>📊 <strong>Monitoring agents</strong> — Node Exporter, Datadog Agent</li>
                        <li>🌐 <strong>Network plugins</strong> — Calico, Cilium, Flannel (CNI)</li>
                        <li>💾 <strong>Storage daemons</strong> — Ceph, GlusterFS</li>
                        <li>🔐 <strong>Security agents</strong> — Falco, Twistlock</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">fluentd-daemonset.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: fluentd
  namespace: kube-system
  labels:
    app: fluentd
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: fluentd
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: fluentd
    spec:
      tolerations:
      <span class="comment"># Tolerate control plane taint to run on ALL nodes</span>
      - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
        operator: Exists
        effect: NoSchedule
      containers:
      - name: fluentd
        image: fluentd:v1.16
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "200Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "200m"
            memory: "400Mi"
        volumeMounts:
        - name: varlog
          mountPath: /var/log
        - name: varlibdockercontainers
          mountPath: /var/lib/docker/containers
          readOnly: true
      volumes:
      - name: varlog
        hostPath:
          path: /var/log
      - name: varlibdockercontainers
        hostPath:
          path: /var/lib/docker/containers</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">DaemonSet — Automatic Pod Per Node</div>
<pre>
  DaemonSet: "fluentd"   (replicas: N/A — auto-managed)

  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐
  │    worker-1      │  │    worker-2      │  │    worker-3      │
  │  ┌────────────┐  │  │  ┌────────────┐  │  │  ┌────────────┐  │
  │  │  fluentd   │  │  │  │  fluentd   │  │  │  │  fluentd   │  │
  │  │  📋 logs   │  │  │  │  📋 logs   │  │  │  │  📋 logs   │  │
  │  └────────────┘  │  │  └────────────┘  │  │  └────────────┘  │
  │  + easypj pods   │  │  + easypj pods   │  │  + easypj pods   │
  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘

  ┌──────────────────┐
  │    worker-4      │  ← NEW node joins cluster!
  │  ┌────────────┐  │
  │  │  fluentd   │  │  DaemonSet controller AUTOMATICALLY
  │  │  📋 logs   │  │  schedules a pod here! 🔄
  │  └────────────┘  │
  └──────────────────┘

  ✅ Every node always has exactly 1 fluentd pod
  ✅ New nodes get a pod automatically
  ✅ Removed nodes — pod is cleaned up automatically
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">DaemonSet Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View DaemonSets</span>
kubectl get daemonsets -A
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE     NAME         DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   UP-TO-DATE   NODE SELECTOR   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system   fluentd      4         4         4       4            &lt;none&gt;          10d</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system   kube-proxy   4         4         4       4            &lt;none&gt;          30d</span>

<span class="comment"># DaemonSet on specific nodes only (using nodeSelector)</span>
<span class="comment"># Add nodeSelector to template.spec to limit which nodes get the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Check which nodes have DaemonSet pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l app=fluentd -o wide</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Tip: No Imperative Command for DaemonSets</h5>
                    <p>Unlike Deployments and Jobs, there's no <code>kubectl create daemonset</code> command. You must write YAML. Quick trick: generate a Deployment YAML, then change <code>kind: Deployment</code> to <code>kind: DaemonSet</code> and remove the <code>replicas</code> field.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>DaemonSet Update Strategy</h4>
                <p>DaemonSets support two update strategies, similar to Deployments:</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Strategy</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>RollingUpdate</strong> (default)</td>
                                <td>Pods updated node by node — old pod killed, new pod started on each node sequentially</td>
                                <td>Most updates — zero gap per node</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>OnDelete</strong></td>
                                <td>Pods only updated when you manually delete them — full control over rollout pace</td>
                                <td>Critical system daemons where you want to control timing (e.g., CNI plugin)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">DaemonSet Update Strategy YAML</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># In the DaemonSet spec:</span>
spec:
  updateStrategy:
    type: RollingUpdate        <span class="comment"># or OnDelete</span>
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 1        <span class="comment"># Update 1 node at a time (default)</span>
      <span class="comment"># maxSurge: 0            # Not supported for DaemonSets</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §16.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s16-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">16.4 StatefulSets — Stateful Applications</h3>
                <p>StatefulSets are for applications that need <strong>stable, unique identity</strong> and <strong>persistent storage</strong>. Unlike Deployments (random pod names, shared storage), StatefulSets provide predictable pod names, ordered deployment, and per-pod persistent volumes.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Deployment</th>
                                <th>StatefulSet</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Pod Names</strong></td>
                                <td>Random: <code>app-x7k2z</code>, <code>app-m9p4q</code></td>
                                <td>Ordered: <code>app-0</code>, <code>app-1</code>, <code>app-2</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>DNS Identity</strong></td>
                                <td>Only via Service (shared)</td>
                                <td>Each pod gets own DNS: <code>app-0.svc.ns</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Storage</strong></td>
                                <td>Shared or none</td>
                                <td>Per-pod PVC (survives pod restarts)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Creation Order</strong></td>
                                <td>All at once (parallel)</td>
                                <td>Sequential: 0 → 1 → 2 (ordered)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Deletion Order</strong></td>
                                <td>Random</td>
                                <td>Reverse: 2 → 1 → 0 (ordered)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Use Case</strong></td>
                                <td>Stateless apps (easypj Django)</td>
                                <td>Databases, Kafka, Redis Cluster, ZooKeeper</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">postgresql-statefulset.yaml — easypj Database</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Headless Service (required for StatefulSet DNS)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres-headless
spec:
  clusterIP: None               <span class="comment"># Headless — no cluster IP</span>
  selector:
    app: postgres
  ports:
  - port: 5432
    name: postgres
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: postgres
spec:
  serviceName: "postgres-headless"  <span class="comment"># Required: links to headless service</span>
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: postgres
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: postgres
        image: postgres:16
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5432
        env:
        - name: POSTGRES_DB
          value: "easypj"
        - name: PGDATA
          value: "/var/lib/postgresql/data/pgdata"
        volumeMounts:
        - name: data
          mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "500m"
            memory: "1Gi"
          limits:
            cpu: "1"
            memory: "2Gi"
  volumeClaimTemplates:            <span class="comment"># Per-pod PVC template</span>
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
      storageClassName: standard
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 10Gi</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">StatefulSet — Ordered Pods with Stable Identity &amp; Per-Pod Storage</div>
<pre>
  StatefulSet: "postgres"    replicas: 3
  serviceName: "postgres-headless"

  Deployment Pods:             StatefulSet Pods:
  (random names)               (ordered, predictable)
  ┌──────────────┐             ┌──────────────┐
  │ app-x7k2z    │             │ postgres-0   │ ← Created FIRST (primary)
  ├──────────────┤             │  DNS: postgres-0.postgres-headless
  │ app-m9p4q    │             ├──────────────┤
  ├──────────────┤             │ postgres-1   │ ← Created SECOND (must wait for 0)
  │ app-j3r8w    │             │  DNS: postgres-1.postgres-headless
  └──────────────┘             ├──────────────┤
                               │ postgres-2   │ ← Created THIRD
                               │  DNS: postgres-2.postgres-headless
                               └──────────────┘

  Per-Pod Persistent Volumes (survive pod restarts!):
  ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐
  │postgres-0│     │postgres-1│     │postgres-2│
  │┌────────┐│     │┌────────┐│     │┌────────┐│
  ││data-   ││     ││data-   ││     ││data-   ││
  ││postgres││     ││postgres││     ││postgres││
  ││-0      ││     ││-1      ││     ││-2      ││
  ││ 10Gi   ││     ││ 10Gi   ││     ││ 10Gi   ││
  │└───┬────┘│     │└───┬────┘│     │└───┬────┘│
  └────┼─────┘     └────┼─────┘     └────┼─────┘
       ▼                ▼                ▼
  ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐
  │  PV-0   │     │  PV-1   │     │  PV-2   │
  │ (disk)  │     │ (disk)  │     │ (disk)  │
  └─────────┘     └─────────┘     └─────────┘

  Key guarantees:
  ✅ Stable network identity  — postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc
  ✅ Ordered deployment       — 0 → 1 → 2 (each must be Ready before next)
  ✅ Ordered deletion         — 2 → 1 → 0 (reverse order)
  ✅ Stable storage           — PVC survives pod restart (data preserved!)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ StatefulSet Requires a Headless Service</h5>
                    <p>You <strong>must</strong> create a headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>) for the StatefulSet. The <code>serviceName</code> field in the StatefulSet spec must match the Service name. This enables DNS records for each pod like <code>pod-name.service-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 StatefulSet Deletion — PVCs Are NOT Deleted!</h5>
                    <p>When you delete a StatefulSet (even with <code>--cascade=foreground</code>), the <strong>PersistentVolumeClaims are preserved</strong>. This is by design to prevent data loss. You must manually delete them:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>kubectl delete statefulset postgres</code> → Pods deleted, PVCs remain ✅</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl get pvc</code> → <code>data-postgres-0</code>, <code>data-postgres-1</code>, <code>data-postgres-2</code> still exist</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl delete pvc data-postgres-0 data-postgres-1 data-postgres-2</code> → Manually clean up</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>This is intentional — recreating the StatefulSet will automatically reattach to existing PVCs, preserving all data!</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">StatefulSet Scaling — Ordered Operations</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Scale up — pods created in order (3, 4, 5...)</span>
kubectl scale statefulset postgres --replicas=5
<span class="comment"># postgres-3 created (waits until Ready)</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-4 created (waits until Ready)</span>

<span class="comment"># Scale down — pods deleted in REVERSE order (4, 3)</span>
kubectl scale statefulset postgres --replicas=3
<span class="comment"># postgres-4 terminated first</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-3 terminated second</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-0, 1, 2 untouched ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># PVCs from scaled-down pods are PRESERVED</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># data-postgres-3 and data-postgres-4 still exist</span>
<span class="comment"># Re-scaling up will reattach to them!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Choosing the Right Workload Type</h4>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚀</div>
                        <h5>Deployment</h5>
                        <p>Stateless apps that run forever.<br><strong>easypj Django web server</strong>, nginx, API services.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗄️</div>
                        <h5>StatefulSet</h5>
                        <p>Apps needing stable identity &amp; storage.<br><strong>PostgreSQL</strong>, MySQL, Kafka, Redis Cluster.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>DaemonSet</h5>
                        <p>One pod on every node.<br><strong>Log collectors</strong>, monitoring agents, CNI plugins.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Job / CronJob</h5>
                        <p>Run-to-completion / scheduled tasks.<br><strong>DB migrations</strong>, backups, batch processing.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Decision Tree: Which Workload Type Do I Need?</div>
<pre>
                        ┌──────────────────┐
                        │  New Workload?   │
                        └────────┬─────────┘
                                 │
                    ┌────────────┴────────────┐
                    │                         │
              Runs forever?              Runs once/scheduled?
                    │                         │
              ┌─────┴─────┐             ┌─────┴─────┐
              │           │             │           │
        All nodes?   Some pods?    On schedule?   One-time?
              │           │             │           │
         DaemonSet  ┌─────┴─────┐   CronJob      Job
                    │           │
              Needs stable   Stateless?
              identity/PVC?     │
                    │       Deployment
              StatefulSet

  ╔════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  easypj Stack Mapping:                                ║
  ║                                                        ║
  ║  📦 easypj Django      → Deployment (stateless)       ║
  ║  🗄️ PostgreSQL         → StatefulSet (needs storage)  ║
  ║  📋 Fluentd logging    → DaemonSet (every node)       ║
  ║  🔄 DB migration       → Job (run once)               ║
  ║  💾 Nightly backup     → CronJob (scheduled)          ║
  ╚════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Each Workload Controller Manages Pod Lifecycles: Job Completion, CronJob Scheduling, DaemonSet Placement, and StatefulSet Identity</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Controller Comparison — Four Different Pod Management Strategies</h5>
            <p>Jobs, CronJobs, DaemonSets, and StatefulSets each solve a different problem. Their controllers use fundamentally different reconciliation strategies. Understanding these differences helps you choose the right workload type.</p>
            <table>
            <tr><th>Controller</th><th>Pod Lifecycle</th><th>Identity</th><th>When Pod Dies</th><th>Use Case</th></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Deployment</strong></td><td>Pod runs indefinitely</td><td>Random suffix (-abcde)</td><td>Replaced by RS controller</td><td>Stateless web apps</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Job</strong></td><td>Pod runs to COMPLETION</td><td>Random suffix</td><td>NOT replaced (if succeeded)</td><td>Batch processing, DB migrations</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>CronJob</strong></td><td>Creates Job on schedule</td><td>Job name + timestamp</td><td>Jobs auto-cleaned (history limit)</td><td>Scheduled tasks, backups</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>DaemonSet</strong></td><td>ONE pod per node</td><td>Pod name = &lt;ds-name&gt;-&lt;node&gt;</td><td>Recreated on same node</td><td>Logging, monitoring agents</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>StatefulSet</strong></td><td>Ordered, predictable</td><td>Ordinal index (-0, -1, -2)</td><td>Replaced with SAME name</td><td>Databases, Kafka, ZK</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h5>🔬 Job Controller — Completion and Parallelism Internals</h5>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  JOB CONTROLLER — Completion Tracking                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Job: easypj-db-migrate (completions=1, backoffLimit=6)                      │
│                                                                              │
│  1. Job Controller creates Pod: easypj-db-migrate-abcde                       │
│  2. Pod runs "python manage.py migrate"                                      │
│  3. Pod exits with code 0 (SUCCESS)                                           │
│  4. Job Controller updates Job status:                                        │
│     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐                  │
│     │ status.succeeded: 1                                   │                  │
│     │ status.completionTime: 2026-06-02T10:00:05Z          │                  │
│     │                                                       │                  │
│     │ Job is COMPLETE (status.conditions[type=Complete])    │                  │
│     │ Pod is NOT deleted (kept for logs)                    │                  │
│     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Failure Handling:                                                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Pod fails → backoffLimit counter increments                         │    │
│  │ Pod retries with exponential backoff (10s, 20s, 40s, 80s, ...)      │    │
│  │ After 6 failures → Job marked as FAILED                              │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ PARALLELISM:                                                         │    │
│  │ completions=10, parallelism=3                                        │    │
│  │ → Job controller maintains 3 running pods at all times               │    │
│  │ → As each pod completes, a new one starts (up to 10 total)           │    │
│  │ → Total time: ~ceil(10/3) × single-pod-time ≈ 3-4x speedup          │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>

            <h5>🔬 CronJob Controller — Scheduling Internals</h5>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CRONJOB CONTROLLER — Schedule Evaluation                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  CronJob: easypj-backup (schedule: "0 2 * * *")                              │
│                                                                              │
│  1. CronJob controller checks clock every 10 seconds                         │
│  2. Evaluates schedule: "Should a job have been created since last check?"   │
│  3. If YES → creates Job: easypj-backup-1717296000 (epoch timestamp)         │
│  4. Job controller takes over (creates pods, tracks completion)              │
│                                                                              │
│  overlap POLICY (concurrencyPolicy):                                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Allow (default):    New job runs even if previous job still running │    │
│  │ Forbid:             Skip this run if previous job hasn't finished   │    │
│  │ Replace:            Kill previous job, start new one                │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  missed SCHEDULES (startingDeadlineSeconds):                                  │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ If control plane was DOWN when a scheduled run was due:              │    │
│  │   startingDeadlineSeconds: 200 (default)                             │    │
│  │   → If CronJob is 100s late, it still runs the missed job            │    │
│  │   → If CronJob is 300s late, it SKIPS the missed job                 │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>

            <h5>🔬 DaemonSet Controller — One-Per-Node Guarantee</h5>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  DAEMONSET CONTROLLER — Node-by-Node Placement                               │
│                                                                              │
│  DaemonSet: fluentd-logger                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  1. DaemonSet Controller WATCHES node list                                   │
│  2. For each node that matches nodeSelector/tolerations:                     │
│     → Check: Does a fluentd-logger pod already run on this node?            │
│     → NO  → Create pod (scheduled directly via nodeName, bypassing scheduler)│
│     → YES → Do nothing                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  3. NEW NODE joins cluster:                                                  │
│     → DaemonSet Controller detects new node                                  │
│     → Creates fluentd-logger-&lt;newnode&gt;                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  4. NODE is DELETED:                                                         │
│     → DaemonSet pod on that node is garbage collected (orphaned)             │
│                                                                              │
│  DaemonSet uses nodeName for DIRECT placement:                               │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ pod.spec.nodeName = "worker-1"  <span class="comment">← Scheduler is BYPASSED             </span>│    │
│  │ pod.spec.affinity.nodeAffinity:                                      │    │
│  │   requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:                    │    │
│  │     nodeSelectorTerms:                                               │    │
│  │     - matchFields:                                                   │    │
│  │       - key: metadata.name                                           │    │
│  │         operator: In                                                 │    │
│  │         values: ["worker-1"]                                         │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>

            <h5>🔬 StatefulSet Controller — Stable Identity and Ordered Operations</h5>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STATEFULSET CONTROLLER — Identity + Ordering                                │
│                                                                              │
│  StatefulSet: easypj-db (replicas=3, serviceName=easypj-db)                  │
│                                                                              │
│  POD NAMING (predictable, stable):                                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ easypj-db-0  <span class="comment">← Created FIRST (ordinal 0)                            </span>│    │
│  │ easypj-db-1  <span class="comment">← Created AFTER db-0 is Ready                          </span>│    │
│  │ easypj-db-2  <span class="comment">← Created AFTER db-1 is Ready                          </span>│    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ POD IDENTITY COMPONENTS:                                             │    │
│  │ 1. Stable hostname: easypj-db-0.easypj-db.default.svc.cluster.local │    │
│  │ 2. Stable storage: PVC easypj-data-easypj-db-0 (unique per pod)     │    │
│  │ 3. Stable network ID: ordinal index preserved across rescheduling   │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ IF easypj-db-0 DIES:                                                 │    │
│  │ → Controller creates easypj-db-0 again (SAME name)                   │    │
│  │ → Reattaches SAME PVC (easypj-data-easypj-db-0)                      │    │
│  │ → Gets SAME network identity                                         │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  SCALE DOWN (reverse order):                                                 │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ easypj-db-2 terminated FIRST (highest ordinal)                       │    │
│  │ easypj-db-1 terminated SECOND                                        │    │
│  │ This ensures the "leader" (often db-0) is not accidentally removed  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  HEADLESS SERVICE (required for StatefulSet):                                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ apiVersion: v1                                                       │    │
│  │ kind: Service                                                        │    │
│  │ spec:                                                                │    │
│  │   clusterIP: None  <span class="comment">← HEADLESS (no virtual IP)                       </span>│    │
│  │   selector: {app: easypj-db}                                        │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ DNS returns individual pod IPs (not a single ClusterIP):            │    │
│  │ $ nslookup easypj-db                                                │    │
│  │ easypj-db-0.easypj-db → 10.244.1.5                                  │    │
│  │ easypj-db-1.easypj-db → 10.244.2.3                                  │    │
│  │ easypj-db-2.easypj-db → 10.244.3.7                                  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The CKA tests workload controller selection. Remember: Deployment for stateless web apps (random names, rolling updates), Job for one-time tasks (exits on completion), CronJob for scheduled tasks (cron syntax), DaemonSet for node-level agents (one per node), StatefulSet for stateful apps (stable identity, ordered scaling, headless service required). Matching the wrong controller to a use case is a common exam mistake.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Job <code>backoffLimit</code></strong> — Default is 6 retries. If your job fails 6 times, it stops. Set <code>backoffLimit: 0</code> for no retries, or increase for flaky tasks</li>
                    <li><strong>CronJob schedule syntax</strong> — <code>*/5 * * * *</code> = every 5 minutes. The exam expects you to write cron expressions. Fields: minute, hour, day-of-month, month, day-of-week</li>
                    <li><strong>DaemonSet skips control plane</strong> — By default, DaemonSets don't run on control plane nodes (they have a <code>NoSchedule</code> taint). Add a toleration if needed</li>
                    <li><strong>StatefulSet pods are ordered</strong> — <code>web-0</code> must be Running before <code>web-1</code> starts. Deletion is reverse order. This matters for databases</li>
                    <li><strong>StatefulSet needs a headless Service</strong> — <code>clusterIP: None</code>. Each pod gets a stable DNS: <code>web-0.headless-svc.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Create workloads imperatively:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create job my-job --image=busybox -- echo "hello"</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create cronjob my-cron --image=busybox --schedule="*/5 * * * *" -- echo "tick"</code><br>
                For DaemonSets and StatefulSets, generate a Deployment YAML and change <code>kind:</code> manually — no imperative command exists.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 16)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Command</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Create Job</td><td><code>kubectl create job NAME --image=IMG -- CMD</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create CronJob</td><td><code>kubectl create cronjob NAME --image=IMG --schedule="EXPR" -- CMD</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View Jobs</td><td><code>kubectl get jobs</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View CronJobs</td><td><code>kubectl get cronjobs</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View DaemonSets</td><td><code>kubectl get daemonsets -A</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View StatefulSets</td><td><code>kubectl get statefulsets</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Job logs</td><td><code>kubectl logs job/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Delete completed jobs</td><td><code>kubectl delete jobs --field-selector status.successful=1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Suspend CronJob</td><td><code>kubectl patch cronjob NAME -p '{"spec":{"suspend":true}}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Trigger CronJob now</td><td><code>kubectl create job NAME-manual --from=cronjob/NAME</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 5 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a Job that runs <code>busybox</code> and executes <code>echo CKA-PASS</code>, completions=3, parallelism=2</li>
                    <li>2. Create a CronJob that runs the same command every 2 minutes</li>
                    <li>3. Manually trigger the CronJob: <code>kubectl create job manual-run --from=cronjob/&lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                    <li>4. Check Job status: <code>kubectl get jobs</code> — verify 3/3 completions</li>
                    <li>5. Suspend the CronJob: <code>kubectl patch cronjob &lt;name&gt; -p '{"spec":{"suspend":true}}'</code></li>
                </ul>
