            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 16 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch16-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 16 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of workload-controller behavior — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Job named <code>batch-job</code> that runs <code>busybox</code>, executes <code>echo "Processing complete"</code>, and requires 5 successful completions with a parallelism of 2. Show the imperative and YAML approach.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative: generate base yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative: generate base YAML</span>
kubectl create job batch-job --image=busybox \
--dry-run=client -o yaml -- /bin/sh -c "echo Processing complete" \
> batch-job.yaml

<span class="comment"># Edit batch-job.yaml to add completions and parallelism:</span>
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
name: batch-job
spec:
completions: 5       <span class="comment"># Total successful runs needed</span>
parallelism: 2       <span class="comment"># Run 2 pods at a time</span>
backoffLimit: 4      <span class="comment"># Max retries before marking failed</span>
template:
spec:
  containers:
  - name: batch-job
    image: busybox
    command: ["/bin/sh", "-c", "echo Processing complete"]
  restartPolicy: Never  <span class="comment"># Jobs require Never or OnFailure</span>

kubectl apply -f batch-job.yaml

<span class="comment"># Monitor progress</span>
kubectl get jobs batch-job -w
<span class="comment"># NAME       COMPLETIONS   DURATION</span>
<span class="comment"># batch-job  0/5           5s</span>
<span class="comment"># batch-job  2/5           12s</span>
<span class="comment"># batch-job  4/5           20s</span>
<span class="comment"># batch-job  5/5           28s</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Jobs run pods to completion. <code>completions</code> = how many TOTAL successful runs, <code>parallelism</code> = how many pods run simultaneously. With completions=5 and parallelism=2, Kubernetes runs 2 pods at a time, waits for them to succeed, then starts the next batch, until 5 total succeed. <code>restartPolicy</code> MUST be <code>Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code> (never <code>Always</code> — that's for Deployments). <code>backoffLimit</code> controls how many failures before the Job gives up. On the CKA, you'll likely need to add completions/parallelism to a generated YAML since the imperative command doesn't support them directly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a CronJob that runs <code>curl http://health-check:8080/ping</code> every 5 minutes. Set it to keep 3 successful and 1 failed history.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative:</span>
kubectl create cronjob health-check \
--image=curlimages/curl \
--schedule="*/5 * * * *" \
-- curl -s http://health-check:8080/ping

<span class="comment"># Full YAML with history settings:</span>
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
name: health-check
spec:
schedule: "*/5 * * * *"     <span class="comment"># Every 5 minutes</span>
successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3  <span class="comment"># Keep 3 successful</span>
failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1      <span class="comment"># Keep 1 failed</span>
concurrencyPolicy: Forbid      <span class="comment"># Don't overlap runs</span>
jobTemplate:
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: health-check
        image: curlimages/curl
        command: ["curl", "-s", "http://health-check:8080/ping"]
      restartPolicy: OnFailure

<span class="comment"># Cron schedule reference:</span>
<span class="comment"># ┌───────── minute (0-59)</span>
<span class="comment"># │ ┌─────── hour (0-23)</span>
<span class="comment"># │ │ ┌───── day of month (1-31)</span>
<span class="comment"># │ │ │ ┌─── month (1-12)</span>
<span class="comment"># │ │ │ │ ┌─ day of week (0-6, Sun=0)</span>
<span class="comment"># * * * * *</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CronJobs create Jobs on a schedule (like Unix cron). <code>successfulJobsHistoryLimit</code> and <code>failedJobsHistoryLimit</code> control how many old Job objects are kept (default: 3 successful, 1 failed). <code>concurrencyPolicy</code> can be: <code>Allow</code> (default, overlapping ok), <code>Forbid</code> (skip if previous still running), or <code>Replace</code> (kill previous, start new). On the CKA, <code>kubectl create cronjob</code> handles the basics imperatively, but you'll need to edit the YAML for history limits and concurrency policy. The cron schedule format is the same as standard Unix cron — memorize the 5-field order.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you manually trigger a one-time run from an existing CronJob? Show the command and explain what happens.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create a job from an existing cronjob</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a Job from an existing CronJob</span>
kubectl create job manual-health-check \
--from=cronjob/health-check

<span class="comment"># This creates a Job using the CronJob's jobTemplate</span>
<span class="comment"># The Job runs immediately, regardless of schedule</span>

<span class="comment"># Monitor the triggered job</span>
kubectl get jobs
<span class="comment"># NAME                    COMPLETIONS  DURATION</span>
<span class="comment"># manual-health-check     1/1          5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the logs</span>
kubectl logs job/manual-health-check
<span class="comment"># OK</span>

<span class="comment"># The manually created job is independent:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not counted against history limits</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not affected by concurrencyPolicy</span>
<span class="comment"># - Must be deleted manually if needed</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl create job --from=cronjob/NAME</code> creates a one-off Job using the CronJob's template. This is essential for testing CronJobs without waiting for the next scheduled run. On the CKA, this command appears in scenarios like "verify the CronJob works before waiting." The <code>--from</code> flag copies the jobTemplate spec from the CronJob. The resulting Job is a standalone object — it won't appear in the CronJob's history, and the CronJob's concurrencyPolicy won't block it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between a DaemonSet and a Deployment? When must you use a DaemonSet?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Aspect</th>
                                        <th>Deployment</th>
                                        <th>DaemonSet</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Pod placement</td>
                                        <td>Scheduler decides which nodes</td>
                                        <td>Exactly ONE pod per node</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Scaling</td>
                                        <td>Manual or HPA (replicas count)</td>
                                        <td>Auto (scales with cluster size)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Use case</td>
                                        <td>Stateless apps, web servers</td>
                                        <td>Node-level agents, log collectors</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Examples</td>
                                        <td>nginx, app servers</td>
                                        <td>fluentd, kube-proxy, node-exporter</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">daemonset yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># DaemonSet YAML:</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
name: log-collector
spec:
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: log-collector
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: log-collector
spec:
  containers:
  - name: fluentd
    image: fluentd:latest
    volumeMounts:
    - name: varlog
      mountPath: /var/log
  volumes:
  - name: varlog
    hostPath:
      path: /var/log</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>DaemonSets guarantee exactly ONE pod per node (or per matching node with nodeSelector). When a new node is added to the cluster, the DaemonSet automatically adds a pod to it. When a node is removed, the pod is garbage-collected. Use DaemonSets for: log collection (fluentd, filebeat), monitoring agents (node-exporter, datadog), network plugins (calico, weave). On the CKA, DaemonSets don't have a <code>replicas</code> field — the replica count equals the number of eligible nodes. There's no <code>kubectl create daemonset</code> — you must write YAML (tip: generate a Deployment YAML and change <code>kind: DaemonSet</code>, remove <code>replicas</code>).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a StatefulSet and how does it differ from a Deployment? What are its three guarantees?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">statefulset's 3 guarantees</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># StatefulSet's 3 guarantees:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Stable network identity → web-0, web-1, web-2 (not random)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Ordered deployment      → web-0 before web-1 before web-2</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Persistent storage      → Each pod gets its own PVC</span>

<span class="comment"># StatefulSet requires a headless Service:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: web-headless
spec:
clusterIP: None       # ← Headless (no cluster IP)
selector:
app: web
ports:
- port: 80
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
name: web
spec:
serviceName: "web-headless"    # ← Required
replicas: 3
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: web
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
volumeClaimTemplates:     # ← Each pod gets its own PVC
- metadata:
  name: data
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi

<span class="comment"># DNS entries created:</span>
<span class="comment"># web-0.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># web-1.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># web-2.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>StatefulSets are for stateful applications like databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL, Redis cluster, Kafka). Pods get stable hostnames (web-0, web-1, web-2 — not random hashes), ordered startup/shutdown, and each pod gets its own PersistentVolumeClaim. A headless Service (clusterIP: None) is required to provide individual DNS names. On the CKA, know that: (1) deleting a StatefulSet does NOT delete the PVCs (data is preserved), (2) scaling down goes in REVERSE order (web-2 first, then web-1), (3) <code>serviceName</code> must match the headless Service name. StatefulSets are less commonly tested than Deployments but appear in advanced scenarios.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Job has <code>backoffLimit: 4</code> and <code>activeDeadlineSeconds: 120</code>. Explain what each does and what happens when either limit is reached.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
name: deadline-job
spec:
backoffLimit: 4              <span class="comment"># Max 4 retries</span>
activeDeadlineSeconds: 120   <span class="comment"># Max 2 minutes total</span>
template:
spec:
  containers:
  - name: worker
    image: busybox
    command: ["sh", "-c", "exit 1"]   <span class="comment"># Always fails</span>
  restartPolicy: Never

<span class="comment"># Scenario 1: backoffLimit reached first</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod fails → retry after ~10s → fails → retry after ~20s →</span>
<span class="comment"># fails → retry after ~40s → fails (4th failure)</span>
<span class="comment"># Job status: Failed, reason: BackoffLimitExceeded</span>

<span class="comment"># Scenario 2: activeDeadlineSeconds reached first</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod is still running at 120s → KILLED</span>
<span class="comment"># Job status: Failed, reason: DeadlineExceeded</span>
<span class="comment"># ALL running pods for this Job are terminated</span>

<span class="comment"># Check why a Job failed:</span>
kubectl describe job deadline-job | grep -A3 "Conditions"
<span class="comment"># Type    Status  Reason</span>
<span class="comment"># Failed  True    BackoffLimitExceeded</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>backoffLimit</code> limits the number of retry attempts (default: 6). Failed pods are retried with exponential backoff (10s, 20s, 40s, ..., capped at 6 minutes). <code>activeDeadlineSeconds</code> is a hard wall clock deadline — if the Job isn't complete by then, ALL pods are killed regardless of progress. On the CKA, <code>activeDeadlineSeconds</code> is critical for preventing runaway Jobs. <code>backoffLimit: 0</code> means no retries — fail immediately. These two limits work independently: whichever is reached first causes the Job to fail.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you suspend and resume a CronJob? What happens to already-running Jobs when you suspend?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">suspend a cronjob (prevent future scheduled runs)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Suspend a CronJob (prevent future scheduled runs)</span>
kubectl patch cronjob health-check \
-p '{"spec":{"suspend":true}}'

<span class="comment"># Or use kubectl edit</span>
kubectl edit cronjob health-check
<span class="comment"># Set spec.suspend: true</span>

<span class="comment"># Check suspension status</span>
kubectl get cronjob health-check
<span class="comment"># NAME           SCHEDULE      SUSPEND  ACTIVE  LAST SCHEDULE</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check   */5 * * * *   True     0       2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Resume</span>
kubectl patch cronjob health-check \
-p '{"spec":{"suspend":false}}'

<span class="comment"># Important: suspending does NOT kill running Jobs!</span>
<span class="comment"># Already-running Jobs continue to completion</span>
<span class="comment"># Only NEW scheduled runs are blocked</span>

<span class="comment"># To also stop running Jobs:</span>
kubectl delete job &lt;running-job-name&gt;</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>spec.suspend: true</code> prevents the CronJob from creating new Jobs at the scheduled time. Already-running Jobs continue — suspension only affects FUTURE runs. To completely stop everything, you need to also delete the active Jobs. On the CKA, suspending CronJobs is tested in maintenance scenarios: "pause this scheduled task while we perform an upgrade." The SUSPEND column in <code>kubectl get cronjob</code> quickly shows whether it's suspended. Missed schedules are not retroactively created when you resume — they're simply skipped.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a DaemonSet that runs a <code>node-exporter</code> container on ALL nodes including the control plane. What toleration is needed?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
name: node-exporter
spec:
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: node-exporter
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: node-exporter
spec:
  tolerations:
  <span class="comment"># Tolerate control-plane taint to run on master</span>
  - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
    operator: Exists
    effect: NoSchedule
  <span class="comment"># Or tolerate ALL taints:</span>
  <span class="comment"># - operator: Exists</span>
  containers:
  - name: node-exporter
    image: prom/node-exporter:latest
    ports:
    - containerPort: 9100
      hostPort: 9100    # ← Expose on node's port

<span class="comment"># Verify it runs on ALL nodes:</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=node-exporter -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME                 READY  NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># node-exporter-abc    1/1    controlplane</span>
<span class="comment"># node-exporter-def    1/1    worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># node-exporter-ghi    1/1    worker-2</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Control-plane nodes have the taint <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code>. Without a matching toleration, the DaemonSet pod won't run on control-plane nodes. <code>operator: Exists</code> (without specifying a value) matches the taint regardless of its value. Use <code>hostPort</code> with DaemonSets to expose the pod on the node's IP — useful for monitoring agents. On the CKA, the "run on ALL nodes including master" phrasing signals that you need the control-plane toleration. Tip: generate a Deployment YAML, change <code>kind</code> to DaemonSet, remove <code>replicas</code>, and add the toleration.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What <code>restartPolicy</code> settings are valid for Jobs? What happens if you use <code>restartPolicy: Always</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">valid restartpolicy for jobs</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Valid restartPolicy for Jobs:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Never:     Don't restart failed containers, create new pods</span>
<span class="comment"># - OnFailure: Restart failed containers in the SAME pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Invalid for Jobs:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Always:    REJECTED by the API server</span>
<span class="comment">#   Error: spec.template.spec.restartPolicy: Unsupported value:</span>
<span class="comment">#   "Always": supported values: "OnFailure", "Never"</span>

<span class="comment"># Behavior comparison:</span>
<span class="comment"># restartPolicy: Never</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Failed container? Create a NEW pod</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Each attempt = new pod (can see all pods with kubectl get pods)</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Useful for debugging (logs preserved in each pod)</span>

<span class="comment"># restartPolicy: OnFailure</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Failed container? Restart in SAME pod</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Pod's restart count increments</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Uses less resources (no new pod overhead)</span>
<span class="comment">#   → kubectl get pods shows restart count increasing</span>

<span class="comment"># For CronJobs, the same rules apply (CronJob creates Jobs)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Jobs MUST use <code>Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code>. <code>Always</code> would create an infinite loop — the container completes, restarts, completes, restarts... which defeats the purpose of a Job (run to completion). With <code>Never</code>, each failure creates a new pod (up to <code>backoffLimit</code>), which is useful for debugging because each pod's logs are preserved. With <code>OnFailure</code>, the container restarts in-place, using fewer resources. On the CKA, if you generate a pod YAML and convert it to a Job, remember to change <code>restartPolicy</code> from <code>Always</code> to <code>Never</code> — it's a common mistake that causes a validation error.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List all Jobs created by a CronJob, find failed ones, and check their logs. Show the complete troubleshooting flow.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all jobs owned by the cronjob</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: List all Jobs owned by the CronJob</span>
kubectl get jobs --selector=job-name
<span class="comment"># Or by owner reference:</span>
kubectl get jobs | grep health-check
<span class="comment"># NAME                        COMPLETIONS  DURATION  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412345       1/1          5s        10m</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412350       1/1          4s        5m</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412355       0/1          2m        2m  ← FAILED?</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the failed Job</span>
kubectl describe job health-check-28412355
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning BackoffLimitExceeded ... Job has reached the specified backoff limit</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Find the pods created by the Job</span>
kubectl get pods --selector=job-name=health-check-28412355
<span class="comment"># NAME                              READY  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412355-abc         0/1    Error</span>
<span class="comment"># health-check-28412355-def         0/1    Error</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check logs of the failed pod</span>
kubectl logs health-check-28412355-abc
<span class="comment"># curl: (7) Failed to connect to health-check port 8080: Connection refused</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check events</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=health-check-28412355 \
--sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CronJobs create Jobs with predictable names: <code>cronjob-name-timestamp</code>. Each Job creates pods. The debugging chain is: CronJob → Job → Pod → Logs. On the CKA, troubleshooting failed CronJobs follows this exact flow. Use <code>kubectl get jobs</code> to find failed Jobs (COMPLETIONS shows 0/1), <code>kubectl describe job</code> to see why, and <code>kubectl logs</code> on the pod for application errors. The <code>successfulJobsHistoryLimit</code> and <code>failedJobsHistoryLimit</code> control how many old Jobs are kept — if you can't find old Jobs, the limit may have cleaned them up.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 16 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART 4 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part4-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 4 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Workloads &amp; Scheduling — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You arrived able to run a single Pod; you leave able to run a FLEET. easypj is now a self-healing Deployment that rolls out new versions with zero downtime, rolls back instantly, and autoscales under load. You control exactly where its pods land, budget their CPU/memory so the cluster stays fair, and reach for the right specialized controller — Job, CronJob, DaemonSet, or StatefulSet — whenever a plain Deployment is the wrong tool. This is the CKA’s 15% Workloads &amp; Scheduling domain.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Part 4 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Deployments (Ch 13):</strong> Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods. Rolling update + <code>rollout undo</code> = safe ship + instant rollback; <code>maxSurge:1</code> / <code>maxUnavailable:0</code> = true zero downtime; HPA needs resource requests AND metrics-server.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scheduling (Ch 14):</strong> filter → score → bind. nodeSelector/affinity ATTRACT, taints REPEL, tolerations PERMIT; topology spread for even HA; cordon → drain → uncordon (with a PodDisruptionBudget) for safe node maintenance.</li>
                    <li><strong>Resources (Ch 15):</strong> requests = scheduling floor, limits = enforced ceiling; CPU over-limit → throttled, memory over-limit → OOMKilled (137); QoS (Guaranteed/Burstable/BestEffort) sets eviction order; LimitRange + ResourceQuota govern the namespace.</li>
                    <li><strong>Workload types (Ch 16):</strong> Deployment (stateless), StatefulSet (stable id + per-pod PVC + headless Service), DaemonSet (one per node), Job (run-to-completion), CronJob (scheduled).</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> 15% lives here — scale/rollout/rollback, affinity/taints, “why is this pod Pending?”, and create Job/CronJob/DaemonSet are fast, high-yield points.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 4 Visual Summary — a fragile Pod → a governed, self-healing fleet</div>
<pre>
   📦 one fragile Pod
        │  Ch 13: Deployment + ReplicaSet + HPA → self-healing, scalable
        ▼
   🚀 easypj fleet (3+ replicas · zero-downtime rollouts · instant rollback)
        │  Ch 14: scheduler filter▶score▶bind · affinity/taints/topology · cordon/drain
        │  Ch 15: requests/limits · QoS · LimitRange · ResourceQuota
        ▼
   🧩 right tool per job — Job · CronJob · DaemonSet · StatefulSet (Ch 16)
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   easypj now survives node loss, upgrades cleanly, grows under traffic, lands on
   the right hardware, and lives inside a fair resource budget. ☰
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 4 turned easypj from a single hand-launched Pod into an operated fleet. A Deployment keeps N replicas alive and ships new versions one pod at a time (rolling update) with instant <code>rollout undo</code>; an HPA grows it under load. The scheduler decides placement via filter→score→bind, and you bias it with nodeSelector/affinity (attract), taints/tolerations (repel/permit), and topology spread (even HA) — draining nodes safely with a PodDisruptionBudget for maintenance.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> requests/limits set the resource budget and QoS (eviction order), while LimitRange and ResourceQuota govern the namespace. Finally, you pick the right controller for the shape of the work — Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job, or CronJob. Master these and you own the 15% Workloads &amp; Scheduling domain. Next: Part 5 connects and exposes the fleet with Services &amp; networking.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

                <!-- ==================== PART5 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part5">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 5 · Services &amp; Networking</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Connect &amp; Expose the Fleet</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 17–20 · CKA 20% · <em>"How traffic actually reaches a pod"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">A running fleet is useless if nothing can reach it. Part 5 is the CKA’s second-biggest domain: the flat pod network and the CNI that implements it, the four Service types that give pods stable addresses, CoreDNS that turns names into ClusterIPs, and Ingress + NetworkPolicies that route and firewall HTTP traffic into easypj.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> trace a packet from browser → Ingress → Service → pod; create ClusterIP/NodePort/LoadBalancer/headless Services; debug a broken CoreDNS lookup; and write a NetworkPolicy that isolates the easypj database tier.</div>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Real-World Analogy &amp; Myth-Busting Table</h4>
                <p>A part-specific real-world analogy (with an ASCII mapping table) and a "Before You Start" myth-vs-reality comparison table go here — matching the pattern established in Parts 1–3.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — packet’s-eye view</div>
<pre>
   Ch 17 NETWORK MODEL  ──▶  Ch 18 SERVICES  ──▶  Ch 19 DNS
   flat pod net · CNI         ClusterIP/NodePort/LB    CoreDNS · records
   kube-proxy                 Endpoints · selectors    search domains
            └────────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
                                     ▼
            Ch 20 INGRESS &amp; NETWORK POLICIES (route + firewall)
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: a browser request reaches easypj, and only the pods
   you allow can talk to each other.
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch17">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 17 · Networking Fundamentals</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Networking is the <strong>backbone of Kubernetes</strong>. Every pod, service, and DNS lookup depends on a correctly configured network. In this chapter you'll master the 4 fundamental networking rules, understand how CNI plugins implement the network, trace packets between pods on the same and different nodes, explore how kube-proxy routes service traffic, and build a troubleshooting toolkit for common network issues — all using our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django stack as a real-world example.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🌐 Networking</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The 4 fundamental Kubernetes networking rules — why NAT-free networking matters</li>
                    <li>✅ How CNI plugins (Calico, Cilium, Flannel) implement the network layer</li>
                    <li>✅ Pod-to-Pod communication — same-node vs cross-node packet paths</li>
                    <li>✅ How kube-proxy translates Service IPs into pod endpoints using iptables/IPVS</li>
                    <li>✅ Network troubleshooting commands and strategies for the CKA exam</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Services &amp; Networking <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Networking Is 20% of the CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>Networking questions on the CKA range from creating Services and Ingress to troubleshooting DNS and pod connectivity. Key exam skills:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Know which CNI plugin is installed: <code>ls /etc/cni/net.d/</code></li>
                    <li>Check kube-proxy mode: <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-proxy-xxxxx</code></li>
                    <li>Test connectivity: <code>kubectl exec -it pod -- curl http://service-name</code></li>
                    <li>Debug DNS: <code>kubectl exec -it pod -- nslookup service-name</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §17.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s17-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.1 The Kubernetes Networking Model</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes enforces a <strong>flat, NAT-free networking model</strong>. This is fundamentally different from Docker's default bridge networking, and understanding these rules is critical for every CKA topic that follows.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">1️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Every Pod Gets a Unique IP</h5>
                        <p>No two pods share an IP address. Containers within the same pod share the pod's IP and communicate via <code>localhost</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">2️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Pod-to-Pod Without NAT</h5>
                        <p>Any pod can reach any other pod directly using its IP — even across nodes. No network address translation needed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">3️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Node-to-Pod Without NAT</h5>
                        <p>Nodes can communicate directly with any pod IP in the cluster without address translation.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">4️⃣</div>
                        <h5>Pod Sees Its Own Real IP</h5>
                        <p>Inside a pod, the IP it sees for itself is the same IP that every other pod and node sees. No surprises.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The 4 Fundamental Kubernetes Networking Rules</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  KUBERNETES FLAT NETWORKING MODEL                                        │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Rule 1: Every Pod gets a unique IP                                     │
  │  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐            │
  │  │  easypj-abc    │  │  postgres-0    │  │  redis-xyz     │            │
  │  │  10.244.1.5    │  │  10.244.2.8    │  │  10.244.3.2    │            │
  │  │  (Node 1)      │  │  (Node 2)      │  │  (Node 1)      │            │
  │  └────────────────┘  └────────────────┘  └────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Rule 2: Pod-to-Pod — direct, no NAT                                    │
  │  easypj (10.244.1.5) ────────────────────→ postgres (10.244.2.8) ✅    │
  │                       no translation needed!                             │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Rule 3: Node-to-Pod — direct, no NAT                                  │
  │  Node 1 (192.168.1.10) ──────────────────→ postgres (10.244.2.8) ✅    │
  │                          on Node 2, still direct!                        │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Rule 4: Pod sees its own real IP                                       │
  │  Inside easypj:     hostname -i  →  10.244.1.5                         │
  │  From postgres:     ping easypj  →  10.244.1.5   ✅ Same IP!          │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
  │  CONTRAST: Docker default networking (WITHOUT Kubernetes)                │
  │  Container A → NAT → Host IP → NAT → Container B   ❌ Complex!        │
  │  Container sees 172.17.0.2, outside sees host IP    ❌ Different!      │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why This Model Matters</h5>
                    <p>The flat networking model means you can take any application that communicates over TCP/IP and run it in Kubernetes <strong>without modifying the code</strong>. Your easypj Django app connects to <code>postgres:5432</code> exactly the same way whether running locally or in a Kubernetes cluster. No port mapping gymnastics, no NAT tables to debug.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Containers Sharing a Pod Network Namespace</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────── Pod: easypj-abc ─────────────────────────┐
  │                   IP: 10.244.1.5                                  │
  │                                                                    │
  │  ┌────────────────┐  ┌────────────────┐  ┌───────────────────┐   │
  │  │  Container 1   │  │  Container 2   │  │  Container 3      │   │
  │  │  django        │  │  nginx sidecar │  │  log-collector    │   │
  │  │  :8000         │  │  :80           │  │  (no port)        │   │
  │  └───────┬────────┘  └───────┬────────┘  └────────┬──────────┘   │
  │          │                   │                     │              │
  │  ════════╧═══════════════════╧═════════════════════╧═══════════  │
  │                     Shared Network Namespace                      │
  │                     (same IP, same localhost)                      │
  │                                                                    │
  │  nginx → curl http://localhost:8000  ✅  (reaches django)        │
  │  django → curl http://localhost:80   ✅  (reaches nginx)         │
  │                                                                    │
  │  ⚠️  Port conflicts! Two containers can't both use :8000        │
  │                                                                    │
  │  eth0 ──── 10.244.1.5 ──── connected to node bridge             │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Multi-Container Pod Networking</h5>
                    <p>All containers in a pod share the <strong>same network namespace</strong> — same IP, same port space, same <code>localhost</code>. This is why sidecar containers (like nginx reverse proxies or log collectors) can communicate with the main container via <code>localhost</code>. But beware: <strong>two containers can't listen on the same port</strong>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>IP Address Ranges in Kubernetes</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Kubernetes Network CIDRs</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🖥️</span> Node Network</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Physical/VM network: <code>192.168.1.0/24</code> — each node gets an IP from your infrastructure</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> Pod Network (CIDR)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>10.244.0.0/16</code> (default for Flannel) or <code>192.168.0.0/16</code> (Calico). Each node gets a /24 subnet.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔗</span> Service Network</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>10.96.0.0/12</code> (default). Virtual IPs — no real interface, handled by kube-proxy iptables rules.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Inspecting Cluster Network CIDRs</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pod CIDR — check kubeadm config or CNI</span>
kubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m 1 cluster-cidr
<span class="comment"># --cluster-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</span>

<span class="comment"># Service CIDR</span>
kubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m 1 service-cluster-ip-range
<span class="comment"># --service-cluster-ip-range=10.96.0.0/12</span>

<span class="comment"># Each node's pod subnet</span>
kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.podCIDR}{"\n"}{end}'
<span class="comment"># worker-1    10.244.1.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2    10.244.2.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-3    10.244.3.0/24</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod IPs — verify flat networking</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME           READY   IP            NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-abc     1/1     10.244.1.5    worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-0     1/1     10.244.2.8    worker-2</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §17.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s17-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.2 CNI Plugins — How Networking Is Implemented</h3>
                <p>The <strong>Container Network Interface (CNI)</strong> is a specification that defines how networking is set up for containers. Kubernetes itself does NOT implement networking — it delegates to a CNI plugin. As a CKA admin, you must know how to identify, install, and troubleshoot the CNI.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CNI Plugin — The Missing Piece</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  KUBERNETES says: "I need networking. Here's my spec."   │
  │                                                          │
  │  ┌──────────┐     ┌────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │ kubelet  │ ──→ │  CNI Plugin (your choice)       │    │
  │  │          │     │                                  │    │
  │  │ "Create  │     │  1. Assign pod IP               │    │
  │  │  network │     │  2. Set up veth pair             │    │
  │  │  for new │     │  3. Configure bridge/routing     │    │
  │  │  pod"    │     │  4. Enable cross-node traffic    │    │
  │  └──────────┘     └────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                          │
  │  Without CNI → pods have NO networking → cluster broken  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>CNI Plugin</th>
                                <th>Networking Mode</th>
                                <th>Network Policy</th>
                                <th>Performance</th>
                                <th>Best For</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Calico</strong></td>
                                <td>BGP (Layer 3) / VXLAN overlay</td>
                                <td>✅ Full support</td>
                                <td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</td>
                                <td>Production, CKA labs, enterprise</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Cilium</strong></td>
                                <td>eBPF (kernel-level)</td>
                                <td>✅ Advanced (L7)</td>
                                <td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</td>
                                <td>High-performance, observability</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Flannel</strong></td>
                                <td>VXLAN overlay (Layer 2)</td>
                                <td>❌ None</td>
                                <td>⭐⭐⭐☆☆</td>
                                <td>Simple clusters, learning</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Weave Net</strong></td>
                                <td>Mesh overlay</td>
                                <td>✅ Basic</td>
                                <td>⭐⭐⭐☆☆</td>
                                <td>Easy setup, small clusters</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Canal</strong></td>
                                <td>Flannel networking + Calico policies</td>
                                <td>✅ Full support</td>
                                <td>⭐⭐⭐⭐☆</td>
                                <td>Flannel users needing NetworkPolicy</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Identifying and Inspecting the CNI Plugin</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Which CNI is installed? Check the config directory</span>
ls /etc/cni/net.d/
<span class="comment"># 10-calico.conflist    ← Calico is installed</span>

<span class="comment"># Check CNI pods running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E "calico|cilium|flannel|weave"
<span class="comment"># calico-node-abc12    1/1    Running</span>
<span class="comment"># calico-node-def34    1/1    Running</span>

<span class="comment"># View CNI binary directory</span>
ls /opt/cni/bin/
<span class="comment"># calico  calico-ipam  bandwidth  bridge  host-local  loopback  portmap</span>

<span class="comment"># Check pod CIDR assigned to each node</span>
kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}: {.spec.podCIDR}{"\n"}{end}'

<span class="comment"># If CNI pods are crashing — cluster networking is broken!</span>
kubectl describe pod calico-node-abc12 -n kube-system</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam: CNI Must Be Installed</h5>
                    <p>After <code>kubeadm init</code>, nodes show <code>NotReady</code> until you install a CNI. The exam may ask you to install one:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Calico: <code>kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml</code></li>
                        <li>Flannel: <code>kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml</code></li>
                        <li>If nodes are <code>NotReady</code> — first check if CNI pods exist in <code>kube-system</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CNI Plugin Lifecycle — What Happens When a Pod Is Created</div>
<pre>
  kubectl run easypj --image=easypj:latest
         │
         ▼
  ┌────────────────────┐
  │  1. API Server      │  Stores pod spec in etcd
  └────────┬───────────┘
           ▼
  ┌────────────────────┐
  │  2. Scheduler       │  Assigns pod to worker-1
  └────────┬───────────┘
           ▼
  ┌────────────────────┐
  │  3. kubelet         │  On worker-1, sees new pod assignment
  │     (worker-1)      │
  └────────┬───────────┘
           ▼
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  4. kubelet calls CNI plugin:                       │
  │     /opt/cni/bin/calico ADD                         │
  │                                                     │
  │     CNI plugin performs:                             │
  │     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │     │ a. Create network namespace for pod       │   │
  │     │ b. Create veth pair (pod eth0 ↔ host)    │   │
  │     │ c. Assign IP: 10.244.1.5  (from IPAM)   │   │
  │     │ d. Set up routing to/from pod            │   │
  │     │ e. Connect to bridge (cbr0/cni0)         │   │
  │     │ f. Apply any NetworkPolicy rules         │   │
  │     └──────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                     │
  │     Returns: { "ips": [{"address": "10.244.1.5"}] } │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
           ▼
  ┌────────────────────┐
  │  5. kubelet starts  │  Container runtime starts containers
  │     containers      │  Pod is now RUNNING with networking ✅
  └────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §17.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s17-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.3 Pod-to-Pod Communication</h3>
                <p>Understanding how packets travel between pods is essential for troubleshooting. The path differs depending on whether pods are on the <strong>same node</strong> or <strong>different nodes</strong>.</p>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Key Network Primitives — Building Blocks</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> veth pair</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">A virtual Ethernet cable with two ends: one inside the pod (<code>eth0</code>), one on the host (connected to the bridge). Like a network patch cable between two switches.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌉</span> Linux bridge (cbr0/cni0)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">A virtual layer-2 switch that connects all pod veth endpoints on a single node. Forwards frames between pods.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> Network namespace</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">An isolated network stack (interfaces, routes, iptables). Each pod gets its own. Containers within a pod share one.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> VXLAN tunnel</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Overlay encapsulation: the pod packet is wrapped in an outer UDP packet to cross the physical network between nodes.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Same-Node Communication — Via Linux Bridge</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────── Node 1 ────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                             │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐          ┌─────────────────┐          │
  │  │ easypj-abc      │          │ redis-xyz        │          │
  │  │ 10.244.1.5      │          │ 10.244.1.12      │          │
  │  │ eth0 ──────┐    │          │    ┌────── eth0  │          │
  │  └────────────┼────┘          └────┼─────────────┘          │
  │               │  veth pair         │  veth pair              │
  │          ┌────▼────────────────────▼────┐                   │
  │          │       cbr0 / cni0 (bridge)   │                   │
  │          │       10.244.1.1             │                   │
  │          └──────────────────────────────┘                   │
  │                                                             │
  │  Packet path: easypj eth0 → veth → bridge → veth → redis  │
  │  ✅ Fast — stays entirely within the node kernel           │
  │  ✅ No encapsulation overhead                               │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Cross-Node Communication — Via Overlay Network (VXLAN/BGP)</div>
<pre>
  ┌────────── Node 1 ──────────┐         ┌────────── Node 2 ──────────┐
  │                              │         │                              │
  │ ┌─────────────────┐         │         │         ┌─────────────────┐ │
  │ │ easypj-abc      │         │         │         │ postgres-0      │ │
  │ │ 10.244.1.5      │         │         │         │ 10.244.2.8      │ │
  │ │ eth0            │         │         │         │        eth0     │ │
  │ └──────┬──────────┘         │         │         └────────▲────────┘ │
  │        │ veth                │         │                  │ veth     │
  │ ┌──────▼──────┐             │         │         ┌────────┴────┐    │
  │ │ cbr0 bridge │             │         │         │ cbr0 bridge │    │
  │ │ 10.244.1.1  │             │         │         │ 10.244.2.1  │    │
  │ └──────┬──────┘             │         │         └────────▲────┘    │
  │        │ routing             │         │                  │         │
  │ ┌──────▼──────┐             │         │         ┌────────┴────┐    │
  │ │  eth0 (NIC) │═════════════╪═════════╪═════════│  eth0 (NIC) │    │
  │ │ 192.168.1.10│   physical  │         │         │ 192.168.1.11│    │
  │ └─────────────┘   network   │         │         └─────────────┘    │
  └──────────────────────────────┘         └──────────────────────────────┘

  Packet journey:
  1. easypj (10.244.1.5) → sends to postgres (10.244.2.8)
  2. Pod eth0 → veth → cbr0 bridge → node routing table
  3. CNI plugin encapsulates packet (VXLAN header or BGP route)
  4. Node 1 eth0 ═══ physical network ═══ Node 2 eth0
  5. Node 2 decapsulates → cbr0 → veth → postgres eth0
  6. postgres receives packet from 10.244.1.5 ✅
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>Overlay (VXLAN)</h5>
                        <p>Pod packets wrapped in outer UDP packet. Works anywhere (cloud, bare metal). Slight overhead. Used by: <strong>Flannel, Calico (VXLAN mode), Cilium</strong>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>Routing (BGP)</h5>
                        <p>Node routing tables updated to forward pod traffic directly. No encapsulation overhead. Requires L3 network. Used by: <strong>Calico (BGP mode)</strong>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Testing Pod-to-Pod Connectivity</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Get pod IPs</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME           IP            NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-abc     10.244.1.5    worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-0     10.244.2.8    worker-2</span>

<span class="comment"># Test direct pod-to-pod (cross-node)</span>
kubectl exec easypj-abc -- curl -s --max-time 3 http://10.244.2.8:5432
<span class="comment"># OR for TCP check</span>
kubectl exec easypj-abc -- nc -zv 10.244.2.8 5432
<span class="comment"># 10.244.2.8 (10.244.2.8:5432) open</span>

<span class="comment"># Test same-node connectivity</span>
kubectl exec easypj-abc -- ping -c 2 10.244.1.12
<span class="comment"># PING 10.244.1.12: 64 bytes, time=0.1ms  ← very fast (same node)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §17.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s17-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.4 kube-proxy — Service Networking</h3>
                <p><code>kube-proxy</code> runs on every node as a DaemonSet. Its job is to implement <strong>Service routing</strong> — when a pod sends traffic to a Service ClusterIP, kube-proxy's rules redirect that traffic to one of the Service's backend pods.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kube-proxy — How Service ClusterIP Routing Works</div>
<pre>
  easypj pod calls: http://easypj-svc:80   (ClusterIP: 10.96.45.2)

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Step 1: Pod sends packet to 10.96.45.2:80 (Service ClusterIP)  │
  │                                                                  │
  │  Step 2: Packet hits iptables/IPVS rules (set by kube-proxy)   │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  kube-proxy rules on this node:                          │   │
  │  │                                                          │   │
  │  │  10.96.45.2:80 → DNAT to one of:                        │   │
  │  │    ├── 10.244.1.5:8000  (easypj-abc on worker-1) 33%   │   │
  │  │    ├── 10.244.2.3:8000  (easypj-def on worker-2) 33%   │   │
  │  │    └── 10.244.3.7:8000  (easypj-ghi on worker-3) 33%   │   │
  │  │                                                          │   │
  │  │  Random selection (iptables) or round-robin (IPVS)      │   │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                                  │
  │  Step 3: Packet destination rewritten to 10.244.2.3:8000        │
  │          → Normal pod-to-pod routing delivers it                │
  │                                                                  │
  │  Step 4: Response comes back, source NAT reverses               │
  │          Pod sees response from 10.96.45.2 (Service IP)         │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐                    │
  │  │  Key insight: 10.96.45.2 is VIRTUAL     │                    │
  │  │  No network interface has this IP!       │                    │
  │  │  It only exists in iptables/IPVS rules  │                    │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────┘                    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>kube-proxy Mode</th>
                                <th>How It Works</th>
                                <th>Performance</th>
                                <th>When to Use</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>iptables</strong></td>
                                <td>Creates iptables NAT rules for each Service/Endpoint. Random backend selection.</td>
                                <td>Good for &lt; 1000 Services</td>
                                <td>Default — most clusters</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>IPVS</strong></td>
                                <td>Uses Linux kernel IPVS module. Hash-based load balancing (round-robin, least connections, etc.).</td>
                                <td>Excellent for 1000+ Services</td>
                                <td>Large-scale production</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>nftables</strong></td>
                                <td>Uses nftables (successor to iptables). More efficient rule processing.</td>
                                <td>Better than iptables</td>
                                <td>Newer kernels (K8s 1.29+)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Inspecting kube-proxy</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check kube-proxy pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy
<span class="comment"># NAME              READY   STATUS    NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-abc12  1/1     Running   worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-def34  1/1     Running   worker-2</span>

<span class="comment"># Check kube-proxy mode</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-proxy-abc12 | grep "Using"
<span class="comment"># I0401 Using iptables proxier    ← iptables mode</span>

<span class="comment"># View kube-proxy config</span>
kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode
<span class="comment"># mode: ""   ← empty means iptables (default)</span>

<span class="comment"># View actual iptables rules for a Service (on a node)</span>
<span class="comment"># ssh worker-1</span>
iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES | grep easypj-svc
<span class="comment"># KUBE-SVC-xxx  tcp  --  anywhere  10.96.45.2  tcp dpt:http</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Tip: kube-proxy Crash = Services Stop Working</h5>
                    <p>If kube-proxy pods are down, <strong>Services still resolve via DNS</strong> but traffic can't be routed to pods. Symptoms: DNS lookup works but <code>curl http://service:port</code> hangs. Always check kube-proxy if Service connectivity fails but DNS is fine.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kube-proxy Mode Decision Flow</div>
<pre>
                     ┌─────────────────────────┐
                     │  How many Services in    │
                     │  your cluster?            │
                     └────────┬────────────────┘
                              │
               ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
               │              │              │
          &lt; 500         500 – 5000       &gt; 5000
               │              │              │
               ▼              ▼              ▼
     ┌─────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐
     │  iptables   │  │  Need LB   │  │    IPVS      │
     │  (default)  │  │  algos?    │  │  (required)  │
     │  ✅ Simple  │  └─────┬──────┘  │  ✅ O(1)     │
     │  ✅ Works   │    yes │ no      │  ✅ Scalable  │
     │  everywhere │        │         └──────────────┘
     └─────────────┘   ┌────┴────┐
                        │  IPVS  │
                        │ ✅ RR  │
                        │ ✅ LC  │   RR  = Round Robin
                        │ ✅ WRR │   LC  = Least Connections
                        │ ✅ SH  │   WRR = Weighted Round Robin
                        └────────┘   SH  = Source Hashing

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Running K8s 1.29+ on modern kernel?                 │
  │  Consider nftables mode — better rule processing    │
  │  Set: mode: "nftables" in kube-proxy ConfigMap      │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Switching kube-proxy to IPVS Mode</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Edit the kube-proxy ConfigMap</span>
kubectl edit configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Change: mode: ""  →  mode: "ipvs"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Restart kube-proxy DaemonSet to pick up the change</span>
kubectl rollout restart daemonset kube-proxy -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify IPVS mode is active</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy | grep "Using"
<span class="comment"># I0401 Using ipvs proxier    ← IPVS mode!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: View IPVS rules (on a node via ssh)</span>
ipvsadm -Ln
<span class="comment"># TCP  10.96.45.2:80 rr         ← round-robin load balancing</span>
<span class="comment">#   -> 10.244.1.5:8000  Masq  1  0  0</span>
<span class="comment">#   -> 10.244.2.3:8000  Masq  1  0  0</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §17.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s17-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">17.5 Network Troubleshooting for Admins</h3>
                <p>The CKA exam frequently tests your ability to <strong>diagnose and fix networking issues</strong>. Here's your systematic troubleshooting playbook.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Check CNI</h5>
                        <p>Are CNI pods running? <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep calico</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Check kube-proxy</h5>
                        <p>Is kube-proxy running? <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep proxy</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Test Pod IP</h5>
                        <p>Can you reach the pod directly? <code>kubectl exec -- curl POD_IP:PORT</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Test Service IP</h5>
                        <p>Can you reach the Service? <code>kubectl exec -- curl SVC_IP:PORT</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Test DNS</h5>
                        <p>Does name resolution work? <code>kubectl exec -- nslookup svc-name</code></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Symptom</th>
                                <th>Likely Cause</th>
                                <th>Diagnostic Command</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Nodes <code>NotReady</code></td>
                                <td>CNI not installed or crashing</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></td>
                                <td>Install/fix CNI plugin</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Pod can't reach pod on other node</td>
                                <td>CNI overlay broken, firewall blocking</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl exec -- ping &lt;pod-ip&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Check CNI pods, node firewall rules</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>DNS works but Service unreachable</td>
                                <td>kube-proxy down or misconfigured</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy</code></td>
                                <td>Restart kube-proxy, check ConfigMap</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Service returns no endpoints</td>
                                <td>Selector doesn't match pod labels</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get endpoints svc-name</code></td>
                                <td>Fix selector labels to match pods</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>nslookup</code> fails</td>
                                <td>CoreDNS pods down</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></td>
                                <td>Check CoreDNS pods and logs</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Traffic blocked between pods</td>
                                <td>NetworkPolicy denying traffic</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get networkpolicies -A</code></td>
                                <td>Review/update NetworkPolicy rules</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Network Troubleshooting Toolkit</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Quick connectivity test from a debug pod</span>
kubectl run nettest --image=nicolaka/netshoot --rm -it -- bash

<span class="comment"># Inside netshoot pod:</span>
curl -s http://easypj-svc:80          <span class="comment"># Test Service by name</span>
curl -s http://10.96.45.2:80          <span class="comment"># Test Service by IP</span>
ping -c 2 10.244.2.8                  <span class="comment"># Test pod IP directly</span>
nslookup easypj-svc                   <span class="comment"># Test DNS resolution</span>
nslookup easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local  <span class="comment"># Full FQDN</span>
traceroute 10.244.2.8                 <span class="comment"># Trace packet path</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check endpoints (are pods registered with the Service?)</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME          ENDPOINTS                                    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-svc   10.244.1.5:8000,10.244.2.3:8000,10.244.3.7:8000   5m</span>
<span class="comment"># If ENDPOINTS is empty → selector doesn't match any pods!</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Verify Service selector matches pod labels</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-svc -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'
<span class="comment"># {"app":"easypj"}</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj
<span class="comment"># Should show your pods — if empty, labels don't match!</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Check if NetworkPolicies are blocking traffic</span>
kubectl get networkpolicies -A
kubectl describe networkpolicy -n easypj-prod</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h4>🔑 Chapter 17 Quick Reference</h4>
                    <div class="comparison-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Action</th>
                                    <th>Command</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Check CNI plugin</td><td><code>ls /etc/cni/net.d/</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Check CNI pods</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep calico</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Check kube-proxy</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>View pod IPs</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>View pod CIDRs</td><td><code>kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.podCIDR}'</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Test pod connectivity</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- curl -s http://IP:PORT</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Test DNS</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- nslookup SVC-NAME</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Check endpoints</td><td><code>kubectl get endpoints SVC-NAME</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Debug networking</td><td><code>kubectl run nettest --image=nicolaka/netshoot --rm -it -- bash</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Check NetworkPolicies</td><td><code>kubectl get networkpolicies -A</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🧪 CKA Practice Scenario: Network Debugging</h5>
                    <p><strong>Situation:</strong> A junior admin reports that the easypj Django app can't connect to the postgres database. Pods are Running, but <code>curl http://postgres-svc:5432</code> from the easypj pod times out.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">Step-by-Step Diagnosis</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Can DNS resolve the service name?</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- nslookup postgres-svc
<span class="comment"># ✅ Returns 10.96.88.5 — DNS is fine</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Can we reach the pod directly by IP?</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide | grep postgres
<span class="comment"># postgres-0   10.244.2.8   worker-2</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- nc -zv 10.244.2.8 5432
<span class="comment"># ✅ open — pod-to-pod networking works</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Can we reach the SERVICE IP?</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- nc -zv 10.96.88.5 5432
<span class="comment"># ❌ TIMEOUT — Service routing is broken!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kube-proxy</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-abc12  0/1  CrashLoopBackOff  ← FOUND IT!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check kube-proxy logs</span>
kubectl logs kube-proxy-abc12 -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># "failed to read config: invalid mode 'ipvss'"</span>
<span class="comment"># Typo in config! mode should be "ipvs" not "ipvss"</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Edit the kube-proxy ConfigMap</span>
kubectl edit configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Fix the mode value, then restart kube-proxy pods</span>
kubectl rollout restart daemonset kube-proxy -n kube-system</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How the Kubernetes Network Model Actually Works: CNI Plugin Architecture, Pod-to-Pod Communication Across Nodes, and kube-proxy Modes</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ The 4 Fundamental Kubernetes Networking Rules</h5>
            <p>Kubernetes networking is built on a flat, routable pod network. Every pod gets its own IP, and pods on different nodes communicate without NAT. The CNI plugin implements this, and kube-proxy handles Services on top.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUBERNETES NETWORK MODEL — The 4 Rules                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  RULE 1: All pods can communicate with each other WITHOUT NAT                │
│  RULE 2: All nodes can communicate with all pods WITHOUT NAT                │
│  RULE 3: Each pod sees its own IP as what others see (no source NAT)        │
│  RULE 4: All containers in a pod share the SAME network namespace           │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  HOW POD-TO-POD COMMUNICATION WORKS (across nodes):                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Pod-A (10.244.1.5) on Node-1          Pod-B (10.244.2.7) on Node-2         │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐            ┌─────────────────────────┐         │
│  │  Pod-A (netns: podA)    │            │  Pod-B (netns: podB)    │         │
│  │  eth0: 10.244.1.5       │            │  eth0: 10.244.2.7       │         │
│  │  default route → eth0   │            │                         │         │
│  └──────────┬──────────────┘            └──────────┬──────────────┘         │
│             │ veth pair                              │ veth pair               │
│             ▼                                       ▼                         │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐            ┌─────────────────────────┐         │
│  │  Node-1 root netns      │            │  Node-2 root netns      │         │
│  │                         │            │                         │         │
│  │  cni0 bridge: 10.244.1.1│            │  cni0 bridge: 10.244.2.1│         │
│  │                         │            │                         │         │
│  │  Route:                 │   VXLAN/   │  Route:                 │         │
│  │  10.244.2.0/24 via      │───BGP/etc──│  10.244.1.0/24 via      │         │
│  │  Node-2 (192.168.1.11)  │   TUNNEL   │  Node-1 (192.168.1.10)  │         │
│  │                         │            │                         │         │
│  │  eth0: 192.168.1.10     │            │  eth0: 192.168.1.11     │         │
│  └─────────────────────────┘            └─────────────────────────┘         │
│                                                                              │
│  PACKET FLOW (Pod-A → Pod-B):                                                │
│  1. Pod-A sends to 10.244.2.7 (Pod-B IP)                                    │
│  2. Pod-A veth → Node-1 cni0 bridge                                         │
│  3. Node-1 kernel routes: 10.244.2.0/24 via Node-2 (192.168.1.11)          │
│  4. CNI encapsulates packet (VXLAN tunnel) or routes directly (BGP)         │
│  5. Node-2 receives → decapsulates → cni0 bridge → Pod-B veth              │
│  6. Pod-B receives packet with source 10.244.1.5 (NO NAT!)                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CNI PLUGIN ARCHITECTURE:                                                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ kubelet calls CNI binary at pod creation/deletion:                   │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  CNI_ADD:  Create network namespace, assign IP, set up routes        │    │
│  │  CNI_DEL:  Tear down network namespace, release IP                   │    │
│  │  CNI_CHECK: Verify network config is still valid                     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  Popular CNI Plugins:                                                │    │
│  │  ┌──────────┬────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│  │  │ Calico   │ BGP-based  │ No overlay — direct routing, best    │    │    │
│  │  │          │            │ performance, NetworkPolicy support   │    │    │
│  │  │ Flannel  │ VXLAN/host-│ Simple overlay, easy setup           │    │    │
│  │  │          │ gw         │                                      │    │    │
│  │  │ Cilium   │ eBPF-based │ Highest performance, advanced        │    │    │
│  │  │          │            │ observability, NetworkPolicy         │    │    │
│  │  │ Weave    │ VXLAN      │ Encryption built-in, mesh topology   │    │    │
│  │  └──────────┴────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  KUBE-PROXY MODES:                                                           │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  ┌──────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │    │
│  │  │ iptables │ Default mode. Rules in nat table. Random         │   │    │
│  │  │          │ load balancing. O(n) rules for n services.       │   │    │
│  │  │          │ Good for small-medium clusters.                   │   │    │
│  │  ├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤   │    │
│  │  │ IPVS     │ Kernel L4 load balancer. O(1) lookup, supports   │   │    │
│  │  │          │ multiple scheduling algorithms (rr, lc, sh, dh). │   │    │
│  │  │          │ Better for large clusters (>1000 services).       │   │    │
│  │  ├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤   │    │
│  │  │ nftables │ Newer, replacing iptables. Simpler ruleset.      │   │    │
│  │  └──────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  CHECK CURRENT MODE:                                                 │    │
│  │  $ kubectl get cm kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, you may need to troubleshoot pod-to-pod communication. Verify: (1) <code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> → both pods have IPs? (2) <code>kubectl exec pod-a -- ping &lt;pod-b-ip&gt;</code> → reachable? (3) If no CNI, pods show "ContainerCreating" indefinitely. Install CNI: <code>kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/flannel-io/flannel/master/Documentation/kube-flannel.yml</code>. (4) Check kube-proxy: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>4 networking rules</strong> — Every pod gets its own IP. Pods can communicate across nodes without NAT. Agents on a node can talk to all pods on that node. The CKA tests that you understand this flat network model</li>
                    <li><strong>CNI plugin must be installed</strong> — Without it, nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> and pods stay <code>Pending</code>. The exam tells you which CNI to install (usually Calico or Flannel)</li>
                    <li><strong>kube-proxy modes</strong> — <code>iptables</code> (default) vs <code>IPVS</code>. kube-proxy creates rules that route Service VIPs to pod IPs. If kube-proxy is down, services stop working but direct pod-to-pod still works</li>
                    <li><strong>Pod CIDR vs Service CIDR</strong> — These are different ranges. Pod IPs come from <code>--pod-network-cidr</code>. Service IPs come from <code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code>. Don't confuse them</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Network debugging toolkit:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl run nettest --image=nicolaka/netshoot --rm -it -- bash</code> — launch a debug pod with all network tools<br>
                <code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> — see pod IPs and node placement<br>
                <code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- curl -s &lt;service-ip&gt;:&lt;port&gt;</code> — test connectivity<br>
                <code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- nslookup &lt;service-name&gt;</code> — test DNS resolution</p>
            </div>

            

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 17)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Command</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Check CNI plugin</td><td><code>ls /etc/cni/net.d/</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check CNI pods</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep calico</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check kube-proxy</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View pod IPs</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View pod CIDRs</td><td><code>kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.podCIDR}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Test pod connectivity</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- curl -s http://IP:PORT</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Test DNS</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- nslookup SVC-NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check endpoints</td><td><code>kubectl get endpoints SVC-NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Debug networking</td><td><code>kubectl run nettest --image=nicolaka/netshoot --rm -it -- bash</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check NetworkPolicies</td><td><code>kubectl get networkpolicies -A</code></td></tr>
                                            <tr><td>verify kube-proxy running on all nodes</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -A -o wide | grep kube-proxy</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>kube-proxy logs</td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>check proxy mode</td><td><code>kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>node-level routing (from inside the node)</td><td><code>ip route</code> / <code>iptables -t nat -L</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

