            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 19 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 19 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of DNS resolution &amp; CoreDNS — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the full DNS format for a Service in Kubernetes? Show the FQDN for a Service named <code>web-svc</code> in namespace <code>production</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">service dns format</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Service DNS format:</span>
<span class="comment"># &lt;service-name&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Full FQDN for web-svc in production:</span>
<span class="comment"># web-svc.production.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS resolution from different contexts:</span>
<span class="comment"># Same namespace (production):</span>
nslookup web-svc                    <span class="comment"># Works!</span>
nslookup web-svc.production         <span class="comment"># Works!</span>
nslookup web-svc.production.svc     <span class="comment"># Works!</span>
nslookup web-svc.production.svc.cluster.local  <span class="comment"># Full FQDN</span>

<span class="comment"># Different namespace (from default):</span>
nslookup web-svc                    <span class="comment"># FAILS (wrong ns)</span>
nslookup web-svc.production         <span class="comment"># Works!</span>
nslookup web-svc.production.svc.cluster.local  <span class="comment"># Works!</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod DNS format (different!):</span>
<span class="comment"># &lt;pod-ip-dashed&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.pod.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod IP 10.244.1.5 → 10-244-1-5.production.pod.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># StatefulSet pod DNS:</span>
<span class="comment"># &lt;pod-name&gt;.&lt;headless-svc&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># web-0.web-headless.production.svc.cluster.local</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubernetes DNS has strict formats: Services use <code>svc-name.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>, pods use <code>ip-dashed.ns.pod.cluster.local</code>, StatefulSet pods use <code>pod-name.headless-svc.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>. Short names like <code>web-svc</code> only work within the SAME namespace because of the search domain in <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>. For cross-namespace access, you MUST use at least <code>svc-name.namespace</code>. On the CKA, DNS FQDN questions are common — memorize the format.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CoreDNS is not working — all pods fail DNS resolution. Show the complete troubleshooting flow.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">test dns from a pod</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Test DNS from a pod</span>
kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it -- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># ;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># NAME                      READY  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-5d78c9869d-abc    0/1    CrashLoopBackOff  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># "Loop detected" ← DNS forwarding loop!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kube-dns Service</span>
kubectl get svc -n kube-system kube-dns
<span class="comment"># NAME       TYPE       CLUSTER-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-dns   ClusterIP  10.96.0.10   53/UDP,53/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check CoreDNS ConfigMap</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for: forward . /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># If node's resolv.conf points to 127.0.0.53 → loop!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Change forward to explicit upstream DNS</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Change: forward . /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># To:     forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</span>

<span class="comment"># Restart CoreDNS to apply</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The DNS loop is a classic CKA troubleshooting scenario. On many Linux systems, <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to <code>127.0.0.53</code> (systemd-resolved), which forwards to... CoreDNS, creating a loop. Fix by pointing to external DNS (8.8.8.8). The troubleshooting flow: (1) test DNS from a pod, (2) check CoreDNS pods in <code>kube-system</code>, (3) check logs for errors, (4) check the <code>coredns</code> ConfigMap. Remember: the Service is named <code>kube-dns</code> (not coredns) but the pods are <code>coredns-*</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the CoreDNS Corefile and how do you modify it? Show how to add a custom DNS record.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the corefile is stored in the coredns configmap</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The Corefile is stored in the coredns ConfigMap:</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml

<span class="comment"># Default Corefile:</span>
<span class="comment"># .:53 {</span>
<span class="comment">#     errors</span>
<span class="comment">#     health { lazystart }</span>
<span class="comment">#     ready</span>
<span class="comment">#     kubernetes cluster.local in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa {</span>
<span class="comment">#         pods insecure</span>
<span class="comment">#         fallthrough in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa</span>
<span class="comment">#     }</span>
<span class="comment">#     prometheus :9153</span>
<span class="comment">#     forward . /etc/resolv.conf {</span>
<span class="comment">#         max_concurrent 1000</span>
<span class="comment">#     }</span>
<span class="comment">#     cache 30</span>
<span class="comment">#     loop</span>
<span class="comment">#     reload</span>
<span class="comment">#     loadbalance</span>
<span class="comment"># }</span>

<span class="comment"># Add a custom DNS rewrite rule:</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Add inside the .:53 block:</span>
<span class="comment">#     rewrite name db.myapp.local postgres-svc.production.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Add a custom hosts entry:</span>
<span class="comment"># Add inside the .:53 block:</span>
<span class="comment">#     hosts {</span>
<span class="comment">#         192.168.1.100 legacy-server.company.com</span>
<span class="comment">#         fallthrough</span>
<span class="comment">#     }</span>

<span class="comment"># CoreDNS auto-reloads (reload plugin)</span>
<span class="comment"># Check reload:</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns | grep reload</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The Corefile configures CoreDNS behavior. Key plugins: <code>kubernetes</code> (resolves cluster DNS), <code>forward</code> (upstream DNS for external names), <code>cache</code> (caching), <code>loop</code> (loop detection), <code>reload</code> (hot-reload on ConfigMap changes). On the CKA, you may be asked to: (1) modify the forward directive, (2) add custom rewrites, (3) fix a broken Corefile. The <code>reload</code> plugin means CoreDNS picks up ConfigMap changes automatically (within ~30 seconds) without pod restart. However, <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system</code> forces immediate reload.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check what DNS configuration a pod is using? What does <code>ndots: 5</code> mean and why does it matter?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check a pod's dns config</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check a pod's DNS config:</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># nameserver 10.96.0.10</span>
<span class="comment"># search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># options ndots:5</span>

<span class="comment"># ndots:5 means:</span>
<span class="comment"># If a name has FEWER than 5 dots, append search domains first</span>
<span class="comment"># "google.com" has 1 dot (< 5), so DNS tries:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1. google.com.default.svc.cluster.local → NXDOMAIN</span>
<span class="comment">#   2. google.com.svc.cluster.local → NXDOMAIN</span>
<span class="comment">#   3. google.com.cluster.local → NXDOMAIN</span>
<span class="comment">#   4. google.com → RESOLVED! (4 queries for 1 lookup!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Optimization: add trailing dot for external names</span>
<span class="comment"># "google.com." → treated as FQDN, only 1 query</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- nslookup google.com.

<span class="comment"># Override DNS config in pod spec:</span>
spec:
dnsPolicy: "None"
dnsConfig:
nameservers:
- 8.8.8.8
searches:
- default.svc.cluster.local
options:
- name: ndots
  value: "2"            # ← Reduce DNS queries</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>ndots:5</code> is the Kubernetes default — any name with fewer than 5 dots tries all search domains first. This is why looking up <code>google.com</code> generates 4+ DNS queries before the final resolution. For performance-critical apps, lower <code>ndots</code> or use FQDNs (trailing dot). On the CKA, <code>dnsPolicy</code> options are: <code>ClusterFirst</code> (default, use CoreDNS), <code>Default</code> (use node's DNS), <code>None</code> (custom DNS config). Know how to read <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> inside a pod and understand why DNS might be slow (ndots causing extra queries).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">From namespace <code>frontend</code>, how do you connect to a Service named <code>api-svc</code> in namespace <code>backend</code>? What DNS name do you use?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cross-namespace dns access</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Cross-namespace DNS access:</span>
<span class="comment"># Short form (usually enough):</span>
curl http://api-svc.backend:8080

<span class="comment"># Full FQDN:</span>
curl http://api-svc.backend.svc.cluster.local:8080

<span class="comment"># Why "api-svc" alone doesn't work:</span>
<span class="comment"># The pod's /etc/resolv.conf has:</span>
<span class="comment"># search frontend.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># So "api-svc" resolves to:</span>
<span class="comment">#   api-svc.frontend.svc.cluster.local → NOT what we want!</span>

<span class="comment"># You MUST include the namespace:</span>
kubectl exec frontend-pod -n frontend -- \
curl -s http://api-svc.backend:8080
<span class="comment"># Response from backend API</span>

<span class="comment"># Test DNS resolution:</span>
kubectl exec frontend-pod -n frontend -- \
nslookup api-svc.backend
<span class="comment"># Name: api-svc.backend.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.78.90</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Cross-namespace Service access requires including the target namespace in the DNS name: <code>svc-name.namespace</code>. This is because the search domain in a pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> only includes its own namespace. Using just <code>api-svc</code> would resolve to <code>api-svc.frontend.svc.cluster.local</code> (wrong namespace). On the CKA, cross-namespace communication is commonly tested — remember to include the namespace. Note: there are no network restrictions by default between namespaces — you need NetworkPolicies to restrict cross-namespace traffic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>kube-dns</code> Service and how does it relate to CoreDNS? Why the naming confusion?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">coredns components</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># CoreDNS components:</span>
kubectl get all -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># Deployment: coredns (runs CoreDNS pods)</span>
<span class="comment"># Pods: coredns-5d78c9869d-abc, coredns-5d78c9869d-def (2 replicas)</span>
<span class="comment"># Service: kube-dns (ClusterIP: 10.96.0.10) ← Named "kube-dns"!</span>

<span class="comment"># Why "kube-dns" not "coredns"?</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-dns was the original DNS server (before v1.12)</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS replaced it, but the Service kept the name</span>
<span class="comment"># for backward compatibility</span>

<span class="comment"># All pods' /etc/resolv.conf points to:</span>
<span class="comment"># nameserver 10.96.0.10  ← kube-dns Service IP</span>
<span class="comment"># This IP is configured in kubelet:</span>
<span class="comment">#   --cluster-dns=10.96.0.10</span>

<span class="comment"># Key facts:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Deployment name: coredns</span>
<span class="comment"># - Service name: kube-dns (confusing but intentional)</span>
<span class="comment"># - ConfigMap name: coredns</span>
<span class="comment"># - Label: k8s-app=kube-dns (on everything)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This naming mismatch is a classic CKA trap. The Deployment is <code>coredns</code>, the ConfigMap is <code>coredns</code>, but the Service is <code>kube-dns</code>. All share the label <code>k8s-app=kube-dns</code>. The Service IP (usually 10.96.0.10) is hardcoded in kubelet's <code>--cluster-dns</code> flag and injected into every pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>. On the CKA, if you're asked to check DNS, remember: <code>kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system</code> (not coredns). CoreDNS runs as a Deployment (typically 2 replicas for HA), NOT as a DaemonSet.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you set a pod's <code>dnsPolicy</code>? What are the available policies and when do you use each?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Policy</th>
                                        <th>Behavior</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>ClusterFirst</td>
                                        <td>Use CoreDNS, fall back to node DNS (default)</td>
                                        <td>Normal pods</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>ClusterFirstWithHostNet</td>
                                        <td>ClusterFirst for hostNetwork pods</td>
                                        <td>hostNetwork: true pods needing cluster DNS</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Default</td>
                                        <td>Use node's /etc/resolv.conf</td>
                                        <td>Pods needing host DNS only</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>None</td>
                                        <td>Custom — must set dnsConfig</td>
                                        <td>Full DNS control</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">hostnetwork pod that needs cluster dns</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># hostNetwork pod that needs cluster DNS:</span>
spec:
hostNetwork: true
dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet  # ← Required!
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The default <code>ClusterFirst</code> policy sends all DNS queries to CoreDNS first. For hostNetwork pods, the default would use the node's DNS — so you need <code>ClusterFirstWithHostNet</code> explicitly to use CoreDNS. <code>None</code> requires <code>dnsConfig</code> to be set manually. On the CKA, <code>ClusterFirstWithHostNet</code> appears when a <code>hostNetwork: true</code> pod can't resolve Service names — that's the fix. Confusing name: <code>Default</code> is NOT the default policy; <code>ClusterFirst</code> is.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you quickly test if DNS is working in a cluster? What is the fastest one-liner?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">fastest one-liner</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Fastest one-liner:</span>
kubectl run dnscheck --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it --restart=Never \
-- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Server:    10.96.0.10</span>
<span class="comment"># Address 1: 10.96.0.10 kube-dns.kube-system.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Name:      kubernetes.default</span>
<span class="comment"># Address 1: 10.96.0.1  ← API server's Service IP</span>

<span class="comment"># If DNS works, kubernetes.default always resolves</span>
<span class="comment"># It's the built-in kubernetes API Service</span>

<span class="comment"># If DNS fails, deeper checks:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Is CoreDNS running?</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># 2. Is kube-dns Service accessible?</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># ClusterIP: 10.96.0.10, Ports: 53/UDP, 53/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Can we reach CoreDNS directly?</span>
kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it --restart=Never \
-- nslookup kubernetes.default 10.96.0.10

<span class="comment"># 4. Is the pod's resolv.conf correct?</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat /etc/resolv.conf</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubernetes.default</code> is the built-in Service that points to the API server. It always exists in every cluster. If <code>nslookup kubernetes.default</code> works, DNS is functioning. If it fails, CoreDNS is broken. On the CKA, this one-liner is your go-to DNS test. Use <code>busybox:1.36</code> (not latest — some versions have broken nslookup). The <code>--rm -it --restart=Never</code> flags create a temporary pod that's cleaned up after use.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does DNS work for a headless Service backing a StatefulSet? Show the DNS records created.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">setup</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Setup:</span>
<span class="comment"># StatefulSet: web (3 replicas)</span>
<span class="comment"># Headless Service: web-headless (clusterIP: None)</span>
<span class="comment"># Namespace: default</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS records created:</span>

<span class="comment"># Service DNS (returns ALL pod IPs):</span>
nslookup web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.5   (web-0)</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.2.8   (web-1)</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.12  (web-2)</span>

<span class="comment"># Individual pod DNS:</span>
nslookup web-0.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.5   (only web-0)</span>

nslookup web-1.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.2.8   (only web-1)</span>

nslookup web-2.web-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.12  (only web-2)</span>

<span class="comment"># Format: &lt;pod-name&gt;.&lt;headless-svc&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># This is why StatefulSets REQUIRE a headless Service:</span>
<span class="comment"># It creates per-pod DNS entries!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Headless Services for StatefulSets create two types of DNS entries: (1) the Service DNS returns all pod IPs (for discovery), (2) individual pod DNS records (<code>pod-name.svc-name.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>) for direct pod access. This is how database replicas find each other — <code>web-0</code> can always reach <code>web-1</code> by DNS name, even if its IP changes. On the CKA, knowing this format is essential for StatefulSet questions. The Service name in the DNS must match the StatefulSet's <code>spec.serviceName</code> field.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">CoreDNS pods keep crashing with <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>. The logs show <em>"plugin/loop: Loop ... detected"</em>. How do you fix this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">problem: dns forwarding loop</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Problem: DNS forwarding loop</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS forwards to /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># Node's resolv.conf points to 127.0.0.53 (systemd-resolved)</span>
<span class="comment"># systemd-resolved forwards to... CoreDNS → infinite loop!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 1: Edit CoreDNS ConfigMap (recommended for CKA)</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Change:</span>
<span class="comment">#   forward . /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># To:</span>
<span class="comment">#   forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</span>

<span class="comment"># Restart CoreDNS pods to pick up change immediately:</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Fix 2: Disable loop detection (NOT recommended)</span>
<span class="comment"># Remove the "loop" line from Corefile</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 3: Fix node's resolv.conf (node-level fix)</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit /etc/resolv.conf on each node:</span>
<span class="comment"># Change nameserver 127.0.0.53 to nameserver 8.8.8.8</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify fix:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># Both coredns pods: Running</span>

kubectl run test --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it -- nslookup google.com
<span class="comment"># Name: google.com</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 142.250.x.x ← Working!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The DNS loop is the most common CoreDNS failure on kubeadm clusters. On systems with systemd-resolved, <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to <code>127.0.0.53</code> — a local stub resolver that may forward back to CoreDNS, creating an infinite loop. The <code>loop</code> plugin detects this and crashes CoreDNS intentionally (better to crash than infinite loop). On the CKA, the fix is always: change the <code>forward</code> directive in the <code>coredns</code> ConfigMap to explicit upstream servers (8.8.8.8). Edit the ConfigMap, restart the Deployment, verify with nslookup.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 19 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch20">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 20 · Ingress &amp; Network Policies</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">While Services handle internal routing, <strong>Ingress</strong> manages external HTTP/HTTPS traffic with path-based routing, TLS termination, and virtual hosts. <strong>Network Policies</strong> act as a firewall for pod-to-pod traffic — controlling which pods can talk to each other. Together, these are the <strong>most heavily tested networking topics</strong> on the CKA exam.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🛡️ Network Security</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Ingress — HTTP/HTTPS routing with path and host rules</li>
                    <li>✅ Setting up an Ingress Controller (NGINX) in a cluster</li>
                    <li>✅ Network Policies — the pod-level firewall for zero-trust networking</li>
                    <li>✅ Policy types: Ingress (incoming), Egress (outgoing), and combined</li>
                    <li>✅ Real-world YAML patterns for common Network Policy scenarios</li>
                    <li>✅ Testing and verifying Network Policy enforcement</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Services &amp; Networking <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Ingress + NetworkPolicy = High-Value CKA Points</h5>
                <p>These are among the highest-weighted questions on the CKA. Tips:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Know the Ingress YAML by heart — the exam won't have auto-complete for all fields</li>
                    <li>NetworkPolicy: remember that <strong>empty podSelector {}</strong> selects ALL pods, and an empty <code>ingress: [{}]</code> allows ALL traffic</li>
                    <li>If asked to "deny all traffic" — apply a NetworkPolicy with <code>podSelector: {}</code> and <code>policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]</code> with empty rules</li>
                    <li>An Ingress Controller must be running for Ingress resources to work</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.1 Ingress — HTTP/HTTPS Routing</h3>
                <p>An <strong>Ingress</strong> is an API object that manages external HTTP/HTTPS access to Services. It provides <strong>path-based routing</strong>, <strong>host-based routing</strong>, and <strong>TLS termination</strong> — all features that NodePort/LoadBalancer Services lack.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Ingress — How External HTTP Traffic Is Routed</div>
<pre>
  Internet user: https://easypj.example.com/api/jobs/
                 https://easypj.example.com/admin/

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
  │  │  Ingress Controller (NGINX / Traefik / HAProxy)      │       │
  │  │  Deployed as a pod, exposed via LoadBalancer/NodePort │       │
  │  │  Reads Ingress rules from API server                 │       │
  │  └───────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘       │
  │                      │                                           │
  │          ┌───────────┼────────────┐                             │
  │          │           │            │                              │
  │   path: /api/*  path: /admin/*  path: /static/*                │
  │          │           │            │                              │
  │   ┌──────▼─────┐ ┌──▼────────┐ ┌─▼───────────┐                │
  │   │ easypj-svc │ │ admin-svc │ │  static-svc │                │
  │   │ port: 80   │ │ port: 80  │ │  port: 80   │                │
  │   └──────┬─────┘ └──┬────────┘ └─┬───────────┘                │
  │          │           │            │                              │
  │   ┌──────▼─────┐ ┌──▼────────┐ ┌─▼───────────┐                │
  │   │ easypj     │ │ easypj    │ │  nginx      │                │
  │   │ pods       │ │ pods      │ │  pods       │                │
  │   └────────────┘ └───────────┘ └─────────────┘                │
  │                                                                  │
  │  Benefits over NodePort/LoadBalancer:                           │
  │  ✅ Single external IP for multiple services                   │
  │  ✅ Path-based and host-based routing                          │
  │  ✅ TLS termination at the edge                                │
  │  ✅ Centralized SSL certificate management                     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-ingress.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress
  namespace: default
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx         <span class="comment"># Which Ingress Controller to use</span>
  tls:                             <span class="comment"># TLS termination</span>
    - hosts:
        - easypj.example.com
      secretName: easypj-tls       <span class="comment"># TLS cert stored in a Secret</span>
  rules:
    - host: easypj.example.com     <span class="comment"># Host-based routing</span>
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /api              <span class="comment"># Path-based routing</span>
            pathType: Prefix        <span class="comment"># Prefix or Exact</span>
            backend:
              service:
                name: easypj-svc   <span class="comment"># Route to this Service</span>
                port:
                  number: 80
          - path: /admin
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: easypj-svc
                port:
                  number: 80
    - host: docs.example.com       <span class="comment"># Different host → different Service</span>
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: docs-svc
                port:
                  number: 80</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>pathType</th>
                                <th>Match Behavior</th>
                                <th>Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Prefix</strong></td>
                                <td>Matches URL path prefix by <code>/</code> segments</td>
                                <td><code>/api</code> matches <code>/api</code>, <code>/api/</code>, <code>/api/jobs</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Exact</strong></td>
                                <td>Exact URL path match (case-sensitive)</td>
                                <td><code>/api</code> matches only <code>/api</code>, NOT <code>/api/</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ImplementationSpecific</strong></td>
                                <td>Depends on the IngressClass / controller</td>
                                <td>Behavior varies — avoid on exam</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Working with Ingress Resources</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply the Ingress</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-ingress.yaml

<span class="comment"># Check Ingress — look for ADDRESS (external IP)</span>
kubectl get ingress
<span class="comment"># NAME             CLASS   HOSTS                  ADDRESS        PORTS     AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-ingress  nginx   easypj.example.com     203.0.113.50   80, 443   5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Describe for detailed routing info</span>
kubectl describe ingress easypj-ingress
<span class="comment"># Rules:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Host                  Path  Backends</span>
<span class="comment">#   ----                  ----  --------</span>
<span class="comment">#   easypj.example.com</span>
<span class="comment">#                         /api    easypj-svc:80 (10.244.1.5:8000,10.244.2.3:8000)</span>
<span class="comment">#                         /admin  easypj-svc:80 (10.244.1.5:8000,10.244.2.3:8000)</span>

<span class="comment"># Create TLS Secret for HTTPS</span>
kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \
  --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key -n default</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">TLS Termination at Ingress — HTTPS Flow</div>
<pre>
  Browser: https://easypj.example.com/api/jobs/

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────┐     HTTPS (443)      ┌──────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  Client   │ ═══════════════════▶ │  Ingress Controller  │    │
  │  │ (Browser) │    TLS encrypted     │  (NGINX pod)         │    │
  │  └──────────┘                       │                      │    │
  │                                     │  1. TLS handshake    │    │
  │                                     │  2. Reads TLS cert   │    │
  │                                     │     from Secret      │    │
  │                                     │     "easypj-tls"     │    │
  │                                     │  3. Decrypts HTTPS   │    │
  │                                     │  4. Reads path /api  │    │
  │                                     │  5. Routes to        │    │
  │                                     │     easypj-svc:80    │    │
  │                                     └──────────┬───────────┘    │
  │                                                │                 │
  │                                     HTTP (80) — unencrypted     │
  │                                                │                 │
  │                                     ┌──────────▼───────────┐    │
  │                                     │  easypj pod          │    │
  │                                     │  :8000 (Django)      │    │
  │                                     └──────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ⚡ TLS terminated at Ingress — backend pods use plain HTTP    │
  │  🔐 Certificate stored as K8s Secret (type: kubernetes.io/tls) │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Default Backend &amp; 404 Handling</h5>
                    <p>If no Ingress rule matches the request path, the controller returns a <strong>404</strong>. You can configure a <strong>default backend</strong> in the Ingress spec to serve a custom error page: <code>spec.defaultBackend.service.name: default-http-backend</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.2 Ingress Controller Setup</h3>
                <p>An <strong>Ingress resource is just configuration</strong> — it does nothing without an <strong>Ingress Controller</strong>. The controller is the actual reverse proxy (NGINX, Traefik, HAProxy) that reads Ingress rules and routes traffic.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Ingress Resource</h5>
                        <p>The <strong>rules</strong> — path routing, TLS config, host rules. Just a YAML API object. Does nothing by itself.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚙️</div>
                        <h5>Ingress Controller</h5>
                        <p>The <strong>implementation</strong> — NGINX/Traefik pod that reads rules and configures the reverse proxy. Must be installed separately.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Ingress Controller</th>
                                <th>Provider</th>
                                <th>Features</th>
                                <th>Best For</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>NGINX Ingress</strong></td>
                                <td>Kubernetes community</td>
                                <td>Path/host routing, TLS, rate limiting, auth</td>
                                <td>CKA exam, most clusters</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Traefik</strong></td>
                                <td>Traefik Labs</td>
                                <td>Auto-discovery, Let's Encrypt, middleware</td>
                                <td>Dynamic environments</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>HAProxy</strong></td>
                                <td>HAProxy Technologies</td>
                                <td>High performance, TCP/HTTP load balancing</td>
                                <td>High-traffic production</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>AWS ALB</strong></td>
                                <td>AWS</td>
                                <td>AWS Application Load Balancer integration</td>
                                <td>EKS clusters</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Installing NGINX Ingress Controller</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Install NGINX Ingress Controller (bare metal / kubeadm)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/ingress-nginx/controller-v1.10.0/deploy/static/provider/baremetal/deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify the controller is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx
<span class="comment"># NAME                                       READY   STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># ingress-nginx-controller-abc12-xyz         1/1     Running</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the IngressClass</span>
kubectl get ingressclass
<span class="comment"># NAME    CONTROLLER                           PARAMETERS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># nginx   k8s.io/ingress-nginx                 &lt;none&gt;       30s</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify the controller Service</span>
kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx
<span class="comment"># NAME                        TYPE       EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># ingress-nginx-controller    NodePort   &lt;none&gt;        80:31080/TCP,443:31443/TCP</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam: Ingress Controller Must Be Running</h5>
                    <p>If the exam asks you to create an Ingress resource and it's not working, <strong>first check if an Ingress Controller is installed</strong>: <code>kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx</code>. An Ingress resource without a controller does nothing — the ADDRESS field will be empty.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.3 Network Policies — Firewall for Pods</h3>
                <p>By default, Kubernetes allows <strong>all pods to communicate with all other pods</strong> — no restrictions. <strong>Network Policies</strong> let you define fine-grained rules controlling which pods can send traffic to which other pods, implementing a <strong>zero-trust network</strong>.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Default Behavior vs Network Policy Applied</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────── DEFAULT (No NetworkPolicy) ──────────────┐
  │                                                                   │
  │  easypj ─────────→ postgres  ✅                                  │
  │  easypj ─────────→ redis     ✅                                  │
  │  random-pod ─────→ postgres  ✅  ← anyone can access DB!        │
  │  random-pod ─────→ easypj   ✅  ← no isolation at all          │
  │                                                                   │
  │  EVERYTHING is allowed. Any pod talks to any pod.                │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ┌──────────────── WITH NetworkPolicy on postgres ──────────────────┐
  │                                                                   │
  │  NetworkPolicy: "only pods with app=easypj can reach postgres"  │
  │                                                                   │
  │  easypj ─────────→ postgres  ✅  (label matches)                │
  │  random-pod ─────→ postgres  ❌  BLOCKED                        │
  │  redis ──────────→ postgres  ❌  BLOCKED                        │
  │                                                                   │
  │  easypj ─────────→ redis     ✅  (no policy on redis)           │
  │  random-pod ─────→ easypj   ✅  (no policy on easypj)          │
  │                                                                   │
  │  ⚠️ Policy only affects the TARGET pod (podSelector)            │
  │  ⚠️ Pods with NO policy → still fully open                     │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>Key Concepts</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Policies are <strong>additive</strong> — multiple policies are OR'd together</li>
                            <li>Applying ANY policy to a pod makes it <strong>deny by default</strong></li>
                            <li>No policy = all traffic allowed (default open)</li>
                            <li>Requires CNI with NetworkPolicy support (Calico, Cilium)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Critical Requirement</h5>
                        <p><strong>Flannel does NOT support NetworkPolicies</strong>. If you apply a NetworkPolicy with Flannel, it's silently ignored. The CKA exam uses Calico or Cilium — both support NetworkPolicy.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.4 Network Policy Types &amp; Patterns</h3>
                <p>Network Policies have two directions: <strong>Ingress</strong> (incoming traffic TO the pods) and <strong>Egress</strong> (outgoing traffic FROM the pods). You can control both independently.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Policy Type</th>
                                <th>Direction</th>
                                <th>Controls</th>
                                <th>Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Ingress</strong></td>
                                <td>Incoming → to selected pods</td>
                                <td>Who can SEND traffic to these pods</td>
                                <td>Only easypj pods can reach postgres on port 5432</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Egress</strong></td>
                                <td>Outgoing ← from selected pods</td>
                                <td>Where these pods can SEND traffic</td>
                                <td>easypj pods can only reach postgres and DNS</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Both</strong></td>
                                <td>Bidirectional</td>
                                <td>Controls both directions</td>
                                <td>Fully locked-down pods — explicit allow required</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Network Policy Selectors — Who Can Talk?</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> podSelector</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Match pods by labels in the <strong>same namespace</strong>. <code>matchLabels: { app: easypj }</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📁</span> namespaceSelector</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Match all pods in namespaces with specific labels. <code>matchLabels: { env: prod }</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> ipBlock</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Match traffic from/to specific IP ranges. <code>cidr: 10.0.0.0/8, except: [10.0.1.0/24]</code></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>Here are the most common Network Policy patterns you'll encounter on the CKA exam, using our easypj stack as context.</p>

                <h4>Pattern 1: Deny All Ingress (Default Deny)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deny-all-ingress.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Deny ALL incoming traffic to ALL pods in "default" namespace</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-all-ingress
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}          <span class="comment"># Empty = selects ALL pods in namespace</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress              <span class="comment"># Only control incoming traffic</span>
  <span class="comment"># ingress: []            # Omitted = no traffic allowed in</span>
                           <span class="comment"># (there are no "allow" rules)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 2: Allow Specific Pod Access</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">allow-easypj-to-postgres.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Only easypj pods can reach postgres on port 5432</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-easypj-to-postgres
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:              <span class="comment"># Target: postgres pods</span>
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:                  <span class="comment"># Allow FROM:</span>
        - podSelector:       <span class="comment"># Pods with these labels</span>
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj
      ports:                 <span class="comment"># On these ports only</span>
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 3: Allow from Specific Namespace</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">allow-from-monitoring-ns.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Allow monitoring namespace to scrape all pods in default namespace</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-monitoring
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}           <span class="comment"># All pods in default</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              name: monitoring    <span class="comment"># Namespace must have this label!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 4: Egress — Restrict Outgoing Traffic</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-egress-policy.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj pods can only reach postgres and DNS</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: easypj-egress
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    - to:                    <span class="comment"># Allow TO postgres</span>
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: postgres
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432
    - to: []                 <span class="comment"># Allow DNS (required!)</span>
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 5: Deny All Ingress + Egress (Total Lockdown)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deny-all-traffic.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Complete isolation — no traffic in or out</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-all
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}           <span class="comment"># All pods</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    - Egress
  <span class="comment"># No ingress or egress rules = deny everything</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: AND vs OR Logic in Network Policies</h5>
                    <p>Within a single <code>from</code> block, multiple selectors are <strong>AND</strong>ed. Separate <code>- from:</code> entries are <strong>OR</strong>ed.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">AND vs OR — Critical Difference</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># AND — pod must match BOTH selectors</span>
ingress:
  - from:
      - namespaceSelector:          <span class="comment"># AND</span>
          matchLabels: { env: prod }
        podSelector:                <span class="comment"># AND (same list item!)</span>
          matchLabels: { app: easypj }

<span class="comment"># OR — pod matches EITHER selector</span>
ingress:
  - from:
      - namespaceSelector:          <span class="comment"># OR</span>
          matchLabels: { env: prod }
      - podSelector:                <span class="comment"># OR (separate list item!)</span>
          matchLabels: { app: easypj }</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 6: Allow from Specific IP Range (ipBlock)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">allow-from-office-ips.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Allow only office network to reach the easypj admin panel</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-office-only
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 10.0.0.0/8        <span class="comment"># Allow entire 10.x.x.x range</span>
            except:
              - 10.0.99.0/24         <span class="comment"># Except the guest WiFi subnet</span>
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: nginx-ingress     <span class="comment"># Also allow Ingress controller</span>
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pattern 7: Full Production Lockdown (Ingress + Egress)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-full-lockdown.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj pods: accept from Ingress only, talk only to postgres + DNS</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: easypj-lockdown
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    - Egress
  ingress:
    - from:                         <span class="comment"># Only accept from Ingress controller</span>
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              name: ingress-nginx
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app.kubernetes.io/name: ingress-nginx
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8000
  egress:
    - to:                           <span class="comment"># Can reach postgres</span>
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: postgres
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432
    - to: []                        <span class="comment"># Can reach DNS (required!)</span>
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §20.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s20-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">20.5 Testing Network Policies</h3>
                <p>Always verify your Network Policies work as expected. Here's a systematic testing approach using our easypj stack.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Testing Network Policy Enforcement</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Apply the "allow-easypj-to-postgres" policy</span>
kubectl apply -f allow-easypj-to-postgres.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify policy is created</span>
kubectl get networkpolicies
<span class="comment"># NAME                        POD-SELECTOR     AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># allow-easypj-to-postgres   app=postgres     5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Describe the policy for details</span>
kubectl describe networkpolicy allow-easypj-to-postgres
<span class="comment"># Allowing ingress traffic:</span>
<span class="comment">#   To Port: 5432/TCP</span>
<span class="comment">#   From:</span>
<span class="comment">#     PodSelector: app=easypj</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Test ALLOWED traffic (easypj → postgres)</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- nc -zv postgres-svc 5432
<span class="comment"># postgres-svc (10.96.88.5:5432) open   ✅ ALLOWED</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Test BLOCKED traffic (random pod → postgres)</span>
kubectl run test-blocked --image=busybox --rm -it -- nc -zv postgres-svc 5432
<span class="comment"># → hangs/timeout  ❌ BLOCKED!   (This pod has no "app: easypj" label)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Test that non-policy pods are still open</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- curl -s http://redis-svc:6379
<span class="comment"># ✅ Works — redis has no NetworkPolicy, so it's still fully open</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>List Policies</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get networkpolicies -A</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Check Target Pods</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe netpol POLICY</code> — verify podSelector matches</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Test Allowed Path</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl exec ALLOWED-POD -- nc -zv TARGET PORT</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Test Blocked Path</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl run test -- nc -zv TARGET PORT</code> — should timeout</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Verify CNI Support</h5>
                        <p>If nothing is blocked — CNI may not support policies (Flannel)</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🧪 CKA Practice Scenario: Network Policy Challenge</h5>
                    <p><strong>Task:</strong> In the <code>easypj-prod</code> namespace, create a NetworkPolicy so that <strong>only</strong> the easypj web pods can access the postgres pods on port 5432. All other ingress traffic to postgres must be denied. Pods must still be able to resolve DNS.</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">Solution</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the NetworkPolicy YAML</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: postgres-allow-easypj
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres           <span class="comment"># Target: postgres pods</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress                 <span class="comment"># Control incoming traffic</span>
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj     <span class="comment"># Only easypj pods can connect</span>
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432          <span class="comment"># Only on postgres port</span>
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify the policy</span>
kubectl get netpol -n easypj-prod
<span class="comment"># NAME                    POD-SELECTOR    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-allow-easypj  app=postgres    5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Test — easypj CAN reach postgres</span>
kubectl exec -n easypj-prod easypj-pod -- nc -zv postgres-svc 5432
<span class="comment"># open  ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Test — random pod CANNOT reach postgres</span>
kubectl run -n easypj-prod test --image=busybox --rm -it -- \
  nc -w 3 -zv postgres-svc 5432
<span class="comment"># timeout  ❌ BLOCKED — policy works!</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Part 5 Complete Network Architecture — easypj Stack</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    INTERNET                                        │
  │                       │                                             │
  │              ┌────────▼────────┐                                   │
  │              │  Cloud LB /     │  TLS Termination                  │
  │              │  Ingress (443)  │  easypj.example.com               │
  │              └────────┬────────┘                                   │
  │                  Ingress Rules:                                    │
  │                  /api  → easypj-svc:80                            │
  │                  /admin → easypj-svc:80                           │
  │                       │                                             │
  │   ┌───────────────────▼──────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │   │              easypj-svc (ClusterIP: 10.96.45.2:80)      │    │
  │   │              selector: app=easypj                         │    │
  │   └────────┬────────────────────┬────────────────┬───────────┘    │
  │            │                    │                │                  │
  │   ┌────────▼──────┐  ┌─────────▼─────┐  ┌──────▼─────────┐      │
  │   │ easypj-abc    │  │ easypj-def    │  │ easypj-ghi    │      │
  │   │ 10.244.1.5    │  │ 10.244.2.3    │  │ 10.244.3.7    │      │
  │   │ :8000 Django  │  │ :8000 Django  │  │ :8000 Django  │      │
  │   └───────┬───────┘  └───────┬───────┘  └──────┬────────┘      │
  │           │ NetworkPolicy     │                  │                  │
  │           │ allows :5432      │                  │                  │
  │   ┌───────▼──────────────────────────────────────▼──────────┐    │
  │   │        postgres-headless (clusterIP: None)              │    │
  │   │        selector: app=postgres                            │    │
  │   └────────┬────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┘    │
  │   ┌────────▼──────┐   ┌────────▼──────┐                         │
  │   │ postgres-0    │   │ postgres-1    │   StatefulSet           │
  │   │ 10.244.1.15   │   │ 10.244.2.18   │   + PVC per replica    │
  │   │ PRIMARY       │   │ REPLICA       │                         │
  │   └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘                         │
  │                                                                    │
  │   DNS: easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local → 10.96.45.2        │
  │   DNS: postgres-0.postgres-headless.default... → 10.244.1.15     │
  │   CNI: Calico (VXLAN) │ kube-proxy: iptables                     │
  │   CoreDNS: 10.96.0.10 (kube-dns Service)                        │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How NetworkPolicy Is Enforced at the Kernel Level: From YAML Rules to iptables/eBPF Filters, and How Ingress Controllers Route External Traffic</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ NetworkPolicy — From YAML to Kernel Rules</h5>
            <p>NetworkPolicy YAML looks simple, but it translates into complex iptables rules (or eBPF programs with Cilium). Understanding the translation helps you debug "why isn't my policy working" scenarios.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NETWORKPOLICY → IPTABLES TRANSLATION (Calico example)                       │
│                                                                              │
│  YAML:                                                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1                                      │   │
│  │ kind: NetworkPolicy                                                    │   │
│  │   podSelector:                                                        │   │
│  │     matchLabels: {app: easypj, tier: backend}                        │   │
│  │   ingress:                                                             │   │
│  │   - from:                                                              │   │
│  │     - podSelector: {role: frontend}                                   │   │
│  │     ports:                                                             │   │
│  │     - port: 8000                                                       │   │
│  │       protocol: TCP                                                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  GENERATED IPTABLES RULES (on every node where matching pods run):          │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 1. Mark packets from frontend pods as ALLOWED                       │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -m comment --comment "allow frontend→backend" \ │   │
│  │   -m set --match-set cali40-frontend-src src \                       │   │
│  │   -p tcp --dport 8000 -j MARK --set-mark 0x10000                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 2. Allow MARKED packets (matching policy)                           │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -m mark --mark 0x10000 -j ACCEPT                │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 3. DEFAULT DENY (no matching mark → DROP)                           │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -j DROP                                          │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  NETWORKPOLICY RULES (memorize for CKA):                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. Policies are ADDITIVE — multiple policies UNION their rules       │   │
│  │ 2. Empty podSelector = ALL pods in namespace                         │   │
│  │ 3. Empty ingress = ALLOW ALL traffic (not useful alone)              │   │
│  │ 4. Empty from = ALL sources (any pod, any namespace, any IP)         │   │
│  │ 5. namespaceSelector + podSelector in same rule = AND (both must     │   │
│  │    match: pods from namespace X AND with label Y)                    │   │
│  │ 6. namespaceSelector OR podSelector in DIFFERENT rules = OR          │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ DEFAULT DENY ALL ingress:                                             │   │
│  │   podSelector: {}          <span class="comment"># selects all pods                        │   │</span>
│  │   ingress: []              <span class="comment"># empty list = deny all                   │   │</span>
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>

            <h5>🔬 Ingress Controller — External Traffic Routing</h5>
            <p>Ingress is NOT a built-in controller — you must install one (nginx, traefik, etc.). It runs as a pod and watches Ingress resources to configure itself:</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  INGRESS CONTROLLER — How External Traffic Reaches Your Pod                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  INTERNET                                                              │   │
│  │    │                                                                   │   │
│  │    │  https://easypj.com/api/users                                    │   │
│  │    ▼                                                                   │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────┐                                                  │   │
│  │  │ Cloud LB / MetalLB│  (External IP: 203.0.113.5)                     │   │
│  │  └────────┬─────────┘                                                  │   │
│  │           │ NodePort 30080-30081 OR LoadBalancer                        │   │
│  │           ▼                                                              │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │   │
│  │  │  INGRESS CONTROLLER POD (nginx-ingress)                        │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │  1. Watches Ingress resources via K8s API                      │     │   │
│  │  │  2. Generates nginx.conf from Ingress rules:                   │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │     server {                                                   │     │   │
│  │  │       listen 443 ssl;                                          │     │   │
│  │  │       server_name easypj.com;                                   │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │       location /api {                                          │     │   │
│  │  │         proxy_pass http://backend-svc:8080;                    │     │   │
│  │  │       }                                                        │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │       location / {                                             │     │   │
│  │  │         proxy_pass http://frontend-svc:80;                     │     │   │
│  │  │       }                                                        │     │   │
│  │  │     }                                                          │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │  3. Reloads nginx on Ingress changes                           │     │   │
│  │  └──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘     │   │
│  │                             │ proxy_pass                               │   │
│  │                             ▼                                          │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐                           │   │
│  │  │ backend-svc:8080 │  │ frontend-svc:80  │  (ClusterIP Services)     │   │
│  │  │ → backend pods   │  │ → frontend pods  │                           │   │
│  │  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘                           │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  INGRESS RESOURCE (routes — NOT the controller!):                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1                                      │   │
│  │ kind: Ingress                                                          │   │
│  │ spec:                                                                   │   │
│  │   rules:                                                                │   │
│  │   - host: easypj.com                                                    │   │
│  │     http:                                                                │   │
│  │       paths:                                                             │   │
│  │       - path: /api                                                       │   │
│  │         pathType: Prefix                                                  │   │
│  │         backend:                                                          │   │
│  │           service:                                                        │   │
│  │             name: backend-svc                                             │   │
│  │             port: {number: 8080}                                          │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> NetworkPolicy requires a CNI that supports it (Calico, Cilium, Weave). Flannel does NOT support NetworkPolicy by default. On the CKA, if you create a NetworkPolicy and it doesn't work, verify: (1) <code>kubectl get networkpolicy</code> — does the policy exist in the right namespace? (2) Does your CNI support NetworkPolicy? (3) Policies are namespace-scoped — a policy in namespace A cannot affect pods in namespace B (except via namespaceSelector).
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Ingress requires a controller</strong> — Creating an Ingress resource does nothing without an Ingress Controller (nginx, traefik). The exam cluster has one pre-installed — check with <code>kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Ingress <code>pathType</code> is REQUIRED</strong> — Must be <code>Prefix</code>, <code>Exact</code>, or <code>ImplementationSpecific</code>. Missing it = validation error. <code>Prefix</code> matches <code>/app</code> AND <code>/app/sub</code></li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy default behavior</strong> — By default, ALL traffic is allowed. Once you apply ANY NetworkPolicy that selects a pod, all non-matching traffic to that pod is DENIED. This catches people off guard</li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy is namespace-scoped</strong> — A policy in <code>prod</code> doesn't affect <code>dev</code>. To allow cross-namespace traffic, use <code>namespaceSelector</code> in the ingress/egress rules</li>
                    <li><strong>Egress policies block DNS too</strong> — If you create an egress policy, you must explicitly allow DNS (port 53 UDP to <code>kube-system</code>) or all name resolution breaks</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — NetworkPolicy deny-all then allow pattern:</strong><br>
                Step 1: Create a deny-all ingress policy for the namespace<br>
                Step 2: Create specific allow policies for needed traffic<br>
                This is the most common exam pattern. Memorize the deny-all YAML:</p>
            </div>

            

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 20)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Command / YAML</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>List Ingress resources</td><td><code>kubectl get ingress -A</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Describe Ingress routing</td><td><code>kubectl describe ingress NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check Ingress Controller</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create TLS Secret</td><td><code>kubectl create secret tls NAME --cert=FILE --key=FILE</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List NetworkPolicies</td><td><code>kubectl get networkpolicies -A</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Describe policy details</td><td><code>kubectl describe netpol NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deny all ingress</td><td><code>podSelector: {} / policyTypes: [Ingress]</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Allow specific pod</td><td><code>from: [podSelector: {matchLabels: {app: X}}]</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Allow DNS in egress</td><td><code>to: [] / ports: [{port: 53, protocol: UDP}]</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Test connectivity</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- nc -zv TARGET PORT</code></td></tr>
                                            <tr><td>Create an Ingress</td><td><code>kubectl create ingress myingress --rule="host.com/path=svc:port" --class=nginx</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>check ADDRESS column (should have an IP)</td><td><code>kubectl get ingress</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>see rules and backend services</td><td><code>kubectl describe ingress &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>list all network policies</td><td><code>kubectl get networkpolicy -A</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>see allowed traffic rules</td><td><code>kubectl describe networkpolicy &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 8 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a deny-all NetworkPolicy for namespace <code>secure</code></li>
                    <li>2. Create an allow policy: only pods with label <code>role=frontend</code> can reach <code>role=backend</code> on port 8080</li>
                    <li>3. Verify: exec into a frontend pod and <code>curl backend-svc:8080</code> — should work</li>
                    <li>4. Exec into an unlabeled pod and curl — should be blocked</li>
                    <li>5. Create an Ingress routing <code>/api</code> to backend-svc:8080 and <code>/</code> to frontend-svc:80</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> NetworkPolicy + Ingress = 20% of CKA networking domain.</p>

                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 20 — How NetworkPolicy Is Enforced at the Kernel Level ═══ -->
                <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How NetworkPolicy Is Enforced at the Kernel Level: From YAML Rules to iptables/eBPF Filters, and How Ingress Controllers Route External Traffic</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                <h5>🗺️ NetworkPolicy — From YAML to Kernel Rules</h5>
                <p>NetworkPolicy YAML looks simple, but it translates into complex iptables rules (or eBPF programs with Cilium). Understanding the translation helps you debug "why isn't my policy working" scenarios.</p>
                <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NETWORKPOLICY → IPTABLES TRANSLATION (Calico example)                       │
│                                                                              │
│  YAML:                                                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1                                      │   │
│  │ kind: NetworkPolicy                                                    │   │
│  │   podSelector:                                                        │   │
│  │     matchLabels: {app: easypj, tier: backend}                        │   │
│  │   ingress:                                                             │   │
│  │   - from:                                                              │   │
│  │     - podSelector: {role: frontend}                                   │   │
│  │     ports:                                                             │   │
│  │     - port: 8000                                                       │   │
│  │       protocol: TCP                                                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  GENERATED IPTABLES RULES (on every node where matching pods run):          │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 1. Mark packets from frontend pods as ALLOWED                       │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -m comment --comment "allow frontend→backend" \ │   │
│  │   -m set --match-set cali40-frontend-src src \                       │   │
│  │   -p tcp --dport 8000 -j MARK --set-mark 0x10000                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 2. Allow MARKED packets (matching policy)                           │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -m mark --mark 0x10000 -j ACCEPT                │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># 3. DEFAULT DENY (no matching mark → DROP)                           │</span>   │
│  │ -A cali-fw-cali12345 -j DROP                                          │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  NETWORKPOLICY RULES (memorize for CKA):                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. Policies are ADDITIVE — multiple policies UNION their rules       │   │
│  │ 2. Empty podSelector = ALL pods in namespace                         │   │
│  │ 3. Empty ingress = ALLOW ALL traffic (not useful alone)              │   │
│  │ 4. Empty from = ALL sources (any pod, any namespace, any IP)         │   │
│  │ 5. namespaceSelector + podSelector in same rule = AND (both must     │   │
│  │    match: pods from namespace X AND with label Y)                    │   │
│  │ 6. namespaceSelector OR podSelector in DIFFERENT rules = OR          │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ DEFAULT DENY ALL ingress:                                             │   │
│  │   podSelector: {}          <span class="comment"># selects all pods                        │   │</span>
│  │   ingress: []              <span class="comment"># empty list = deny all                   │   │</span>
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>

                <h5>🔬 Ingress Controller — External Traffic Routing</h5>
                <p>Ingress is NOT a built-in controller — you must install one (nginx, traefik, etc.). It runs as a pod and watches Ingress resources to configure itself:</p>
                <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  INGRESS CONTROLLER — How External Traffic Reaches Your Pod                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  INTERNET                                                              │   │
│  │    │                                                                   │   │
│  │    │  https://easypj.com/api/users                                    │   │
│  │    ▼                                                                   │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────┐                                                  │   │
│  │  │ Cloud LB / MetalLB│  (External IP: 203.0.113.5)                     │   │
│  │  └────────┬─────────┘                                                  │   │
│  │           │ NodePort 30080-30081 OR LoadBalancer                        │   │
│  │           ▼                                                              │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │   │
│  │  │  INGRESS CONTROLLER POD (nginx-ingress)                        │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │  1. Watches Ingress resources via K8s API                      │     │   │
│  │  │  2. Generates nginx.conf from Ingress rules:                   │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │     server {                                                   │     │   │
│  │  │       listen 443 ssl;                                          │     │   │
│  │  │       server_name easypj.com;                                   │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │       location /api {                                          │     │   │
│  │  │         proxy_pass http://backend-svc:8080;                    │     │   │
│  │  │       }                                                        │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │       location / {                                             │     │   │
│  │  │         proxy_pass http://frontend-svc:80;                     │     │   │
│  │  │       }                                                        │     │   │
│  │  │     }                                                          │     │   │
│  │  │                                                                │     │   │
│  │  │  3. Reloads nginx on Ingress changes                           │     │   │
│  │  └──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘     │   │
│  │                             │ proxy_pass                               │   │
│  │                             ▼                                          │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐                           │   │
│  │  │ backend-svc:8080 │  │ frontend-svc:80  │  (ClusterIP Services)     │   │
│  │  │ → backend pods   │  │ → frontend pods  │                           │   │
│  │  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘                           │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  INGRESS RESOURCE (routes — NOT the controller!):                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1                                      │   │
│  │ kind: Ingress                                                          │   │
│  │ spec:                                                                   │   │
│  │   rules:                                                                │   │
│  │   - host: easypj.com                                                    │   │
│  │     http:                                                                │   │
│  │       paths:                                                             │   │
│  │       - path: /api                                                       │   │
│  │         pathType: Prefix                                                  │   │
│  │         backend:                                                          │   │
│  │           service:                                                        │   │
│  │             name: backend-svc                                             │   │
│  │             port: {number: 8080}                                          │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                <div class="uth-callout">
                <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> NetworkPolicy requires a CNI that supports it (Calico, Cilium, Weave). Flannel does NOT support NetworkPolicy by default. On the CKA, if you create a NetworkPolicy and it doesn't work, verify: (1) <code>kubectl get networkpolicy</code> — does the policy exist in the right namespace? (2) Does your CNI support NetworkPolicy? (3) Policies are namespace-scoped — a policy in namespace A cannot affect pods in namespace B (except via namespaceSelector).
                </div>
                </div>
                </details>

            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 20 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch20-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 20 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of Ingress routing &amp; NetworkPolicy rules — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a <code>deny-all</code> NetworkPolicy that blocks ALL ingress traffic to pods in namespace <code>secure</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
name: deny-all-ingress
namespace: secure
spec:
podSelector: {}        # ← Empty = ALL pods in namespace
policyTypes:
- Ingress              # ← Only block ingress (not egress)
<span class="comment"># No ingress rules → all ingress denied!</span>

<span class="comment"># Apply:</span>
kubectl apply -f deny-all.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify — nothing can reach pods in "secure" ns:</span>
kubectl exec -n default test-pod -- curl -s --max-time 3 \
http://web-svc.secure:80
<span class="comment"># curl: (28) Connection timed out ← Blocked!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>podSelector: {}</code> selects ALL pods in the namespace. Listing <code>Ingress</code> in <code>policyTypes</code> without any <code>ingress:</code> rules means zero ingress is allowed — effectively a deny-all. This is the most common CKA NetworkPolicy question. Without this policy, all pods accept traffic from anywhere (Kubernetes is open by default). Once ANY NetworkPolicy selects a pod, that pod switches to "default deny" for the listed policyTypes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Allow pods with label <code>role=frontend</code> to access pods with label <code>role=backend</code> on port 8080 in the same namespace. Write the NetworkPolicy.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
name: allow-frontend-to-backend
namespace: production
spec:
podSelector:
matchLabels:
role: backend          # ← Applies to backend pods
policyTypes:
- Ingress
ingress:
- from:
- podSelector:
matchLabels:
  role: frontend     # ← Allow from frontend pods
ports:
- protocol: TCP
port: 8080             # ← Only on port 8080

<span class="comment"># Test:</span>
<span class="comment"># From frontend pod → backend: ALLOWED</span>
kubectl exec frontend-pod -- curl -s backend-svc:8080 ✓

<span class="comment"># From random pod → backend: DENIED</span>
kubectl exec other-pod -- curl -s backend-svc:8080 ✗</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>podSelector</code> (target) defines WHICH pods the policy applies to (backend). The <code>ingress.from.podSelector</code> (source) defines WHO can send traffic (frontend). The <code>ports</code> restrict to specific ports. On the CKA, pay attention to: (1) which pods the policy selects (target), (2) which pods are allowed (source), (3) the port. A common mistake is confusing target vs source selectors.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create an Ingress resource that routes <code>/api</code> to <code>api-svc</code> on port 8080 and <code>/</code> to <code>web-svc</code> on port 80. Use pathType <code>Prefix</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
name: app-ingress
namespace: default
annotations:
nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
ingressClassName: nginx      # ← Required since K8s 1.18+
rules:
- http:
paths:
- path: /api
pathType: Prefix       # ← Matches /api, /api/v1, /api/users
backend:
  service:
    name: api-svc
    port:
      number: 8080
- path: /
pathType: Prefix       # ← Matches everything else
backend:
  service:
    name: web-svc
    port:
      number: 80

<span class="comment"># Apply and verify:</span>
kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml
kubectl get ingress app-ingress
<span class="comment"># NAME          CLASS   HOSTS  ADDRESS        PORTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># app-ingress   nginx   *      192.168.1.50   80     10s</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Ingress provides HTTP/HTTPS routing to Services. Key fields: <code>ingressClassName</code> selects which controller handles it, <code>pathType</code> defines matching (<code>Prefix</code> = prefix match, <code>Exact</code> = exact match, <code>ImplementationSpecific</code> = controller decides). Order matters — more specific paths should come first (<code>/api</code> before <code>/</code>). On the CKA, always include <code>ingressClassName</code> — it's required. An Ingress without a deployed Ingress Controller does nothing.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that an Ingress Controller is installed and running? What happens if you create an Ingress without a controller?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check for ingress controller pods</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check for Ingress Controller pods:</span>
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx
<span class="comment"># NAME                                       READY  STATUS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># ingress-nginx-controller-6b94c75599-abc    1/1    Running  5d</span>

<span class="comment"># Check IngressClass:</span>
kubectl get ingressclass
<span class="comment"># NAME    CONTROLLER                           PARAMETERS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># nginx   k8s.io/ingress-nginx                 &lt;none&gt;      5d</span>

<span class="comment"># Check if Ingress has an ADDRESS:</span>
kubectl get ingress
<span class="comment"># NAME      CLASS   HOSTS  ADDRESS         PORTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># my-ing    nginx   *      192.168.1.50    80     1h  ← Controller assigned</span>
<span class="comment"># bad-ing   nginx   *      &lt;none&gt;          80     1h  ← No controller!</span>

<span class="comment"># Without a controller:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Ingress resource is created (kubectl get ingress works)</span>
<span class="comment"># - ADDRESS remains &lt;none&gt; forever</span>
<span class="comment"># - No traffic routing occurs</span>
<span class="comment"># - No errors — it just silently does nothing!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>An Ingress resource is just a config object — it needs an Ingress Controller (like NGINX) to actually route traffic. On the CKA, the controller is usually pre-installed. Check with <code>kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx</code> and <code>kubectl get ingressclass</code>. If the Ingress shows no ADDRESS, the controller isn't processing it (wrong <code>ingressClassName</code> or no controller). Common CKA trap: creating a valid Ingress but routing doesn't work because the Service name or port is wrong.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a NetworkPolicy that allows pods in namespace <code>web</code> to access pods in namespace <code>db</code> on port 5432. Use <code>namespaceSelector</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">first, label the source namespace</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># First, label the source namespace:</span>
kubectl label namespace web purpose=web-tier

<span class="comment"># NetworkPolicy in the db namespace:</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
name: allow-web-to-db
namespace: db              # ← Applied in TARGET namespace
spec:
podSelector:
matchLabels:
app: postgres          # ← Protect postgres pods
policyTypes:
- Ingress
ingress:
- from:
- namespaceSelector:
matchLabels:
  purpose: web-tier  # ← Allow from "web" namespace
ports:
- protocol: TCP
port: 5432

<span class="comment"># Important: namespaceSelector uses NAMESPACE labels</span>
<span class="comment"># You must label the namespace first!</span>
kubectl get namespaces --show-labels | grep web</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Cross-namespace NetworkPolicies use <code>namespaceSelector</code> which matches on namespace labels (not names). You must label the source namespace first (<code>kubectl label ns web purpose=web-tier</code>). The policy goes in the TARGET namespace (where the pods being protected live). On the CKA, forgetting to label the namespace is a very common mistake. A <code>namespaceSelector</code> without a <code>podSelector</code> allows ALL pods from that namespace. Combine both to restrict to specific pods in specific namespaces.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>pathType: Prefix</code>, <code>pathType: Exact</code>, and <code>pathType: ImplementationSpecific</code> in an Ingress?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>pathType</th>
                                        <th>Matches</th>
                                        <th>Example</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>Prefix</code></td>
                                        <td>URL prefix (path segment level)</td>
                                        <td><code>/api</code> → matches <code>/api</code>, <code>/api/</code>, <code>/api/v1</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>Exact</code></td>
                                        <td>Exact URL match (case-sensitive)</td>
                                        <td><code>/api</code> → only <code>/api</code> (NOT <code>/api/</code>)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ImplementationSpecific</code></td>
                                        <td>Depends on IngressClass</td>
                                        <td>Controller-specific behavior</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">prefix matching rules</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Prefix matching rules:</span>
<span class="comment"># path: /api</span>
<span class="comment"># /api     → MATCH ✓</span>
<span class="comment"># /api/    → MATCH ✓</span>
<span class="comment"># /api/v1  → MATCH ✓</span>
<span class="comment"># /apis    → NO MATCH ✗ (not a segment boundary)</span>

<span class="comment"># Exam tip: pathType is REQUIRED since K8s 1.22</span>
<span class="comment"># Omitting it causes validation error</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>Prefix</code> matching works on path segments — <code>/api</code> matches <code>/api/v1</code> but NOT <code>/apis</code>. <code>Exact</code> matches the exact path only. <code>ImplementationSpecific</code> defers to the controller. On the CKA, almost always use <code>Prefix</code>. Know that <code>pathType</code> is mandatory (since v1.22) — omitting it fails validation. A trailing slash matters for <code>Exact</code> but not for <code>Prefix</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a NetworkPolicy that allows egress ONLY to DNS (port 53) and blocks all other outgoing traffic.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
name: allow-dns-only
namespace: restricted
spec:
podSelector:
matchLabels:
app: locked-down
policyTypes:
- Egress                  # ← Control outgoing traffic
egress:
- ports:
- protocol: UDP
port: 53              # ← DNS UDP
- protocol: TCP
port: 53              # ← DNS TCP (for large responses)
<span class="comment"># No "to:" selector = allow DNS to ANY destination</span>
<span class="comment"># (CoreDNS, external DNS servers, etc.)</span>

<span class="comment"># Result:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ DNS queries work (nslookup succeeds)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ curl google.com → blocked (port 80/443 not allowed)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Any non-DNS traffic → blocked</span>

<span class="comment"># Common mistake: forgetting TCP/53</span>
<span class="comment"># Some DNS queries use TCP (large responses, zone transfers)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Egress NetworkPolicies control outgoing traffic. Blocking all egress except DNS (port 53) is a common CKA scenario. Without the DNS exception, pods can't resolve ANY names (even internal Service names). Always include both UDP/53 and TCP/53. On the CKA, if a question says "block all egress but allow the pod to resolve DNS," this is the answer. Omitting the <code>to:</code> field means DNS is allowed to any destination (needed because CoreDNS pod IPs change).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An Ingress is created but traffic returns 404. The Service and pods are running. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check ingress resource</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check Ingress resource</span>
kubectl describe ingress my-ingress
<span class="comment"># Look for:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Default backend: &lt;default&gt;</span>
<span class="comment">#   Rules:</span>
<span class="comment">#     Host  Path  Backend</span>
<span class="comment">#     *     /app  app-svc:8080 (10.244.1.5:8080, 10.244.2.8:8080)</span>
<span class="comment">#                              ↑ Endpoints listed = good</span>
<span class="comment">#                 app-svc:8080 ()  ← Empty = broken!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify Service has endpoints</span>
kubectl get endpoints app-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME      ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># app-svc   10.244.1.5:8080,10.244.2.8:8080  ← Good</span>
<span class="comment"># app-svc   &lt;none&gt;  ← Labels don't match!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check Service selector matches pod labels</span>
kubectl get svc app-svc -o yaml | grep -A2 selector
kubectl get pods --show-labels | grep app

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check Service port matches Ingress backend port</span>
kubectl get svc app-svc
<span class="comment"># PORT(S): 80/TCP  ← Ingress says 8080, Service is 80!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check Ingress Controller logs</span>
kubectl logs -n ingress-nginx -l app.kubernetes.io/name=ingress-nginx

<span class="comment"># Common causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Service port mismatch (Ingress port ≠ Service port)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Service selector doesn't match pod labels</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Wrong namespace</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Wrong pathType causing no match</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The 404 on Ingress troubleshooting flow: (1) <code>kubectl describe ingress</code> to check rules and endpoints, (2) verify Service has endpoints, (3) match Service selector to pod labels, (4) verify port numbers match across Ingress → Service → Pod. On the CKA, the most common issues are: port mismatch (Ingress backend port must match Service port, not container port), wrong Service name, or labels not matching. Always use <code>describe</code> first — it shows the endpoint IPs right in the Ingress output.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you combine <code>podSelector</code> AND <code>namespaceSelector</code> in the same NetworkPolicy rule? What's the difference between putting them in the same item vs separate items?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">same item = and (both must match)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># SAME item = AND (both must match):</span>
ingress:
- from:
- namespaceSelector:        # ← Single rule
matchLabels:
env: production
podSelector:              # ← AND condition
matchLabels:
role: frontend
<span class="comment"># Allows: frontend pods IN production namespace</span>

<span class="comment"># SEPARATE items = OR (either matches):</span>
ingress:
- from:
- namespaceSelector:        # ← Rule 1
matchLabels:
env: production
- podSelector:              # ← Rule 2 (separate item)
matchLabels:
role: frontend
<span class="comment"># Allows: ANY pod from production namespace</span>
<span class="comment">#     OR: frontend pods from ANY namespace</span>
<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Much more permissive!</span>

<span class="comment"># The difference is the YAML array dash "-":</span>
<span class="comment"># No dash before podSelector = AND (combined)</span>
<span class="comment"># Dash before podSelector = OR (separate rule)</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA TRAP: One small dash changes everything!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is the #1 CKA NetworkPolicy trap. A single <code>from:</code> item with both selectors means AND (namespace AND pod labels). Two separate <code>from:</code> items means OR (either namespace OR pod labels). The difference is a single YAML dash (<code>-</code>). The OR version is MUCH more permissive — it allows any pod from the namespace, plus any matching pod from any namespace. On the CKA, read the YAML carefully: are the selectors in the same array item (AND) or different items (OR)?</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you add TLS termination to an Ingress? Show the complete steps from creating the TLS secret to the Ingress spec.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create tls secret from cert files</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create TLS secret from cert files:</span>
kubectl create secret tls my-tls-secret \
--cert=tls.crt \
--key=tls.key \
-n default

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Ingress with TLS:</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
name: secure-ingress
spec:
ingressClassName: nginx
tls:
- hosts:
- myapp.example.com      # ← Must match rules host
secretName: my-tls-secret # ← TLS secret name
rules:
- host: myapp.example.com  # ← Must match tls host
http:
paths:
- path: /
pathType: Prefix
backend:
  service:
    name: web-svc
    port:
      number: 80

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl describe ingress secure-ingress
<span class="comment"># TLS:</span>
<span class="comment">#   my-tls-secret terminates myapp.example.com</span>

<span class="comment"># Test:</span>
curl -k https://myapp.example.com
<span class="comment"># -k to skip cert verification in testing</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>TLS in Ingress has 3 components: (1) a TLS Secret of type <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> with <code>tls.crt</code> and <code>tls.key</code>, (2) the <code>spec.tls</code> section listing hosts and the secret name, (3) the <code>rules.host</code> must match the TLS host. On the CKA, use <code>kubectl create secret tls</code> (not generic!) — it validates the cert format. The Ingress Controller handles TLS termination: HTTPS outside, HTTP between controller and backend Service. Common mistake: host in <code>tls</code> doesn't match host in <code>rules</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

