        <!-- ==================== PART5 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part5-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 5 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Services &amp; Networking — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You can now move a packet end to end: from the flat pod network and its CNI, through the Service that gives pods a stable virtual IP, to CoreDNS that resolves names, to the Ingress and NetworkPolicies that route and firewall HTTP into easypj. This is the CKA’s 20% Services &amp; Networking domain.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 🟡 Part 5 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Network model (Ch 17):</strong> every pod gets a routable IP on a flat network; the CNI plugin implements it and kube-proxy programs Service routing (iptables/IPVS).</li>
                    <li><strong>Services (Ch 18):</strong> ClusterIP (internal) · NodePort (node:port) · LoadBalancer (cloud LB) · headless (per-pod DNS). A Service selects pods → Endpoints; no ready endpoints = no traffic.</li>
                    <li><strong>DNS (Ch 19):</strong> CoreDNS resolves <code>svc.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>; search domains + ndots explain short-name lookups; broken DNS is a top exam failure.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ingress &amp; NetworkPolicy (Ch 20):</strong> Ingress = L7 HTTP routing via a controller; NetworkPolicy = default-deny + allow rules to isolate tiers (e.g. only easypj-web → easypj-db).</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> 20% here — expose a Deployment, fix a Service with no endpoints, debug CoreDNS, and write a NetworkPolicy are recurring tasks.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 5 Visual Summary — browser → Ingress → Service → pod, with DNS &amp; policy</div>
<pre>
   🌐 browser ──▶ 🚪 Ingress (L7 host/path) ──▶ 🔀 Service (ClusterIP)
                                                   │ selector → Endpoints
                                                   ▼
                              📦 easypj pod  ◀── 🔎 CoreDNS resolves the name
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   🛡️ NetworkPolicy fences who may talk to whom (default-deny + allow).
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 5 connected the fleet. A pod gets a flat, routable IP (CNI); a Service gives a stable virtual IP and load-balances across the pods its selector matches (Endpoints); CoreDNS turns Service names into those IPs; and Ingress routes external HTTP in while NetworkPolicies decide which pods may talk.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> master the chain browser → Ingress → Service → Endpoints → pod, plus CoreDNS resolution and default-deny NetworkPolicies, and you own the 20% Services &amp; Networking domain — second only to Troubleshooting. Next: Part 6 keeps easypj’s data alive with persistent storage.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART6 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part6">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 6 · Storage</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Don’t Lose the Data</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 21–23 · CKA 10% · <em>"State that survives a pod"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Pods are ephemeral; data must not be. Part 6 covers the volume model and its lifecycle, the PV/PVC binding that decouples storage requests from storage supply, StorageClasses for dynamic provisioning, and how ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets inject configuration and credentials into easypj.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> attach emptyDir/hostPath volumes; statically and dynamically provision PVs via PVCs and StorageClasses; resize and reclaim volumes; and mount ConfigMaps/Secrets as env vars and files for easypj.</div>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Real-World Analogy &amp; Myth-Busting Table</h4>
