                <h4>📌 Chapter 21 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch22">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 22 · PersistentVolumes, PVCs &amp; StorageClasses</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">This is the <strong>most heavily tested storage topic</strong> on the CKA exam. The PV/PVC model separates storage provisioning (admin responsibility) from storage consumption (developer responsibility). You'll create PersistentVolumes, bind them with PersistentVolumeClaims, mount them into pods, and set up StorageClasses for automatic dynamic provisioning — all demonstrated with our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> PostgreSQL database setup.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">💾 PV/PVC</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The PV/PVC model — how admin provisioning and developer consumption are separated</li>
                    <li>✅ How to create PersistentVolumes with correct access modes and reclaim policies</li>
                    <li>✅ How to create PersistentVolumeClaims and understand binding rules</li>
                    <li>✅ How to mount PVCs into Pods and Deployments for persistent storage</li>
                    <li>✅ StorageClasses for dynamic provisioning — eliminate manual PV creation</li>
                    <li>✅ Complete PostgreSQL + easypj storage setup using volumeClaimTemplates</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Storage <span class="cka-weight">10%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>PV/PVC Is the #1 Storage Topic on the CKA</h5>
                <p>Expect at least 1-2 questions on PV/PVC creation and binding. Master these commands:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pv,pvc</code> — check PV and PVC status</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt;</code> — debug why a PVC is Pending</li>
                    <li>Know all three access modes: <code>ReadWriteOnce</code>, <code>ReadOnlyMany</code>, <code>ReadWriteMany</code></li>
                    <li>Know reclaim policies: <code>Retain</code> (keep data), <code>Delete</code> (destroy data)</li>
                    <li>Write PV + PVC YAML from scratch — the exam doesn't always have templates</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>PV + PVC + Pod — Complete Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">This 3-part pattern is the most tested storage question on the CKA. Write it from memory.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># 1. PersistentVolume (admin creates)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: my-pv                       <span class="comment"># cluster-scoped, no namespace</span>
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi                    # ◀── total capacity
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                    # ◀── RWO / ROX / RWX
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain  # ◀── Retain / Delete
  storageClassName: manual           # ◀── must match PVC
  hostPath:
    path: /mnt/data                  # ◀── node path (exam default)
---
<span class="comment"># 2. PersistentVolumeClaim (developer creates)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: my-pvc                      <span class="comment"># namespaced</span>
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                    # ◀── must match PV
  storageClassName: manual           # ◀── must match PV
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi                   # ◀── ≤ PV capacity
---
<span class="comment"># 3. Pod using PVC</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: my-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    volumeMounts:
    - name: data-vol
      mountPath: /data
  volumes:
  - name: data-vol
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: my-pvc              # ◀── reference PVC by name</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find PV/PVC YAML Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain pv.spec                        <span class="comment"># PV spec fields</span>
kubectl explain pv.spec.capacity               <span class="comment"># storage size</span>
kubectl explain pv.spec.accessModes            <span class="comment"># RWO/ROX/RWX</span>
kubectl explain pv.spec.persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy  <span class="comment"># reclaim policy</span>
kubectl explain pvc.spec                       <span class="comment"># PVC spec fields</span>
kubectl explain pvc.spec.resources.requests    <span class="comment"># request size</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="flow-diagram">
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🔧</span><span>Admin creates PV</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">👨‍💻</span><span>Dev creates PVC</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🔗</span><span>K8s binds PVC→PV</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">📦</span><span>Pod mounts PVC</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">✅</span><span>Data persists!</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">3</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Access Modes (RWO/ROX/RWX)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">2</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Reclaim Policies (Retain/Delete)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-purple">
                    <span class="metric-value">4</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">PV Statuses</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">1:1</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">PV↔PVC Binding</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.1 The PV/PVC Model — Separation of Concerns</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes storage uses a <strong>two-layer model</strong> to separate the "how" from the "what". The cluster admin provisions storage (PersistentVolume), and the developer requests storage (PersistentVolumeClaim). Kubernetes automatically binds matching PVs to PVCs based on size, access mode, and StorageClass.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The PV/PVC Model — Admin vs Developer Responsibilities</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  THE PV / PVC MODEL — SEPARATION OF CONCERNS                            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  🔧 CLUSTER ADMIN                    👨‍💻 DEVELOPER                       │
  │  "I provision storage"               "I request storage"                 │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────┐           ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │  │  PersistentVolume    │           │  PersistentVolume    │            │
  │  │  (PV)                │           │  Claim (PVC)         │            │
  │  │                      │           │                      │            │
  │  │  capacity: 10Gi      │           │  request: 5Gi        │            │
  │  │  accessModes:        │           │  accessModes:        │            │
  │  │    - ReadWriteOnce   │◀─── BIND ─▶│    - ReadWriteOnce   │            │
  │  │  storageClass: fast  │           │  storageClass: fast  │            │
  │  │  hostPath:           │           │                      │            │
  │  │    /data/postgres    │           │                      │            │
  │  └──────────────────────┘           └──────────┬───────────┘            │
  │                                                │                        │
  │  Admin doesn't need to know                    │ Developer doesn't      │
  │  WHICH pod will use it                         │ need to know WHERE     │
  │                                                │ the storage is         │
  │                                                ▼                        │
  │                                     ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │                                     │  Pod / Deployment    │            │
  │                                     │                      │            │
  │                                     │  volumes:            │            │
  │                                     │  - persistentVolume  │            │
  │                                     │    Claim:            │            │
  │                                     │    claimName: my-pvc │            │
  │                                     │                      │            │
  │                                     │  volumeMounts:       │            │
  │                                     │  - mountPath: /data  │            │
  │                                     └──────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
  │  BINDING RULES:                                                          │
  │  ✅ PVC.request ≤ PV.capacity        (size must fit)                    │
  │  ✅ PVC.accessModes ⊆ PV.accessModes (modes must match)                │
  │  ✅ PVC.storageClass = PV.storageClass (class must match)               │
  │  ❌ No match? → PVC stays "Pending" until a matching PV appears        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Admin Creates PV</h5>
                        <p>Defines capacity, access modes, reclaim policy, and points to actual storage (hostPath, NFS, cloud disk)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Developer Creates PVC</h5>
                        <p>Requests storage size and access mode. Doesn't need to know where storage lives.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Kubernetes Binds</h5>
                        <p>Finds a PV that matches the PVC's requirements. PVC status changes from Pending → Bound.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Pod Mounts PVC</h5>
                        <p>Pod references the PVC by name. The bound PV's storage appears at the specified mountPath.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Data Persists ✅</h5>
                        <p>Pod dies, restarts, gets rescheduled — PVC binding survives. New pod mounts same data.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔬 PV/PVC Status Lifecycle</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> Available</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">PV exists but no PVC has claimed it yet. It's ready to be bound.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔗</span> Bound</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">PV is bound to a PVC. One-to-one relationship. The PV is now in use.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔓</span> Released</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">PVC was deleted but PV still has data from the old claim. Admin must manually reclaim it (if Retain policy).</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">❌</span> Failed</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Automatic reclamation failed. PV needs manual intervention.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.2 Creating PersistentVolumes</h3>
                <p>A PersistentVolume is a <strong>cluster-level resource</strong> — it doesn't belong to any namespace. The admin creates it with a specific capacity, access mode, reclaim policy, and storage backend.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pv-hostpath.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PersistentVolume with hostPath (for local testing / CKA exam)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: easypj-data-pv           # ◀── cluster-scoped, no namespace
  labels:
    type: local
    app: easypj
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi                 # ◀── total storage this PV provides
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                 # ◀── can be mounted read-write by ONE node
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain    # ◀── keep data when PVC deleted
  storageClassName: manual        # ◀── must match PVC's storageClassName
  hostPath:
    path: /data/easypj-postgres   # ◀── directory on the node
    type: DirectoryOrCreate</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Access Mode</th>
                                <th>Abbreviation</th>
                                <th>Meaning</th>
                                <th>Typical Use</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ReadWriteOnce</code></td>
                                <td>RWO</td>
                                <td>Read-write by a <strong>single node</strong></td>
                                <td>Databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ReadOnlyMany</code></td>
                                <td>ROX</td>
                                <td>Read-only by <strong>multiple nodes</strong></td>
                                <td>Shared config, static assets</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ReadWriteMany</code></td>
                                <td>RWX</td>
                                <td>Read-write by <strong>multiple nodes</strong></td>
                                <td>Shared uploads, NFS-backed</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ReadWriteOncePod</code></td>
                                <td>RWOP</td>
                                <td>Read-write by a <strong>single pod</strong></td>
                                <td>Exclusive access (K8s 1.29+)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Access Modes Are About NODES, Not Pods</h5>
                    <p><code>ReadWriteOnce</code> means only <strong>one node</strong> can mount the volume for writing. Multiple pods on that same node CAN all mount it. This is a common CKA misconception. For true single-pod exclusivity, use <code>ReadWriteOncePod</code> (v1.29+).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Reclaim Policy</th>
                                <th>What Happens When PVC Deleted</th>
                                <th>Use When</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Retain</code></td>
                                <td>PV keeps data, status → Released. Admin must manually clean up and re-provision.</td>
                                <td>Production databases, critical data you can't lose</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Delete</code></td>
                                <td>PV AND underlying storage deleted automatically. Data is gone.</td>
                                <td>Dynamic provisioning, temporary/recreatable data</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Recycle</code></td>
                                <td>Runs <code>rm -rf</code> on the volume and makes it Available again. <strong>Deprecated.</strong></td>
                                <td>Don't use — deprecated since K8s 1.15</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pv-nfs.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PersistentVolume backed by NFS (multi-node shared storage)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: easypj-uploads-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 20Gi
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteMany                 # ◀── NFS supports multi-node read-write
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  storageClassName: nfs
  nfs:
    server: 192.168.1.100         # ◀── NFS server IP
    path: /exports/easypj-uploads # ◀── shared directory on NFS server</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.3 Creating PersistentVolumeClaims</h3>
                <p>A PVC is the developer's side of the equation — a <strong>request</strong> for storage. Unlike PVs (cluster-scoped), PVCs are <strong>namespaced</strong>. When you create a PVC, Kubernetes searches for a PV that meets the requirements. If found, the PVC binds to it. If not, the PVC stays in <code>Pending</code> state.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pvc-postgres.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PVC requesting storage for easypj PostgreSQL database</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: easypj-postgres-pvc      # ◀── namespaced resource
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                 # ◀── must match PV's access modes
  storageClassName: manual        # ◀── must match PV's storageClassName
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi                # ◀── must be ≤ PV's capacity (10Gi)
<span class="comment"># Binding: K8s finds easypj-data-pv (10Gi, RWO, manual) → MATCH → Bound!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">PVC Binding Logic — How Kubernetes Matches PVCs to PVs</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  PVC BINDING DECISION TREE                                               │
  │                                                                          │
  │  New PVC created: requests 5Gi, RWO, storageClass: "manual"             │
  │       │                                                                  │
  │       ▼                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                           │
  │  │  Step 1: Filter by storageClassName       │                           │
  │  │  Looking for PVs with class = "manual"    │                           │
  │  └────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘                           │
  │                   │                                                      │
  │       ┌───────────┴───────────┐                                         │
  │       ▼                       ▼                                         │
  │  ┌─────────┐            ┌─────────┐                                    │
  │  │ PV-A    │            │ PV-B    │                                    │
  │  │ 10Gi    │            │ 5Gi     │                                    │
  │  │ manual  │ ✅          │ fast    │ ❌ wrong class                     │
  │  └────┬────┘            └─────────┘                                    │
  │       │                                                                  │
  │       ▼                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                           │
  │  │  Step 2: Filter by accessModes            │                           │
  │  │  PVC needs RWO → PV-A has RWO?           │                           │
  │  │  YES ✅                                   │                           │
  │  └────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘                           │
  │                   │                                                      │
  │       ▼                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                           │
  │  │  Step 3: Check capacity                   │                           │
  │  │  PVC requests 5Gi ≤ PV-A has 10Gi?       │                           │
  │  │  YES ✅                                   │                           │
  │  └────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘                           │
  │                   │                                                      │
  │       ▼                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                           │
  │  │  ✅ BOUND!                                │                           │
  │  │  PVC → PV-A                               │                           │
  │  │  PVC gets full 10Gi (even though it       │                           │
  │  │  only asked for 5Gi — smallest match)     │                           │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────┘                           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Debugging: PVC Stuck in Pending</h5>
                    <p>If a PVC shows <code>Pending</code> status, run <code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt;</code> and check the Events section. Common causes:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>No PV with matching <code>storageClassName</code></li>
                        <li>No PV with sufficient capacity</li>
                        <li>No PV with matching access modes</li>
                        <li>All matching PVs are already <code>Bound</code> to other PVCs</li>
                        <li>If using dynamic provisioning: the StorageClass provisioner is not installed</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Verify PV/PVC binding</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check PV and PVC status</span>
kubectl get pv
<span class="comment"># NAME              CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   RECLAIM POLICY   STATUS   CLAIM</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-data-pv    10Gi       RWO            Retain           Bound    easypj-production/easypj-postgres-pvc</span>

kubectl get pvc -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># NAME                   STATUS   VOLUME            CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-postgres-pvc    Bound    easypj-data-pv    10Gi       RWO</span>

<span class="comment"># Debug a Pending PVC</span>
kubectl describe pvc easypj-postgres-pvc -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  ProvisioningFailed  no persistent volumes available for this claim</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.4 Using PVCs in Pods</h3>
                <p>Once a PVC is Bound, you reference it in your Pod spec exactly like any other volume. The pod doesn't know or care whether the PVC is backed by a local disk, NFS, or cloud storage — it just mounts a directory.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deployment-with-pvc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj Deployment using PVC for PostgreSQL data</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        volumeMounts:
        - name: user-uploads          # ◀── mount name (matches volumes[])
          mountPath: /app/media       # ◀── path INSIDE the container
        - name: static-files
          mountPath: /app/staticfiles
          readOnly: true
      volumes:
      - name: user-uploads            # ◀── references the PVC
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: easypj-uploads-pvc   # ◀── name of the PVC
      - name: static-files
        configMap:
          name: easypj-static-config</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete Flow: PV → PVC → Pod → Container Mount</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  COMPLETE MOUNT FLOW                                                     │
  │                                                                          │
  │  PersistentVolume          PVC                   Pod                     │
  │  ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐       │
  │  │ easypj-pv    │    │ easypj-pvc   │    │ easypj-web-abc       │       │
  │  │              │    │              │    │                      │       │
  │  │ /data/uploads│◀──▶│ BOUND        │◀───│ volumes:             │       │
  │  │ on node disk │    │              │    │ - pvc: easypj-pvc   │       │
  │  │              │    │              │    │                      │       │
  │  │ 10Gi, RWO    │    │ 5Gi request  │    │ containers:          │       │
  │  └──────────────┘    └──────────────┘    │ - volumeMounts:      │       │
  │                                          │   - /app/media ◀─┐  │       │
  │                                          └──────────────────┼──┘       │
  │                                                             │           │
  │  What the container sees:                                   │           │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐                       │           │
  │  │  / (container root)              │                       │           │
  │  │  ├── app/                        │                       │           │
  │  │  │   ├── manage.py               │                       │           │
  │  │  │   ├── easypj/                 │                       │           │
  │  │  │   └── media/ ◀───────────────────── PVC mounted here  │           │
  │  │  │       ├── resumes/            │                       │           │
  │  │  │       │   └── john_doe.pdf    │   Data on external    │           │
  │  │  │       └── photos/             │   storage, not in     │           │
  │  │  │           └── profile.jpg     │   the container!      │           │
  │  │  └── ...                         │                       │           │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────┘                       │           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 PVC Must Be in the Same Namespace as the Pod</h5>
                    <p>PVCs are namespaced resources. A pod in <code>easypj-production</code> can only mount PVCs that also exist in <code>easypj-production</code>. PVs themselves are cluster-scoped, but the PVC (the claim) must be co-located with the pod.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.5 StorageClasses — Dynamic Provisioning</h3>
                <p>StorageClasses eliminate the need for an admin to pre-create PVs. When a developer creates a PVC with a <code>storageClassName</code>, and that class has a <strong>provisioner</strong>, Kubernetes automatically creates a matching PV on the fly. This is how most production clusters work.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Static vs Dynamic Provisioning</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STATIC PROVISIONING (manual)        DYNAMIC PROVISIONING (automatic)    │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐            ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │  │ 1. Admin creates PV │            │ 1. Admin creates     │            │
  │  │    (10Gi, RWO, host)│            │    StorageClass      │            │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘            │    (provisioner,     │            │
  │            │                        │     params)          │            │
  │            ▼                        └──────────┬───────────┘            │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐                       │  (one-time setup)      │
  │  │ 2. Dev creates PVC  │                       │                        │
  │  │    (5Gi, RWO)       │                       ▼                        │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘            ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │            │                        │ 2. Dev creates PVC   │            │
  │            ▼                        │    with storageClass │            │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐            └──────────┬───────────┘            │
  │  │ 3. K8s binds        │                       │                        │
  │  │    PVC → PV         │                       ▼                        │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘            ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │            │                        │ 3. StorageClass auto │            │
  │            ▼                        │    creates PV! 🎉    │            │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐            └──────────┬───────────┘            │
  │  │ 4. Pod mounts PVC   │                       │                        │
  │  └─────────────────────┘                       ▼                        │
  │                                     ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │  Every new PVC = admin must         │ 4. Auto-bind + mount │            │
  │  manually create a PV first 😰     └──────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                          │
  │                                     Admin sets up ONCE.                  │
  │                                     Every new PVC = auto PV! 🎉        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">storageclass.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># StorageClass for dynamic provisioning</span>
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-ssd                      # ◀── the name PVCs reference
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner  # ◀── for local testing
#provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com         # ◀── AWS EBS

#provisioner: pd.csi.storage.gke.io   # ◀── GCE Persistent Disk

#provisioner: disk.csi.azure.com      # ◀── Azure Disk

reclaimPolicy: Delete                  # ◀── auto-delete PV when PVC deleted
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer  # ◀── delay binding until pod scheduled
allowVolumeExpansion: true             # ◀── allow PVC resize</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pvc-dynamic.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PVC using dynamic provisioning — no PV needed, auto-created!</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: easypj-dynamic-pvc
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: fast-ssd          # ◀── references StorageClass above
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi
<span class="comment"># Result: StorageClass provisioner creates a 10Gi PV automatically</span>
<span class="comment"># PVC binds to the new PV → Ready to use in pods!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Static Provisioning</th>
                                <th>Dynamic Provisioning</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>PV creation</td>
                                <td>Manual by admin</td>
                                <td>Automatic by StorageClass</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Admin workload</td>
                                <td>High — create PV per request</td>
                                <td>Low — one-time StorageClass setup</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Storage waste</td>
                                <td>PVs may sit unused</td>
                                <td>PV created on-demand, no waste</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>CKA exam</td>
                                <td>✅ Tested (manual PV+PVC)</td>
                                <td>✅ Tested (StorageClass config)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Production use</td>
                                <td>Rare (legacy/special cases)</td>
                                <td>Standard practice in most clusters</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Default StorageClass</h5>
                    <p>Most clusters have a <strong>default StorageClass</strong>. If you create a PVC without specifying <code>storageClassName</code>, the default class is used automatically. Check with <code>kubectl get storageclass</code> — the default has <code>(default)</code> annotation. On the CKA exam, there's usually a default class already configured.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.6 PostgreSQL Storage for easypj</h3>
                <p>Let's put everything together: a <strong>complete production storage setup</strong> for PostgreSQL backing our easypj/jobpost Django application. This uses a StorageClass, PVC via <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> in a StatefulSet, and proper data directory mounting.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete easypj PostgreSQL Storage Architecture</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  easypj POSTGRESQL STORAGE — COMPLETE SETUP                              │
  │                                                                          │
  │  StorageClass: "postgres-storage"                                        │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────┐                                      │
  │  │  provisioner: local/hostPath   │                                      │
  │  │  reclaimPolicy: Retain         │ ◀── never delete DB data            │
  │  │  volumeBindingMode: WFFC       │                                      │
  │  └──────────────┬─────────────────┘                                      │
  │                 │ creates PV automatically                               │
  │                 ▼                                                         │
  │  PersistentVolumeClaim: "postgres-data-postgres-0"                       │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────┐                                      │
  │  │  10Gi, ReadWriteOnce           │                                      │
  │  │  storageClass: postgres-storage│                                      │
  │  │  STATUS: Bound ✅              │                                      │
  │  └──────────────┬─────────────────┘                                      │
  │                 │ mounted into                                           │
  │                 ▼                                                         │
  │  StatefulSet: "postgres"                                                 │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐             │
  │  │  Pod: postgres-0                                       │             │
  │  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │             │
  │  │  │  Container: postgres                              │  │             │
  │  │  │  image: postgres:15                               │  │             │
  │  │  │                                                    │  │             │
  │  │  │  /var/lib/postgresql/data  ◀── PVC mounted here   │  │             │
  │  │  │  ├── base/        (databases)                     │  │             │
  │  │  │  ├── global/      (cluster-wide tables)           │  │             │
  │  │  │  ├── pg_wal/      (write-ahead logs)              │  │             │
  │  │  │  └── postgresql.conf                              │  │             │
  │  │  │                                                    │  │             │
  │  │  │  Data survives:                                    │  │             │
  │  │  │  ✅ Container restart                              │  │             │
  │  │  │  ✅ Pod deletion                                   │  │             │
  │  │  │  ✅ StatefulSet scaling                            │  │             │
  │  │  │  ✅ Rolling updates                                │  │             │
  │  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │             │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-postgres-complete.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ── 1. StorageClass ──
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: postgres-storage
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner
reclaimPolicy: Retain                 # ◀── NEVER auto-delete DB data
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
---
<span class="comment"># ── 2. PersistentVolume (for local/exam clusters) ──</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: postgres-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  storageClassName: postgres-storage
  hostPath:
    path: /data/postgres
    type: DirectoryOrCreate
---
<span class="comment"># ── 3. StatefulSet with volumeClaimTemplates ──</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: postgres
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  serviceName: postgres               # ◀── headless service name
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: postgres
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: postgres
        image: postgres:15
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5432
        env:
        - name: POSTGRES_DB
          value: easypj_db
        - name: POSTGRES_USER
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: postgres-credentials
              key: username
        - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: postgres-credentials
              key: password
        - name: PGDATA                 # ◀── PostgreSQL data subdirectory
          value: /var/lib/postgresql/data/pgdata
        volumeMounts:
        - name: postgres-data          # ◀── matches volumeClaimTemplates
          mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 250m
            memory: 256Mi
          limits:
            cpu: "1"
            memory: 1Gi
  volumeClaimTemplates:                # ◀── auto-creates PVC per replica
  - metadata:
      name: postgres-data              # ◀── PVC name = postgres-data-postgres-0
    spec:
      accessModes:
      - ReadWriteOnce
      storageClassName: postgres-storage
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 10Gi
---
<span class="comment"># ── 4. Headless Service for StatefulSet ──</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  clusterIP: None                      # ◀── headless = direct pod DNS
  selector:
    app: postgres
  ports:
  - port: 5432
<span class="comment"># DNS: postgres-0.postgres.easypj-production.svc.cluster.local</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Verify the complete setup</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply the complete storage stack</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-postgres-complete.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify StorageClass</span>
kubectl get storageclass postgres-storage
<span class="comment"># NAME               PROVISIONER                    RECLAIMPOLICY</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-storage   kubernetes.io/no-provisioner   Retain</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify PV is bound</span>
kubectl get pv postgres-pv
<span class="comment"># NAME          CAPACITY   ACCESS   STATUS   CLAIM</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-pv   10Gi       RWO      Bound    easypj-production/postgres-data-postgres-0</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify PVC is bound</span>
kubectl get pvc -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># NAME                       STATUS   VOLUME        CAPACITY</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-data-postgres-0   Bound    postgres-pv   10Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify pod is running with mounted volume</span>
kubectl describe pod postgres-0 -n easypj-production | grep -A5 "Mounts:"
<span class="comment">#     Mounts:</span>
<span class="comment">#       /var/lib/postgresql/data from postgres-data (rw)</span>

<span class="comment"># Test data persistence: kill the pod, check data survives</span>
kubectl delete pod postgres-0 -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># StatefulSet recreates postgres-0 → PVC re-mounts → data intact! ✅</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🏋️ CKA Practice Scenario: Create PV and PVC from Scratch</h5>
                    <div class="scenario-content">
                        <p><strong>Task:</strong> Create a PersistentVolume named <code>exam-pv</code> with 2Gi capacity, <code>ReadWriteOnce</code>, storageClass <code>manual</code>, backed by hostPath <code>/mnt/data</code>. Then create a PVC named <code>exam-pvc</code> requesting 1Gi from that PV. Verify binding.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header">
                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                                <span class="code-lang">CKA Solution</span>
                            </div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create PV</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: exam-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 2Gi
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: manual
  hostPath:
    path: /mnt/data
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create PVC</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: exam-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: manual
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify binding</span>
kubectl get pv exam-pv
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Bound   CLAIM: default/exam-pvc ✅</span>

kubectl get pvc exam-pvc
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Bound   VOLUME: exam-pv ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Chapter 22 PV Lifecycle State Machine -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔄 PersistentVolume State Machine — Complete Lifecycle</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  PV LIFECYCLE STATE MACHINE                                              │
  │                                                                          │
  │                   ┌──────────────┐                                       │
  │                   │  PV Created  │                                       │
  │                   │  by Admin    │                                       │
  │                   └──────┬───────┘                                       │
  │                          │                                               │
  │                          ▼                                               │
  │               ┌──────────────────┐                                       │
  │         ┌────▶│   AVAILABLE ☁️    │ No PVC has claimed this PV           │
  │         │     └────────┬─────────┘                                       │
  │         │              │ PVC created with matching criteria               │
  │         │              ▼                                                  │
  │         │     ┌──────────────────┐                                       │
  │         │     │    BOUND 🔗      │ PV bound to PVC (1:1 relationship)    │
  │         │     └────────┬─────────┘                                       │
  │         │              │ PVC deleted                                      │
  │         │              ▼                                                  │
  │         │     ┌──────────────────┐                                       │
  │         │     │   RELEASED 🔓   │ PV still has old data                  │
  │         │     └────────┬─────────┘                                       │
  │         │              │                                                  │
  │         │    ┌─────────┴─────────┐                                       │
  │         │    │                   │                                        │
  │         │    ▼                   ▼                                        │
  │         │  Retain policy      Delete policy                              │
  │         │  ┌────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐                           │
  │         │  │Admin must  │     │PV + storage  │                           │
  │         │  │clean up PV │     │deleted auto  │                           │
  │         │  │manually    │     │💀 Data gone! │                           │
  │         │  └─────┬──────┘     └──────────────┘                           │
  │         │        │ kubectl delete pv + recreate                           │
  │         └────────┘                                                       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ❌ FAILED: Auto-reclaim failed → admin must fix manually               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Storage Capacity Planning Card -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>📊 Storage Capacity Planning — easypj</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>PostgreSQL data:</strong> 10Gi (grows ~1Gi/month with 10K job posts)</li>
                                <li><strong>User uploads (resumes):</strong> 20Gi (avg 200KB × 100K files)</li>
                                <li><strong>Static files (CSS/JS):</strong> emptyDir (regenerated, ~50MB)</li>
                                <li><strong>Redis cache:</strong> emptyDir with <code>medium: Memory</code> (256Mi)</li>
                                <li><strong>Logs:</strong> emptyDir (100Mi, forwarded to log aggregator)</li>
                                <li><strong>Total persistent storage needed:</strong> ~30Gi</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Time-Saver: PVC Expansion</h5>
                    <p>If a PVC needs more storage and the StorageClass has <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code>, you can simply <code>kubectl edit pvc &lt;name&gt;</code> and increase <code>requests.storage</code>. The PV resizes automatically (for cloud-backed storage). On the CKA exam, check if expansion is enabled: <code>kubectl get sc &lt;name&gt; -o yaml | grep allowVolumeExpansion</code>.</p>
                </div>

            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How PVs and PVCs Bind: The Matching Algorithm, Access Modes, Dynamic Provisioning, and CSI Driver Architecture</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ PV-PVC Binding Algorithm — How Kubernetes Matches Claims to Volumes</h5>
            <p>The persistent volume controller runs a matching loop that pairs PVCs with PVs. Understanding the exact matching criteria helps you debug "PVC stuck in Pending" issues.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  PV-PVC BINDING ALGORITHM — Step by Step                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  PVC: easypj-media (requests: storage=10Gi, accessModes=[RWO])               │
│  Available PVs:                                                               │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PV-1: 100Gi, RWO, Retain    PV-2: 10Gi, RWO, Delete                │    │
│  │ PV-3: 10Gi, RWX, Retain     PV-4: 5Gi,  RWO, Retain                │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: FILTER by storage size (PVC requests 10Gi)                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PV-1: 100Gi >= 10Gi ✅     PV-2: 10Gi >= 10Gi ✅                    │    │
│  │ PV-3: 10Gi >= 10Gi ✅     PV-4: 5Gi >= 10Gi  ❌ (too small)        │    │
│  │ Remaining: PV-1, PV-2, PV-3                                          │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: FILTER by access mode (PVC requests RWO)                             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PV-1: RWO ✅              PV-2: RWO ✅                               │    │
│  │ PV-3: RWX ✅ (RWX includes RWO capability)                           │    │
│  │ Remaining: PV-1, PV-2, PV-3                                          │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ ACCESS MODE COMPATIBILITY:                                            │    │
│  │ ┌──────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│  │ │ RWO      │ ReadWriteOnce — single node read/write             │    │    │
│  │ │ ROX      │ ReadOnlyMany — many nodes read only               │    │    │
│  │ │ RWX      │ ReadWriteMany — many nodes read/write              │    │    │
│  │ │ RWOP     │ ReadWriteOncePod — single pod (new in 1.29)        │    │    │
│  │ └──────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: SELECT best match (smallest sufficient PV wins)                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PV-1: 100Gi → EXCESSIVE (wastes 90Gi)                               │    │
│  │ PV-2: 10Gi  → PERFECT MATCH 🏆 (exact size)                        │    │
│  │ PV-3: 10Gi  → Also matches, but PV-2 selected first                 │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ SELECTION RULES:                                                     │    │
│  │ 1. Smallest PV >= PVC request wins                                   │    │
│  │ 2. If same size, PV created earliest wins                            │    │
│  │ 3. Binding is EXCLUSIVE — one PV = one PVC                           │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  AFTER BINDING:                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PVC status: Bound → PV-2                                              │    │
│  │ PV status:   Bound → Claim: default/easypj-media                     │    │
│  │ PV-2 is now UNAVAILABLE for any other PVC                             │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  STORAGECLASS — Dynamic Provisioning:                                        │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Without StorageClass: Admin creates PV manually → PVC binds          │    │
│  │ With StorageClass:     PVC specifies StorageClass → provisioner      │    │
│  │                        AUTO-CREATES PV → PVC binds automatically     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  CSI (Container Storage Interface) Drivers:                          │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ 1. PVC created with storageClassName: aws-ebs               │     │    │
│  │  │ 2. StorageClass provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com                │     │    │
│  │  │ 3. CSI provisioner calls AWS API: CreateVolume(10Gi)        │     │    │
│  │  │ 4. AWS creates EBS volume → returns volumeID                 │     │    │
│  │  │ 5. CSI creates PV object with volumeHandle=&lt;volumeID&gt;       │     │    │
│  │  │ 6. PV binds to PVC                                             │     │    │
│  │  │ 7. Pod created → CSI node plugin ATTACHES volume to node    │     │    │
│  │  │ 8. CSI node plugin MOUNTS volume into container              │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  RECLAIM POLICIES — What Happens When PVC Is Deleted:                        │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Retain:  PV stays (Released). Admin must MANUALLY delete + clean    │    │
│  │          up underlying storage. Data persists.                       │    │
│  │ Delete:  PV + underlying storage DELETED automatically. Data gone.  │    │
│  │ Recycle: DEPRECATED. Was: scrub data (rm -rf) → make Available.    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> PVC stuck in Pending? Check: (1) <code>kubectl describe pvc</code> — what's the error? "no persistent volumes available" = create a PV or StorageClass. (2) <code>kubectl get pv</code> — any Available PVs with sufficient capacity and matching access mode? (3) StorageClass with <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> delays binding until a pod uses the PVC — this is NORMAL (pod must be scheduled first for topology-aware provisioning).
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">PVC size must exactly match PV size</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">PVC request must be ≤ PV capacity — K8s picks the smallest matching PV</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">ReadWriteOnce means only one pod can mount it</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">RWO means one NODE. Multiple pods on same node can all mount it</span></li>
                    <li><code>storageClassName</code> must match EXACTLY between PV and PVC — empty string ≠ omitted</li>
                    <li>PV is <strong>cluster-scoped</strong> but PVC is <strong>namespaced</strong> — pod and PVC must be in same namespace</li>
                    <li><code>Recycle</code> reclaim policy is <strong>deprecated</strong> — use <code>Retain</code> or <code>Delete</code></li>
                    <li>After PVC deletion with <code>Retain</code> policy, PV stays <code>Released</code> — must delete and recreate PV to reuse</li>
                </ul>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>PVC stuck in <code>Pending</code></strong> — Top 3 causes: (1) No PV matches the requested size/accessMode, (2) StorageClass name mismatch, (3) No default StorageClass and PVC doesn't specify one. Debug with <code>kubectl describe pvc</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Access modes must match</strong> — PV offers <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> but PVC requests <code>ReadWriteMany</code>? Won't bind. Know the modes: <code>RWO</code> (single node), <code>ROX</code> (many nodes read-only), <code>RWX</code> (many nodes read-write)</li>
                        <li><strong>Reclaim policies</strong> — <code>Retain</code>: PV data preserved after PVC deletion (manual cleanup). <code>Delete</code>: PV and backing storage removed. <code>Recycle</code>: deprecated. Default for dynamic PVs is <code>Delete</code> — dangerous for production!</li>
                        <li><strong><code>storageClassName: ""</code> vs omitting it</strong> — Empty string means "no StorageClass" (only matches PVs with no class). Omitting it uses the cluster's default StorageClass. This subtle difference is a classic CKA trap</li>
                        <li><strong>PV is cluster-scoped, PVC is namespace-scoped</strong> — <code>kubectl get pv</code> (no -n flag needed). <code>kubectl get pvc -n &lt;ns&gt;</code>. A PVC in namespace <code>prod</code> can bind to any available PV</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — PV/PVC binding checklist (memorize this):</strong><br>
                For a PVC to bind to a PV, ALL must match:<br>
                ✅ Sufficient capacity (PV size ≥ PVC request)<br>
                ✅ Compatible access mode (at least one in common)<br>
                ✅ Matching StorageClass (or both empty)<br>
                ✅ PV status must be <code>Available</code> (not <code>Released</code> or <code>Bound</code>)<br>
                ✅ Label selectors match (if PVC uses <code>selector</code>)</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 22)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>list all PVs (cluster-wide)</td><td><code>kubectl get pv</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List PVCs in namespace</td><td><code>kubectl get pvc -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>list PVCs across all namespaces</td><td><code>kubectl get pvc -A</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>debug binding issues</td><td><code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>list StorageClasses</td><td><code>kubectl get sc</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>change reclaim policy</td><td><code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{"spec":{"persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy":"Retain"}}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>find provisioning errors</td><td><code>kubectl get events --field-selector reason=ProvisioningFailed</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check PV details</td><td><code>kubectl describe pv &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Delete PV</td><td><code>kubectl delete pv &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>PVs sorted by size</td><td><code>kubectl get pv --sort-by=.spec.capacity.storage</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>unstick a terminating PVC (last resort)</td><td><code>kubectl delete pvc &lt;name&gt; --grace-period=0 --force</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 8 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a PV: 5Gi, <code>ReadWriteOnce</code>, <code>hostPath: /mnt/data</code>, reclaim policy <code>Retain</code></li>
                    <li>2. Create a PVC requesting 3Gi with <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> — verify it binds</li>
                    <li>3. Create a pod that mounts the PVC at <code>/data</code></li>
                    <li>4. Write a file, delete the pod, recreate it — verify data persists</li>
                    <li>5. Delete the PVC — check PV status (should be <code>Released</code>, not <code>Available</code>)</li>
                    <li>6. Create a StorageClass with <code>provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner</code> and <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 22 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 22 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the PV/PVC binding lifecycle — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a PersistentVolume with 10Gi capacity, <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> access mode, <code>hostPath</code> at <code>/mnt/data</code>, and reclaim policy <code>Retain</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
name: pv-data
<span class="comment"># Note: PVs are cluster-scoped — no namespace!</span>
spec:
capacity:
storage: 10Gi
accessModes:
- ReadWriteOnce            # ← Single node read-write
persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain   # ← Keep data after PVC deletion
hostPath:
path: /mnt/data
type: DirectoryOrCreate

<span class="comment"># Apply and verify:</span>
kubectl apply -f pv.yaml
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># NAME     CAPACITY  ACCESS MODES  RECLAIM POLICY  STATUS     AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-data  10Gi      RWO           Retain          Available  5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Access mode abbreviations:</span>
<span class="comment"># RWO = ReadWriteOnce (single node)</span>
<span class="comment"># ROX = ReadOnlyMany  (many nodes, read-only)</span>
<span class="comment"># RWX = ReadWriteMany (many nodes, read-write)</span>
<span class="comment"># RWOP = ReadWriteOncePod (single pod, K8s 1.22+)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PersistentVolumes are cluster-scoped resources (no namespace). Key fields: <code>capacity</code> (must match/exceed PVC request), <code>accessModes</code> (must be compatible), <code>persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy</code> (what happens when PVC is deleted). <code>Retain</code> preserves data for manual cleanup, <code>Delete</code> removes backing storage. On the CKA, <code>hostPath</code> is the most common PV type (lab environments). Always use <code>Retain</code> for important data — the default for dynamically provisioned PVs is <code>Delete</code>!</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a PVC that binds to the PV from Q1. Then create a pod that mounts it at <code>/data</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pvc</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PVC:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
name: pvc-data
namespace: default         # ← PVCs are namespace-scoped!
spec:
accessModes:
- ReadWriteOnce            # ← Must match PV
resources:
requests:
  storage: 5Gi           # ← Can be ≤ PV capacity (10Gi)
<span class="comment"># storageClassName: ""     # ← Omit for static binding</span>

---
<span class="comment"># Pod using the PVC:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: data-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: data-vol
persistentVolumeClaim:
  claimName: pvc-data    # ← Reference the PVC name
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: data-vol
  mountPath: /data

<span class="comment"># Verify binding:</span>
kubectl get pvc pvc-data
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS  VOLUME   CAPACITY  ACCESS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pvc-data   Bound   pv-data  10Gi      RWO     5s</span>

kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Bound  ← PV is now bound to pvc-data</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PVC binding rules: PVC request ≤ PV capacity, access modes compatible, StorageClass matches. Once bound, the PVC gets the FULL PV capacity (5Gi requested but 10Gi available). The pod references the PVC by name in <code>persistentVolumeClaim.claimName</code>. On the CKA, the three-object chain is: PV (admin creates) → PVC (developer creates) → Pod (developer mounts PVC). If the PVC stays <code>Pending</code>, check: size, access mode, and StorageClass matching.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A PVC is stuck in <code>Pending</code> state. How do you diagnose the issue?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">describe the pvc for events</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Describe the PVC for events</span>
kubectl describe pvc stuck-pvc
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   "no persistent volumes available for this claim"</span>
<span class="comment">#   OR "storageclass 'fast' not found"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check available PVs</span>
kubectl get pv
<span class="comment"># NAME     CAPACITY  ACCESS  RECLAIM  STATUS      CLASS    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-1     5Gi       RWO     Retain   Available   slow     5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-2     10Gi      ROX     Delete   Available   fast     5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-3     5Gi       RWO     Retain   Bound       -        5m  ← Already taken!</span>

<span class="comment"># Common causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. No PV with enough capacity</span>
kubectl get pvc stuck-pvc -o yaml | grep storage
<span class="comment">#   requests: storage: 20Gi  ← But no PV ≥ 20Gi!</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Access mode mismatch</span>
<span class="comment"># PVC requests RWX but PV only offers RWO</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. StorageClass mismatch</span>
kubectl get pvc stuck-pvc -o yaml | grep storageClassName
<span class="comment"># storageClassName: "premium"  ← No PV with class "premium"!</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. No default StorageClass</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># No default → PVC with no class specified stays Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. WaitForFirstConsumer binding mode</span>
<span class="comment"># PVC stays Pending until a pod references it — this is NORMAL!</span>
kubectl get sc local-storage -o yaml | grep volumeBindingMode
<span class="comment"># volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PVC <code>Pending</code> is the most common PV troubleshooting scenario on the CKA. Systematic approach: (1) <code>describe</code> the PVC for error events, (2) check available PVs for capacity/access/class, (3) verify StorageClass exists. Note that <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> is NOT a bug — the PVC intentionally stays Pending until a pod is created, so the PV can be provisioned on the same node as the pod. Always start with <code>kubectl describe pvc</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to a PV when its PVC is deleted? Explain all reclaim policies.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Policy</th>
                                        <th>PV Status After PVC Delete</th>
                                        <th>Data</th>
                                        <th>Next Steps</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>Retain</code></td>
                                        <td><code>Released</code></td>
                                        <td>Preserved</td>
                                        <td>Manual cleanup, edit PV to remove claimRef</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>Delete</code></td>
                                        <td>PV deleted</td>
                                        <td>Deleted</td>
                                        <td>Nothing — PV and data are gone</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>Recycle</code></td>
                                        <td><code>Available</code></td>
                                        <td>Scrubbed (rm -rf)</td>
                                        <td>Deprecated — don't use</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">to reuse a retained pv after pvc deletion</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># To reuse a Retained PV after PVC deletion:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. PV shows "Released" — can't bind a new PVC yet</span>
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Released</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Remove the old claimRef:</span>
kubectl patch pv pv-data -p '{"spec":{"claimRef":null}}'
<span class="comment"># OR:</span>
kubectl edit pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># Delete the entire claimRef section</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. PV returns to Available:</span>
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Available ← Ready for new PVC</span>

<span class="comment"># Change reclaim policy on existing PV:</span>
kubectl patch pv pv-data -p \
'{"spec":{"persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy":"Retain"}}'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Reclaim policy controls PV fate after PVC deletion. <code>Retain</code> is safest — data preserved, PV goes to <code>Released</code> state (NOT <code>Available</code>). To reuse a <code>Released</code> PV, you must remove the <code>claimRef</code>. <code>Delete</code> removes both PV and backing storage (default for dynamic provisioning — dangerous!). On the CKA, changing policy from <code>Delete</code> to <code>Retain</code> with <code>kubectl patch</code> is a common task. Know the <code>Released</code> → <code>Available</code> transition requires manual intervention.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a StorageClass with <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code>. Why is this important?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
name: local-storage
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner  # ← Manual provisioning
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer    # ← KEY!
reclaimPolicy: Retain

<span class="comment"># What it does:</span>
<span class="comment"># Immediate (default):</span>
<span class="comment">#   PVC → PV binding happens immediately</span>
<span class="comment">#   PV might be on node-1</span>
<span class="comment">#   Pod gets scheduled to node-2</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Pod stuck Pending! PV is on wrong node!</span>

<span class="comment"># WaitForFirstConsumer:</span>
<span class="comment">#   PVC → Stays Pending (normal!)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Pod created → scheduler picks node-2</span>
<span class="comment">#   PV binding happens on node-2</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Pod starts successfully!</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get sc local-storage
<span class="comment"># NAME            PROVISIONER                    RECLAIMPOLICY  VOLUMEBINDINGMODE</span>
<span class="comment"># local-storage   kubernetes.io/no-provisioner   Retain         WaitForFirstConsumer</span>

<span class="comment"># PVC with this StorageClass:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
name: local-pvc
spec:
storageClassName: local-storage
accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
resources:
requests:
  storage: 5Gi
<span class="comment"># This PVC stays Pending until a pod uses it!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> delays PV binding until a pod using the PVC is scheduled. This is critical for local storage (hostPath, local PVs) to ensure the PV is on the same node as the pod. Without it, the PV could bind to a node where no pod runs. On the CKA, if you see a PVC stuck in Pending with <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> and NO pod using it — that's expected behavior, not an error. Create the pod and it will bind. <code>kubernetes.io/no-provisioner</code> means admins must manually create PVs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>storageClassName: ""</code> and omitting <code>storageClassName</code> in a PVC?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">scenario: cluster has a default storageclass…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Scenario: Cluster has a default StorageClass "standard"</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 1: Omit storageClassName</span>
spec:
accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
resources:
requests:
  storage: 5Gi
<span class="comment"># storageClassName: ← not specified</span>
<span class="comment"># Result: Uses DEFAULT StorageClass ("standard")</span>
<span class="comment"># → Dynamic provisioning creates PV automatically</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 2: storageClassName: ""</span>
spec:
accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
resources:
requests:
  storage: 5Gi
storageClassName: ""     # ← Explicitly empty
<span class="comment"># Result: NO StorageClass used</span>
<span class="comment"># → Only binds to PVs that also have NO class</span>
<span class="comment"># → No dynamic provisioning</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 3: storageClassName: "fast"</span>
spec:
storageClassName: "fast"
<span class="comment"># Result: Only binds to PVs with class "fast"</span>
<span class="comment"># → Or dynamic provisioning via "fast" StorageClass</span>

<span class="comment"># Check default StorageClass:</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># NAME                  PROVISIONER          AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># standard (default)    rancher.io/local     5d</span>
<span class="comment">#          ↑ "(default)" annotation</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is one of the most subtle CKA traps. Omitting <code>storageClassName</code> uses the cluster's default StorageClass (dynamic provisioning). Setting <code>storageClassName: ""</code> explicitly means "no class" — only matches PVs with no class. On the CKA, if you want static binding to a specific PV without dynamic provisioning, use <code>storageClassName: ""</code>. To check the default: <code>kubectl get sc</code> — look for <code>(default)</code> annotation.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Expand a PVC from 5Gi to 10Gi. What conditions must be met?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">requirements for pvc expansion</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Requirements for PVC expansion:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. StorageClass must have allowVolumeExpansion: true</span>
kubectl get sc standard -o yaml | grep allowVolumeExpansion
<span class="comment"># allowVolumeExpansion: true  ← Required!</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Simply edit the PVC size:</span>
kubectl edit pvc my-pvc
<span class="comment"># Change: storage: 5Gi → storage: 10Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Or use patch:</span>
kubectl patch pvc my-pvc -p \
'{"spec":{"resources":{"requests":{"storage":"10Gi"}}}}'

<span class="comment"># 3. Check status:</span>
kubectl get pvc my-pvc
<span class="comment"># CAPACITY: 10Gi ← Expanded!</span>

<span class="comment"># For some volume types, expansion requires pod restart:</span>
kubectl describe pvc my-pvc
<span class="comment"># Conditions:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Type: FileSystemResizePending</span>
<span class="comment">#   Message: Waiting for user to restart the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Restart the pod:</span>
kubectl delete pod my-pod
<span class="comment"># Deployment recreates pod → filesystem expands</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Cannot SHRINK a PVC! Only expansion is supported.</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PVC expansion requires <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code> on the StorageClass. Just edit the PVC spec to request more storage. Some provisioners expand online (no pod restart needed), others need pod restart (check for <code>FileSystemResizePending</code> condition). On the CKA, this is a straightforward task but watch for: (1) StorageClass doesn't allow expansion (verify first), (2) can never shrink — only grow, (3) may need to restart the pod for filesystem-level resize.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you sort PersistentVolumes by capacity and find all PVCs across all namespaces?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">sort pvs by capacity</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Sort PVs by capacity:</span>
kubectl get pv --sort-by=.spec.capacity.storage
<span class="comment"># NAME     CAPACITY  ACCESS  RECLAIM  STATUS     CLASS     AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-sm    1Gi       RWO     Retain   Available  local     5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-md    5Gi       RWO     Delete   Bound      standard  10m</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-lg    20Gi      RWX     Retain   Available  fast      2m</span>

<span class="comment"># All PVCs in all namespaces:</span>
kubectl get pvc -A
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE  NAME       STATUS  VOLUME  CAPACITY  ACCESS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># default    data-pvc   Bound   pv-md   5Gi       RWO     10m</span>
<span class="comment"># prod       db-pvc     Bound   pv-lg   20Gi      RWX     5m</span>
<span class="comment"># dev        cache-pvc  Pending -       -         -       2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Custom columns for overview:</span>
kubectl get pv -o custom-columns=\
NAME:.metadata.name,\
SIZE:.spec.capacity.storage,\
STATUS:.status.phase,\
CLAIM:.spec.claimRef.name,\
CLASS:.spec.storageClassName

<span class="comment"># Find which PV is bound to which PVC:</span>
kubectl get pv -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.claimRef.namespace}/{.spec.claimRef.name}{"\n"}{end}'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PVs are cluster-scoped (no -n flag needed), PVCs are namespaced (<code>-A</code> to see all). <code>--sort-by</code> uses JSONPath for sorting. On the CKA, you may be asked to find a specific PV/PVC relationship — use the <code>claimRef</code> field on a PV to see which PVC it's bound to. Custom columns are faster than <code>-o wide</code> for specific information. Remember: <code>kubectl get pv</code> (no namespace), <code>kubectl get pvc -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> (needs namespace).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>ReadWriteOnce</code>, <code>ReadOnlyMany</code>, <code>ReadWriteMany</code>, and <code>ReadWriteOncePod</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Mode</th>
                                        <th>Short</th>
                                        <th>Description</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ReadWriteOnce</code></td>
                                        <td>RWO</td>
                                        <td>Read-write by ONE node</td>
                                        <td>Single database pod</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ReadOnlyMany</code></td>
                                        <td>ROX</td>
                                        <td>Read-only by many nodes</td>
                                        <td>Shared config/static assets</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ReadWriteMany</code></td>
                                        <td>RWX</td>
                                        <td>Read-write by many nodes</td>
                                        <td>Shared uploads (NFS,EFS)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ReadWriteOncePod</code></td>
                                        <td>RWOP</td>
                                        <td>Read-write by ONE pod</td>
                                        <td>Exclusive pod access (1.22+)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cka gotcha</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># CKA gotcha:</span>
<span class="comment"># RWO = "one NODE" not "one pod"!</span>
<span class="comment"># Multiple pods on the SAME node can share an RWO volume</span>
<span class="comment"># RWOP = "one POD" — exclusive access guarantee</span>

<span class="comment"># hostPath supports: RWO only</span>
<span class="comment"># NFS supports: RWO, ROX, RWX</span>
<span class="comment"># AWS EBS supports: RWO only</span>
<span class="comment"># AWS EFS supports: RWX</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The biggest trap: <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> means one NODE, not one pod. Multiple pods on the same node CAN mount the same RWO volume simultaneously. For true single-pod exclusivity, use <code>ReadWriteOncePod</code> (K8s 1.22+). On the CKA, access mode mismatches are a top reason for PVC binding failure. If a PVC requests <code>RWX</code> but only <code>RWO</code> PVs exist, it stays <code>Pending</code>. Not all storage backends support all modes — <code>hostPath</code> only supports <code>RWO</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A PV shows status <code>Released</code> after its PVC was deleted. How do you make it <code>Available</code> again for a new PVC?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check current state</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check current state:</span>
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># NAME     CAPACITY  ACCESS  RECLAIM  STATUS    CLAIM               AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-data  10Gi      RWO     Retain   Released  default/old-pvc     1h</span>

<span class="comment"># The PV has a claimRef to the old PVC — must remove it:</span>
kubectl edit pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># Delete the entire claimRef section:</span>
<span class="comment"># spec:</span>
<span class="comment">#   claimRef:          ← DELETE these 4 lines</span>
<span class="comment">#     apiVersion: v1</span>
<span class="comment">#     kind: PersistentVolumeClaim</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: old-pvc</span>
<span class="comment">#     namespace: default</span>
<span class="comment">#     uid: abc-123</span>

<span class="comment"># Or use patch:</span>
kubectl patch pv pv-data --type json \
-p '[{"op": "remove", "path": "/spec/claimRef"}]'

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Available ← Ready for new PVC!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ The data from the old PVC is still on disk!</span>
<span class="comment"># You may want to clean it before reuse:</span>
<span class="comment"># SSH to node → rm -rf /mnt/data/*</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>When a PVC is deleted and the PV has <code>Retain</code> policy, the PV goes to <code>Released</code> (not <code>Available</code>). The <code>claimRef</code> still references the old PVC, preventing new bindings. Remove it to return to <code>Available</code>. On the CKA, this is a multi-step task: (1) identify the Released PV, (2) remove claimRef, (3) verify Available status. Note that old data remains — the admin must decide whether to clean it. This is why <code>Retain</code> requires manual intervention by design.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a StatefulSet with <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> for a PostgreSQL database. How do <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> differ from regular PVCs? What happens to the volumes when pods are deleted?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
name: postgres
spec:
serviceName: postgres-headless
replicas: 3
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: postgres
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: postgres
spec:
  containers:
  - name: postgres
    image: postgres:15
    ports:
    - containerPort: 5432
    volumeMounts:
    - name: pgdata
      mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
volumeClaimTemplates:         # ← Not under template!
- metadata:
  name: pgdata               <span class="comment"># Matches volumeMount name</span>
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  storageClassName: standard
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi

<span class="comment"># What gets created:</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-0 → PVC: pgdata-postgres-0 → PV (auto-provisioned)</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-1 → PVC: pgdata-postgres-1 → PV (auto-provisioned)</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-2 → PVC: pgdata-postgres-2 → PV (auto-provisioned)</span>

<span class="comment"># Key differences from regular PVCs:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Each pod gets its OWN unique PVC (not shared)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. PVC name = &lt;template-name&gt;-&lt;statefulset-name&gt;-&lt;ordinal&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. PVCs are NOT deleted when pods or StatefulSet are deleted</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. When pod restarts, it reattaches to the SAME PVC</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># pgdata-postgres-0   Bound   pv-abc   10Gi   RWO    standard</span>
<span class="comment"># pgdata-postgres-1   Bound   pv-def   10Gi   RWO    standard</span>
<span class="comment"># pgdata-postgres-2   Bound   pv-ghi   10Gi   RWO    standard</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Deleting StatefulSet does NOT delete PVCs:</span>
kubectl delete sts postgres
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># All PVCs still exist! Data is preserved.</span>
<span class="comment"># Manual cleanup: kubectl delete pvc pgdata-postgres-{0,1,2}</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> are unique to StatefulSets and create a <strong>dedicated PVC per pod replica</strong>. Unlike Deployments where all pods share the same PVC (or have no persistent storage), StatefulSets guarantee that pod <code>postgres-0</code> always gets <code>pgdata-postgres-0</code>. PVCs survive pod restarts, rescheduling, and even StatefulSet deletion — this is by design to prevent accidental data loss. On the CKA, remember: <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> is a <strong>top-level field of StatefulSet spec</strong> (same level as <code>template</code>), NOT nested inside the pod template. The naming convention <code>&lt;vct-name&gt;-&lt;sts-name&gt;-&lt;ordinal&gt;</code> is deterministic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 22 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch23">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 23 · ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Hard-coding configuration into container images is an anti-pattern. Every time you change a setting, you'd need to rebuild and redeploy the image. <strong>ConfigMaps</strong> and <strong>Secrets</strong> solve this by externalizing configuration — injecting settings into pods at runtime via environment variables or mounted files. For our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django app, this means database URLs, Django settings, and credentials all live outside the image, making it portable across dev, staging, and production environments.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔐 Config</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ How to create ConfigMaps from literals, files, and env-files</li>
                    <li>✅ How to inject ConfigMap data as environment variables and mounted files</li>
                    <li>✅ How Secrets differ from ConfigMaps — base64 encoding, types, and security caveats</li>
                    <li>✅ Best practices for managing configuration in production clusters</li>
                    <li>✅ Immutable ConfigMaps and Secrets for performance and safety</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Storage <span class="cka-weight">10%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets Appear in Multiple CKA Domains</h5>
                <p>These topics cross storage and workload domains. You'll be asked to create them imperatively and mount them into pods. Master these commands:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-literal=key=value</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-literal=password=abc123</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get configmap myconfig -o yaml</code> — inspect contents</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-file=config.properties</code></li>
                    <li>Know both injection methods: <code>envFrom</code> (env vars) and <code>volumeMounts</code> (files)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ConfigMap + Secret + Pod Injection — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">You MUST know both injection methods. Env-vars for single values, volume mounts for whole files.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># ConfigMap</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: app-config
data:
  APP_ENV: production              # ◀── key-value pair
  config.properties: |             # ◀── multi-line file content
    server.port=8080
    log.level=INFO
---
<span class="comment"># Secret</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: app-secret
type: Opaque
data:
  DB_PASS: cGFzc3dvcmQxMjM=       # ◀── base64 encoded
stringData:
  API_KEY: my-plain-text-key       # ◀── auto-encoded at creation
---
<span class="comment"># Pod — env var injection</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: app-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    envFrom:                       # ◀── inject ALL keys as env vars
    - configMapRef:
        name: app-config
    env:
    - name: DB_PASSWORD            # ◀── inject SINGLE key
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:
          name: app-secret
          key: DB_PASS
    volumeMounts:                  # ◀── inject as FILES
    - name: config-vol
      mountPath: /etc/config
      readOnly: true
    - name: secret-vol
      mountPath: /etc/secrets
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: config-vol
    configMap:
      name: app-config
  - name: secret-vol
    secret:
      secretName: app-secret</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find ConfigMap &amp; Secret YAML Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain configmap.data                 <span class="comment"># key-value data</span>
kubectl explain secret.data                    <span class="comment"># base64-encoded data</span>
kubectl explain secret.stringData              <span class="comment"># plain text (auto-encoded)</span>
kubectl explain secret.type                    <span class="comment"># Opaque, tls, etc.</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.envFrom    <span class="comment"># bulk injection</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.env.valueFrom  <span class="comment"># single key injection</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.volumes.configMap     <span class="comment"># CM volume mount</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.volumes.secret        <span class="comment"># Secret volume mount</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>✅ DO</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Use <code>stringData</code> for plain-text Secret creation</li>
                        <li>Mount Secrets as <code>readOnly</code> volumes</li>
                        <li>Use <code>envFrom</code> to inject all CM keys at once</li>
                        <li>Use <code>--from-literal</code> for quick exam answers</li>
                        <li>Check Secret data with <code>base64 -d</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>❌ DON'T</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Store sensitive data in ConfigMaps</li>
                        <li>Assume Secrets are encrypted at rest</li>
                        <li>Forget <code>readOnly: true</code> on Secret mounts</li>
                        <li>Expect env var updates without Pod restart</li>
                        <li>Exceed the 1 MiB size limit</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">2</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Config Types (CM + Secret)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">2</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Injection Methods (env / vol)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">1 MiB</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Max Size Per Object</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-red">
                    <span class="metric-value">base64</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Secret Encoding (NOT encryption!)</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §23.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.1 ConfigMaps — Non-Sensitive Configuration</h3>
                <p>A ConfigMap stores <strong>key-value pairs</strong> of non-sensitive configuration data. It decouples application configuration from the container image, so the same image can run with different settings in different environments.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ConfigMap — Same Image, Different Config Per Environment</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ONE IMAGE — THREE ENVIRONMENTS — THREE CONFIGMAPS                       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                                    │
  │  │  easypj:latest   │  ◀── same image everywhere                        │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                                    │
  │           │                                                              │
  │     ┌─────┼──────────────────┐                                          │
  │     │     │                  │                                          │
  │     ▼     ▼                  ▼                                          │
  │  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐                      │
  │  │ DEV      │  │ STAGING      │  │ PRODUCTION   │                      │
  │  │          │  │              │  │              │                      │
  │  │ ConfigMap│  │ ConfigMap    │  │ ConfigMap    │                      │
  │  │ DEBUG=   │  │ DEBUG=       │  │ DEBUG=       │                      │
  │  │  True    │  │  True        │  │  False       │                      │
  │  │ DB_HOST= │  │ DB_HOST=     │  │ DB_HOST=     │                      │
  │  │  localhost│  │  staging-db  │  │  prod-db     │                      │
  │  │ REPLICAS=│  │ REPLICAS=    │  │ REPLICAS=    │                      │
  │  │  1       │  │  2           │  │  5           │                      │
  │  │ LOG=     │  │ LOG=         │  │ LOG=         │                      │
  │  │  verbose │  │  info        │  │  warning     │                      │
  │  └──────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘                      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  No image rebuild needed! Just change the ConfigMap and restart pods.   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>Create from Literals</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DEBUG=False --from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres</code> — quick key-value pairs, great for simple settings.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📄</div>
                        <h5>Create from File</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap nginx-conf --from-file=nginx.conf</code> — entire file becomes a key (filename) with file contents as value. Perfect for config files.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Create from Env File</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap app-env --from-env-file=.env</code> — reads a <code>.env</code> file and creates one key per line. Great for migrating from Docker Compose.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>Create from YAML</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml</code> — full control over keys and values using declarative YAML. Best for version-controlled configs.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-configmap.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ConfigMap for easypj Django settings</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj-production
data:
  <span class="comment"># Simple key-value pairs → injected as env vars</span>
  DEBUG: "False"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*.easypj.com,localhost"
  DB_HOST: "postgres.easypj-production.svc.cluster.local"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DB_NAME: "easypj_db"
  STATIC_URL: "/static/"
  MEDIA_URL: "/media/"

  <span class="comment"># Multi-line value → best mounted as a file</span>
  settings.ini: |
    [database]
    engine = django.db.backends.postgresql
    host = postgres
    port = 5432
    name = easypj_db

    [cache]
    backend = django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache
    location = redis://redis:6379/0

    [email]
    backend = django.core.mail.backends.smtp.EmailBackend
    host = smtp.easypj.com
    port = 587</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Two Ways to Inject ConfigMap Data into Pods</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  METHOD 1: Environment Variables        METHOD 2: Mounted Files          │
  │  (envFrom / env)                        (volumeMounts)                   │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ConfigMap: easypj-config               ConfigMap: easypj-config         │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐                ┌─────────────────────┐          │
  │  │ DEBUG=False          │                │ settings.ini: |     │          │
  │  │ DB_HOST=postgres     │                │   [database]        │          │
  │  │ DB_PORT=5432         │                │   engine=postgresql │          │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘                └─────────┬───────────┘          │
  │            │                                      │                      │
  │            ▼                                      ▼                      │
  │  Pod sees env vars:                     Pod sees files:                  │
  │  $ echo $DEBUG                          $ ls /etc/config/                │
  │  False                                  settings.ini                     │
  │  $ echo $DB_HOST                        $ cat /etc/config/settings.ini   │
  │  postgres                               [database]                       │
  │                                         engine=postgresql                │
  │  ✅ Simple, fast access                 ✅ Complex configs, auto-update  │
  │  ❌ Requires pod restart to update      ✅ Updates without pod restart   │
  │  ❌ All keys become env vars            ✅ Selective file mounting       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deployment-with-configmap.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj Deployment using ConfigMap — both env vars AND mounted files</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 1: All keys as env vars ──</span>
        envFrom:
        - configMapRef:
            name: easypj-config       # ◀── all keys become env vars
                                       <span class="comment">#     DEBUG, DB_HOST, DB_PORT, etc.</span>

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 1b: Selective env vars ──</span>
        env:
        - name: DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE
          value: easypj.settings       # ◀── hardcoded value
        - name: DATABASE_HOST           # ◀── rename the key
          valueFrom:
            configMapKeyRef:
              name: easypj-config
              key: DB_HOST              # ◀── specific key from ConfigMap

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 2: Mount as files ──</span>
        volumeMounts:
        - name: config-volume
          mountPath: /etc/easypj/config  # ◀── files appear here
          readOnly: true

      volumes:
      - name: config-volume
        configMap:
          name: easypj-config
          items:                         # ◀── selective: only mount these keys
          - key: settings.ini
            path: settings.ini           # ◀── /etc/easypj/config/settings.ini</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 ConfigMap Updates and Pod Behavior</h5>
                    <p>When you update a ConfigMap, <strong>volume-mounted files update automatically</strong> (within ~60 seconds, or on kubelet sync period). However, <strong>environment variables do NOT update</strong> — the pod must be restarted. This is a key distinction for the CKA exam. If you need live config reloads, use volume mounts.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 23.2: Secrets — Sensitive Configuration -->
            <!-- ─────────── §23.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.2 Secrets — Sensitive Configuration</h3>
                <div class="chapter-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    <span class="badge badge-important">CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-warning">SECURITY</span>
                </div>
                <p>Secrets are the companion to ConfigMaps — they store <strong>sensitive</strong> data like passwords, API keys, and TLS certificates. While structurally similar to ConfigMaps, Secrets have additional protections and CKA exam-specific nuances you must understand.</p>

                <!-- ConfigMap vs Secret Comparison -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>📄 ConfigMap</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Non-sensitive config</li>
                                <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Plain text in etcd</li>
                                <li><strong>Data field:</strong> <code>data:</code> (plain text)</li>
                                <li><strong>Example:</strong> DB_HOST, LOG_LEVEL</li>
                                <li><strong>Safe to log:</strong> ✅ Yes</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>🔒 Secret</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Sensitive data</li>
                                <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Base64 in etcd (not encrypted by default!)</li>
                                <li><strong>Data field:</strong> <code>data:</code> (base64) or <code>stringData:</code> (plain)</li>
                                <li><strong>Example:</strong> DB_PASSWORD, SECRET_KEY</li>
                                <li><strong>Safe to log:</strong> ❌ Never</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Critical Warning: Base64 is NOT encryption -->
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Base64 is NOT Encryption!</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes Secrets are <strong>base64 encoded, NOT encrypted</strong> by default. Anyone with <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code> access can decode them instantly. Base64 is just encoding — like translating English to pig Latin. It provides <strong>zero security</strong> on its own.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Base64 Encoding Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔍 Base64 Encoding — What Actually Happens</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  Creating a Secret:                         Reading a Secret:

  "postgres123"                              "cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM="
       │                                          │
       ▼                                          ▼
  ┌─────────────┐                          ┌─────────────┐
  │  base64     │                          │  base64     │
  │  encode     │                          │  decode     │
  └──────┬──────┘                          └──────┬──────┘
         ▼                                        ▼
  "cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM="                      "postgres123"
         │                                        │
         ▼                                        ▼
  ┌──────────────────┐                    ┌────────────────┐
  │  Stored in etcd  │                    │  Anyone with   │
  │  as base64       │                    │  RBAC access   │
  │                  │                    │  can read it!  │
  └──────────────────┘                    └────────────────┘

  $ echo -n "postgres123" | base64         $ echo "cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=" | base64 -d
  cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=                         postgres123
</pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Secret Types Table -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📋 Kubernetes Secret Types</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Type</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                <th>Required Keys</th>
                                <th>CKA Exam</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Opaque</code></td>
                                <td>Generic key-value (default)</td>
                                <td>Any</td>
                                <td>⭐ Most common</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/tls</code></td>
                                <td>TLS certificate + key</td>
                                <td><code>tls.crt</code>, <code>tls.key</code></td>
                                <td>⭐ Ingress TLS</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code></td>
                                <td>Private registry credentials</td>
                                <td><code>.dockerconfigjson</code></td>
                                <td>Sometimes tested</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/basic-auth</code></td>
                                <td>Basic authentication</td>
                                <td><code>username</code>, <code>password</code></td>
                                <td>Rare</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/ssh-auth</code></td>
                                <td>SSH private key</td>
                                <td><code>ssh-privatekey</code></td>
                                <td>Rare</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code></td>
                                <td>ServiceAccount token (legacy)</td>
                                <td>Auto-generated</td>
                                <td>Know it exists</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- Creating Secrets -->
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🛠️ Creating Secrets — Imperative Commands (CKA Speed)</h5>
                    <p>On the CKA exam, imperative commands save precious time. Master these:</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">bash — imperative Secret creation</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. From literal values (most common on CKA)</span>
kubectl create secret generic easypj-secrets \
  --from-literal=SECRET_KEY=django-insecure-k8s-prod-key-2024 \
  --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=postgres123 \
  --from-literal=DB_USER=easypj_admin

<span class="comment"># 2. From a file (e.g., TLS cert)</span>
kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \
  --cert=./tls.crt \
  --key=./tls.key

<span class="comment"># 3. From a docker registry (private images)</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com \
  --docker-username=admin \
  --docker-password=secret123

<span class="comment"># 4. Generate YAML without applying (--dry-run trick)</span>
kubectl create secret generic my-secret \
  --from-literal=password=supersecret \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; secret.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify (notice values are hidden by default)</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secrets -o yaml
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   SECRET_KEY: ZGphbmdvLWluc2VjdXJlLWs4cy1wcm9kLWtleS0yMDI0</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_PASSWORD: cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Declarative Secret YAML -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-secrets.yaml — declarative Secret</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secrets
  namespace: easypj
type: Opaque
<span class="comment"># Option A: base64-encoded (required for `data:`)</span>
data:
  SECRET_KEY: ZGphbmdvLWluc2VjdXJlLWs4cy1wcm9kLWtleS0yMDI0   # ◀── base64
  DB_PASSWORD: cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=                                  # ◀── base64
  DB_USER: YW5paHBqX2FkbWlu                                      # ◀── base64

<span class="comment"># Option B: plain text (Kubernetes encodes it for you)</span>
<span class="comment"># stringData:                   # ◀── use this to avoid manual base64</span>
<span class="comment">#   SECRET_KEY: django-insecure-k8s-prod-key-2024</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_PASSWORD: postgres123</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_USER: easypj_admin</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 stringData vs data</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>stringData:</code> when writing YAML by hand — it accepts plain text and Kubernetes base64-encodes it automatically. Use <code>data:</code> when you already have base64-encoded values (e.g., from <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code>). On the CKA exam, <code>stringData:</code> saves time and prevents encoding mistakes.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Using Secrets in Pods -->
                <h4>📦 Injecting Secrets into Pods</h4>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔌 Two Injection Methods — Same as ConfigMaps</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                        easypj Pod                               │
  │                                                                 │
  │  Method 1: Environment Variables          Method 2: Volume      │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  env:                       │    │  /etc/easypj/secrets/│   │
  │  │    SECRET_KEY=django-ins... │    │  ├── SECRET_KEY      │   │
  │  │    DB_PASSWORD=postgres123  │    │  ├── DB_PASSWORD     │   │
  │  │    DB_USER=easypj_admin     │    │  └── DB_USER         │   │
  │  │                             │    │                      │   │
  │  │  ✅ Easy to use in code    │    │  ✅ Auto-updates     │   │
  │  │  ❌ Visible in env dump    │    │  ✅ One file per key │   │
  │  │  ❌ No auto-update         │    │  ❌ Read file in code│   │
  │  └─────────────────────────────┘    └──────────────────────┘   │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-deployment-with-secrets.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj/web:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000

        <span class="comment"># ── Method 1: Inject ALL Secret keys as env vars ──</span>
        envFrom:
        - secretRef:
            name: easypj-secrets        # ◀── all keys become env vars

        <span class="comment"># ── Method 2: Inject specific keys as env vars ──</span>
        env:
        - name: DATABASE_URL
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: easypj-secrets
              key: DB_PASSWORD           # ◀── just one key

        <span class="comment"># ── Method 3: Mount as files ──</span>
        volumeMounts:
        - name: secret-volume
          mountPath: /etc/easypj/secrets
          readOnly: true                 # ◀── ALWAYS set readOnly for secrets

      volumes:
      - name: secret-volume
        secret:
          secretName: easypj-secrets
          defaultMode: 0400              # ◀── restrict file permissions (owner read only)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>🔐 Secret Volume Best Practices</h5>
                    <p>Always set <code>readOnly: true</code> on secret volume mounts and use restrictive <code>defaultMode: 0400</code> (owner read-only). This prevents containers from accidentally modifying secret files and follows the principle of least privilege. For the CKA exam, remember: <code>secret</code> volume type (not <code>configMap</code>), and <code>secretName</code> (not <code>name</code>) in the volume spec.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Verification Commands -->
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔍 Verifying Secrets (CKA Exam Commands)</h5>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">bash — Secret verification commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List secrets in namespace</span>
kubectl get secrets -n easypj

<span class="comment"># View secret (values are hidden)</span>
kubectl describe secret easypj-secrets -n easypj

<span class="comment"># View base64-encoded values</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secrets -n easypj -o yaml

<span class="comment"># Decode a specific value</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secrets -n easypj \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.DB_PASSWORD}' | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># Output: postgres123</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify secret is mounted in pod</span>
kubectl exec -it deploy/easypj-web -n easypj -- ls /etc/easypj/secrets/
<span class="comment"># Output: DB_PASSWORD  DB_USER  SECRET_KEY</span>

kubectl exec -it deploy/easypj-web -n easypj -- cat /etc/easypj/secrets/DB_PASSWORD
<span class="comment"># Output: postgres123  (automatically decoded when mounted!)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Secrets Are Decoded When Mounted</h5>
                    <p>When Secrets are injected as environment variables or mounted as volume files, Kubernetes <strong>automatically decodes the base64</strong>. Your application receives plain text — no decoding needed in your code. The base64 encoding only exists in etcd and the API response.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 23.3: ConfigMap and Secret Best Practices -->
            <!-- ─────────── §23.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.3 ConfigMap and Secret Best Practices</h3>
                <div class="chapter-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    <span class="badge badge-concept">BEST PRACTICES</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
                <p>Real-world Kubernetes security depends on following strict practices around configuration management. These rules apply equally to the CKA exam and production clusters.</p>

                <!-- DO / DON'T Cards -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>✅ DO — Best Practices</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>ConfigMaps</strong> for non-sensitive config (DB_HOST, LOG_LEVEL, feature flags)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>Secrets</strong> for sensitive data (passwords, API keys, TLS certs)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>envFrom</code> for <strong>bulk injection</strong> — fewer lines, fewer mistakes</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>volume mounts</strong> when config is a complete file (nginx.conf, settings.ini)</li>
                                <li>✅ Enable <strong>encryption at rest</strong> for Secrets (<code>EncryptionConfiguration</code>)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>RBAC</strong> to restrict who can <code>get</code>/<code>list</code> Secrets</li>
                                <li>✅ Set <code>readOnly: true</code> on Secret volume mounts</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>stringData:</code> in YAML for readability</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>immutable: true</code> for configs that should never change</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>❌ DON'T — Common Mistakes</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never commit Secrets to Git</strong> — use sealed-secrets or external vaults</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never hardcode secrets in Dockerfiles</strong> — they persist in image layers</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never log secret values</strong> — use placeholder text in logs</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never use ConfigMaps for passwords</strong> — they have no access restrictions</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never share Secrets across namespaces</strong> without good reason</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never give broad Secret access</strong> — <code>get secrets *</code> is dangerous</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never skip RBAC</strong> for Secret access — default allows too much</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never assume base64 = encrypted</strong> — it's just encoding</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never store large files in Secrets</strong> — 1MB limit per Secret</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Encryption at Rest Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔐 Secret Security Layers — Defense in Depth</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  Layer 1: RBAC (WHO can access)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Role: secret-reader                                         │
  │    rules:                                                    │
  │    - apiGroups: [""]                                         │
  │      resources: ["secrets"]                                  │
  │      verbs: ["get"]          ◀── only get, not list/watch   │
  │      resourceNames: ["easypj-secrets"]  ◀── specific secret │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Layer 2: Encryption at Rest (HOW it's stored in etcd)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Without encryption:  Secret → base64 → etcd (readable!)    │
  │  With encryption:     Secret → base64 → AES-CBC → etcd ✅  │
  │                                                              │
  │  Configure via: /etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml                 │
  │  API server flag: --encryption-provider-config               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Layer 3: Network (HOW it's transmitted)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  API server ←──TLS──→ kubelet ←──tmpfs──→ Pod              │
  │  Secrets mounted as tmpfs (memory) — never written to disk  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <!-- easypj Complete Config Architecture -->
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🏗️ easypj — Complete Configuration Architecture</h5>
                    <p>Putting it all together — how the easypj Django project separates configuration from code:</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>📐 easypj Configuration Flow</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    easypj Namespace                                 │
  │                                                                     │
  │  ConfigMap: easypj-config          Secret: easypj-secrets          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────┐          ┌──────────────────────┐        │
  │  │ DB_HOST=postgres-svc │          │ SECRET_KEY=django... │        │
  │  │ DB_NAME=easypj_db    │          │ DB_PASSWORD=post...  │        │
  │  │ DB_PORT=5432          │          │ DB_USER=easypj_admin│        │
  │  │ DEBUG=False            │          │                      │        │
  │  │ ALLOWED_HOSTS=*       │          │  (base64 encoded)    │        │
  │  │ settings.ini (file)   │          └──────────┬───────────┘        │
  │  └──────────┬───────────┘                      │                    │
  │             │                                  │                    │
  │             ▼                                  ▼                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │
  │  │               easypj-web Deployment                      │      │
  │  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │      │
  │  │  │  django container                                   │  │      │
  │  │  │                                                     │  │      │
  │  │  │  ENV:  DB_HOST, DB_NAME, DB_PORT  ◀── configMapRef │  │      │
  │  │  │        SECRET_KEY, DB_PASSWORD    ◀── secretRef    │  │      │
  │  │  │                                                     │  │      │
  │  │  │  FILES: /etc/easypj/config/settings.ini ◀── CM vol │  │      │
  │  │  │         /etc/easypj/secrets/       ◀── Secret vol  │  │      │
  │  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │      │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Exam Quick Reference</h5>
                    <p>On the exam, you'll typically need to: (1) create a ConfigMap or Secret from literals, (2) mount it in a Pod/Deployment as env vars or volume, and (3) verify the values are accessible inside the container. Practice the full flow: <code>kubectl create → kubectl apply deployment → kubectl exec -- printenv</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔐 Enabling Encryption at Rest for Secrets (CKA Advanced)</h5>
                    <p>By default, Secrets are stored <strong>unencrypted</strong> in etcd. To encrypt them, you configure the API server with an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> file. This is a key topic for cluster hardening.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml — Encryption at Rest</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Encryption configuration for Secrets at rest in etcd</span>
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources:
    - secrets                             # ◀── only encrypt Secrets
    providers:
    - aescbc:                             # ◀── AES-CBC encryption
        keys:
        - name: key1
          secret: dGhpcyBpcyBhIDMyIGJ5dGUga2V5  # ◀── base64 of a 32-byte key
    - identity: {}                        # ◀── fallback: read unencrypted Secrets

<span class="comment"># API server flag:</span>
<span class="comment"># --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># After enabling, re-encrypt existing Secrets:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📋 ConfigMap vs Secret — Complete Comparison</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>ConfigMap</th>
                                <th>Secret</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Purpose</strong></td>
                                <td>Non-sensitive config</td>
                                <td>Sensitive data</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Data encoding</strong></td>
                                <td>Plain text</td>
                                <td>Base64 (<code>data:</code>) or plain (<code>stringData:</code>)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>etcd storage</strong></td>
                                <td>Plain text</td>
                                <td>Base64 (optionally encrypted at rest)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Size limit</strong></td>
                                <td>1 MiB per ConfigMap</td>
                                <td>1 MiB per Secret</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>RBAC</strong></td>
                                <td>Standard resource rules</td>
                                <td>Should have restricted access rules</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Volume type</strong></td>
                                <td><code>configMap</code></td>
                                <td><code>secret</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Ref in pod spec</strong></td>
                                <td><code>configMapRef</code> / <code>configMapKeyRef</code></td>
                                <td><code>secretRef</code> / <code>secretKeyRef</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Volume field</strong></td>
                                <td><code>configMap.name</code></td>
                                <td><code>secret.secretName</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>tmpfs mount</strong></td>
                                <td>No (on-disk)</td>
                                <td>Yes (in-memory by default) — never on disk</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>kubectl describe</strong></td>
                                <td>Shows values</td>
                                <td>Shows sizes only (hides values)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Immutable support</strong></td>
                                <td>✅ <code>immutable: true</code></td>
                                <td>✅ <code>immutable: true</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 23.4: Immutable ConfigMaps and Secrets -->
            <!-- ─────────── §23.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.4 Immutable ConfigMaps and Secrets</h3>
                <div class="chapter-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-performance">PERFORMANCE</span>
                </div>
                <p>Kubernetes v1.21+ supports marking ConfigMaps and Secrets as <strong>immutable</strong> — once created, they cannot be modified. This provides safety and performance benefits for production clusters.</p>

                <!-- Why Immutable? Cards -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>🛡️ Safety Benefits</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Prevents accidental changes</strong> — can't <code>kubectl edit</code> or <code>kubectl apply</code> updates</li>
                                <li><strong>Protects production</strong> — config drift becomes impossible</li>
                                <li><strong>Audit trail</strong> — changes require delete + recreate (visible in audit logs)</li>
                                <li><strong>GitOps friendly</strong> — ensures deployed config matches Git source</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>⚡ Performance Benefits</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Kubelet optimization</strong> — stops watching immutable objects for changes</li>
                                <li><strong>Reduced API server load</strong> — fewer watch connections per node</li>
                                <li><strong>Scales better</strong> — significant at 1000+ ConfigMaps/Secrets</li>
                                <li><strong>Faster sync</strong> — kubelet skips polling cycles for immutable objects</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Immutable ConfigMap YAML -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">immutable-configmap.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config-v2          # ◀── version in name (required for updates)
  namespace: easypj
immutable: true                     # ◀── THE KEY FIELD — cannot be changed once set
data:
  DB_HOST: "postgres-svc"
  DB_NAME: "easypj_db"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DEBUG: "False"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Immutable Secret YAML -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">immutable-secret.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secrets-v2         # ◀── version in name
  namespace: easypj
type: Opaque
immutable: true                    # ◀── same field works for Secrets too
stringData:
  SECRET_KEY: django-insecure-k8s-prod-key-2024
  DB_PASSWORD: postgres123
  DB_USER: easypj_admin</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- What happens when you try to modify -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🚫 What Happens When You Try to Modify an Immutable Object</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  $ kubectl edit configmap easypj-config-v2
  error: configmaps "easypj-config-v2" is immutable

  $ kubectl apply -f updated-config.yaml
  The ConfigMap "easypj-config-v2" is invalid: data: Forbidden:
  field is immutable when `immutable` is set

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Update Strategy for Immutable Configs:                     │
  │                                                             │
  │  1. Create NEW ConfigMap with new version name              │
  │     easypj-config-v2 → easypj-config-v3                   │
  │                                                             │
  │  2. Update Deployment to reference new name                 │
  │     configMapRef:                                           │
  │       name: easypj-config-v3   ◀── point to new version   │
  │                                                             │
  │  3. Rolling update deploys pods with new config             │
  │                                                             │
  │  4. Delete old ConfigMap when no pods reference it          │
  │     kubectl delete configmap easypj-config-v2              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Immutability Rule of Thumb</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>immutable: true</code> for <strong>production configs that are versioned via CI/CD</strong>. Skip it for development environments where you frequently tweak settings. On the CKA exam, know that the <code>immutable</code> field exists and that the only way to "update" an immutable ConfigMap is to delete it and create a new one — which requires restarting pods that reference it.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Scenario Box -->
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🏋️ CKA Practice Scenario — ConfigMaps and Secrets</h5>
                    <div class="scenario-content">
                        <p><strong>Task:</strong> Create a namespace <code>webapp</code>. Create a ConfigMap named <code>app-config</code> with keys <code>APP_COLOR=blue</code> and <code>APP_MODE=prod</code>. Create a Secret named <code>app-secret</code> with key <code>DB_PASS=supersecret</code>. Create a Pod named <code>webapp-pod</code> using image <code>nginx</code> that loads the ConfigMap as environment variables and mounts the Secret at <code>/etc/secrets</code>. Verify the values are accessible.</p>
                        <p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header">
                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                                <span class="code-lang">bash — CKA scenario solution</span>
                            </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace webapp

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create ConfigMap</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config \
  --from-literal=APP_COLOR=blue \
  --from-literal=APP_MODE=prod \
  -n webapp

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create Secret</span>
kubectl create secret generic app-secret \
  --from-literal=DB_PASS=supersecret \
  -n webapp

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create Pod with both ConfigMap and Secret</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: webapp-pod
  namespace: webapp
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
    envFrom:
    - configMapRef:
        name: app-config         # ◀── all CM keys as env vars
    volumeMounts:
    - name: secret-vol
      mountPath: /etc/secrets
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: secret-vol
    secret:
      secretName: app-secret     # ◀── mount Secret as files
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify</span>
kubectl exec webapp-pod -n webapp -- printenv APP_COLOR
<span class="comment"># Output: blue</span>

kubectl exec webapp-pod -n webapp -- cat /etc/secrets/DB_PASS
<span class="comment"># Output: supersecret</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How ConfigMaps and Secrets Are Injected Into Pods: Volume Mounts vs Environment Variables, Atomic Updates, and Encryption at Rest</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ ConfigMap/Secret Injection — Two Mechanisms, Different Behaviors</h5>
            <p>ConfigMaps and Secrets can be consumed as environment variables OR volume mounts. These mechanisms behave differently — especially when config is updated at runtime.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  INJECTION METHOD 1: ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES                                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  apiVersion: v1                                                      │    │
│  │  spec:                                                                │    │
│  │    containers:                                                        │    │
│  │    - name: easypj                                                     │    │
│  │      env:                                                             │    │
│  │      - name: DB_HOST           <span class="comment">← Single key                          </span>│    │
│  │        valueFrom:                                                     │    │
│  │          configMapKeyRef:                                             │    │
│  │            name: easypj-config                                        │    │
│  │            key: DB_HOST                                               │    │
│  │      envFrom:                  <span class="comment">← ALL keys at once                    </span>│    │
│  │      - configMapRef:                                                  │    │
│  │          name: easypj-config                                          │    │
│  │      - secretRef:                                                     │    │
│  │          name: easypj-db-creds                                        │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  BEHAVIOR:                                                            │    │
│  │  ✅ Simple, well-understood by applications                          │    │
│  │  ❌ VALUES ARE FROZEN at pod start — updating ConfigMap does NOT    │    │
│  │     update env vars in running pods. Pod must be RESTARTED.         │    │
│  │  ❌ All Secret data visible in /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/environ (for root)      │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  INJECTION METHOD 2: VOLUME MOUNTS                                           │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  apiVersion: v1                                                      │    │
│  │  spec:                                                                │    │
│  │    containers:                                                        │    │
│  │    - name: easypj                                                     │    │
│  │      volumeMounts:                                                    │    │
│  │      - name: config                                                   │    │
│  │        mountPath: /etc/config   <span class="comment">← Each key becomes a FILE            </span>│    │
│  │        readOnly: true                                                   │    │
│  │  volumes:                                                             │    │
│  │  - name: config                                                       │    │
│  │    configMap:                                                         │    │
│  │      name: easypj-config                                              │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  RESULT: /etc/config/                                                │    │
│  │  ├── DB_HOST     (file containing "postgres")                        │    │
│  │  ├── DB_PORT     (file containing "5432")                            │    │
│  │  └── DJANGO_DEBUG (file containing "false")                          │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  BEHAVIOR:                                                            │    │
│  │  ✅ AUTO-UPDATES: When ConfigMap changes, kubelet updates mount      │    │
│  │     files within ~60 seconds (configurable via kubelet sync)         │    │
│  │  ✅ Atomic: Symlink swap — no partial updates visible               │    │
│  │  ✅ SubPath: Mount single key as file at specific path              │    │
│  │  ⚠️  App must watch files for changes (or use inotify)              │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  SECRET ENCRYPTION — Defense in Depth:                                       │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  LAYER 1: etcd at rest — Secrets are base64 encoded in etcd. This  │    │
│  │           is NOT encryption, just encoding. Anyone with etcd        │    │
│  │           access can decode them.                                    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  LAYER 2: Encryption at rest — API Server encrypts Secret data      │    │
│  │           BEFORE writing to etcd. Requires EncryptionConfiguration. │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  LAYER 3: RBAC — Only authorized ServiceAccounts/pods/users can     │    │
│  │           GET the Secret.                                            │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  LAYER 4: External secrets management — Sealed Secrets, External    │    │
│  │           Secrets Operator (ESO), Vault injector. Secrets never     │    │
│  │           stored in K8s at all.                                      │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  ⚠️  CKA FACT: By default, Secrets in etcd are base64 ENCODED, not │    │
│  │     ENCRYPTED. `echo c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQ= | base64 -d` → supersecret!  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  ATOMIC CONFIGMAP UPDATES — How Kubelet Swaps Config Without Downtime:      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  1. ConfigMap updated via kubectl                                    │    │
│  │  2. kubelet detects change (periodic sync, default ~60s)            │    │
│  │  3. kubelet creates NEW directory: ..data_tmp/&lt;timestamp&gt;/          │    │
│  │  4. kubelet writes updated files to new directory                   │    │
│  │  5. kubelet atomically swaps symlink:                                │    │
│  │     ..data → ..data_tmp/&lt;timestamp&gt;/  (atomic rename)              │    │
│  │  6. Container sees updated files INSTANTLY (no restart needed)      │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  $ ls -la /etc/config/                                               │    │
│  │  ..data -> ..2026_06_02_10_05_23.123456789  <span class="comment">← Updated symlink!      </span>│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, you'll frequently create ConfigMaps and Secrets. Key differences to remember: ConfigMap for non-sensitive config (DB_HOST, DEBUG), Secret for sensitive data (DB_PASSWORD, API_KEY). Use <code>--from-literal</code> for single values, <code>--from-file</code> for files. Secrets must be base64 encoded in YAML but NOT in imperative commands: <code>kubectl create secret generic --from-literal=key=value</code> auto-encodes. A pod referencing a non-existent ConfigMap will fail to start with <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Secrets are NOT encrypted by default</strong> — They are only base64-encoded (not encryption!). Anyone with API access can decode them. Enable encryption at rest via <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> on the API server. The CKA tests this knowledge</li>
                    <li><strong><code>envFrom</code> vs <code>env</code></strong> — <code>envFrom</code> imports ALL keys from a ConfigMap/Secret as env vars. <code>env</code> with <code>valueFrom</code> imports one specific key. If the exam says "inject the key <code>DB_HOST</code> from configmap <code>app-config</code>", use <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Env vars do NOT auto-update</strong> — If you change a ConfigMap, pods using <code>envFrom</code> won't see the change until restarted. Volume-mounted ConfigMaps DO auto-update (within ~60s). This is a classic trick question</li>
                    <li><strong><code>immutable: true</code></strong> — Once set, the ConfigMap/Secret cannot be changed. This improves kubelet performance (stops watching for changes). You must delete and recreate to modify</li>
                    <li><strong>Secret types matter</strong> — <code>generic</code> (Opaque) for arbitrary data, <code>docker-registry</code> for image pull credentials, <code>tls</code> for TLS cert/key pairs. The exam may specify which type to create</li>
                    <li>The ConfigMap/Secret must exist BEFORE the Pod — otherwise the Pod stays <code>Pending</code> (unless marked <code>optional: true</code>)</li>
                    <li><code>data:</code> values are base64 in Secrets, but <code>stringData:</code> accepts plain text (auto-encoded)</li>
                    <li>Max ConfigMap/Secret size: <strong>1 MiB</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — ConfigMap/Secret creation cheat sheet:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-literal=key1=val1 --from-literal=key2=val2</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-file=config.properties</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-literal=password=S3cret!</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create secret tls mytls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key</code><br>
                <strong>Decode a secret:</strong> <code>kubectl get secret mysecret -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d</code></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 23)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>CM from file</td><td><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-file=app.conf</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>CM from env-file</td><td><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-env-file=.env</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Secret generic literal</td><td><code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-literal=pass=abc123</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Secret from file</td><td><code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-file=cert.pem</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Secret TLS</td><td><code>kubectl create secret tls tls-secret --cert=cert.crt --key=key.pem</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Inspect CM</td><td><code>kubectl get cm myconfig -o yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Decode Secret</td><td><code>kubectl get secret mysecret -o jsonpath='{.data.pass}' | base64 -d</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List ConfigMaps / Secrets</td><td><code>kubectl get configmaps</code> / <code>kubectl get secrets</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>see all key-value pairs</td><td><code>kubectl describe configmap &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>import from .env file</td><td><code>kubectl create configmap &lt;name&gt; --from-env-file=.env</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>see base64 values</td><td><code>kubectl get secret &lt;name&gt; -o yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create image-pull secret for private registry</td><td><code>kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred --docker-server=&lt;url&gt; --docker-username=&lt;user&gt; --docker-password=&lt;pw&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 6 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a ConfigMap <code>app-config</code> with keys: <code>DB_HOST=postgres</code>, <code>DB_PORT=5432</code></li>
                    <li>2. Create a Secret <code>app-secret</code> with key: <code>DB_PASSWORD=MyP@ssw0rd</code></li>
                    <li>3. Create a pod that loads ALL ConfigMap keys via <code>envFrom</code></li>
                    <li>4. In the same pod, inject only <code>DB_PASSWORD</code> from the Secret via <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code></li>
                    <li>5. Exec in and verify: <code>echo $DB_HOST $DB_PORT $DB_PASSWORD</code></li>
                    <li>6. Mount the ConfigMap as a volume at <code>/etc/config</code> and verify files exist</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 23 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 23 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of config injection &amp; Secret handling — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ConfigMap named <code>app-config</code> with keys <code>DB_HOST=postgres</code> and <code>DB_PORT=5432</code> using an imperative command.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative creation (fastest for cka)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Imperative creation (fastest for CKA):</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres \
--from-literal=DB_PORT=5432

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get configmap app-config -o yaml
<span class="comment"># apiVersion: v1</span>
<span class="comment"># kind: ConfigMap</span>
<span class="comment"># metadata:</span>
<span class="comment">#   name: app-config</span>
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_HOST: postgres</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_PORT: "5432"     ← Note: stored as STRING</span>

<span class="comment"># Other creation methods:</span>
<span class="comment"># From file:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-file=config.properties

<span class="comment"># From env file:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-env-file=.env

<span class="comment"># From directory (each file becomes a key):</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-file=./configs/

<span class="comment"># Dry-run to generate YAML:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > configmap.yaml</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ConfigMaps store non-sensitive configuration data as key-value pairs. On the CKA, <code>--from-literal</code> is the fastest for simple key-value pairs. <code>--from-file</code> uses the filename as the key and file content as the value. <code>--from-env-file</code> reads KEY=VALUE lines from a .env file. Always use <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate YAML that you can modify. All values are stored as strings — even numbers like "5432".</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Secret named <code>db-creds</code> with <code>username=admin</code> and <code>password=S3cret!</code>. How do you decode the values?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create:</span>
kubectl create secret generic db-creds \
--from-literal=username=admin \
--from-literal=password='S3cret!'

<span class="comment"># View (values are base64-encoded):</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds -o yaml
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   password: UzNjcmV0IQ==</span>
<span class="comment">#   username: YWRtaW4=</span>

<span class="comment"># Decode a specific value:</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds \
-o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># Decode all values:</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds -o go-template='
{{range $k, $v := .data}}
{{$k}}: {{$v | base64decode}}
{{end}}'
<span class="comment"># username: admin</span>
<span class="comment"># password: S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ base64 is NOT encryption!</span>
<span class="comment"># Anyone with API access can decode secrets</span>
<span class="comment"># Enable EncryptionConfiguration for at-rest encryption</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Secrets are base64-encoded, NOT encrypted. On the CKA, decoding secrets with <code>jsonpath + base64 -d</code> is a commonly tested skill. The imperative command auto-encodes values to base64. If creating via YAML, you must base64-encode values yourself (<code>echo -n "admin" | base64</code>) or use <code>stringData</code> (plain text, auto-encoded on apply). Secret types: <code>generic</code> (Opaque), <code>docker-registry</code> (image pull), <code>tls</code> (cert/key).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that injects ALL keys from a ConfigMap as environment variables using <code>envFrom</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: env-pod
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
envFrom:                       # ← Import ALL keys
- configMapRef:
    name: app-config           # ← All keys become env vars
- secretRef:
    name: db-creds             # ← All secret keys too

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl exec env-pod -- env | grep -E 'DB_|username|password'
<span class="comment"># DB_HOST=postgres</span>
<span class="comment"># DB_PORT=5432</span>
<span class="comment"># username=admin</span>
<span class="comment"># password=S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># vs. importing a SINGLE key:</span>
env:
- name: DATABASE_HOST              # ← Custom env var name
valueFrom:
configMapKeyRef:
  name: app-config
  key: DB_HOST                 # ← Specific key
- name: DB_PASS
valueFrom:
secretKeyRef:
  name: db-creds
  key: password</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>envFrom</code> imports ALL keys from a ConfigMap/Secret as environment variables. <code>env</code> with <code>valueFrom</code> imports ONE specific key with a custom name. On the CKA, know both patterns: (1) "inject all config" → use <code>envFrom</code>, (2) "inject specific key as <code>DATABASE_URL</code>" → use <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code>. Gotcha: if two sources have the same key name, the last one in the list wins. Invalid env var names (keys with dashes) are silently skipped with <code>envFrom</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Mount a ConfigMap as a volume. What happens when you update the ConfigMap?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">mount configmap as volume</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Mount ConfigMap as volume:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: config-vol-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: config-vol
configMap:
  name: app-config
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: config-vol
  mountPath: /etc/config

<span class="comment"># Each ConfigMap key becomes a file:</span>
kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- ls /etc/config
<span class="comment"># DB_HOST  DB_PORT</span>

kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- cat /etc/config/DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># postgres</span>

<span class="comment"># Update the ConfigMap:</span>
kubectl edit configmap app-config
<span class="comment"># Change DB_HOST to "mysql"</span>

<span class="comment"># After ~60 seconds (kubelet sync period):</span>
kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- cat /etc/config/DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># mysql  ← Auto-updated!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ BUT env vars do NOT auto-update!</span>
<span class="comment"># envFrom values only change on pod restart</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ subPath mounts do NOT auto-update either!</span>
<span class="comment"># volumeMounts with subPath are frozen at pod creation</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Volume-mounted ConfigMaps auto-update within ~60 seconds (kubelet sync period). Environment variables do NOT auto-update. <code>subPath</code> mounts do NOT auto-update. On the CKA, this is a classic trick question: "How do you make your app pick up ConfigMap changes without restart?" Answer: mount as a volume (not env vars). Some apps also need to watch for file changes or receive SIGHUP to reload configuration. The auto-update uses symlinks internally — <code>/etc/config/DB_HOST</code> is actually a symlink to a timestamped directory.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>stringData</code> in a Secret YAML and how is it different from <code>data</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">using 'data' — values must be base64-encoded</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Using 'data' — values must be base64-encoded:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: my-secret
type: Opaque
data:
username: YWRtaW4=          <span class="comment"># echo -n "admin" | base64</span>
password: UzNjcmV0IQ==     <span class="comment"># echo -n "S3cret!" | base64</span>

<span class="comment"># Using 'stringData' — plain text (auto-encoded):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: my-secret
type: Opaque
stringData:                   # ← Plain text!
username: admin
password: S3cret!

<span class="comment"># Both produce the SAME Secret!</span>
<span class="comment"># stringData is write-only — after apply, API returns 'data' (base64)</span>

<span class="comment"># Mix both:</span>
data:
existing-key: YWJj
stringData:
new-key: plain text value   # ← Easier for new keys

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ stringData doesn't appear in kubectl get -o yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># After creation, everything is stored under 'data' as base64</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>stringData</code> is a convenience field that accepts plain text — Kubernetes auto-encodes to base64 on creation. <code>data</code> requires pre-encoded base64 values. On the CKA, <code>stringData</code> saves time when writing YAML manually (no need to run <code>echo -n | base64</code>). After creation, <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code> only shows <code>data</code> (base64). If both <code>data</code> and <code>stringData</code> have the same key, <code>stringData</code> wins. Use <code>stringData</code> to avoid base64 encoding mistakes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that mounts a Secret as a volume with specific file permissions (<code>0400</code>).</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: secret-vol-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: secret-vol
secret:
  secretName: db-creds
  defaultMode: 0400           # ← Octal: owner read-only
  <span class="comment"># Per-key permissions:</span>
  items:
  - key: password
    path: db-password         # ← Custom filename
    mode: 0400                # ← Per-file mode
  - key: username
    path: db-username
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: secret-vol
  mountPath: /etc/secrets
  readOnly: true              # ← Extra safety

<span class="comment"># Verify permissions:</span>
kubectl exec secret-vol-pod -- ls -la /etc/secrets/
<span class="comment"># -r--------  1 root root  7 Jan 1 00:00 db-password</span>
<span class="comment"># -r--------  1 root root  5 Jan 1 00:00 db-username</span>

kubectl exec secret-vol-pod -- cat /etc/secrets/db-password
<span class="comment"># S3cret!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>defaultMode</code> sets file permissions for all keys (octal format: 0644 = rw-r--r--, 0400 = r--------). Per-key <code>mode</code> overrides defaultMode for individual files. On the CKA, Secret volumes default to 0644 — which might be too permissive for passwords. Set <code>0400</code> for sensitive data. Adding <code>readOnly: true</code> on the volumeMount adds an extra layer. The <code>items</code> field controls which keys to mount and their filenames, similar to ConfigMap volumes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you make a ConfigMap immutable? What are the benefits?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
name: prod-config
data:
DB_HOST: prod-db.example.com
DB_PORT: "5432"
immutable: true                   # ← Cannot be modified after apply!

<span class="comment"># Trying to edit:</span>
kubectl edit configmap prod-config
<span class="comment"># error: configmaps "prod-config" is immutable</span>

<span class="comment"># To change: must delete and recreate</span>
kubectl delete configmap prod-config
kubectl create configmap prod-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=new-db.example.com

<span class="comment"># Benefits of immutable:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Protection against accidental changes</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Performance: kubelet stops watching for updates</span>
<span class="comment">#    (reduces API server load significantly)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Clear intent: "this config MUST NOT change"</span>

<span class="comment"># Works for Secrets too:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: prod-secret
immutable: true
stringData:
API_KEY: my-key-that-never-changes</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>immutable: true</code> prevents any modification. The main benefit is performance: kubelet stops watching for changes, reducing API server load (significant with many ConfigMaps). Once set, you CANNOT unset it — you must delete and recreate. On the CKA, this might appear as: "Ensure this ConfigMap cannot be accidentally modified." Apply <code>immutable: true</code>. Works for both ConfigMaps and Secrets. Note: pods mounting an immutable ConfigMap must be restarted after recreating the ConfigMap.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>docker-registry</code> secret type and how do you use it to pull images from a private registry?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create docker-registry secret</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Create docker-registry secret:</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
--docker-server=registry.example.com \
--docker-username=myuser \
--docker-password=mypass \
--docker-email=user@example.com

<span class="comment"># Use in a pod:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: private-app
spec:
imagePullSecrets:               # ← Pod-level field
- name: regcred                 # ← Reference the secret
containers:
- name: app
image: registry.example.com/myapp:v1

<span class="comment"># Or attach to a ServiceAccount (all pods inherit):</span>
kubectl patch serviceaccount default \
-p '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "regcred"}]}'

<span class="comment"># Verify the secret content:</span>
kubectl get secret regcred -o jsonpath='{.data.\.dockerconfigjson}' \
| base64 -d | python -m json.tool
<span class="comment"># {</span>
<span class="comment">#   "auths": {</span>
<span class="comment">#     "registry.example.com": {</span>
<span class="comment">#       "username": "myuser",</span>
<span class="comment">#       "password": "mypass",</span>
<span class="comment">#       "auth": "bXl1c2VyOm15cGFzcw=="</span>
<span class="comment">#     }</span>
<span class="comment">#   }</span>
<span class="comment"># }</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>docker-registry</code> secret type stores Docker credentials in <code>.dockerconfigjson</code> format. Use it with <code>imagePullSecrets</code> in the pod spec or attach to a ServiceAccount for automatic injection. On the CKA, if a pod fails with <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> from a private registry, create a <code>docker-registry</code> secret and reference it. Attaching to the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount affects all pods in the namespace that don't specify a different SA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod can't access its ConfigMap values. The ConfigMap exists and the pod is running. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check 1: is the configmap in the same namespace?</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check 1: Is the ConfigMap in the same namespace?</span>
kubectl get configmap app-config -n production
<span class="comment"># Error: not found ← ConfigMap is in wrong namespace!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 2: Pod spec references correct name?</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o yaml | grep -A5 configMapRef
<span class="comment">#   configMapRef:</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: app-configg  ← Typo! Extra 'g'</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 3: env vs envFrom?</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- env | grep DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># (empty) ← Env var not set!</span>

<span class="comment"># Possible causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - envFrom only imports keys that are valid env var names</span>
<span class="comment">#   Key "my-config.key" with dot → SKIPPED silently!</span>
<span class="comment"># - env with key that doesn't exist → pod won't start</span>
<span class="comment">#   (pod stays ContainerCreating with error event)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 4: Volume mount present?</span>
kubectl describe pod my-pod | grep -A5 Mounts
kubectl exec my-pod -- ls /etc/config/
<span class="comment"># ls: /etc/config/: No such file or directory ← Mount missing!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 5: ConfigMap has the right keys?</span>
kubectl describe configmap app-config
<span class="comment"># Data</span>
<span class="comment"># ====</span>
<span class="comment"># db_host:   ← lowercase!</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod expects DB_HOST ← case mismatch!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ConfigMap troubleshooting checklist: (1) same namespace? (2) name spelled correctly? (3) key names valid for env vars? (4) volume mount correct? (5) key case match? On the CKA, the most common issues are namespace mismatch (ConfigMap in different ns than pod) and typos in names/keys. If using <code>envFrom</code>, keys with special characters are silently skipped. If using <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code> with a non-existent key, the pod fails to start — check Events with <code>describe</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you create a Secret from a certificate file? Show creating a TLS Secret and verifying it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create tls secret from cert and key files</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create TLS Secret from cert and key files:</span>
kubectl create secret tls app-tls \
--cert=server.crt \
--key=server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get secret app-tls -o yaml
<span class="comment"># type: kubernetes.io/tls</span>
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   tls.crt: LS0tLS1CRUd...   ← base64 of cert</span>
<span class="comment">#   tls.key: LS0tLS1CRUd...   ← base64 of key</span>

<span class="comment"># Decode and inspect the certificate:</span>
kubectl get secret app-tls \
-o jsonpath='{.data.tls\.crt}' | base64 -d | openssl x509 -text -noout
<span class="comment"># Subject: CN = myapp.example.com</span>
<span class="comment"># Issuer: ...</span>
<span class="comment"># Validity: ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Use in an Ingress:</span>
spec:
tls:
- hosts:
- myapp.example.com
secretName: app-tls      # ← Reference TLS secret

<span class="comment"># Create generic secret from file:</span>
kubectl create secret generic ssh-key \
--from-file=id_rsa=/path/to/private-key
<span class="comment"># Key = "id_rsa", value = file contents</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl create secret tls</code> validates that the cert and key are valid PEM format. The Secret type is <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> with required keys <code>tls.crt</code> and <code>tls.key</code>. On the CKA, TLS secrets are used with Ingress TLS termination and are covered in the security domain. Use <code>--from-file</code> on <code>generic</code> secrets for arbitrary files (SSH keys, config files). The filename becomes the key unless you use <code>--from-file=custom-key=/path</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 23 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
                </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART6 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part6-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 6 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Storage — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You can now give easypj durable, dynamically-provisioned storage and inject its configuration cleanly. From the volume model and its lifecycle, through the PV/PVC binding and StorageClasses, to ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets — easypj’s Postgres data survives any pod, and its settings live outside the image. This is the CKA’s 10% Storage domain.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 🟡 Part 6 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Volumes (Ch 21):</strong> a volume is tied to the pod’s lifetime; emptyDir = scratch that survives container (not pod) restarts; hostPath = a specific node’s path; CSI is the storage plug-point.</li>
                    <li><strong>PV/PVC (Ch 22):</strong> a PVC requests storage, a PV supplies it; they bind by size + accessModes + StorageClass. StorageClasses enable <em>dynamic</em> provisioning; reclaimPolicy decides delete vs retain.</li>
                    <li><strong>ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets (Ch 23):</strong> inject config/credentials as env vars or mounted files; Secrets are base64 (not encrypted by default) — enable encryption-at-rest and RBAC.</li>
                    <li><strong>Access modes:</strong> RWO (one node) · ROX (many read) · RWX (many read-write) — must match between PVC and PV.</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> 10% here — create a PVC and mount it, bind a static PV, and project a ConfigMap/Secret are fast points.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 6 Visual Summary — request → bind → mount, plus config injection</div>
<pre>
   📝 PVC (request: 10Gi, RWO)  ──bind──▶  💽 PV (supply)  ◀──dynamic── 🏷️ StorageClass
            │ mounted at /var/lib/postgresql/data
            ▼
   📦 easypj-db pod  ◀── ⚙️ ConfigMap (settings) + 🔒 Secret (password) as env/files
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   data survives pod deletion; config lives outside the image.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 6 made easypj stateful and configurable. A PVC asks for storage and binds to a PV (statically or dynamically via a StorageClass); the pod mounts that PVC so its data outlives any restart. ConfigMaps and Secrets feed settings and credentials in as env vars or files.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> remember request (PVC) → supply (PV) → mount, the access-mode match, and that Secrets aren’t encrypted by default, and you own the 10% Storage domain. Next: Part 7 locks the whole thing down with RBAC, identity, PKI, and Pod Security.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART7 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part7">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 7 · Security</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Lock It Down</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 24–27 · RBAC · PKI · <em>"Least privilege, end to end"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">A cluster anyone can do anything on is a breach waiting to happen. Part 7 is the access-control core: RBAC (Roles/Bindings) that grant least privilege, ServiceAccounts and projected tokens that identify workloads, the cluster PKI and certificate workflow that authenticates everyone, and Pod Security + admission control that constrain what pods may do.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> write Roles/ClusterRoles and bind them to users and ServiceAccounts; test access with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>; read the cluster PKI and sign a CertificateSigningRequest; and enforce Pod Security Standards via admission.</div>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Real-World Analogy &amp; Myth-Busting Table</h4>
                <p>A part-specific real-world analogy (with an ASCII mapping table) and a "Before You Start" myth-vs-reality comparison table go here — matching the pattern established in Parts 1–3.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — who are you, and what may you do?</div>
<pre>
   Ch 24 RBAC  ──▶  Ch 25 SERVICE ACCOUNTS  ──▶  Ch 26 TLS / PKI
   Role · Binding     workload identity · tokens      certs · CSR · CA
   can-i              projected tokens                kubeconfig auth
            └────────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                                 ▼
            Ch 27 POD SECURITY &amp; ADMISSION CONTROL (constrain pods)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: every request to easypj is authenticated, authorized,
   and admitted only if it meets the cluster’s security baseline.
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch24">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 24 · RBAC — Role-Based Access Control</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">In a production Kubernetes cluster, you don't want every developer deleting nodes or every CI pipeline creating ClusterRoleBindings. <strong>RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)</strong> is Kubernetes' built-in authorization system that controls <strong>WHO</strong> can perform <strong>WHAT actions</strong> on <strong>WHICH resources</strong>. For our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django app, RBAC lets us give developers permission to deploy pods while preventing them from modifying secrets or disrupting the cluster. This chapter covers all four RBAC objects, how to create and test them, and real-world scenarios you'll face on the CKA exam.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔒 RBAC</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Why RBAC exists and what happens without it — the blast radius of unrestricted access</li>
                    <li>✅ The four RBAC objects: Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding</li>
                    <li>✅ How to create Roles with specific verbs, resources, and API groups</li>
                    <li>✅ How to bind Roles to users, groups, and ServiceAccounts</li>
                    <li>✅ Testing permissions with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> and the <code>--as</code> flag</li>
                    <li>✅ Building a complete RBAC setup for the easypj project with dev, QA, and ops roles</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>RBAC Is Heavily Tested on the CKA</h5>
                <p>Expect 2-3 questions creating Roles/Bindings and debugging access issues. Key exam skills:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Create Role and RoleBinding imperatively with <code>kubectl create role</code> and <code>kubectl create rolebinding</code></li>
                    <li>Know the difference between Role (namespaced) and ClusterRole (cluster-wide)</li>
                    <li>Use <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> with <code>--as</code> to verify permissions</li>
                    <li>Understand that a ClusterRole can be bound at namespace level with a RoleBinding</li>
                    <li>Write RBAC YAML from scratch — practice until it's muscle memory</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Role + RoleBinding — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">This is the most common RBAC pattern on the CKA. Memorize it.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># 1. Role — WHAT permissions (namespaced)</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader                   # ◀── role name
  namespace: easypj                  # ◀── MUST specify namespace
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]                    # ◀── "" = core API group
  resources: ["pods"]                # ◀── resource type
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]    # ◀── allowed actions
- apiGroups: ["apps"]                # ◀── apps API group
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
<span class="comment"># 2. RoleBinding — WHO gets the permissions</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: read-pods-binding
  namespace: easypj                  # ◀── same namespace as Role
subjects:
- kind: User                        # ◀── User, Group, or ServiceAccount
  name: jane                        # ◀── the WHO
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role                        # ◀── Role or ClusterRole
  name: pod-reader                   # ◀── must match Role name
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h4>RBAC Exam Traps</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">ClusterRoleBinding can reference a Role</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">ClusterRoleBinding can ONLY reference a ClusterRole — never a Role</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">A RoleBinding with a ClusterRole gives cluster-wide access</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">RoleBinding + ClusterRole = ClusterRole permissions scoped to THAT namespace only</span></li>
                    <li><code>apiGroups: [""]</code> means core API — pods, services, configmaps. Apps group needs <code>["apps"]</code></li>
                    <li>Verbs are: <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>watch</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>deletecollection</code></li>
                    <li>ServiceAccount subject format: <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> returns "yes"/"no" — use <code>--list</code> to see ALL permissions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find RBAC YAML Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain role.rules                     <span class="comment"># permission rules</span>
kubectl explain role.rules.apiGroups           <span class="comment"># API group filter</span>
kubectl explain role.rules.resources           <span class="comment"># resource types</span>
kubectl explain role.rules.verbs               <span class="comment"># allowed actions</span>
kubectl explain rolebinding.subjects           <span class="comment"># who gets access</span>
kubectl explain rolebinding.roleRef            <span class="comment"># which role to bind</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="flow-diagram">
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">👤</span><span>User / SA</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🔐</span><span>Authentication</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">✅</span><span>RBAC Check</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🛡️</span><span>Admission</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">💾</span><span>etcd</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">4</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">RBAC Objects</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">8</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Verbs (get, list...delete)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">25%</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">CKA Exam Domain Weight</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-red">
                    <span class="metric-value">3</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Subject Types (User/Group/SA)</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.1 What is RBAC and Why It Matters</h3>
                <p>RBAC answers one question: <strong>"Is this user allowed to do this action on this resource?"</strong> Without RBAC, anyone who can reach the Kubernetes API server can create, delete, or modify anything — pods, secrets, nodes, the entire cluster.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Without RBAC vs With RBAC — The Security Gate</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ WITHOUT RBAC (or permissive RBAC)         ✅ WITH PROPER RBAC            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────┐                          ┌──────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 👨‍💻 Developer  │──┐                      │ 👨‍💻 Developer  │──┐              │
  │  └──────────────┘  │                      └──────────────┘  │              │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  │   ┌──────────┐       ┌──────────────┐  │  ┌────────┐ │
  │  │ 🤖 CI/CD Bot  │──┼──▶│ API      │       │ 🤖 CI/CD Bot  │──┼─▶│ RBAC   │ │
  │  └──────────────┘  │   │ Server   │       └──────────────┘  │  │ Gate   │ │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  │   │          │       ┌──────────────┐  │  │        │ │
  │  │ 🧪 Intern    │──┘   │ FULL     │       │ 🧪 Intern    │──┘  │ ✅ pods │ │
  │  └──────────────┘      │ ACCESS   │       └──────────────┘     │ ❌ nodes│ │
  │                         │ TO       │                            │ ❌ sec. │ │
  │  Everyone can:         │ EVERY-   │       Only allowed          │        │ │
  │  • Delete nodes 💀    │ THING    │       actions pass →       └───┬────┘ │
  │  • Read secrets 🔓    │          │                               │      │
  │  • Modify RBAC 😱     │          │                          ┌────▼─────┐ │
  │  • Break cluster 💣   └──────────┘                          │ API      │ │
  │                                                              │ Server   │ │
  │                                                              └──────────┘ │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Real-World Disaster Without RBAC</h5>
                    <p>In 2018, Tesla's Kubernetes dashboard was left open without RBAC. Attackers accessed the cluster, found AWS credentials in environment variables, and used Tesla's infrastructure for cryptocurrency mining. <strong>RBAC would have prevented this entirely.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔑 The Three Questions RBAC Answers</h5>
                    <p>Every API request is checked against these three dimensions:</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👤</div>
                        <h5>WHO?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Subject</strong> — User, Group, or ServiceAccount making the request</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>WHAT?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Verb</strong> — The action: get, list, create, update, delete, watch, patch</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>WHICH?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Resource</strong> — pods, services, secrets, nodes, deployments, etc.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">check-rbac-enabled.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify RBAC is enabled on your cluster</span>
kubectl api-versions | grep rbac
<span class="comment"># Output: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</span>

<span class="comment"># Check API server authorization mode</span>
kubectl describe pod kube-apiserver-controlplane -n kube-system | grep authorization-mode
<span class="comment"># Output: --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.2 The Four RBAC Objects</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes RBAC uses exactly <strong>four objects</strong> that work in pairs: two define permissions (Role, ClusterRole) and two assign permissions (RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Four RBAC Objects — How They Connect</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                          RBAC OBJECT MAP                                     │
  │                                                                              │
  │         DEFINE PERMISSIONS              ASSIGN PERMISSIONS                   │
  │         (What can be done?)            (Who gets them?)                      │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────────────┐            │
  │  │   📋 Role               │     │   🔗 RoleBinding             │            │
  │  │   Scope: ONE namespace  │◀────│   Scope: ONE namespace      │            │
  │  │                         │     │                             │            │
  │  │   "pods: get,list"      │     │   "jane → pod-reader"      │            │
  │  │   in namespace: easypj  │     │   in namespace: easypj     │            │
  │  └─────────────────────────┘     └──────────────┬──────────────┘            │
  │                                                  │                          │
  │                                        subjects: │                          │
  │                                    ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐            │
  │                                    │             │             │            │
  │                                 ┌──▼──┐    ┌────▼───┐   ┌────▼────┐       │
  │                                 │User │    │ Group  │   │Service  │       │
  │                                 │jane │    │dev-team│   │Account  │       │
  │                                 └─────┘    └────────┘   └─────────┘       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────────────┐            │
  │  │   📋 ClusterRole        │     │   🔗 ClusterRoleBinding      │            │
  │  │   Scope: ENTIRE cluster │◀────│   Scope: ENTIRE cluster     │            │
  │  │                         │     │                             │            │
  │  │   "nodes: get,list"     │     │   "ops-admin → node-viewer"│            │
  │  │   cluster-wide          │     │   cluster-wide              │            │
  │  └─────────────────────────┘     └─────────────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  💡 TRICK: A RoleBinding CAN reference a ClusterRole!                       │
  │     This gives ClusterRole permissions scoped to ONE namespace.              │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Object</th>
                                <th>Scope</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                <th>Can Reference</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Role</strong></td>
                                <td>Namespace</td>
                                <td>Define permissions within a single namespace</td>
                                <td>—</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ClusterRole</strong></td>
                                <td>Cluster</td>
                                <td>Define permissions cluster-wide (nodes, PVs, namespaces)</td>
                                <td>—</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>RoleBinding</strong></td>
                                <td>Namespace</td>
                                <td>Grant Role OR ClusterRole to subjects in a namespace</td>
                                <td>Role or ClusterRole</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ClusterRoleBinding</strong></td>
                                <td>Cluster</td>
                                <td>Grant ClusterRole to subjects cluster-wide</td>
                                <td>ClusterRole only</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="relationship-map">
                    <div class="rel-hub">RBAC Decision</div>
                    <div class="rel-spokes">
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">👤</span>
                            <strong>Subject</strong>
                            <small>User, Group, or ServiceAccount</small>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">⚡</span>
                            <strong>Verb</strong>
                            <small>get, list, create, delete...</small>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">📦</span>
                            <strong>Resource</strong>
                            <small>pods, services, secrets, nodes</small>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">🏷️</span>
                            <strong>API Group</strong>
                            <small>"" (core), apps, rbac, networking</small>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">🌐</span>
                            <strong>Namespace</strong>
                            <small>Scoped or cluster-wide</small>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 When to Use ClusterRole + RoleBinding</h5>
                    <p>Create ONE ClusterRole (e.g., "pod-reader") and bind it in multiple namespaces with separate RoleBindings. This avoids duplicating Role definitions in every namespace. The ClusterRole is reused, but access is scoped per namespace.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.3 Creating Roles and ClusterRoles</h3>
                <p>A Role is a set of <strong>rules</strong>. Each rule specifies an API group, a list of resources, and the verbs (actions) allowed on them.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 The Three Parts of Every RBAC Rule</h5>
                    <p><code>apiGroups</code> → <code>resources</code> → <code>verbs</code> — "In this API group, on these resources, allow these actions"</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>API Group</th>
                                <th>Value in YAML</th>
                                <th>Resources</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Core</td><td><code>""</code></td><td>pods, services, configmaps, secrets, PVCs, nodes, namespaces</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Apps</td><td><code>"apps"</code></td><td>deployments, replicasets, statefulsets, daemonsets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Batch</td><td><code>"batch"</code></td><td>jobs, cronjobs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Networking</td><td><code>"networking.k8s.io"</code></td><td>networkpolicies, ingresses</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>RBAC</td><td><code>"rbac.authorization.k8s.io"</code></td><td>roles, rolebindings, clusterroles, clusterrolebindings</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Storage</td><td><code>"storage.k8s.io"</code></td><td>storageclasses, csinodes</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">role-pod-reader.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Role: Read-only access to pods in easypj namespace</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]              <span class="comment"># core API group</span>
  resources: ["pods", "pods/log"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["services"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">clusterrole-node-viewer.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ClusterRole: View nodes cluster-wide (no namespace)</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: node-viewer              <span class="comment"># no namespace field!</span>
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["nodes"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["persistentvolumes"]   <span class="comment"># PVs are cluster-scoped</span>
  verbs: ["get", "list"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">imperative-role-creation.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Imperative — faster on the exam!</span>
kubectl create role pod-reader \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=pods,pods/log \
  -n easypj

kubectl create clusterrole node-viewer \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=nodes

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML to inspect before applying</span>
kubectl create role deploy-manager \
  --verb=get,list,create,update,delete \
  --resource=deployments.apps \
  -n easypj \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 Resource Sub-resources</h5>
                    <p>Some resources have sub-resources like <code>pods/log</code>, <code>pods/exec</code>, <code>pods/portforward</code>. These must be listed separately in the <code>resources</code> array. Getting pod logs requires both <code>pods</code> and <code>pods/log</code> permissions.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.4 Creating RoleBindings and ClusterRoleBindings</h3>
                <p>A Binding connects a <strong>subject</strong> (who) to a <strong>role</strong> (what permissions). The binding itself determines the scope.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Binding Combinations — All 4 Valid Configurations</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    ALL VALID BINDING COMBINATIONS                             │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 1️⃣ Role         │───▶│ RoleBinding       │───▶│ Access in ONE       │     │
  │  │   (namespaced)  │    │ (namespaced)      │    │ namespace           │     │
  │  └─────────────────┘    └──────────────────┘    └─────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 2️⃣ ClusterRole  │───▶│ ClusterRoleBinding│───▶│ Access EVERYWHERE   │     │
  │  │   (cluster)     │    │ (cluster)         │    │ (all namespaces)    │     │
  │  └─────────────────┘    └──────────────────┘    └─────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 3️⃣ ClusterRole  │───▶│ RoleBinding       │───▶│ ClusterRole scoped  │     │
  │  │   (cluster)     │    │ (namespaced)      │    │ to ONE namespace!   │     │
  │  └─────────────────┘    └──────────────────┘    └─────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                  ☝️ Reuse ClusterRole       │
  │                                                     across namespaces       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐                                │
  │  │ 4️⃣ Role         │ ✘  │ ClusterRoleBinding│    ❌ INVALID! Never works    │
  │  │   (namespaced)  │    │ (cluster)         │                                │
  │  └─────────────────┘    └──────────────────┘                                │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">rolebinding-user.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Bind "pod-reader" role to user "jane" in easypj namespace</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: jane-pod-reader
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: jane
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">clusterrolebinding-admin.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Give "ops-admin" cluster-wide node viewing access</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: ops-node-viewer
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: ops-admin
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole          <span class="comment"># ClusterRoleBinding MUST ref ClusterRole</span>
  name: node-viewer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">imperative-bindings.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Imperative RoleBinding — FASTEST for exam</span>
kubectl create rolebinding jane-pod-reader \
  --role=pod-reader \
  --user=jane \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># ClusterRoleBinding</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding ops-node-viewer \
  --clusterrole=node-viewer \
  --user=ops-admin

<span class="comment"># Bind ClusterRole to namespace (combination #3)</span>
kubectl create rolebinding dev-pod-reader \
  --clusterrole=pod-reader-cluster \
  --group=dev-team \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Bind to ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create rolebinding sa-deployer \
  --role=deploy-manager \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ RoleBindings Are Immutable in roleRef</h5>
                    <p>You <strong>cannot change</strong> the <code>roleRef</code> field after creating a RoleBinding. To change which Role is referenced, you must delete and recreate the binding. You CAN add/remove subjects though.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.5 Testing RBAC</h3>
                <p>The <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> command is your best friend for RBAC verification. On the CKA exam, always verify after creating roles and bindings.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">testing-rbac.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Test specific permission</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=jane -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=jane -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (jane only has get,list,watch)</span>

kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌</span>

<span class="comment"># Test cluster-scoped resources</span>
kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=ops-admin
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=ops-admin
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (only get,list,watch)</span>

<span class="comment"># List ALL permissions for a user in a namespace</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Resources       Non-Resource URLs   Resource Names   Verbs</span>
<span class="comment"># pods             []                  []               [get list watch]</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/log         []                  []               [get list watch]</span>
<span class="comment"># services         []                  []               [get list]</span>

<span class="comment"># Test as a ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># SelfSubjectAccessReview — check YOUR OWN permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes (checks your current kubeconfig identity)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">RBAC Testing Decision Tree</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                   RBAC TESTING WORKFLOW                          │
  │                                                                  │
  │     Step 1: Create Role/ClusterRole                             │
  │         │                                                        │
  │         ▼                                                        │
  │     Step 2: Create Binding                                      │
  │         │                                                        │
  │         ▼                                                        │
  │     Step 3: Test with can-i                                     │
  │         │                                                        │
  │    ┌────┴────┐                                                  │
  │    │         │                                                  │
  │  "yes" ✅  "no" ❌                                              │
  │    │         │                                                  │
  │    ▼         ▼                                                  │
  │  DONE    Debug:                                                 │
  │          ├── kubectl get rolebinding -n &lt;ns&gt; -o yaml           │
  │          ├── Check: namespace matches?                          │
  │          ├── Check: role name matches?                          │
  │          ├── Check: subject name correct?                       │
  │          ├── Check: apiGroup correct?                           │
  │          └── Check: verb and resource correct?                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Speed Tip: Use --list for Debugging</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n easypj</code> shows ALL permissions at once. Much faster than testing individual verbs when debugging why something is denied.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.6 RBAC for easypj — Practical Example</h3>
                <p>Let's build a complete RBAC setup for the <strong>easypj</strong> job board application with three team roles: <strong>developers</strong> (full deploy access), <strong>QA team</strong> (read-only), and <strong>monitoring</strong> (read across all namespaces).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj RBAC Architecture — Three Teams, Three Access Levels</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    easypj RBAC SETUP                                          │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐                                                       │
  │  │ 👨‍💻 DEVELOPERS     │    Role: easypj-developer                            │
  │  │ (group: dev-team)│───▶ pods: get,list,watch,create,update,delete         │
  │  │                  │    deployments: get,list,create,update                 │
  │  │ Full deploy      │    services: get,list,create                          │
  │  │ access in easypj │    configmaps: get,list                               │
  │  └──────────────────┘    Scope: namespace "easypj" only                     │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐                                                       │
  │  │ 🧪 QA TEAM        │    Role: easypj-qa-viewer                             │
  │  │ (group: qa-team) │───▶ pods: get,list,watch                              │
  │  │                  │    pods/log: get                                       │
  │  │ Read-only in     │    deployments: get,list                              │
  │  │ easypj           │    services: get,list                                 │
  │  └──────────────────┘    Scope: namespace "easypj" only                     │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐                                                       │
  │  │ 📊 MONITORING     │    ClusterRole: monitoring-reader                     │
  │  │ (user: monitor)  │───▶ pods: get,list,watch (ALL namespaces)             │
  │  │                  │    nodes: get,list                                     │
  │  │ Read access      │    events: get,list                                   │
  │  │ across cluster   │    Scope: cluster-wide (ClusterRoleBinding)           │
  │  └──────────────────┘                                                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-rbac-complete.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ── 1. Developer Role ──
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-developer
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "pods/log", "pods/exec", "services", "configmaps"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "create", "update", "delete"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments", "replicasets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "create", "update", "patch"]
---
<span class="comment"># ── 2. Developer RoleBinding ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: dev-team-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: Group
  name: dev-team
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-developer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
<span class="comment"># ── 3. QA Read-Only Role ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-qa-viewer
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "pods/log", "services", "events"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
<span class="comment"># ── 4. QA RoleBinding ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: qa-team-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: Group
  name: qa-team
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-qa-viewer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
<span class="comment"># ── 5. Monitoring ClusterRole ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: monitoring-reader
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "nodes", "services", "events"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments", "replicasets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
<span class="comment"># ── 6. Monitoring ClusterRoleBinding ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: monitoring-binding
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: monitor
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: monitoring-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">verify-easypj-rbac.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply all RBAC resources</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-rbac-complete.yaml

<span class="comment"># ── Test developer access ──</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods --as-group=dev-team --as=dev-user -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as-group=dev-team --as=dev-user
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (no cluster access)</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Test QA access ──</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods --as-group=qa-team --as=qa-user -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as-group=qa-team --as=qa-user -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (read-only)</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Test monitoring access ──</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=monitor -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># yes ✅ (all namespaces)</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=monitor -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (read-only)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ RBAC Best Practices</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Use <strong>Groups</strong> over individual Users</li>
                            <li>Apply <strong>least privilege</strong> — only verbs actually needed</li>
                            <li>Use ClusterRole + RoleBinding for reusable rules</li>
                            <li>Always <code>can-i</code> test after creating bindings</li>
                            <li>Name roles descriptively: <code>easypj-developer</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ RBAC Anti-Patterns</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Binding <code>cluster-admin</code> to regular users</li>
                            <li>Using <code>*</code> wildcard verbs and resources</li>
                            <li>Not testing after creating bindings</li>
                            <li>One huge Role instead of multiple specific ones</li>
                            <li>Forgetting namespace on RoleBindings</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─── RBAC Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>RBAC Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=USER -n NS</code> → verify access granted/denied</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get roles -n NS</code> → confirm role exists with correct name</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get rolebindings -n NS</code> → confirm binding exists</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe rolebinding NAME -n NS</code> → verify subject and roleRef match</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=USER -n NS</code> → list ALL permissions for user</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get clusterroles | grep NAME</code> → confirm ClusterRole exists</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe clusterrolebinding NAME</code> → verify cluster-wide binding</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.7 Practice Exercises</h3>
                <p>Practice these scenarios until you can complete each one in under 5 minutes — that's CKA exam speed.</p>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 4 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 24.1 — Create Role and RoleBinding</h5>
                        <p>Create RBAC resources so user <code>jane</code> can read pods in a specific namespace.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create namespace <code>exam-practice</code></li>
                            <li>Create Role <code>pod-reader</code> allowing <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>watch</code> on pods</li>
                            <li>Create RoleBinding binding <code>pod-reader</code> to user <code>jane</code></li>
                            <li>Test: <code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=jane -n exam-practice</code> → should return <strong>yes</strong></li>
                            <li>Test: <code>kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=jane -n exam-practice</code> → should return <strong>no</strong></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Use imperative commands — <code>kubectl create role</code> and <code>kubectl create rolebinding</code> are fastest.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Role</span>
kubectl create role pod-reader \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=pods \
  -n exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-binding \
  --role=pod-reader \
  --user=jane \
  -n exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=jane -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># yes</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=jane -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># no</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 3 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 24.2 — ClusterRole for Node Viewer</h5>
                        <p>Create a ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding so user <code>ops-admin</code> can view nodes cluster-wide.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create ClusterRole <code>node-viewer</code> allowing <code>get</code>, <code>list</code> on nodes</li>
                            <li>Create ClusterRoleBinding for user <code>ops-admin</code></li>
                            <li>Verify: <code>kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=ops-admin</code> → <strong>yes</strong></li>
                            <li>Verify: <code>kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=ops-admin</code> → <strong>no</strong></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding = cluster-wide access (no namespace flag).</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create ClusterRole</span>
kubectl create clusterrole node-viewer \
  --verb=get,list \
  --resource=nodes

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create ClusterRoleBinding</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding node-viewer-binding \
  --clusterrole=node-viewer \
  --user=ops-admin

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=ops-admin
<span class="comment"># yes</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=ops-admin
<span class="comment"># no</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 24.3 — ServiceAccount RBAC</h5>
                        <p>Grant the <code>easypj-deployer</code> ServiceAccount permissions to manage deployments and services in the <code>easypj</code> namespace.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create ServiceAccount <code>easypj-deployer</code> in namespace <code>easypj</code></li>
                            <li>Create Role allowing <code>create</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>update</code> on deployments and services</li>
                            <li>Bind ServiceAccount to the Role</li>
                            <li>Test: <code>kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-deployer -n easypj</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: The <code>--as</code> impersonation for ServiceAccounts uses the format <code>system:serviceaccount:NAMESPACE:SA_NAME</code>.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-deployer -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Role with multiple resources</span>
kubectl create role easypj-deploy-role \
  --verb=create,get,list,update \
  --resource=deployments,services \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Bind SA to Role</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-deploy-binding \
  --role=easypj-deploy-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no — only deployments and services are allowed</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 24.4 — Debug RBAC Issues</h5>
                        <p>Intentionally create a broken Role, identify the issue, and fix it.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create a Role with the wrong verb (e.g., <code>getlist</code> instead of <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>)</li>
                            <li>Bind it and test — observe the failure</li>
                            <li>Use <code>kubectl auth can-i --list</code> to find what the user CAN do</li>
                            <li>Fix the Role and verify access is restored</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Use <code>kubectl edit role</code> to fix the verbs. Each verb must be a separate list item, not concatenated.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create broken Role (wrong verb)</span>
kubectl create role broken-role \
  --verb=getlist \
  --resource=pods \
  -n exam-practice

kubectl create rolebinding broken-binding \
  --role=broken-role \
  --user=testuser \
  -n exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Test — fails because "getlist" isn't a valid verb</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=testuser -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># no</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Debug — see what user CAN do</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=testuser -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># Shows "getlist" verb — not "get" or "list"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Fix the Role</span>
kubectl edit role broken-role -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># Change verbs from ["getlist"] to ["get", "list"]</span>
<span class="comment"># Or delete and recreate:</span>
kubectl delete role broken-role -n exam-practice
kubectl create role broken-role \
  --verb=get,list \
  --resource=pods \
  -n exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify — now works</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=testuser -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>⏱️ Target: 5 Minutes Per Exercise</h5>
                    <p>On the CKA exam, RBAC questions must be fast. Use imperative commands (<code>kubectl create role</code>, <code>kubectl create rolebinding</code>) not YAML files. Only fall back to YAML for complex multi-rule roles.</p>
                </div>

            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How RBAC Authorization Is Evaluated: Role/ClusterRole Resolution, Binding Matching, and the can-i Algorithm</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ RBAC Evaluation Algorithm — How the API Server Decides ALLOW/DENY</h5>
            <p>Every API request passes through the RBAC authorizer. Understanding the exact evaluation order helps you debug "why can't this user do X" and craft correct policies.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  RBAC AUTHORIZATION PIPELINE — Request Evaluation                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Request: User "jane" wants to GET /api/v1/namespaces/dev/pods               │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: Extract Subject Identity                                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ From TLS cert: CN=jane, O=developers, O=viewers                      │   │
│  │ OR from JWT token: sub=jane, groups=[developers, viewers]            │   │
│  │ → Subject: User=jane, Groups=[developers, viewers]                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: Build ALL Potential Roles (union of ALL bindings)                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Find RoleBindings matching jane OR [developers] OR [viewers]:         │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RoleBinding: pod-reader (namespace: dev)                               │   │
│  │   subjects: [{kind:User, name:jane}]  <span class="comment">← Direct match!                 </span>│   │
│  │   roleRef: {kind:Role, name:pod-reader}                               │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RoleBinding: view-ns (namespace: dev)                                  │   │
│  │   subjects: [{kind:Group, name:viewers}] <span class="comment">← Group match!               </span>│   │
│  │   roleRef: {kind:ClusterRole, name:view}                              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ → Collected roles: [pod-reader (Role), view (ClusterRole)]            │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: Check EACH Role for Matching Rules (UNION of all rules)             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Role: pod-reader (namespace: dev):                                     │   │
│  │   rules:                                                               │   │
│  │   - apiGroups: [""]     <span class="comment">← core API group (v1)                        </span>│   │
│  │     resources: ["pods"]  <span class="comment">← Match! (request is for pods)              </span>│   │
│  │     verbs: ["get","list","watch"] <span class="comment">← Match! (request verb is "get")   </span>│   │
│  │   → RULE MATCHED! Pod reader allows get pods in dev.                  │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ ClusterRole: view:                                                     │   │
│  │   rules:                                                               │   │
│  │   - apiGroups: [""]                                                    │   │
│  │     resources: ["pods","services","deployments",...]                 │   │
│  │     verbs: ["get","list","watch"]                                    │   │
│  │   → RULE ALSO MATCHED (but redundant — pod-reader already covers it) │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 4: FINAL DECISION                                                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ At least one matching rule found → ALLOW ✅                           │   │
│  │ No matching rules found → DENY ❌                                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RBAC IS ADDITIVE ONLY:                                                 │   │
│  │   ✅ Roles ADD permissions (union of all matching rules)              │   │
│  │   ❌ There is NO "deny" rule in RBAC                                 │   │
│  │   ❌ You cannot "subtract" a permission once granted                  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CLUSTERROLE vs ROLE — SCOPE MATTERS:                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Rule:     apiGroups: [""]                                             │   │
│  │           resources: ["nodes"]                                        │   │
│  │           verbs: ["get"]                                              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ In a ROLE (namespaced):        In a CLUSTERROLE (cluster-wide):       │   │
│  │ → Can get pods in dev          → Can get pods in ALL namespaces      │   │
│  │ → Can get services in dev      → Can get nodes (cluster-scoped)       │   │
│  │ → CANNOT get nodes!             → Can get namespaces (cluster-scoped) │   │
│  │   (nodes are cluster-scoped)                                           │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ A ClusterRole can be bound via:                                        │   │
│  │   RoleBinding → grants ClusterRole permissions in ONE namespace       │   │
│  │   ClusterRoleBinding → grants ClusterRole permissions CLUSTER-WIDE    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  IMPERSONATION — Testing RBAC (CKA Superpower):                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n dev             │   │
│  │ yes  /  no                                                            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ $ kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n dev                          │   │
│  │ Resources  Non-Resource URLs  Resource Names  Verbs                   │   │
│  │ pods       []                 []              [get list watch]        │   │
│  │ configmaps []                 []              [get]                   │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> RBAC is a guaranteed CKA topic. The exam tests creating Roles + RoleBindings for specific scenarios. Key tips: (1) RBAC is additive only — no deny rules, (2) ClusterRole + RoleBinding = namespaced permissions, (3) ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding = cluster-wide, (4) Always test with <code>kubectl auth can-i --as=&lt;user&gt;</code> before submitting your answer, (5) RoleBinding subjects support User, Group, and ServiceAccount.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">ClusterRoleBinding can reference a Role</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">ClusterRoleBinding can ONLY reference a ClusterRole — never a Role</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">A RoleBinding with a ClusterRole gives cluster-wide access</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">RoleBinding + ClusterRole = ClusterRole permissions scoped to THAT namespace only</span></li>
                    <li><code>apiGroups: [""]</code> means core API — pods, services, configmaps. Apps group needs <code>["apps"]</code></li>
                    <li>Verbs are: <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>watch</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>deletecollection</code></li>
                    <li>ServiceAccount subject format: <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> returns "yes"/"no" — use <code>--list</code> to see ALL permissions</li>
                </ul>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Role vs ClusterRole</strong> — <code>Role</code> is namespace-scoped (pods, services). <code>ClusterRole</code> is cluster-wide (nodes, PVs, namespaces). If the exam says "allow listing nodes", you MUST use ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding</li>
                        <li><strong>RoleBinding can reference a ClusterRole</strong> — This is powerful and confusing. A RoleBinding in namespace <code>prod</code> pointing to a ClusterRole limits those permissions to <code>prod</code> only. The exam tests this</li>
                        <li><strong>Verbs are comma-separated strings, not arrays in CLI</strong> — <code>--verb=get,list,watch</code> NOT <code>--verb=[get,list,watch]</code>. In YAML they ARE arrays: <code>["get", "list"]</code></li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> is your verification tool</strong> — Always verify after creating RBAC: <code>kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n dev</code>. The <code>--as</code> flag impersonates a user</li>
                        <li><strong>API groups matter</strong> — Core resources (pods, services) use <code>apiGroups: [""]</code>. Deployments use <code>apiGroups: ["apps"]</code>. Forgetting the API group = silent permission denial</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — RBAC in 4 imperative commands:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n dev</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-binding --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n dev</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create clusterrole node-reader --verb=get,list --resource=nodes</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding node-reader-binding --clusterrole=node-reader --user=jane</code><br>
                <strong>Never write RBAC YAML from scratch on the exam unless forced to.</strong></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 24)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Create Role</td><td><code>kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create ClusterRole</td><td><code>kubectl create clusterrole node-viewer --verb=get,list --resource=nodes</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create RoleBinding</td><td><code>kubectl create rolebinding dev-bind --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create ClusterRoleBinding</td><td><code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding admin-bind --clusterrole=cluster-admin --user=admin</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Bind to ServiceAccount</td><td><code>kubectl create rolebinding sa-bind --role=pod-reader --serviceaccount=easypj:default -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Test permissions</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=jane -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List all permissions</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View roles</td><td><code>kubectl get roles,rolebindings -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-drill"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧪 Drill</h4>
                <p>A hands-on practice drill goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 24 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 24 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of Role/ClusterRole authorization — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Role named <code>pod-reader</code> in namespace <code>dev</code> that allows <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, and <code>watch</code> on pods. Then bind it to user <code>jane</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create role</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create Role:</span>
kubectl create role pod-reader \
--verb=get,list,watch \
--resource=pods \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># Create RoleBinding:</span>
kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-binding \
--role=pod-reader \
--user=jane \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n dev
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=jane -n dev
<span class="comment"># no ← Only get, list, watch!</span>

kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n prod
<span class="comment"># no ← Role only in dev namespace!</span>

<span class="comment"># View created objects:</span>
kubectl describe role pod-reader -n dev
kubectl describe rolebinding pod-reader-binding -n dev</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Roles define WHAT actions on WHICH resources. RoleBindings connect WHO (user/group/SA) to a Role. Both are namespace-scoped. On the CKA, use imperative commands — never write RBAC YAML from scratch unless forced. The <code>--verb</code> flag accepts comma-separated verbs. Verify with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>. Common verbs: <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>watch</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>deletecollection</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between Role/RoleBinding and ClusterRole/ClusterRoleBinding? When must you use ClusterRole?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Aspect</th>
                                        <th>Role + RoleBinding</th>
                                        <th>ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Scope</td>
                                        <td>Single namespace</td>
                                        <td>Cluster-wide</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Resources</td>
                                        <td>Namespaced (pods, svc, cm)</td>
                                        <td>All + non-namespaced (nodes, PVs, ns)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Use case</td>
                                        <td>Dev permissions in one ns</td>
                                        <td>Cluster admin, node ops</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Combo</td>
                                        <td>Role → RoleBinding only</td>
                                        <td>ClusterRole → RoleBinding OR ClusterRoleBinding</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">must use clusterrole for</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># MUST use ClusterRole for:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Nodes (non-namespaced)</span>
<span class="comment"># - PersistentVolumes (non-namespaced)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Namespaces (non-namespaced)</span>
<span class="comment"># - CSRs, ClusterRoles, ClusterRoleBindings</span>

<span class="comment"># Trick: ClusterRole + RoleBinding = scoped power!</span>
<span class="comment"># Grant ClusterRole permissions in ONE namespace:</span>
kubectl create rolebinding scoped-admin \
--clusterrole=admin \
--user=jane \
-n dev    # ← jane is admin ONLY in dev</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ClusterRoles are needed for non-namespaced resources (nodes, PVs, namespaces) and cross-namespace access. The power combo: ClusterRole + RoleBinding limits ClusterRole permissions to a single namespace. On the CKA, if you see "allow user to list nodes" → ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding (nodes are cluster-scoped). If "allow user to list pods in namespace dev" → Role + RoleBinding (or ClusterRole + RoleBinding in dev).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check what permissions a user has? List all permissions for user <code>dev-user</code> in namespace <code>production</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check specific permission</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check specific permission:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=dev-user -n production
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete deployments --as=dev-user -n production
<span class="comment"># no</span>

<span class="comment"># List ALL permissions:</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=dev-user -n production
<span class="comment"># Resources          Non-Resource URLs    Verbs</span>
<span class="comment"># pods               []                   [get list watch]</span>
<span class="comment"># services           []                   [get list]</span>
<span class="comment"># configmaps         []                   [get]</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments.apps   []                   [get list watch create update]</span>

<span class="comment"># For a ServiceAccount:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:production:deploy-sa -n production
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

<span class="comment"># Check if you (current user) can do something:</span>
kubectl auth can-i create clusterroles
<span class="comment"># no  (unless you're cluster-admin)</span>

<span class="comment"># Wildcard check:</span>
kubectl auth can-i '*' '*' --as=admin-user
<span class="comment"># yes ← Full admin!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> is the essential RBAC verification tool. Use <code>--as</code> to impersonate users, <code>--as=system:serviceaccount:NS:NAME</code> for ServiceAccounts. <code>--list</code> shows ALL permissions in a namespace. On the CKA, ALWAYS verify after creating RBAC objects — <code>auth can-i</code> confirms your configuration works. This is also how you debug "permission denied" errors: check what the user/SA can actually do.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What API group do you use for Deployments? For Pods? How does this affect RBAC rules?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">core api resources (apigroups: [""])</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Core API resources (apiGroups: [""]):</span>
<span class="comment"># pods, services, configmaps, secrets, PVCs, endpoints, nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># apps group (apiGroups: ["apps"]):</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments, replicasets, daemonsets, statefulsets</span>

<span class="comment"># networking.k8s.io:</span>
<span class="comment"># networkpolicies, ingresses</span>

<span class="comment"># rbac.authorization.k8s.io:</span>
<span class="comment"># roles, rolebindings, clusterroles, clusterrolebindings</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick lookup:</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep -i deployment
<span class="comment"># NAME          SHORTNAMES  APIVERSION  NAMESPACED  KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy      apps/v1     true        Deployment</span>
<span class="comment">#                           ↑ apps group!</span>

<span class="comment"># RBAC rule for Deployments:</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
name: deploy-manager
namespace: dev
rules:
- apiGroups: ["apps"]        # ← NOT empty string!
resources: ["deployments"]
verbs: ["get", "list", "create", "update", "delete"]
- apiGroups: [""]            # ← Core API (pods)
resources: ["pods"]
verbs: ["get", "list"]

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Forgetting apiGroups or using wrong group = silent denial!</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>API groups are critical in RBAC rules. Core resources (pods, services) use empty string <code>""</code>. Deployments use <code>"apps"</code>. NetworkPolicies use <code>"networking.k8s.io"</code>. Use <code>kubectl api-resources</code> to find the right group. On the CKA, wrong API group = the role won't grant access (silently fails). Most common mistake: using <code>apiGroups: [""]</code> for Deployments when it should be <code>apiGroups: ["apps"]</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ClusterRole that allows reading nodes and PersistentVolumes. Bind it to user <code>infra-admin</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create clusterrole</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ClusterRole:</span>
kubectl create clusterrole infra-reader \
--verb=get,list,watch \
--resource=nodes,persistentvolumes

<span class="comment"># Create ClusterRoleBinding:</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding infra-reader-binding \
--clusterrole=infra-reader \
--user=infra-admin

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=infra-admin
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i list pv --as=infra-admin
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=infra-admin
<span class="comment"># no ← Only read verbs!</span>

kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=infra-admin
<span class="comment"># no ← Only nodes and PVs!</span>

<span class="comment"># Multiple resources in one rule:</span>
<span class="comment"># The CLI creates:</span>
<span class="comment"># rules:</span>
<span class="comment"># - apiGroups: [""]</span>
<span class="comment">#   resources: ["nodes", "persistentvolumes"]</span>
<span class="comment">#   verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Nodes and PVs are cluster-scoped resources — they require ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding. A regular Role can't grant access to cluster-scoped resources. On the CKA, the imperative command handles multiple resources with comma separation. Both nodes and PVs are in the core API group (<code>""</code>). Verify with <code>auth can-i</code> — check both positive (what's allowed) and negative (what's denied) cases.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A user runs <code>kubectl get pods -n production</code> and gets <code>Forbidden</code>. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check what the user can do</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check what the user CAN do:</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># If empty → no permissions at all</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check RoleBindings in the namespace:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n production -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME           ROLE                 SUBJECTS</span>
<span class="comment"># dev-binding    Role/dev-role        User:jane  ← Found!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check the Role:</span>
kubectl describe role dev-role -n production
<span class="comment"># Rules:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Resources: [deployments]</span>
<span class="comment">#   Verbs: [get list]</span>
<span class="comment"># ← pods not included!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Fix — add pods to the role:</span>
kubectl edit role dev-role -n production
<span class="comment"># Add:</span>
<span class="comment"># - apiGroups: [""]</span>
<span class="comment">#   resources: ["pods"]</span>
<span class="comment">#   verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]</span>

<span class="comment"># Or create a new role and binding:</span>
kubectl create role pod-reader \
--verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n production
kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-jane \
--role=pod-reader --user=jane -n production

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify fix:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>RBAC troubleshooting flow: (1) check current permissions with <code>auth can-i --list</code>, (2) find RoleBindings with <code>-o wide</code> to see subjects, (3) describe the Role to see rules, (4) fix the gap. On the CKA, common issues: wrong namespace, missing verb (has <code>get</code> but not <code>list</code>), wrong API group, or no binding at all. The <code>-o wide</code> flag on rolebindings shows the subject (user/group/SA) — essential for debugging.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you grant a ClusterRole's permissions in only ONE namespace? What is this pattern called?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pattern: clusterrole + rolebinding (scoped power)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pattern: ClusterRole + RoleBinding (scoped power)</span>
<span class="comment"># Grant built-in "edit" ClusterRole in just "dev" namespace:</span>
kubectl create rolebinding dev-editor \
--clusterrole=edit \
--user=jane \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># jane now has edit permissions in dev ONLY:</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n dev
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># no ← Not in other namespaces!</span>

<span class="comment"># Built-in ClusterRoles you can reuse:</span>
<span class="comment"># cluster-admin   → full access to everything</span>
<span class="comment"># admin           → full namespace access (except quotas/ns)</span>
<span class="comment"># edit            → read-write (no RBAC or quotas)</span>
<span class="comment"># view            → read-only</span>

<span class="comment"># This pattern is called "role aggregation" or "scoped ClusterRole"</span>
<span class="comment"># It avoids creating duplicate Roles in every namespace</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A RoleBinding can reference a ClusterRole — the ClusterRole's permissions are then scoped to the RoleBinding's namespace. This is efficient: define once as ClusterRole, bind in specific namespaces with RoleBindings. On the CKA, use the built-in ClusterRoles (<code>view</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>admin</code>) with namespace-scoped RoleBindings. Note: a RoleBinding can NEVER reference a regular Role from another namespace — it can only reference a ClusterRole or a Role in the same namespace.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an RBAC rule that allows accessing the <code>logs</code> subresource of pods. What is a subresource?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">subresources are actions on a resource's child…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Subresources are actions on a resource's child endpoints:</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/log        → kubectl logs</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/exec       → kubectl exec</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/portforward → kubectl port-forward</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/status     → pod status updates</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments/scale → kubectl scale</span>

<span class="comment"># Role allowing logs and exec:</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
name: pod-debugger
namespace: dev
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods"]
verbs: ["get", "list"]         # ← Read pods
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods/log"]        # ← Log subresource
verbs: ["get"]
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods/exec"]       # ← Exec subresource
verbs: ["create"]              # ← exec uses create verb!

<span class="comment"># Imperative:</span>
kubectl create role pod-debugger \
--verb=get,list --resource=pods \
--verb=get --resource=pods/log \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ exec uses verb "create" not "get"!</span>
<span class="comment"># It creates an exec session</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Subresources extend resources with specific operations: <code>pods/log</code>, <code>pods/exec</code>, <code>pods/portforward</code>, <code>deployments/scale</code>. On the CKA, a common task: "allow a user to view logs but not exec into pods." Grant <code>pods/log</code> with <code>get</code> verb, but NOT <code>pods/exec</code>. Critical gotcha: <code>kubectl exec</code> uses the <code>create</code> verb (not <code>get</code>), because it creates a new exec session. Granting permission on <code>pods</code> does NOT automatically grant subresource access.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you grant permissions only on specific named resources (e.g., only pod <code>important-pod</code>)?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">use resourcenames to restrict to specific objects</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Use resourceNames to restrict to specific objects:</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
name: specific-pod-reader
namespace: production
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods"]
resourceNames: ["important-pod", "critical-pod"]  # ← Only these!
verbs: ["get", "watch", "delete"]

<span class="comment"># Result:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pod/important-pod \
--as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i get pod/other-pod \
--as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># no ← Not in resourceNames!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ resourceNames doesn't work with "list":</span>
<span class="comment"># You can't "list" specific resources</span>
<span class="comment"># "list" returns ALL resources — it's always all-or-nothing</span>
<span class="comment"># Use get for specific names, list for all</span>

<span class="comment"># Also works with secrets:</span>
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["secrets"]
resourceNames: ["db-password"]
verbs: ["get"]</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>resourceNames</code> narrows access to specific named objects. Useful for secrets (only allow reading one specific secret). On the CKA, this is used in least-privilege scenarios: "grant access to secret <code>db-creds</code> only." Key limitation: <code>list</code> and <code>create</code> verbs cannot use <code>resourceNames</code> (list returns everything, create doesn't have a name yet). Only <code>get</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>, and <code>watch</code> work with resourceNames.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you quickly find which RoleBindings/ClusterRoleBindings apply to a specific user or ServiceAccount?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check namespace rolebindings</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check namespace RoleBindings:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n dev -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME              ROLE                 SUBJECTS</span>
<span class="comment"># pod-reader-rb     Role/pod-reader      User:jane</span>
<span class="comment"># deploy-editor     ClusterRole/edit     Group:developers</span>

<span class="comment"># Check ClusterRoleBindings:</span>
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep jane
<span class="comment"># node-reader-crb   ClusterRole/node-reader   User:jane</span>

<span class="comment"># Search ALL namespaces:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -A -o wide | grep jane
<span class="comment"># dev        pod-reader-rb    Role/pod-reader    User:jane</span>
<span class="comment"># staging    deploy-rb        Role/deploy-mgr    User:jane</span>

<span class="comment"># For ServiceAccounts:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -A -o wide | grep deploy-sa
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep deploy-sa

<span class="comment"># Describe for full details:</span>
kubectl describe rolebinding pod-reader-rb -n dev
<span class="comment"># Role:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Kind: Role</span>
<span class="comment">#   Name: pod-reader</span>
<span class="comment"># Subjects:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Kind   Name   Namespace</span>
<span class="comment">#   ----   ----   ---------</span>
<span class="comment">#   User   jane</span>

<span class="comment"># All permissions summary:</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n dev</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Finding all RBAC bindings for a user: (1) <code>kubectl get rolebindings -A -o wide | grep &lt;user&gt;</code> for namespace bindings, (2) <code>kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep &lt;user&gt;</code> for cluster bindings. The <code>-o wide</code> flag shows the SUBJECTS column, making it greppable. On the CKA, when debugging permissions, check BOTH RoleBindings (per namespace) and ClusterRoleBindings (global). <code>auth can-i --list</code> gives the final computed permissions after all bindings are evaluated.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set up a multi-role RBAC system: a <code>dev</code> user who can manage pods/deployments in the <code>dev</code> namespace, a <code>qa</code> user who can only view resources in all namespaces, and an <code>ops</code> user with full cluster admin. Show all the Roles, ClusterRoles, and Bindings.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">dev user — manage pods/deployments in "dev"…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. DEV user — manage pods/deployments in "dev" namespace only</span>
kubectl create role dev-role -n dev \
--verb=get,list,watch,create,update,delete \
--resource=pods,deployments,services

kubectl create rolebinding dev-binding -n dev \
--role=dev-role --user=dev

<span class="comment"># 2. QA user — read-only across ALL namespaces</span>
<span class="comment"># Use built-in ClusterRole "view"</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding qa-binding \
--clusterrole=view --user=qa

<span class="comment"># 3. OPS user — full cluster admin</span>
<span class="comment"># Use built-in ClusterRole "cluster-admin"</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding ops-binding \
--clusterrole=cluster-admin --user=ops

<span class="comment"># Verify each user's permissions:</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n dev --as=dev
<span class="comment"># yes</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n production --as=dev
<span class="comment"># no ← dev only has access to "dev" namespace</span>

kubectl auth can-i get pods -n kube-system --as=qa
<span class="comment"># yes ← "view" ClusterRole works in all namespaces</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n dev --as=qa
<span class="comment"># no ← view is read-only</span>

kubectl auth can-i '*' '*' --as=ops
<span class="comment"># yes ← cluster-admin can do everything</span>

<span class="comment"># List all bindings for verification:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n dev -o wide
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep -E 'qa|ops'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This pattern demonstrates the three common RBAC tiers: (1) <strong>Namespace-scoped</strong> via Role + RoleBinding — limits access to one namespace, (2) <strong>Cluster-wide read-only</strong> via built-in <code>view</code> ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding, (3) <strong>Full admin</strong> via built-in <code>cluster-admin</code> ClusterRole. Kubernetes ships with four default ClusterRoles: <code>view</code> (read-only), <code>edit</code> (read+write, no RBAC), <code>admin</code> (namespace admin including RBAC), <code>cluster-admin</code> (everything). On the CKA, always prefer imperative commands for speed: <code>kubectl create role</code>, <code>kubectl create rolebinding</code>, <code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding</code>. Use <code>--as=</code> to impersonate and verify.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 24 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch25">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 25 · Service Accounts &amp; API Access</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Humans use <code>kubectl</code> with certificates to talk to the API server, but what about pods? When your <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django application needs to read ConfigMaps, list pods, or interact with the Kubernetes API, it does so through a <strong>Service Account</strong>. Every pod in Kubernetes automatically gets a Service Account, but the default one has minimal permissions. In this chapter, you'll learn how to create dedicated Service Accounts, control their permissions with RBAC, manage token projection, and follow security best practices.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">👤 Service Accounts</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The difference between User Accounts (humans) and Service Accounts (pods)</li>
                    <li>✅ How the default Service Account works and why it's insufficient for production</li>
                    <li>✅ Creating and assigning Service Accounts to pods</li>
                    <li>✅ Token projection — how modern K8s handles SA tokens securely</li>
                    <li>✅ Binding RBAC roles to Service Accounts for precise pod-level permissions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Service Accounts on the CKA</h5>
                <p>Expect questions that combine SA creation with RBAC binding. Key skills:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl create serviceaccount &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> — know this by heart</li>
                    <li>Assign a SA to a Pod via <code>spec.serviceAccountName</code></li>
                    <li>Disable auto-mount with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></li>
                    <li>The SA subject format: <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ServiceAccount + Pod — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Know how to create a SA, assign it to a pod, and disable auto-mount.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
automountServiceAccountToken: true   # ◀── default; set false to disable
---
<span class="comment"># Pod using the ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa      # ◀── assign SA to pod
  automountServiceAccountToken: true  # ◀── pod-level override
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:latest
    <span class="comment"># Token is auto-mounted at:</span>
    <span class="comment"># /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</span>
    <span class="comment"># /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt</span>
    <span class="comment"># /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find Service Account Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain serviceaccount                         <span class="comment"># SA spec</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.serviceAccountName            <span class="comment"># assign SA to pod</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.automountServiceAccountToken  <span class="comment"># disable token mount</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">2</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Account Types (User vs SA)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">1</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Default SA per Namespace</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">3</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Auto-Mounted Files</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-purple">
                    <span class="metric-value">1h</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Default Token TTL</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.1 User Accounts vs Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes has two types of identities, but only one is managed by the API server. Understanding this distinction is critical for RBAC and the CKA exam.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>User Account (Human)</th>
                                <th>Service Account (Pod)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Used by</strong></td><td>Humans (kubectl, dashboard)</td><td>Pods / Applications</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Created by</strong></td><td>External system (LDAP, OIDC, certs)</td><td>Kubernetes API (<code>kubectl create sa</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>Cluster-wide (no namespace)</td><td>Namespaced</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Credentials</strong></td><td>X.509 certificates / OIDC tokens</td><td>Auto-mounted projected tokens</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Managed by K8s</strong></td><td>❌ No — external to cluster</td><td>✅ Yes — API objects</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubectl create</strong></td><td>❌ Not possible</td><td>✅ <code>kubectl create sa</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RBAC subject kind</strong></td><td><code>User</code> or <code>Group</code></td><td><code>ServiceAccount</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">User Account vs Service Account — Who Talks to the API Server?</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │    HUMAN (User Account)                    POD (Service Account)             │
  │                                                                              │
  │    ┌──────────────┐                       ┌──────────────────────┐           │
  │    │  👨‍💻 kubectl   │                       │  📦 easypj-web pod   │           │
  │    │              │                       │                      │           │
  │    │  ~/.kube/    │                       │  /var/run/secrets/   │           │
  │    │  config      │                       │  kubernetes.io/      │           │
  │    │              │                       │  serviceaccount/     │           │
  │    │  🔑 x509 cert│                       │  ├── token  🎫      │           │
  │    │  or OIDC     │                       │  ├── ca.crt 📜      │           │
  │    │  token       │                       │  └── namespace 📝   │           │
  │    └──────┬───────┘                       └──────────┬───────────┘           │
  │           │                                          │                       │
  │           │     HTTPS + Certificate/Token             │     HTTPS + SA Token │
  │           │                                          │                       │
  │           └──────────────┬───────────────────────────┘                       │
  │                          ▼                                                   │
  │                 ┌────────────────┐                                           │
  │                 │  🏛️ API Server  │                                           │
  │                 │                │                                           │
  │                 │  1. Auth-N ────│── Verify identity                         │
  │                 │  2. Auth-Z ────│── Check RBAC                              │
  │                 │  3. Admit  ────│── Apply policies                          │
  │                 │  4. Persist ───│── Write to etcd                           │
  │                 └────────────────┘                                           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 Key Insight</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes does NOT manage User Accounts — there's no <code>kubectl create user</code> command. Users are created externally (certificates, LDAP, OIDC). But Service Accounts ARE Kubernetes objects that you create, list, and delete with kubectl.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.2 Default Service Account</h3>
                <p>Every namespace automatically gets a <code>default</code> Service Account. If you don't specify a <code>serviceAccountName</code> in your pod spec, it uses this one automatically.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">inspect-default-sa.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Every namespace has a "default" SA</span>
kubectl get sa -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME      SECRETS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># default   0         30d</span>

<span class="comment"># See what the default SA can do (spoiler: almost nothing)</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:default \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Check which SA a running pod uses</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># default</span>

<span class="comment"># See the mounted token inside a pod</span>
kubectl exec easypj-web -n easypj -- \
  cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Default SA is Not Safe for Production</h5>
                    <p>The default SA has a token auto-mounted into every pod. Even though it has minimal permissions, <strong>if your pod is compromised, an attacker gets that token</strong>. Best practice: create specific SAs per application and disable auto-mount where not needed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>3 Auto-Mounted Files in Every Pod</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎫</span> token</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> — JWT token for authenticating to the API server. Auto-refreshed by kubelet before expiry (~1 hour).</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📜</span> ca.crt</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt</code> — Cluster CA certificate. Used to verify the API server's TLS certificate when making HTTPS calls.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> namespace</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace</code> — Plain text file containing the pod's namespace name. Used by SDK clients to discover their namespace.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>When to Use Default SA</h5>
                        <p>Simple pods that never call the Kubernetes API. Disable the token with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>When to Create Custom SA</h5>
                        <p>Any pod that needs API access: reading ConfigMaps, listing pods, managing deployments, or running as a controller.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.3 Creating and Using Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>Creating a dedicated SA for your easypj application is straightforward — create it, then reference it in the pod spec.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Create the SA</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Create RBAC Role</h5>
                        <p>Define what permissions the SA needs</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Bind SA to Role</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create rolebinding sa-bind --role=... --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Assign to Pod</h5>
                        <p>Set <code>spec.serviceAccountName: easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Verify</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-serviceaccount-setup.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># 1. ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
---
<span class="comment"># 2. Role — what easypj needs</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-app-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["configmaps", "secrets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]          <span class="comment"># read config at startup</span>
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]                   <span class="comment"># health-check readiness</span>
---
<span class="comment"># 3. RoleBinding</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-app-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
<span class="comment"># 4. Pod using the ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa            # ◀── use our custom SA
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:latest
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">disable-auto-mount.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod that does NOT need API access — disable token mount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-worker
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
  automountServiceAccountToken: false      # ◀── no token mounted!
  containers:
  - name: worker
    image: easypj:latest
    command: ["python", "manage.py", "process_jobs"]</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.4 Token Projection and Token Request API</h3>
                <p>Before Kubernetes 1.22, SA tokens were stored as Secrets and never expired. Modern K8s uses <strong>projected tokens</strong> — they're time-limited, audience-bound, and automatically refreshed by the kubelet.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Legacy Tokens vs Projected Tokens — Security Evolution</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ LEGACY (pre-1.22)                  ✅ PROJECTED (1.22+)                  │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌────────────────────┐               ┌────────────────────────┐            │
  │  │  Secret object     │               │  TokenRequest API      │            │
  │  │  ├── token: xyz... │               │  ├── audience: api     │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  ├── expiry: 1 hour    │            │
  │  │  │  ⚠️ NEVER       │               │  ├── auto-refresh: ✅  │            │
  │  │  │    EXPIRES!     │               │  │                      │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  │  🔒 Time-limited    │            │
  │  │  │  ⚠️ NO audience│               │  │  🔒 Audience-bound  │            │
  │  │  │    binding      │               │  │  🔒 Auto-rotated    │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  │                      │            │
  │  │  │  Stored in      │               │  │  Generated on       │            │
  │  │  │  etcd forever   │               │  │  demand by kubelet  │            │
  │  └────────────────────┘               └────────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  If token is stolen →                 If token is stolen →                   │
  │  UNLIMITED access 💀                  Expires in ≤1 hour 🛡️                 │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">projected-token-volume.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod with explicit projected token volume (advanced scenario)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-api-client
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
  containers:
  - name: api-client
    image: easypj:latest
    volumeMounts:
    - name: token-vol
      mountPath: /var/run/secrets/tokens
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: token-vol
    projected:
      sources:
      - serviceAccountToken:
          path: api-token               <span class="comment"># file name</span>
          expirationSeconds: 3600       <span class="comment"># 1 hour</span>
          audience: api                 <span class="comment"># intended audience</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">create-token-cli.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a short-lived token from CLI (TokenRequest API)</span>
kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj --duration=3600s

<span class="comment"># Create a token for a specific audience</span>
kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj --audience=https://my-api.example.com

<span class="comment"># Decode and inspect a token (JWT)</span>
TOKEN=$(kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj)
echo $TOKEN | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq .
<span class="comment"># Shows: iss, sub, aud, exp, iat, kubernetes.io fields</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 CKA Exam Note</h5>
                    <p>The CKA rarely tests projected token details, but you must know that <code>kubectl create token</code> is the modern way to generate tokens, and that auto-mounted tokens now expire. Legacy Secret-based tokens are deprecated.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>Projected Token Lifecycle</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Pod Scheduled</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Kubelet calls TokenRequest API → gets a time-limited JWT token for the pod's ServiceAccount</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Token Mounted</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Token written to <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> as a projected volume</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>App Uses Token</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Application reads token → sends in <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header to API server</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Auto-Refresh</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Before token expires (~80% of TTL), kubelet automatically requests a new token and updates the mounted file</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.5 Binding RBAC to Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>The real power comes when you combine Service Accounts with RBAC — giving each application precisely the permissions it needs, nothing more.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔑 SA Subject Format in RBAC</h5>
                    <p>When using <code>--as</code> flag or in RoleBinding subjects, the format is:<br><code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;sa-name&gt;</code></p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj SA + RBAC — Least Privilege in Action</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │             easypj SERVICE ACCOUNT RBAC ARCHITECTURE                         │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 📦 easypj-web    │    │ 🔗 RoleBinding  │    │ 📋 Role              │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │ easypj-app-role      │     │
  │  │ SA: easypj-sa    │───▶│ easypj-sa ───▶│───▶│ configmaps: get,list │     │
  │  │                  │    │ Role           │    │ secrets: get         │     │
  │  │ Can read config  │    │                │    │ pods: get,list       │     │
  │  │ ✅ configmaps    │    └────────────────┘    └──────────────────────┘     │
  │  │ ✅ secrets       │                                                       │
  │  │ ❌ delete pods   │                                                       │
  │  │ ❌ create deploy │                                                       │
  │  └──────────────────┘                                                       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 📦 easypj-worker │    │ No binding     │    │ No permissions       │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │                      │     │
  │  │ SA: easypj-sa    │    │ automount:     │    │ Token not mounted    │     │
  │  │ automount: false │    │ false          │    │ Can't call API       │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │ 🔒 Maximum security  │     │
  │  │ ❌ everything    │    └────────────────┘    └──────────────────────┘     │
  │  └──────────────────┘                                                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">bind-sa-complete.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Complete SA + RBAC setup (imperative — exam speed!)</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Create ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 2. Create Role</span>
kubectl create role easypj-app-role \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=configmaps,secrets,pods \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 3. Bind SA to Role</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-sa-binding \
  --role=easypj-app-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i get configmaps \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (least privilege working!)</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. List all SA permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ SA Best Practices</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>One SA per application/workload</li>
                            <li><code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> if no API access needed</li>
                            <li>Bind with specific Role, not <code>cluster-admin</code></li>
                            <li>Use projected tokens (K8s 1.22+)</li>
                            <li>Always verify with <code>can-i</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ SA Anti-Patterns</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Using <code>default</code> SA for everything</li>
                            <li>Binding <code>cluster-admin</code> to a SA</li>
                            <li>Leaving auto-mount on when not needed</li>
                            <li>Sharing SAs across unrelated apps</li>
                            <li>Using legacy Secret-based tokens</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <!-- ─── Service Account Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>Service Account Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get sa -n NS</code> → confirm SA exists</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe sa SA_NAME -n NS</code> → check token and secrets</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=system:serviceaccount:NS:SA -n NS</code> → verify RBAC</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code> → check token mount</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> → inspect mounted token</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl create token SA_NAME -n NS</code> → verify TokenRequest API works</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🏋️ Chapter 25 Practice Exercises</h5>
                    <p>Complete each exercise in under 5 minutes to build CKA-level speed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 25.1 — Create SA with RBAC</h5>
                        <p>Set up a ServiceAccount with read-only ConfigMap access in a dedicated namespace.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create namespace <code>sa-practice</code></li>
                            <li>Create SA <code>app-sa</code> in that namespace</li>
                            <li>Create Role <code>configmap-reader</code> allowing <code>get</code>, <code>list</code> on configmaps</li>
                            <li>Bind <code>app-sa</code> to the role</li>
                            <li>Verify: <code>kubectl auth can-i list configmaps --as=system:serviceaccount:sa-practice:app-sa -n sa-practice</code> → <strong>yes</strong></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Use <code>--serviceaccount=NS:SA</code> in rolebinding command, not <code>--user</code>.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create sa app-sa -n sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create Role</span>
kubectl create role configmap-reader \
  --verb=get,list \
  --resource=configmaps \
  -n sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Bind SA to Role</span>
kubectl create rolebinding app-sa-cm-binding \
  --role=configmap-reader \
  --serviceaccount=sa-practice:app-sa \
  -n sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list configmaps \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:sa-practice:app-sa \
  -n sa-practice
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 4 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 25.2 — Disable Token Auto-Mount</h5>
                        <p>Deploy a pod that does NOT mount a ServiceAccount token (for pods that don't need API access).</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create a pod <code>no-api-pod</code> using SA <code>app-sa</code></li>
                            <li>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></li>
                            <li>Exec into the pod and verify: <code>ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code> → should fail (directory doesn't exist)</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: <code>automountServiceAccountToken</code> goes at <code>spec</code> level, not container level.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create the pod with no token mount</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: no-api-pod
  namespace: sa-practice
spec:
  serviceAccountName: app-sa
  automountServiceAccountToken: false
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: busybox
    command: ["sleep", "3600"]
EOF

<span class="comment"># Wait for pod to be ready</span>
kubectl wait --for=condition=ready pod/no-api-pod -n sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Verify no token is mounted</span>
kubectl exec no-api-pod -n sa-practice -- \
  ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># ls: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/: No such file or directory</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 3 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 25.3 — Create Short-Lived Token</h5>
                        <p>Generate a time-limited token using the TokenRequest API and inspect its expiration.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create a token for <code>app-sa</code> valid for 10 minutes: <code>kubectl create token app-sa -n sa-practice --duration=600s</code></li>
                            <li>Decode the JWT payload: pipe through <code>cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d</code></li>
                            <li>Verify the <code>exp</code> field is ~10 minutes from <code>iat</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: JWT has 3 dot-separated parts (header.payload.signature). The payload (part 2) is base64-encoded JSON.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create a 10-minute token</span>
TOKEN=$(kubectl create token app-sa -n sa-practice --duration=600s)

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Decode the JWT payload (part 2)</span>
echo $TOKEN | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq .
<span class="comment"># Output includes:</span>
<span class="comment"># {</span>
<span class="comment">#   "iss": "https://kubernetes.default.svc",</span>
<span class="comment">#   "sub": "system:serviceaccount:sa-practice:app-sa",</span>
<span class="comment">#   "iat": 1700000000,      ← issued at</span>
<span class="comment">#   "exp": 1700000600,      ← expires (iat + 600s)</span>
<span class="comment">#   ...</span>
<span class="comment"># }</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify expiration (exp - iat should be 600)</span>
echo $TOKEN | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | \
  jq '{iat, exp, duration_seconds: (.exp - .iat)}'
<span class="comment"># duration_seconds: 600</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How ServiceAccount Token Authentication Works: JWT Lifecycle, TokenRequest API, and the Token Projection Flow</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ ServiceAccount Token Lifecycle — From Creation to API Call</h5>
            <p>Every pod gets a ServiceAccount token mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>. This JWT is the pod's identity and is used for ALL API calls from within the pod.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SERVICEACCOUNT — Complete Identity System                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  WHAT A SA GIVES A POD:                                               │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/                        │   │
│  │  ├── token           <span class="comment">← JWT (JSON Web Token) — pod's identity          </span>│   │
│  │  ├── ca.crt          <span class="comment">← Cluster CA certificate — validate API Server  </span>│   │
│  │  └── namespace       <span class="comment">← Namespace name — "easypj-dev"                  </span>│   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  JWT PAYLOAD (decoded):                                                │   │
│  │  {                                                                     │   │
│  │    "iss": "kubernetes/serviceaccount",                                  │   │
│  │    "sub": "system:serviceaccount:easypj-dev:default",  <span class="comment">← IDENTITY    </span>│   │
│  │    "kubernetes.io": {                                                 │   │
│  │      "namespace": "easypj-dev",                                       │   │
│  │      "serviceaccount": {                                              │   │
│  │        "name": "default",                                             │   │
│  │        "uid": "abc-123-def"                                           │   │
│  │      }                                                                 │   │
│  │    },                                                                  │   │
│  │    "exp": 1717333899,  <span class="comment">← EXPIRATION (typically 1 hour)                </span>│   │
│  │    "iat": 1717330299   <span class="comment">← Issued at                                    </span>│   │
│  │  }                                                                     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  TOKEN LIFECYCLE (Automated TokenRequest API — K8s 1.24+):                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  OLD WAY (pre-1.24):                                                  │   │
│  │   1. SA created → Secret auto-created (non-expiring token)            │   │
│  │   2. Pod mounts Secret → permanent token                              │   │
│  │   3. ⚠️  Token NEVER expires — if stolen, valid forever              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  NEW WAY (1.24+, TokenRequest API):                                    │   │
│  │   1. SA created → NO automatic Secret                                 │   │
│  │   2. kubelet calls TokenRequest API for EACH pod start                │   │
│  │   3. API Server creates time-limited token (1h, bounded by SA)        │   │
│  │   4. kubelet projects token into pod's tmpfs volume                   │   │
│  │   5. kubelet REFRESHES token before expiry (every ~20 min)            │   │
│  │   6. ✅ Token auto-expires + auto-refreshes — secure by default       │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  TO VERIFY CURRENT TOKEN EXPIRY:                                       │   │
│  │  $ kubectl exec pod -- cat /var/run/secrets/.../token | cut -d. -f2  │   │
│  │    | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq .exp                                  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  RBAC WITH SERVICEACCOUNTS — The Complete Chain:                             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  ServiceAccount (identity)                                            │   │
│  │      │                                                                │   │
│  │      │  Bound via ClusterRoleBinding/RoleBinding                      │   │
│  │      ▼                                                                │   │
│  │  Role/ClusterRole (permissions)                                        │   │
│  │      │                                                                │   │
│  │      │  Defines: apiGroups, resources, verbs                         │   │
│  │      ▼                                                                │   │
│  │  Pod uses token → API Server validates → RBAC authorizes → ALLOW/DENY│   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Example binding:                                                      │   │
│  │  kubectl create clusterrolebinding easypj-deployer \                  │   │
│  │    --clusterrole=edit \                                                │   │
│  │    --serviceaccount=easypj-dev:deploy-sa                               │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, you'll create SAs and bind them to Roles. Remember: <code>kubectl create sa &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> creates the SA. <code>kubectl create rolebinding &lt;name&gt; --role=&lt;role&gt; --serviceaccount=&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;sa&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> binds it. In pod YAML: <code>spec.serviceAccountName: &lt;sa&gt;</code>. SA subjects in RBAC use format: <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">The default SA has full access to the namespace</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">The default SA has almost NO permissions — create specific SAs with explicit RBAC</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">SA tokens never expire</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Since K8s 1.22, projected tokens are time-limited and audience-bound. Legacy tokens are deprecated</span></li>
                    <li><code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on the pod overrides the SA-level setting</li>
                    <li>SA is <strong>namespaced</strong> — you cannot use a SA from namespace A in namespace B</li>
                    <li>Deleting a SA doesn't delete its RoleBindings — orphaned bindings remain</li>
                </ul>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Default SA has no useful permissions</strong> — Every namespace has a <code>default</code> ServiceAccount, but it can't do much. The exam expects you to create dedicated SAs and bind RBAC roles to them</li>
                        <li><strong>K8s 1.24+ changed token behavior</strong> — Tokens are no longer auto-mounted as persistent secrets. Use <code>kubectl create token &lt;sa&gt;</code> for time-limited tokens. The exam uses 1.29+, so use the new approach</li>
                        <li><strong><code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></strong> — Set this on pods that don't need API access (most app pods). This is a security hardening best practice the exam may ask about</li>
                        <li><strong>SA name in pod spec</strong> — Use <code>serviceAccountName</code> (NOT the deprecated <code>serviceAccount</code> field). The SA must exist before the pod is created or the pod stays Pending</li>
                        <li><strong>Token audience and expiry</strong> — <code>kubectl create token sa-name --duration=3600s --audience=https://myapi</code>. Projected tokens have expiry, bound to specific audiences. Know this for security questions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Service Account workflow:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create sa my-sa -n prod</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create role my-role --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n prod</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create rolebinding my-rb --role=my-role --serviceaccount=prod:my-sa -n prod</code><br>
                <strong>Note the SA format in rolebinding:</strong> <code>--serviceaccount=NAMESPACE:SA-NAME</code></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 25)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Create SA</td><td><code>kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List SAs</td><td><code>kubectl get serviceaccounts -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Describe SA</td><td><code>kubectl describe sa easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create token</td><td><code>kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create token (1hr)</td><td><code>kubectl create token easypj-sa --duration=3600s -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Bind SA to Role</td><td><code>kubectl create rolebinding sa-bind --role=pod-reader --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Test SA permissions</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>create service account</td><td><code>kubectl create sa &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>generate a bound token</td><td><code>kubectl create token &lt;sa-name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>list service accounts</td><td><code>kubectl get sa -n &lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>test SA permissions</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;sa&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>assign SA to existing deployment</td><td><code>kubectl set sa deployment/&lt;name&gt; &lt;sa-name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-drill"></span>
            <div class="scenario-box">
                <div class="scenario-header">
                    <span class="scenario-icon">🔥</span>
                    <h4>Scenario: "Pod Can't Read ConfigMaps — 403 Forbidden"</h4>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-content">
                    <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> easypj pod logs show <code>Error: configmaps "app-config" is forbidden: User "system:serviceaccount:easypj:default" cannot get resource</code></p>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <span class="step-label">Step 1 — Identify</span>
                            <p>The pod is using the <code>default</code> SA, which has no permissions. It needs a custom SA with RBAC.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <span class="step-label">Step 2 — Create SA + Role + Binding</span>
                            <p><code>kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj</code><br>
                            <code>kubectl create role cm-reader --verb=get,list --resource=configmaps -n easypj</code><br>
                            <code>kubectl create rolebinding cm-rb --role=cm-reader --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <span class="step-label">Step 3 — Assign SA to Pod</span>
                            <p><code>kubectl set sa deployment/easypj easypj-sa -n easypj</code> — pod restarts with new SA, ConfigMap reads succeed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-outcome">
                        <strong>Lesson:</strong> Never rely on the default SA for API access. Create dedicated SAs with least-privilege RBAC. The CKA tests this workflow frequently.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 25 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 25 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of ServiceAccount auth &amp; token flow — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ServiceAccount named <code>deploy-sa</code> in namespace <code>production</code>. Then create a pod that uses this ServiceAccount.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create serviceaccount</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ServiceAccount:</span>
kubectl create sa deploy-sa -n production

<span class="comment"># Create pod using the SA:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: deploy-pod
namespace: production
spec:
serviceAccountName: deploy-sa    # ← Use custom SA
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx

<span class="comment"># Or imperatively with dry-run and edit:</span>
kubectl run deploy-pod --image=nginx -n production \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > pod.yaml
<span class="comment"># Add serviceAccountName: deploy-sa to spec</span>
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pod deploy-pod -n production \
-o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># deploy-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Check token is mounted:</span>
kubectl exec deploy-pod -n production -- \
ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  namespace  token</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ServiceAccounts provide pod identity for API access. Use <code>serviceAccountName</code> (not the deprecated <code>serviceAccount</code>). The SA must exist BEFORE the pod — otherwise the pod stays Pending. The token, CA cert, and namespace are auto-mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code>. On the CKA, creating a SA, binding RBAC, and using it in a pod is a common multi-step task.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you prevent a pod from automatically mounting a ServiceAccount token?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">at the pod level (overrides sa setting)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Method 1: At the pod level (overrides SA setting):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: no-token-pod
spec:
automountServiceAccountToken: false  # ← Pod level
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx

<span class="comment"># Method 2: At the ServiceAccount level (affects all pods using it):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
name: no-api-sa
namespace: default
automountServiceAccountToken: false    # ← SA level

<span class="comment"># Verify no token mounted:</span>
kubectl exec no-token-pod -- \
ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ 2>&1
<span class="comment"># ls: No such file or directory ← Token not mounted!</span>

<span class="comment"># When to use this:</span>
<span class="comment"># - App pods that never call the Kubernetes API</span>
<span class="comment"># - Security hardening (principle of least privilege)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Reducing attack surface if pod is compromised</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod-level overrides SA-level:</span>
<span class="comment"># SA: automount=false + Pod: automount=true → token IS mounted</span>
<span class="comment"># SA: automount=true + Pod: automount=false → token NOT mounted</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> at pod or SA level. Pod-level overrides SA-level. On the CKA, this is a security hardening best practice — most application pods don't need API access. If a compromised pod has a token, an attacker could interact with the Kubernetes API. Disabling auto-mount reduces this risk. Set this on ALL pods that don't need API access.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ServiceAccount, bind a Role to it, and verify it can list pods but not delete them.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create sa</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create SA</span>
kubectl create sa app-sa -n dev

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Role</span>
kubectl create role pod-lister \
--verb=get,list,watch \
--resource=pods \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Bind Role to SA</span>
<span class="comment"># Note: SA format is namespace:name</span>
kubectl create rolebinding app-sa-binding \
--role=pod-lister \
--serviceaccount=dev:app-sa \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:dev:app-sa -n dev
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:dev:app-sa -n dev
<span class="comment"># no</span>

kubectl auth can-i list pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:dev:app-sa -n production
<span class="comment"># no ← Only in dev!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Create pod with this SA</span>
kubectl run test-pod --image=nginx -n dev \
--overrides='{"spec":{"serviceAccountName":"app-sa"}}'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The full SA workflow: (1) create SA, (2) create Role, (3) bind with RoleBinding using <code>--serviceaccount=NAMESPACE:NAME</code>. For <code>auth can-i</code>, the SA format is <code>system:serviceaccount:NAMESPACE:NAME</code>. On the CKA, this three-step workflow is heavily tested. Common mistakes: forgetting the namespace prefix in <code>--serviceaccount</code>, or using the wrong format in <code>--as</code>. <code>--overrides</code> with <code>kubectl run</code> is the fastest way to set a SA imperatively.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you generate a short-lived token for a ServiceAccount? What changed in Kubernetes 1.24+?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate a time-limited token (1.24+ way)</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Generate a time-limited token (1.24+ way):</span>
kubectl create token deploy-sa -n production
<span class="comment"># eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...  ← JWT token (1 hour default)</span>

<span class="comment"># Custom duration:</span>
kubectl create token deploy-sa -n production --duration=3600s
<span class="comment"># Token valid for 1 hour</span>

<span class="comment"># Custom audience:</span>
kubectl create token deploy-sa -n production \
--audience=https://my-api.example.com

<span class="comment"># Pre-1.24 (old way — still works but deprecated):</span>
<span class="comment"># Secrets with type kubernetes.io/service-account-token</span>
<span class="comment"># were auto-created for each SA. No longer auto-created!</span>

<span class="comment"># If you need a long-lived token (NOT recommended):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: deploy-sa-token
namespace: production
annotations:
kubernetes.io/service-account.name: deploy-sa
type: kubernetes.io/service-account-token
<span class="comment"># K8s auto-populates the token data</span>

kubectl get secret deploy-sa-token -n production \
-o jsonpath='{.data.token}' | base64 -d</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubernetes 1.24+ removed auto-creation of Secret-based tokens. Now use <code>kubectl create token</code> for time-limited, projected tokens (more secure). On the CKA (K8s 1.29+), use <code>kubectl create token</code> — it generates a JWT with automatic expiration. For legacy compatibility, you can manually create a Secret with type <code>kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code> and annotate it with the SA name. But the exam prefers the new approach: short-lived, audience-bound tokens.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you assign a ServiceAccount to an existing Deployment without editing the YAML?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">use kubectl set</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Use kubectl set:</span>
kubectl set sa deployment/web-app deploy-sa -n production
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/web-app serviceaccount updated</span>

<span class="comment"># This triggers a rolling update (new pods get the SA)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get deployment web-app -n production \
-o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># deploy-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: kubectl patch</span>
kubectl patch deployment web-app -n production \
-p '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"serviceAccountName":"deploy-sa"}}}}'

<span class="comment"># Check the new pods:</span>
kubectl get pods -n production -l app=web-app
<span class="comment"># NAME                      READY  STATUS   RESTARTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># web-app-6f7b8c-new1       1/1    Running  0         10s ← New pod with SA</span>

kubectl exec web-app-6f7b8c-new1 -n production -- \
cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># {"iss":"https://kubernetes.default.svc","sub":"system:serviceaccount:production:deploy-sa"...}</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl set sa</code> is the fastest way to assign a SA to a Deployment. It triggers a rolling update — old pods are replaced with new ones that use the new SA. On the CKA, this is faster than editing YAML. The SA must exist before assignment, or new pods will fail to start. You can verify by checking the token's <code>sub</code> (subject) field inside the pod.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What files are automatically mounted in a pod from its ServiceAccount? Where are they?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">mounted at:…</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Mounted at: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- ls -la \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># lrwxrwxrwx 1  token     → projected token (JWT)</span>
<span class="comment"># lrwxrwxrwx 1  ca.crt    → cluster CA certificate</span>
<span class="comment"># lrwxrwxrwx 1  namespace → pod's namespace name</span>

<span class="comment"># token: JWT for API authentication</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
<span class="comment"># eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...</span>

<span class="comment"># ca.crt: Used to verify API server's TLS certificate</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt
<span class="comment"># -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----</span>
<span class="comment"># MIICyDCCAbC...</span>

<span class="comment"># namespace: The pod's namespace</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace
<span class="comment"># production</span>

<span class="comment"># Using these from inside a pod:</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- sh -c '
TOKEN=$(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)
curl -s --cacert /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt \
-H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/production/pods
'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three files are projected: <code>token</code> (JWT for authentication), <code>ca.crt</code> (CA to verify API server TLS), <code>namespace</code> (pod's namespace). In K8s 1.24+, the token is a *projected* volume with automatic rotation and expiry. On the CKA, knowing this path is important for: (1) debugging API access from pods, (2) security hardening (disable with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>), (3) understanding how pods authenticate to the API server.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you attach <code>imagePullSecrets</code> to a ServiceAccount so all pods using it can pull from a private registry?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create the docker-registry secret</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the docker-registry secret:</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
--docker-server=registry.example.com \
--docker-username=myuser \
--docker-password=mypass \
-n production

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Attach to ServiceAccount:</span>
kubectl patch sa deploy-sa -n production \
-p '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "regcred"}]}'

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get sa deploy-sa -n production -o yaml
<span class="comment"># imagePullSecrets:</span>
<span class="comment"># - name: regcred</span>

<span class="comment"># Now ALL pods using deploy-sa automatically inherit imagePullSecrets!</span>
<span class="comment"># No need to add imagePullSecrets to each pod spec</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: attach to default SA (affects ALL pods in ns):</span>
kubectl patch sa default -n production \
-p '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "regcred"}]}'</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Attaching <code>imagePullSecrets</code> to a ServiceAccount means every pod using that SA inherits the credentials — no need to specify <code>imagePullSecrets</code> in each pod spec. On the CKA, patching the <code>default</code> SA in a namespace affects all pods in that namespace (quick fix for <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> on private images). This is more maintainable than adding secrets to every Deployment.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>serviceAccountName</code> and <code>serviceAccount</code> in a pod spec?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"># ✓ Correct (use this):
spec:
serviceAccountName: deploy-sa   # ← Current field

<span class="comment"># ✗ Deprecated (do NOT use):</span>
spec:
serviceAccount: deploy-sa       # ← Deprecated since 1.16

<span class="comment"># Both work, but:</span>
<span class="comment"># - serviceAccountName is the official field</span>
<span class="comment"># - serviceAccount is deprecated and may be removed</span>
<span class="comment"># - If both are set and different → error</span>
<span class="comment"># - CKA expects serviceAccountName</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick check which field a pod uses:</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># deploy-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Default SA:</span>
<span class="comment"># If no serviceAccountName is specified, pods use "default" SA</span>
kubectl get pod unset-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># default</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Always use <code>serviceAccountName</code> — <code>serviceAccount</code> is deprecated. On the CKA, using the deprecated field may not cause errors, but it's bad practice. If you see both in a YAML and they differ, the API server rejects the pod. When no SA is specified, pods use the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount in their namespace.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod using a custom ServiceAccount can't call the Kubernetes API. How do you debug?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check sa assignment</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check SA assignment</span>
kubectl get pod api-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># deploy-sa ← Correct SA</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check token is mounted</span>
kubectl exec api-pod -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  namespace  token  ← Token present</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check SA permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:default:deploy-sa
<span class="comment"># no ← Missing RBAC!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check for RoleBindings</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -o wide | grep deploy-sa
<span class="comment"># (empty) ← No binding!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix — create Role and RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create role api-reader \
--verb=get,list --resource=pods
kubectl create rolebinding api-rb \
--role=api-reader \
--serviceaccount=default:deploy-sa

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:default:deploy-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Check automountServiceAccountToken</span>
kubectl get pod api-pod -o yaml | grep automount
<span class="comment"># automountServiceAccountToken: false  ← Token not mounted!</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: set to true or remove the field</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>SA API access debugging: (1) verify SA is assigned, (2) check token is mounted, (3) test permissions with <code>auth can-i</code>, (4) find/create RBAC bindings, (5) check <code>automountServiceAccountToken</code>. On the CKA, the most common issue is: SA exists and is assigned, but no Role/RoleBinding grants it permissions. The default SA has no useful permissions — you must always create RBAC explicitly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a projected ServiceAccount token and how does it differ from legacy tokens?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Feature</th>
                                        <th>Legacy Token (pre-1.24)</th>
                                        <th>Projected Token (1.24+)</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Storage</td>
                                        <td>Stored in Secret object</td>
                                        <td>Projected volume (not in Secret)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Expiry</td>
                                        <td>Never expires</td>
                                        <td>Expires (default 1h, auto-rotated)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Audience</td>
                                        <td>Any API server</td>
                                        <td>Bound to specific audience</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Auto-created</td>
                                        <td>Yes (auto Secret per SA)</td>
                                        <td>No Secret created</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Security</td>
                                        <td>Risky (leaked = permanent)</td>
                                        <td>Better (expires, rotates)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">projected token is auto-rotated by kubelet</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Projected token is auto-rotated by kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># Check token expiry from inside pod:</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token \
| cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | python3 -m json.tool
<span class="comment"># "exp": 1735689600  ← Expiry timestamp</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Projected tokens (K8s 1.24+) are more secure: they expire, are bound to a specific audience, and are auto-rotated by the kubelet. Legacy tokens were stored as Secrets and never expired — a leaked token gave permanent access. On the CKA (K8s 1.29+), projected tokens are the default. If a question asks about token security, mention: time-limited, audience-bound, auto-rotated. For legacy apps needing long-lived tokens, manually create a Secret with the SA annotation.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 25 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch26">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 26 · TLS Certificates &amp; PKI</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Every component in a Kubernetes cluster communicates over <strong>TLS-encrypted connections</strong>. The API server, etcd, kubelet, scheduler, and controller-manager all present certificates to prove their identity. For the CKA exam, you must understand where these certificates live, how to inspect them, how to approve Certificate Signing Requests (CSRs), check expiry, and renew certificates using <code>kubeadm</code>. This chapter covers the entire Kubernetes PKI (Public Key Infrastructure) from root CA to individual component certificates.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔐 TLS/PKI</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The Kubernetes PKI architecture — what the CA signs and why</li>
                    <li>✅ Certificate file locations under <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code></li>
                    <li>✅ How to create and approve Certificate Signing Requests (CSRs)</li>
                    <li>✅ Checking certificate expiry with <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                    <li>✅ Renewing certificates with <code>kubeadm certs renew</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>TLS/Certificates on the CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>Certificate questions are frequently tested. You must be able to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Generate a private key, create a CSR, submit it to Kubernetes, approve it, and extract the certificate</li>
                    <li>Know certificate file locations: <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code></li>
                    <li>Check certificate expiry with <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                    <li>Renew certificates with <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></li>
                    <li>Use <code>openssl x509</code> to inspect certificate details</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>

            

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>CertificateSigningRequest — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">CSR creation is a common CKA task. Know this pattern cold.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate key + CSR (on command line)</span>
openssl genrsa -out dev-user.key 2048
openssl req -new -key dev-user.key \
  -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team" \
  -out dev-user.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Kubernetes CSR object</span>
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: dev-user                         # ◀── CSR name
spec:
  request: $(cat dev-user.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')  # ◀── base64-encoded CSR
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client       # ◀── REQUIRED signer
  usages:
  - client auth                          # ◀── for user authentication
  expirationSeconds: 86400               # ◀── optional: 24 hours

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Approve and extract</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl certificate approve dev-user</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl get csr dev-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > dev-user.crt</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find Certificate Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain csr.spec                       <span class="comment"># CSR spec</span>
kubectl explain csr.spec.request               <span class="comment"># base64-encoded CSR</span>
kubectl explain csr.spec.signerName            <span class="comment"># required signer</span>
kubectl explain csr.spec.usages                <span class="comment"># client auth, server auth, etc.</span>
kubectl explain csr.spec.expirationSeconds     <span class="comment"># cert lifetime</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">6+</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Component Certificates</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">1 yr</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Default Cert Lifetime</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">/etc/kubernetes/pki/</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Default Cert Location</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-red">
                    <span class="metric-value">1</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Root CA Signs Everything</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.1 Kubernetes PKI Overview</h3>
                <p><strong>PKI (Public Key Infrastructure)</strong> is the system of certificates that secure all communication in a Kubernetes cluster. At the top is a <strong>Root Certificate Authority (CA)</strong> that signs certificates for every cluster component.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes PKI Architecture — The Certificate Tree</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                      KUBERNETES PKI HIERARCHY                                │
  │                                                                              │
  │                    ┌──────────────────────┐                                  │
  │                    │   🏛️ ROOT CA          │                                  │
  │                    │   /etc/kubernetes/    │                                  │
  │                    │   pki/ca.crt          │                                  │
  │                    │       ca.key          │                                  │
  │                    └──────────┬───────────┘                                  │
  │                               │ signs                                        │
  │          ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐                         │
  │          │                    │                    │                         │
  │          ▼                    ▼                    ▼                         │
  │  ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 🌐 API Server │   │ 💾 etcd       │   │ 📡 kubelet    │                 │
  │  │               │   │               │   │               │                 │
  │  │ apiserver.crt │   │ server.crt   │   │ kubelet.crt   │                 │
  │  │ apiserver.key │   │ server.key   │   │ kubelet.key   │                 │
  │  │               │   │               │   │               │                 │
  │  │ Also needs:   │   │ peer.crt     │   │ Authenticates │                 │
  │  │ kubelet-client│   │ peer.key     │   │ to API server │                 │
  │  │ .crt/.key     │   │               │   └───────────────┘                 │
  │  └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘                                      │
  │          │                                                                   │
  │          │ also serves as client to:                                         │
  │          ▼                                                                   │
  │  ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 📋 scheduler  │   │ 🔄 controller │   │ 🌐 front-proxy│                 │
  │  │               │   │   manager     │   │               │                 │
  │  │ Uses kubeconf │   │ Uses kubeconf │   │ front-proxy-  │                 │
  │  │ with embedded │   │ with embedded │   │ ca.crt/key    │                 │
  │  │ client cert   │   │ client cert   │   │ (aggregation) │                 │
  │  └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘                 │
  │                                                                              │
  │  📁 All certificates live under: /etc/kubernetes/pki/                        │
  │  📁 etcd certs specifically: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>Encryption</h5>
                        <p>All component communication uses TLS — no plaintext data ever crosses the network.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🪪</div>
                        <h5>Identity</h5>
                        <p>Certificates prove component identity. The API server verifies kubelet is who it claims to be.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🤝</div>
                        <h5>Mutual TLS</h5>
                        <p>Both sides present certificates. API server authenticates kubelet AND kubelet authenticates API server.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.2 Certificate Locations and Purpose</h3>
                <p>Every certificate in the Kubernetes cluster has a specific purpose. For the CKA, you must know where they live and what they do.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>File</th>
                                <th>Used By</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>ca.crt</code> / <code>ca.key</code></td><td>Cluster CA</td><td>Root CA — signs all other certificates</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>Serve HTTPS on port 6443</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver-kubelet-client.crt</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>API server authenticating to kubelets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver-etcd-client.crt</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>API server authenticating to etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcd/server.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>etcd</td><td>etcd serving TLS to API server</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcd/peer.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>etcd</td><td>etcd-to-etcd peer communication (HA)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>front-proxy-ca.crt</code></td><td>Aggregation</td><td>API aggregation layer (metrics server)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sa.pub</code> / <code>sa.key</code></td><td>SA tokens</td><td>Signing/verifying ServiceAccount tokens</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">inspect-certificates.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all certificate files</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  ca.key  apiserver.crt  apiserver.key  ...</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  ca.key  server.crt  server.key  peer.crt  peer.key</span>

<span class="comment"># Inspect API server certificate details</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout

<span class="comment"># Key fields to check:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Issuer: kubernetes (the CA that signed it)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Subject: CN=kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not After: expiry date</span>
<span class="comment"># - Subject Alternative Names (SANs): all valid hostnames/IPs</span>

<span class="comment"># Check who signed a certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -issuer -noout
<span class="comment"># issuer=CN = kubernetes</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify cert is signed by the CA</span>
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
  /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
<span class="comment"># /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt: OK</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Certificate File Layout on Disk</div>
<pre>
  /etc/kubernetes/
  ├── pki/                                 ◀── Main PKI directory
  │   ├── ca.crt                           ◀── Root CA certificate
  │   ├── ca.key                           ◀── Root CA private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver.crt                    ◀── API server TLS cert
  │   ├── apiserver.key                    ◀── API server private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.crt     ◀── API→kubelet client cert
  │   ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.key     ◀── API→kubelet private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver-etcd-client.crt        ◀── API→etcd client cert
  │   ├── apiserver-etcd-client.key        ◀── API→etcd private key 🔒
  │   ├── front-proxy-ca.crt              ◀── Aggregation CA
  │   ├── front-proxy-ca.key              ◀── Aggregation CA key 🔒
  │   ├── front-proxy-client.crt          ◀── Aggregation client cert
  │   ├── front-proxy-client.key          ◀── Aggregation client key 🔒
  │   ├── sa.pub                           ◀── SA token verification key
  │   ├── sa.key                           ◀── SA token signing key 🔒
  │   └── etcd/                            ◀── etcd-specific certs
  │       ├── ca.crt                       ◀── etcd CA (may be separate)
  │       ├── ca.key
  │       ├── server.crt                   ◀── etcd server TLS
  │       ├── server.key
  │       ├── peer.crt                     ◀── etcd peer communication
  │       ├── peer.key
  │       ├── healthcheck-client.crt       ◀── etcd health checks
  │       └── healthcheck-client.key
  ├── admin.conf                           ◀── cluster-admin kubeconfig
  ├── controller-manager.conf              ◀── controller-manager kubeconfig
  ├── scheduler.conf                       ◀── scheduler kubeconfig
  └── kubelet.conf                         ◀── kubelet kubeconfig
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Private Keys Are Secrets</h5>
                    <p>The <code>.key</code> files are private keys — anyone with access can impersonate that component. In production, restrict access to <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> to root only. On the CKA exam, you'll SSH into the control plane node to read these files.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>X.509 Certificate Fields — What to Read</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👤</span> Subject (CN)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>CN=kube-apiserver</code> — The Common Name identifies the component. For user certs, CN becomes the username in Kubernetes RBAC.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👥</span> Subject (O)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>O=system:masters</code> — The Organization becomes the group. <code>system:masters</code> maps to cluster-admin.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏛️</span> Issuer</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>CN=kubernetes</code> — The CA that signed this certificate. All cluster certs should show the same issuer.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📅</span> Not After</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Expiry date. Default 1 year for component certs. <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all expiry dates at once.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> SANs</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Subject Alternative Names — list of valid hostnames &amp; IPs (<code>kubernetes</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc</code>, <code>10.96.0.1</code>, control plane IP).</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.3 Certificate Signing Requests (CSR)</h3>
                <p>To add a new user to the cluster, you create a CSR and have the K8s CA sign it. This process is <strong>heavily tested on the CKA exam</strong>.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Generate Key</h5>
                        <p><code>openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Create CSR</h5>
                        <p><code>openssl req -new -key user.key -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team"</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Submit to K8s</h5>
                        <p>Create CertificateSigningRequest YAML with base64-encoded CSR</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Approve</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl certificate approve dev-user</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Extract Cert</h5>
                        <p>Get the signed certificate from the CSR's <code>.status.certificate</code></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">csr-complete-workflow.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── COMPLETE CSR WORKFLOW — CKA EXAM PATTERN ──

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate a private key</span>
openssl genrsa -out dev-user.key 2048

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create a Certificate Signing Request</span>
<span class="comment"># CN = username, O = group (for RBAC)</span>
openssl req -new -key dev-user.key \
  -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team" \
  -out dev-user.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Encode the CSR in base64 (single line, no wrapping)</span>
CSR_BASE64=$(cat dev-user.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create the K8s CSR YAML</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: dev-user
spec:
  request: ${CSR_BASE64}
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
  usages:
  - client auth
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check CSR status</span>
kubectl get csr
<span class="comment"># NAME       AGE   SIGNERNAME                          CONDITION</span>
<span class="comment"># dev-user   5s    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client  Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Approve the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve dev-user

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Extract the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr dev-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' \
  | base64 -d > dev-user.crt

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Verify the certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in dev-user.crt -text -noout
<span class="comment"># Subject: CN=dev-user, O=dev-team</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 9: Add user to kubeconfig</span>
kubectl config set-credentials dev-user \
  --client-certificate=dev-user.crt \
  --client-key=dev-user.key

kubectl config set-context dev-user@kubernetes \
  --cluster=kubernetes \
  --user=dev-user \
  --namespace=easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Shortcut: CSR Encoding</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>cat dev-user.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n'</code> to get the single-line base64 string for the CSR YAML. The <code>tr -d '\n'</code> removes line breaks that would break the YAML.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>CSR Approval Flow — From Key to Kubeconfig</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Generate Private Key</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048</code> — creates RSA private key (stays with the user, never shared)</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Create CSR File</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>openssl req -new -key user.key -subj "/CN=user/O=group"</code> — CN becomes username, O becomes group in RBAC</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>Submit to Kubernetes</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Base64-encode the CSR → create CertificateSigningRequest YAML → <code>kubectl apply</code>. Status: <strong>Pending</strong></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Admin Approves</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>kubectl certificate approve user</code> — K8s CA signs the cert. Status: <strong>Approved, Issued</strong></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">5</span>
                                <h5>Extract &amp; Use</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Extract signed cert from <code>.status.certificate</code> → add to kubeconfig with <code>kubectl config set-credentials</code></p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.4 Checking Certificate Expiry</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes certificates expire after <strong>1 year</strong> by default. If they expire, cluster components stop communicating and the cluster goes down. Monitoring expiry is critical.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">check-expiry.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── METHOD 1: kubeadm (EASIEST — use on CKA exam) ──
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client      Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager.conf    Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-healthcheck-client    Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-peer                  Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-server                Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client         Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf             Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>

<span class="comment"># ── METHOD 2: openssl (manual — for specific cert) ──</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
  -noout -enddate
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Apr  1 00:00:00 2027 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># ── METHOD 3: Check specific fields ──</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
  -noout -subject -issuer -dates
<span class="comment"># subject=CN = kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># issuer=CN = kubernetes</span>
<span class="comment"># notBefore=Apr  1 00:00:00 2026 GMT</span>
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Apr  1 00:00:00 2027 GMT</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Expired Certificates = Cluster Down</h5>
                    <p>If the API server certificate expires, <code>kubectl</code> stops working entirely. If etcd certificates expire, the API server can't read/write state. <strong>Always check expiry at least monthly in production.</strong> On the CKA exam, <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> is the answer.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.5 Renewing Certificates</h3>
                <p>Certificate renewal is a critical cluster maintenance task and a common CKA exam topic.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">renew-certificates.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Renew ALL certificates at once ──
kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate embedded in admin.conf renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver-kubelet-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Renew a SPECIFIC certificate ──</span>
kubeadm certs renew apiserver
kubeadm certs renew apiserver-kubelet-client
kubeadm certs renew etcd-server

<span class="comment"># ── CRITICAL: Restart control plane after renewal ──</span>
<span class="comment"># Control plane pods load certs at startup — they need restart!</span>

<span class="comment"># Option A: If using static pods (kubeadm default)</span>
<span class="comment"># Move and restore manifests to trigger restart:</span>
cd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
mv kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait for pod to stop...</span>
mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml .
<span class="comment"># Repeat for controller-manager, scheduler, etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># Option B: Force restart via crictl</span>
crictl pods --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs crictl stopp
crictl pods --name kube-controller-manager -q | xargs crictl stopp
crictl pods --name kube-scheduler -q | xargs crictl stopp

<span class="comment"># ── Verify renewal worked ──</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># All dates should now show ~1 year from today</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Certificate Renewal Workflow</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              CERTIFICATE RENEWAL PROCEDURE                       │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 1. Check Expiry │─── kubeadm certs check-expiration          │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                            │
  │           │                                                      │
  │           ▼                                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 2. Renew Certs  │─── kubeadm certs renew all                │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                            │
  │           │                                                      │
  │           ▼                                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 3. Restart      │─── Move/restore manifests or crictl       │
  │  │    Components   │    (API server, controller, scheduler)     │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                            │
  │           │                                                      │
  │           ▼                                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 4. Verify       │─── kubeadm certs check-expiration          │
  │  │    New Dates    │    (should show ~1 year from now)          │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                            │
  │           │                                                      │
  │           ▼                                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 5. Update       │─── Copy new admin.conf to ~/.kube/config  │
  │  │    kubeconfig   │    cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf           │
  │  └─────────────────┘    ~/.kube/config                          │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Don't Forget kubeconfig!</h5>
                    <p>After renewing certs, the kubeconfig files (<code>admin.conf</code>, etc.) also get updated. Copy the new admin.conf to <code>~/.kube/config</code> or <code>kubectl</code> will stop working with "certificate expired" errors!</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Automatic Renewal</h5>
                    <p><code>kubeadm upgrade</code> automatically renews all certificates during a cluster upgrade. If you upgrade regularly (recommended), you may never need manual renewal.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Certificate Troubleshooting — Common Errors &amp; Fixes</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │            CERTIFICATE TROUBLESHOOTING QUICK REFERENCE                       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: "x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid"              │
  │  ✅ FIX:   kubeadm certs renew all → restart control plane pods            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: "unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate             │
  │            signed by unknown authority"                                      │
  │  ✅ FIX:   Copy updated admin.conf: cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf           │
  │            ~/.kube/config                                                    │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: "error: You must be logged in to the server (Unauthorized)"      │
  │  ✅ FIX:   Check certificate CN matches expected user. Verify CSR was       │
  │            approved. Check RBAC bindings.                                    │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: API server won't start after cert renewal                        │
  │  ✅ FIX:   Check /var/log/containers/ for kube-apiserver logs.              │
  │            Verify cert files exist in /etc/kubernetes/pki/.                  │
  │            Check file permissions (certs should be 644, keys 600).          │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: "tls: bad certificate" on etcd                                   │
  │  ✅ FIX:   Renew etcd-specific certs: kubeadm certs renew etcd-server      │
  │            kubeadm certs renew etcd-peer                                    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ Certificate DO</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Check expiry monthly: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                            <li>Always restart control plane after renewal</li>
                            <li>Update <code>~/.kube/config</code> after renewing admin.conf</li>
                            <li>Use <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> — it renews certs automatically</li>
                            <li>Set correct CN/O in CSRs (CN=username, O=group)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ Certificate DON'T</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Ignore certificate expiry warnings</li>
                            <li>Share private keys (<code>.key</code> files) across teams</li>
                            <li>Skip the <code>signerName</code> field on CSR objects (required since 1.22)</li>
                            <li>Forget to base64-encode the CSR <code>request</code> field</li>
                            <li>Assume certs renew themselves — only <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> auto-renews</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <!-- ─── TLS/PKI Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>TLS Certificate Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>openssl x509 -in CERT -text -noout | grep -A1 Subject:</code> → verify CN and O fields</li>
                    <li><code>openssl x509 -in CERT -text -noout | grep "Not After"</code> → check expiry date</li>
                    <li><code>openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt CERT</code> → confirm signed by cluster CA</li>
                    <li><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> → view all certificate expiry dates at once</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get csr</code> → check pending/approved CSR objects</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe csr NAME</code> → verify signerName, status, and conditions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🏋️ Chapter 26 Practice Exercises</h5>
                    <p>These exercises simulate real CKA certificate questions.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 8 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 26.1 — Create User Certificate via CSR</h5>
                        <p>Generate a private key, create a CSR, submit it to Kubernetes, approve it, and extract the signed certificate.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Generate key: <code>openssl genrsa -out exam-user.key 2048</code></li>
                            <li>Create CSR: <code>openssl req -new -key exam-user.key -subj "/CN=exam-user/O=exam-team"</code></li>
                            <li>Submit K8s CSR object with <code>signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code></li>
                            <li>Approve: <code>kubectl certificate approve exam-user</code></li>
                            <li>Extract signed cert and verify CN with <code>openssl x509 -text</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: The CSR <code>request</code> field must be base64-encoded with <strong>no line wrapping</strong> — use <code>base64 -w 0</code>.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate private key</span>
openssl genrsa -out exam-user.key 2048

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create CSR (PEM format)</span>
openssl req -new -key exam-user.key \
  -subj "/CN=exam-user/O=exam-team" \
  -out exam-user.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Base64-encode the CSR (no line wrapping!)</span>
CSR_B64=$(cat exam-user.csr | base64 -w 0)

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create K8s CSR object</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: exam-user
spec:
  request: ${CSR_B64}
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
  usages:
  - client auth
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Approve the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve exam-user

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Extract the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr exam-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' \
  | base64 -d > exam-user.crt

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify the certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in exam-user.crt -text -noout | grep Subject
<span class="comment"># Subject: O = exam-team, CN = exam-user</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 3 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 26.2 — Inspect API Server Certificate</h5>
                        <p>Examine the API server's TLS certificate to identify key fields tested on the CKA exam.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>SSH to the control plane node</li>
                            <li>Run: <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout</code></li>
                            <li>Identify: Subject (CN), Issuer, expiry date, and Subject Alternative Names (SANs)</li>
                            <li>Verify it was signed by the cluster CA: <code>openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: SANs are in the <code>X509v3 Subject Alternative Name</code> section of the certificate output.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># SSH to control plane</span>
ssh controlplane

<span class="comment"># Inspect the API server certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout

<span class="comment"># Key fields to identify:</span>
<span class="comment"># Subject: CN = kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># Issuer:  CN = kubernetes   (signed by cluster CA)</span>
<span class="comment"># Not After: (expiry date — typically 1 year)</span>
<span class="comment"># X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes, DNS:kubernetes.default,</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes.default.svc,</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local,</span>
<span class="comment">#   IP Address:10.96.0.1, IP Address:192.168.x.x</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify CA signature</span>
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
  /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
<span class="comment"># apiserver.crt: OK</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 26.3 — Check and Renew Certificates</h5>
                        <p>Check certificate expiry dates and renew the API server certificate.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Check all certificate expiry dates: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                            <li>Renew the API server certificate only: <code>kubeadm certs renew apiserver</code></li>
                            <li>Restart the API server (move/restore static pod manifest)</li>
                            <li>Verify the new expiry date matches today + 1 year</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Moving the manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> stops the pod. Moving it back triggers a restart with the new cert.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check all certificate expiry dates</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Jan 01, 2026 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Jan 01, 2026 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Jan 01, 2026 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Renew API server certificate only</span>
kubeadm certs renew apiserver

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Restart API server (static pod)</span>
mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait for pod to stop (~10 seconds)</span>
sleep 10
mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># Wait for restart</span>
sleep 30

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify new expiry</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout \
  | grep "Not After"
<span class="comment"># Not After : (should be ~1 year from today)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify API server is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver</code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Kubernetes PKI Works: Certificate Chains, kubeadm cert renewal, CSR API, and TLS Bootstrap</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Kubernetes PKI — The Certificate Trust Chain</h5>
            <p>Every component in Kubernetes authenticates using TLS certificates. Understanding the trust chain helps you debug "x509: certificate signed by unknown authority" errors and manage certificate rotation.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUBERNETES PKI — Certificate Hierarchy                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │                     ROOT CA (ca.crt + ca.key)                         │   │
│  │                     /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.*                           │   │
│  │                     Self-signed, 10-year validity                     │   │
│  │                     Signs ALL cluster certificates                    │   │
│  └───────────────┬──────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                  │                      │                                     │
│         ┌────────▼────────┐    ┌────────▼────────┐                          │
│         │  API SERVER CERT │    │  ETCD CA        │                          │
│         │  apiserver.crt   │    │  etcd/ca.crt     │                          │
│         │  SANs:            │    │  Signs etcd certs│                          │
│         │   kubernetes      │    └────────┬────────┘                          │
│         │   kubernetes.     │             │                                    │
│         │   default         │    ┌────────▼────────┐                          │
│         │   127.0.0.1       │    │  ETCD SERVER     │                          │
│         │   10.96.0.1       │    │  etcd/server.crt │                          │
│         └────────┬──────────┘    │  CN=etcd-&lt;node&gt;  │                          │
│                  │               └──────────────────┘                          │
│                  │                                                            │
│         ┌────────▼──────────┐   ┌──────────────────────────┐                 │
│         │  KUBELET CLIENTS  │   │  FRONT-PROXY CA          │                 │
│         │  (signed by CA)   │   │  front-proxy-ca.crt      │                 │
│         │                    │   │  Signs aggregator certs   │                 │
│         │  apiserver-kubelet-│   └──────────────────────────┘                 │
│         │  client.crt       │                                                │
│         │  (API→kubelet auth)│                                                │
│         └───────────────────┘                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CERTIFICATE RENEWAL (CKA Exam Topic):                                       │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubeadm certs check-expiration                                      │   │
│  │ CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME     │   │
│  │ admin.conf                 Mar 10, 2025 09:00 UTC   364d              │   │
│  │ apiserver                  Mar 10, 2025 09:00 UTC   364d              │   │
│  │ apiserver-kubelet-client   Mar 10, 2025 09:00 UTC   364d              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ $ kubeadm certs renew all                                              │   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Renews ALL certificates. Restart control plane pods after.          │</span>   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ $ kubeadm certs renew apiserver  <span class="comment"># Renew only API server cert         │   │</span>
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CSR API — Requesting Client Certificates Programmatically:                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. User generates private key + CSR                                   │   │
│  │ 2. User creates CertificateSigningRequest K8s object                  │   │
│  │ 3. Admin approves: kubectl certificate approve &lt;csr-name&gt;            │   │
│  │ 4. controller-manager signs the CSR with CA key                       │   │
│  │ 5. Signed certificate stored in CSR.status.certificate                │   │
│  │ 6. User extracts: kubectl get csr &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.      │   │
│  │    certificate}' | base64 -d > user.crt                               │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Certificate management is directly tested on the CKA. Memorize: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> (view expiry), <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> (renew all), <code>kubeadm certs renew apiserver</code> (renew specific). After renewal, restart the affected control plane pod by moving its manifest temporarily. CSR API: <code>kubectl certificate approve</code> / <code>deny</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">CSR <code>request</code> field takes the raw CSR file content</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">The <code>request</code> field requires <strong>base64-encoded</strong> CSR content</span></li>
                    <li><code>signerName</code> is <strong>required</strong> since K8s 1.22 — use <code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code> for user certs</li>
                    <li>Certificates expire after <strong>1 year</strong> by default — <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all dates</li>
                    <li>After renewing certs, you MUST <strong>restart control plane pods</strong> (they load certs at startup)</li>
                    <li>The CN (Common Name) in the CSR becomes the <strong>username</strong> in K8s. The O (Organization) becomes the <strong>group</strong></li>
                    <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> is the default cert location — memorize this path</li>
                </ul>
                <ul>
                        <li><strong>Certificate locations</strong> — All certs live under <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code>. API server: <code>apiserver.crt</code>, <code>apiserver-key.crt</code>. CA: <code>ca.crt</code>, <code>ca.key</code>. etcd has its own directory: <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code></li>
                        <li><strong>CSR approval</strong> — <code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;csr-name&gt;</code>. The exam may ask you to create a CSR for a new user and approve it. Don't forget: the CSR object needs the right <code>signerName</code> (<code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code>)</li>
                        <li><strong>Certificate expiry</strong> — <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all cert expiry dates. Certs default to 1 year. CA cert is 10 years. If a cert expires, that component stops working</li>
                        <li><strong>After renewing certs, restart the component</strong> — For static pods (apiserver, controller-manager, scheduler): move the manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>, wait, move it back. This is the tested pattern</li>
                        <li><strong><code>openssl x509 -in cert.crt -text -noout</code></strong> — Memorize this for inspecting any cert. Look at Subject, Issuer, Not After, and SANs (Subject Alternative Names)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Certificate inspection one-liners:</strong><br>
                <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A2 "Validity"</code> — check expiry<br>
                <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Alternative"</code> — check SANs<br>
                <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> — all certs at once<br>
                <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> — renew everything (then restart control plane)</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 26)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Generate private key</td><td><code>openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create CSR</td><td><code>openssl req -new -key user.key -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team" -out user.csr</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Submit K8s CSR</td><td><code>kubectl apply -f csr.yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Approve CSR</td><td><code>kubectl certificate approve dev-user</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deny CSR</td><td><code>kubectl certificate deny dev-user</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Get signed cert</td><td><code>kubectl get csr dev-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > user.crt</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check expiry</td><td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Renew all certs</td><td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Inspect cert</td><td><code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>renew a specific cert</td><td><code>kubeadm certs renew apiserver</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>list pending certificate signing requests</td><td><code>kubectl get csr</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>approve a CSR</td><td><code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;csr-name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>deny a CSR</td><td><code>kubectl certificate deny &lt;csr-name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 8 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Check all certificate expiration dates: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                    <li>2. Inspect the API server cert's SANs: <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Alternative"</code></li>
                    <li>3. Generate a private key and CSR for user "devuser": <code>openssl genrsa -out devuser.key 2048</code> then <code>openssl req -new -key devuser.key -subj "/CN=devuser/O=dev" -out devuser.csr</code></li>
                    <li>4. Create and approve a Kubernetes CSR object for devuser</li>
                    <li>5. Extract the signed certificate and create a kubeconfig for devuser</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-lab"></span>
            <div class="scenario-box">
                <div class="scenario-header">
                    <span class="scenario-icon">🔥</span>
                    <h4>Scenario: "API Server Unreachable — Expired Certificates"</h4>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-content">
                    <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> <code>kubectl get nodes</code> returns <code>Unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate has expired</code>. The cluster appears completely down.</p>
                    <div class="scenario-steps">
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <span class="step-label">Step 1 — SSH to control plane</span>
                            <p>Can't use kubectl (API down). SSH directly: <code>ssh controlplane</code>. Check cert expiry: <code>sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> — shows expired dates.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <span class="step-label">Step 2 — Renew certificates</span>
                            <p><code>sudo kubeadm certs renew all</code> — regenerates all component certificates signed by the CA.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <span class="step-label">Step 3 — Restart control plane</span>
                            <p>Static pods don't auto-reload certs. Move manifests: <code>sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml /tmp/</code>, wait 10s, move back. Or: <code>sudo systemctl restart kubelet</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="scenario-step">
                            <span class="step-label">Step 4 — Update kubeconfig</span>
                            <p><code>sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config</code> — the admin.conf was also renewed. Now <code>kubectl get nodes</code> works again.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-outcome">
                        <strong>Lesson:</strong> Cert expiry is a silent killer — the cluster works fine until the exact moment certs expire, then everything breaks at once. Monitor with <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> proactively.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 26 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the cluster's PKI &amp; certificate flow — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where are the Kubernetes PKI certificates stored on a kubeadm cluster? List the key files and their purpose.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">default pki directory</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Default PKI directory:</span>
/etc/kubernetes/pki/

<span class="comment"># Key files:</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt              ← Cluster CA certificate</span>
<span class="comment"># ca.key              ← Cluster CA private key (PROTECT THIS!)</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver.crt       ← API server TLS certificate</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver.key       ← API server TLS private key</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client.crt  ← API→kubelet auth</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client.key</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client.crt     ← API→etcd auth</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client.key</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-ca.crt  ← Front proxy CA</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-ca.key</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client.key</span>
<span class="comment"># sa.key              ← SA signing key</span>
<span class="comment"># sa.pub              ← SA verification key</span>

<span class="comment"># etcd certificates:</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt, ca.key, server.crt, server.key,</span>
<span class="comment"># peer.crt, peer.key, healthcheck-client.crt/key</span>

<span class="comment"># Kubeconfig files (contain embedded certs):</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/
<span class="comment"># admin.conf, controller-manager.conf,</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf, kubelet.conf</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>All kubeadm PKI files live in <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code>. The CA cert/key pair is the root of trust — <code>ca.key</code> must be protected. On the CKA, you need to know this path for: checking certificate expiry, renewing certificates, creating CSRs signed by the cluster CA, and troubleshooting TLS errors. The etcd subdirectory has separate CA and certificates for etcd mTLS.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check when Kubernetes certificates expire using <code>kubeadm</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check all certificate expirations</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check ALL certificate expirations:</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client      Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager.conf    Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-healthcheck-client    Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-peer                  Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-server                Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client         Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf             Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>

<span class="comment"># Check a specific cert with openssl:</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -enddate
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Dec 15 10:30:00 2025 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># Check with days remaining:</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -enddate -checkend 2592000
<span class="comment"># Certificate will not expire  ← (within 30 days)</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all cert expiry dates at once — this is the fastest CKA method. Kubeadm certs are valid for 1 year by default. The CA cert is valid for 10 years. <code>openssl x509 -enddate</code> checks individual certs. <code>-checkend SECONDS</code> returns exit code 1 if cert expires within that time — useful in scripts. On the CKA, if you see API server TLS errors, cert expiry is the first thing to check.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you inspect the SANs (Subject Alternative Names) on the API server certificate?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">view full certificate details</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View full certificate details:</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -text | grep -A1 "Subject Alternative Name"
<span class="comment"># X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:controlplane, DNS:kubernetes, DNS:kubernetes.default,</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes.default.svc,</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local,</span>
<span class="comment">#   IP Address:10.96.0.1, IP Address:192.168.1.10</span>

<span class="comment"># Just extract SANs cleanly:</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -ext subjectAltName

<span class="comment"># Check Subject (CN):</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -subject
<span class="comment"># subject= /CN=kube-apiserver</span>

<span class="comment"># Check Issuer (who signed it):</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -issuer
<span class="comment"># issuer= /CN=kubernetes</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>SANs define which hostnames/IPs the cert is valid for. The API server cert must include: <code>kubernetes</code>, <code>kubernetes.default</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local</code>, the ClusterIP of the kubernetes service (usually 10.96.0.1), and the node's IP. On the CKA, if you add a load balancer or new DNS name, you must regenerate the API server cert with updated SANs using <code>--apiserver-cert-extra-sans</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a CertificateSigningRequest (CSR) for a new user <code>devuser</code> in Kubernetes and approve it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate private key</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate private key</span>
openssl genrsa -out devuser.key 2048

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create CSR (CN=username, O=group)</span>
openssl req -new -key devuser.key \
-subj "/CN=devuser/O=developers" -out devuser.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Base64 encode the CSR</span>
cat devuser.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n'

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create K8s CSR object</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
name: devuser-csr
spec:
request: $(cat devuser.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')
signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
expirationSeconds: 86400    <span class="comment"># 24 hours</span>
usages:
- client auth
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Approve the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve devuser-csr

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Extract the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr devuser-csr \
-o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > devuser.crt

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
openssl x509 -in devuser.crt -noout -subject
<span class="comment"># subject= /CN=devuser/O=developers</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The CSR workflow is a HIGH-WEIGHT CKA task. Key details: (1) <code>CN</code> becomes the username, <code>O</code> becomes the group, (2) <code>signerName</code> must be <code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code> for user certs, (3) <code>usages</code> must include <code>client auth</code>, (4) the <code>request</code> field must be base64-encoded with no newlines (<code>tr -d '\n'</code>). After approval, extract the cert from <code>.status.certificate</code>. Common mistake: forgetting to base64-encode the CSR or using the wrong signerName.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you renew all kubeadm certificates? What happens to control plane components?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">renew all certificates at once</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Renew ALL certificates at once:</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate embedded in admin.conf renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate front-proxy-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ... all renewed</span>

<span class="comment"># Renew specific certificate:</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver-kubelet-client

<span class="comment"># CRITICAL: Restart control plane after renewal!</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm static pods auto-restart on cert change?</span>
<span class="comment"># NO — you must restart them:</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 1: Move and restore manifests</span>
cd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
sudo mv kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait ~20s for kubelet to stop the pod</span>
sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml .
<span class="comment"># Repeat for controller-manager and scheduler</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Restart kubelet (easier)</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Method 3: If using crictl</span>
sudo crictl pods --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs sudo crictl stopp

<span class="comment"># Verify new certs:</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># ← Should show dates ~1 year from now</span>

<span class="comment"># Also update admin kubeconfig:</span>
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) ~/.kube/config</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> renews every certificate. CRITICAL: after renewal, restart the control plane components so they pick up new certs. Static pods read certs from disk — if you restart kubelet, it detects the manifest changes and recreates the pods. Don't forget to update <code>~/.kube/config</code> (copy the new <code>admin.conf</code>). On the CKA, cert renewal + restart + kubeconfig update is a common multi-step question. Certs renewed via kubeadm inherit the same 1-year validity.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a kubeconfig file for user <code>devuser</code> using a signed certificate.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">assuming you have: devuser.key, devuser.crt, ca.crt</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Assuming you have: devuser.key, devuser.crt, ca.crt</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Set cluster</span>
kubectl config set-cluster my-cluster \
--certificate-authority=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
--server=https://192.168.1.10:6443 \
--embed-certs=true \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Set credentials</span>
kubectl config set-credentials devuser \
--client-certificate=devuser.crt \
--client-key=devuser.key \
--embed-certs=true \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Set context</span>
kubectl config set-context devuser-context \
--cluster=my-cluster \
--user=devuser \
--namespace=dev \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Use context</span>
kubectl config use-context devuser-context \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Test</span>
kubectl get pods \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig -n dev
<span class="comment"># Error (Forbidden) ← Need RBAC!</span>
<span class="comment"># This is expected — cert gives identity, RBAC gives permissions</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubeconfig creation is a 4-step process: set-cluster, set-credentials, set-context, use-context. <code>--embed-certs=true</code> embeds certs directly in the kubeconfig (portable). On the CKA, the order matters: cluster → credentials → context → use-context. After creating the kubeconfig, the user still needs RBAC (Role+RoleBinding) to do anything. Without RBAC, they authenticate successfully but all requests return Forbidden.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The API server won't start and you see TLS certificate errors in the kubelet logs. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check kubelet logs for api server errors</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kubelet logs for API server errors</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -l --no-pager | grep -i "tls\|cert\|x509" | tail -20
<span class="comment"># "x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid"</span>
<span class="comment"># "tls: private key does not match public key"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check cert expiry</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -enddate -checkend 0
<span class="comment"># Certificate has expired ← Problem found!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check cert-key match</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -modulus | md5sum
openssl rsa -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key \
-noout -modulus | md5sum
<span class="comment"># If hashes differ → cert/key mismatch!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check static pod manifest references correct files</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep -E "cert|key"
<span class="comment">#   --tls-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt</span>
<span class="comment">#   --tls-private-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix expired certs</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver-kubelet-client

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Restart</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify API is back</span>
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>TLS troubleshooting: check kubelet logs for x509 errors, verify cert expiry, confirm cert/key match (modulus comparison), verify manifest paths. Common causes: (1) expired certs — renew with kubeadm, (2) cert/key mismatch — regenerate, (3) wrong file paths in manifests — fix the static pod YAML. On the CKA, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> is your first stop when the API server is down.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between the cluster CA certificate and component certificates?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Certificate</th>
                                        <th>Type</th>
                                        <th>Purpose</th>
                                        <th>Validity</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ca.crt/key</code></td>
                                        <td>Root CA</td>
                                        <td>Signs all other certs</td>
                                        <td>10 years</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>apiserver.crt</code></td>
                                        <td>Server cert</td>
                                        <td>API server TLS</td>
                                        <td>1 year</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>apiserver-kubelet-client</code></td>
                                        <td>Client cert</td>
                                        <td>API→kubelet auth</td>
                                        <td>1 year</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>apiserver-etcd-client</code></td>
                                        <td>Client cert</td>
                                        <td>API→etcd auth</td>
                                        <td>1 year</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>front-proxy-*</code></td>
                                        <td>Proxy CA+cert</td>
                                        <td>Aggregation layer</td>
                                        <td>1 year</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>etcd/ca.crt</code></td>
                                        <td>etcd CA</td>
                                        <td>etcd internal TLS</td>
                                        <td>10 years</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify cert is signed by cluster ca</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify cert is signed by cluster CA:</span>
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
<span class="comment"># apiserver.crt: OK</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The cluster CA (<code>ca.crt/ca.key</code>) is the trust root — it signs all component certs. CAs last 10 years; component certs last 1 year. There are TWO CAs: the cluster CA (for API server, kubelets) and the etcd CA (for etcd peers/clients). On the CKA, <code>openssl verify</code> checks that a cert was signed by a specific CA. Client certs authenticate components to each other (mutual TLS). If any component cert doesn't chain to the correct CA, communication fails.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you deny a CertificateSigningRequest and list all CSRs?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all csrs</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all CSRs:</span>
kubectl get csr
<span class="comment"># NAME          AGE   SIGNERNAME                         REQUESTOR       CONDITION</span>
<span class="comment"># devuser-csr   2m    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client admin           Approved,Issued</span>
<span class="comment"># baduser-csr   1m    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client admin           Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Deny a CSR:</span>
kubectl certificate deny baduser-csr
<span class="comment"># certificatesigningrequest.certificates.k8s.io/baduser-csr denied</span>

<span class="comment"># Delete a CSR:</span>
kubectl delete csr baduser-csr

<span class="comment"># View CSR details:</span>
kubectl get csr devuser-csr -o yaml
<span class="comment"># status:</span>
<span class="comment">#   conditions:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - type: Approved</span>
<span class="comment">#     reason: KubectlApprove</span>
<span class="comment">#     message: "This CSR was approved by kubectl certificate approve."</span>

<span class="comment"># Describe for more info:</span>
kubectl describe csr devuser-csr
<span class="comment"># Requesting User:  kubernetes-admin</span>
<span class="comment"># Signer:          kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</span>
<span class="comment"># Status:          Approved,Issued</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CSR management: <code>kubectl certificate approve|deny</code> to handle requests. CSRs have three possible states: Pending, Approved, Denied. On the CKA, you'll see Pending CSRs (e.g., from node bootstrapping or user cert requests). <code>kubectl get csr</code> lists all with status. Key: CSRs don't auto-delete — clean up with <code>kubectl delete csr</code>. The CONDITION column shows the current state. Node bootstrap CSRs use <code>kubernetes.io/kubelet-serving</code> as signerName.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you add extra SANs to the API server certificate for a new load balancer IP <code>10.0.0.100</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">delete old api server cert</span></div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Delete old API server cert</span>
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Regenerate with extra SAN</span>
sudo kubeadm init phase certs apiserver \
--apiserver-cert-extra-sans=10.0.0.100,lb.example.com

<span class="comment"># OR: Edit kubeadm config first, then regenerate:</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get cm kubeadm-config -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Add to ClusterConfiguration:</span>
<span class="comment">#   apiServer:</span>
<span class="comment">#     certSANs:</span>
<span class="comment">#     - 10.0.0.100</span>
<span class="comment">#     - lb.example.com</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Restart API server</span>
sudo crictl pods --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs sudo crictl stopp
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify new SANs</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -text | grep -A2 "Subject Alternative"
<span class="comment"># DNS:kubernetes, DNS:lb.example.com, ...</span>
<span class="comment"># IP:10.96.0.1, IP:192.168.1.10, IP:10.0.0.100 ← New!</span>

<span class="comment"># CAUTION: Never delete ca.crt/ca.key!</span>
<span class="comment"># Only apiserver.crt/key are safe to regenerate</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>To add SANs: (1) delete old API server cert/key, (2) regenerate with <code>kubeadm init phase certs apiserver --apiserver-cert-extra-sans=...</code>, (3) restart API server. On the CKA, this is needed when adding a load balancer or additional access point. NEVER delete <code>ca.crt/ca.key</code> — only the API server cert is safe to regenerate. The kubeadm config in the <code>kube-system/kubeadm-config</code> ConfigMap stores the cluster configuration including extra SANs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 26 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch27">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 27 · Pod Security &amp; Admission Control</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Kubernetes provides multiple layers of pod-level security. <strong>Pod Security Standards (PSS)</strong> define three security profiles (Privileged, Baseline, Restricted). <strong>Pod Security Admission (PSA)</strong> enforces these standards at the namespace level using labels. <strong>Security Contexts</strong> let you set user IDs, capabilities, and read-only filesystems per pod/container. <strong>Admission Controllers</strong> intercept API requests to validate and mutate objects before they hit etcd. Mastering these is essential for CKA and real-world cluster hardening.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🛡️ Pod Security</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Pod Security Standards — Privileged, Baseline, and Restricted profiles</li>
                    <li>✅ Pod Security Admission — enforce/audit/warn modes via namespace labels</li>
                    <li>✅ Security Contexts — runAsNonRoot, capabilities, readOnlyRootFilesystem</li>
                    <li>✅ Admission Controllers — built-in and webhook-based controllers</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Pod Security on the CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>Expect questions that require you to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Apply PSA labels to namespaces to enforce security standards</li>
                    <li>Configure SecurityContext fields (runAsNonRoot, runAsUser, capabilities)</li>
                    <li>Know which admission controllers are enabled by default</li>
                    <li>Troubleshoot pods rejected by admission policies</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
