        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-a">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">⌨️ Appendix A · kubectl Admin Command Reference</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">Complete kubectl admin command reference with CKA speed aliases — sections A.1 through A.7. Every command uses the <strong>easypj</strong> project context. Bookmark this page for the exam.</p>

        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>Exam Day Setup</strong> — Run these aliases <strong>first</strong> when your exam terminal opens:</p>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">~/.bashrc — exam aliases</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash">alias k=kubectl
alias kgp='kubectl get pods'
alias kgs='kubectl get svc'
alias kgn='kubectl get nodes'
alias kd='kubectl describe'
alias kaf='kubectl apply -f'
alias kdel='kubectl delete'
export do='--dry-run=client -o yaml'
export now='--force --grace-period=0'

<span class="comment"># Apply immediately:</span>
source ~/.bashrc</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="info-box warning">
            <h5>⚠️ Context Switching — #1 Exam Killer</h5>
            <p>The CKA exam uses <strong>multiple clusters</strong>. Each question tells you which cluster to use. If you forget to switch, you'll modify the <strong>wrong cluster</strong> and lose points with no way to undo. Run the context command <strong>first</strong> for every question:</p>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — context switching (EVERY question!)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The exam tells you: "Use context: k8s-cluster-1"</span>
kubectl config use-context k8s-cluster-1

<span class="comment"># Verify you're on the right cluster</span>
kubectl config current-context
<span class="comment"># k8s-cluster-1</span>

<span class="comment"># List all available contexts</span>
kubectl config get-contexts
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME              CLUSTER           AUTHINFO</span>
<span class="comment"># *         k8s-cluster-1     k8s-cluster-1     admin</span>
<span class="comment">#           k8s-cluster-2     k8s-cluster-2     admin</span>

<span class="comment"># Set default namespace (saves typing -n every time)</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>kubectl Command Flow — What Happens When You Run a Command</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
 ┌─────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐
 │  YOU type:   │    │  ~/.kube/    │    │  API Server  │    │    etcd      │
 │  kubectl ... │───▶│  config      │───▶│  :6443       │───▶│  (store)     │
 │              │    │  (auth+ctx)  │    │  (AuthN/Z)   │    │              │
 └─────────────┘    └──────────────┘    └──────┬───────┘    └──────────────┘
                                               │
                    ┌──────────────────────────┬┴──────────────────────┐
                    ▼                          ▼                      ▼
             ┌─────────────┐         ┌──────────────┐       ┌──────────────┐
             │  Scheduler  │         │  Controller  │       │   kubelet    │
             │  (assigns   │         │  Manager     │       │  (runs pods  │
             │   nodes)    │         │  (reconcile) │       │   on nodes)  │
             └─────────────┘         └──────────────┘       └──────────────┘
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── Section A.1: Cluster Management Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.1 Cluster Management Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>These commands help you inspect the overall health and capabilities of your cluster. Use them <strong>first</strong> when troubleshooting any cluster issue.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Cluster Health Check Decision Tree</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
                        🏥 Cluster Health Check
                        ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
                                │
                    ┌───────────┼───────────┐
                    ▼           ▼           ▼
              kubectl       kubectl     kubectl
             cluster-info  get nodes   get cs
                    │           │           │
                    ▼           ▼           ▼
              ✅ API server  ✅ Nodes    ⚠️ Deprecated
              reachable?     Ready?      in v1.19+
                    │           │           │
                    ▼           ▼           ▼
               If ❌:       If NotReady:  Use instead:
              Check         SSH to node,  kubectl get
              kubeconfig    check kubelet componentstatuses
              + API pod     + containerd  (still works)
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Cluster Inspection Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Shows</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl cluster-info</code></td>
                            <td>API server URL + CoreDNS URL</td>
                            <td>First check — is the API reachable?</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code></td>
                            <td>scheduler, controller-manager, etcd health</td>
                            <td>Control plane component health check</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl top nodes</code></td>
                            <td>CPU/memory usage per node</td>
                            <td>Resource pressure diagnosis</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl top pods --all-namespaces</code></td>
                            <td>CPU/memory usage per pod</td>
                            <td>Find resource-hungry pods</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code></td>
                            <td>Recent cluster events (newest last)</td>
                            <td>Troubleshooting — what just happened?</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl api-resources</code></td>
                            <td>All resource types + short names + API group</td>
                            <td>Discover resource types and their short names</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl api-versions</code></td>
                            <td>All API group/versions available</td>
                            <td>Check if an API version exists</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — cluster inspection</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check cluster is reachable</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes control plane is running at https://10.0.0.10:6443</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS is running at https://10.0.0.10:6443/api/v1/namespaces/kube-system/...</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick health check</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses
<span class="comment"># NAME                 STATUS    MESSAGE</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler            Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager   Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-0               Healthy   ok</span>

<span class="comment"># Resource usage (requires metrics-server)</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME     CPU(cores)   CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># cp-1     213m         10%    1024Mi          25%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1 156m         7%     768Mi           19%</span>

<span class="comment"># Find the most recent events</span>
kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | tail -20

<span class="comment"># Discover all resource types with short names</span>
kubectl api-resources --sort-by=name | head -20
<span class="comment"># NAME          SHORTNAMES   APIVERSION   NAMESPACED   KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># configmaps    cm           v1           true         ConfigMap</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy       apps/v1      true         Deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># pods          po           v1           true         Pod</span>
<span class="comment"># services      svc          v1           true         Service</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Short Names Save Time</h5>
                <p>Use short names on the exam: <code>po</code> (pods), <code>svc</code> (services), <code>deploy</code> (deployments), <code>cm</code> (configmaps), <code>ns</code> (namespaces), <code>no</code> (nodes), <code>pv</code> (persistentvolumes), <code>pvc</code> (persistentvolumeclaims), <code>sa</code> (serviceaccounts), <code>netpol</code> (networkpolicies), <code>ing</code> (ingresses).</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.2: Node Management ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.2 Node Management</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Node management is a core CKA skill — you'll cordon, drain, and uncordon nodes during upgrades and maintenance.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Node Maintenance Lifecycle</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    NODE MAINTENANCE WORKFLOW                            │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                         │
  │   ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────┐     │
  │   │  NORMAL  │    │ CORDON   │    │    DRAIN     │    │ UNCORDON │     │
  │   │          │───▶│          │───▶│              │───▶│          │     │
  │   │ Schedul- │    │ No new   │    │ Evict all    │    │ Allow    │     │
  │   │ able ✅  │    │ pods ⛔  │    │ pods 🚀      │    │ pods ✅  │     │
  │   └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────────┘    └──────────┘     │
  │                                          │                              │
  │                                   ┌──────┴──────┐                      │
  │                                   │  PERFORM    │                      │
  │                                   │  MAINTENANCE│                      │
  │                                   │  (upgrade,  │                      │
  │                                   │   reboot)   │                      │
  │                                   └─────────────┘                      │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Node Management Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th><th>CKA Context</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code></td>
                            <td>List nodes with IP, OS, kernel, container runtime</td>
                            <td>First check during troubleshooting</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Full node details: conditions, capacity, pods, taints</td>
                            <td>Diagnose NotReady, resource pressure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Mark unschedulable (existing pods stay)</td>
                            <td>Pre-maintenance — stop new pods</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code></td>
                            <td>Cordon + evict all pods (except DaemonSets)</td>
                            <td>Safely evacuate before maintenance</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Mark schedulable again</td>
                            <td>After maintenance — resume scheduling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node&gt; key=value:NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Add taint — only pods with matching toleration can schedule</td>
                            <td>Isolate nodes for specific workloads</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl label nodes &lt;node&gt; disk=ssd</code></td>
                            <td>Add label — used with nodeSelector for scheduling</td>
                            <td>Target pods to specific nodes</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — node maintenance for easypj</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check current node status</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS   ROLES           VERSION   INTERNAL-IP   OS-IMAGE</span>
<span class="comment"># cp-1       Ready    control-plane   v1.31.0   10.0.0.10     Ubuntu 22.04</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   Ready    &lt;none&gt;          v1.31.0   10.0.0.11     Ubuntu 22.04</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2   Ready    &lt;none&gt;          v1.31.0   10.0.0.12     Ubuntu 22.04</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Prepare worker-1 for maintenance</span>
kubectl cordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 cordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Drain it (move easypj pods to worker-2)</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># evicting pod easypj/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-xk2vn</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-xk2vn evicted</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify pods moved</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME                          READY   NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q   1/1     worker-2  ← moved here</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Perform maintenance (upgrade kubelet, etc.)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Bring node back</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 uncordoned</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Drain Fails? Common Causes</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Unmanaged pods</strong> — Pods not managed by a controller (bare pods). Fix: add <code>--force</code> flag (pods will be deleted, not rescheduled!)</li>
                    <li><strong>PodDisruptionBudget violated</strong> — PDB prevents eviction. Fix: check PDB with <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code>, adjust <code>minAvailable</code></li>
                    <li><strong>emptyDir data loss</strong> — Need <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> flag to acknowledge data loss</li>
                    <li><strong>DaemonSet pods</strong> — Always need <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> (they're present on every node)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.3: Workload Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.3 Workload Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Deployment lifecycle commands — create, scale, update, rollback. These are your bread and butter on the CKA exam.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Deployment Rollout Lifecycle</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  CREATE ──▶ SCALE ──▶ UPDATE ──▶ ROLLBACK
    │          │         │          │
    ▼          ▼         ▼          ▼
  kubectl    kubectl   kubectl    kubectl
  create     scale     set image  rollout undo
  deploy     deploy    deploy/    deploy/
  --image    --rep=5   name       name
    │          │         │          │
    ▼          ▼         ▼          ▼
  1 replica   5 reps   Rolling    Previous
  created     running  update     revision
                       started    restored

  📊 MONITOR: kubectl rollout status deploy/name
  📜 HISTORY: kubectl rollout history deploy/name
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Deployment &amp; Workload Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th><th>CKA Speed Tip</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3</code></td>
                            <td>Create deployment with 3 replicas</td>
                            <td>Fastest way — no YAML needed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5</code></td>
                            <td>Scale to 5 replicas</td>
                            <td>Instant — no rolling update</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web web=easypj:v2</code></td>
                            <td>Update container image (triggers rolling update)</td>
                            <td>Container name must match spec</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Watch rollout progress</td>
                            <td>Blocks until complete or failed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Show revision history</td>
                            <td>See all past versions</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Rollback to previous revision</td>
                            <td>Instant rollback — saves exam time</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web --to-revision=2</code></td>
                            <td>Rollback to specific revision</td>
                            <td>When previous isn't enough</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — easypj deployment lifecycle</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create deployment imperatively</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML without creating (exam trick!)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Scale up for traffic</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Update to new version (triggers rolling update)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web web=easypj:v2 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "easypj-web" rollout to finish: 2 of 5 updated...</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment "easypj-web" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># Something wrong? Rollback!</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web rolled back</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p>The <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> combo is your best friend. Use it to generate YAML for any resource, then edit as needed. With the <code>$do</code> alias: <code>k create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; deploy.yaml</code></p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.4: Debugging Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.4 Debugging Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Debugging is <strong>30% of the CKA exam</strong>. Master these commands — they're your diagnostic toolkit.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Pod Debugging Decision Tree</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
                        🔍 Pod Not Working?
                        ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
                                │
               ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
               ▼                ▼                ▼
          Pod Status?      Container        Can't reach
               │           crashing?        the service?
               │                │                │
    ┌──────────┼──────┐        ▼                ▼
    ▼          ▼      ▼   kubectl logs     kubectl get
  Pending  CrashLoop  Image  pod -c ctr    endpoints svc
    │      BackOff    Pull     │                │
    ▼        ▼       BackOff   ▼                ▼
  describe  logs     describe  Fix app      Empty? →
  pod →     pod →    pod →     crash or     Labels don't
  check     check    check     missing      match between
  events    app      image/    config       pod &amp; service
            error    secret
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Debug &amp; Inspect Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Shows</th><th>Debug Scenario</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Full pod details, events, conditions</td>
                            <td>First stop for ANY pod issue</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Container stdout/stderr</td>
                            <td>App crash, startup failure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            <td>Logs from previous (crashed) container</td>
                            <td>CrashLoopBackOff — see WHY it crashed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Logs from specific container in multi-container pod</td>
                            <td>Sidecar / init container issues</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></td>
                            <td>Open shell inside container</td>
                            <td>Check files, env vars, network from inside</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl port-forward pod/&lt;pod&gt; 8080:8000</code></td>
                            <td>Forward local 8080 → pod 8000</td>
                            <td>Test pod directly without Service</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=&lt;pod&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Events for specific pod only</td>
                            <td>Targeted event search</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt; --as=&lt;user&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Check RBAC permissions</td>
                            <td>Forbidden errors — who can do what?</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — debugging easypj pods</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pod stuck in CrashLoopBackOff — investigate</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  BackOff  kubelet  Back-off restarting failed container</span>

<span class="comment"># Get logs from the crashed container</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash -n easypj --previous
<span class="comment"># ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'easypj.settings'</span>

<span class="comment"># Shell into a running pod to investigate</span>
kubectl exec -it easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q -n easypj -- /bin/sh
<span class="comment"># $ env | grep DB          ← check environment variables</span>
<span class="comment"># $ cat /app/manage.py     ← check files exist</span>
<span class="comment"># $ python manage.py check ← test Django</span>
<span class="comment"># $ exit</span>

<span class="comment"># Test pod connectivity directly</span>
kubectl port-forward pod/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q 8080:8000 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Then: curl http://localhost:8080/jobs/</span>

<span class="comment"># Check who can create pods in easypj namespace</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n easypj --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Log Gotcha: Multi-Container Pods</h5>
                <p>If a pod has multiple containers (sidecar pattern), <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> will fail with <strong>"a container name must be specified"</strong>. Always use <code>-c &lt;container-name&gt;</code>. List containers with: <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'</code></p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.5: RBAC Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.5 RBAC Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) commands for managing who can do what in your cluster. These are frequently tested on the CKA exam.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>RBAC Components — The Permission Chain</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    RBAC PERMISSION MODEL                           │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                    │
  │   WHO (Subject)         BINDING              WHAT (Role)           │
  │   ──────────────    ─────────────────    ──────────────────        │
  │                                                                    │
  │   👤 User            RoleBinding           Role                    │
  │   👥 Group           (namespace-scoped)    (namespace-scoped)      │
  │   🤖 ServiceAccount  ─────────────────    ──────────────────       │
  │                      ClusterRoleBinding    ClusterRole              │
  │                      (cluster-wide)        (cluster-wide)          │
  │                                                                    │
  │   Example for easypj:                                              │
  │   ┌────────────┐  ┌─────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐       │
  │   │ easypj-sa  │──│ easypj-binding  │──│ easypj-role      │       │
  │   │ (SA)       │  │ (RoleBinding)   │  │ get,list pods    │       │
  │   └────────────┘  └─────────────────┘  │ in ns: easypj   │       │
  │                                         └──────────────────┘       │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>RBAC Imperative Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Creates</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create role easypj-role --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Namespace-scoped Role</td>
                            <td>easypj namespace only</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create clusterrole node-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=nodes</code></td>
                            <td>Cluster-wide ClusterRole</td>
                            <td>All namespaces</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding --role=easypj-role --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>RoleBinding (links SA → Role)</td>
                            <td>easypj namespace</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding admin-binding --clusterrole=cluster-admin --user=admin</code></td>
                            <td>ClusterRoleBinding</td>
                            <td>Cluster-wide</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa</code></td>
                            <td>Lists all permissions for that SA</td>
                            <td>Debug RBAC issues</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — RBAC setup for easypj</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ServiceAccount for easypj app</span>
kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Create Role: can read pods and services</span>
kubectl create role easypj-role \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=pods,services \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Bind the role to the ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding \
  --role=easypj-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Verify permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods -n easypj \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n easypj \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># no  ← correct! We only granted get,list,watch</span>

<span class="comment"># List ALL permissions for the SA</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.6: Certificate Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.6 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-6">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.6 Certificate Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Kubernetes uses TLS certificates extensively. Certificate management — checking expiry, renewing, and inspecting certs — is a core CKA topic.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Kubernetes Certificate Architecture</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              KUBERNETES PKI — /etc/kubernetes/pki/                  │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                     │
  │  📁 /etc/kubernetes/pki/                                            │
  │  ├── ca.crt + ca.key               ← Cluster CA (root of trust)    │
  │  ├── apiserver.crt + apiserver.key  ← API Server cert               │
  │  ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.crt   ← API → kubelet auth           │
  │  ├── front-proxy-ca.crt            ← Aggregation layer CA          │
  │  ├── sa.key + sa.pub               ← ServiceAccount signing        │
  │  └── etcd/                                                          │
  │      ├── ca.crt + ca.key           ← etcd CA (separate!)           │
  │      ├── server.crt + server.key   ← etcd server cert              │
  │      ├── peer.crt + peer.key       ← etcd peer-to-peer             │
  │      └── healthcheck-client.crt    ← etcd health check             │
  │                                                                     │
  │  ⏰ Default validity: 1 year (except CA: 10 years)                  │
  │  🔄 kubeadm certs renew all ← renews all 1-year certs              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Certificate Management Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Does</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></td>
                            <td>Show expiry dates for all certs</td>
                            <td>Regular health check / before renewal</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td>
                            <td>Renew all kubeadm-managed certificates</td>
                            <td>Before expiry or after upgrade</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>openssl x509 -in &lt;cert&gt; -text -noout</code></td>
                            <td>Inspect certificate details (CN, SANs, dates)</td>
                            <td>Verify specific cert properties</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — certificate management</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check expiration dates for ALL certs</span>
sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client      Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager.conf    Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-healthcheck-client    Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-peer                  Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-server                Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client         Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf             Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># CA                         Dec 12, 2035 10:30 UTC   10y  ← CA lasts 10 years</span>

<span class="comment"># Renew all certificates</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver-kubelet-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># IMPORTANT: Restart control plane after renewal!</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Inspect a specific certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A2 "Validity"
<span class="comment">#     Validity</span>
<span class="comment">#         Not Before: Dec 15 10:30:00 2025 GMT</span>
<span class="comment">#         Not After : Dec 15 10:30:00 2026 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># Check Subject Alternative Names (SANs)</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Subject Alternative"
<span class="comment">#     X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:</span>
<span class="comment">#         DNS:cp-1, DNS:kubernetes, DNS:kubernetes.default, IP:10.96.0.1, IP:10.0.0.10</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ After Renewing Certs</h5>
                <p>You <strong>must restart</strong> the control plane components after renewing certificates. With kubeadm, simply restart kubelet: <code>sudo systemctl restart kubelet</code>. The static pods (API server, controller-manager, scheduler) will be recreated automatically. If you forget this step, the old (expired) certs remain loaded in memory!</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.7: etcd Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.7 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-7">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.7 etcd Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>etcd backup and restore is <strong>guaranteed on the CKA exam</strong>. Every command requires TLS flags — memorize the cert paths.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>etcd Backup &amp; Restore Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                 etcd BACKUP &amp; RESTORE WORKFLOW                     │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                     │
  │  📸 BACKUP                          🔄 RESTORE                      │
  │  ──────────                         ─────────                       │
  │                                                                     │
  │  etcdctl snapshot save              etcdctl snapshot restore        │
  │       │                                   │                         │
  │       ▼                                   ▼                         │
  │  /opt/backup/                        /var/lib/etcd-restored/        │
  │  etcd-snapshot.db                    (new data dir)                 │
  │       │                                   │                         │
  │       ▼                                   ▼                         │
  │  etcdctl snapshot                    Update etcd pod manifest:      │
  │  status (verify)                     --data-dir=/var/lib/           │
  │                                      etcd-restored                  │
  │                                           │                         │
  │                                           ▼                         │
  │  ⚠️ ALL COMMANDS REQUIRE:            Restart: etcd auto-restarts    │
  │  --cacert, --cert, --key             from static pod manifest       │
  │  + ETCDCTL_API=3                                                    │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>etcd Admin Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th><th>CKA Exam Note</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save &lt;file&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Create backup snapshot</td>
                            <td>Always specify full path: <code>/opt/backup/snapshot.db</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore &lt;file&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Restore from snapshot</td>
                            <td>Use <code>--data-dir</code> to set new directory</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status &lt;file&gt; --write-table</code></td>
                            <td>Verify backup integrity</td>
                            <td>Shows hash, revision, total keys</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list</code></td>
                            <td>List etcd cluster members</td>
                            <td>Verify HA etcd cluster health</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd backup &amp; restore (CKA exam)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══ STEP 1: BACKUP ═══
<span class="comment"># The TLS flags are MANDATORY — memorize these paths!</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379
<span class="comment"># Snapshot saved at /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 2: VERIFY BACKUP ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --write-table
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | 3c5d8a2f |    48291 |       1043 |     4.1 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 3: RESTORE ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 4: UPDATE ETCD MANIFEST ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the static pod manifest:</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># Change: --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># To:     --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># ALSO update the hostPath volume:</span>
<span class="comment"># path: /var/lib/etcd → path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># etcd will auto-restart with restored data</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ VERIFY ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># Shows: member ID, name, peer URLs, client URLs</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ The 3 Cert Flags — Don't Forget Any!</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</code> — etcd CA certificate</li>
                    <li><code>--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</code> — etcd server certificate</li>
                    <li><code>--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code> — etcd server private key</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Also: <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> is required. Without it, etcdctl defaults to API v2 which uses completely different commands. If your command returns an error about unknown flags, check this first!</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Restore Creates a NEW Data Directory</h5>
                <p>Never restore into the existing <code>/var/lib/etcd</code> directory. Always use a new path like <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>, then update the etcd static pod manifest to point to the new directory. This prevents data corruption and allows you to keep the old data as a safety net.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Appendix A Summary ─── -->
        <div class="summary-box">
            <h4>📋 Appendix A — Quick Reference Card</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.1</strong> — Cluster health: <code>cluster-info</code>, <code>top nodes</code>, <code>get events</code>, <code>api-resources</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.2</strong> — Node lifecycle: <code>cordon</code> → <code>drain</code> → maintain → <code>uncordon</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.3</strong> — Workloads: <code>create deploy</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>set image</code>, <code>rollout undo</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.4</strong> — Debug: <code>describe</code>, <code>logs --previous</code>, <code>exec -it</code>, <code>port-forward</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.5</strong> — RBAC: <code>create role</code>, <code>create rolebinding</code>, <code>auth can-i</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.6</strong> — Certs: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code>, <code>renew all</code>, <code>openssl x509</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.7</strong> — etcd: <code>snapshot save</code>, <code>snapshot restore</code>, always use TLS flags + <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </article>

    
        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-b">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">📝 Appendix B · CKA YAML Templates</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">12 fully annotated YAML templates covering all CKA exam resources — sections B.1 through B.12. Every template uses the <strong>easypj</strong> project with the <strong>jobpost</strong> app. Copy, adapt, deploy.</p>

        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>YAML Template Map — Which Template for Which Task?</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │            CKA YAML TEMPLATE QUICK-FIND MAP                            │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                         │
  │  🏗️ WORKLOADS               🌐 NETWORKING          🔒 SECURITY         │
  │  ─────────────              ─────────────          ─────────────        │
  │  B.1  Pod                   B.3  Service (3 types) B.6  Role+Binding    │
  │  B.2  Deployment            B.8  NetworkPolicy     B.7  ClusterRole     │
